window.QUIZ_DATA = {"exams":[{"examNumber":1,"sdls":[{"sdlNumber":1,"title":"SDL 1 — Embryology of the Foregut","questions":[{"id":"sdl01_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four divisions of the primitive gut tube (pharyngeal gut, foregut, midgut, hindgut) and their corresponding blood supply (celiac, SMA, IMA), including the duodenum's dual foregut/midgut supply.","batch":1,"stem":"In a study of the embryonic gut tube, an investigator maps which adult structures receive their blood supply from the artery of the midgut. Which of the following structures belongs to this territory?","choices":{"A":"Gastric fundus","B":"Sigmoid colon","C":"Pancreatic tail","D":"Terminal ileum","E":"Upper rectum"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The midgut begins just caudal to the liver bud and ends at the junction of the right two-thirds and left one-third of the transverse colon; it forms the distal duodenum, jejunum, ileum, cecum, appendix, ascending colon, and proximal transverse colon, all supplied by the superior mesenteric artery. The terminal ileum therefore lies in the midgut territory. The gastric fundus and the pancreatic tail are foregut derivatives supplied by the celiac artery (the pancreatic tail through the splenic artery). The sigmoid colon and upper rectum are hindgut derivatives supplied by the inferior mesenteric artery. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 1, four divisions of the gut tube); Langman Ch. 15 (celiac, superior mesenteric, and inferior mesenteric supply of the gut).","boardPrep":"Step 1: foregut = celiac, midgut = SMA, hindgut = IMA; the midgut runs from the lower duodenum to the proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon (First Aid 2025 GI embryology). COMLEX: map visceral pain and ischemia to the gut region and its artery.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — the same foregut/midgut/hindgut divisions set the vagal versus pelvic parasympathetic territories","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four divisions of the primitive gut tube (pharyngeal gut, foregut, midgut, hindgut) and their corresponding blood supply (celiac, SMA, IMA), including the duodenum's dual foregut/midgut supply.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Langman, the duodenum receives arterial branches from two separate aortic trunks. Which feature of its development explains this?","choices":{"A":"Dual midgut and hindgut origin","B":"Rotation around the SMA axis","C":"Dual foregut and midgut origin","D":"Secondary retroperitoneal fixation","E":"Origin from the hepatic diverticulum"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The duodenum forms from the terminal part of the foregut and the cephalic part of the midgut. Because the foregut is supplied by the celiac artery and the midgut by the superior mesenteric artery, the duodenum receives branches of both: the superior pancreaticoduodenal artery from the celiac system and the inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery from the SMA. The hindgut contributes nothing to the duodenum. Rotation around the SMA describes the midgut loop, not the source of duodenal arteries. Secondary retroperitoneal fixation by Toldt fascia changes position, not blood supply. The hepatic diverticulum gives rise to the liver and biliary tree. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 1, dual blood supply of the duodenum); Langman Ch. 15 (duodenum).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the foregut-midgut junction lies at the ampulla of Vater, where the bile and pancreatic ducts enter (First Aid 2025 GI embryology). COMLEX: pancreaticoduodenal arcades link the celiac and SMA territories as a collateral route.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 3 — the ventral and dorsal pancreatic buds arise from the duodenum at the foregut–midgut junction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four divisions of the primitive gut tube (pharyngeal gut, foregut, midgut, hindgut) and their corresponding blood supply (celiac, SMA, IMA), including the duodenum's dual foregut/midgut supply.","batch":1,"stem":"An embryology student traces the boundary between the foregut-derived and midgut-derived parts of the duodenum. Which landmark marks this boundary?","choices":{"A":"Pyloric sphincter","B":"Duodenojejunal flexure","C":"Minor duodenal papilla","D":"Superior duodenal flexure","E":"Major duodenal papilla"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The junction between the foregut and midgut lies just distal to the origin of the liver bud, which in the adult corresponds to the entrance of the common bile duct at the major duodenal papilla. Duodenum proximal to the papilla is foregut and celiac supplied; duodenum distal to it is midgut and SMA supplied. The pyloric sphincter and the superior duodenal flexure lie within the foregut portion. The duodenojejunal flexure lies within the midgut. The minor papilla, where the accessory pancreatic duct drains, lies proximal to the major papilla and does not mark the junction. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 1, dual blood supply of the duodenum); Langman Ch. 15 (duodenum); First Aid 2025 GI (normal gastrointestinal embryology).","boardPrep":"Step 1: foregut runs from the esophagus to the duodenum at the level of the pancreatic duct and common bile duct insertion (First Aid 2025 GI embryology). COMLEX: obstruction distal to this point produces bilious vomiting.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 3 — the ventral and dorsal pancreatic buds arise from the duodenum at the foregut–midgut junction","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The duodenojejunal flexure lie"}},{"id":"sdl01_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe formation of the tracheoesophageal septum and explain how abnormal partitioning of the foregut results in esophageal atresia and tracheoesophageal fistula.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Langman, esophageal atresia with tracheoesophageal fistula most often results from which developmental error?","choices":{"A":"Posterior deviation of the septum","B":"Failure of the lung bud to form","C":"Failed fusion of the pleuroperitoneal folds","D":"Arrested lengthening of the esophagus","E":"Abnormal migration of neural crest cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Langman attributes esophageal atresia and tracheoesophageal fistula to spontaneous posterior deviation of the tracheoesophageal septum or to a mechanical force pushing the dorsal foregut wall anteriorly, so the septum partitions the foregut incompletely. Failure of the lung bud to form causes pulmonary agenesis rather than a fistula. Failed fusion of the pleuroperitoneal folds causes congenital diaphragmatic hernia. Arrested lengthening of the esophagus pulls the stomach into the hiatus as a congenital hiatal hernia. Abnormal neural crest migration produces aganglionic segments such as Hirschsprung disease. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 2, pathogenesis of esophageal atresia and TEF); Langman Ch. 15 (esophageal abnormalities).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the most common variant is esophageal atresia with a distal TEF, which presents with polyhydramnios and choking at the first feed; associated with VACTERL (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: an NG tube that will not pass into the stomach confirms the atresia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 4 — muscle type transitions and innervation along the esophagus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe formation of the tracheoesophageal septum and explain how abnormal partitioning of the foregut results in esophageal atresia and tracheoesophageal fistula.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Langman, which variant of esophageal atresia or tracheoesophageal fistula is the most common?","choices":{"A":"Isolated atresia without a fistula","B":"Upper pouch fistula with distal atresia","C":"H-type fistula without atresia","D":"Proximal atresia with distal fistula","E":"Separate fistulas at both segments"},"correct":"D","explanation":"About 90% of cases are a blind proximal esophageal pouch with the distal esophagus joined to the trachea just above the bifurcation. Isolated atresia without a fistula and the H-type fistula without atresia each account for about 4%. A proximal fistula with a blind distal segment and a double fistula each account for about 1%. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 2, anatomic variations and frequency); Langman Ch. 15 (Fig. 15.7, variants in order of frequency). First Aid 2025 GI gives 85% for this variant; Langman and the lecture give 90%.","boardPrep":"Step 1: EA with distal TEF is the most common type; air entering the stomach through the fistula produces a prominent gastric bubble, whereas pure EA gives a gasless abdomen (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: aspiration of gastric acid through the distal fistula causes chemical pneumonitis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 4 — muscle type transitions and innervation along the esophagus","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"A proximal fistula with a blin","E":"A proximal fistula with a blin"}},{"id":"sdl01_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe formation of the tracheoesophageal septum and explain how abnormal partitioning of the foregut results in esophageal atresia and tracheoesophageal fistula.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Langman, why is esophageal atresia associated with polyhydramnios?","choices":{"A":"Fetal kidneys produce excess urine","B":"Fluid leaks from the fistula into the lungs","C":"Swallowed fluid does not reach the intestine","D":"Amniotic membranes secrete more fluid","E":"Placental transfer of fluid to the mother falls"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The fetus normally swallows amniotic fluid, which is absorbed in the intestine. With esophageal atresia the fluid does not pass the blind pouch into the stomach and gut, so it accumulates in the amniotic sac. Polyhydramnios here is a problem of fetal clearance, not of fetal urine production, which is unchanged. Fluid entering the airway through a fistula is not a route of amniotic clearance. The membranes and placenta are not the site of the defect. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 2, clinical features); Langman Ch. 15 (atresia prevents passage of amniotic fluid into the intestinal tract).","boardPrep":"Step 1: polyhydramnios points to an inability to swallow or absorb amniotic fluid, as in esophageal or duodenal atresia; oligohydramnios points to absent fetal urine (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: polyhydramnios on prenatal ultrasound should prompt evaluation for upper GI atresia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 4 — muscle type transitions and innervation along the esophagus","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"The membranes and placenta are","E":"The membranes and placenta are"}},{"id":"sdl01_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain stomach rotation and describe its effects on the orientation of the stomach, vagal innervation, and associated mesenteries.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Langman, after the stomach rotates 90 degrees clockwise around its longitudinal axis, the left vagus nerve innervates which part of the stomach?","choices":{"A":"Posterior wall","B":"Lesser curvature","C":"Greater curvature","D":"Anterior wall","E":"Pyloric antrum"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The 90-degree clockwise rotation turns the original left side of the stomach to face anteriorly and the right side to face posteriorly. The left vagus nerve, which ran along the left side, therefore comes to innervate the anterior wall, and the right vagus nerve innervates the posterior wall. The lesser and greater curvatures form from the slower-growing anterior and faster-growing posterior walls, not from vagal territory. The pyloric antrum moves upward and to the right during rotation around the anteroposterior axis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 3, rotation around the longitudinal axis); Langman Ch. 15 (stomach).","boardPrep":"Step 1: stomach rotates 90 degrees clockwise; left vagus becomes anterior and right vagus becomes posterior (First Aid 2025 GI; Bootcamp Gastroenterology embryology). COMLEX: the anterior vagal trunk lies on the front of the distal esophagus during vagotomy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — distribution of vagal parasympathetic innervation to the GI tract","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"The lesser and greater curvatu"}},{"id":"sdl01_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain stomach rotation and describe its effects on the orientation of the stomach, vagal innervation, and associated mesenteries.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Langman, the greater curvature of the stomach forms because one of the original walls grows faster than the opposite wall during rotation. Which wall is this?","choices":{"A":"Original anterior wall","B":"Original left lateral wall","C":"Original right lateral wall","D":"Original cranial wall","E":"Original posterior wall"},"correct":"E","explanation":"During rotation the original posterior wall of the stomach grows faster than the anterior wall, forming the greater curvature; the slower-growing anterior wall forms the lesser curvature. The original left and right walls become the anterior and posterior surfaces after the 90-degree clockwise rotation and carry the left and right vagal trunks. The original cranial end becomes the cardia and shifts during rotation around the anteroposterior axis; it does not form a curvature. The original anterior wall grows more slowly than the posterior wall and forms the lesser curvature, not the greater. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 3, differential wall growth and curvatures); Langman Ch. 15 (stomach).","boardPrep":"Step 1: dorsal mesogastrium attaches to the greater curvature and becomes the greater omentum; ventral mesogastrium attaches to the lesser curvature and forms the lesser omentum (Bootcamp Gastroenterology embryology). COMLEX: these attachments define the peritoneal ligaments seen in gastric surgery.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — distribution of vagal parasympathetic innervation to the GI tract","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The original anterior wall grows more slowly than the poster","B":"The original left and right wa","C":"The original left and right wa"}},{"id":"sdl01_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain stomach rotation and describe its effects on the orientation of the stomach, vagal innervation, and associated mesenteries.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Langman, the spleen primordium appears during the fifth week of development between the two leaves of which structure?","choices":{"A":"Ventral mesogastrium","B":"Dorsal mesogastrium","C":"Lesser omentum","D":"Dorsal mesoduodenum","E":"Septum transversum"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The spleen primordium arises as a mesodermal proliferation between the leaves of the dorsal mesogastrium. Gastric rotation swings it to the left, and the dorsal mesogastrium between spleen and stomach becomes the gastrosplenic ligament, while the part between spleen and left kidney becomes the splenorenal ligament. The ventral mesogastrium gives rise to the lesser omentum and falciform ligament and contains the developing liver. The dorsal mesoduodenum receives the dorsal pancreatic bud. The septum transversum is the mesoderm into which the liver bud grows. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 3, development and position of the spleen); Langman Ch. 15 (stomach mesenteries and spleen).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the spleen arises in the dorsal mesogastrium and is mesodermal, yet it has a foregut blood supply through the splenic branch of the celiac trunk (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: the gastrosplenic and splenorenal ligaments are divided during splenectomy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — distribution of vagal parasympathetic innervation to the GI tract","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe normal duodenal recanalization and explain how failure of this process results in congenital duodenal obstruction.","batch":1,"stem":"Congenital duodenal atresia most often results from which process?","choices":{"A":"Vascular accident with resorption","B":"Abnormal rotation of the ventral bud","C":"Failed recanalization of the lumen","D":"Posterior deviation of the septum","E":"Hypertrophy of circular muscle"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The duodenal lumen is obliterated by epithelial proliferation and then recanalized by vacuolization; when recanalization fails, the lumen stays occluded, producing atresia, and when it is incomplete it produces stenosis. Vascular accidents with resorption of bowel cause jejunal and ileal atresia. Abnormal rotation of the ventral pancreatic bud creates an annular pancreas that encircles the duodenum. Posterior deviation of the tracheoesophageal septum causes esophageal atresia. Hypertrophy of circular muscle at the pylorus causes hypertrophic pyloric stenosis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 4, failure of recanalization); Langman Ch. 15 (upper duodenal atresias probably from lack of recanalization; Langman attributes atresia from the distal duodenum caudally to vascular accidents, while the lecture applies failed recanalization to the duodenum); First Aid 2025 GI (intestinal atresia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: duodenal atresia = failure to recanalize from the solid cord stage, double bubble on radiograph, associated with Down syndrome; jejunal and ileal atresia = vascular disruption (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: check infants with trisomy 21 for duodenal atresia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 5 — annular pancreas as another cause of congenital duodenal obstruction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe normal duodenal recanalization and explain how failure of this process results in congenital duodenal obstruction.","batch":1,"stem":"During normal development of the duodenum, epithelial proliferation briefly converts the lumen into a solid cord. According to Langman, which event follows this solid stage?","choices":{"A":"Vacuole formation and coalescence","B":"Rotation around the SMA axis","C":"Fusion of the pancreatic buds","D":"Herniation into the umbilical cord","E":"Outgrowth of the hepatic diverticulum"},"correct":"A","explanation":"After the solid stage, vacuoles form within the epithelial cord through apoptosis and cleft formation and then coalesce into a continuous lumen, restoring a patent tube. Rotation around the SMA axis and physiologic herniation into the umbilical cord are events of the midgut loop. Fusion of the dorsal and ventral pancreatic buds follows rotation of the duodenum but does not reopen its lumen. The hepatic diverticulum grows out from the foregut before this stage and forms the liver and biliary tree. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 4, normal duodenal recanalization); Langman Ch. 15 (solid stage and cavity formation, Fig. 15.18).","boardPrep":"Step 1: failure of this vacuolization gives duodenal atresia or stenosis (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: the same failure in the esophagus produces esophageal stenosis, usually in the lower third.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 5 — annular pancreas as another cause of congenital duodenal obstruction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe normal duodenal recanalization and explain how failure of this process results in congenital duodenal obstruction.","batch":1,"stem":"In an infant with congenital duodenal obstruction, the vomitus is bile stained. Where is the obstruction most likely located?","choices":{"A":"Proximal to the major papilla","B":"Distal to the major papilla","C":"At the pyloric sphincter","D":"At the gastroesophageal junction","E":"Within the gastric antrum"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Bile enters the duodenum at the major papilla, so obstruction distal to it produces bilious vomiting; the lecture notes that about 80% to 85% of duodenal obstructions lie distal to the papilla. Obstruction proximal to the papilla, at the pylorus, at the gastroesophageal junction, or in the antrum keeps bile out of the vomitus, which is nonbilious, as in hypertrophic pyloric stenosis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 4, clinical and radiographic presentation); Langman Ch. 15 (entrance of the bile duct at the major papilla); First Aid 2025 GI (intestinal atresia presents with bilious vomiting).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bilious emesis in a neonate signals obstruction distal to the ampulla of Vater, such as duodenal atresia or malrotation with volvulus (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: bilious vomiting in a newborn is a surgical emergency until proven otherwise.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 5 — annular pancreas as another cause of congenital duodenal obstruction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the developmental abnormality underlying hypertrophic pyloric stenosis and recognize its characteristic clinical presentation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Langman, hypertrophic pyloric stenosis results mainly from enlargement of which tissue layer of the pylorus?","choices":{"A":"Circular muscularis externa","B":"Pyloric mucosal epithelium","C":"Submucosal connective tissue","D":"Antral muscularis mucosae","E":"Visceral peritoneal serosa"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Pyloric stenosis occurs when the circular musculature of the pylorus, and to a lesser degree the longitudinal musculature, hypertrophies and narrows the pyloric lumen. The lecture adds that the circular layer of the muscularis propria often thickens by 50% to 100%. The mucosa and submucosa may become edematous and inflamed, but they are secondary changes. The muscularis mucosae is a thin layer within the mucosa that does not form the obstructing mass. The serosa is a covering layer. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 5, underlying developmental abnormality); Langman Ch. 15 (stomach abnormalities); Robbins Ch. 17 (hyperplasia of the pyloric muscularis propria).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypertrophy and hyperplasia of the pyloric sphincter muscle produce the palpable olive; treat with pyloromyotomy (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: the myotomy divides the hypertrophied muscle down to the submucosa and spares the mucosa.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — pyloric resistance during gastric mixing and emptying","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The mucosa and submucosa may b","D":"The muscularis mucosae is a th"}},{"id":"sdl01_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the developmental abnormality underlying hypertrophic pyloric stenosis and recognize its characteristic clinical presentation.","batch":1,"stem":"An investigator examines pyloric muscle from infants with hypertrophic pyloric stenosis and finds few neurons expressing the enzyme that produces the main relaxing transmitter of the sphincter. Which transmitter is deficient?","choices":{"A":"Acetylcholine","B":"Substance P","C":"Serotonin","D":"Nitric oxide","E":"Norepinephrine"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Pyloric relaxation is mediated by nonadrenergic, noncholinergic neurons that release nitric oxide made by neuronal nitric oxide synthase. In hypertrophic pyloric stenosis, neuronal nitric oxide synthase is markedly deficient in the pyloric muscle plexus, so the sphincter fails to relax, and persistent contraction drives work hypertrophy and narrowing. Acetylcholine and substance P are excitatory transmitters that contract GI smooth muscle. Serotonin acts mainly as a sensory and secretory mediator in the enteric nervous system. Norepinephrine from sympathetic fibers relaxes gut wall muscle but contracts sphincters. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 5, nitric oxide synthase deficiency). Among the staged sources this mechanism is stated by the lecture.","boardPrep":"Step 1: loss of NO-mediated relaxation also underlies achalasia, where NO and VIP inhibitory neurons of the myenteric plexus degenerate at the lower esophageal sphincter (First Aid 2025 GI achalasia). COMLEX: think of failed relaxation when a sphincter region thickens in an infant.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — pyloric resistance during gastric mixing and emptying","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the developmental abnormality underlying hypertrophic pyloric stenosis and recognize its characteristic clinical presentation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Langman, postnatal exposure to which drug substantially increases the risk of hypertrophic pyloric stenosis?","choices":{"A":"Amoxicillin","B":"Gentamicin","C":"Ceftriaxone","D":"Metronidazole","E":"Erythromycin"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Langman notes that treatment with erythromycin in the newborn period substantially increases the risk of pyloric stenosis, suggesting that the defect can develop after birth; Robbins and the lecture extend the association to azithromycin, either given directly or through breast milk in the first 2 weeks of life. Amoxicillin, gentamicin, ceftriaxone, and metronidazole are commonly used in neonates but are not macrolides and are not linked to pyloric stenosis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 5, environmental and medication exposure); Langman Ch. 15 (stomach abnormalities); Robbins Ch. 17 (pyloric stenosis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypertrophic pyloric stenosis is more common in firstborn males and is associated with macrolide exposure (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: weigh this risk when choosing pertussis prophylaxis for a young infant.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — pyloric resistance during gastric mixing and emptying","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four divisions of the primitive gut tube (pharyngeal gut, foregut, midgut, hindgut) and their corresponding blood supply (celiac, SMA, IMA), including the duodenum's dual foregut/midgut supply.","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old man has a bleeding posterior ulcer of the duodenal bulb, and the gastroduodenal artery is surgically ligated. Afterward the duodenum distal to the major papilla remains well perfused. This part of the duodenum is perfused mainly by branches of which artery?","choices":{"A":"Superior mesenteric artery","B":"Common hepatic artery","C":"Left gastric artery","D":"Right gastroepiploic artery","E":"Inferior mesenteric artery"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The duodenum distal to the major papilla is midgut and is supplied by the inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery, a branch of the superior mesenteric artery, so it stays perfused after the gastroduodenal artery is tied. The common hepatic artery gives rise to the gastroduodenal artery and supplies the foregut part of the duodenum through the superior pancreaticoduodenal artery. The left gastric artery supplies the lesser curvature of the stomach. The right gastroepiploic artery is a branch of the ligated gastroduodenal artery. The inferior mesenteric artery supplies the hindgut. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 1, dual blood supply of the duodenum); Langman Ch. 15 (duodenum supplied by both celiac and superior mesenteric arteries).","boardPrep":"Step 1: celiac trunk branches are the common hepatic, splenic, and left gastric arteries, and the pancreaticoduodenal arcades join the celiac and SMA territories (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: a posterior duodenal ulcer can erode the gastroduodenal artery, and the arcades protect the duodenum when it is ligated.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 3 — the ventral and dorsal pancreatic buds arise from the duodenum at the foregut–midgut junction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four divisions of the primitive gut tube (pharyngeal gut, foregut, midgut, hindgut) and their corresponding blood supply (celiac, SMA, IMA), including the duodenum's dual foregut/midgut supply.","batch":2,"stem":"A 71-year-old woman develops left-sided abdominal pain and bloody stools after a prolonged episode of hypotension. Colonoscopy shows ischemic mucosa from the distal third of the transverse colon through the sigmoid colon, with a normal cecum and ascending colon. The ischemic bowel is derived from which embryonic gut region?","choices":{"A":"Midgut","B":"Foregut","C":"Pharyngeal gut","D":"Hindgut","E":"Cloaca"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The hindgut extends from the distal one-third of the transverse colon to the cloacal membrane and forms the distal transverse, descending, and sigmoid colon, rectum, and upper anal canal, all supplied by the inferior mesenteric artery; hypoperfusion of that territory explains the pattern. The midgut forms the cecum, ascending colon, and proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon, which are normal. The foregut forms the esophagus through the proximal duodenum. The pharyngeal gut forms the pharynx. The cloaca is the terminal dilation of the hindgut that the urorectal septum divides into the rectum and urogenital sinus; it does not form the colon. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 1, four divisions of the gut tube); Langman Ch. 15 (gut divisions and arteries).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hindgut = IMA, with parasympathetic supply from the pelvic splanchnic nerves rather than the vagus (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: the splenic flexure is a watershed between SMA and IMA territories.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — pelvic nerves rather than the vagus supply parasympathetic fibers to the distal colon","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"The cloaca is the terminal dil"}},{"id":"sdl01_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four divisions of the primitive gut tube (pharyngeal gut, foregut, midgut, hindgut) and their corresponding blood supply (celiac, SMA, IMA), including the duodenum's dual foregut/midgut supply.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man has endoscopic removal of an adenoma in the descending duodenum just proximal to the major duodenal papilla. Several hours later he has bleeding from the main artery supplying the resected segment. Which artery is most likely bleeding?","choices":{"A":"Inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery","B":"Right gastroepiploic artery","C":"Short gastric branch of the splenic artery","D":"Ileocolic branch of the SMA","E":"Superior pancreaticoduodenal artery"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Duodenum proximal to the major papilla is foregut and is supplied by the superior pancreaticoduodenal artery, which arises from the gastroduodenal artery of the celiac system. The inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery is an SMA branch that supplies the midgut part distal to the papilla. The right gastroepiploic artery supplies the greater curvature of the stomach. Short gastric branches of the splenic artery supply the gastric fundus. The ileocolic artery supplies the terminal ileum, cecum, and appendix. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 1, dual blood supply of the duodenum); Langman Ch. 15 (duodenum).","boardPrep":"Step 1: posterior duodenal ulcers erode the gastroduodenal artery, the parent of the superior pancreaticoduodenal artery (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: localize duodenal bleeding by its position relative to the ampulla.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 3 — the ventral and dorsal pancreatic buds arise from the duodenum at the foregut–midgut junction","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"The ileocolic artery supplies "}},{"id":"sdl01_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe formation of the tracheoesophageal septum and explain how abnormal partitioning of the foregut results in esophageal atresia and tracheoesophageal fistula.","batch":2,"stem":"A 1-day-old boy born after a pregnancy complicated by polyhydramnios drools, chokes, and becomes cyanotic during his first feed. A nasogastric tube coils in the upper chest, and a chest radiograph shows a gas-filled stomach. Which additional finding is most likely in this infant?","choices":{"A":"Hypertrophied pyloric muscle","B":"Annular pancreatic tissue","C":"Vertebral segmentation defects","D":"Aganglionic distal colon","E":"Bilateral congenital cataracts"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A blind upper esophageal pouch that stops the nasogastric tube, together with air in the stomach, indicates esophageal atresia with a distal tracheoesophageal fistula. Thirty to fifty percent of these infants have other anomalies, most notably the VACTERL association, which includes vertebral defects. Hypertrophied pyloric muscle presents weeks later with projectile nonbilious vomiting. Annular pancreas is associated with trisomy 21 and duodenal obstruction. Aganglionic colon is Hirschsprung disease, which presents with delayed meconium passage. Congenital cataracts point to infections such as rubella. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 2, clinical features and associated anomalies); Langman Ch. 15 (esophageal abnormalities); First Aid 2025 GI (tracheoesophageal anomalies).","boardPrep":"Step 1: VACTERL = vertebral, anal atresia, cardiac, tracheoesophageal fistula, renal, limb defects; failure to pass an NG tube is the bedside test for EA (First Aid 2025 GI; Bootcamp Gastroenterology embryology). COMLEX: screen with echocardiography and renal ultrasound once EA is found.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 5 — annular pancreas is another foregut anomaly with polyhydramnios and neonatal vomiting","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe formation of the tracheoesophageal septum and explain how abnormal partitioning of the foregut results in esophageal atresia and tracheoesophageal fistula.","batch":2,"stem":"A 5-month-old girl has had three episodes of pneumonia and coughs and gags during most feeds. A contrast study shows a continuous, patent esophagus with contrast entering the trachea through a short oblique tract in the lower neck. Which developmental error best explains this finding?","choices":{"A":"Failed lengthening of the esophagus during descent","B":"Incomplete recanalization of the esophageal lumen","C":"Loss of ganglion cells in the myenteric plexus","D":"Defective fusion of the pleuroperitoneal folds","E":"Incomplete fusion of tracheoesophageal ridges"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A patent esophagus with a fistula to the trachea is the H-type fistula. It results from incomplete partitioning by the tracheoesophageal septum, which forms when the paired tracheoesophageal ridges grow toward each other and fuse; where fusion is incomplete, a communication persists. Failed lengthening of the esophagus causes congenital hiatal hernia. Incomplete recanalization narrows the lumen as esophageal stenosis without a fistula. Loss of myenteric ganglion cells causes motility disorders such as achalasia. Defective pleuroperitoneal fold fusion causes diaphragmatic hernia. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 2, normal formation of the septum and H-type TEF); Langman Ch. 15 (esophagus and esophageal abnormalities).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the H-type fistula resembles the letter H and causes recurrent aspiration pneumonia (First Aid 2025 GI; Bootcamp Gastroenterology embryology). COMLEX: suspect it in an infant with recurrent pneumonia and choking with feeds despite a passable NG tube.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — esophageal stenosis and webs arise from structural narrowing rather than a fistula","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe formation of the tracheoesophageal septum and explain how abnormal partitioning of the foregut results in esophageal atresia and tracheoesophageal fistula.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman at 32 weeks of gestation has a fundal height larger than expected. Ultrasound shows an amniotic fluid index of 32 cm (reference range: 5–25 cm) and a fetal stomach that is not visualized on repeated scans. Which finding is most likely at birth?","choices":{"A":"Failure to pass a nasogastric tube","B":"Bilious vomiting after the first feed","C":"Olive-shaped epigastric mass","D":"Delayed passage of meconium","E":"Bowel loops outside the abdomen"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Polyhydramnios with an absent fetal stomach bubble means swallowed amniotic fluid is not reaching the stomach, which indicates esophageal atresia; at birth a nasogastric tube will stop in the blind upper pouch. Bilious vomiting with polyhydramnios fits duodenal atresia distal to the papilla, but that lesion produces a distended stomach and duodenum on ultrasound. The olive-shaped mass of pyloric stenosis appears weeks after birth. Delayed meconium passage suggests Hirschsprung disease or meconium ileus. Bowel loops outside the abdomen fit gastroschisis or omphalocele, which are seen directly on ultrasound. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 2, in utero polyhydramnios and postnatal presentation); Langman Ch. 15 (atresia and polyhydramnios); First Aid 2025 GI (tracheoesophageal anomalies).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pure EA gives a gasless abdomen, whereas EA with distal TEF gives a prominent gastric bubble (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: a small or absent fetal stomach with polyhydramnios is the prenatal clue to EA.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — duodenal atresia as the other foregut cause of polyhydramnios (double bubble)","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain stomach rotation and describe its effects on the orientation of the stomach, vagal innervation, and associated mesenteries.","batch":2,"stem":"A 49-year-old man with refractory peptic ulcer disease undergoes truncal vagotomy. The surgeon divides the vagal trunk that lies on the anterior surface of the distal esophagus. The fibers in this trunk come mainly from which nerve?","choices":{"A":"Right vagus nerve","B":"Left phrenic nerve","C":"Left vagus nerve","D":"Greater splanchnic nerve","E":"Left recurrent laryngeal nerve"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Before rotation the left and right vagus nerves run along the left and right sides of the stomach. The 90-degree clockwise rotation carries the left vagus anteriorly, where it becomes the anterior vagal trunk innervating the anterior gastric wall. The right vagus becomes the posterior trunk. The phrenic nerve supplies the diaphragm. The greater splanchnic nerve carries sympathetic fibers to the celiac ganglion. The left recurrent laryngeal nerve hooks under the aortic arch and supplies the larynx. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 3, vagal innervation changes); Langman Ch. 15 (stomach rotation and vagus nerves).","boardPrep":"Step 1: after the 90-degree clockwise rotation the left vagus lies anterior and the right vagus posterior (First Aid 2025 GI embryology). COMLEX: vagotomy reduces acid secretion but can delay gastric emptying.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — the vagus distributes parasympathetic fibers to the stomach and proximal GI tract","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The right vagus becomes the po"}},{"id":"sdl01_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain stomach rotation and describe its effects on the orientation of the stomach, vagal innervation, and associated mesenteries.","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old man recovering from acute pancreatitis develops a fluid collection that fills the space directly behind the stomach. This space formed as a result of which effect of gastric rotation?","choices":{"A":"Leftward pull on the ventral mesogastrium","B":"Leftward pull on the dorsal mesogastrium","C":"Rightward swing of the duodenal loop","D":"Counterclockwise rotation of the midgut","E":"Fusion of the transverse mesocolon"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The space behind the stomach is the omental bursa, or lesser peritoneal sac. As the stomach rotates around its longitudinal axis, it pulls the dorsal mesogastrium to the left and opens this space behind it. The ventral mesogastrium, which becomes the lesser omentum, is pulled to the right rather than the left. The rightward swing of the C-shaped duodenum moves the duodenum and pancreatic head against the posterior wall. Counterclockwise rotation of the midgut loop around the SMA positions the intestines. Fusion of the greater omentum with the transverse mesocolon occurs later and closes off the lower part of the bursa. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 3, formation of the omental bursa); Langman Ch. 15 (omental bursa).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the lesser sac communicates with the greater sac through the epiploic foramen of Winslow (First Aid 2025 GI anatomy). COMLEX: pancreatic pseudocysts and posterior gastric ulcer perforations collect in the lesser sac.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 3 — pancreatic pseudocysts are nonneoplastic collections that follow pancreatitis","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain stomach rotation and describe its effects on the orientation of the stomach, vagal innervation, and associated mesenteries.","batch":2,"stem":"A 3D model of a 6-week embryo shows the stomach after its first rotation has turned the original left side to face anteriorly. In a second movement, the caudal pyloric end shifts upward and to the right while the cardiac end moves slightly downward and to the left, giving the adult stomach its oblique long axis. Rotation around which axis produces this second movement?","choices":{"A":"Longitudinal axis","B":"Superior mesenteric artery axis","C":"Transverse left-right axis","D":"Anteroposterior axis","E":"Vitelline duct axis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"After the 90-degree clockwise turn around its longitudinal axis, the stomach rotates around an anteroposterior axis: the caudal pyloric part moves upward and to the right and the cephalic cardiac part moves slightly downward and to the left, so the adult long axis runs from upper left to lower right. The longitudinal-axis rotation is the earlier turn that brings the original left side forward and carries the left vagus anteriorly. The superior mesenteric artery axis is the axis of midgut loop rotation, not stomach rotation. A transverse left-right axis and a vitelline duct axis are not described for the stomach. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 3, rotation around the anteroposterior axis, p5); Langman Ch. 15 (stomach rotates around a longitudinal and an anteroposterior axis, Fig. 15.8).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the stomach rotates around two axes, longitudinal then anteroposterior, which sets the positions of the pylorus and cardia (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the dual rotation places the stomach in its adult position with the long axis running from upper left to lower right (lecture SDL 1).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — longitudinal rotation carries the left vagus anteriorly","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The longitudinal-axis rotation"}},{"id":"sdl01_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe normal duodenal recanalization and explain how failure of this process results in congenital duodenal obstruction.","batch":2,"stem":"A 1-day-old girl with upslanting palpebral fissures and a single palmar crease vomits bile after every feed. An abdominal radiograph shows a dilated stomach and proximal duodenum with no gas beyond them. At surgery the pancreas is normal, and a thin membrane occludes the bowel just distal to the major papilla. Which developmental process failed?","choices":{"A":"Recanalization of the duodenal lumen","B":"Perfusion of the jejunal mesentery","C":"Rotation of the ventral pancreatic bud","D":"Rotation of the midgut loop","E":"Relaxation of the pyloric sphincter"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The double bubble with no distal gas, bilious vomiting, and features of Down syndrome indicate duodenal atresia; an occluding membrane distal to the papilla is the result of failed vacuolization and recanalization after the solid stage. Loss of mesenteric perfusion causes jejunal atresia, with a triple bubble, and ileal atresia, with multiple dilated loops. Abnormal rotation of the ventral pancreatic bud creates an annular pancreas, excluded by the normal pancreas at surgery. Malrotation of the midgut causes obstruction by bands or volvulus rather than an intraluminal membrane. Failed pyloric relaxation causes hypertrophic pyloric stenosis with nonbilious vomiting weeks after birth. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 4, failed recanalization with an occluding membrane, the 80–85% of obstructions distal to the papilla, and associated conditions); First Aid 2025 GI (intestinal atresia); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (triple bubble in jejunal atresia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: duodenal atresia = double bubble, polyhydramnios, bilious vomiting, Down syndrome (First Aid 2025 GI; Bootcamp Gastroenterology embryology). COMLEX: look for associated cardiac defects in infants with trisomy 21.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 5 — annular pancreas is the other congenital cause of a double bubble","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Malrotation of the midgut caus"}},{"id":"sdl01_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe normal duodenal recanalization and explain how failure of this process results in congenital duodenal obstruction.","batch":2,"stem":"A 2-day-old boy has repeated nonbilious vomiting after feeds. An abdominal radiograph shows a distended stomach and duodenal bulb with a small amount of gas in the distal bowel, and a contrast study shows a narrowed duodenal segment with a perforated web. Where is this lesion most likely located?","choices":{"A":"Distal to the major papilla","B":"At the duodenojejunal flexure","C":"In the terminal ileum","D":"At the gastroesophageal junction","E":"Proximal to the major papilla"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A perforated web with some distal gas indicates duodenal stenosis from incomplete recanalization, and nonbilious vomiting places the narrowing proximal to the major papilla, where bile has not yet entered the lumen. A lesion distal to the papilla or at the duodenojejunal flexure would produce bilious vomiting. Terminal ileal obstruction causes bilious vomiting with diffuse distension rather than a dilated duodenal bulb. Obstruction at the gastroesophageal junction prevents feeds from reaching the stomach, so the stomach and duodenum would not distend. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 4, duodenal stenosis and bilious versus nonbilious vomiting); Langman Ch. 15 (atresias and stenoses of the duodenum).","boardPrep":"Step 1: complete atresia gives no distal gas; stenosis or a perforated web lets some gas pass (lecture; First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: bile in the vomitus localizes the obstruction relative to the ampulla.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 3 — the bile duct enters at the major papilla as the duodenum and ventral bud rotate","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A lesion distal to the papilla"}},{"id":"sdl01_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe normal duodenal recanalization and explain how failure of this process results in congenital duodenal obstruction.","batch":2,"stem":"A 36-year-old woman at 24 weeks of gestation has an ultrasound showing polyhydramnios and two fluid-filled structures in the fetal upper abdomen that connect through the pylorus. No other fluid-filled bowel is seen. The fetus is most likely to have which additional condition?","choices":{"A":"Trisomy 18","B":"Trisomy 21","C":"Trisomy 13","D":"Turner syndrome","E":"Klinefelter syndrome"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A fluid-filled stomach and proximal duodenum connected through the pylorus is the prenatal double bubble of duodenal atresia, and polyhydramnios reflects swallowed fluid that does not reach the distal bowel. Duodenal atresia is strongly associated with Down syndrome (trisomy 21), present in up to 30% of cases. Trisomy 18 and Turner syndrome carry other congenital anomalies but are not the classic association. Trisomy 13 is associated with midline defects such as holoprosencephaly. Klinefelter syndrome has no characteristic GI atresia. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 4, associated conditions); Langman Ch. 15 (duodenal atresia); First Aid 2025 GI (intestinal atresia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Down syndrome GI associations include duodenal atresia, annular pancreas, and Hirschsprung disease (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: a prenatal double bubble prompts karyotype counseling.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 5 — annular pancreas as another cause of congenital duodenal obstruction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the developmental abnormality underlying hypertrophic pyloric stenosis and recognize its characteristic clinical presentation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 4-week-old firstborn boy has had projectile, nonbilious vomiting after every feed for 5 days and is eager to feed again right after vomiting. A firm, mobile mass is palpable in the epigastrium. Laboratory studies show a serum chloride of 88 mEq/L (reference range: 95–105 mEq/L), potassium of 3.1 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), and bicarbonate of 33 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). The palpable mass consists mainly of which tissue?","choices":{"A":"Edematous submucosal connective tissue","B":"Hyperplastic antral mucosal glands","C":"Hypertrophied circular smooth muscle","D":"Enlarged submucosal lymphoid follicles","E":"Heterotopic pancreatic tissue"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A hungry, firstborn male infant with projectile nonbilious vomiting, an epigastric olive, and hypochloremic, hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis has hypertrophic pyloric stenosis. The olive is the hypertrophied and hyperplastic circular smooth muscle of the pyloric muscularis propria. Mucosal and submucosal edema are secondary findings and do not form the mass. Antral gland hyperplasia and lymphoid follicles are not part of the lesion; lymphoid hyperplasia is a lead point for intussusception. Heterotopic pancreatic tissue can form a small gastric nodule but does not cause this presentation. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 5, developmental abnormality and clinical presentation); Langman Ch. 15 (stomach abnormalities); Robbins Ch. 17 (pyloric stenosis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypertrophic pyloric stenosis = olive mass, visible peristalsis, projectile nonbilious vomiting at about 3–6 weeks (lecture; First Aid gives 2–6 weeks), hypokalemic hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: correct the fluid and electrolyte deficit before pyloromyotomy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 3 — loss of parietal cell HCl and the alkaline tide explain the metabolic alkalosis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Mucosal and submucosal edema a"}},{"id":"sdl01_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the developmental abnormality underlying hypertrophic pyloric stenosis and recognize its characteristic clinical presentation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-week-old girl whose mother has pertussis was given a 5-day course of a prophylactic antibiotic starting at 6 days of age. She now has projectile, nonbilious vomiting after feeds and an olive-shaped epigastric mass. The prophylactic drug most likely acts by which mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Binding the 30S ribosomal subunit","B":"Binding the 50S ribosomal subunit","C":"Inhibiting cell wall transpeptidase","D":"Inhibiting DNA gyrase","E":"Inhibiting dihydropteroate synthase"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pertussis prophylaxis in a young infant uses a macrolide, and macrolide exposure in the first 2 weeks of life is associated with hypertrophic pyloric stenosis. Macrolides such as erythromycin and azithromycin bind the 50S ribosomal subunit and block translocation. Binding the 30S subunit is the mechanism of aminoglycosides and tetracyclines. Inhibition of cell wall transpeptidases is the mechanism of beta-lactams. Inhibition of DNA gyrase is the mechanism of fluoroquinolones. Inhibition of dihydropteroate synthase is the mechanism of sulfonamides. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 5, macrolide exposure in the first 2 weeks); Langman Ch. 15 (erythromycin in the newborn period); First Aid 2025 GI (macrolide exposure); Katzung Ch. 44 (macrolides bind the 50S ribosomal subunit, Fig. 44–1).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypertrophic pyloric stenosis is associated with macrolide exposure (First Aid 2025 GI); erythromycin also acts as a motilin agonist and prokinetic. COMLEX: counsel families about vomiting after neonatal macrolide therapy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — duodenal feedback and pyloric resistance control gastric emptying","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the developmental abnormality underlying hypertrophic pyloric stenosis and recognize its characteristic clinical presentation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 5-week-old girl has forceful vomiting immediately after feeds and remains hungry afterward. Ultrasound shows a thickened, elongated pyloric channel, and her vomitus contains milk but no bile. Which of the following best explains the absence of bile in her vomitus?","choices":{"A":"Absent bile secretion before 3 months of age","B":"Reflux blocked by the sphincter of Oddi","C":"Bile reabsorbed in the gastric antrum","D":"Obstruction proximal to the major papilla","E":"Bile diverted through the cystic duct"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The pylorus lies proximal to the major duodenal papilla, where bile enters the gut, so a pyloric obstruction keeps bile out of the stomach and the vomitus is nonbilious. Infants secrete bile from birth, so age does not explain it. The sphincter of Oddi regulates bile entry into the duodenum but does not prevent bile from reaching the stomach when the obstruction lies distal to the papilla. The gastric antrum does not reabsorb bile; bile salts are reabsorbed in the terminal ileum. Diversion through the cystic duct stores bile in the gallbladder but does not eliminate it from the duodenum. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 5, forceful projectile nonbilious vomiting); Robbins Ch. 17 (pyloric stenosis); First Aid 2025 GI (hypertrophic pyloric stenosis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: nonbilious projectile vomiting points to pyloric stenosis; bilious vomiting points to obstruction distal to the ampulla (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: an ultrasound of the pylorus confirms the diagnosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 5 — bile enters the duodenum through the sphincter of Oddi under CCK control","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl01_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four divisions of the primitive gut tube (pharyngeal gut, foregut, midgut, hindgut) and their corresponding blood supply (celiac, SMA, IMA), including the duodenum's dual foregut/midgut supply.","batch":3,"stem":"A 76-year-old woman with atrial fibrillation has sudden severe periumbilical pain. CT shows an embolus in the artery that leaves the aorta just below the celiac trunk, with ischemia from the jejunum to the proximal transverse colon. The ischemic bowel comes from which embryonic gut region?","choices":{"A":"Foregut","B":"Hindgut","C":"Midgut","D":"Cloaca","E":"Pharyngeal gut"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The artery just below the celiac trunk is the superior mesenteric artery, the artery of the midgut, which forms the distal duodenum, jejunum, ileum, cecum, ascending colon, and proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon. The foregut is supplied by the celiac artery. The hindgut, from the distal third of the transverse colon onward, is supplied by the inferior mesenteric artery. The cloaca is the terminal hindgut that forms the rectum and urogenital sinus. The pharyngeal gut forms the pharynx. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 1, four divisions of the gut tube); Langman Ch. 15 (gut divisions and arteries).","boardPrep":"Step 1: foregut = celiac, midgut = SMA, hindgut = IMA (First Aid 2025 GI embryology). COMLEX: acute mesenteric ischemia gives pain out of proportion to the examination.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — the same foregut/midgut/hindgut divisions set the vagal versus pelvic parasympathetic territories","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The hindgut, from the distal t"}},{"id":"trial_sdl01_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe formation of the tracheoesophageal septum and explain how abnormal partitioning of the foregut results in esophageal atresia and tracheoesophageal fistula.","batch":3,"stem":"A 2-year-old boy has a congenital tracheal anomaly. His airway primordium first appeared at about 4 weeks of gestation as an outgrowth of which region of the gut tube?","choices":{"A":"Ventral foregut wall","B":"Dorsal foregut wall","C":"Ventral midgut wall","D":"Dorsal pharyngeal wall","E":"Lateral hindgut wall"},"correct":"A","explanation":"At about 4 weeks, the respiratory diverticulum (lung bud) appears on the ventral wall of the foregut at its border with the pharyngeal gut; the ventral part of the partitioned foregut forms the trachea and lung buds, and the dorsal part forms the esophagus. The dorsal foregut forms the esophagus rather than the airway. The midgut, the dorsal pharyngeal wall, and the hindgut do not give rise to the respiratory primordium. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 2, normal formation of the tracheoesophageal septum); Langman Ch. 15 (respiratory diverticulum).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the respiratory diverticulum forms at about 4 weeks of gestation (lecture SDL 1). COMLEX: the foregut also gives rise to the stomach and proximal duodenum (lecture SDL 1).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — epithelium of the esophagus and airway","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl01_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain stomach rotation and describe its effects on the orientation of the stomach, vagal innervation, and associated mesenteries.","batch":3,"stem":"A 35-year-old man has a splenectomy after a motor vehicle collision. While dividing the peritoneal fold that runs from the spleen to the left kidney, the surgeon protects a structure that lies within it. Which structure does this ligament contain?","choices":{"A":"Short gastric arteries","B":"Left gastric artery","C":"Left adrenal gland","D":"Tail of the pancreas","E":"Left gonadal vein"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The part of the dorsal mesogastrium between the spleen and the left kidney becomes the splenorenal ligament, and the tail of the pancreas, which grows from the dorsal mesoduodenum into the dorsal mesogastrium, lies in it along with the splenic artery and vein. The short gastric arteries run in the gastrosplenic ligament. The left gastric artery runs along the lesser curvature in the lesser omentum. The left adrenal gland and left gonadal vein are retroperitoneal structures behind the peritoneum, not within the ligament. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 3, development and position of the spleen and pancreas); First Aid 2025 GI (ligaments: splenorenal ligament contains the splenic vessels and tail of pancreas).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the splenorenal ligament contains the splenic artery and vein and the tail of the pancreas, and the gastrosplenic ligament contains the short gastric and left gastroepiploic vessels (First Aid 2025 GI ligaments). COMLEX: the pancreatic tail can be injured during splenectomy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 3 — pancreatic buds in the dorsal and ventral mesentery","isHighYield":false},{"id":"trial_sdl01_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe normal duodenal recanalization and explain how failure of this process results in congenital duodenal obstruction.","batch":3,"stem":"A 32-year-old woman brings her 3-month-old son for follow-up after repair of a duodenal stenosis caused by a perforated mucosal web. He has no features of a chromosomal syndrome and is now feeding well. She asks when in development the lesion began. The surgeon explains that it arose from a stage when proliferating epithelium temporarily filled the duodenal lumen. During which period of gestation did that stage occur?","choices":{"A":"Weeks 5 to 6","B":"Weeks 2 to 3","C":"Weeks 8 to 10","D":"Weeks 12 to 14","E":"Weeks 20 to 24"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A perforated duodenal web causes stenosis through incomplete recanalization of the solid epithelial cord. During weeks 5 to 6, rapid proliferation of the endodermal epithelium fills the duodenal lumen and forms the solid stage; vacuolization and coalescence of vacuoles then restore the lumen. Weeks 2 to 3 precede folding of the embryo into a gut tube, so there is no duodenal lumen to fill. By weeks 8 to 10 the lumen is normally fully restored, which marks the end of recanalization rather than the solid stage. Weeks 12 to 14 and weeks 20 to 24 come after the lumen has already reopened. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 4, solid stage at weeks 5 to 6 and lumen restoration by weeks 8 to 10, lines 189–203, and duodenal stenosis from a perforated web, lines 210–212); Langman Ch. 15 (duodenum); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (duodenal atresia, failed recanalization).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists VACTERL syndrome and trisomy 21 as associations of duodenal atresia (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, duodenal atresia). COMLEX: First Aid notes that intestinal atresia presents with bilious vomiting and abdominal distension within the first 1–2 days of life (First Aid 2025 GI, intestinal atresia).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 5 — annular pancreas as another cause of congenital duodenal obstruction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl01_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the developmental abnormality underlying hypertrophic pyloric stenosis and recognize its characteristic clinical presentation.","batch":3,"stem":"A 6-week-old boy whose father had a pyloromyotomy as an infant has had forceful nonbilious vomiting right after feeds for 4 days and cries to be fed again afterward. He appears mildly dehydrated. Laboratory studies show a serum chloride of 91 mEq/L (reference range: 95–105 mEq/L) and bicarbonate of 31 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Inspection of his abdomen just before an episode of emesis is most likely to show which finding?","choices":{"A":"Waves moving right to left across the lower abdomen","B":"Waves moving left to right across the epigastrium","C":"A sausage shaped mass in the right upper quadrant","D":"Diffuse distension of the lower abdomen","E":"Bluish periumbilical discoloration"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Forceful nonbilious vomiting right after feeds in a hungry young infant with a family history of the disorder and hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis indicates hypertrophic pyloric stenosis. As the hypertrophied antrum tries to drive chyme past the narrowed pylorus, prominent left-to-right peristaltic waves can be seen crossing the epigastrium just before emesis. Waves running right to left over the lower abdomen do not follow gastric peristalsis toward the pylorus. A sausage shaped right upper quadrant mass suggests intussusception, which causes colicky pain and bloody stools rather than projectile vomiting in a hungry infant. Diffuse lower abdominal distension points to a distal bowel obstruction, whereas pyloric obstruction leaves the lower abdomen flat. Bluish periumbilical discoloration reflects retroperitoneal hemorrhage, as in hemorrhagic pancreatitis, not gastric outlet obstruction. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 5, family history, hungry vomiter, visible gastric peristalsis, and metabolic alkalosis, lines 268–293); First Aid 2025 GI (hypertrophic pyloric stenosis); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (peristaltic wave in epigastrium).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists bottle feeding, nicotine exposure, erythromycin, and azithromycin as risk factors for pyloric stenosis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hypertrophic pyloric stenosis). COMLEX: First Aid notes that ultrasound shows a thickened and lengthened pylorus (First Aid 2025 GI, hypertrophic pyloric stenosis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 3 — pyloric resistance in gastric emptying","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Waves running right to left ov"}},{"id":"trial_sdl01_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four divisions of the primitive gut tube (pharyngeal gut, foregut, midgut, hindgut) and their corresponding blood supply (celiac, SMA, IMA), including the duodenum's dual foregut/midgut supply.","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old man has left-sided abdominal pain and bloody stool after a period of hypotension during cardiac surgery. Colonoscopy shows ischemia centered on the splenic flexure. Why is this site vulnerable?","choices":{"A":"Celiac and SMA boundary","B":"Portal and caval anastomosis","C":"Hindgut and cloaca junction","D":"Single IMA end artery","E":"SMA and IMA watershed"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The splenic flexure lies near the boundary between the midgut territory of the superior mesenteric artery and the hindgut territory of the inferior mesenteric artery, a watershed area that is vulnerable to low flow. The celiac and SMA territories meet at the duodenum. A portal and caval anastomosis is a venous connection in the esophagus, rectum, or umbilical region and does not explain arterial low flow ischemia. The hindgut and cloaca junction is in the pelvis. A single end artery does not describe the flexure, whose supply comes from the edges of two territories, which is what makes it a watershed. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 1, midgut and hindgut boundary at the distal third of the transverse colon); First Aid 2025 GI (colonic ischemia at watershed areas).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ischemic colitis affects watershed areas such as the splenic flexure (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: pain followed by bloody diarrhea after hypotension suggests colonic ischemia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — the midgut–hindgut boundary near the splenic flexure, where vagal and pelvic parasympathetic territories also meet","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":2,"title":"SDL 2 — Histology of the Foregut part 1","questions":[{"id":"sdl02_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four histologic layers of the alimentary canal from lumen outward.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Pawlina, the muscularis mucosae forms the boundary between which two parts of the alimentary canal wall?","choices":{"A":"Epithelium and lamina propria","B":"Inner and outer muscle layers","C":"Submucosa and serosa","D":"Mucosa and submucosa","E":"Lamina propria and serosa"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The muscularis mucosae is the deepest part of the mucosa, a thin band of smooth muscle that separates the mucosa from the submucosa beneath it. The epithelium and lamina propria are both above it, inside the mucosa. The inner circular and outer longitudinal layers belong to the muscularis externa and are separated by the myenteric plexus. The serosa is the outermost coat and does not contact the muscularis mucosae; the submucosa and muscularis externa lie between them. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 1, mucosa); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (the muscularis mucosae forms the boundary between mucosa and submucosa).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gut wall layers from inside out are mucosa, submucosa, muscularis externa, and serosa or adventitia (MSMS); erosions stay in the mucosa, while ulcers extend into the submucosa or deeper (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract anatomy). COMLEX: depth of a lesion relative to the muscularis mucosae separates an erosion from an ulcer.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 1 — layers of the GI wall and location of the enteric plexuses","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"The serosa is the outermost co"}},{"id":"sdl02_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four histologic layers of the alimentary canal from lumen outward.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Pawlina, the thoracic esophagus is fixed to surrounding mediastinal structures for most of its length. Which coat forms its outermost layer?","choices":{"A":"Serosa","B":"Mesothelium","C":"Mesentery","D":"Submucosa","E":"Adventitia"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Where the gut tube is bound to adjacent structures, its outer coat is adventitia, connective tissue that blends with the surrounding connective tissue; the thoracic esophagus is the classic example. Serosa, a simple squamous mesothelium on loose connective tissue, covers only the short abdominal segment after the esophagus enters the peritoneal cavity. Mesothelium is the epithelial part of a serosa, so it is absent where there is no serosa. A mesentery suspends intraperitoneal bowel and does not cover the thoracic esophagus. The submucosa lies inside the muscularis externa, not outside it. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 1, serosa or adventitia); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (esophageal outer layer is adventitia; the abdominal segment is covered by serosa).","boardPrep":"Step 1: serosa covers intraperitoneal segments and adventitia covers retroperitoneal or fixed segments (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract anatomy). COMLEX: the thoracic esophagus lacks a serosal covering.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 1 — layers of the GI wall and location of the enteric plexuses","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Serosa, a simple squamous meso","B":"Mesothelium is the epithelial "}},{"id":"sdl02_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four histologic layers of the alimentary canal from lumen outward.","batch":1,"stem":"Within the muscularis externa of the small intestine, contraction of which component shortens the gut tube during peristalsis?","choices":{"A":"Inner circular layer","B":"Muscularis mucosae","C":"Outer longitudinal layer","D":"Inner oblique layer","E":"Lamina propria myofibroblasts"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The muscularis externa has an inner circular and an outer longitudinal layer of smooth muscle. Contraction of the outer longitudinal layer shortens the tube, while contraction of the inner circular layer constricts the lumen; together they drive peristalsis and segmentation. The muscularis mucosae lies in the mucosa and makes local folds without moving the whole segment. An inner oblique layer is a regional feature of the stomach, not the small intestine. Myofibroblasts in the lamina propria do not form a muscle layer that shortens the bowel. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 1, muscularis externa); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (muscularis externa).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the myenteric (Auerbach) plexus between the two layers of the muscularis externa controls motility (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract anatomy). COMLEX: the stomach adds an inner oblique layer and the colon condenses its longitudinal layer into teniae coli.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 1 — layers of the GI wall and location of the enteric plexuses","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Correlate epithelial types and mucosal specializations with protection, absorption, and secretion in the GI tract.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Pawlina, which epithelium lines the human esophagus and resists abrasion by swallowed food?","choices":{"A":"Keratinized stratified squamous","B":"Nonkeratinized stratified squamous","C":"Simple columnar with goblet cells","D":"Pseudostratified ciliated columnar","E":"Stratified columnar with microvilli"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The esophagus is lined by nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium, whose multiple cell layers resist abrasion from rough food boluses while staying moist. Keratinized stratified squamous epithelium is not the normal human esophageal lining. Simple columnar epithelium with goblet cells is the intestinal pattern and appears in the esophagus only as Barrett metaplasia. Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium lines the airway. Stratified columnar epithelium with microvilli is not a lining of the alimentary canal. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 2, protection); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (esophageal mucosa).","boardPrep":"Step 1: esophagus has nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium; Barrett esophagus replaces it with intestinal columnar epithelium containing goblet cells (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract histology and Barrett esophagus). COMLEX: chronic acid reflux drives this metaplasia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 4 — histologic components of the gastric mucosal barrier","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Correlate epithelial types and mucosal specializations with protection, absorption, and secretion in the GI tract.","batch":1,"stem":"The surface mucous cells of the stomach secrete a thick gel that protects the epithelium from luminal acid. This gel is rich in which ions?","choices":{"A":"Hydrogen and chloride","B":"Ammonium and phosphate","C":"Calcium and magnesium","D":"Bicarbonate and K+","E":"Sodium and sulfate"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Surface mucous cells secrete a viscous, visible mucus layer rich in bicarbonate and K+. The bicarbonate trapped in the gel neutralizes acid at the epithelial surface, and the gel slows pepsin, which protects the mucosa from autodigestion. Hydrogen and chloride ions are the components of the hydrochloric acid secreted by parietal cells, the injury the gel guards against. Ammonium is generated by H. pylori urease, not by surface mucous cells. Phosphate, calcium, magnesium, and sulfate are not the defining ions of this barrier. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 2, surface mucous cells and alkaline mucus layer); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (surface mucous cells).","boardPrep":"Step 1: prostaglandins maintain gastric mucus and bicarbonate secretion; NSAIDs reduce them and predispose to ulcers (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: misoprostol, a prostaglandin analog, restores this protection.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 4 — histologic components of the gastric mucosal barrier","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Ammonium is generated by H. py"}},{"id":"sdl02_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Correlate epithelial types and mucosal specializations with protection, absorption, and secretion in the GI tract.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Pawlina, which crypt-base cell type of the small intestine secretes lysozyme and alpha-defensins?","choices":{"A":"Goblet cells","B":"Enteroendocrine cells","C":"Paneth cells","D":"Enterocytes","E":"Tuft cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Paneth cells at the base of the crypts of Lieberkühn secrete lysozyme and alpha-defensins, antimicrobial products of innate immunity. Goblet cells secrete mucin for lubrication. Enteroendocrine cells release peptide hormones such as CCK, secretin, GIP, and motilin. Enterocytes absorb nutrients through their microvillous brush border. Tuft cells are chemosensory cells and do not secrete these antimicrobial proteins. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 2, secretion in the small intestine); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (Paneth cells).","boardPrep":"Step 1: crypts of Lieberkühn contain stem cells and Paneth cells that secrete defensins, lysozyme, and TNF (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract histology). COMLEX: Paneth cells sit at the crypt base, below the stem cells that renew the epithelium.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 4 — histologic components of the gastric mucosal barrier","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Enteroendocrine cells release ","D":"Enterocytes absorb nutrients t"}},{"id":"sdl02_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses by location and function.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Pawlina, where in the wall of the alimentary canal is the myenteric plexus located?","choices":{"A":"Between the muscle layers","B":"Within the submucosa","C":"Within the lamina propria","D":"Beneath the epithelium","E":"Within the adventitia"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The myenteric (Auerbach) plexus lies in the thin connective tissue between the inner circular and outer longitudinal layers of the muscularis externa, where it innervates both layers. The submucosa holds the submucosal (Meissner) plexus. The lamina propria and the region beneath the epithelium carry nerve fibers but not the myenteric ganglia. The adventitia is the outer connective tissue coat outside the muscularis externa. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 3, anatomic location); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (myenteric plexus between the outer and inner muscle layers).","boardPrep":"Step 1: submucosa contains the Meissner plexus; muscularis externa contains the Auerbach plexus (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract anatomy). COMLEX: Hirschsprung disease shows absent ganglion cells in both plexuses.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system control of motility and secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses by location and function.","batch":1,"stem":"In the enteric nervous system, which plexus mainly regulates local secretion, absorption, and blood flow in each gut segment?","choices":{"A":"Myenteric plexus","B":"Celiac plexus","C":"Hypogastric plexus","D":"Esophageal vagal plexus","E":"Submucosal plexus"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The submucosal (Meissner) plexus innervates mucosal and submucosal glands, local blood vessels, and the muscularis mucosae, controlling secretion, absorption, blood flow, and mucosal folding in each segment. The myenteric (Auerbach) plexus controls motility of the muscularis externa along the length of the gut. The celiac and hypogastric plexuses are extrinsic autonomic plexuses outside the gut wall. The esophageal vagal plexus carries extrinsic vagal fibers on the esophagus surface. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 3, main functional role); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (submucosal plexus).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Meissner plexus regulates secretion and blood flow; Auerbach plexus regulates motility (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract anatomy). COMLEX: both plexuses receive extrinsic vagal and sympathetic modulation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system control of motility and secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses by location and function.","batch":1,"stem":"Inhibitory neurons of the myenteric plexus relax the lower esophageal sphincter to let a bolus enter the stomach. Which transmitter do these neurons release?","choices":{"A":"Acetylcholine","B":"Nitric oxide","C":"Substance P","D":"Norepinephrine","E":"Serotonin"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Inhibitory peptidergic neurons of the myenteric plexus release nitric oxide, together with VIP or ATP, to relax GI sphincters such as the lower esophageal, pyloric, and ileocecal sphincters. Acetylcholine and substance P are excitatory transmitters that contract smooth muscle. Norepinephrine comes from extrinsic sympathetic postganglionic fibers and inhibits motility rather than mediating enteric sphincter relaxation. Serotonin acts mainly as a sensory and paracrine signal in the mucosa. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 3, sphincter control).","boardPrep":"Step 1: achalasia is caused by loss of NO and VIP inhibitory neurons in the myenteric plexus, so the LES fails to relax (First Aid 2025 GI achalasia). COMLEX: bird beak narrowing on barium swallow.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system control of motility and secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the muscle type transitions and their innervation along the esophagus.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Pawlina, the muscularis externa of the upper third of the esophagus consists of which tissue?","choices":{"A":"Striated muscle","B":"Smooth muscle","C":"Mixed striated and smooth","D":"Cardiac muscle","E":"Myoepithelial cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The upper third of the esophageal muscularis externa is striated muscle, a continuation of the pharyngeal musculature. Smooth muscle forms the distal third. Mixed and interwoven striated and smooth bundles form the middle third. Cardiac muscle is not part of the gut wall. Myoepithelial cells surround the secretory units of some glands and do not form the muscularis externa. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 4, muscle type transitions); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (upper one-third is striated muscle).","boardPrep":"Step 1: upper 1/3 of the esophagus is striated muscle, middle 1/3 mixed, and lower 1/3 smooth muscle (lecture; First Aid 2025 GI groups the middle and lower 2/3 as smooth muscle with some overlap at the transition). COMLEX: upper esophageal dysphagia suggests a skeletal muscle or neuromuscular cause.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 1 — neural and muscular coordination of swallowing","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the muscle type transitions and their innervation along the esophagus.","batch":1,"stem":"The striated muscle of the proximal esophagus is supplied by somatic motor neurons that travel in the vagus nerve. From which brainstem nucleus do these neurons arise?","choices":{"A":"Dorsal motor nucleus","B":"Hypoglossal nucleus","C":"Nucleus tractus solitarius","D":"Nucleus ambiguus","E":"Facial motor nucleus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Somatic motor neurons to the striated esophageal muscle arise in the nucleus ambiguus and end directly on motor endplates. The dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus sends preganglionic parasympathetic fibers to the smooth muscle of the distal esophagus. The hypoglossal nucleus supplies tongue muscles. The nucleus tractus solitarius receives visceral afferent input. The facial motor nucleus supplies muscles of facial expression. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 4, innervation of striated musculature).","boardPrep":"Step 1: somatic motor neurons of the nucleus ambiguus travel in the vagus to the striated muscle of the upper esophagus, whereas the dorsal motor nucleus supplies the smooth muscle (Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 2, section 4; Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: a brainstem lesion involving the nucleus ambiguus can cause dysphagia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 1 — neural and muscular coordination of swallowing","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the muscle type transitions and their innervation along the esophagus.","batch":1,"stem":"Preganglionic parasympathetic fibers from the dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus reach the smooth muscle of the distal esophagus. Where do these fibers synapse?","choices":{"A":"Celiac ganglion neurons","B":"Motor endplates on muscle","C":"Myenteric plexus neurons","D":"Stellate ganglion neurons","E":"Submucosal gland cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Vagal preganglionic fibers from the dorsal motor nucleus synapse on postganglionic enteric neurons whose cell bodies lie in the myenteric plexus of the esophageal wall; these neurons then drive contraction and peristalsis. The celiac and stellate ganglia are sympathetic ganglia. Motor endplates are the targets of somatic fibers from the nucleus ambiguus on striated muscle, not of preganglionic parasympathetic fibers. Submucosal gland cells are effectors, not the site of the ganglionic synapse. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 4, parasympathetic control of smooth musculature).","boardPrep":"Step 1: parasympathetic preganglionic fibers are long and synapse in ganglia in or near the target organ, within the myenteric and submucosal plexuses (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: vagal input enhances peristalsis through the enteric neurons.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 1 — neural and muscular coordination of swallowing","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Locate and explain the functions of esophageal glands proper and esophageal cardiac glands.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Pawlina, the esophageal glands proper are located in which layer of the esophageal wall?","choices":{"A":"Lamina propria","B":"Adventitia","C":"Epithelium","D":"Muscularis mucosae","E":"Submucosa"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Esophageal glands proper are small compound tubuloalveolar glands in the submucosa, and their ducts cross the muscularis mucosae and mucosa to open into the lumen. The lamina propria holds the esophageal cardiac glands, not the glands proper. The adventitia is the outer connective tissue coat. The epithelium is crossed only by the ducts. The muscularis mucosae is a smooth muscle layer that the ducts pass through. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 5, esophageal glands proper); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (esophageal glands proper lie in the submucosa).","boardPrep":"Step 1: submucosal glands are found in the esophagus and in the duodenum as Brunner glands (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract histology). COMLEX: identify gland layer to name the gland type on a slide.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 3 — reflux injury and Barrett esophagus","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"The muscularis mucosae is a sm"}},{"id":"sdl02_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Locate and explain the functions of esophageal glands proper and esophageal cardiac glands.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Pawlina, the esophageal cardiac glands near the gastroesophageal junction secrete which product?","choices":{"A":"Neutral mucus","B":"Acidic mucus","C":"Serous fluid","D":"Bicarbonate fluid","E":"Pepsinogen"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Esophageal cardiac glands secrete neutral mucus that protects the distal esophagus from regurgitated gastric contents. The esophageal glands proper secrete a slightly acidic mucus, which makes acidic mucus the wrong gland. Serous fluid comes from serous glands such as the parotid. Bicarbonate fluid is the product of duodenal Brunner glands. Pepsinogen is secreted by gastric chief cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 5, esophageal cardiac glands); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (esophageal cardiac glands produce neutral mucus).","boardPrep":"Step 1: when reflux overwhelms distal protection, GERD can lead to Barrett metaplasia (First Aid 2025 GI Barrett esophagus). COMLEX: heartburn reflects acid and pepsin injury of the distal mucosa.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 3 — reflux injury and Barrett esophagus","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl02_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Locate and explain the functions of esophageal glands proper and esophageal cardiac glands.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Pawlina, the esophageal glands proper secrete a slightly acidic, viscous mucus. What is the main function of this secretion?","choices":{"A":"Neutralizing refluxed acid","B":"Lubricating the lumen","C":"Digesting dietary starch","D":"Absorbing swallowed water","E":"Secreting immunoglobulin A"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The slightly acidic mucus of the esophageal glands proper lubricates the luminal surface so boluses pass smoothly and the squamous lining is protected from mechanical injury. Neutralizing refluxed acid is the role of the neutral mucus from esophageal cardiac glands near the stomach. Starch digestion begins with salivary amylase, not esophageal mucus. The esophagus does not absorb swallowed water. IgA is secreted by plasma cells and transported across epithelium, not produced by these glands. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 5, secretion type and function); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (mucus of glands proper lubricates the luminal wall).","boardPrep":"Step 1: esophageal mucosa is protective, not absorptive (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract histology). COMLEX: impaired lubrication contributes to the sensation of food sticking.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 3 — reflux injury and Barrett esophagus","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"E":"IgA is secreted by plasma cell"}},{"id":"sdl02_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four histologic layers of the alimentary canal from lumen outward.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman who takes daily naproxen for arthritis has epigastric pain. Endoscopic biopsy of a gastric lesion shows that the tissue loss has crossed the muscularis mucosae and ends in dense irregular connective tissue containing large vessels. Which layer forms the base of this lesion?","choices":{"A":"Epithelium","B":"Lamina propria","C":"Adventitia","D":"Mesothelium","E":"Submucosa"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Dense irregular connective tissue with large blood and lymphatic vessels just deep to the muscularis mucosae is the submucosa. A defect that reaches it is an ulcer rather than an erosion. The epithelium and lamina propria lie above the muscularis mucosae, so a lesion ending there would be an erosion. The adventitia and the mesothelium of the serosa lie outside the muscularis externa, much deeper than this lesion. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 1, submucosa); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (submucosa).","boardPrep":"Step 1: erosions are confined to the mucosa; ulcers extend into the submucosa or muscularis (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract anatomy). COMLEX: NSAIDs cause gastric ulcers by reducing prostaglandins.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — NSAIDs in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four histologic layers of the alimentary canal from lumen outward.","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old man has squamous cell carcinoma of the mid-thoracic esophagus. CT shows the tumor extending directly into the surrounding mediastinal fat. Absence of which layer in this segment of the esophageal wall helps explain this spread?","choices":{"A":"Serosa","B":"Mucosa","C":"Submucosa","D":"Adventitia","E":"Epithelium"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The thoracic esophagus has no serosa; its outer coat is adventitia, which blends into the mediastinal connective tissue, so there is no mesothelial boundary between the tumor and the mediastinum. Serosa covers only the short abdominal segment. The mucosa, submucosa, and epithelium are present in the thoracic esophagus, and the tumor arises from its epithelium. Adventitia is present, not absent, and it is the coat the tumor crosses. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 1, adventitia of the thoracic esophagus); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (esophageal adventitia); First Aid 2025 GI (esophageal cancer: lack of serosa in the esophageal wall allows rapid extension).","boardPrep":"Step 1: squamous cell carcinoma involves the upper 2/3 of the esophagus and adenocarcinoma the lower 1/3 (First Aid 2025 GI esophageal cancer). COMLEX: smoking and alcohol are leading risks for squamous cell carcinoma.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 5 — anatomic distribution of esophageal squamous cell carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four histologic layers of the alimentary canal from lumen outward.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman undergoes small-bowel biopsy for bloating. The pathologist points to a thin band of smooth muscle at the base of the crypts that separates the mucosa from the submucosa. Contraction of this band has which effect?","choices":{"A":"Propulsive peristalsis of chyme","B":"Shortening of the bowel segment","C":"Local ridges in the inner lining","D":"Closure of the ileocecal valve","E":"Constriction of the full bowel lumen"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The band is the muscularis mucosae. Its local contractions raise folds and ridges of the mucosa that improve contact with chyme for absorption and secretion, independent of whole-organ peristalsis. Propulsive peristalsis, shortening of the segment, and constriction of the whole lumen come from the outer longitudinal and inner circular layers of the muscularis externa. Closure of the ileocecal valve depends on sphincteric circular muscle of the muscularis externa. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 1, muscularis mucosae); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (muscularis mucosae).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the submucosal plexus controls the muscularis mucosae, local secretion, and blood flow (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract anatomy). COMLEX: mucosal movement aids contact between chyme and the absorptive surface.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 1 — layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Correlate epithelial types and mucosal specializations with protection, absorption, and secretion in the GI tract.","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman has chronic diarrhea and iron deficiency anemia. Duodenal biopsy shows flattened mucosa with loss of the finger-like projections that contain central lacteals. Loss of these projections impairs which function?","choices":{"A":"Acid neutralization","B":"Pathogen sampling","C":"Peristaltic propulsion","D":"Nutrient absorption","E":"Pepsinogen secretion"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The finger-like projections are villi, cores of lamina propria with capillaries and a central lacteal covered by enterocytes. With plicae circulares and microvilli, they amplify absorptive surface area, so villous atrophy impairs nutrient absorption. Acid neutralization in the duodenum depends on Brunner gland and pancreatic bicarbonate. Pathogen sampling is the role of Peyer patches and M cells. Peristaltic propulsion depends on the muscularis externa. Pepsinogen is secreted by gastric chief cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 2, absorption and amplification specializations); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (villi).","boardPrep":"Step 1: celiac disease shows villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia, and intraepithelial lymphocytosis, mostly in the distal duodenum and proximal jejunum (First Aid 2025 GI celiac disease). COMLEX: iron deficiency reflects duodenal malabsorption.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 3 — loss of specialized epithelial cells causing malabsorption, as parietal-cell loss impairs B12 absorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Correlate epithelial types and mucosal specializations with protection, absorption, and secretion in the GI tract.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man with long-standing heartburn undergoes endoscopy. Biopsy of the distal esophagus shows columnar epithelium with goblet cells replacing the normal lining. The replaced lining was specialized for which function?","choices":{"A":"Absorbing nutrients and water","B":"Secreting acid and pepsinogen","C":"Resisting mechanical abrasion","D":"Sampling luminal antigens","E":"Secreting antimicrobial peptides"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The normal esophagus is lined by nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium, whose layered cells resist mechanical abrasion from food boluses. Here it has been replaced by intestinal-type columnar epithelium, which is Barrett esophagus. Absorbing nutrients and water is the role of intestinal enterocytes. Secreting acid and pepsinogen is the role of gastric parietal and chief cells. Sampling luminal antigens is done by M cells over Peyer patches. Secreting antimicrobial peptides is the role of Paneth cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 2, protection, and section 5, cardiac glands and Barrett esophagus); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (esophageal epithelium).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Barrett esophagus is intestinal metaplasia with goblet cells replacing nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium in the distal esophagus, a precursor of adenocarcinoma (First Aid 2025 GI Barrett esophagus). COMLEX: chronic GERD is the driving exposure.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — GERD and Barrett esophagus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Correlate epithelial types and mucosal specializations with protection, absorption, and secretion in the GI tract.","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman who takes high-dose aspirin develops gastric erosions. The drug reduces prostaglandin-driven secretion by the cells that line the gastric surface and pits. Loss of their secretion impairs which protective mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Killing microbes with lysozyme","B":"Neutralizing acid at the epithelium","C":"Resisting abrasion with keratin","D":"Absorbing acid through tight junctions","E":"Trapping antigens in Peyer patches"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Surface mucous cells line the gastric surface and pits and secrete a viscous mucus rich in bicarbonate that neutralizes acid at the epithelium and slows pepsin. Prostaglandins sustain this secretion, so aspirin weakens the barrier. Killing microbes with lysozyme is a Paneth cell function in the intestine. Gastric epithelium is columnar and not keratinized. Tight junctions seal the epithelium against back-diffusion of acid rather than absorbing it. Peyer patches are lymphoid aggregates of the ileum. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 2, surface mucous cells and alkaline mucus layer); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (surface mucous cells).","boardPrep":"Step 1: NSAIDs inhibit COX and reduce prostaglandin-dependent mucus and bicarbonate secretion, causing erosions and ulcers (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: misoprostol is used to prevent NSAID-induced ulcers.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 4 — gastric mucosal barrier and its disruption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses by location and function.","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-day-old boy has not passed meconium and has abdominal distension. Full-thickness rectal biopsy shows no ganglion cells between the circular and longitudinal muscle layers or in the submucosa. Loss of the ganglia between the muscle layers impairs which function?","choices":{"A":"Local secretion by mucosal glands","B":"Regulation of submucosal blood flow","C":"Coordinated propulsive motility","D":"Absorption of nutrients by enterocytes","E":"Antigen sampling by Peyer patches"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Ganglia between the circular and longitudinal layers form the myenteric (Auerbach) plexus, which controls tone, rhythmic contraction, and conduction of peristaltic waves and relaxes sphincters. Without it the aganglionic segment stays contracted and does not propel stool. Local secretion and submucosal blood flow are mainly regulated by the submucosal (Meissner) plexus. Absorption by enterocytes is an epithelial function. Antigen sampling is a lymphoid function. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 3, myenteric plexus function); First Aid 2025 GI (Hirschsprung disease; suction biopsy, which samples mucosa and submucosa, is the usual first test).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hirschsprung disease is absent ganglion cells in both plexuses of the distal colon from failed neural crest migration; diagnosed by rectal suction biopsy; associated with Down syndrome (First Aid 2025 GI Hirschsprung disease). COMLEX: empty rectum with explosive stool on rectal exam.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — enteric nervous system control of motility","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses by location and function.","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman takes part in a study of a toxin that selectively destroys the enteric motor neurons, lying between the circular and longitudinal muscle layers, that relax gut smooth muscle. Failed opening of which of the following would be expected?","choices":{"A":"External anal sphincter","B":"Upper esophageal sphincter","C":"Diaphragmatic crura","D":"Puborectalis sling","E":"Ileocecal sphincter"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Peptidergic relaxing neurons of the Auerbach plexus open the smooth muscle sphincters of the gut, including the pyloric and ileocecal sphincters, so their loss leaves the ileocecal sphincter contracted; their transmitters are nitric oxide, VIP, and ATP. The external anal sphincter and the puborectalis sling are skeletal muscle under somatic control. The upper esophageal sphincter is striated muscle driven by vagal motor neurons from the nucleus ambiguus. The diaphragmatic crura are skeletal muscle that reinforce the lower esophageal sphincter from outside. The submucosal plexus has only a minimal role in sphincter tone. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 3, sphincter control by the myenteric plexus, p13; section 4, striated muscle innervation, p14); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 5, diaphragmatic crural reinforcement, p35).","boardPrep":"Step 1: myenteric inhibitory neurons that release NO and VIP relax the LES, pylorus, and ileocecal sphincter; achalasia is loss of these neurons in the distal esophagus (First Aid 2025 GI achalasia). COMLEX: the submucosal plexus controls secretion and blood flow and has little effect on sphincter tone (lecture SDL 2).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — inhibitory enteric neurons release VIP, nitric oxide, and ATP","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses by location and function.","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old man receives a small-bowel transplant, which severs all extrinsic vagal and sympathetic nerves to the graft. Within days the graft shows coordinated peristalsis and local secretion. Which explains this function?","choices":{"A":"Intrinsic enteric plexuses remain","B":"Sympathetic fibers regenerate","C":"Vagal fibers regrow into the graft","D":"Smooth muscle needs no neurons","E":"Hormones replace neural control"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The enteric nervous system is an intrinsic network of neurons and glia in two plexuses inside the gut wall. The myenteric plexus coordinates peristalsis and the submucosal plexus controls local secretion, and both work without extrinsic input, which only modulates them. Severed sympathetic and vagal fibers do not regenerate into a graft within days. Smooth muscle pacing alone does not coordinate peristalsis. Hormones modulate gut function but do not replace neural reflex circuits. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 3, enteric nervous system and extrinsic modulation, and section 4, intrinsic autonomy); Guyton Ch. 63 (enteric nervous system).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the enteric nervous system can function independently of extrinsic nerves, which modulate it (Guyton Ch. 63). COMLEX: parasympathetic input increases and sympathetic input decreases peristalsis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — extrinsic modulation of the enteric nervous system","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the muscle type transitions and their innervation along the esophagus.","batch":2,"stem":"A 54-year-old woman has a brainstem stroke with hoarseness and difficulty initiating swallowing. Manometry shows weak contractions in the upper esophagus with normal peristalsis in the distal esophagus. Which tissue in the upper esophagus explains this selective weakness?","choices":{"A":"Visceral smooth muscle","B":"Abundant myenteric neurons","C":"Serosal covering","D":"Striated skeletal muscle","E":"Cardiac gland mucosa"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The upper third of the esophagus is striated skeletal muscle supplied by somatic motor neurons from the nucleus ambiguus, which a brainstem stroke can damage. The distal esophagus is smooth muscle driven by the dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus and the myenteric plexus, which can keep running on its own, so distal peristalsis is preserved. Visceral smooth muscle is the distal tissue, the one spared. Myenteric neurons are not selectively abundant in the upper third. The thoracic esophagus has adventitia, not serosa. Cardiac glands are mucosal glands and do not contract. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 4, muscle type transitions and innervation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: upper 1/3 striated, middle 1/3 mixed, lower 1/3 smooth muscle (Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 2, section 4; Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: oropharyngeal dysphagia points to striated muscle or its brainstem control.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 1 — neural coordination of swallowing","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the muscle type transitions and their innervation along the esophagus.","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old woman with Raynaud phenomenon and sclerodactyly has heartburn and dysphagia. Manometry shows absent contractions in the distal esophagus with low lower sphincter pressure and normal proximal contractions. Which tissue is affected?","choices":{"A":"Striated muscle","B":"Pharyngeal constrictors","C":"Diaphragmatic crura","D":"Cricopharyngeus","E":"Smooth muscle"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Scleroderma causes atrophy and fibrosis of esophageal smooth muscle, which makes up the distal esophagus and the lower sphincter, so distal peristalsis and sphincter tone fail. Striated muscle of the proximal third is spared, which fits the normal proximal contractions. The pharyngeal constrictors and cricopharyngeus are striated muscles above or at the upper esophagus. The diaphragmatic crura reinforce the sphincter externally but are skeletal muscle and not the site of disease. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 4, muscle type transitions); First Aid 2025 GI (scleroderma esophageal dysmotility).","boardPrep":"Step 1: scleroderma causes esophageal smooth muscle atrophy with decreased LES pressure and distal dysmotility, leading to reflux and dysphagia; part of CREST (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: GERD from scleroderma can cause strictures and Barrett esophagus.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — esophageal motility disorders","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the muscle type transitions and their innervation along the esophagus.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man has injury to both vagal trunks during a thoracic operation. Afterward, balloon distension of the distal esophagus still triggers peristaltic waves in that segment. What mediates these waves?","choices":{"A":"Nucleus ambiguus output","B":"Myenteric plexus reflexes","C":"Glossopharyngeal afferents","D":"Phrenic nerve reflexes","E":"Sympathetic celiac outflow"},"correct":"B","explanation":"When vagal fibers to the distal esophagus are interrupted, the intrinsic myenteric plexus can still start secondary peristaltic waves in response to local distension. The nucleus ambiguus sends its output through the vagus, which is injured, and it supplies striated muscle. Glossopharyngeal afferents trigger the pharyngeal phase of swallowing, not local esophageal reflexes. The phrenic nerve supplies the diaphragm. Sympathetic celiac outflow suppresses esophageal motility. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 4, intrinsic autonomy).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secondary peristalsis is mediated by the enteric nervous system and begins at the point of distension (Costanzo Ch. 8; Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 6, secondary peristalsis). COMLEX: intrinsic reflexes keep some motility after vagal injury.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 2 — primary versus secondary esophageal peristalsis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Locate and explain the functions of esophageal glands proper and esophageal cardiac glands.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old man with occasional heartburn has biopsies taken just above the gastroesophageal junction. The pathologist notes branched tubular mucous glands in the lamina propria that resemble gastric cardiac glands. What is the main function of these glands?","choices":{"A":"Protecting against refluxed acid","B":"Lubricating the upper esophagus","C":"Secreting acid into the lumen","D":"Secreting pepsinogen for digestion","E":"Absorbing fluid from the bolus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"These are esophageal cardiac glands. Their neutral mucus protects the distal esophageal mucosa from regurgitated acidic gastric juice; when reflux overwhelms them, heartburn and GERD follow. Lubricating the upper esophagus is the role of the esophageal glands proper in the submucosa, which are concentrated in the upper half. Acid is secreted by gastric parietal cells and pepsinogen by chief cells, not by these mucous glands. The esophagus does not absorb fluid from the bolus. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 5, esophageal cardiac glands); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (esophageal cardiac glands).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GERD complications include erosive esophagitis, strictures, and Barrett esophagus (First Aid 2025 GI GERD). COMLEX: heartburn reflects acid and pepsin contact with distal mucosa.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — reflux injury of the distal esophagus","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"Acid is secreted by gastric pa"}},{"id":"sdl02_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Locate and explain the functions of esophageal glands proper and esophageal cardiac glands.","batch":2,"stem":"A pathologist counts submucosal mucous glands with dilated ducts lined by stratified squamous epithelium in sections taken at several levels of an autopsy esophagus. Where are these glands most densely concentrated?","choices":{"A":"Abdominal segment below the hiatus","B":"Upper half of the esophagus","C":"Region just above the cardia","D":"Evenly along its length","E":"Distal third of smooth muscle"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Submucosal tubuloalveolar glands with dilated squamous-lined ducts are esophageal glands proper. They are scattered along the whole esophagus but are most densely concentrated in its upper half, where their viscous mucus lubricates the lumen. The region just above the cardia is where esophageal cardiac glands, which lie in the lamina propria, are found. The abdominal segment and the distal smooth-muscle third are not where glands proper are densest. Their distribution is not even, because they cluster in the upper half. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 5, esophageal glands proper concentrated in the upper half, p15); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 5, comparison table, p20).","boardPrep":"Step 1: esophageal glands proper lie in the submucosa and esophageal cardiac glands in the lamina propria (lecture SDL 2). COMLEX: cardiac glands sit near the gastroesophageal junction, where their neutral mucus meets refluxed acid (lecture SDL 2).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 5 — esophageal versus gastric glands","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"Their distribution is not even"}},{"id":"sdl02_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Locate and explain the functions of esophageal glands proper and esophageal cardiac glands.","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman has endoscopy for intermittent dysphagia. Small cysts are found in the upper esophagus, and biopsy shows dilated submucosal ducts lined by stratified squamous epithelium and filled with mucus. Which property of the secretion favors these cysts?","choices":{"A":"High bicarbonate content","B":"Enzymatic digestion of ducts","C":"Neutral pH of the mucus","D":"High viscosity of the mucus","E":"Serous watery composition"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The ducts belong to esophageal glands proper, which secrete a viscous, slightly acidic mucus. Its viscosity favors transient cysts in the ducts. High bicarbonate content is typical of Brunner gland and pancreatic secretion. These glands make mucus, not enzymes that digest ducts. Neutral mucus is the product of the cardiac glands, not the glands proper. A serous watery secretion would flow freely and is not what these glands make. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 5, esophageal glands proper); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (viscous secretion; transient cysts in the ducts).","boardPrep":"Step 1: esophageal epithelium is nonkeratinized stratified squamous (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract histology). COMLEX: esophageal glands proper are most concentrated in the upper half of the esophagus.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — structural causes of dysphagia","isHighYield":false},{"id":"trial_sdl02_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four histologic layers of the alimentary canal from lumen outward.","batch":3,"stem":"A 56-year-old man has free air under the diaphragm after an anterior gastric ulcer perforates through the outermost coat of the stomach wall. Which tissue lines that coat?","choices":{"A":"Simple columnar epithelium","B":"Stratified squamous epithelium","C":"Transitional epithelium","D":"Dense irregular connective tissue","E":"Simple squamous mesothelium"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The stomach is intraperitoneal, so its outermost coat is a serosa, which consists of a simple squamous mesothelium over a thin layer of connective tissue. Simple columnar epithelium lines the gastric lumen. Stratified squamous epithelium lines the esophagus. Dense irregular connective tissue forms the submucosa and the adventitia of fixed segments. Transitional epithelium lines the urinary tract. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 1, serosa is a simple squamous mesothelium, lines 336–337); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (serosa).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ulcers can extend into the submucosa and muscular layers; erosions stay in the mucosa (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract anatomy). COMLEX: perforation causes peritonitis with free air.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — complications of peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl02_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Correlate epithelial types and mucosal specializations with protection, absorption, and secretion in the GI tract.","batch":3,"stem":"An 18-month-old boy develops an ileocolic intussusception a week after a viral illness. The lead point is enlarged lymphoid tissue in the wall of the distal ileum. What is this lymphoid tissue called?","choices":{"A":"Brunner glands","B":"Peyer patches","C":"Paneth cells","D":"Plicae circulares","E":"Crypts of Lieberkühn"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Peyer patches are aggregated lymphatic nodules in the lamina propria and submucosa of the distal ileum, part of the gut-associated lymphatic tissue that samples and neutralizes ingested pathogens. Brunner glands are submucosal bicarbonate-secreting glands of the duodenum. Paneth cells are crypt-base cells that secrete lysozyme and defensins. Plicae circulares are transverse folds of mucosa and submucosa. Crypts of Lieberkühn are intestinal glands. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 2, immune barrier); First Aid 2025 GI (Peyer patches).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Peyer patches are unencapsulated lymphoid tissue in the lamina propria and submucosa of the ileum, and their B cells make IgA (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: Peyer patch hypertrophy can act as an intussusception lead point in young children (First Aid 2025 GI intussusception).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 5 — mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue, the source of gastric MALT lymphoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl02_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses by location and function.","batch":3,"stem":"A 51-year-old man from rural Brazil has progressive dysphagia, and serology is positive for Trypanosoma cruzi. Biopsy shows destruction of the neurons between the muscle layers of the distal esophagus. Which function is lost?","choices":{"A":"LES relaxation","B":"Gland secretion","C":"Mucosal blood flow","D":"Bolus lubrication","E":"Gastric acid output"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Chagas disease destroys neurons of the myenteric plexus, including the inhibitory NO and VIP neurons that relax the lower esophageal sphincter, producing secondary achalasia. Gland secretion and mucosal blood flow are mainly controlled by the submucosal plexus. Bolus lubrication comes from the esophageal glands proper. Gastric acid output is controlled at the stomach. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 3, myenteric plexus and sphincter control); First Aid 2025 GI (achalasia and Chagas disease).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secondary achalasia can result from Chagas disease (First Aid 2025 GI achalasia). COMLEX: barium swallow shows bird beak narrowing.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — achalasia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl02_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the muscle type transitions and their innervation along the esophagus.","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman has an esophagectomy, and a section from the upper third shows cross-striated fibers in the muscularis externa. What innervates this muscle?","choices":{"A":"Preganglionic vagal fibers","B":"Sympathetic celiac fibers","C":"Glossopharyngeal fibers","D":"Vagal branchial efferents","E":"Phrenic motor fibers"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Striated muscle in the upper third is supplied by motor neurons of the nucleus ambiguus whose axons travel in the vagus and end on motor endplates; the lecture calls them somatic motor neurons, and standard texts classify them as branchial or special visceral efferents. Preganglionic vagal fibers from the dorsal motor nucleus synapse in the myenteric plexus to control the smooth muscle of the distal esophagus. Sympathetic celiac fibers supply smooth muscle and vessels and suppress motility. Glossopharyngeal fibers serve the pharynx. Phrenic fibers supply the diaphragm. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 4, innervation of striated musculature); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (esophageal muscularis externa).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the upper esophagus is striated muscle and the lower esophagus smooth muscle (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract histology). COMLEX: oropharyngeal dysphagia points to striated muscle or its control.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 1 — swallowing coordination","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Phrenic fibers supply the diap"}},{"id":"trial_sdl02_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Locate and explain the functions of esophageal glands proper and esophageal cardiac glands.","batch":3,"stem":"A biopsy taken from the esophagus just above the gastroesophageal junction shows branched, tortuous tubular mucous glands lying in the lamina propria. By which route do these glands deliver their secretion to the lumen?","choices":{"A":"Long squamous-lined ducts through the muscularis mucosae","B":"Intercalated ducts lined by low cuboidal cells","C":"Striated ducts with basal membrane infoldings","D":"Myoepithelial-wrapped ducts into the gastric cardia","E":"Short columnar-lined ducts into shallow mucosal pits"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Lamina propria glands near the gastroesophageal junction are esophageal cardiac glands. They are branched tubular glands of columnar mucous cells that empty through short columnar-lined duct segments into the base of shallow mucosal depressions. Long ducts lined by stratified squamous epithelium that pass through the muscularis mucosae belong to the submucosal esophageal glands proper. Intercalated ducts with low cuboidal cells belong to the exocrine pancreas. Striated ducts with basal infoldings belong to the salivary glands. Ducts wrapped by myoepithelial cells that open into the gastric cardia are not described for these glands. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 5, morphology of esophageal cardiac glands, p15); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (esophageal cardiac glands).","boardPrep":"Step 1: esophageal cardiac glands resemble gastric cardiac glands and secrete neutral mucus that buffers refluxed acid (lecture SDL 2). COMLEX: when reflux overwhelms this protection, GERD can drive Barrett metaplasia (lecture SDL 2).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 5 — cardiac-gland mucus in the reflux barrier","isHighYield":false},{"id":"trial_sdl02_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Correlate epithelial types and mucosal specializations with protection, absorption, and secretion in the GI tract.","batch":3,"stem":"A 4-year-old boy has his gallbladder removed for pigment stones from a hemolytic anemia. Which epithelium lines the mucosa of the resected organ?","choices":{"A":"Simple columnar with microvilli","B":"Simple cuboidal epithelium","C":"Stratified squamous epithelium","D":"Pseudostratified ciliated epithelium","E":"Transitional epithelium"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Simple columnar epithelium with microvilli lines the small and large intestines and the gallbladder. Simple cuboidal epithelium is not the gallbladder lining. Stratified squamous epithelium lines the oral cavity, esophagus, and lower anal canal. Pseudostratified ciliated epithelium lines the airway. Transitional epithelium lines the urinary tract. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 2, absorption: simple columnar epithelium with microvilli). The airway and urinary linings are not in the lecture.","boardPrep":"Step 1: simple columnar cells of the large intestine mainly resorb water and electrolytes (lecture SDL 2). COMLEX: gallbladder epithelium concentrates bile (lecture SDL 13).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 1 — gallbladder wall histology","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":3,"title":"SDL 3 — Histology of the Foregut part 2","questions":[{"id":"sdl03_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the cardiac, fundic, and pyloric regions of the stomach based on their characteristic gastric pits, glands, and predominant cell types.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Pawlina, in which region of the stomach do the gastric pits extend through about half the thickness of the mucosa?","choices":{"A":"Cardiac region","B":"Fundic region","C":"Esophagogastric junction","D":"Pyloric region","E":"Duodenal bulb"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The pyloric region has deep gastric pits that occupy about half of the mucosal thickness, opening onto branched, coiled pyloric glands with wide lumina. The cardiac region has relatively shallow pits over tubular mucous glands. The fundic region has shallow pits, about one-fourth to one-third of the mucosal thickness, with long fundic glands below. The esophagogastric junction is lined by squamous epithelium on its esophageal side and lacks gastric pits there. The duodenal bulb has villi and crypts rather than gastric pits. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 1, pyloric region); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (pyloric glands empty into deep gastric pits that occupy about half the thickness of the mucosa).","boardPrep":"Step 1: G cells in the antrum and duodenum secrete gastrin (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: pit depth and gland shape identify the gastric region on a slide.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 4 — gastrin, histamine, and somatostatin regulation of acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the cardiac, fundic, and pyloric regions of the stomach based on their characteristic gastric pits, glands, and predominant cell types.","batch":1,"stem":"A histology student examines glands that surround the esophageal orifice of the stomach. Which cell type predominates in these glands?","choices":{"A":"Mucous gland cells","B":"Zymogenic chief cells","C":"Parietal cells","D":"Enteroendocrine G cells","E":"Paneth cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Glands around the esophageal orifice are cardiac glands, tubular and somewhat tortuous glands made up mostly of mucus-secreting cells with flattened basal nuclei and pale apical cytoplasm. Chief cells predominate in the base of fundic glands. Parietal cells are most numerous in the neck of fundic glands and appear only occasionally at the cardiac boundary. G cells are concentrated in the pyloric glands. Paneth cells sit at the base of intestinal crypts. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 1, cardiac region); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (cardiac glands are composed of mucus-secreting cells).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Barrett metaplasia arises in the distal esophagus just above the gastroesophageal junction (First Aid 2025 GI Barrett esophagus). COMLEX: mucous cardiac glands resemble the esophageal cardiac glands.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 4 — gastrin, histamine, and somatostatin regulation of acid secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"G cells are concentrated in th"}},{"id":"sdl03_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the cardiac, fundic, and pyloric regions of the stomach based on their characteristic gastric pits, glands, and predominant cell types.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Pawlina, which cell type predominates in the deep base of the fundic glands?","choices":{"A":"Parietal cells","B":"Mucous neck cells","C":"Chief cells","D":"Surface mucous cells","E":"Stem cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Chief cells are concentrated in the base of fundic glands. Parietal cells are most numerous in the neck and upper part of the gland. Mucous neck cells are localized to the neck, among the parietal cells. Surface mucous cells line the surface and the gastric pits. Stem cells lie in the isthmus, between the pit and the gland. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 1, fundic region); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (fundic glands).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chief cells secrete pepsinogen, which is activated to pepsin by acid (First Aid 2025 GI GI secretory products). COMLEX: chief cells are basophilic on H&E.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 4 — gastrin, histamine, and somatostatin regulation of acid secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Parietal cells are most numero"}},{"id":"sdl03_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the major gastric epithelial cell types (surface mucous, mucous neck, parietal, chief, and enteroendocrine cells) and correlate their location and histologic appearance with their secretory products and functions.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Pawlina, which gastric epithelial cell secretes intrinsic factor?","choices":{"A":"Chief cells","B":"Mucous neck cells","C":"Surface mucous cells","D":"ECL cells","E":"Parietal cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Parietal (oxyntic) cells secrete hydrochloric acid and intrinsic factor, a 44-kDa glycoprotein that binds vitamin B12 for absorption in the distal ileum. Chief cells secrete pepsinogen and gastric lipase. Mucous neck cells secrete a soluble mucus that lubricates the gland lumen. Surface mucous cells secrete the insoluble alkaline mucus coat. ECL cells release histamine that stimulates parietal cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 2, parietal cells); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (intrinsic factor is a 44-kDa glycoprotein).","boardPrep":"Step 1: parietal cells make gastric acid and intrinsic factor; autoimmune destruction of parietal cells causes pernicious anemia (First Aid 2025 GI GI secretory products). COMLEX: B12 absorption requires intrinsic factor and an intact terminal ileum.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — parietal cell HCl secretion through the H+/K+-ATPase","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"ECL cells release histamine th"}},{"id":"sdl03_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the major gastric epithelial cell types (surface mucous, mucous neck, parietal, chief, and enteroendocrine cells) and correlate their location and histologic appearance with their secretory products and functions.","batch":1,"stem":"In H&E sections of fundic glands, chief cells show strongly basophilic basal cytoplasm. Which cellular feature produces this staining?","choices":{"A":"Abundant rough ER","B":"Intracellular canaliculi","C":"Apical mucin granules","D":"Abundant mitochondria","E":"Tubulovesicular membranes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Chief cells have the morphology of protein-secreting cells: a basal nucleus, basal cytoplasm packed with rough endoplasmic reticulum whose ribosomal RNA stains basophilic, and apical zymogen granules. Abundant mitochondria, intracellular canaliculi, and a tubulovesicular membrane system are features of parietal cells and produce their eosinophilic staining. Apical mucin granules belong to surface mucous cells and appear pale on H&E. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 2, chief cells); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (chief cells).","boardPrep":"Step 1: parietal cells are eosinophilic (pink) and chief cells are basophilic (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract histology). COMLEX: staining pattern reflects the dominant organelle.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — parietal cell HCl secretion through the H+/K+-ATPase","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the major gastric epithelial cell types (surface mucous, mucous neck, parietal, chief, and enteroendocrine cells) and correlate their location and histologic appearance with their secretory products and functions.","batch":1,"stem":"Chief cells secrete pepsinogen as an inactive proenzyme. Which condition in the gastric lumen converts it to active pepsin?","choices":{"A":"Trypsin cleavage","B":"Enterokinase action","C":"Acidic pH below 5","D":"Bile salt binding","E":"Alkaline pH above 8"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Pepsinogen undergoes autocatalytic cleavage to pepsin on contact with acidic gastric juice at a pH below 5, with an optimum around pH 1.8 to 3.5; pepsin is an endopeptidase that starts protein digestion. Trypsin cleaves pancreatic zymogens in the duodenum, and enterokinase activates trypsinogen to trypsin. Bile salts emulsify fat rather than activate proteases. An alkaline pH inactivates pepsin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 2, chief cells); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (pepsinogen and pepsin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pepsinogen is converted to pepsin in the presence of H+ (First Aid 2025 GI GI secretory products). COMLEX: acid suppression reduces gastric pepsin activity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — parietal cell HCl secretion through the H+/K+-ATPase","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate loss or dysfunction of specific gastric cell types to clinically important consequences, including impaired vitamin B12 absorption and protein digestion.","batch":1,"stem":"In autoimmune atrophic gastritis, what is the principal reason that even crystalline oral vitamin B12 is malabsorbed?","choices":{"A":"Loss of gastric acid","B":"Loss of intrinsic factor","C":"Loss of bile secretion","D":"Loss of ileal receptors","E":"Loss of pancreatic proteases"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Parietal cells are the gastric source of intrinsic factor. When autoimmune destruction removes them, B12 does not form the B12 and intrinsic factor complex needed for receptor-mediated uptake in the distal ileum, which produces pernicious anemia. Crystalline B12 is not bound to food protein, so loss of gastric acid, which with pepsin frees dietary B12, does not explain its malabsorption. Bile secretion is unaffected by gastritis and does not carry B12 to the ileum. The ileal receptors are intact in this disease. Pancreatic proteases are not affected by gastritis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 3, parietal cell loss and vitamin B12 malabsorption); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (pernicious anemia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: autoimmune gastritis has antibodies to the parietal cell H+/K+-ATPase and to intrinsic factor, affects the body and fundus, and raises the risk of pernicious anemia (First Aid 2025 GI chronic gastritis). COMLEX: macrocytic anemia with neurologic signs suggests B12 deficiency.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 1 — autoimmune gastritis and pernicious anemia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate loss or dysfunction of specific gastric cell types to clinically important consequences, including impaired vitamin B12 absorption and protein digestion.","batch":1,"stem":"Autoimmune atrophic gastritis causes achlorhydria and markedly elevated serum gastrin. What causes the gastrin elevation?","choices":{"A":"Direct antibody stimulation of G cells","B":"Increased vagal drive to G cells","C":"Reduced renal gastrin clearance","D":"Gastrin release by ECL cells","E":"Loss of acid feedback on G cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Luminal acid normally provides negative feedback on antral G cells. With parietal cells destroyed, the antral lumen is no longer acidic, the feedback is lost, and G cells secrete gastrin continuously, which leads to G cell and ECL cell hyperplasia. The autoantibodies target parietal cell H+/K+-ATPase and intrinsic factor, not G cells. Vagal drive is not the mechanism of the elevation. Renal clearance is not reduced in this disease. ECL cells release histamine, not gastrin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 3, achlorhydria and hypergastrinemia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic atrophic gastritis causes hypochlorhydria and hypergastrinemia, with risk of gastric cancer (First Aid 2025 GI chronic gastritis). COMLEX: hypergastrinemia with achlorhydria points away from gastrinoma.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 1 — autoimmune gastritis and pernicious anemia","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The autoantibodies target pari"}},{"id":"sdl03_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate loss or dysfunction of specific gastric cell types to clinically important consequences, including impaired vitamin B12 absorption and protein digestion.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with chronic atrophic gastritis has lost gastric pepsin activity, yet protein digestion and absorption remain adequate. What explains this preserved digestion?","choices":{"A":"Pancreatic proteases","B":"Salivary proteases","C":"Gastric lipase activity","D":"Colonic bacterial enzymes","E":"Intestinal pepsin secretion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Pancreatic proteases, including trypsin, chymotrypsin, elastase, and carboxypeptidases, work at neutral to alkaline pH in the duodenum and hydrolyze dietary protein into absorbable peptides and amino acids, so loss of gastric pepsin is compensated. Saliva contains amylase and lingual lipase rather than significant proteases. Gastric lipase digests fat. Colonic bacteria act after the main site of protein absorption. The intestine does not secrete pepsin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 3, chief cell dysfunction and protein digestion).","boardPrep":"Step 1: trypsinogen is activated by enterokinase and then activates the other pancreatic zymogens (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatic secretions). COMLEX: protein malabsorption suggests pancreatic insufficiency rather than gastric loss.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 1 — autoimmune gastritis and pernicious anemia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the histologic components of the gastric mucosal barrier and explain how disruption of this barrier contributes to gastric mucosal injury and ulceration.","batch":1,"stem":"The unstirred layer beneath the gastric mucus gel stays near pH 7 while the lumen is near pH 1. Which component chiefly creates this alkaline microenvironment?","choices":{"A":"Tight junction sealing of cells","B":"Prostaglandin vasodilation","C":"Epithelial bicarbonate secretion","D":"Pepsin inactivation by the mucus gel","E":"Rapid epithelial restitution"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Surface epithelial cells secrete bicarbonate into the fluid trapped beneath the mucus gel, creating an unstirred alkaline layer with a surface pH near 7 that neutralizes back-diffusing hydrogen ions and inactivates pepsin. Tight junctions prevent back-diffusion into the lamina propria but do not alkalinize the surface. Prostaglandins stimulate mucus and bicarbonate and maintain blood flow, but the alkaline layer itself is made by bicarbonate. Pepsin inactivation by the mucus gel is a result of the alkaline layer, not its cause. Restitution repairs defects. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 4, epithelial bicarbonate secretion).","boardPrep":"Step 1: PGE2 increases mucus and bicarbonate secretion and decreases acid; NSAIDs remove this protection (First Aid 2025 GI acute gastritis). COMLEX: misoprostol restores prostaglandin protection.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 2 — H. pylori and NSAIDs in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Pepsin inactivation by the muc"}},{"id":"sdl03_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the histologic components of the gastric mucosal barrier and explain how disruption of this barrier contributes to gastric mucosal injury and ulceration.","batch":1,"stem":"Nonselective NSAIDs injure the gastric mucosa mainly by inhibiting cyclooxygenase. Which change follows?","choices":{"A":"Increased gastrin release","B":"Reduced PGE2 synthesis","C":"Urease ammonia production","D":"Blocked H2 receptors","E":"Direct vagal stimulation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cyclooxygenase inhibition suppresses PGE2 synthesis, which decreases bicarbonate and mucus secretion, reduces mucosal blood flow, and impairs restitution. Increased gastrin release follows loss of acid feedback, not COX inhibition. Urease ammonia production is a mechanism of H. pylori. Blocked H2 receptors would reduce acid and protect the mucosa. Direct vagal stimulation of acid occurs with brain injury in Cushing ulcer. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 4, NSAIDs and aspirin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: NSAIDs reduce PGE2, decreasing gastric mucosal protection; Curling ulcer follows burns and Cushing ulcer follows brain injury (First Aid 2025 GI acute gastritis). COMLEX: co-prescribe misoprostol or a PPI for high-risk NSAID users.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 2 — H. pylori and NSAIDs in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the histologic components of the gastric mucosal barrier and explain how disruption of this barrier contributes to gastric mucosal injury and ulceration.","batch":1,"stem":"Within minutes of a superficial gastric epithelial injury, the defect is sealed without new cell division. Which process accomplishes this?","choices":{"A":"Migration of neck cells","B":"Rapid stem cell mitosis","C":"Fibroblast scar formation","D":"Mucus plug secretion","E":"Platelet clot formation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Restitution seals small epithelial defects as gland neck cells migrate along the basal lamina to restore continuity, without requiring immediate mitosis. Stem cell mitosis renews surface cells over days, not minutes. Fibroblast scarring follows deeper injury such as ulcers. Mucus slows pepsin and abrasion but does not close the epithelial gap. Platelet clotting stops bleeding but does not restore epithelium. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 4, epithelial restitution and continuous cell renewal).","boardPrep":"Step 1: erosions are limited to the mucosa and heal without scarring; ulcers extend deeper (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract anatomy). COMLEX: rapid restitution limits superficial injury.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 2 — H. pylori and NSAIDs in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the normal histologic features of the stomach from those of the esophagus, including epithelial type, mucosal organization, glands, and muscularis externa.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Pawlina, the nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium of the esophagus changes abruptly at the esophagogastric junction to which epithelium?","choices":{"A":"Stratified columnar","B":"Pseudostratified columnar","C":"Simple cuboidal","D":"Simple columnar","E":"Transitional"},"correct":"D","explanation":"At the esophagogastric junction the stratified squamous lining changes abruptly to the simple columnar epithelium of the stomach, made up of surface mucous cells. Stratified columnar epithelium lines some large ducts. Pseudostratified columnar epithelium lines the airway. Simple cuboidal epithelium lines small ducts and the thyroid follicles. Transitional epithelium lines the urinary tract. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 5, lining epithelium); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (esophagogastric junction).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Barrett esophagus replaces squamous epithelium with intestinal columnar epithelium and goblet cells (First Aid 2025 GI Barrett esophagus). COMLEX: the squamocolumnar junction is the endoscopic landmark for metaplasia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 3 — Barrett metaplasia at the esophagogastric junction","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Stratified columnar epithelium"}},{"id":"sdl03_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the normal histologic features of the stomach from those of the esophagus, including epithelial type, mucosal organization, glands, and muscularis externa.","batch":1,"stem":"The muscularis externa of the stomach differs from that of the esophagus by an additional layer. Which layer is this?","choices":{"A":"Outer circular layer","B":"Inner oblique layer","C":"Striated upper layer","D":"Middle longitudinal layer","E":"Submucosal muscle layer"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The stomach has three smooth muscle layers: an innermost oblique layer deep to the circular layer, an inner circular layer, and an outer longitudinal layer. The esophagus has two layers, inner circular and outer longitudinal. The outer layer of both organs is longitudinal, not circular. The striated muscle of the upper esophagus is absent from the stomach. There is no middle longitudinal layer or submucosal muscle layer. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 5, muscularis externa); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (stomach muscularis externa).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the stomach adds an innermost oblique layer deep to the inner circular and outer longitudinal layers (Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 3, section 5; Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: oblique fibers aid churning of chyme.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 4 — muscle type transitions along the esophagus","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The outer layer of both organs"}},{"id":"sdl03_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the normal histologic features of the stomach from those of the esophagus, including epithelial type, mucosal organization, glands, and muscularis externa.","batch":1,"stem":"Unlike the esophagus, the stomach has no submucosal glands. In which layer do all gastric glands lie?","choices":{"A":"Submucosa","B":"Muscularis mucosae","C":"Adventitia","D":"Muscularis externa","E":"Lamina propria"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Gastric glands are mucosal, confined to the lamina propria and opening into the bases of gastric pits; they include cardiac, fundic, and pyloric glands. The submucosa holds esophageal glands proper and duodenal Brunner glands, not gastric glands. The muscularis mucosae lies beneath the gland bases. The adventitia and muscularis externa are outer layers without glands. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 5, glands); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (gastric mucosa).","boardPrep":"Step 1: submucosal glands identify the esophagus and the duodenum (Brunner glands) on a slide (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract histology). COMLEX: gland layer separates esophageal from gastric biopsies.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 5 — esophageal glands proper and cardiac glands","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The submucosa holds esophageal"}},{"id":"sdl03_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the cardiac, fundic, and pyloric regions of the stomach based on their characteristic gastric pits, glands, and predominant cell types.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with dyspepsia has an endoscopic biopsy labeled gastric body. The section shows deep pits occupying half of the mucosa, coiled glands with wide lumina lined by mucous cells, and many gastrin-producing cells. Where was the biopsy most likely taken?","choices":{"A":"Gastric fundus","B":"Gastric body","C":"Cardiac region","D":"Pyloric antrum","E":"Duodenal bulb"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Deep pits occupying about half of the mucosa, branched coiled glands with wide lumina, mucous lining cells, and abundant G cells define the pyloric region in the antrum. The fundus and body share fundic glands with shallow pits and many parietal and chief cells. The cardiac region has shallow pits over tubular mucous glands without concentrated G cells. The duodenal bulb has villi and submucosal Brunner glands. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 1, pyloric region); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (pyloric glands).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pyloric glands are lined mainly by mucus secreting cells (lecture SDL 3). COMLEX: H. pylori gastritis affects the antrum first.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — source and release triggers of gastrin","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The fundus and body share fund"}},{"id":"sdl03_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the cardiac, fundic, and pyloric regions of the stomach based on their characteristic gastric pits, glands, and predominant cell types.","batch":2,"stem":"A 33-year-old woman with vitiligo has fatigue and macrocytic anemia. Serum antibodies against the parietal cell proton pump are present. Which gastric region is the main target of this destruction?","choices":{"A":"Fundus and body","B":"Antrum and pylorus","C":"Cardia and esophagus","D":"Duodenal bulb","E":"Lesser curvature"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Parietal cells are concentrated in the fundic glands of the fundus and body, so autoimmune gastritis targets these regions. The antrum and pylorus contain pyloric glands with few parietal cells and are spared; H. pylori gastritis begins there instead. The cardia has mucous cardiac glands, and the esophagus has squamous mucosa. The duodenal bulb is intestinal mucosa. The lesser curvature is an anatomic margin, not a gland region. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 1, fundic region, and section 3, parietal cell loss); First Aid 2025 GI (autoimmune gastritis affects the body and fundus).","boardPrep":"Step 1: autoimmune gastritis affects the body and fundus; H. pylori affects the antrum first and spreads to the body (First Aid 2025 GI chronic gastritis). COMLEX: autoimmune disease clusters, including vitiligo and thyroiditis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — autoimmune gastritis anatomic distribution","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the cardiac, fundic, and pyloric regions of the stomach based on their characteristic gastric pits, glands, and predominant cell types.","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old man undergoes antrectomy for a bleeding ulcer. Afterward his serum gastrin falls well below his preoperative value. Removal of which cells explains this change?","choices":{"A":"Fundic ECL cells","B":"Pyloric G cells","C":"Cardiac mucous cells","D":"Fundic chief cells","E":"Duodenal S cells"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Gastrin-producing G cells are concentrated in the pyloric glands of the antrum, so antrectomy removes the main source of gastrin. Fundic ECL cells release histamine and remain after antrectomy. Cardiac mucous cells secrete mucus. Fundic chief cells secrete pepsinogen. Duodenal S cells secrete secretin, not gastrin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 1, pyloric region, and section 2, enteroendocrine cells); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (pyloric glands).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastrin is released by G cells in the antrum and duodenum in response to peptides, amino acids, and gastric distension (First Aid 2025 GI GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: antrectomy reduces gastrin-driven acid.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — gastrin regulation of acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the major gastric epithelial cell types (surface mucous, mucous neck, parietal, chief, and enteroendocrine cells) and correlate their location and histologic appearance with their secretory products and functions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 59-year-old woman has a gastric biopsy. In the neck of the fundic glands are large triangular cells with central nuclei and intensely eosinophilic cytoplasm full of mitochondria and canaliculi. What do these cells secrete into the lumen?","choices":{"A":"Pepsinogen and lipase","B":"Soluble neck mucus","C":"Histamine","D":"Somatostatin","E":"Hydrochloric acid"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Large triangular eosinophilic cells with central nuclei, abundant mitochondria, and intracellular canaliculi in the gland neck are parietal cells. Their apical H+/K+-ATPase secretes hydrochloric acid, and they also secrete intrinsic factor. Pepsinogen and lipase come from basophilic chief cells in the gland base. Soluble neck mucus comes from mucous neck cells. Histamine is a paracrine product of ECL cells, and somatostatin of D cells, released toward the lamina propria rather than the lumen. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 2, parietal cells); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (parietal cells).","boardPrep":"Step 1: parietal cells secrete gastric acid, stimulated by histamine, ACh, and gastrin and inhibited by somatostatin (First Aid 2025 GI GI secretory products). COMLEX: PPIs block the H+/K+-ATPase.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 3 — cellular mechanism of HCl secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the major gastric epithelial cell types (surface mucous, mucous neck, parietal, chief, and enteroendocrine cells) and correlate their location and histologic appearance with their secretory products and functions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old healthy volunteer in a physiology study receives a gastrin infusion, which raises gastric acid output. Pretreatment with an H2 receptor blocker abolishes most of this rise. Gastrin acts mainly through which paracrine product?","choices":{"A":"D cell somatostatin","B":"Chief cell pepsinogen","C":"Mucous neck cell mucus","D":"ECL cell histamine","E":"Parietal cell intrinsic factor"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Gastrin raises acid mainly by stimulating enterochromaffin-like (ECL) cells in the fundic glands to release histamine, which acts on parietal cell H2 receptors; that is why H2 blockade abolishes most of the response. D cell somatostatin inhibits acid and gastrin release. Chief cell pepsinogen is a digestive proenzyme, not a paracrine signal. Mucous neck cell mucus lubricates the gland. Parietal cell intrinsic factor binds B12. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 2, enteroendocrine cells); First Aid 2025 GI (gastrin acts mainly through ECL cells).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastrin increases acid secretion primarily through its effects on ECL cells, leading to histamine release, rather than through its direct effect on parietal cells (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: H2 blockers reduce gastrin-driven acid.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 5 — histamine and somatostatin control of acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the major gastric epithelial cell types (surface mucous, mucous neck, parietal, chief, and enteroendocrine cells) and correlate their location and histologic appearance with their secretory products and functions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 65-year-old woman has a gastric biopsy stained with PAS. Tall columnar cells lining the surface and pits have large apical cups that stain intensely. Their secretion contributes which component of gastric defense?","choices":{"A":"Soluble lubricating gland mucus","B":"Acid into the gland lumen","C":"Insoluble alkaline gel coat","D":"Pepsinogen for protein digestion","E":"Intrinsic factor for B12"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Tall columnar cells with PAS-positive apical mucous cups lining the surface and pits are surface mucous cells. They secrete an insoluble visible mucus rich in bicarbonate and potassium that forms a protective alkaline gel. Soluble lubricating mucus in the gland lumen comes from mucous neck cells, which lack prominent apical cups. Acid and intrinsic factor come from parietal cells. Pepsinogen comes from chief cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 2, surface mucous cells and mucous neck cells); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (surface mucous cells).","boardPrep":"Step 1: prostaglandins increase gastric mucus and bicarbonate secretion (First Aid 2025 GI acute gastritis). COMLEX: the mucus layer limits pepsin contact with the epithelium.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — mechanisms that protect the gastric mucosa","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate loss or dysfunction of specific gastric cell types to clinically important consequences, including impaired vitamin B12 absorption and protein digestion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man had a total gastrectomy for cancer 6 years ago and takes no supplements. He now has numbness in both feet and decreased vibration sense, hemoglobin 9.8 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL), and mean corpuscular volume 112 fL (reference range: 80–100 fL). Which explains his findings?","choices":{"A":"Absent gastric pepsin","B":"Ileal bacterial overgrowth","C":"Reduced folate absorption","D":"Pancreatic protease loss","E":"Absent intrinsic factor"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Total gastrectomy removes all parietal cells, the gastric source of intrinsic factor. Without it, B12 is not absorbed in the distal ileum, and liver stores are depleted over several years, producing megaloblastic anemia and posterior and lateral column demyelination. Absent pepsin reduces release of B12 from food but is not the main cause, because intrinsic factor is also gone and supplements are needed even in crystalline form. Ileal bacterial overgrowth can follow gastric surgery but is not the main cause of this delayed, stores-depleted pattern. Folate deficiency causes macrocytosis without neurologic signs. Pancreatic proteases are preserved. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 3, parietal cell loss and vitamin B12 malabsorption).","boardPrep":"Step 1: B12 absorption requires intrinsic factor; deficiency causes megaloblastic anemia with subacute combined degeneration (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: give parenteral B12 after total gastrectomy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — pernicious anemia from loss of parietal cells","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Absent pepsin reduces release "}},{"id":"sdl03_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate loss or dysfunction of specific gastric cell types to clinically important consequences, including impaired vitamin B12 absorption and protein digestion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 42-year-old woman with antibodies to intrinsic factor has paresthesias, ataxia, and loss of position sense in both feet. Her vitamin B12 deficiency has gone untreated. Demyelination in which spinal cord region explains her neurologic signs?","choices":{"A":"Posterior and lateral columns","B":"Anterior horn motor neurons","C":"Anterior spinothalamic tracts","D":"Cerebellar vermis and flocculus","E":"Ventral nerve roots"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Severe B12 deficiency causes demyelination of the posterior and lateral columns of the spinal cord, producing sensory loss, ataxia, and weakness. Loss of position sense reflects the posterior columns, and the lateral columns carry the corticospinal tracts. Anterior horn neurons are lost in motor neuron diseases. Anterior spinothalamic tracts carry crude touch and are not the classic target. The cerebellar vermis and flocculus are brain structures, not spinal cord regions. Ventral roots carry motor fibers and are not the primary site. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 3, hematologic and neurologic impairment).","boardPrep":"Step 1: B12 deficiency causes subacute combined degeneration (Bootcamp Gastroenterology), with demyelination of the posterior and lateral columns of the spinal cord (Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 3, section 3). COMLEX: neurologic damage may persist after treatment.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — complications of autoimmune gastritis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate loss or dysfunction of specific gastric cell types to clinically important consequences, including impaired vitamin B12 absorption and protein digestion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with long-standing autoimmune atrophic gastritis has a serum gastrin of 1,200 pg/mL (reference range: 0–100 pg/mL). Endoscopy shows small nodules in the gastric body, and biopsy shows proliferating small uniform cells with neuroendocrine granules. Which cell type is most likely proliferating?","choices":{"A":"G cells","B":"ECL cells","C":"D cells","D":"EC cells","E":"Ghrelin cells"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Loss of acid removes negative feedback on antral G cells, and the resulting hypergastrinemia drives hyperplasia of the gastrin-responsive ECL cells in the body, which can form small nodules. G cells also become hyperplastic, but they lie in the antrum, not in body nodules. D cells secrete somatostatin, which inhibits gastrin and acid, and are not driven by gastrin. EC cells release serotonin and are not the gastrin-responsive population named in the lecture. Ghrelin cells of the fundus modulate appetite and are not the cells that gastrin drives to proliferate. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 2, enteroendocrine cells, and section 3, achlorhydria and hypergastrinemia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic atrophic gastritis leads to hypochlorhydria and hypergastrinemia (First Aid 2025 GI chronic gastritis). COMLEX: hypergastrinemia from achlorhydria is distinguished from gastrinoma by gastric pH.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 5 — gastric neuroendocrine tumors","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"G cells also become hyperplast","C":"D cells secrete somatostatin, "}},{"id":"sdl03_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the histologic components of the gastric mucosal barrier and explain how disruption of this barrier contributes to gastric mucosal injury and ulceration.","batch":2,"stem":"A 37-year-old man with epigastric pain has a positive stool antigen test. Biopsy shows curved rods within the mucus over the surface foveolar cells. Which bacterial mechanism lets the organism survive at this acidic site?","choices":{"A":"Bicarbonate secretion","B":"Proton pump inhibition","C":"Gastrin receptor blockade","D":"Capsule blocking acid entry","E":"Urease generating ammonia"},"correct":"E","explanation":"H. pylori uses urease to convert urea into an ammonia cloud that buffers local acid, the basis of the urea breath test, and uses flagella to move through mucus. Bicarbonate is secreted by the host epithelium, not the bacterium. The organism does not inhibit the proton pump. The organism does not block gastrin receptors. A capsule is not its mechanism of acid survival. Its cytotoxins, such as CagA, then damage tight junctions and the mucus gel. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 4, Helicobacter pylori infection).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H. pylori is the most common cause of chronic gastritis, increases risk of peptic ulcer disease and MALT lymphoma, and affects the antrum first (First Aid 2025 GI chronic gastritis). COMLEX: urea breath test and stool antigen confirm infection.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — H. pylori in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"A capsule is not its mechanism"}},{"id":"sdl03_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the histologic components of the gastric mucosal barrier and explain how disruption of this barrier contributes to gastric mucosal injury and ulceration.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man with burns over 40% of his body surface area develops upper GI bleeding on day 3. Endoscopy shows multiple superficial gastric erosions. Failure of which barrier component is most responsible?","choices":{"A":"Tight junction proteins","B":"Surface cell turnover","C":"Mucosal blood flow","D":"Mucus gel thickness","E":"Parietal cell number"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Severe burns cause hypovolemia and mucosal ischemia. Reduced blood flow lowers oxygen and bicarbonate delivery and fails to wash away back-diffused hydrogen ions, leading to local acidosis, cell death, and erosions (Curling ulcer). Tight junctions are disrupted by H. pylori toxins and aspirin. Surface cell turnover and mucus thickness fall secondarily but are not the initiating failure. Parietal cell number is unchanged. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 4, ischemia, shock, alcohol, and stress); First Aid 2025 GI (Curling ulcer).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Curling ulcer follows burns from hypovolemia and mucosal ischemia; Cushing ulcer follows brain injury from vagal stimulation and increased acid (First Aid 2025 GI acute gastritis). COMLEX: give stress ulcer prophylaxis to critically ill patients.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — acute stress gastropathy","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the histologic components of the gastric mucosal barrier and explain how disruption of this barrier contributes to gastric mucosal injury and ulceration.","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman takes high-dose aspirin for headaches and develops gastric erosions. Apart from inhibiting cyclooxygenase, aspirin injures the gastric surface by which direct action?","choices":{"A":"Damages hydrophobic membranes","B":"Stimulates gastrin release","C":"Blocks bicarbonate channels","D":"Activates pepsinogen in cells","E":"Raises vagal acid output"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Aspirin directly damages the hydrophobic apical cell membranes, allowing acid to leak down its electrochemical gradient into the tissue, in addition to suppressing PGE2. It does not stimulate gastrin release. Its effect on bicarbonate comes through loss of prostaglandins, not channel blockade. Pepsinogen is activated in the lumen, not inside cells. Vagal acid output rises in Cushing ulcer after brain injury. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 4, NSAIDs and aspirin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: NSAIDs reduce PGE2 and gastric mucosal protection (First Aid 2025 GI acute gastritis). COMLEX: misoprostol prevents NSAID-induced ulcers.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — NSAID-associated ulcer prevention","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the normal histologic features of the stomach from those of the esophagus, including epithelial type, mucosal organization, glands, and muscularis externa.","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old woman has a biopsy labeled distal esophagus. It shows simple columnar surface cells, gastric pits, and glands packed with numerous parietal and chief cells in the lamina propria, with no glands in the submucosa. Where was the tissue taken?","choices":{"A":"Distal esophagus","B":"Gastric cardia","C":"Duodenal bulb","D":"Gastric body","E":"Pyloric antrum"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Simple columnar epithelium, gastric pits, and mucosal glands rich in parietal and chief cells, without submucosal glands, define fundic mucosa of the gastric body. The distal esophagus has stratified squamous epithelium and submucosal glands proper. The cardia has mucous glands with few parietal cells. The duodenal bulb has villi and Brunner glands in the submucosa. The pyloric antrum has deep pits over mucous glands with G cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 5, histologic comparison); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (fundic glands).","boardPrep":"Step 1: esophagus has nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium; stomach has gastric glands with parietal and chief cells (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract histology). COMLEX: a mislabeled specimen is identified by its epithelium and glands.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — epithelial types along the GI tract","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The distal esophagus has strat"}},{"id":"sdl03_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the normal histologic features of the stomach from those of the esophagus, including epithelial type, mucosal organization, glands, and muscularis externa.","batch":2,"stem":"A 39-year-old man has leg edema and an albumin of 2.4 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.0 g/dL). Endoscopy shows giant folds in the body of the stomach resembling brain gyri, and biopsy shows foveolar hyperplasia with loss of parietal cells. Which normal structure is enlarged?","choices":{"A":"Intestinal villi","B":"Gastric rugae","C":"Plicae circulares","D":"Colonic haustra","E":"Esophageal papillae"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Gastric rugae are longitudinal folds of mucosa and submucosa that flatten when the stomach fills; in Ménétrier disease they become hypertrophied, with excess mucus production and protein loss. Intestinal villi are mucosal projections of the small intestine. Plicae circulares are permanent transverse folds of the small intestine. Colonic haustra are sacculations of the colon. Esophageal papillae are connective tissue projections into the esophageal epithelium. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 5, mucosal surface organization); First Aid 2025 GI (Ménétrier disease).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ménétrier disease is gastric mucosal hyperplasia with hypertrophied rugae, excess mucus, protein loss, and parietal cell atrophy with reduced acid (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: protein-losing gastropathy presents with edema.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — hypertrophic gastropathy (Ménétrier disease)","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl03_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the normal histologic features of the stomach from those of the esophagus, including epithelial type, mucosal organization, glands, and muscularis externa.","batch":2,"stem":"A pathologist compares the luminal surfaces of the esophagus and stomach with a hand lens. Only the gastric surface is divided by shallow grooves into small, slightly bulging, cobblestone-like patches that contain mucosa alone. What are these patches called?","choices":{"A":"Gastric rugae","B":"Plicae circulares","C":"Mamillated areas","D":"Intestinal villi","E":"Haustral folds"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Shallow grooves divide the gastric surface into slightly bulging patches called mamillated areas, which consist of mucosa only and slightly increase the surface area for secretion. The esophagus has a smooth surface with temporary longitudinal folds and no such areas. Gastric rugae are larger longitudinal folds that include submucosa and flatten as the stomach fills. Plicae circulares are permanent folds of the small intestine. Intestinal villi are finger-like mucosal projections of the small bowel. Haustral folds belong to the colon. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 5, mucosal surface organization, p20); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (mamillated areas consist only of mucosa).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the stomach mucosa shows rugae, mamillated areas, and gastric pits, while the esophagus lacks gastric pits (lecture SDL 3). COMLEX: rugae flatten when the stomach is distended (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 1 — gastric pits and regional glands","isHighYield":false},{"id":"trial_sdl03_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the cardiac, fundic, and pyloric regions of the stomach based on their characteristic gastric pits, glands, and predominant cell types.","batch":3,"stem":"A 36-year-old man has an endoscopic biopsy taken just below the gastroesophageal junction. It shows shallow gastric pits over tubular, somewhat tortuous mucous glands with only occasional parietal cells. Which gastric region was sampled?","choices":{"A":"Fundic region","B":"Pyloric region","C":"Cardiac region","D":"Distal esophagus","E":"Duodenal bulb"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Shallow pits over tubular, tortuous glands composed mostly of mucous cells, with occasional parietal cells at the border, define the cardiac region around the esophageal orifice. The fundic region has shallow pits over long glands full of parietal and chief cells. The pyloric region has deep pits that occupy about half of the mucosa. The distal esophagus is lined by stratified squamous epithelium without gastric pits. The duodenal bulb has villi and Brunner glands. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 1, cardiac region); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (cardiac glands).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the cardia is the junction zone where Barrett metaplasia is assessed (First Aid 2025 GI Barrett esophagus). COMLEX: biopsy site matters when reporting intestinal metaplasia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 5 — esophageal cardiac glands","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl03_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the major gastric epithelial cell types (surface mucous, mucous neck, parietal, chief, and enteroendocrine cells) and correlate their location and histologic appearance with their secretory products and functions.","batch":3,"stem":"A 47-year-old man takes a drug that irreversibly blocks the apical H+/K+-ATPase of large cells in the neck of the fundic glands. Which feature identifies these cells on H&E staining?","choices":{"A":"Intensely basophilic basal cytoplasm","B":"Clear apical mucous granule cups","C":"Flattened nuclei at the cell base","D":"Apical zymogen granule clusters","E":"Intensely eosinophilic cytoplasm"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The drug targets parietal cells, large triangular cells with central nuclei and intensely eosinophilic cytoplasm packed with mitochondria and canaliculi. Basophilic basal cytoplasm and apical zymogen granules identify chief cells, whose apical cytoplasm is also eosinophilic but less intensely. Clear apical mucous cups identify surface mucous cells. Flattened basal nuclei are typical of mucous gland cells in cardiac glands. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 2, parietal cells); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (parietal cells).","boardPrep":"Step 1: parietal cells are eosinophilic and chief cells are basophilic (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract histology). COMLEX: PPIs heal 85 to 90 percent of erosive esophagitis (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — proton pump inhibitors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl03_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate loss or dysfunction of specific gastric cell types to clinically important consequences, including impaired vitamin B12 absorption and protein digestion.","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman has autoimmune gastritis and pernicious anemia. Her B12 absorption returns to normal when oral B12 is given together with intrinsic factor. Where is the B12 and intrinsic factor complex normally absorbed?","choices":{"A":"Distal ileum","B":"Duodenal bulb","C":"Proximal jejunum","D":"Gastric antrum","E":"Ascending colon"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Intrinsic factor from parietal cells binds B12, and the complex is taken up by receptor mediated endocytosis in the distal ileum, so an intact ileum explains why added intrinsic factor corrects absorption. The duodenal bulb and proximal jejunum absorb iron, folate, and most nutrients but not the B12 complex. The gastric antrum does not absorb B12. The ascending colon absorbs water and electrolytes. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 3, B12 and intrinsic factor uptake in the distal ileum, lines 596 and 632); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (pernicious anemia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: autoimmune gastritis targets parietal cells and intrinsic factor and causes pernicious anemia (First Aid 2025 GI chronic gastritis). COMLEX: treat with parenteral B12.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — autoimmune gastritis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl03_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the histologic components of the gastric mucosal barrier and explain how disruption of this barrier contributes to gastric mucosal injury and ulceration.","batch":3,"stem":"A 23-year-old woman develops hematemesis after a night of binge drinking. Endoscopy shows superficial gastric erosions. How does ethanol injure the gastric surface?","choices":{"A":"COX enzyme inhibition","B":"Urease ammonia production","C":"Vagal acid stimulation","D":"Direct cell toxicity","E":"Gastrin hypersecretion"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Alcohol injures the gastric surface by direct chemical cytotoxicity, leading to local mucosal acidosis and cell death. COX inhibition is the mechanism of NSAIDs. Urease ammonia production is how H. pylori buffers acid to survive, not how ethanol injures the mucosa. Vagal acid stimulation causes Cushing ulcer after brain injury. Gastrin hypersecretion occurs with a gastrinoma. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 4, ischemia, shock, alcohol, and stress).","boardPrep":"Step 1: acute gastritis risk factors include alcohol use disorder and daily NSAID use (First Aid 2025 GI acute gastritis). COMLEX: forceful retching can also cause Mallory-Weiss tears.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — acute gastropathy","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl03_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the normal histologic features of the stomach from those of the esophagus, including epithelial type, mucosal organization, glands, and muscularis externa.","batch":3,"stem":"A 56-year-old man has a resection specimen from the upper abdomen. The organ has simple columnar epithelium, three layers of smooth muscle in the muscularis externa, and a serosal covering. Which organ is this?","choices":{"A":"Esophagus","B":"Stomach","C":"Duodenum","D":"Jejunum","E":"Colon"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Simple columnar epithelium with an extra innermost oblique layer, giving three muscle layers, and a serosal coat is characteristic of the stomach. The esophagus has stratified squamous epithelium, two muscle layers, and mostly adventitia. The duodenum and jejunum have villi and two muscle layers. The colon has crypts and teniae coli in two muscle layers. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 5, histologic comparison); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (stomach muscularis externa).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the stomach has gastric glands with parietal and chief cells (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract histology). COMLEX: oblique fibers aid gastric churning.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 1 — layers of the alimentary canal","isHighYield":false},{"id":"trial_sdl03_q06","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the histologic components of the gastric mucosal barrier and explain how disruption of this barrier contributes to gastric mucosal injury and ulceration.","batch":3,"stem":"A 34-year-old man has dyspepsia, and gastric biopsy shows curved rods on the foveolar cells with chronic active inflammation. The strain carries the CagA gene. How does this toxin weaken the mucosal barrier?","choices":{"A":"Tight junction disruption","B":"COX enzyme inhibition","C":"Reduced mucosal blood flow","D":"Parietal cell autoimmunity","E":"Vagal overstimulation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"H. pylori cytotoxins such as CagA destroy tight junctions, disrupt the mucus gel, and trigger chronic active inflammation, letting acid and pepsin reach the epithelium. COX inhibition is the NSAID mechanism. Reduced mucosal blood flow is the mechanism of shock and burns. Parietal cell autoimmunity causes autoimmune gastritis. Vagal overstimulation causes Cushing ulcer. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 4, Helicobacter pylori infection).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H. pylori increases the risk of peptic ulcer disease and MALT lymphoma (First Aid 2025 GI chronic gastritis). COMLEX: treat with triple therapy and confirm eradication.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — H. pylori in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"grid_sdl03_q01","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate loss or dysfunction of specific gastric cell types to clinically important consequences, including impaired vitamin B12 absorption and protein digestion.","batch":4,"stem":"A 53-year-old woman with Hashimoto thyroiditis and vitiligo has fatigue, a sore smooth tongue, and numbness in both feet. Biopsy of the gastric body shows thin mucosa with few glands, while the antrum looks normal. Which of the following sets of laboratory findings is most likely in this patient?","grid":{"headers":["Serum gastrin","Gastric acid","Serum B12"]},"choices":{"A":["↑","↑","↔"],"B":["↑","↓","↓"],"C":["↔","↔","↔"],"D":["↓","↓","↓"],"E":["↔","↔","↓"]},"correct":"B","explanation":"Autoimmune atrophic gastritis destroys parietal cells in the body and fundus, so the pattern (↑ serum gastrin, ↓ gastric acid, ↓ serum B12) is correct. Acid falls, gastrin from the spared antrum rises, and serum B12 falls, which fits the glossitis and neuropathy. The pattern (↑ serum gastrin, ↑ gastric acid, ↔ serum B12) is a gastrinoma (Zollinger-Ellison syndrome), in which tumor gastrin drives intact parietal cells to secrete excess acid; intrinsic factor is still made, so B12 is typically normal, and although very low duodenal pH can inactivate the pancreatic proteases that cleave R-binder and free B12 to bind intrinsic factor (inferred: Costanzo describes low duodenal pH inactivating pancreatic lipase in Zollinger-Ellison syndrome and pancreatic proteases degrading R proteins), the high acid level excludes this row whatever the B12 value. The pattern (↔ serum gastrin, ↔ gastric acid, ↔ serum B12) is folate deficiency, which causes a macrocytic anemia almost identical to pernicious anemia but leaves gastric secretion and B12 unchanged and does not explain the numbness in her feet. The pattern (↓ serum gastrin, ↓ gastric acid, ↓ serum B12) follows total gastrectomy, which removes the antral G cells, so gastrin falls, and removes the parietal cells, so acid and intrinsic factor are lost and B12 is not absorbed; the low gastrin separates this row from the key. The pattern (↔ serum gastrin, ↔ gastric acid, ↓ serum B12) follows resection of the distal ileum, where the B12 and intrinsic factor complex is absorbed, with normal gastric function. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 3, parietal cell loss, achlorhydria and hypergastrinemia); Pawlina Ch. 17 (pernicious anemia); Guyton supplemental (folic acid deficiency causes a macrocytic anemia almost identical to pernicious anemia); First Aid 2025 GI (autoimmune gastritis; Zollinger-Ellison syndrome); Costanzo Ch. 8 (antral resection decreases gastrin and H+ secretion; gastrectomy removes the source of intrinsic factor, so B12 must be injected); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, total or partial gastrectomy causes B12 deficiency); the low gastrin in row D is inferred from removal of the antrum, and the B12 value in row A is inferred from preserved intrinsic factor.","boardPrep":"Step 1: autoimmune gastritis has antibodies to the parietal cell H+/K+-ATPase and intrinsic factor, affects the body and fundus, and causes hypochlorhydria, hypergastrinemia, and pernicious anemia (First Aid 2025 GI chronic gastritis). COMLEX: a high gastrin level with low acid points to atrophic gastritis or acid suppression rather than gastrinoma.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — regulation of gastric acid secretion","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":4,"title":"SDL 4 — Physiology - GI Innervation","questions":[{"id":"sdl04_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the layers of the GI tract wall and locate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses. (Fig. 8.1)","batch":1,"stem":"Within the muscularis externa, contraction of the inner circular layer has which effect on a gut segment?","choices":{"A":"Narrows the lumen","B":"Shortens the segment","C":"Folds the mucosa","D":"Widens the lumen","E":"Lengthens the segment"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Contraction of the inner circular layer decreases the luminal diameter. Contraction of the outer longitudinal layer shortens the segment. Folding of the mucosa is produced by the muscularis mucosae. Widening of the lumen follows relaxation of circular muscle ahead of a bolus, and lengthening follows relaxation of longitudinal muscle; neither is produced by circular contraction. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 1, inner circular muscle layer); Costanzo Ch. 8 (circular muscle decreases diameter, longitudinal muscle decreases length).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the myenteric (Auerbach) plexus between the two muscle layers controls motility (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract anatomy). COMLEX: circular and longitudinal layers are reciprocally innervated during peristalsis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 3 — submucosal versus myenteric plexus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the layers of the GI tract wall and locate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses. (Fig. 8.1)","batch":1,"stem":"Which layer of the gut wall alters the folding and surface area of the inner lining to aid local absorption and secretion?","choices":{"A":"Inner circular layer","B":"Outer longitudinal layer","C":"Muscularis mucosae","D":"Lamina propria","E":"Serosal layer"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The muscularis mucosae is a thin smooth muscle layer at the base of the mucosa whose local contractions change mucosal shape and surface area. The inner circular and outer longitudinal layers of the muscularis externa move the whole segment. The lamina propria is loose connective tissue with vessels and immune cells. The serosa is not the layer described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 1, muscularis mucosae).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gut wall layers are mucosa, submucosa, muscularis externa, and serosa or adventitia (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract anatomy). COMLEX: the submucosal plexus controls the muscularis mucosae.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 3 — submucosal versus myenteric plexus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the layers of the GI tract wall and locate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses. (Fig. 8.1)","batch":1,"stem":"During peristalsis, when the circular muscle of a segment contracts, what does the longitudinal muscle of that segment do?","choices":{"A":"Contracts","B":"Relaxes","C":"Is unchanged","D":"Fatigues","E":"Depolarizes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Circular and longitudinal muscle are reciprocally innervated, so when the circular muscle of a segment contracts, its longitudinal muscle relaxes, and the segment narrows and lengthens behind the bolus. Simultaneous contraction is what reciprocal innervation prevents. The longitudinal layer changes its tone rather than staying unchanged. Fatigue is not part of the reflex. Depolarization would lead to contraction, not relaxation. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (reciprocal innervation and peristalsis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: behind the bolus, circular muscle contracts; ahead of it, circular muscle relaxes (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the segment ahead of the bolus relaxes so the bolus can advance (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 3 — submucosal versus myenteric plexus","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Simultaneous contraction is wh","E":"Depolarization would lead to c"}},{"id":"sdl04_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic innervation of the GI tract, including ganglion location, fiber length, neurotransmitters, and effects on GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":1,"stem":"Preganglionic sympathetic fibers to the gastrointestinal tract synapse in which location?","choices":{"A":"Myenteric plexus","B":"Submucosal plexus","C":"Dorsal root ganglia","D":"Inferior vagal ganglion","E":"Prevertebral ganglia"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Sympathetic preganglionic fibers are short and synapse outside the gut in four prevertebral ganglia: celiac, superior mesenteric, inferior mesenteric, and hypogastric. The myenteric and submucosal plexuses are where parasympathetic preganglionic fibers synapse and where sympathetic postganglionic fibers end. Dorsal root ganglia are not sympathetic synapse sites. The inferior vagal ganglion is not a sympathetic synapse site. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 2, ganglion location); Costanzo Ch. 8 (sympathetic innervation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sympathetic splanchnic supply is T5 to T9 for the foregut, T10 to T12 for the midgut, and L1 to L2 for the hindgut (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, embryology). COMLEX: the lecture also lists paravertebral chain ganglia in the sympathetic pathway.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic control of salivary secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic innervation of the GI tract, including ganglion location, fiber length, neurotransmitters, and effects on GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":1,"stem":"Which description fits the parasympathetic preganglionic fibers that supply the gastrointestinal tract?","choices":{"A":"Short with prevertebral ganglia","B":"Short with intramural ganglia","C":"Long with chain ganglia","D":"Long with intramural ganglia","E":"Short with chain ganglia"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Parasympathetic preganglionic fibers are long, running from the brainstem or sacral cord to the gut, and synapse in ganglia within the wall, in the myenteric and submucosal plexuses, so the postganglionic fibers are short. Short preganglionic fibers synapsing in prevertebral ganglia describe the sympathetic supply. Short fibers ending in the wall describe no autonomic division. Chain ganglia are sympathetic paravertebral ganglia. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 2, fiber length); Costanzo Ch. 8 (parasympathetic innervation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: parasympathetic ganglia lie in or near the target organ; sympathetic ganglia lie outside it (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: vagal preganglionic fibers synapse in the myenteric and submucosal plexuses.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic control of salivary secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Short fibers ending in the wal","E":"Chain ganglia are sympathetic "}},{"id":"sdl04_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic innervation of the GI tract, including ganglion location, fiber length, neurotransmitters, and effects on GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":1,"stem":"Activation of sympathetic postganglionic fibers to the gut produces which effect?","choices":{"A":"Contraction of sphincters","B":"Relaxation of sphincters","C":"Increased gland secretion","D":"Increased wall motility","E":"Mucosal vasodilation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Sympathetic activity generally inhibits gut function: it relaxes the smooth muscle of the walls, contracts GI sphincters, decreases motility, inhibits secretion, and constricts GI vessels. Relaxation of sphincters, increased gland secretion, and increased wall motility are parasympathetic effects. Mucosal vasodilation accompanies parasympathetic stimulation during digestion, whereas sympathetic activity causes vasoconstriction. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 2, effects on GI function); Guyton Ch. 63 (sympathetic stimulation inhibits activity in the gut).","boardPrep":"Step 1: norepinephrine inhibits gut motility, mostly through inhibition of enteric neurons (Guyton Ch. 63). COMLEX: intense sympathetic activity can stop movement of food through the gut.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic control of salivary secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the distribution of parasympathetic innervation to the GI tract by the vagus and pelvic nerves. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":1,"stem":"The pelvic nerve supplies parasympathetic fibers to which part of the gastrointestinal tract?","choices":{"A":"Stomach and proximal duodenum","B":"Jejunum and terminal ileum","C":"Cecum and ascending colon","D":"Upper third of esophagus","E":"Descending and sigmoid colon"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The pelvic nerve, from sacral segments S2 to S4, supplies the lower GI tract: the distal transverse, descending, and sigmoid colon, rectum, and anal canal. The stomach, duodenum, jejunum, ileum, cecum, and ascending colon receive vagal parasympathetic fibers. The upper third of the esophagus is striated muscle supplied by vagal fibers from the nucleus ambiguus. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 3, pelvic nerve); Costanzo Ch. 8 (parasympathetic innervation; Costanzo lists the transverse, descending, and sigmoid colon, and the lecture adds the rectum and anal canal).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hindgut parasympathetic supply is from S2 to S4 splanchnic nerves (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, embryology). COMLEX: sacral parasympathetic fibers coordinate defecation reflexes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — vagal innervation of the rotated stomach","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the distribution of parasympathetic innervation to the GI tract by the vagus and pelvic nerves. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, besides coordinating defecation reflexes, which colonic function does parasympathetic outflow through the pelvic nerve stimulate?","choices":{"A":"Mucus secretion","B":"Water absorption","C":"Bile acid uptake","D":"Motility inhibition","E":"Sphincter contraction"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Pelvic nerve fibers from S2 to S4 are heavily concentrated in the sigmoid, rectal, and anal regions, where they coordinate defecation reflexes and stimulate colonic mucus secretion. Water absorption by the colonic epithelium is not the function the lecture assigns to pelvic outflow. Bile acid uptake occurs mainly in the terminal ileum, which is in vagal territory. Parasympathetic activity increases rather than inhibits colonic motility. Sphincter contraction is a sympathetic effect. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 3, pelvic nerve distribution and functions, lines 843–850; section 2, sympathetic contraction of sphincters); Costanzo Ch. 8 (parasympathetic innervation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp notes that parasympathetic activity increases peristalsis and secretions while lowering sphincter tone (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, stomach physiology). COMLEX: sacral cord lesions impair defecation reflexes and colonic mucus secretion (lecture SDL 4).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — vagal innervation of the rotated stomach","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the distribution of parasympathetic innervation to the GI tract by the vagus and pelvic nerves. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":1,"stem":"Which is the most distal part of the gastrointestinal tract that receives vagal parasympathetic fibers?","choices":{"A":"Distal transverse colon","B":"Proximal transverse colon","C":"Proximal descending colon","D":"Proximal sigmoid colon","E":"Rectosigmoid junction"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The vagus supplies the esophagus, stomach, pancreas, liver and gallbladder, small intestine, cecum, appendix, ascending colon, and proximal transverse colon. The distal transverse colon, descending colon, sigmoid colon, and rectosigmoid region are supplied by the pelvic nerve. This boundary matches the midgut and hindgut junction. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 3, vagus nerve distribution).","boardPrep":"Step 1: midgut parasympathetic supply is the vagus; hindgut supply is S2 to S4 (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, embryology). COMLEX: the distal third of the transverse colon is hindgut.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — vagal innervation of the rotated stomach","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The distal transverse colon, d"}},{"id":"sdl04_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the afferent and efferent pathways of a vagovagal reflex and their role in regulating GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":1,"stem":"About what proportion of the fibers in the vagus nerve are afferent?","choices":{"A":"About 25 percent","B":"About 50 percent","C":"About 10 percent","D":"About 75 percent","E":"About 90 percent"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Costanzo states that about 75 percent of vagal fibers are afferent and 25 percent efferent; the lecture gives 75 to 80 percent afferent. The 25 percent figure is the efferent fraction. About 50 percent does not match the vagal split. About 10 and 90 percent match no source. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (vagus is 75% afferent and 25% efferent); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 4, structural composition).","boardPrep":"Step 1: vagovagal reflexes carry both afferent and efferent limbs in the vagus (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: vagal afferents relay stretch and chemical signals from the gut.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 5 — phases of gastric acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the afferent and efferent pathways of a vagovagal reflex and their role in regulating GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":1,"stem":"In a vagovagal reflex, the vagal afferent fibers from the gut terminate in which brainstem nucleus?","choices":{"A":"Dorsal motor nucleus of vagus","B":"Nucleus ambiguus of medulla","C":"Nucleus tractus solitarius","D":"Spinal trigeminal nucleus","E":"Hypoglossal motor nucleus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Vagal afferents from the gut end in the nucleus tractus solitarius of the medulla. The dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus is the origin of the efferent limb. The nucleus ambiguus, spinal trigeminal nucleus, and hypoglossal nucleus are not the termination site of vagal afferents in the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 4, afferent and efferent pathways).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in vagovagal reflexes both limbs travel in the vagus (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: receptive relaxation and distension-driven acid secretion are vagovagal reflexes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 5 — phases of gastric acid secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"The nucleus ambiguus, spinal t"}},{"id":"sdl04_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the afferent and efferent pathways of a vagovagal reflex and their role in regulating GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":1,"stem":"Distension of the orad stomach triggers a vagovagal reflex that relaxes the gastric wall. Which transmitters do the postganglionic neurons release?","choices":{"A":"ACh and substance P","B":"VIP and nitric oxide","C":"Gastrin and CCK","D":"Secretin and somatostatin","E":"GRP and histamine"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Receptive relaxation is a vagovagal reflex in which peptidergic postganglionic neurons release VIP and nitric oxide, relaxing the orad stomach so it can hold up to 1.5 L without a rise in pressure. ACh and substance P are excitatory transmitters that contract smooth muscle. Gastrin and CCK are hormones, not reflex transmitters. Secretin and somatostatin are a hormone and a paracrine inhibitor. GRP stimulates G cells, and histamine is a paracrine stimulant of parietal cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 4, receptive relaxation); Costanzo Ch. 8 (receptive relaxation; the neurotransmitter is VIP).","boardPrep":"Step 1: vagotomy eliminates receptive relaxation (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: loss of accommodation causes early satiety.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 5 — phases of gastric acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain how the enteric nervous system independently regulates GI motility, secretion, and endocrine function and how extrinsic autonomic input modulates its activity. (Fig. 8.3)","batch":1,"stem":"In the peristaltic reflex, intrinsic primary afferent neurons in the gut wall receive chemical activation from which signal?","choices":{"A":"Norepinephrine from sympathetic fibers","B":"ACh from vagal efferent fibers","C":"Gastrin from antral G endocrine cells","D":"Histamine from mucosal mast cells","E":"Serotonin from enterochromaffin cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A bolus stimulates enterochromaffin cells in the mucosa to release serotonin, which binds intrinsic primary afferent neurons (IPANs) and starts the peristaltic reflex in that segment. Norepinephrine from sympathetic fibers suppresses enteric activity. ACh from vagal efferent fibers is extrinsic modulation, not the local trigger. Gastrin from antral G cells is a hormone acting on parietal cells. Histamine from mast cells is not the trigger described in the lecture. IPANs also respond directly to stretch. Histamine is secreted by endocrine-type cells of the gastric mucosa and acts with gastrin and ACh to stimulate parietal cell H+ secretion, not to activate IPANs in the peristaltic reflex. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 5, sensorimotor architecture); Costanzo Ch. 8 (5-HT binds IPANs to initiate the peristaltic reflex).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the enteric nervous system contains more than 100 million neurons (Guyton Ch. 63). COMLEX: local reflexes work without central processing.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 2 — enteric control of secondary peristalsis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Histamine is secreted by endocrine-type cells of the gastric"}},{"id":"sdl04_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain how the enteric nervous system independently regulates GI motility, secretion, and endocrine function and how extrinsic autonomic input modulates its activity. (Fig. 8.3)","batch":1,"stem":"During the peristaltic reflex, excitatory enteric neurons contract the circular muscle behind the bolus. Which transmitters do they release?","choices":{"A":"VIP and nitric oxide","B":"Norepinephrine and dopamine","C":"Secretin and CCK","D":"ACh and substance P","E":"Gastrin and histamine"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Excitatory enteric neurons release acetylcholine and substance P to contract muscle behind the bolus. VIP and nitric oxide are released by inhibitory neurons that relax muscle ahead of the bolus and relax sphincters. Norepinephrine is released by extrinsic sympathetic fibers and inhibits motility. Secretin and CCK are hormones. Gastrin and histamine regulate acid secretion. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 5, motility control via the myenteric plexus); Costanzo Ch. 8 (peristalsis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: acetylcholine most often excites GI activity, and norepinephrine usually inhibits it (Guyton Ch. 63). COMLEX: anticholinergic drugs slow gut motility.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 2 — enteric control of secondary peristalsis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain how the enteric nervous system independently regulates GI motility, secretion, and endocrine function and how extrinsic autonomic input modulates its activity. (Fig. 8.3)","batch":1,"stem":"Sympathetic postganglionic fibers suppress gut motility and secretion. Where do most of these fibers end?","choices":{"A":"On smooth muscle cells","B":"On mucosal epithelium","C":"On enteric neurons","D":"On vagal ganglia","E":"On dorsal root ganglia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Sympathetic postganglionic fibers end mainly on neurons of the enteric nervous system, where norepinephrine inhibits transmitter release; only a small share acts directly on smooth muscle and vessels. Smooth muscle cells receive a minor direct input. Mucosal epithelium is controlled mainly through enteric neurons. Vagal ganglia and dorsal root ganglia are not sympathetic targets. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 5, sympathetic input); Guyton Ch. 63 (sympathetic fibers end mainly on enteric neurons).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sympathetic stimulation inhibits gut activity mainly through the enteric nervous system (Guyton Ch. 63). COMLEX: sympathetic activity also contracts sphincters and constricts gut vessels (lecture SDL 4).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 2 — enteric control of secondary peristalsis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Smooth muscle cells receive a ","B":"Mucosal epithelium is controll"}},{"id":"sdl04_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the layers of the GI tract wall and locate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses. (Fig. 8.1)","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man has a full-thickness colon biopsy for chronic constipation. Ganglion cells are seen between the dense connective tissue of the submucosa and the inner circular muscle layer. Which function does this network mainly control?","choices":{"A":"Muscularis externa contraction","B":"Relaxation of the GI sphincters","C":"Local secretion and blood flow","D":"Initiation of the swallow reflex","E":"Gastric receptive relaxation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Ganglia between the submucosal connective tissue and the inner circular layer form the submucosal (Meissner) plexus, which controls local fluid and electrolyte secretion, absorption, local blood flow, and movement of the muscularis mucosae. Contraction of the muscularis externa and relaxation of sphincters are controlled by the myenteric plexus. The swallow reflex is initiated by the medullary swallowing center. Receptive relaxation is a vagovagal reflex of the stomach. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 1, submucosal plexus location, and section 5, submucosal plexus control).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the submucosal plexus integrates epithelial sensory signals to control fluid secretion and local vasodilation (lecture SDL 4). COMLEX: the submucosal plexus also controls the muscularis mucosae (lecture SDL 4).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 3 — submucosal versus myenteric plexus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the layers of the GI tract wall and locate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses. (Fig. 8.1)","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old woman has an endoscopic submucosal injection before removal of a colon polyp. The needle tip lies in dense irregular connective tissue with large vessels, just deep to the muscularis mucosae. Which neural network lies in this layer?","choices":{"A":"Auerbach plexus","B":"Celiac plexus","C":"Vagal trunk plexus","D":"Meissner plexus","E":"Pelvic plexus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Dense irregular connective tissue with large vessels just deep to the muscularis mucosae is the submucosa, which contains the submucosal (Meissner) plexus. The Auerbach (myenteric) plexus lies deeper, between the circular and longitudinal muscle layers. The celiac and pelvic plexuses are extrinsic autonomic plexuses outside the gut wall. Vagal trunks lie on the esophagus and stomach, outside the wall. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 1, submucosa and submucosal plexus); First Aid 2025 GI (digestive tract anatomy).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the submucosa contains the Meissner plexus and vessels; the muscularis externa contains the Auerbach plexus (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract anatomy). COMLEX: the submucosa carries the larger vessels of the gut wall (lecture SDL 4).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 1 — layers of the alimentary canal","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the layers of the GI tract wall and locate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses. (Fig. 8.1)","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman in a pharmacology study receives an agent that blocks synaptic transmission in the ganglia between the circular and longitudinal muscle layers. Which function is reduced?","choices":{"A":"Peristaltic wave velocity","B":"Mucosal fluid secretion","C":"Muscularis mucosae folding","D":"Local mucosal vasodilation","E":"Epithelial absorption"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Ganglia between the two muscle layers form the myenteric plexus, which sets smooth muscle tone, the rate and intensity of rhythmic contractions, and the conduction velocity of peristaltic waves. Mucosal fluid secretion, muscularis mucosae folding, and local vasodilation are controlled by the submucosal plexus. Epithelial absorption depends on enterocytes and submucosal control, not on the myenteric plexus. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 1, myenteric plexus location, and section 5, motility control).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the Auerbach plexus controls motility (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract anatomy). COMLEX: loss of myenteric neurons causes achalasia in the esophagus.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 2 — enteric control of esophageal peristalsis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic innervation of the GI tract, including ganglion location, fiber length, neurotransmitters, and effects on GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":2,"stem":"A 49-year-old woman has episodes of severe hypertension, headaches, and sweating, and imaging shows an adrenal mass. She also reports constipation. Which gastrointestinal effect of her excess catecholamine most likely contributes to the constipation?","choices":{"A":"Faster transit with relaxed sphincters","B":"Slow transit and tight sphincters","C":"Increased glandular fluid secretion","D":"Mucosal vasodilation and hyperemia","E":"Destruction of myenteric neurons"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Norepinephrine, the sympathetic postganglionic transmitter, relaxes the smooth muscle of the gut wall, contracts sphincters, decreases motility and transit, and inhibits secretion, which together most likely favor constipation; Robbins does not list constipation among pheochromocytoma features, so the clinical outcome is inferred from the mechanism. Faster transit with relaxed sphincters and increased glandular secretion are parasympathetic effects. Sympathetic activity constricts, rather than dilates, gut vessels. Norepinephrine inhibits enteric neurons but does not destroy them. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 2, effects on GI function); Guyton Ch. 63 (norepinephrine usually inhibits GI activity).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sympathetic activity inhibits gut motility and secretion and contracts sphincters (Costanzo Ch. 8; Guyton Ch. 63). COMLEX: norepinephrine acts mainly through enteric neurons (Guyton Ch. 63).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 2 — sympathetic control of salivary secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Sympathetic activity constrict","E":"Norepinephrine inhibits enteri"}},{"id":"sdl04_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic innervation of the GI tract, including ganglion location, fiber length, neurotransmitters, and effects on GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman takes an overdose of a drug that blocks muscarinic receptors. She has a dry mouth, flushed skin, and absent bowel sounds. Which normal parasympathetic action has been blocked?","choices":{"A":"Relaxation of gut smooth muscle","B":"Vasoconstriction of gut vessels","C":"Inhibition of glandular secretion","D":"Contraction of gut smooth muscle","E":"Release of norepinephrine"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Parasympathetic postganglionic cholinergic neurons release ACh onto muscarinic receptors to increase smooth muscle contraction and motility, relax sphincters, and increase glandular secretion. Blocking muscarinic receptors removes these effects, causing ileus and dry mouth. Relaxation of gut smooth muscle, vasoconstriction, and inhibition of secretion are sympathetic effects. Norepinephrine is released by sympathetic, not parasympathetic, fibers. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 2, neurotransmitters and effects on GI function).","boardPrep":"Step 1: acetylcholine most often excites GI activity (Guyton Ch. 63). COMLEX: parasympathetic activity relaxes GI sphincters (lecture SDL 4).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 2 — parasympathetic control of salivary secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Relaxation of gut smooth muscl","B":"Relaxation of gut smooth muscl","C":"Relaxation of gut smooth muscl"}},{"id":"sdl04_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic innervation of the GI tract, including ganglion location, fiber length, neurotransmitters, and effects on GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":2,"stem":"A 57-year-old man with pancreatic cancer has severe abdominal pain and receives a celiac plexus block. The injected ganglion lies outside the gut wall in front of the aorta. Which neurons have cell bodies in this ganglion?","choices":{"A":"Parasympathetic postganglionic neurons","B":"Vagal preganglionic neurons","C":"Sympathetic postganglionic neurons","D":"Enteric interneurons of the wall","E":"Dorsal root sensory neurons"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The celiac ganglion is a prevertebral sympathetic ganglion where short preganglionic fibers synapse on postganglionic neurons whose long adrenergic fibers run to the gut. Parasympathetic postganglionic cell bodies lie in the myenteric and submucosal plexuses within the gut wall. Vagal preganglionic cell bodies lie in the dorsal motor nucleus of the brainstem, and vagal fibers pass through the celiac plexus without synapsing; Bootcamp labels the celiac ganglia parasympathetic, but the lecture lists them as sympathetic prevertebral ganglia. Enteric interneurons lie in the gut wall. Dorsal root sensory neurons do not lie in the celiac ganglion. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 2, ganglion location and fiber length); Costanzo Ch. 8 (sympathetic innervation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: foregut sympathetic supply runs through the T5 to T9 splanchnic nerves (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, embryology). COMLEX: the lecture lists the celiac, superior mesenteric, inferior mesenteric, and hypogastric ganglia as prevertebral.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 4 — pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the distribution of parasympathetic innervation to the GI tract by the vagus and pelvic nerves. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":2,"stem":"Which of the following digestive organs receives its parasympathetic supply from the cranial division rather than from the S2 to S4 sacral division?","choices":{"A":"Gallbladder","B":"Sigmoid colon","C":"Rectum","D":"Anal canal","E":"Descending colon"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The vagus nerve, the cranial division, supplies parasympathetic fibers to the esophagus, stomach, exocrine pancreas, liver and gallbladder, small intestine, cecum, appendix, ascending colon, and proximal transverse colon. The pelvic nerve from S2 to S4 supplies the distal transverse, descending, and sigmoid colon, the rectum, and the anal canal, so the sigmoid colon, rectum, anal canal, and descending colon all depend on the sacral outflow. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 3, vagus and pelvic nerve distribution, p24); Costanzo Ch. 8 (parasympathetic innervation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: vagal supply ends at the proximal transverse colon, and S2 to S4 supply begins at the distal transverse colon (lecture SDL 4). COMLEX: pelvic nerve outflow is concentrated in the sigmoid, rectum, and anus to coordinate defecation (lecture SDL 4).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 3 — vagal acetylcholine drives the cephalic phase","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the distribution of parasympathetic innervation to the GI tract by the vagus and pelvic nerves. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman undergoes truncal vagotomy, with both vagal trunks divided at the esophageal hiatus, during surgery for recurrent bleeding from a duodenal ulcer. Her sacral spinal cord and pelvic nerves are intact. Which segment of the gut loses its parasympathetic supply?","choices":{"A":"Distal transverse colon","B":"Ascending colon","C":"Descending colon","D":"Sigmoid colon","E":"Anal canal"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Dividing both vagal trunks removes parasympathetic supply from the whole vagal territory, which runs from the esophagus and stomach through the small intestine, cecum, appendix, ascending colon, and proximal transverse colon. The distal transverse colon, descending colon, sigmoid colon, and anal canal receive parasympathetic fibers from the pelvic nerve, which arises from S2 to S4 and is intact in this patient. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 3, vagus and pelvic nerve distribution, lines 835–850); Costanzo Ch. 8 (parasympathetic innervation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid gives the vagus as the parasympathetic nerve of the foregut and of the midgut, which extends to the proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon (First Aid 2025 GI, gastrointestinal blood supply and innervation). COMLEX: after truncal vagotomy, the left colon still receives sacral parasympathetic outflow (lecture SDL 4).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 3 — anterior and posterior vagal trunks","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the distribution of parasympathetic innervation to the GI tract by the vagus and pelvic nerves. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":2,"stem":"A 32-year-old woman with a sacral myelomeningocele has absent function of the S2 to S4 cord segments. Which gastrointestinal function is most affected?","choices":{"A":"Gastric acid secretion","B":"Gallbladder contraction","C":"Pancreatic enzyme secretion","D":"Small bowel peristalsis","E":"Rectal defecation reflexes"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Pelvic nerve innervation from S2 to S4 is concentrated in the sigmoid colon, rectum, and anal canal, where it coordinates defecation reflexes and stimulates colonic mucus secretion. Gastric acid secretion, gallbladder contraction, and pancreatic enzyme secretion receive vagal parasympathetic input. Small bowel peristalsis is driven by the myenteric plexus with vagal modulation. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 3, pelvic nerve).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the pelvic nerve arises from the second, third, and fourth sacral cord segments (lecture SDL 4). COMLEX: neurogenic bowel follows sacral cord injury.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — hindgut derivatives","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the afferent and efferent pathways of a vagovagal reflex and their role in regulating GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":2,"stem":"A 53-year-old man has early satiety and epigastric fullness after small meals since a truncal vagotomy. Loss of which reflex best explains his symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Receptive relaxation","B":"Gastrocolic reflex","C":"Enterogastric reflex","D":"Peristaltic reflex","E":"Defecation reflex"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Receptive relaxation is a vagovagal reflex in which distension of the orad stomach relaxes the gastric wall so it can hold a meal without a rise in pressure; vagotomy eliminates it, causing early fullness. The gastrocolic reflex increases colonic motility after a meal. The enterogastric reflex slows gastric emptying in response to duodenal contents, and its loss would not cause early satiety. The peristaltic reflex is a local enteric reflex that survives vagotomy. The defecation reflex is sacral. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 4, receptive relaxation); Costanzo Ch. 8 (vagotomy eliminates receptive relaxation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: receptive relaxation is a vagovagal reflex mediated by VIP (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: impaired gastric accommodation causes early satiety.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 2 — receptive relaxation of the orad stomach","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the afferent and efferent pathways of a vagovagal reflex and their role in regulating GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman in a physiology study has a balloon inflated in her stomach, and her serum gastrin rises. Postganglionic vagal neurons release which transmitter onto the antral G cells?","choices":{"A":"VIP","B":"ACh","C":"CCK","D":"NO","E":"GRP"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Gastric distension initiates vagovagal reflexes in which postganglionic neurons release ACh onto parietal cells and gastrin-releasing peptide (GRP) onto antral G cells, increasing acid secretion. VIP and nitric oxide relax the orad stomach in receptive relaxation. ACh is the transmitter acting on parietal cells rather than on G cells. CCK is a hormone from duodenal I cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 4, gastric secretion).","boardPrep":"Step 1: vagal stimulation releases GRP to increase gastrin (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: atropine does not block vagal stimulation of gastrin because GRP is not cholinergic (First Aid 2025 GI acid secretion diagram: atropine acts on the ACh limb).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — neural regulation of acid secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"VIP and nitric oxide relax the"}},{"id":"sdl04_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the afferent and efferent pathways of a vagovagal reflex and their role in regulating GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":2,"stem":"A 39-year-old woman notices an urge to defecate within minutes after each large meal. Which reflex is responsible?","choices":{"A":"Receptive relaxation","B":"Gastrocolic reflex","C":"Enterogastric reflex","D":"Rectosphincteric reflex","E":"Vomiting reflex"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Gastric distension triggers the gastrocolic reflex, a long arc reflex that increases colonic motility and starts mass movements. The lecture describes it as a long arc vagovagal reflex, which is the exam convention; Costanzo instead places the afferent limb in parasympathetic nerves and the efferent limb in CCK and gastrin. Receptive relaxation relaxes the stomach itself. The enterogastric reflex slows gastric emptying. The rectosphincteric reflex relaxes the internal anal sphincter when the rectum fills, after mass movements arrive. The vomiting reflex is coordinated in the medulla. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 4, gastrocolic reflex); Costanzo Ch. 8 (gastrocolic reflex).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the gastrocolic reflex increases colonic motility and mass movements after a meal (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: mass movements carry colonic contents toward the rectum (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — enterogastric reflexes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain how the enteric nervous system independently regulates GI motility, secretion, and endocrine function and how extrinsic autonomic input modulates its activity. (Fig. 8.3)","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman has a segment of jejunum studied after all of its extrinsic nerves are cut. Stroking the mucosa still triggers a local peristaltic reflex. Where are the cell bodies of the sensory neurons that detect this stimulus?","choices":{"A":"Dorsal root ganglia","B":"Nodose ganglion","C":"Prevertebral ganglia","D":"Within the gut submucosa","E":"Nucleus tractus solitarius"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Intrinsic primary afferent neurons (IPANs) are embedded in the submucosa. They detect stretch or chemical stimuli, often after enterochromaffin cells release serotonin, and signal enteric interneurons that coordinate motor, secretory, and endocrine responses without central processing, so the reflex survives extrinsic denervation. Dorsal root ganglia and the nodose ganglion hold the cell bodies of extrinsic spinal and vagal afferents, which were cut. Prevertebral ganglia hold sympathetic postganglionic neurons. The nucleus tractus solitarius is the medullary site where vagal afferents end. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 5, IPANs embedded in the submucosa, p25; section 4, vagal afferents end in the nucleus tractus solitarius, p24).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the enteric nervous system has over 100 million intrinsic neurons and can run local reflexes on its own (lecture SDL 4). COMLEX: extrinsic sympathetic and parasympathetic fibers modulate, but are not needed for, intrinsic reflexes (lecture SDL 4).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 4 — vagal afferents in the vagovagal reflex","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain how the enteric nervous system independently regulates GI motility, secretion, and endocrine function and how extrinsic autonomic input modulates its activity. (Fig. 8.3)","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old man has paralytic ileus after a long abdominal operation with intense sympathetic activation. Norepinephrine suppresses his motility mainly through which effect on the enteric nervous system?","choices":{"A":"Increased vagal outflow","B":"Loss of enteric neurons","C":"Blocked nicotinic receptors","D":"Increased VIP and NO release","E":"Reduced enteric ACh release"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Sympathetic postganglionic fibers end mainly on enteric neurons, and norepinephrine binds presynaptic receptors that inhibit release of excitatory transmitters such as ACh, suppressing motility. Increased vagal outflow would increase motility. Enteric neurons are inhibited, not lost. Norepinephrine does not block nicotinic receptors. Increased VIP and NO release would relax muscle by a different pathway and is not the sympathetic mechanism. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 5, sympathetic input); Guyton Ch. 63 (inhibitory effect of norepinephrine on enteric neurons).","boardPrep":"Step 1: strong sympathetic stimulation can inhibit gut movement enough to block passage of food (Guyton Ch. 63). COMLEX: enterogastric reflexes also use prevertebral sympathetic ganglia (lecture SDL 6).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — sympathetic limb of the enterogastric reflex","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Enteric neurons are inhibited,"}},{"id":"sdl04_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain how the enteric nervous system independently regulates GI motility, secretion, and endocrine function and how extrinsic autonomic input modulates its activity. (Fig. 8.3)","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman is studied after her extrinsic gut nerves are blocked pharmacologically. Luminal peptides still increase gastrin release from her antral G cells. Which neural element can still modulate the G cells?","choices":{"A":"Vagal afferent fibers","B":"Prevertebral ganglia","C":"Enteric interneurons","D":"Pelvic splanchnic fibers","E":"Spinal motor neurons"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Enteric interneurons synapse with and modulate enteroendocrine cells, regulating the release of local hormones such as gastrin, CCK, secretin, and somatostatin in response to luminal conditions, even without extrinsic input. Vagal afferents, prevertebral sympathetic ganglia, and pelvic splanchnic fibers are extrinsic and are blocked. Spinal motor neurons supply skeletal muscle. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 5, endocrine modulation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: G cells release gastrin in response to small peptides and amino acids (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: the enteric nervous system functions independently of extrinsic nerves.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — gastrin release triggers","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl04_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the layers of the GI tract wall and locate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses. (Fig. 8.1)","batch":3,"stem":"A 79-year-old woman has a segment of ileum resected and examined. Which layer lies immediately outside its outer longitudinal muscle?","choices":{"A":"Mucosa","B":"Serosa","C":"Adventitia","D":"Submucosa","E":"Muscularis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Intraperitoneal segments such as the ileum are covered by a serosa, a serous membrane of simple squamous mesothelium outside the outer longitudinal muscle. The mucosa and submucosa lie inside the muscularis externa. Adventitia is the connective tissue coat of fixed, retroperitoneal segments. The muscularis is the muscle layer itself. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 1, serosa or adventitia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the gut wall layers from lumen outward are mucosa, submucosa, muscularis externa, and serosa or adventitia (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract anatomy). COMLEX: retroperitoneal segments have an adventitia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 1 — layers of the alimentary canal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"The muscularis is the muscle l"}},{"id":"trial_sdl04_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic innervation of the GI tract, including ganglion location, fiber length, neurotransmitters, and effects on GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":3,"stem":"An 81-year-old man has midgut visceral pain carried with sympathetic fibers from T10 to T12. In which ganglion do the preganglionic sympathetic fibers to the midgut synapse?","choices":{"A":"Inferior mesenteric ganglion","B":"Superior cervical ganglion","C":"Hypogastric plexus ganglion","D":"Superior mesenteric ganglion","E":"Myenteric plexus ganglia"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The midgut is supplied by the superior mesenteric artery with sympathetic fibers from T10 to T12, which synapse in the superior mesenteric ganglion wrapped around the superior mesenteric artery; its postganglionic axons reach the territory of that artery. The inferior mesenteric and hypogastric ganglia serve the hindgut and pelvis. The superior cervical ganglion serves the head and neck. Myenteric ganglia are intrinsic. Sources: Bootcamp Gastroenterology (midgut: SMA, T10 to T12); Foundations of Osteopathic Medicine Ch. 6 (superior mesenteric ganglion, lines 3976–3981).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the midgut has vagal parasympathetic and T10 to T12 splanchnic sympathetic supply (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, embryology). COMLEX: hindgut sympathetic supply is L1 to L2.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — foregut, midgut, and hindgut","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl04_q03","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the afferent and efferent pathways of a vagovagal reflex and their role in regulating GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":3,"stem":"A 7-year-old girl eats a small protein meal that barely distends her stomach, yet a vagovagal reflex raises her acid secretion. Which receptors most likely start this reflex?","choices":{"A":"Mucosal chemoreceptors","B":"Wall stretch receptors","C":"Visceral pain nociceptors","D":"Mucosal thermoreceptors","E":"Carotid baroreceptors"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Vagovagal afferents start from mechanoreceptors that detect stretch or distension and from chemoreceptors that detect pH, osmolarity, nutrients, or mucosal irritation; with little distension, digestion products act on mucosal chemoreceptors. Stretch receptors need distension. Pain nociceptors are not the afferents of this reflex. Thermoreceptors and carotid baroreceptors are not afferents of this reflex either. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 4, afferent pathway and gastric secretion).","boardPrep":"Step 1: vagovagal reflexes stimulate parietal cells through ACh and G cells through GRP (lecture SDL 4). COMLEX: luminal digestion products trigger the gastric phase of acid secretion (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — phases of gastric acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl04_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the afferent and efferent pathways of a vagovagal reflex and their role in regulating GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":3,"stem":"An 83-year-old man has a vagovagal reflex studied after a meal. Where are the cell bodies of the vagal afferent neurons that carry the sensory limb?","choices":{"A":"Celiac ganglion","B":"Myenteric plexus","C":"Geniculate ganglion","D":"Ventral horn","E":"Nodose ganglion"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Afferent axons traveling with the vagus have their cell bodies in the nodose (inferior vagal) ganglion, and their central processes end in the solitary nucleus of the medulla. The celiac ganglion is a sympathetic prevertebral ganglion. The myenteric plexus holds enteric neurons. The geniculate ganglion belongs to the facial nerve. The ventral horn holds motor neurons. Sources: Foundations of Osteopathic Medicine Ch. 6 (vagal afferent cell bodies in the nodose ganglion, lines 2891–2893 and 5126–5127).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in a vagovagal reflex both limbs travel in the vagus (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: vagal afferents also sense plasma glucose and portal osmotic pressure (Foundations of Osteopathic Medicine Ch. 6).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 1 — medullary swallowing center","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl04_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain how the enteric nervous system independently regulates GI motility, secretion, and endocrine function and how extrinsic autonomic input modulates its activity. (Fig. 8.3)","batch":3,"stem":"A 20-year-old woman joins a motility study of the enteric neurons that relax muscle ahead of a bolus. Which transmitter is released by these inhibitory motor neurons?","choices":{"A":"ACh","B":"GRP","C":"ATP","D":"CCK","E":"Gastrin"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Inhibitory enteric motor neurons use ATP among other cotransmitters to relax muscle ahead of the bolus and relax GI sphincters. ACh is excitatory and contracts muscle behind the bolus. GRP stimulates G cells. CCK and gastrin are hormones. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 5, motility control via the myenteric plexus).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the myenteric plexus regulates muscle tone and peristaltic wave velocity (lecture SDL 4). COMLEX: excitatory enteric neurons contract muscle behind a bolus.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 2 — LES relaxation ahead of the bolus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl04_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic innervation of the GI tract, including ganglion location, fiber length, neurotransmitters, and effects on GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":3,"stem":"A 24-year-old woman has visceral abdominal pain carried by afferent fibers that run with the splanchnic sympathetic nerves. Where are the cell bodies of these afferent neurons?","choices":{"A":"Dorsal root ganglia","B":"Inferior vagal ganglion","C":"Celiac ganglion","D":"Myenteric plexus","E":"Dorsal horn of cord"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Visceral afferent fibers associated with the splanchnic nerves have their cell bodies in the dorsal root ganglia of spinal nerves, and their central processes end in the dorsal horn. The inferior vagal (nodose) ganglion holds vagal afferent cell bodies. The celiac ganglion holds sympathetic postganglionic neurons. The myenteric plexus holds enteric neurons. The dorsal horn receives the central processes rather than the cell bodies. Sources: Foundations of Osteopathic Medicine Ch. 6 (visceral afferents with splanchnic nerves, lines 2888–2890 and 5137–5138).","boardPrep":"Step 1: splanchnic visceral afferents end in the dorsal horn of the spinal cord (Foundations of Osteopathic Medicine Ch. 6). COMLEX: about half of sympathetic nerve fibers are afferent (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — foregut, midgut, and hindgut","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":5,"title":"SDL 5 — Physiology - GI Hormones, Paracrines, & Appetite","questions":[{"id":"sdl05_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — For each of the three categories- hormone, paracrine, neurocrine- list examples of each, where each is released from, and how it travels to reach its target cell. Fig 8.4","batch":1,"stem":"According to Costanzo, how does a GI paracrine reach its target cell?","choices":{"A":"Portal then systemic circulation","B":"Release into a synaptic cleft","C":"Local interstitial diffusion","D":"Secretion into the gut lumen","E":"Retrograde axonal transport"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Paracrines are secreted by endocrine cells of the GI mucosa and act locally, diffusing short distances through interstitial fluid to adjacent target cells. Travel through the portal and then systemic circulation describes hormones. Release into a synaptic cleft after an action potential describes neurocrines. Secretion into the lumen describes exocrine products such as enzymes. Retrograde axonal transport carries signals within neurons, not between cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 1, paracrines); Costanzo Ch. 8 (classification of GI peptides).","boardPrep":"Step 1: somatostatin and histamine are the classic GI paracrines (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: H2 blockers interrupt a paracrine signal.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric neurons and their neurocrines","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — For each of the three categories- hormone, paracrine, neurocrine- list examples of each, where each is released from, and how it travels to reach its target cell. Fig 8.4","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which GI peptide acts as a neurocrine released by enteric neurons?","choices":{"A":"GIP","B":"VIP","C":"CCK","D":"PYY","E":"Gastrin"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Vasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP) is synthesized in enteric neurons and released at synapses, so it is a neurocrine. GIP, CCK, and gastrin are official GI hormones secreted by K, I, and G cells into the blood. Peptide YY is a candidate hormone from L cells of the ileum and colon. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 1, neurocrines); Costanzo Ch. 8 (neurocrines).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a VIPoma causes watery diarrhea, hypokalemia, and achlorhydria (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors). COMLEX: VIP relaxes GI smooth muscle and sphincters.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric neurons and their neurocrines","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Peptide YY is a candidate horm"}},{"id":"sdl05_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — For each of the three categories- hormone, paracrine, neurocrine- list examples of each, where each is released from, and how it travels to reach its target cell. Fig 8.4","batch":1,"stem":"Which paracrine is released by enterochromaffin-like cells in the oxyntic mucosa?","choices":{"A":"Histamine","B":"Somatostatin","C":"Gastrin","D":"Secretin","E":"Ghrelin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"ECL cells in the oxyntic region release histamine, which diffuses to neighboring parietal cells and binds H2 receptors. Somatostatin is a paracrine, but it comes from D cells. Gastrin is a hormone from antral G cells. Secretin is a hormone from duodenal S cells. Ghrelin is a hormone from gastric oxyntic cells that acts on the hypothalamus. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 1, paracrines, and section 6, ghrelin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: histamine binds H2 receptors on parietal cells through a Gs cascade (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acid secretion pathways). COMLEX: H2 receptor antagonists reduce acid by blocking this paracrine.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric neurons and their neurocrines","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Gastrin is a hormone from antr"}},{"id":"sdl05_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — For each of the 4 GI hormones: explain where each is secreted from, what triggers its release, and its main action(s). Table 8.2","batch":1,"stem":"According to Costanzo, S cells release secretin in response to which stimulus?","choices":{"A":"Amino acids in the stomach","B":"Glucose in the jejunum","C":"Distension of the antrum","D":"Vagal release of GRP","E":"Acid in the duodenum"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Secretin is released by duodenal S cells when acidic chyme below pH 4.5 enters the duodenum, and fatty acids also stimulate it. Amino acids in the stomach, antral distension, and vagal GRP release are stimuli for gastrin. Glucose in the small intestine stimulates GIP. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 2, secretin); Costanzo Ch. 8 (secretin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secretin increases pancreatic HCO3- secretion and decreases gastric acid secretion (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: secretin also inhibits gastrin release.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — enterogastric reflexes, CCK, secretin, and GIP","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — For each of the 4 GI hormones: explain where each is secreted from, what triggers its release, and its main action(s). Table 8.2","batch":1,"stem":"Fatty acids entering the duodenum release a hormone from I cells that contracts the gallbladder. Which hormone is this?","choices":{"A":"CCK","B":"PYY","C":"VIP","D":"GIP","E":"Gastrin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cholecystokinin (CCK) is secreted by I cells of the duodenum and jejunum in response to monoglycerides, fatty acids, small peptides, and amino acids; it contracts the gallbladder, relaxes the sphincter of Oddi, and stimulates pancreatic enzyme secretion. GIP comes from K cells and stimulates insulin release. PYY comes from L cells and decreases appetite. VIP is a neurocrine. Gastrin comes from G cells and stimulates acid. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 2, cholecystokinin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CCK increases pancreatic secretion and gallbladder contraction and decreases gastric emptying (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: fatty meals provoke biliary colic through CCK.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — enterogastric reflexes, CCK, secretin, and GIP","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — For each of the 4 GI hormones: explain where each is secreted from, what triggers its release, and its main action(s). Table 8.2","batch":1,"stem":"According to Costanzo, one GI hormone is secreted in response to all three nutrient types: glucose, amino acids, and fatty acids. Which cells secrete it?","choices":{"A":"I cells","B":"S cells","C":"K cells","D":"G cells","E":"L cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"GIP is secreted by K cells of the duodenal, jejunal, and ileal mucosa and, unlike the other GI hormones, is released by glucose, amino acids, and fatty acids. I cells secrete CCK in response to fats and protein products. S cells secrete secretin in response to acid and fatty acids. G cells secrete gastrin in response to peptides and amino acids. L cells secrete GLP-1 and PYY. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (GIP); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 2, GIP).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GIP increases insulin release and decreases gastric acid secretion (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: GIP is an incretin.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — enterogastric reflexes, CCK, secretin, and GIP","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"I cells secrete CCK in respons","B":"S cells secrete secretin in re","D":"G cells secrete gastrin in res","E":"L cells secrete GLP-1 and PYY."}},{"id":"sdl05_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Name the tumor responsible for Zollinger-Ellison syndrome and list its three consequences (H+ secretion, mucosal effect, ulcer location).","batch":1,"stem":"According to Costanzo, Zollinger-Ellison syndrome can cause steatorrhea. Which mechanism explains it?","choices":{"A":"Loss of bile acid secretion","B":"Acid inactivation of lipase","C":"Gastrin block of CCK release","D":"Villous atrophy from gluten","E":"Bacterial overgrowth"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In Zollinger-Ellison syndrome, excess acid acidifies the intestinal lumen and inactivates pancreatic lipase, so dietary fat is not digested and appears in the stool. Bile acid secretion is not reduced by gastrin. Gastrin does not block CCK release. Villous atrophy from gluten is celiac disease. Bacterial overgrowth causes malabsorption by a different mechanism. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Zollinger-Ellison syndrome).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastrinoma presents with refractory ulcers, diarrhea, steatorrhea, and weight loss (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, peptic ulcer disease). COMLEX: gastrinoma is associated with MEN1.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 4 — regulation of gastric acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Name the tumor responsible for Zollinger-Ellison syndrome and list its three consequences (H+ secretion, mucosal effect, ulcer location).","batch":1,"stem":"In Zollinger-Ellison syndrome, chronically high gastrin levels change the gastric oxyntic mucosa in which way?","choices":{"A":"Atrophy and parietal loss","B":"Intestinal type metaplasia","C":"Fibrosis and scarring of glands","D":"Necrosis and mucosal sloughing","E":"Hypertrophy and hyperplasia"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Gastrin has a trophic effect, so chronically elevated gastrin causes hypertrophy and hyperplasia of the oxyntic mucosa with marked parietal cell hyperplasia. Atrophy with parietal cell loss occurs after antrectomy or in autoimmune gastritis. Intestinal metaplasia follows chronic gastritis. Fibrosis and necrosis are not effects of gastrin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 3, mucosal effect); Costanzo Ch. 8 (trophic effect of gastrin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastrin increases growth of the gastric mucosa (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: thickened gastric folds with refractory ulcers suggest gastrinoma.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 4 — regulation of gastric acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Name the tumor responsible for Zollinger-Ellison syndrome and list its three consequences (H+ secretion, mucosal effect, ulcer location).","batch":1,"stem":"Which peptic ulcer location most strongly suggests Zollinger-Ellison syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Duodenal bulb","B":"Lesser curvature","C":"Gastric antrum","D":"Proximal jejunum","E":"Gastric cardia"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Zollinger-Ellison syndrome causes severe recurrent ulcers in the duodenum and stomach and frequently in unusual distal sites such as the jejunum, because excess unbuffered acid reaches the small intestine. Duodenal bulb ulcers are common in Zollinger-Ellison syndrome but also in ordinary H. pylori disease, so they are less specific. Lesser curvature and antral ulcers are typical gastric ulcers. The cardia is an uncommon ulcer site with no link to gastrinoma. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 3, ulcer location).","boardPrep":"Step 1: duodenal ulcers are most common in gastrinoma, but jejunal ulcers are the most specific clue (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, peptic ulcer disease). COMLEX: ulcers that do not respond to therapy suggest gastrinoma.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 4 — regulation of gastric acid secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Lesser curvature and antral ul"}},{"id":"sdl05_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand which route of glucose administration (oral vs. IV) causes more insulin release for the same blood glucose level, and name the hormone responsible.","batch":1,"stem":"Oral glucose releases more insulin than an intravenous glucose load that produces the same blood glucose concentration. Which hormone is mainly responsible?","choices":{"A":"GIP from K cells","B":"CCK from I cells","C":"Secretin from S cells","D":"Gastrin from G cells","E":"Ghrelin from the stomach"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Oral glucose stimulates GIP release from K cells, and GIP binds receptors on pancreatic beta cells to amplify glucose-induced insulin secretion; GLP-1 from L cells contributes. Intravenous glucose bypasses the gut and relies on direct beta cell sensing. CCK, secretin, and gastrin are not incretins in the lecture. Ghrelin increases appetite and is suppressed by food. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 4, incretin action); Costanzo Ch. 8 (GIP).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GIP increases insulin release and is stimulated by oral glucose (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: oral glucose produces a larger insulin response than intravenous glucose.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — GIP and duodenal control of the stomach","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand which route of glucose administration (oral vs. IV) causes more insulin release for the same blood glucose level, and name the hormone responsible.","batch":1,"stem":"The greater insulin response to oral glucose than to an equivalent intravenous glucose load has which name?","choices":{"A":"Somogyi effect","B":"Incretin effect","C":"Dawn phenomenon","D":"Glucose toxicity","E":"Insulin resistance"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The larger insulin release after oral glucose is the incretin effect, mediated by GIP and GLP-1. The Somogyi effect is rebound hyperglycemia after nocturnal hypoglycemia. The dawn phenomenon is early morning hyperglycemia from counterregulatory hormones. Glucose toxicity is beta cell impairment from chronic hyperglycemia. Insulin resistance is a reduced tissue response to insulin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 4, incretin effect).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GIP and GLP-1 are incretins (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: GLP-1 analogs treat type 2 diabetes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — GIP and duodenal control of the stomach","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand which route of glucose administration (oral vs. IV) causes more insulin release for the same blood glucose level, and name the hormone responsible.","batch":1,"stem":"Besides GIP, the incretin GLP-1 amplifies insulin release. Which cells secrete GLP-1?","choices":{"A":"K cells of duodenum and jejunum","B":"I cells of duodenum and jejunum","C":"S cells of the duodenum","D":"L cells of ileum and colon","E":"G cells of the gastric antrum"},"correct":"D","explanation":"GLP-1 is cleaved from proglucagon and secreted by L cells of the distal ileum and colon in response to carbohydrates and fats. K cells secrete GIP. I cells secrete CCK. S cells secrete secretin. Antral G cells secrete gastrin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 4, incretin action); Costanzo Ch. 8 (GLP-1).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GLP-1 increases insulin secretion, decreases gastric emptying, and decreases appetite (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: GLP-1 analogs promote weight loss.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — GIP and duodenal control of the stomach","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"K cells secrete GIP.","B":"I cells secrete CCK.","C":"S cells secrete secretin.","E":"Antral G cells secrete gastrin"}},{"id":"sdl05_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain what somatostatin does to gastrin secretion, and what histamine does to parietal cell acid secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"Somatostatin from antral D cells diffuses to neighboring G cells. What effect does it have on them?","choices":{"A":"Stimulates gastrin release","B":"Inhibits gastrin release","C":"Stimulates histamine release","D":"Blocks gastrin receptors","E":"Destroys G cells"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Somatostatin binds receptors on G cells and suppresses gastrin synthesis and release. Stimulation of gastrin release is produced by peptides, distension, and vagal GRP. Somatostatin also inhibits, rather than stimulates, histamine release. It acts on G cells, not on gastrin receptors of parietal cells. It inhibits G cells without destroying them. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 5, somatostatin effect on gastrin secretion).","boardPrep":"Step 1: somatostatin decreases gastric acid and pepsinogen secretion and many hormones (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: octreotide is a somatostatin analog.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 4 — somatostatin, histamine, and acid secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"It acts on G cells, not on gas"}},{"id":"sdl05_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain what somatostatin does to gastrin secretion, and what histamine does to parietal cell acid secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, D cells release somatostatin in response to which luminal condition?","choices":{"A":"High gastric pH","B":"Gastric distension","C":"Low gastric pH","D":"Luminal amino acids","E":"Vagal GRP release"},"correct":"C","explanation":"D cells release somatostatin in response to low luminal pH, providing negative feedback that suppresses gastrin and acid. A high gastric pH, distension, luminal amino acids, and vagal GRP stimulate gastrin release instead. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 5, somatostatin); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (low pH stimulates somatostatin, which inhibits parietal and G cells).","boardPrep":"Step 1: somatostatin is increased by acid and decreased by vagal stimulation (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: this feedback is lost in gastrinoma.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 4 — somatostatin, histamine, and acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain what somatostatin does to gastrin secretion, and what histamine does to parietal cell acid secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"Histamine from ECL cells increases parietal cell acid secretion through which receptor and second messenger?","choices":{"A":"H2 receptor and cAMP","B":"H1 receptor and IP3","C":"CCK2 receptor and calcium","D":"M3 receptor and calcium","E":"SST receptor and Gi"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Histamine binds H2 receptors on parietal cells, activating Gs and adenylyl cyclase to raise cAMP and drive the H+/K+-ATPase. H1 receptors act through IP3 in other tissues. Gastrin acts on CCK2 receptors through calcium. ACh acts on M3 receptors through calcium. A somatostatin receptor coupled to Gi lowers cAMP, which is how somatostatin inhibits acid secretion. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 5, histamine effect on parietal cell acid secretion); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (ECL cell histamine and D cell somatostatin pathways).","boardPrep":"Step 1: histamine potentiates the acid stimulating effects of ACh and gastrin (lecture SDL 5). COMLEX: H2 blockers reduce acid stimulated by all three secretagogues.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 4 — somatostatin, histamine, and acid secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"A somatostatin receptor couple"}},{"id":"sdl05_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — List which hormones/peptides increase appetite and which decrease it, then explain the orexigenic and anorexigenic pathways. Table 8.3, Fig 8.7","batch":1,"stem":"Which peptide rises before meals and during fasting to increase food intake?","choices":{"A":"Leptin","B":"Peptide YY","C":"Ghrelin","D":"Insulin","E":"Serotonin"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Ghrelin is secreted by gastric oxyntic cells before meals and during fasting and is orexigenic. Leptin from adipocytes, PYY from L cells, insulin from beta cells, and serotonin are anorexigenic in the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 6, hormones and peptides influencing appetite).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ghrelin is increased in the fasting state and in Prader-Willi syndrome and decreased after gastric bypass (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: ghrelin also increases gastric motility.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — CCK and GIP slow gastric emptying","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Leptin from adipocytes, PYY fr"}},{"id":"sdl05_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — List which hormones/peptides increase appetite and which decrease it, then explain the orexigenic and anorexigenic pathways. Table 8.3, Fig 8.7","batch":1,"stem":"Leptin is an anorexigenic signal secreted in proportion to body fat mass. Which cells secrete it?","choices":{"A":"Hepatocytes","B":"Enterocytes","C":"Beta cells","D":"Myocytes","E":"Adipocytes"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Leptin is secreted by adipocytes in proportion to fat mass and activates POMC neurons while inhibiting NPY and AGRP neurons. Hepatocytes and myocytes are not listed sources of appetite peptides. Enterocytes do not secrete leptin, although L and I cells of the intestine secrete PYY, GLP-1, and CCK. Beta cells secrete insulin, another anorexigenic signal. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 6, anorexigenic hormones).","boardPrep":"Step 1: leptin and insulin stimulate the anorexigenic pathway (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: leptin rises with fat mass.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — CCK and GIP slow gastric emptying","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — List which hormones/peptides increase appetite and which decrease it, then explain the orexigenic and anorexigenic pathways. Table 8.3, Fig 8.7","batch":1,"stem":"In the orexigenic pathway, AGRP released from arcuate neurons acts at melanocortin-4 receptors in the paraventricular nucleus. How does it act there?","choices":{"A":"Competitive antagonist","B":"Partial receptor agonist","C":"Full receptor agonist","D":"Positive allosteric enhancer","E":"Irreversible agonist"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AGRP is a competitive antagonist at MC4R in the paraventricular nucleus, blocking satiety signaling while NPY activates the lateral hypothalamus. Partial, full, and irreversible agonists would activate MC4R and reduce appetite, which is the role of alpha-MSH. A positive allosteric enhancer would increase MC4R signaling. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 6, orexigenic pathway).","boardPrep":"Step 1: MC4R defects cause hyperphagia and early onset obesity (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: AGRP and NPY increase appetite.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — CCK and GIP slow gastric emptying","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q19","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — For each of the three categories- hormone, paracrine, neurocrine- list examples of each, where each is released from, and how it travels to reach its target cell. Fig 8.4","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old man with reflux takes a drug that blocks receptors for a substance released by ECL cells. Which mode of signaling does the drug interrupt?","choices":{"A":"Paracrine diffusion","B":"Endocrine circulation","C":"Neurocrine release","D":"Autocrine feedback","E":"Luminal secretion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Histamine from ECL cells diffuses through interstitial fluid to adjacent parietal cells, which is paracrine signaling. Endocrine signaling uses the portal and systemic circulation. Neurocrine signaling is release by neurons into a synapse. Autocrine signaling acts on the releasing cell itself. Luminal secretion releases products into the gut lumen. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 1, paracrines); Costanzo Ch. 8 (paracrines).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H2 receptor antagonists block histamine on parietal cells (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: H2 blockers are used for reflux and peptic ulcer disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — acid suppressing drugs","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q20","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — For each of the three categories- hormone, paracrine, neurocrine- list examples of each, where each is released from, and how it travels to reach its target cell. Fig 8.4","batch":2,"stem":"A 25-year-old woman eats a fatty meal. A peptide from duodenal I cells contracts her gallbladder. How is this peptide classified?","choices":{"A":"Paracrine","B":"Autocrine","C":"Neurocrine","D":"GI hormone","E":"Exocrine enzyme"},"correct":"D","explanation":"CCK is secreted by I cells into the portal circulation, passes through the liver into the systemic circulation, and acts on distant targets, so it is a GI hormone. Paracrines act locally by diffusion. Neurocrines are released by neurons at synapses. Autocrines act on the cell that secretes them. Exocrine enzymes are secreted into the lumen. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 1, hormones); Costanzo Ch. 8 (gastrointestinal hormones).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the four official GI hormones are gastrin, CCK, secretin, and GIP (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: CCK release explains postprandial biliary colic.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — CCK and duodenal control of the stomach","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q21","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — For each of the three categories- hormone, paracrine, neurocrine- list examples of each, where each is released from, and how it travels to reach its target cell. Fig 8.4","batch":2,"stem":"A 42-year-old man in a physiology study has electrical stimulation of the vagus nerve, and his serum gastrin rises even after atropine. The mediator is a peptide released by an action potential. How is this mediator classified?","choices":{"A":"Circulating hormone","B":"Paracrine peptide","C":"Neurocrine peptide","D":"Autocrine signal","E":"Luminal secretion"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Gastrin-releasing peptide is released by postganglionic vagal and enteric neurons onto G cells after an action potential, so it is a neurocrine; it is not cholinergic, which is why atropine does not block it. Circulating hormones travel in the blood. Paracrines are released by endocrine cells and diffuse locally. Autocrine signals act on the releasing cell. Luminal secretions enter the gut lumen. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 1, neurocrines).","boardPrep":"Step 1: vagal stimulation increases gastrin through GRP (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: GRP is also called bombesin.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 4 — vagovagal reflexes and gastric secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q22","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — For each of the 4 GI hormones: explain where each is secreted from, what triggers its release, and its main action(s). Table 8.2","batch":2,"stem":"A 43-year-old man has a study in which acidic chyme with a pH of 4 is infused into his duodenum. His pancreatic juice volume and bicarbonate concentration rise. Which hormone is responsible?","choices":{"A":"Gastrin","B":"Motilin","C":"Ghrelin","D":"Secretin","E":"Somatostatin"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Acid below pH 4.5 in the duodenum releases secretin from S cells, which stimulates pancreatic ductal and biliary bicarbonate secretion to neutralize the acid. Gastrin release is inhibited by acid. Motilin is released during fasting and drives migrating motor complexes. Ghrelin increases appetite. Somatostatin is also released by acid but inhibits pancreatic secretion. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 2, secretin); Costanzo Ch. 8 (secretin, motilin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secretin increases pancreatic HCO3- and decreases gastric acid (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: secretin is secreted by S cells in the duodenal mucosa (lecture SDL 5).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — secretin and duodenal acid","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Somatostatin is also released "}},{"id":"sdl05_q23","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — For each of the 4 GI hormones: explain where each is secreted from, what triggers its release, and its main action(s). Table 8.2","batch":2,"stem":"A 54-year-old man has hepatobiliary scintigraphy and receives an analog of the duodenal hormone that contracts the gallbladder. Which additional effect does this hormone produce?","choices":{"A":"Faster gastric emptying","B":"Increased gastric acid","C":"Inhibition of enzyme release","D":"Contraction of Oddi sphincter","E":"Slower gastric emptying"},"correct":"E","explanation":"CCK contracts the gallbladder, relaxes the sphincter of Oddi, stimulates pancreatic enzyme secretion, and delays gastric emptying so that fat enters the duodenum slowly. Faster emptying is the opposite effect. Increased gastric acid is an action of gastrin. CCK relaxes rather than contracts the sphincter of Oddi. CCK stimulates, rather than inhibits, enzyme release. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 2, cholecystokinin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CCK decreases gastric emptying and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: CCK analogs are used to measure gallbladder ejection fraction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — CCK and gastric emptying","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"CCK relaxes rather than contra"}},{"id":"sdl05_q24","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — For each of the 4 GI hormones: explain where each is secreted from, what triggers its release, and its main action(s). Table 8.2","batch":2,"stem":"A 33-year-old man eats a steak dinner, and his serum gastrin rises within minutes. Which luminal component stimulates his antral G cells?","choices":{"A":"Intact dietary triglycerides","B":"Small peptides and amino acids","C":"Free fatty acids and monoglycerides","D":"Luminal acid below pH 3","E":"Glucose and galactose"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Small peptides and amino acids in the stomach, distension, and vagal GRP stimulate gastrin release. Intact triglycerides do not release gastrin. Fatty acids and monoglycerides in the duodenum release CCK. Acid below pH 3 inhibits gastrin release. Glucose stimulates GIP rather than gastrin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 2, gastrin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastrin is increased by distension, alkalinization, amino acids, peptides, and vagal GRP, and decreased by gastric acid; the lecture uses pH below 3, while First Aid gives pH below 1.5 (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: protein meals raise gastrin.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — gastric phase of acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q25","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Name the tumor responsible for Zollinger-Ellison syndrome and list its three consequences (H+ secretion, mucosal effect, ulcer location).","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old man has ulcers in the distal duodenum, chronic diarrhea, and weight loss. His fasting serum gastrin is markedly elevated while his gastric pH is low. Which tumor is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Gastrinoma","B":"Insulinoma","C":"VIPoma","D":"Somatostatinoma","E":"Glucagonoma"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Jejunal ulcers with high gastrin and low gastric pH indicate Zollinger-Ellison syndrome, caused by a gastrinoma. An insulinoma causes hypoglycemia. A VIPoma causes watery diarrhea with achlorhydria, so gastric pH would be high. A somatostatinoma is a delta cell tumor that does not raise gastrin. A glucagonoma causes diabetes and necrolytic migratory erythema. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 3, responsible tumor); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (gastrinoma and pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a gastrinoma is a gastrin secreting neuroendocrine tumor of the pancreas or duodenum (lecture SDL 5). COMLEX: gastrinomas often lie in the gastrinoma triangle.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 5 — insulinoma, gastrinoma, glucagonoma, and VIPoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"A VIPoma causes watery diarrhe","D":"A somatostatinoma is a delta c"}},{"id":"sdl05_q26","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Name the tumor responsible for Zollinger-Ellison syndrome and list its three consequences (H+ secretion, mucosal effect, ulcer location).","batch":2,"stem":"A 65-year-old man has a gastrinoma with a gastric pH near 1. In a healthy stomach, this acidity would sharply reduce gastrin release. Why does his gastrin stay high?","choices":{"A":"Somatostatin excess","B":"Parietal cell autoimmunity","C":"Loss of histamine receptors","D":"Tumor ignores pH feedback","E":"Vagal hyperactivity"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Gastrin from the tumor is not subject to negative feedback by low gastric pH, so acid secretion becomes massive and autonomous. Somatostatin excess would lower gastrin. Parietal cell autoimmunity raises gastrin by removing acid, which is the opposite pH. Loss of histamine receptors would reduce acid. Vagal hyperactivity increases gastrin through GRP but would still be subject to acid feedback. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 3, H+ secretion effect).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastrin is decreased by gastric acid in normal physiology (lecture pH below 3; First Aid pH below 1.5) and increased in Zollinger-Ellison syndrome (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: treatment uses proton pump inhibitors and tumor resection.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — feedback control of acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q27","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Name the tumor responsible for Zollinger-Ellison syndrome and list its three consequences (H+ secretion, mucosal effect, ulcer location).","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old man has recurrent duodenal ulcers despite treatment. Endoscopy shows prominent thickened folds in the gastric body, and his serum gastrin is markedly elevated. Which mechanism explains the thickened folds?","choices":{"A":"Gastrin induced edema","B":"Trophic effect of gastrin","C":"Lymphocytic infiltration","D":"Foveolar mucous hyperplasia","E":"Submucosal fibrosis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Chronically elevated gastrin from a gastrinoma has a trophic effect that causes hypertrophy and hyperplasia of the oxyntic mucosa, including parietal cell hyperplasia, which thickens the folds. Gastrin acts by growth of the mucosa, not by edema. Lymphocytic infiltration is a feature of gastritis or lymphoma. Foveolar mucous hyperplasia without high gastrin is Ménétrier disease. Submucosal fibrosis follows ulcer healing, not gastrin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 3, mucosal effect); Costanzo Ch. 8 (trophic effect of gastrin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastrin stimulates trophic growth of the gastric oxyntic mucosa (lecture SDL 5). COMLEX: refractory ulcers with thick folds warrant a fasting gastrin level.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q28","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand which route of glucose administration (oral vs. IV) causes more insulin release for the same blood glucose level, and name the hormone responsible.","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old man in a fasting study has a normal plasma glucose. He receives an intravenous infusion of GIP with no glucose. How does his insulin secretion respond?","choices":{"A":"Large rise causing hypoglycemia","B":"Fall below the fasting level","C":"Rise matching an oral glucose load","D":"Little change from fasting level","E":"Delayed rise after several hours"},"correct":"D","explanation":"GIP stimulates insulin secretion from beta cells in a glucose-dependent manner: it amplifies glucose-induced insulin release, so with normal fasting glucose and no glucose load, insulin changes little. A large rise with hypoglycemia would require insulin release independent of glucose. GIP does not suppress insulin below fasting levels. A rise matching an oral glucose load needs both the glucose stimulus and the incretins. A delayed rise after several hours is not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 2, GIP stimulates insulin in a glucose-dependent manner, p29; section 4, incretins amplify glucose-induced insulin secretion, p29).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GIP is the only GI hormone released by glucose, amino acids, and fatty acids (lecture SDL 5). COMLEX: GIP and GLP-1 act through G-protein coupled receptors on beta cells (lecture SDL 5).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — GIP release and actions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q29","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand which route of glucose administration (oral vs. IV) causes more insulin release for the same blood glucose level, and name the hormone responsible.","batch":2,"stem":"A 51-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes starts a weekly injected analog of the incretin secreted by ileal L cells. Besides improving insulin secretion, which effect helps her lose weight?","choices":{"A":"Increased ghrelin","B":"Increased glucagon release","C":"Reduced appetite","D":"Faster gastric emptying","E":"Lower leptin levels"},"correct":"C","explanation":"GLP-1 analogs amplify glucose-dependent insulin release, slow gastric emptying, and are anorexigenic, reducing appetite. Increased ghrelin would promote weight gain. GLP-1 inhibits rather than increases glucagon release. GLP-1 slows rather than speeds gastric emptying. Lower leptin would increase appetite. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 4 and 6, GLP-1); Costanzo Ch. 8 (GLP-1).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GLP-1 analogs treat type 2 diabetes and promote weight loss (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: nausea from slowed gastric emptying is a common adverse effect.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — hormones that slow gastric emptying","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q30","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand which route of glucose administration (oral vs. IV) causes more insulin release for the same blood glucose level, and name the hormone responsible.","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman eats a mixed meal, and her duodenal K cells release a hormone that amplifies her insulin secretion. Which other action does this hormone have?","choices":{"A":"Stimulates gastric acid","B":"Contracts the gallbladder","C":"Speeds gastric emptying","D":"Increases appetite","E":"Inhibits gastric acid"},"correct":"E","explanation":"GIP from K cells stimulates insulin release and also inhibits gastric H+ secretion, inhibits gastrin, and slows gastric motility. Stimulation of acid is an action of gastrin. Gallbladder contraction is an action of CCK. GIP slows rather than speeds gastric emptying. GIP is listed as anorexigenic, so it does not increase appetite. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 2, GIP, and section 6, anorexigenic hormones); Costanzo Ch. 8 (GIP).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GIP decreases gastric H+ secretion and increases insulin release (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: GIP was first named gastric inhibitory peptide.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — inhibitors of acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q31","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain what somatostatin does to gastrin secretion, and what histamine does to parietal cell acid secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 56-year-old woman with acromegaly starts octreotide, an analog of a D cell paracrine. Her serum gastrin falls. Which mechanism explains the fall?","choices":{"A":"Inhibition of G cells","B":"Inhibition of parietal cells","C":"Blockade of H2 receptors","D":"Rise in gastric pH","E":"Loss of vagal GRP"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Octreotide mimics somatostatin, which binds receptors on G cells and suppresses gastrin release. Inhibition of parietal cells lowers acid but would raise, not lower, gastrin through loss of acid feedback. Blockade of H2 receptors is the action of H2 antagonists. A rise in gastric pH would increase gastrin. Octreotide does not remove vagal GRP. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 5, somatostatin effect on gastrin secretion).","boardPrep":"Step 1: octreotide is a somatostatin analog used for acromegaly, carcinoid syndrome, VIPoma, and variceal bleeding (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: octreotide can cause gallstones.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — somatostatin","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q32","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain what somatostatin does to gastrin secretion, and what histamine does to parietal cell acid secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old woman takes a drug that blocks histamine receptors on parietal cells, and her acid secretion falls. Which second messenger falls in her parietal cells?","choices":{"A":"IP3 and calcium","B":"Cyclic GMP","C":"Diacylglycerol","D":"Nitric oxide","E":"Cyclic AMP"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Histamine binds H2 receptors, activating Gs and adenylyl cyclase to raise cyclic AMP and protein kinase A activity, which drives the H+/K+-ATPase; blocking H2 receptors lowers cyclic AMP. IP3 and calcium mediate ACh and gastrin. Cyclic GMP and nitric oxide mediate smooth muscle relaxation. Diacylglycerol accompanies IP3 in the calcium pathway. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 5, histamine effect).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H2 receptors use a Gs cascade to increase H+/K+-ATPase activity (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acid secretion pathways). COMLEX: H2 blockers reduce nocturnal acid secretion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — H2 receptor antagonists","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"IP3 and calcium mediate ACh an"}},{"id":"sdl05_q33","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain what somatostatin does to gastrin secretion, and what histamine does to parietal cell acid secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 23-year-old man has antral H. pylori gastritis and a duodenal ulcer. His serum gastrin is elevated and his gastric acid output is high. Loss or suppression of which gastric cell best explains these findings?","choices":{"A":"G cells","B":"Parietal cells","C":"D cells","D":"ECL cells","E":"Chief cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Antral gastritis causes loss or suppression of D cell somatostatin (Bootcamp describes D cell destruction; Costanzo describes inhibited somatostatin secretion), which removes inhibition of G cells and parietal cells and raises gastrin and acid. Loss of G cells would lower gastrin. Loss of parietal cells would lower acid. Loss of ECL cells would lower histamine and acid. Chief cells secrete pepsinogen and do not control gastrin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 5, somatostatin); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (destruction of antral D cells lowers somatostatin and raises acid).","boardPrep":"Step 1: duodenal ulcers are associated with H. pylori and gastrinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, peptic ulcer disease). COMLEX: eradicating H. pylori lowers ulcer recurrence.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — H. pylori in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Loss of G cells would lower ga","B":"Loss of parietal cells would l"}},{"id":"sdl05_q34","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — List which hormones/peptides increase appetite and which decrease it, then explain the orexigenic and anorexigenic pathways. Table 8.3, Fig 8.7","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-year-old boy has severe hyperphagia and obesity, and his serum leptin is undetectable because of a gene mutation. Which change in his arcuate nucleus explains his hunger?","choices":{"A":"Less POMC neuron activity","B":"Less AGRP neuron activity","C":"Less NPY neuron activity","D":"More MC4R activation","E":"Lower ghrelin secretion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Leptin activates POMC and CART neurons and inhibits NPY and AGRP neurons. Without leptin, POMC activity falls, alpha-MSH no longer activates MC4R, and NPY and AGRP activity rises, increasing appetite. Less AGRP activity, less NPY activity, and more MC4R activation would reduce appetite. Lower ghrelin would also reduce hunger. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 6, anorexigenic pathway); Costanzo Ch. 8 (leptin stimulates the anorexigenic pathway).","boardPrep":"Step 1: leptin activates POMC and CART neurons while inhibiting NPY and AGRP neurons (lecture SDL 5). COMLEX: leptin acts on the arcuate nucleus.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 1 — obesity and metabolic syndrome as the drivers of MASLD","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Less AGRP activity, less NPY a","D":"Less AGRP activity, less NPY a"}},{"id":"sdl05_q35","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — List which hormones/peptides increase appetite and which decrease it, then explain the orexigenic and anorexigenic pathways. Table 8.3, Fig 8.7","batch":2,"stem":"A 12-year-old girl with Prader-Willi syndrome has constant hunger, and her plasma ghrelin is markedly elevated. Which arcuate neurons does ghrelin activate to increase her food intake?","choices":{"A":"POMC and CART neurons","B":"NPY and AGRP neurons","C":"Lateral hypothalamic neurons","D":"Paraventricular MC4R neurons","E":"Brainstem NTS neurons"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ghrelin stimulates NPY and AGRP neurons in the arcuate nucleus; NPY activates the lateral hypothalamus and AGRP blocks MC4R in the paraventricular nucleus. POMC and CART neurons are anorexigenic and are activated by leptin and insulin. The lateral hypothalamus and paraventricular nucleus are downstream targets, not arcuate neurons. The NTS receives MC4R projections in the satiety pathway. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 6, orexigenic pathway).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ghrelin is increased in Prader-Willi syndrome (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: ghrelin rises with fasting and sleep deprivation (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, stomach).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — gastric enteroendocrine cells","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"The lateral hypothalamus and p"}},{"id":"sdl05_q36","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — List which hormones/peptides increase appetite and which decrease it, then explain the orexigenic and anorexigenic pathways. Table 8.3, Fig 8.7","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old woman reports much less hunger after a gastric bypass operation. Which hormonal change most likely contributes?","choices":{"A":"Higher orexin release","B":"Lower PYY levels","C":"Higher NPY release","D":"Reduced leptin action","E":"Lower ghrelin levels"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Ghrelin is decreased after gastric bypass, reducing stimulation of NPY and AGRP neurons and lowering hunger. Higher orexin release, higher NPY release, and reduced leptin action would increase hunger, because orexins and NPY are orexigenic. PYY is anorexigenic, so lower PYY would also increase hunger. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 6, appetite hormones); First Aid 2025 GI (ghrelin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ghrelin is decreased after gastric bypass surgery (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: PYY decreases appetite by inhibiting ghrelin secretion (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — gastric enteroendocrine cells","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl05_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — For each of the three categories- hormone, paracrine, neurocrine- list examples of each, where each is released from, and how it travels to reach its target cell. Fig 8.4","batch":3,"stem":"A 63-year-old woman has a tumor of the cells that secrete the GI paracrine somatostatin. Besides the GI mucosa and hypothalamus, where do these cells normally reside?","choices":{"A":"Pancreatic islets","B":"Adrenal medulla","C":"Anterior pituitary","D":"Thyroid follicles","E":"Renal cortex"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Somatostatin is secreted by D cells throughout the GI mucosa, by delta cells of the pancreatic islets, and by the hypothalamus. The adrenal medulla secretes catecholamines. The anterior pituitary is a target of hypothalamic somatostatin, which inhibits growth hormone, rather than a source. Thyroid follicles make thyroid hormone. The renal cortex is not a somatostatin source. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 1, paracrines).","boardPrep":"Step 1: somatostatinomas arise from pancreatic delta cells (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors). COMLEX: somatostatin inhibits growth hormone release from the anterior pituitary.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — gastric enteroendocrine cells","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl05_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — For each of the 4 GI hormones: explain where each is secreted from, what triggers its release, and its main action(s). Table 8.2","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman in a pancreatic function study receives secretin, and her bicarbonate output rises modestly. Adding CCK raises bicarbonate output much further. Which action of CCK explains this?","choices":{"A":"Stimulation of gastrin release","B":"Gallbladder contraction","C":"Potentiation of secretin","D":"Inhibition of secretin release","E":"Stimulation of ghrelin release"},"correct":"C","explanation":"CCK potentiates secretin-induced pancreatic bicarbonate secretion, so the two together produce more bicarbonate than secretin by itself. CCK does not stimulate gastrin release or inhibit secretin release. CCK is anorexigenic, so it does not stimulate ghrelin. Gallbladder contraction is a real CCK action, but it does not raise pancreatic bicarbonate output. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 2, cholecystokinin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CCK increases pancreatic secretion and gallbladder contraction (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: secretin mainly raises pancreatic bicarbonate while CCK mainly raises enzyme output (lecture SDL 5).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — CCK and secretin","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl05_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Name the tumor responsible for Zollinger-Ellison syndrome and list its three consequences (H+ secretion, mucosal effect, ulcer location).","batch":3,"stem":"A 30-year-old man has hypercalcemia from parathyroid hyperplasia and peptic ulcers that do not heal with treatment. His fasting gastrin is high and his gastric pH is low. Which test best confirms the suspected tumor?","choices":{"A":"Oral glucose tolerance test","B":"Urea breath test","C":"Serum glucagon level","D":"Secretin stimulation test","E":"Dexamethasone suppression"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Refractory ulcers with high gastrin, low gastric pH, and MEN1 features suggest a gastrinoma, and the secretin stimulation test follows fasting gastrin and gastric pH in the workup. An oral glucose tolerance test evaluates diabetes. A urea breath test detects H. pylori. A serum glucagon level is used for glucagonoma. Dexamethasone suppression evaluates Cushing syndrome. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 3, Zollinger-Ellison syndrome); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (gastrinoma diagnostics: fasting serum gastrin and gastric pH, then secretin stimulation test).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Zollinger Ellison syndrome is caused by a gastrin secreting neuroendocrine tumor in the pancreas or duodenum (lecture SDL 7). COMLEX: screen for MEN1 in a patient with gastrinoma and hypercalcemia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 5 — pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl05_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand which route of glucose administration (oral vs. IV) causes more insulin release for the same blood glucose level, and name the hormone responsible.","batch":3,"stem":"In a 74-year-old man, oral glucose releases gut incretins that amplify insulin secretion. Through which receptor type do these incretins act on his pancreatic beta cells?","choices":{"A":"G protein coupled receptors","B":"Receptor tyrosine kinases","C":"Ligand gated ion channels","D":"Nuclear hormone receptors","E":"JAK STAT cytokine receptors"},"correct":"A","explanation":"GIP and GLP-1 bind G protein coupled receptors on beta cells to amplify glucose-induced insulin secretion. Receptor tyrosine kinases mediate insulin action on target tissues. Ligand gated ion channels mediate fast synaptic transmission. Nuclear hormone receptors bind steroid and thyroid hormones. JAK STAT receptors bind cytokines and growth hormone. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 4, incretin action).","boardPrep":"Step 1: oral glucose releases GIP and GLP 1 from intestinal K and L cells (lecture SDL 5). COMLEX: incretins amplify insulin release in a glucose-dependent manner.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — GIP","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl05_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain what somatostatin does to gastrin secretion, and what histamine does to parietal cell acid secretion.","batch":3,"stem":"A 22-year-old woman takes famotidine, and her acid response to a protein meal falls. Which signals normally stimulate release of the paracrine whose action this drug opposes?","choices":{"A":"Secretin and GIP","B":"Gastrin and ACh","C":"Somatostatin and PGE2","D":"Leptin and insulin","E":"VIP and nitric oxide"},"correct":"B","explanation":"ECL cells release histamine in response to gastrin and ACh, and histamine then binds H2 receptors on parietal cells and potentiates the direct effects of ACh and gastrin. Secretin and GIP inhibit acid secretion. Somatostatin and prostaglandins inhibit acid secretion. Leptin and insulin are anorexigenic signals. VIP and nitric oxide relax GI smooth muscle. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 5, histamine effect on parietal cell acid secretion).","boardPrep":"Step 1: acid secretion is stimulated by ACh, gastrin, and histamine and inhibited by somatostatin, secretin, prostaglandins, and GIP (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, parietal cells). COMLEX: H2 blockers blunt acid responses to meals.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — regulation of gastric acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl05_q06","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — List which hormones/peptides increase appetite and which decrease it, then explain the orexigenic and anorexigenic pathways. Table 8.3, Fig 8.7","batch":3,"stem":"A 16-year-old boy has early onset obesity and hyperphagia from a loss of function mutation in melanocortin-4 receptors. Besides increased appetite, which change is expected?","choices":{"A":"Higher sympathetic tone","B":"Lower ghrelin secretion","C":"Higher leptin sensitivity","D":"Faster gastric emptying","E":"Lower energy expenditure"},"correct":"E","explanation":"MC4R activation in the paraventricular nucleus decreases food intake and increases energy expenditure through projections to the NTS and increased sympathetic activity. Loss of MC4R function therefore lowers energy expenditure and sympathetic tone. Lower ghrelin would reduce appetite. MC4R loss does not increase leptin sensitivity, since leptin acts upstream of MC4R. Gastric emptying is not an MC4R effect in the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 6, anorexigenic pathway); Costanzo Ch. 8 (ghrelin from the gastric fundus is orexigenic and stimulates NPY and AGRP neurons (lecture SDL 5).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ghrelin from the gastric fundus is orexigenic and stimulates NPY and AGRP neurons (lecture SDL 5). COMLEX: alpha-MSH acts at MC4R to promote satiety.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 1 — obesity and metabolic syndrome as the drivers of MASLD","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Loss of MC4R function therefor"}},{"id":"grid_sdl05_q01","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain what somatostatin does to gastrin secretion, and what histamine does to parietal cell acid secretion.","batch":4,"stem":"A 67-year-old woman has new diabetes and gallstones and no prior abdominal surgery. CT shows a mass in the pancreas, and she has lost weight. Which of the following sets of laboratory findings is most likely in this patient?","grid":{"headers":["Serum gastrin","Gastric acid","Fecal fat"]},"choices":{"A":["↑","↑","↑"],"B":["↓","↓","↑"],"C":["↓","↓","↔"],"D":["↔","↔","↔"],"E":["↑","↓","↔"]},"correct":"B","explanation":"A somatostatinoma releases excess somatostatin from tumor delta cells, so the pattern (↓ serum gastrin, ↓ gastric acid, ↑ fecal fat) is correct. Somatostatin suppresses gastrin release from G cells and inhibits parietal cell acid secretion, causing hypochlorhydria, and it inhibits pancreatic secretion, causing steatorrhea; the diabetes and gallstones reflect inhibition of insulin release and gallbladder contraction. The pattern (↑ serum gastrin, ↑ gastric acid, ↑ fecal fat) is a gastrinoma, which raises gastrin and acid, and the acid entering the duodenum inactivates pancreatic lipase and causes steatorrhea. The pattern (↓ serum gastrin, ↓ gastric acid, ↔ fecal fat) follows resection of the gastric antrum, which removes the G cells so gastrin and acid fall. The pattern (↔ serum gastrin, ↔ gastric acid, ↔ fecal fat) is a glucagonoma, which causes diabetes, weight loss, and necrolytic migratory erythema without changing gastric secretion. The pattern (↑ serum gastrin, ↓ gastric acid, ↔ fecal fat) is autoimmune atrophic gastritis, in which loss of parietal cells lowers acid and removes feedback on G cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 5, somatostatin inhibits gastrin secretion; section 3, Zollinger-Ellison syndrome); Costanzo Ch. 8 (antral resection decreases gastrin and H+ secretion; acid inactivates pancreatic lipase in Zollinger-Ellison syndrome); Robbins (SDL 19 pathology text: somatostatinomas cause diabetes, cholelithiasis, steatorrhea, and hypochlorhydria); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors: gastrinoma steatorrhea, glucagonoma, and somatostatinoma); First Aid 2025 GI (somatostatin inhibits gastric acid, pancreatic secretion, gallbladder contraction, and insulin release). Rows C, D, and E list ↔ for analytes their sources do not describe as changed.","boardPrep":"Step 1: somatostatin inhibits gastric acid, pancreatic secretion, gallbladder contraction, and insulin and glucagon release (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: octreotide, a somatostatin analog, can cause gallstones and steatorrhea.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — regulation of gastric acid secretion","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":6,"title":"SDL 6 — Physiology - GI Motility: Chewing, Swallowing, Esophageal and Gastric Motility","questions":[{"id":"sdl06_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the neural and muscular mechanisms that coordinate the three stages of swallowing.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which sensory receptors detect the bolus and initiate the involuntary swallowing reflex?","choices":{"A":"Gastric stretch receptors","B":"Carotid sinus baroreceptors","C":"Laryngeal chemoreceptors","D":"Esophageal pain receptors","E":"Tonsillar pillar receptors"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Tactile receptors around the pharyngeal opening, especially on the tonsillar pillars, detect the bolus pushed back by the tongue and start the automatic swallowing reflex. Gastric stretch receptors drive gastric reflexes. Carotid sinus baroreceptors sense blood pressure. Laryngeal chemoreceptors trigger cough, not swallowing. Esophageal pain receptors do not start swallowing. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 1, voluntary stage); Costanzo Ch. 8 (oral phase).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the oral phase of swallowing is voluntary and the pharyngeal and esophageal phases are reflexes (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: oropharyngeal dysphagia points to neural or striated muscle disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 4 — striated and smooth muscle of the esophagus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the neural and muscular mechanisms that coordinate the three stages of swallowing.","batch":1,"stem":"Which cranial nerves carry the afferent limb of the swallowing reflex from the pharynx to the medulla?","choices":{"A":"CN VII and CN XII","B":"CN X and CN XI","C":"CN III and CN IV","D":"CN V and CN IX","E":"CN VI and CN XII"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Sensory impulses from pharyngeal tactile receptors travel in the trigeminal (CN V) and glossopharyngeal (CN IX) nerves to the tractus solitarius in the medullary swallowing center. The other nerve pairs are not the afferent limb in the lecture. The lecture gives the pharyngeal afferents as the trigeminal (CN V) and glossopharyngeal (CN IX) nerves, and Costanzo gives the vagus and glossopharyngeal nerves; neither names CN VII or CN XII. CN XI is not named by either source: the lecture gives CN V and CN IX as the pharyngeal afferents, and Costanzo gives the glossopharyngeal and vagus nerves. Neither the lecture (CN V and IX) nor Costanzo (glossopharyngeal and vagus) names CN III and CN IV as the afferent limb of swallowing. Neither the lecture (CN V and IX) nor Costanzo (glossopharyngeal and vagus) names CN VI and CN XII as the afferent limb of swallowing. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 1, involuntary pharyngeal stage).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the swallowing center lies in the medulla (lecture SDL 6). COMLEX: brainstem strokes impair swallowing.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 4 — striated and smooth muscle of the esophagus","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The lecture gives the pharyngeal afferents as the trigeminal","B":"CN XI is not named by either source: the lecture gives CN V ","C":"Neither the lecture (CN V and IX) nor Costanzo (glossopharyngeal and vagus) names CN III and CN IV a","E":"Neither the lecture (CN V and IX) nor Costanzo (glossopharyngeal and vagus) names CN VI and CN XII a"}},{"id":"sdl06_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the neural and muscular mechanisms that coordinate the three stages of swallowing.","batch":1,"stem":"During the pharyngeal stage of swallowing, which event prevents food from refluxing into the nasal cavity?","choices":{"A":"Epiglottis tilting back","B":"Soft palate elevation","C":"Vocal cord closure","D":"Upper sphincter relaxation","E":"Tongue base retraction"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The soft palate elevates to close the posterior nares and prevent reflux into the nasal cavity. The epiglottis swings back and the vocal cords approximate to protect the airway, not the nose. Relaxation of the upper esophageal sphincter opens the esophagus. Tongue movement pushes the bolus back in the voluntary stage. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 1, pharyngeal stage); Costanzo Ch. 8 (pharyngeal phase).","boardPrep":"Step 1: breathing is inhibited during the pharyngeal phase (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: nasal regurgitation suggests palatal weakness.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 4 — striated and smooth muscle of the esophagus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate between primary and secondary esophageal peristalsis, including their initiation and functional roles.","batch":1,"stem":"Primary esophageal peristalsis is initiated by which event?","choices":{"A":"Esophageal distension","B":"Refluxed gastric acid","C":"Gastric receptive relaxation","D":"Duodenal fat delivery","E":"A voluntary swallow"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Primary peristalsis begins with the voluntary act of swallowing as a continuation of the pharyngeal peristaltic wave, coordinated by the medullary swallowing center. Esophageal distension and refluxed gastric contents initiate secondary peristalsis. Receptive relaxation is a gastric response to the bolus. Duodenal fat slows gastric emptying. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 2, primary esophageal peristalsis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the LES opens and the orad stomach relaxes as the primary wave travels down (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: primary waves propel the swallowed bolus.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 4 — vagovagal reflexes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl06_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate between primary and secondary esophageal peristalsis, including their initiation and functional roles.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, how long does primary peristalsis take to carry a bolus through the esophagus in an upright person?","choices":{"A":"5 to 8 seconds","B":"8 to 10 seconds","C":"1 to 2 seconds","D":"20 to 30 seconds","E":"60 to 90 seconds"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Transit takes about 5 to 8 seconds upright because gravity assists the wave. Lying down or stationary, it takes about 8 to 10 seconds. The pharyngeal stage, not esophageal transit, lasts under 2 seconds. Twenty seconds or more is far longer than normal transit. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 2, primary esophageal peristalsis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gravity accelerates esophageal transit when sitting or standing (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: delayed transit suggests a motility disorder.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 4 — vagovagal reflexes","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"Twenty seconds or more is far ","E":"Twenty seconds or more is far "}},{"id":"sdl06_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate between primary and secondary esophageal peristalsis, including their initiation and functional roles.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, secondary esophageal peristalsis can still occur after the vagal supply is interrupted. Which mechanism allows this?","choices":{"A":"Medullary swallowing center","B":"Intrinsic myenteric circuits","C":"Somatic motor reflex arcs","D":"Sympathetic chain ganglia input","E":"Voluntary cortical input"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Intrinsic myenteric networks can drive secondary peristaltic waves on their own after a brief period of adaptation, even when vagal supply is interrupted. The medullary swallowing center acts through the vagus and is cut off. Somatic motor reflexes do not drive smooth muscle peristalsis. Sympathetic input inhibits motility. Voluntary cortical input starts primary peristalsis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 2, secondary esophageal peristalsis); Costanzo Ch. 8 (secondary wave is mediated by the enteric nervous system).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secondary peristalsis clears residual food and refluxed contents (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: impaired clearance prolongs acid exposure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 4 — vagovagal reflexes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl06_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the process of gastric mixing and propulsion, including the roles of peristaltic waves, retropulsion, and pyloric resistance.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, interstitial cells of Cajal in the caudad stomach generate slow waves at about what rate?","choices":{"A":"12 per minute","B":"8 to 10 per minute","C":"1 per minute","D":"3 to 5 per minute","E":"20 to 30 per minute"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Interstitial cells of Cajal generate slow waves of about 3 to 5 per minute in the caudad stomach. About 12 per minute is the duodenal rate in Costanzo. Eight to 10, 1, and 20 to 30 per minute match no gastric source. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 3, basic electrical rhythm); Costanzo Ch. 8 (slow wave frequency).","boardPrep":"Step 1: slow waves are not action potentials; action potentials on top of them cause phasic contractions (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: gastric pacing disorders slow emptying.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 1 — muscle layers of the gut wall","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Eight to 10, 1, and 20 to 30 p"}},{"id":"sdl06_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the process of gastric mixing and propulsion, including the roles of peristaltic waves, retropulsion, and pyloric resistance.","batch":1,"stem":"What does the term retropulsion describe in gastric motility?","choices":{"A":"Antral contents jetting backward","B":"Chyme entering the duodenum","C":"Bile refluxing into the stomach","D":"Fundic receptive relaxation","E":"Duodenal acid inhibiting emptying"},"correct":"A","explanation":"As a peristaltic ring nears the closed pylorus, most antral contents are squirted backward through the ring toward the body, mixing them with acid. Chyme entering the duodenum is emptying. Bile reflux is retrograde duodenal flow. Receptive relaxation is fundic accommodation. Duodenal acid acts through enterogastric feedback. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 3, retropulsion and mechanical grinding).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the caudad stomach mixes and grinds, and the orad stomach receives the bolus (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: solids leave the stomach more slowly than liquids.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 1 — muscle layers of the gut wall","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Duodenal acid acts through ent"}},{"id":"sdl06_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the process of gastric mixing and propulsion, including the roles of peristaltic waves, retropulsion, and pyloric resistance.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, how does the circular muscle wall of the pylorus compare with the antral wall before it?","choices":{"A":"About the same thickness","B":"10 to 20 percent thicker","C":"50 to 100 percent thicker","D":"Two to three times thinner","E":"Five times thicker"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The pyloric sphincter is 50% to 100% thicker than the preceding antral wall. Equal thickness, 10 to 20 percent, thinner walls, and five times thicker do not match the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 3, pyloric resistance).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypertrophic pyloric stenosis is hypertrophy of this circular muscle (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: hypertrophic pyloric stenosis presents with nonbilious projectile vomiting at about 2 to 6 weeks of age (First Aid 2025 GI; Robbins and the lecture give the third to sixth weeks).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 1 — muscle layers of the gut wall","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the roles of enterogastric reflexes, CCK, secretin, and GIP, and how duodenal stimuli (e.g., fat, acid, osmolality) modulate pyloric function.","batch":1,"stem":"CCK is the most potent hormonal inhibitor of gastric emptying. Besides slowing antral propulsion, which effect does it produce?","choices":{"A":"Increased antral propulsion","B":"Faster fundic contraction","C":"Relaxed pyloric sphincter","D":"Increased gastric acid","E":"Increased pyloric tone"},"correct":"E","explanation":"CCK slows antral propulsion and increases pyloric sphincter tone, giving time for bile emulsification and pancreatic digestion of fat. Increased antral propulsion and faster fundic contraction would speed emptying. A relaxed pyloric sphincter would let chyme pass faster. CCK does not act by raising gastric acid. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 4, hormonal feedback); Costanzo Ch. 8 (CCK inhibits gastric emptying).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CCK decreases gastric emptying (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: fatty meals stay in the stomach longer.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — the four GI hormones","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Increased antral propulsion an"}},{"id":"sdl06_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the roles of enterogastric reflexes, CCK, secretin, and GIP, and how duodenal stimuli (e.g., fat, acid, osmolality) modulate pyloric function.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, how quickly do enterogastric reflexes act after duodenal stimulation?","choices":{"A":"Within 5 minutes","B":"Within 2 hours","C":"Within 30 seconds","D":"Within 30 minutes","E":"Within 24 hours"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Neural enterogastric reflexes from the duodenal wall act within 30 seconds through myenteric, sympathetic, and vagal routes. Five minutes, 30 minutes, and longer intervals are slower than these neural reflexes. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 4, enterogastric reflexes).","boardPrep":"Step 1: duodenal feedback keeps chyme delivery within intestinal digestive and buffering capacity (lecture SDL 6). COMLEX: rapid emptying overwhelms the duodenum.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — the four GI hormones","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Five minutes, 30 minutes, and ","E":"Five minutes, 30 minutes, and "}},{"id":"sdl06_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the roles of enterogastric reflexes, CCK, secretin, and GIP, and how duodenal stimuli (e.g., fat, acid, osmolality) modulate pyloric function.","batch":1,"stem":"Acid that lowers duodenal pH below 4.5 releases a hormone that inhibits gastric motility and acid secretion. Which hormone and source is this?","choices":{"A":"Serotonin from EC cells","B":"GIP from K cells","C":"Gastrin from G cells","D":"Secretin from S cells","E":"Motilin from M cells"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Duodenal S cells release secretin in response to acid below pH 4.5; secretin inhibits gastrin release, gastric acid secretion, and gastric motility until bicarbonate neutralizes the chyme. Serotonin from enterochromaffin cells activates enteric sensory neurons rather than acting as this duodenal hormone. GIP is released by fat, amino acids, and carbohydrate. Gastrin stimulates acid. Motilin is released during fasting and drives interdigestive motility. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 4, hormonal feedback); Costanzo Ch. 8 (motilin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GIP exerts a mild inhibitory effect on gastric motility and emptying (lecture SDL 6). COMLEX: duodenal acid slows gastric emptying.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — the four GI hormones","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Gastrin stimulates acid.","E":"Motilin is released during fas"}},{"id":"sdl06_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the mechanisms preventing gastroesophageal reflux, including the role of the lower esophageal sphincter and intra-abdominal pressure.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about what is the resting pressure of the lower esophageal sphincter?","choices":{"A":"5 mm Hg","B":"100 mm Hg","C":"60 mm Hg","D":"Minus 5 mm Hg","E":"30 mm Hg"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The resting LES is tonically contracted at about 30 mm Hg, which overcomes the negative intrathoracic pressure of the middle esophagus. Five mm Hg would be too low to prevent reflux. A negative value describes intrathoracic esophageal pressure. Values of 100 and 60 mm Hg are higher than normal resting tone. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 5, LES resting tone).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GERD is inappropriate relaxation of the LES (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, GERD). COMLEX: smoking, alcohol, and caffeine are GERD risk factors.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 3 — GERD pathogenesis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Five mm Hg would be too low to","D":"A negative value describes int"}},{"id":"sdl06_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the mechanisms preventing gastroesophageal reflux, including the role of the lower esophageal sphincter and intra-abdominal pressure.","batch":1,"stem":"Which structure provides extrinsic pinch valve reinforcement of the lower esophageal sphincter during coughing or straining?","choices":{"A":"Abdominal rectus muscles","B":"Diaphragmatic crura","C":"Upper esophageal sphincter","D":"Intercostal muscles","E":"Pyloric sphincter"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Skeletal muscle of the diaphragmatic crura surrounds the LES at the esophageal hiatus and reinforces it during inspiration, coughing, and straining. Rectus muscles raise abdominal pressure but do not encircle the LES. The upper esophageal sphincter keeps air out of the upper esophagus. Intercostal muscles move the chest wall. The pyloric sphincter controls gastric emptying. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 5, diaphragmatic crural reinforcement).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a sliding hiatal hernia moves the gastroesophageal junction into the mediastinum and raises GERD risk (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, sliding hiatal hernia). COMLEX: obesity raises intra-abdominal pressure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 3 — GERD pathogenesis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the mechanisms preventing gastroesophageal reflux, including the role of the lower esophageal sphincter and intra-abdominal pressure.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, increased intra-abdominal pressure helps prevent reflux by compressing which structure?","choices":{"A":"Distal esophageal segment","B":"Thoracic esophageal body","C":"Wall of the gastric fundus","D":"Upper esophageal sphincter","E":"Pyloric sphincter muscle"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A 1 to 2 cm segment of distal esophagus lies in the abdomen, so rising intra-abdominal pressure compresses it inward, creating a flap valve closure. The thoracic esophagus is exposed to intrathoracic pressure. Compression of the fundus would push contents upward. The upper esophageal sphincter and pylorus are not compressed by intra-abdominal pressure in this mechanism. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 5, intra-abdominal esophageal segment).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the 1 to 2 cm abdominal esophagus is compressed by intra-abdominal pressure (lecture SDL 6). COMLEX: chronically raised intra-abdominal pressure from obesity or pregnancy and hiatal hernia still promote GERD (lecture SDL 8; Bootcamp Gastroenterology, GERD).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 3 — GERD pathogenesis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"The upper esophageal sphincter"}},{"id":"sdl06_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the neural and muscular mechanisms that coordinate the three stages of swallowing.","batch":2,"stem":"A 73-year-old man has a stroke in the medulla, and liquids enter his trachea during swallowing. Failure of which pharyngeal stage event best explains the aspiration?","choices":{"A":"Soft palate elevation","B":"Lower sphincter relaxation","C":"Secondary esophageal waves","D":"Gastric receptive relaxation","E":"Airway closure by larynx"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In the pharyngeal stage the vocal cords approximate, the larynx moves up and forward, and the epiglottis covers the laryngeal opening to prevent aspiration; this sequence is coordinated by the medullary swallowing center. Soft palate elevation protects the nasal cavity. LES relaxation, secondary peristalsis, and receptive relaxation occur after the bolus leaves the pharynx. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 1, pharyngeal stage).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the swallowing center coordinates CN V, IX, X, and XII (lecture SDL 6). COMLEX: aspiration risk is high after brainstem stroke.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 4 — brainstem nuclei of vagal reflexes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"LES relaxation, secondary peri"}},{"id":"sdl06_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the neural and muscular mechanisms that coordinate the three stages of swallowing.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old man has anterior neck surgery that tethers the hyoid, and food sticks at the top of his esophagus. Which normal event opens the upper esophageal sphincter?","choices":{"A":"VIP release at the LES","B":"Laryngeal stretch","C":"Gastric receptive relaxation","D":"Secondary peristalsis","E":"Epiglottic closure"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Upward laryngeal movement stretches and relaxes the upper esophageal sphincter, opening the entrance to the esophagus. VIP release relaxes the lower, not upper, sphincter. Secondary peristalsis begins in the esophagus after the bolus enters. Receptive relaxation occurs in the orad stomach. Epiglottic closure protects the airway. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 1, pharyngeal stage); Costanzo Ch. 8 (upper esophageal sphincter relaxes in the pharyngeal phase).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the upper esophageal sphincter is striated muscle (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: impaired laryngeal elevation causes oropharyngeal dysphagia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 4 — striated muscle of the upper esophagus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the neural and muscular mechanisms that coordinate the three stages of swallowing.","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old man has a videofluoroscopic swallow study, and his breathing pauses briefly with each swallow. Which mechanism causes the pause?","choices":{"A":"Voluntary cortical breath holding","B":"Reflex vocal cord paralysis","C":"Medullary respiratory inhibition","D":"Fatigue of the phrenic nerve","E":"Nasal block by the epiglottis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Medullary signals temporarily inhibit respiration throughout the pharyngeal stage, which lasts under 2 seconds. The pause is reflex, not voluntary. The vocal cords approximate actively rather than becoming paralyzed. Phrenic nerve fatigue would not cycle with each swallow. The soft palate, not the epiglottis, closes the nasal passage. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 1, pharyngeal stage); Costanzo Ch. 8 (breathing is inhibited during the pharyngeal phase).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the pharyngeal phase is a reflex controlled in the medulla (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: impaired coordination of breathing and swallowing raises aspiration risk.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 4 — medullary nuclei","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate between primary and secondary esophageal peristalsis, including their initiation and functional roles.","batch":2,"stem":"A 75-year-old woman has nighttime reflux, and esophageal manometry records a peristaltic wave that begins in the midesophagus without a swallow. What started this wave?","choices":{"A":"A voluntary pharyngeal swallow","B":"Distension of the wall","C":"Gastric receptive relaxation","D":"Cortical motor command","E":"Duodenal acid reflex"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Secondary peristalsis is started locally by distension of the esophageal wall from residual food or refluxed gastric contents and does not require a swallow. A voluntary swallow starts primary peristalsis in the pharynx. Receptive relaxation is a gastric response. A cortical command is part of the voluntary stage. Duodenal acid acts on gastric emptying. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 2, secondary esophageal peristalsis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secondary peristalsis is mediated by the enteric nervous system and partly by vagovagal reflexes (lecture SDL 6; Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: nocturnal reflux is cleared by secondary waves.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — intrinsic enteric circuits","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl06_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate between primary and secondary esophageal peristalsis, including their initiation and functional roles.","batch":2,"stem":"A 22-year-old man swallows a large bolus that the primary wave does not fully clear. A second wave clears it without another swallow. Where does this second wave begin?","choices":{"A":"In the upper pharyngeal wall","B":"At the lower sphincter","C":"In the gastric fundus","D":"At the site of distension","E":"At the cricopharyngeus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A secondary peristaltic contraction begins at the point of distension and travels downward until the lumen is cleared. Waves starting in the pharynx are primary waves. The lower sphincter relaxes rather than starting the wave. The gastric fundus is beyond the esophagus. The cricopharyngeus is part of the upper esophageal sphincter. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (secondary peristaltic contraction begins at the point of distention); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 2, secondary peristalsis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secondary waves repeat until the lumen is cleared (lecture SDL 6). COMLEX: impaired secondary peristalsis worsens reflux esophagitis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — the peristaltic reflex","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Waves starting in the pharynx "}},{"id":"sdl06_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate between primary and secondary esophageal peristalsis, including their initiation and functional roles.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man has a 24-hour pH and manometry study. During one reflux episode, refluxed acid distends his midesophagus while he is not swallowing. Which pattern of esophageal motor activity follows?","choices":{"A":"A single wave followed by quiescence","B":"Simultaneous contraction of the whole body","C":"Retrograde waves toward the pharynx","D":"Tonic contraction of the esophageal body","E":"Repeated waves until the lumen clears"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Distension from refluxed contents starts secondary peristalsis without a swallow, and secondary waves repeat until the esophageal lumen is fully cleared. A single wave followed by quiescence would leave residual refluxate when one wave fails to clear it. Simultaneous contraction of the whole body is not a clearing pattern. Peristaltic waves travel caudally toward the stomach, not toward the pharynx. A tonic contraction of the esophageal body would not propel the refluxate. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 2, secondary waves repeat until the lumen is cleared, p33; section 5, secondary clearance of reflux, p35).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secondary peristalsis is mediated partly by myenteric circuits and partly by vagovagal reflexes (lecture SDL 6). COMLEX: local distension during reflux triggers secondary waves that return fluid to the stomach (lecture SDL 6).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 5 — mechanisms preventing gastroesophageal reflux","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl06_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the process of gastric mixing and propulsion, including the roles of peristaltic waves, retropulsion, and pyloric resistance.","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old man has a gastric emptying study. Solid food leaves his stomach only after it has been broken into particles smaller than 1 mm. Which mechanism grinds the solids?","choices":{"A":"Fundic relaxation","B":"Duodenal segmentation","C":"Antral retropulsion","D":"Migrating complexes","E":"Pyloric relaxation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Strong antral rings push contents against a nearly closed pylorus, so most chyme is squirted back toward the body, shearing solids into particles under 1 mm. Fundic relaxation stores the meal. Duodenal segmentation mixes chyme after it leaves the stomach. Migrating motor complexes clear the fasting stomach. Pyloric relaxation would let solids leave without grinding. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 3, retropulsion and mechanical grinding).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the caudad stomach has the thickest muscle and produces the strongest contractions (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: impaired antral grinding delays solid emptying.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 1 — circular and longitudinal muscle","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the process of gastric mixing and propulsion, including the roles of peristaltic waves, retropulsion, and pyloric resistance.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman has a gastric emptying study. Liquid leaves her stomach quickly, while solid food stays until it is mixed into chyme. Which feature explains the difference?","choices":{"A":"Tonic pyloric contraction","B":"Weak tone in the fundus","C":"Faster gastric slow waves","D":"Acidic duodenal contents","E":"Absent antral peristalsis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The thick, tonically contracted pyloric sphincter lets fluids pass easily but restricts solids until they are mixed into fluid chyme. Weak fundic tone would affect storage, not particle size. Faster slow waves would increase contraction rate for both phases. Duodenal acid slows emptying of all contents. Absent antral peristalsis would slow both liquids and solids. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 3, pyloric resistance).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the pyloric sphincter is circular smooth muscle (lecture SDL 6). COMLEX: gastric emptying studies use radiolabeled solid meals.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 5 — the pyloric circular muscle","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the process of gastric mixing and propulsion, including the roles of peristaltic waves, retropulsion, and pyloric resistance.","batch":2,"stem":"A 32-year-old man has antral manometry after a meal. For about one fifth of the recording, powerful antral contractions generate 50 to 70 cm H2O of pressure. What is this pattern called?","choices":{"A":"Mixing waves","B":"Pyloric pump","C":"Mass movement","D":"Segmentation","E":"Receptive relaxation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"For about 20% of the time after a meal, gastric peristalsis turns into powerful contractions called the pyloric pump, generating 50 to 70 cm H2O and driving emptying. Mixing waves are weak constrictor waves that do not empty the stomach. Mass movements occur in the colon. Segmentation mixes small intestinal contents. Receptive relaxation lowers orad gastric pressure. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 3, basic electrical rhythm and propulsion).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the pyloric pump generates 50 to 70 cm H2O and drives emptying (lecture SDL 6). COMLEX: enterogastric reflexes inhibit the pyloric pump.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — enteric control of motility","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the roles of enterogastric reflexes, CCK, secretin, and GIP, and how duodenal stimuli (e.g., fat, acid, osmolality) modulate pyloric function.","batch":2,"stem":"A 69-year-old woman feels full for hours after fatty meals but not after meals of fruit and bread. Which response in her duodenum most slows emptying after the fatty meal?","choices":{"A":"Gastrin release by peptides","B":"GIP release by glucose","C":"CCK release by lipids","D":"Motilin release by fasting","E":"Ghrelin release by fasting"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Fatty acids and monoglycerides release CCK from I cells, and CCK is the most potent hormonal inhibitor of gastric emptying. Gastrin is released in the stomach and stimulates acid. GIP has only a mild inhibitory effect on emptying. Motilin and ghrelin rise during fasting. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 4, hormonal feedback).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CCK increases gallbladder contraction and decreases gastric emptying (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: low fat meals help patients with delayed emptying.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — CCK","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Gastrin is released in the sto"}},{"id":"sdl06_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the roles of enterogastric reflexes, CCK, secretin, and GIP, and how duodenal stimuli (e.g., fat, acid, osmolality) modulate pyloric function.","batch":2,"stem":"A 25-year-old man drinks a hyperosmolar sports drink, and it empties from his stomach more slowly than the same volume of water. Which mechanism slows emptying of the drink?","choices":{"A":"Duodenal osmoreceptor reflex","B":"Gastric acid hypersecretion","C":"Pyloric sphincter relaxation","D":"Faster antral slow waves","E":"Vagal receptive relaxation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Duodenal receptors monitor osmolality, and hypertonic chyme triggers enterogastric reflexes that inhibit the pyloric pump and increase pyloric tone. Gastric acid secretion does not depend on drink osmolality. Pyloric relaxation and faster antral waves would speed emptying. Receptive relaxation accommodates both drinks equally. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 4, duodenal stimuli and enterogastric reflexes).","boardPrep":"Step 1: duodenal distension, acid, osmolality, and fat and protein products slow gastric emptying (lecture SDL 6). COMLEX: isotonic fluids empty faster than hypertonic ones.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 4 — reflex control of motility","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Pyloric relaxation and faster "}},{"id":"sdl06_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the roles of enterogastric reflexes, CCK, secretin, and GIP, and how duodenal stimuli (e.g., fat, acid, osmolality) modulate pyloric function.","batch":2,"stem":"A 77-year-old man has a vagotomy, and duodenal distension still inhibits his antral contractions within seconds. Which routes of the enterogastric reflex remain?","choices":{"A":"Vagal efferent pathways","B":"Circulating gastrin release","C":"Pelvic splanchnic reflexes","D":"Somatic spinal reflexes","E":"Myenteric and sympathetic"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Enterogastric reflexes act through intrinsic myenteric circuits, sympathetic nerves via prevertebral ganglia, and the vagus; after vagotomy the myenteric and sympathetic routes remain. Vagal efferents are cut. Gastrin stimulates rather than inhibits gastric activity. Pelvic splanchnic fibers supply the distal colon. Somatic reflexes do not control gastric smooth muscle. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 4, enterogastric reflexes).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sympathetic activity decreases peristalsis and increases sphincter tone (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, general principles). COMLEX: vagotomy also removes receptive relaxation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — sympathetic effects on motility","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Gastrin stimulates rather than"}},{"id":"sdl06_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the mechanisms preventing gastroesophageal reflux, including the role of the lower esophageal sphincter and intra-abdominal pressure.","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old woman with a sliding hiatal hernia has daily heartburn. Her gastroesophageal junction lies above the diaphragm. Which antireflux mechanism has she lost?","choices":{"A":"Abdominal segment compression","B":"Esophageal cardiac gland mucus","C":"Secondary peristaltic clearance","D":"Upper sphincter tone","E":"Gastric receptive relaxation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"When the gastroesophageal junction moves into the mediastinum, the distal esophagus no longer lies in the abdomen, so intra-abdominal pressure no longer compresses it into a flap valve, and crural reinforcement is lost. Cardiac gland mucus and secondary peristalsis are separate clearance and buffering defenses. Upper sphincter tone keeps air out of the esophagus. Receptive relaxation is gastric accommodation. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 5, intra-abdominal segment and crural reinforcement); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (sliding hiatal hernia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sliding hiatal hernia is the most common type and increases GERD risk (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, sliding hiatal hernia). COMLEX: obesity and pregnancy are risk factors.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — sliding hiatal hernia in GERD pathogenesis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the mechanisms preventing gastroesophageal reflux, including the role of the lower esophageal sphincter and intra-abdominal pressure.","batch":2,"stem":"A 78-year-old man has heartburn and regurgitation. Manometry shows normal resting sphincter pressure, and pH monitoring shows acid exposure episodes that coincide with periods of complete sphincter relaxation without a preceding swallow. Which mechanism explains his reflux?","choices":{"A":"Excess LES resting tone","B":"Absent gastric acid","C":"Strong crural contraction","D":"Transient LES relaxations","E":"Rapid gastric emptying"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Abnormal transient relaxations of the LES permit gastric reflux even when resting tone is normal. Excess resting tone would oppose reflux and suggests achalasia. Absent acid would reduce injury. Strong crural contraction reinforces the barrier. Rapid gastric emptying would lower the volume available to reflux. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 5, LES resting tone); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (GERD is inappropriate relaxation of the LES).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GERD presents with burning retrosternal pain, regurgitation, and cough (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, GERD). COMLEX: proton pump inhibitors are first line.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 2 — reflux esophagitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the mechanisms preventing gastroesophageal reflux, including the role of the lower esophageal sphincter and intra-abdominal pressure.","batch":2,"stem":"A 21-year-old woman in a manometry study has a zone at the gastroesophageal junction with pressure higher than in both the esophagus and the stomach. Which property produces this zone?","choices":{"A":"Cholinergic mucosal secretion","B":"Tonic circular contraction","C":"Longitudinal muscle shortening","D":"Submucosal venous engorgement","E":"Constant VIP release"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The LES is circular smooth muscle at the distal esophagus that stays tonically contracted at rest, creating a pressure barrier above both intrathoracic esophageal and gastric pressure. Mucosal secretion does not generate sphincter pressure. Longitudinal shortening does not create a sphincter zone. Venous engorgement is not the mechanism. VIP relaxes the LES. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 5, LES resting tone); Costanzo Ch. 8 (LES resting pressure exceeds esophageal and gastric pressure).","boardPrep":"Step 1: LES resting pressure exceeds intrathoracic and gastric pressure (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: low LES pressure predisposes to GERD.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — esophageal motility disorders","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl06_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the neural and muscular mechanisms that coordinate the three stages of swallowing.","batch":3,"stem":"A 20-year-old man has a small stroke involving the medullary nucleus that moves his tongue, and he cannot push a chewed bolus back toward his pharynx. Which cranial nerve carries this motor output?","choices":{"A":"Facial motor nerve","B":"Hypoglossal nerve","C":"Spinal accessory nerve","D":"Phrenic nerve","E":"Abducens nerve"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The swallowing center coordinates motor output through cranial nerves V, IX, X, and XII and upper cervical nerves; the hypoglossal nerve (CN XII) moves the tongue, which rolls the bolus back against the hard palate. The facial nerve is not in the lecture list of swallowing motor nerves. The spinal accessory nerve supplies neck and shoulder muscles. The phrenic nerve supplies the diaphragm. The abducens nerve moves the eye. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 1, voluntary and pharyngeal stages).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the oral phase of swallowing is voluntary (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: tongue weakness causes oral phase dysphagia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 4 — medullary nuclei","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl06_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate between primary and secondary esophageal peristalsis, including their initiation and functional roles.","batch":3,"stem":"An 18-year-old woman has esophageal manometry during a swallow. At what point does her lower esophageal sphincter open?","choices":{"A":"Just after the UES closes","B":"After the bolus enters the stomach","C":"Before the pharyngeal stage","D":"After gastric contraction","E":"During exhalation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The LES opens just after the upper esophageal sphincter returns to resting tone, so it is open for the whole time the bolus moves down the esophagus. Opening only after the bolus enters the stomach would block transit. Opening before the pharyngeal stage would precede the swallow. Gastric contraction is not the trigger. Respiratory phase does not time LES opening. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (esophageal motility, step 3: the LES opens just after the UES returns to resting tone); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 2, primary peristalsis: relaxation ahead of the advancing bolus).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the LES stays open for the whole esophageal transit (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: failure of LES relaxation is achalasia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — vagal transmitters","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"E":"Respiratory phase does not tim"}},{"id":"trial_sdl06_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the process of gastric mixing and propulsion, including the roles of peristaltic waves, retropulsion, and pyloric resistance.","batch":3,"stem":"A 77-year-old woman has gastric manometry after a meal. Where do the weak constrictor mixing waves of her stomach begin?","choices":{"A":"Gastric fundus","B":"Pyloric sphincter","C":"Midstomach","D":"Duodenal bulb","E":"Gastric cardia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Weak peristaltic mixing waves originate in the midstomach and travel toward the antrum every 15 to 20 seconds, intensifying as they reach the thick caudad wall. The fundus is part of the thin walled orad stomach that stores food. The pyloric sphincter resists outflow. The duodenal bulb lies beyond the stomach. The cardia surrounds the gastroesophageal junction. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 3, basic electrical rhythm and propulsion).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the orad stomach receives the meal and the caudad stomach mixes and grinds it (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the caudad stomach wall is thickest.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 1 — regions of the stomach","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl06_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the roles of enterogastric reflexes, CCK, secretin, and GIP, and how duodenal stimuli (e.g., fat, acid, osmolality) modulate pyloric function.","batch":3,"stem":"An 80-year-old man has a gastric emptying study after a meal. Besides acid and fat, duodenal chyme with which property slows emptying?","choices":{"A":"Isotonic osmolality","B":"Neutral pH near 7","C":"Low fat and protein","D":"Small duodenal volume","E":"Hypotonic osmolality"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Duodenal receptors monitor distension, acidity, hypertonic or hypotonic osmolality, fat and protein products, and irritation, and each can slow emptying. Isotonic chyme, a neutral pH, low fat and protein content, and a small duodenal volume do not trigger this feedback. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 4, duodenal stimuli).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CCK, secretin, and GIP slow gastric emptying (lecture SDL 6). COMLEX: duodenal irritation also slows gastric emptying (lecture SDL 6).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — duodenal hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl06_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the mechanisms preventing gastroesophageal reflux, including the role of the lower esophageal sphincter and intra-abdominal pressure.","batch":3,"stem":"A 19-year-old man has esophageal manometry that maps his lower esophageal sphincter. About how far above the gastric junction does this sphincter zone extend?","choices":{"A":"About 10 cm","B":"About 25 cm","C":"About 0.5 cm","D":"About 3 cm","E":"About 15 cm"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The LES is the circular smooth muscle at the distal end of the esophagus, extending about 3 cm above the junction with the stomach. Ten, 15, and 25 cm would include much of the thoracic esophagus. Half a centimeter is shorter than the sphincter zone. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 5, LES resting tone).","boardPrep":"Step 1: LES pressure exceeds gastric pressure at rest (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: GERD presents with heartburn and regurgitation (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, GERD).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 2 — reflux esophagitis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Ten, 15, and 25 cm would inclu","C":"Half a centimeter is shorter t"}},{"id":"trial_sdl06_q06","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the process of gastric mixing and propulsion, including the roles of peristaltic waves, retropulsion, and pyloric resistance.","batch":3,"stem":"An 82-year-old woman has antral recordings that show contractions occurring at a fixed maximum rate. What sets this maximum rate?","choices":{"A":"Slow wave frequency","B":"Vagal firing rate","C":"Serum gastrin level","D":"Pyloric resistance","E":"Fundic muscle tone"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The frequency of slow waves generated by interstitial cells of Cajal sets the frequency of action potentials and therefore the maximum frequency of contractions. Vagal firing and gastrin change the number of action potentials and the force of each contraction, not the maximum rate. Pyloric resistance controls outflow. Fundic tone affects storage. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (frequency of slow waves); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 3, basic electrical rhythm).","boardPrep":"Step 1: neural and hormonal input changes the number of action potentials on each slow wave (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: stronger antral contractions speed gastric mixing.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — enteric pacemaking","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Vagal firing and gastrin chang"}},{"id":"grid_sdl06_q01","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the mechanisms preventing gastroesophageal reflux, including the role of the lower esophageal sphincter and intra-abdominal pressure.","batch":4,"stem":"A 73-year-old woman has Raynaud phenomenon, tight thickened skin over her fingers, heartburn, and dysphagia to both solids and liquids. Esophageal manometry is performed. Which of the following sets of manometry findings is most likely in this patient?","grid":{"headers":["LES resting pressure","LES relaxation","Distal contractions"]},"choices":{"A":["↑","↓","↓"],"B":["↔","↔","↑"],"C":["↓","↔","↓"],"D":["↓","↔","↔"],"E":["↔","↔","↔"]},"correct":"C","explanation":"Systemic sclerosis causes smooth muscle atrophy and fibrosis of the distal esophagus, so the pattern (↓ LES resting pressure, ↔ LES relaxation, ↓ distal contractions) is correct. The weak sphincter loses the positive pressure barrier that normally prevents reflux, and the weak distal smooth muscle clears refluxed acid poorly, producing heartburn and dysphagia. The pattern (↑ LES resting pressure, ↓ LES relaxation, ↓ distal contractions) is achalasia, from loss of inhibitory myenteric neurons. The pattern (↔ LES resting pressure, ↔ LES relaxation, ↑ distal contractions) is distal esophageal spasm, with uncoordinated high amplitude contractions. The pattern (↓ LES resting pressure, ↔ LES relaxation, ↔ distal contractions) is reflux from LES incompetence with intact peristalsis. The pattern (↔ LES resting pressure, ↔ LES relaxation, ↔ distal contractions) is cardiovascular dysphagia from external compression by an enlarged left atrium, which has normal manometry. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 5, LES resting tone and LES incompetence); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (esophageal disorders of motility: achalasia, distal esophageal spasm, cardiovascular dysphagia, scleroderma esophageal dysmotility; GERD); First Aid 2025 GI (achalasia). Cells shown as ↔ are values the sources do not describe as changed; LES resting pressure in row D is inferred from the lecture term incompetence.","boardPrep":"Step 1: scleroderma esophageal dysmotility shows ↓ LES resting pressure and ↓ esophageal contractions, and achalasia shows ↑ LES resting pressure with ↓ contractions (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, esophageal disorders of motility). COMLEX: scleroderma is a risk factor for GERD.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — esophageal motility disorders","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":7,"title":"SDL 7 — Physiology - Salivary and Gastric Secretion","questions":[{"id":"sdl07_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the formation and modification of saliva, including the roles of acinar cells, ductal cells, myoepithelial cells, and how salivary flow rate affects electrolyte composition.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what is the tonicity of the initial saliva secreted by acinar cells?","choices":{"A":"Hypotonic to plasma","B":"Hypertonic to plasma","C":"Varies with the diet","D":"Twice plasma osmolarity","E":"Isotonic to plasma"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Acinar cells secrete an initial saliva that is isotonic, with Na+, K+, Cl−, and HCO3− concentrations similar to plasma. Saliva becomes hypotonic later, in the ducts. It does not become hypertonic or reach twice plasma osmolarity, and diet does not set its tonicity. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 1, acinar cell secretion); Costanzo Ch. 8 (formation of saliva, step 1).","boardPrep":"Step 1: serous salivary secretions contain alpha amylase and lipase (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, salivary glands). COMLEX: the parotid gland is primarily serous.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — epithelial specializations for secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the formation and modification of saliva, including the roles of acinar cells, ductal cells, myoepithelial cells, and how salivary flow rate affects electrolyte composition.","batch":1,"stem":"Which property of salivary ductal cells allows the final saliva to become hypotonic?","choices":{"A":"Active water secretion","B":"Potassium reabsorption","C":"Rapid acinar flow","D":"Low water permeability","E":"Bicarbonate absorption"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Ductal cells absorb more NaCl than they secrete KHCO3, and because they are relatively impermeable to water, water does not follow the absorbed solute, so the saliva becomes hypotonic. Ducts do not secrete water. They secrete rather than reabsorb potassium and bicarbonate. Rapid flow makes saliva closer to isotonic. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 1, ductal cell modification); Costanzo Ch. 8 (formation of saliva, step 2).","boardPrep":"Step 1: final saliva is hypotonic with lower Na+ and Cl− and higher K+ and HCO3− than plasma (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the submandibular gland makes most of the saliva in the mouth (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, salivary glands).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — epithelial specializations for secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Ducts do not secrete water."}},{"id":"sdl07_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the formation and modification of saliva, including the roles of acinar cells, ductal cells, myoepithelial cells, and how salivary flow rate affects electrolyte composition.","batch":1,"stem":"What does contraction of salivary myoepithelial cells accomplish?","choices":{"A":"Secretes salivary amylase","B":"Expels stored saliva","C":"Reabsorbs ductal sodium","D":"Dilates gland arterioles","E":"Lowers saliva tonicity"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Myoepithelial (basket) cells lie between the basal membranes of acinar and ductal cells and the basal lamina; on neural stimulation they contract, compressing the acini and shortening the ducts to eject saliva. Acinar cells secrete amylase. Ductal cells reabsorb sodium and lower tonicity. Bradykinin from kallikrein dilates the arterioles. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 1, myoepithelial cell function); Costanzo Ch. 8 (structure of the salivary glands).","boardPrep":"Step 1: parasympathetic stimulation contracts myoepithelial cells (lecture SDL 7). COMLEX: the sublingual gland is primarily mucous (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, salivary glands).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — epithelial specializations for secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic regulation of salivary secretion, including neurotransmitters, receptors, intracellular signaling pathways, and factors that increase or decrease salivary secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which second messenger pathway mediates parasympathetic stimulation of salivary acinar cells?","choices":{"A":"IP3 and Ca2+","B":"Cyclic AMP","C":"Cyclic GMP","D":"Nitric oxide","E":"Tyrosine kinase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Postganglionic parasympathetic neurons release ACh onto muscarinic M3 receptors, which act through IP3 and intracellular Ca2+ to produce a large volume of watery, enzyme rich saliva. Cyclic AMP is the sympathetic beta receptor messenger. Cyclic GMP, nitric oxide, and tyrosine kinase are not the salivary parasympathetic pathway. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 2, parasympathetic pathway); Costanzo Ch. 8 (parasympathetic innervation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the parasympathetic input to the salivary glands travels in CN VII and CN IX (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: anticholinergic drugs cause dry mouth.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic ganglia and transmitters","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl07_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic regulation of salivary secretion, including neurotransmitters, receptors, intracellular signaling pathways, and factors that increase or decrease salivary secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"Where do sympathetic preganglionic fibers to the salivary glands synapse?","choices":{"A":"Submandibular ganglion","B":"Pterygopalatine ganglion","C":"Inferior cervical ganglion","D":"Geniculate ganglion","E":"Superior cervical ganglion"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Sympathetic preganglionic fibers arise from thoracic segments T1 to T3 and synapse in the superior cervical ganglion; postganglionic fibers release norepinephrine onto the glands. The submandibular ganglion is a parasympathetic relay for the submandibular gland. The pterygopalatine, inferior cervical, and geniculate ganglia are not the salivary sympathetic relay in the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 2, sympathetic pathway); Costanzo Ch. 8 (sympathetic innervation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: both parasympathetic and sympathetic stimulation increase saliva, and parasympathetic control is dominant (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: saliva is regulated by autonomic nerves rather than GI hormones.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic ganglia and transmitters","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl07_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic regulation of salivary secretion, including neurotransmitters, receptors, intracellular signaling pathways, and factors that increase or decrease salivary secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"Which factor decreases parasympathetic drive to the salivary glands?","choices":{"A":"Oral tactile input","B":"Dehydration","C":"Sour taste","D":"Smell of food","E":"Conditioned reflexes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Parasympathetic drive to the salivary glands is decreased by fear, sleep, and dehydration. It is increased by food, taste (especially sour stimuli), smell, tactile oral stimuli, nausea, and conditioned reflexes. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 2, parasympathetic pathway); Costanzo Ch. 8 (factors that modulate parasympathetic input).","boardPrep":"Step 1: salivary stasis from dehydration is associated with salivary duct stones (First Aid 2025 GI, sialolithiasis). COMLEX: Pavlov conditioned reflexes increase salivation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic ganglia and transmitters","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl07_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the cellular mechanisms of gastric HCl secretion by parietal cells, including the functions of carbonic anhydrase, H⁺/K⁺-ATPase, Cl⁻ channels, and the alkaline tide.","batch":1,"stem":"Which enzyme in the parietal cell cytoplasm generates the H+ that is secreted into the gastric lumen?","choices":{"A":"Adenylyl cyclase","B":"Phospholipase C","C":"Protein kinase A","D":"Carbonic anhydrase","E":"Histidine decarboxylase"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Carbonic anhydrase combines CO2 and H2O into H2CO3, which dissociates into H+ for secretion and HCO3− for absorption into the blood. Adenylyl cyclase, phospholipase C, and protein kinase A are signaling enzymes that regulate the pump. Histidine decarboxylase makes histamine. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 3, intracellular generation of H+ and HCO3−); Costanzo Ch. 8 (cellular mechanism of HCl secretion, step 1).","boardPrep":"Step 1: parietal cells secrete gastric acid and intrinsic factor (First Aid 2025 GI, gastrointestinal secretory products). COMLEX: gastric pH is normally between 1 and 2 (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 2 — parietal cells and their products","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the cellular mechanisms of gastric HCl secretion by parietal cells, including the functions of carbonic anhydrase, H⁺/K⁺-ATPase, Cl⁻ channels, and the alkaline tide.","batch":1,"stem":"Through which apical pathway does chloride enter the gastric lumen during HCl secretion?","choices":{"A":"Chloride bicarbonate exchanger","B":"Sodium chloride cotransporter","C":"Chloride ATPase pump","D":"Potassium chloride symport","E":"Passive chloride channels"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Cl− diffuses passively into the canalicular lumen through apical Cl− channels and combines with secreted H+ to form HCl. The chloride bicarbonate exchanger sits on the basolateral membrane and brings Cl− into the cell. Sodium chloride and potassium chloride cotransport and a chloride ATPase are not part of the lecture mechanism. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 3, apical active transport and Cl− channels); Costanzo Ch. 8 (cellular mechanism of HCl secretion, step 2).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the parietal cell apical membrane contains the H+/K+ ATPase and Cl− channels (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: vomiting gastric acid causes hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis (First Aid 2025 GI).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 2 — parietal cells and their products","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the cellular mechanisms of gastric HCl secretion by parietal cells, including the functions of carbonic anhydrase, H⁺/K⁺-ATPase, Cl⁻ channels, and the alkaline tide.","batch":1,"stem":"Where does the bicarbonate generated in parietal cells go during acid secretion?","choices":{"A":"Into the gastric lumen","B":"Into the canaliculus","C":"Into gastric venous blood","D":"Into parietal cell vesicles","E":"Into the mucus gel layer"},"correct":"C","explanation":"HCO3− made by carbonic anhydrase leaves across the basolateral membrane into the blood, producing the alkaline tide. It is not secreted into the lumen or canaliculus, where H+ goes. It is not stored in vesicles. Bicarbonate trapped in surface mucus comes from surface epithelial cells, not parietal cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 3, basolateral transport and the alkaline tide); Costanzo Ch. 8 (cellular mechanism of HCl secretion, step 3).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the alkaline tide raises blood pH after gastric acid secretion, such as after meals or vomiting (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: absorbed HCO3− is later secreted back into the gut in pancreatic juice (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 2 — parietal cells and their products","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of gastric acid secretion by acetylcholine, gastrin, histamine, somatostatin, and prostaglandins, including receptor signaling pathways, potentiation, and mechanisms of pharmacologic inhibition.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what explains potentiation among the gastric acid secretagogues?","choices":{"A":"Different second messengers","B":"Shared receptor binding","C":"Common G protein coupling","D":"Competitive receptor antagonism","E":"Increased gastrin clearance"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Histamine acts through cAMP while ACh and gastrin act through IP3/Ca2+, and ACh and gastrin also release histamine from ECL cells, so combined stimuli give more acid than the sum of their separate effects. Each agent has its own receptor, so they do not share binding or one G protein. Antagonism and gastrin clearance reduce rather than amplify the response. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 4, potentiation); Costanzo Ch. 8 (potentiation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: carbonic anhydrase in parietal cells supplies the H+ that the apical pump secretes (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: combined secretagogues produce more acid than expected.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 5 — somatostatin and histamine actions on acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of gastric acid secretion by acetylcholine, gastrin, histamine, somatostatin, and prostaglandins, including receptor signaling pathways, potentiation, and mechanisms of pharmacologic inhibition.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, gastrin from antral G cells binds its receptors on which two cell types in the oxyntic mucosa?","choices":{"A":"Chief and ECL cells","B":"Parietal and D cells","C":"Chief and mucous cells","D":"Parietal and ECL cells","E":"Mucous and D cells"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Gastrin binds its receptors on both parietal cells and ECL cells, so it can stimulate parietal cells directly and also act through ECL cells. Chief cells secrete pepsinogen and are not the gastrin targets named in the lecture. D cells release somatostatin, which inhibits acid. Mucous cells secrete mucus. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 4, secretagogues and receptor pathways); Costanzo Ch. 8 (gastrin acts on parietal cells and ECL cells).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastrin is released from G cells of the gastric antrum (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: PPIs are used to treat Zollinger-Ellison syndrome (First Aid 2025 GI, proton pump inhibitors).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 5 — somatostatin and histamine actions on acid secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"D cells release somatostatin, ","C":"Chief cells secrete pepsinogen"}},{"id":"sdl07_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of gastric acid secretion by acetylcholine, gastrin, histamine, somatostatin, and prostaglandins, including receptor signaling pathways, potentiation, and mechanisms of pharmacologic inhibition.","batch":1,"stem":"Which receptor mechanism does somatostatin use directly on parietal cells to reduce acid secretion?","choices":{"A":"Gq protein and IP3","B":"Receptor tyrosine kinase","C":"Inhibitory G protein","D":"Ligand gated channel","E":"Nuclear receptor"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Somatostatin binds Gi coupled receptors on parietal cells, inhibiting adenylyl cyclase and lowering cAMP, which antagonizes histamine. Gq and IP3 are the ACh and gastrin pathway. Tyrosine kinase receptors, ligand gated channels, and nuclear receptors are not the somatostatin mechanism. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 4, inhibitory pathways); Costanzo Ch. 8 (inhibition of HCl secretion).","boardPrep":"Step 1: D cells release somatostatin, which acts through a Gi cascade to reduce H+/K+ ATPase activity (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, parietal cell physiology). COMLEX: lower cAMP reduces protein kinase A activity in the parietal cell (lecture SDL 7).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 5 — somatostatin and histamine actions on acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the physiologic phases of gastric acid secretion and the mechanisms that protect the gastric mucosa, including the pathophysiology of peptic ulcer disease, H. pylori infection, and Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about what share of meal-induced acid secretion occurs in the gastric phase?","choices":{"A":"About 60 percent","B":"About 30 percent","C":"About 10 percent","D":"About 90 percent","E":"About 45 percent"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The gastric phase accounts for about 60% of the acid response, the cephalic phase about 30%, and the intestinal phase about 10%. Ninety and 45 percent do not match any phase. About 30 percent is the cephalic phase's share of acid response, not the gastric phase's. About 10 percent is the intestinal phase's share of acid response, not the gastric phase's. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 5, phases of gastric acid secretion); Costanzo Ch. 8 (phases of HCl secretion).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the gastric phase is triggered by distension and protein digestion products (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the intestinal phase accounts for about 10 percent of acid secretion (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 2 — H. pylori in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"About 30 percent is the cephalic phase's share of acid respo","C":"About 10 percent is the intestinal phase's share of acid res","D":"Ninety and 45 percent do not m"}},{"id":"sdl07_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the physiologic phases of gastric acid secretion and the mechanisms that protect the gastric mucosa, including the pathophysiology of peptic ulcer disease, H. pylori infection, and Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":1,"stem":"Which gastric mucosal defense rapidly seals small erosions?","choices":{"A":"Pepsin secretion","B":"Mucosal fibrosis","C":"Cell restitution","D":"Parietal hyperplasia","E":"Gastrin release"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Rapid epithelial cell restitution seals small erosions, alongside mucus and trapped HCO3−, tight junctions, and mucosal blood flow. Pepsin is a damaging factor. Fibrosis is scarring rather than a rapid defense. Parietal cell hyperplasia and gastrin increase acid. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 5, gastric mucosal barrier).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bismuth and sucralfate bind the gastric mucosa and give physical protection from acid (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: peptic ulcers reflect an imbalance of protective and damaging factors.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 2 — H. pylori in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Parietal cell hyperplasia and "}},{"id":"sdl07_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the physiologic phases of gastric acid secretion and the mechanisms that protect the gastric mucosa, including the pathophysiology of peptic ulcer disease, H. pylori infection, and Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":1,"stem":"Which H. pylori enzyme lets the organism survive in gastric acid?","choices":{"A":"Catalase","B":"Urease","C":"Lipase","D":"Protease","E":"Mucinase"},"correct":"B","explanation":"H. pylori urease converts urea into ammonia and CO2, forming an alkaline ammonia cloud that protects it from luminal acid. Catalase, lipase, protease, and mucinase are not the lecture mechanism for acid survival. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 5, peptic ulcer disease and H. pylori); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (urease hydrolyzes urea to form ammonia, which neutralizes gastric acid).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the urea breath test detects H. pylori urease activity (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: H. pylori is treated with triple or quadruple therapy that includes a PPI (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, H. pylori infection).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 2 — H. pylori in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the formation and modification of saliva, including the roles of acinar cells, ductal cells, myoepithelial cells, and how salivary flow rate affects electrolyte composition.","batch":2,"stem":"An 81-year-old woman is dehydrated, and her salivary flow falls below 1 mL/min. Compared with her saliva at a high flow rate, which change in her final saliva is expected?","choices":{"A":"Higher sodium concentration","B":"Higher chloride concentration","C":"Higher bicarbonate concentration","D":"Higher potassium concentration","E":"Isotonic osmolarity"},"correct":"D","explanation":"At low flow rates, saliva spends more time in contact with ductal cells, so more Na+ and Cl− are reabsorbed and more K+ is secreted; final saliva is most hypotonic with the highest K+. Sodium and chloride are lowest at low flow. Bicarbonate is lowest at low flow because its secretion is selectively stimulated during active salivation. Isotonic saliva is seen at high flow. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 1, effect of flow rate on electrolyte composition); Costanzo Ch. 8 (effect of flow rate on composition of saliva).","boardPrep":"Step 1: at the highest flow rates saliva most closely resembles plasma (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: dehydration reduces parasympathetic drive to the salivary glands.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — epithelial specializations for secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the formation and modification of saliva, including the roles of acinar cells, ductal cells, myoepithelial cells, and how salivary flow rate affects electrolyte composition.","batch":2,"stem":"A 17-year-old boy chews sour candy, and his salivary flow rises to about 4 mL/min. His salivary bicarbonate is higher than at rest even though the saliva spends less time in the ducts. Which mechanism explains this?","choices":{"A":"Ductal bicarbonate reabsorption","B":"Higher plasma bicarbonate","C":"Water reabsorption by ducts","D":"Slower acinar fluid secretion","E":"Stimulated ductal secretion"},"correct":"E","explanation":"HCO3− is the exception to the contact time rule: its ductal secretion is selectively stimulated when saliva production is stimulated, so its concentration rises with flow rate. Ducts secrete rather than reabsorb bicarbonate. Plasma bicarbonate does not change with a sour stimulus. Ductal cells are relatively impermeable to water. Acinar secretion speeds up, not slows, during stimulation. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 1, effect of flow rate on electrolyte composition); Costanzo Ch. 8 (HCO3− is not described by the contact time explanation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sour taste increases parasympathetic drive to the salivary glands (lecture SDL 7). COMLEX: salivary bicarbonate buffers ingested food.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — epithelial specializations for secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Ductal cells are relatively im","D":"Acinar secretion speeds up, no"}},{"id":"sdl07_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the formation and modification of saliva, including the roles of acinar cells, ductal cells, myoepithelial cells, and how salivary flow rate affects electrolyte composition.","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman in a physiology study has saliva collected at a low flow rate, and its Na+ concentration is far below that of plasma. Through which luminal membrane transporter do her ductal cells take up this Na+?","choices":{"A":"Sodium potassium pumps","B":"Sodium glucose carriers","C":"Epithelial sodium channels","D":"Sodium hydrogen exchangers","E":"Sodium phosphate carriers"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The luminal membrane of salivary ductal cells contains epithelial Na+ channels (ENaC), CFTR Cl− channels, Cl−/HCO3− exchange, and H+/K+ exchange; Na+ enters through ENaC and is pumped out across the basolateral membrane. The Na+/K+ ATPase sits on the basolateral membrane, not the luminal one. Sodium glucose, sodium hydrogen, and sodium phosphate carriers are not the ductal luminal transporters listed. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (formation of saliva, step 2: ductal luminal and basolateral transporters); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 1, ductal cell modification).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CFTR is the luminal Cl− channel of salivary ductal cells (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: saliva is not a simple ultrafiltrate of plasma.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — epithelial specializations for secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The Na+/K+ ATPase sits on the "}},{"id":"sdl07_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic regulation of salivary secretion, including neurotransmitters, receptors, intracellular signaling pathways, and factors that increase or decrease salivary secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 75-year-old man starts an anticholinergic drug for bladder spasm and now has a dry mouth during meals. Blockade of which receptors on his salivary cells best explains the loss of watery saliva?","choices":{"A":"Beta adrenergic receptors","B":"Alpha adrenergic receptors","C":"Histamine H2 receptors","D":"Muscarinic M3 receptors","E":"Dopamine D2 receptors"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Parasympathetic postganglionic neurons release ACh onto muscarinic M3 receptors on acinar and ductal cells, and the IP3/Ca2+ cascade produces a high volume of watery, enzyme rich saliva; blocking M3 removes this dominant drive. Beta and alpha adrenergic receptors mediate the smaller sympathetic response. H2 and D2 receptors are not the salivary parasympathetic receptors. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 2, parasympathetic pathway); Costanzo Ch. 8 (parasympathetic innervation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: atropine blocks muscarinic receptors (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: parasympathetic control of saliva is dominant.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic transmitters","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl07_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic regulation of salivary secretion, including neurotransmitters, receptors, intracellular signaling pathways, and factors that increase or decrease salivary secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man gives a stressful speech and notices that his saliva becomes thick and scant. Activation of which pathway in his salivary cells produces this protein rich secretion?","choices":{"A":"M3 receptors raising IP3","B":"Nicotinic receptors on acini","C":"H2 receptors raising cAMP","D":"Gi receptors lowering cAMP","E":"Beta receptors raising cAMP"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Postganglionic sympathetic fibers from the superior cervical ganglion release norepinephrine onto beta adrenergic receptors, which activate adenylyl cyclase and raise cAMP, producing a smaller volume of viscous, protein and mucin rich saliva. M3 and IP3 mediate the watery parasympathetic response. Nicotinic receptors are on ganglion cells, not acini. H2 receptors are on parietal cells. A Gi pathway would lower cAMP. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 2, sympathetic pathway); Costanzo Ch. 8 (sympathetic innervation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sympathetic preganglionic fibers to the salivary glands arise from T1 to T3 (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: sympathetic alpha receptors are also present on acinar cells.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic transmitters","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl07_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic regulation of salivary secretion, including neurotransmitters, receptors, intracellular signaling pathways, and factors that increase or decrease salivary secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old man in a study has parasympathetic stimulation of his submandibular gland, and gland blood flow rises sharply along with secretion. Which acinar product mediates this vasodilation?","choices":{"A":"Alpha amylase","B":"Lingual lipase","C":"Kallikrein","D":"Mucin glycoprotein","E":"Secretory IgA"},"correct":"C","explanation":"During high salivary activity, acinar cells secrete kallikrein, which cleaves high molecular weight kininogen into bradykinin, a potent local vasodilator that accounts for the high salivary blood flow. Alpha amylase and lingual lipase begin carbohydrate and lipid digestion. Mucin lubricates food. Secretory IgA is an immune product. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 2, parasympathetic pathway); Costanzo Ch. 8 (organic constituents of saliva).","boardPrep":"Step 1: maximal salivary blood flow is more than 10 times that of exercising muscle when corrected for size (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the submandibular duct is Wharton duct (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, salivary glands).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic transmitters","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl07_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the cellular mechanisms of gastric HCl secretion by parietal cells, including the functions of carbonic anhydrase, H⁺/K⁺-ATPase, Cl⁻ channels, and the alkaline tide.","batch":2,"stem":"A 79-year-old man has parietal cells studied during acid secretion. As each HCO3− ion leaves across the basolateral membrane, which ion enters the cell in exchange?","choices":{"A":"Sodium","B":"Chloride","C":"Potassium","D":"Hydrogen","E":"Phosphate"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The basolateral membrane of the parietal cell carries a Cl−/HCO3− exchanger, so HCO3− leaves for the blood as Cl− enters; that Cl− then diffuses into the lumen through apical Cl− channels to accompany the secreted H+. Na+ and K+ are moved by the basolateral Na+/K+ ATPase, not by this exchanger. H+ is pumped into the lumen. Phosphate is not part of HCl secretion. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (cellular mechanism of HCl secretion: basolateral Cl−/HCO3− exchangers, apical Cl− channels); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 3, basolateral transport). The direction of Cl− entry is inferred from the exchanger swapping Cl− for HCO3−.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the parietal cell basolateral membrane also contains the Na+/K+ ATPase (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: net HCl secretion is paired with net HCO3− absorption (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — parietal cells and their products","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Na+ and K+ are moved by the ba","C":"Na+ and K+ are moved by the ba","D":"H+ is pumped into the lumen."}},{"id":"sdl07_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the cellular mechanisms of gastric HCl secretion by parietal cells, including the functions of carbonic anhydrase, H⁺/K⁺-ATPase, Cl⁻ channels, and the alkaline tide.","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old woman has gastric glands studied after surgery. Blocking the apical K+ channels of her parietal cells lowers acid output even though the pump is intact. Why?","choices":{"A":"Carbonic anhydrase is inhibited","B":"Chloride channels close","C":"Pump loses its exchange ion","D":"Histamine release falls","E":"Bicarbonate exits apically"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The H+/K+ ATPase secretes H+ in exchange for luminal K+, and K+ recycles back into the canaliculus through apical K+ channels; without that recycling, little K+ is available for exchange and H+ secretion falls. Carbonic anhydrase, chloride channels, and histamine release are not directly affected. Bicarbonate exits across the basolateral membrane. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 3, apical active transport and Cl− channels).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the H+/K+ ATPase is a primary active transporter that moves H+ and K+ against their gradients (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: proton pump inhibitors block this pump.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — parietal cells and their products","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the cellular mechanisms of gastric HCl secretion by parietal cells, including the functions of carbonic anhydrase, H⁺/K⁺-ATPase, Cl⁻ channels, and the alkaline tide.","batch":2,"stem":"An 82-year-old man takes omeprazole for a gastric ulcer, and his acid output falls in response to every stimulus, including histamine and gastrin. Which step does the drug block?","choices":{"A":"Apical proton pump","B":"Parietal H2 receptors","C":"Parietal M3 receptors","D":"CCK2 receptors","E":"Carbonic anhydrase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Proton pump inhibitors such as omeprazole irreversibly block the apical H+/K+ ATPase, the final step of acid secretion shared by every secretagogue, which suppresses both meal stimulated and nocturnal acid secretion. H2, M3, and CCK2 receptor blockade would each remove one stimulus. Carbonic anhydrase is not the drug target. H2 antagonists block histamine at the parietal H2 receptor and only blunt gastrin and cholinergic stimulation, whereas omeprazole blocks the shared final pump step. Atropine blocks muscarinic receptors on parietal cells and so blocks only the direct action of ACh, whereas omeprazole also abolishes histamine- and gastrin-stimulated secretion. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 4, pharmacologic inhibition); Costanzo Ch. 8 (cellular mechanism of HCl secretion, step 2).","boardPrep":"Step 1: PPIs can cause malabsorption of vitamin B12, iron, calcium, and magnesium (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: omeprazole inhibits CYP2C19.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — acid suppressing drug classes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"H2 antagonists block histamine at the parietal H2 receptor a","C":"Atropine blocks muscarinic receptors on parietal cells and s"}},{"id":"sdl07_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of gastric acid secretion by acetylcholine, gastrin, histamine, somatostatin, and prostaglandins, including receptor signaling pathways, potentiation, and mechanisms of pharmacologic inhibition.","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old woman takes cimetidine, and her acid response to vagal stimulation and to gastrin falls much more than expected from blocking one secretagogue. Which mechanism explains this larger effect?","choices":{"A":"Direct M3 receptor blockade","B":"Lost histamine potentiation","C":"CCK2 receptor blockade","D":"Proton pump inhibition","E":"Gastrin release inhibition"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Histamine potentiates the actions of ACh and gastrin, so an H2 blocker removes both the direct histamine effect and the histamine-potentiated effects of ACh and gastrin. Cimetidine does not block M3 or CCK2 receptors, inhibit the proton pump, or stop gastrin release. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 4, pharmacologic inhibition); Costanzo Ch. 8 (potentiation and H2 receptor blocking agents).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cimetidine inhibits cytochrome P450 and has antiandrogenic effects (First Aid 2025 GI, H2 blockers). COMLEX: famotidine and nizatidine are other H2 antagonists.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — acid suppressing drug classes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of gastric acid secretion by acetylcholine, gastrin, histamine, somatostatin, and prostaglandins, including receptor signaling pathways, potentiation, and mechanisms of pharmacologic inhibition.","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old man has gastric pH recorded through a meal. The pH stays relatively high while food is in his stomach but drops sharply once the meal empties into the duodenum. What explains the drop?","choices":{"A":"Gastrin secretion rises","B":"Somatostatin release falls","C":"Vagal tone increases","D":"Pepsin activity increases","E":"Less food buffer remains"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Food buffers secreted H+, so gastric contents are less acidic while the meal is present; once chyme moves into the small intestine, buffering falls and continued H+ secretion lowers pH, which then inhibits gastrin and triggers somatostatin release to slow acid secretion. Gastrin falls and somatostatin rises as pH drops. Vagal tone falls rather than rises as the meal leaves, and pepsin activity does not lower pH. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (inhibition of HCl secretion); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 4, inhibitory pathways).","boardPrep":"Step 1: distension of the stomach drives gastric phase acid secretion through vagovagal and local reflexes (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the cephalic phase is mediated by the vagus (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 5 — somatostatin inhibits gastrin secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Gastrin falls and somatostatin"}},{"id":"sdl07_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of gastric acid secretion by acetylcholine, gastrin, histamine, somatostatin, and prostaglandins, including receptor signaling pathways, potentiation, and mechanisms of pharmacologic inhibition.","batch":2,"stem":"An 84-year-old woman in a study has her vagus nerves stimulated, and ACh binds muscarinic receptors on her parietal cells. Which enzyme does this receptor activate to drive acid secretion?","choices":{"A":"Phospholipase C","B":"Adenylyl cyclase","C":"Guanylyl cyclase","D":"Tyrosine kinase","E":"Lipoxygenase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"M3 receptors activate phospholipase C, which liberates diacylglycerol and IP3; IP3 releases intracellular Ca2+, and Ca2+ and diacylglycerol activate protein kinases that drive H+ secretion. Adenylyl cyclase is the histamine H2 pathway. Guanylyl cyclase, tyrosine kinase, and lipoxygenase are not the parietal M3 pathway. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (substances that alter HCl secretion); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 4, secretagogues and receptor pathways).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ACh acts on M3 receptors through a Gq cascade to increase H+/K+ ATPase activity (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, parietal cell physiology). COMLEX: atropine blocks direct vagal stimulation of parietal cells.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 4 — vagovagal reflexes and gastric secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the physiologic phases of gastric acid secretion and the mechanisms that protect the gastric mucosa, including the pathophysiology of peptic ulcer disease, H. pylori infection, and Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":2,"stem":"An 85-year-old man has H. pylori infection and a duodenal ulcer. The infection also spreads to the duodenum. Which duodenal defense does it impair?","choices":{"A":"Pancreatic enzyme output","B":"Bile salt secretion","C":"Duodenal villus height","D":"Local bicarbonate output","E":"Duodenal mucosal IgA"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Spread of H. pylori to the duodenum inhibits duodenal HCO3− secretion, so acid delivered from the stomach is poorly neutralized and erodes the mucosa. Pancreatic enzymes, bile salts, villus height, and mucosal IgA are not the described mechanism. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (duodenal ulcers); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 5, peptic ulcer disease and H. pylori infection).","boardPrep":"Step 1: duodenal ulcers are caused by H. pylori and gastrinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, peptic ulcer disease). COMLEX: H. pylori is diagnosed by urea breath test, fecal antigen, or biopsy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — H. pylori in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the physiologic phases of gastric acid secretion and the mechanisms that protect the gastric mucosa, including the pathophysiology of peptic ulcer disease, H. pylori infection, and Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":2,"stem":"An 80-year-old woman has an H. pylori gastric ulcer. Her measured net acid secretion is lower than normal, and her serum gastrin is raised. What explains the low net acid secretion?","choices":{"A":"Parietal cell autoimmunity","B":"Back leak into the mucosa","C":"Gastrin receptor loss","D":"Excess somatostatin release","E":"Vagal nerve injury"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In gastric ulcers the mucosal barrier is defective, so some secreted H+ leaks back into the damaged mucosa and net H+ secretion is lower than normal; the reduced net acid removes feedback on G cells and raises gastrin. Autoimmunity against parietal cells causes atrophic gastritis. Gastrin receptor loss, excess somatostatin, and vagal injury are not the described mechanism. Somatostatin inhibits gastrin secretion, so excess somatostatin would not fit her raised serum gastrin. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (gastric ulcers and Table 8.5).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H. pylori releases CagA toxin after attaching to epithelial cells (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, H. pylori infection). COMLEX: chronic NSAID use is a cause of gastric ulcers (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, peptic ulcer disease).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — H. pylori in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Somatostatin inhibits gastrin secretion, so excess somatosta"}},{"id":"sdl07_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the physiologic phases of gastric acid secretion and the mechanisms that protect the gastric mucosa, including the pathophysiology of peptic ulcer disease, H. pylori infection, and Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old woman in a study smells and chews food and then spits it out, and her acid output rises before food reaches her stomach. Which vagal actions drive this response?","choices":{"A":"ACh and GRP release","B":"Gastrin and CCK release","C":"Secretin and GIP release","D":"Motilin and GIP release","E":"Histamine and somatostatin release"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In the cephalic phase, vagal efferents release ACh onto parietal cells and GRP onto antral G cells, so acid rises directly and through gastrin. Gastrin is released secondarily via GRP rather than by the vagus itself, and CCK is not part of the cephalic phase. Secretin, GIP, and motilin are not vagal transmitters. Histamine comes from ECL cells, and somatostatin inhibits acid. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 5, phases of gastric acid secretion); Costanzo Ch. 8 (cephalic phase).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the cephalic phase accounts for about 30% of meal stimulated acid secretion (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: smell, taste, chewing, and conditioned reflexes start the cephalic phase (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 4 — vagovagal reflexes and gastric secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl07_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the formation and modification of saliva, including the roles of acinar cells, ductal cells, myoepithelial cells, and how salivary flow rate affects electrolyte composition.","batch":3,"stem":"A 6-year-old boy chews bread until it tastes sweet, but digestion of the starch by his salivary enzyme stops soon after he swallows. Why?","choices":{"A":"Pepsin degrades the starch","B":"Gastric acid inactivates it","C":"Intrinsic factor binds it","D":"Pancreatic lipase blocks it","E":"Bile salts inhibit it"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Acinar cells secrete alpha amylase, which begins starch digestion in the mouth, but salivary amylase is inactivated by the low pH of the gastric contents, so it plays little role overall. Pepsin digests protein, not starch. Intrinsic factor binds vitamin B12. Pancreatic lipase and bile salts act in the duodenum on fat. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 1, acinar cell secretion); Costanzo Ch. 8 (digestion of carbohydrates: salivary amylase is inactivated by low gastric pH).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic amylase completes starch digestion in the small intestine (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: lingual lipase begins the digestion of lipids in the mouth (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — epithelial specializations for secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl07_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic regulation of salivary secretion, including neurotransmitters, receptors, intracellular signaling pathways, and factors that increase or decrease salivary secretion.","batch":3,"stem":"A 9-year-old boy is badly frightened before a school play, and his sympathetic activity is high. His mouth is dry even though sympathetic input stimulates his acinar cells. Which sympathetic effect also limits his fluid output?","choices":{"A":"Beta receptor blockade","B":"Ductal water absorption","C":"Reduced gland blood flow","D":"Myoepithelial relaxation","E":"Acinar cell apoptosis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Sympathetic stimulation raises cAMP and produces a small volume of viscous saliva, but concurrent sympathetic vasoconstriction reduces local blood supply and blunts overall fluid output; fear also lowers parasympathetic drive. Norepinephrine activates rather than blocks beta receptors. Ductal water absorption is not a sympathetic effect. Stimulation contracts rather than relaxes myoepithelial cells. Fear does not kill acinar cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 2, sympathetic pathway and parasympathetic pathway).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sympathetic preganglionic fibers to the salivary glands arise from spinal segments T1 to T3 (lecture SDL 7). COMLEX: salivary glands have a very high blood flow that rises when saliva production is stimulated (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic transmitters","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"E":"Fear does not kill acinar cell"}},{"id":"trial_sdl07_q03","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of gastric acid secretion by acetylcholine, gastrin, histamine, somatostatin, and prostaglandins, including receptor signaling pathways, potentiation, and mechanisms of pharmacologic inhibition.","batch":3,"stem":"A 16-year-old girl in a study receives an infusion of CCK, and her parietal cells secrete acid much as they do in response to gastrin. Which mechanism explains this?","choices":{"A":"Both bind CCK2 receptors","B":"CCK triggers vagal ACh","C":"CCK releases antral gastrin","D":"Both bind H2 receptors","E":"CCK inhibits D cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Gastrin acts on parietal cells through CCK2 receptors, which have equal affinity for gastrin and CCK, whereas CCK1 receptors are specific for CCK; CCK can therefore act at the same receptor. CCK does not act by releasing vagal ACh or antral gastrin. H2 receptors bind histamine. Inhibiting D cells is not the described mechanism. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (gastrin binds CCK2 receptors on parietal cells); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 4, secretagogues and receptor pathways).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastrin acts through a Gq cascade to increase H+/K+ ATPase activity (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, parietal cell physiology). COMLEX: CCK1 receptors are specific for CCK (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — the four GI hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl07_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of gastric acid secretion by acetylcholine, gastrin, histamine, somatostatin, and prostaglandins, including receptor signaling pathways, potentiation, and mechanisms of pharmacologic inhibition.","batch":3,"stem":"An 86-year-old man in a study receives somatostatin, and his acid output falls more than its direct parietal cell effect predicts. Which paracrine action adds to the fall?","choices":{"A":"More vagal ACh release","B":"Blocking of H2 receptors","C":"Inhibited carbonic anhydrase","D":"Less ECL histamine release","E":"Closed apical chloride channels"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Besides its direct action on parietal cells, somatostatin acts by a paracrine mechanism to inhibit histamine release from ECL cells, reducing the stimulatory action of histamine. It does not raise vagal ACh, block H2 receptors, inhibit carbonic anhydrase, or close chloride channels. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 4, inhibitory pathways); Costanzo Ch. 8 (inhibition of HCl secretion, indirect pathways).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the somatostatin analog octreotide is used to manage carcinoid tumors (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, carcinoid tumor management). COMLEX: somatostatin inhibits acid secretion by both direct and indirect pathways (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 5 — somatostatin inhibits gastrin secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl07_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the physiologic phases of gastric acid secretion and the mechanisms that protect the gastric mucosa, including the pathophysiology of peptic ulcer disease, H. pylori infection, and Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":3,"stem":"A 17-year-old girl has healthy gastric mucosa. Some pepsin from her gastric juice diffuses into the surface mucus layer. Why does it not digest her epithelium?","choices":{"A":"High pH inactivates it","B":"Mucus digests the pepsin","C":"Tight junctions bind it","D":"Prostaglandins degrade it","E":"Blood flow carries it off"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Surface cells secrete mucus that traps HCO3−, creating a relatively alkaline layer at the cell surface; pepsin that penetrates this layer is inactivated in the high HCO3− environment. Mucus does not digest pepsin. Tight junctions limit back-diffusion of acid rather than bind pepsin. Prostaglandins do not degrade pepsin. Mucosal blood flow removes back-diffusing H+, not pepsin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 5, gastric mucosal barrier); Costanzo Ch. 8 (gastric diffusion barrier).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the alkaline mucus gel neutralizes acid and inactivates pepsin to prevent autodigestion (lecture SDL 2). COMLEX: H+ and pepsin together erode the mucosa in peptic ulcer disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 4 — the gastric mucosal barrier","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Mucus does not digest pepsin."}},{"id":"trial_sdl07_q06","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the physiologic phases of gastric acid secretion and the mechanisms that protect the gastric mucosa, including the pathophysiology of peptic ulcer disease, H. pylori infection, and Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":3,"stem":"A 78-year-old woman has a duodenal ulcer, and imaging shows no pancreatic or duodenal tumor. Serum gastrin is measured while fasting and again after a test meal. Which pattern is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Low fasting and normal after meals","B":"Low fasting and low after meals","C":"High fasting and normal after meals","D":"Normal fasting and low after meals","E":"Normal fasting and high after meals"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In persons with duodenal ulcers, baseline gastrin may be normal, but gastrin secretion in response to a meal is increased. Low fasting and low or normal meal values do not fit duodenal ulcer disease. A high fasting value with a normal meal response, or a normal fasting value with a low meal response, is not the described pattern. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (duodenal ulcers: normal baseline gastrin, increased meal response; Zollinger-Ellison syndrome). The fasting and meal values in the wrong rows are not described by the sources.","boardPrep":"Step 1: duodenal ulcer pain improves with meals, whereas gastric ulcer pain follows meals (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, peptic ulcer disease). COMLEX: a gastrinoma can sit in the pancreas or duodenum within the gastrinoma triangle (lecture SDL 7).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — H. pylori in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Low fasting and low or normal ","B":"Low fasting and low or normal ","C":"A high fasting value with a no","D":"A high fasting value with a no"}},{"id":"grid_sdl07_q01","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the physiologic phases of gastric acid secretion and the mechanisms that protect the gastric mucosa, including the pathophysiology of peptic ulcer disease, H. pylori infection, and Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":4,"stem":"An 83-year-old woman with osteoarthritis has taken a nonprescription anti-inflammatory pill at high dose every day for several months and now has burning epigastric pain. Endoscopy shows multiple gastric erosions. Which of the following sets of gastric mucosal findings is most likely in this patient?","grid":{"headers":["Acid secretion","Bicarbonate secretion","Mucosal blood flow"]},"choices":{"A":["↓","↔","↔"],"B":["↑","↔","↔"],"C":["↑","↓","↓"],"D":["↔","↔","↓"],"E":["↑","↓","↔"]},"correct":"C","explanation":"NSAIDs inhibit cyclooxygenase and lower PGE2, so the pattern (↑ acid secretion, ↓ bicarbonate secretion, ↓ mucosal blood flow) is correct. PGE2 normally acts through Gi receptors on parietal cells to oppose histamine, stimulates mucus and HCO3− secretion, and maintains mucosal blood flow; losing it tips the balance toward damaging factors. In the barrier-loss view, which Robbins emphasizes, prostaglandins support nearly every mucosal defense (mucus, bicarbonate, blood flow, and restitution), so the injury comes mainly from weakened protection rather than from extra acid; Robbins notes that prostaglandins also reduce acid secretion, so their loss fits the ↑ acid that Bootcamp gives. The pattern (↓ acid secretion, ↔ bicarbonate secretion, ↔ mucosal blood flow) is an H2 receptor antagonist such as famotidine, which reversibly blocks parietal cell H2 receptors and lowers acid secretion. The pattern (↑ acid secretion, ↔ bicarbonate secretion, ↔ mucosal blood flow) is a Cushing ulcer, in which brain injury and raised intracranial pressure increase vagal stimulation of parietal cells. The pattern (↔ acid secretion, ↔ bicarbonate secretion, ↓ mucosal blood flow) is a Curling ulcer, in which burns or trauma cause hypovolemia and reduced gastric blood flow. The pattern (↑ acid secretion, ↓ bicarbonate secretion, ↔ mucosal blood flow) is antral H. pylori infection in duodenal ulcer disease, in which loss of D cell somatostatin raises gastrin and acid delivery while duodenal bicarbonate secretion falls. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 4, lines 1368–1370, prostaglandins bind Gi receptors on parietal cells; section 5, PGE2 preserves the mucosal barrier; SDL 9, section 2, lines 1715–1719, antral H. pylori in duodenal ulcer disease; SDL 10, section 1, H2 receptor antagonists); Costanzo Ch. 8 (PGE2 stimulates HCO3− and mucus secretion and increases mucosal blood flow); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (line 1044, NSAIDs lower PGE2, raising H+ secretion and lowering bicarbonate secretion and gastric blood flow; lines 1045–1046, Cushing and Curling ulcers); Robbins Ch. 17 (NSAIDs remove prostaglandin support for mucus, bicarbonate, blood flow, and restitution, and prostaglandins reduce acid secretion). The ↔ cells in rows A, B, D, and E are inferred because the sources do not describe those values as changed.","boardPrep":"Step 1: misoprostol is used for NSAID ulcer prophylaxis and is contraindicated in pregnancy (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: Curling ulcers follow burns and hypovolemia, and Cushing ulcers follow brain injury (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, peptic ulcer disease).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — NSAID associated ulcer treatment and prevention","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":8,"title":"SDL 8 — Pathology of the Esophagus","questions":[{"id":"sdl08_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathogenesis, morphologic features, and clinical manifestations of esophageal obstructive and motility disorders, including stenosis, webs & rings, and achalasia, and correlate the underlying structural or neuromuscular abnormality with patterns of dysphagia.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, loss of which neurons causes primary achalasia?","choices":{"A":"Excitatory cholinergic neurons","B":"Vagal sensory neurons","C":"Sympathetic adrenergic neurons","D":"Interstitial pacemaker cells","E":"Nitric oxide neurons"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Primary achalasia results from idiopathic degeneration or autoimmune loss of nitric oxide producing inhibitory enteric neurons in the myenteric plexus, so the LES fails to relax and the distal esophagus loses peristalsis. Loss of excitatory cholinergic neurons would weaken contraction. Vagal sensory, sympathetic, and pacemaker cells are not the lesion. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 1, achalasia); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (achalasia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: achalasia shows a bird beak sign on barium swallow (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, achalasia). COMLEX: achalasia raises the risk of esophageal cancer.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 5 — the lower esophageal sphincter","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathogenesis, morphologic features, and clinical manifestations of esophageal obstructive and motility disorders, including stenosis, webs & rings, and achalasia, and correlate the underlying structural or neuromuscular abnormality with patterns of dysphagia.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, where does a B ring of the distal esophagus lie?","choices":{"A":"In the upper cervical esophagus","B":"At the squamocolumnar junction","C":"At the cricopharyngeal muscle","D":"In the middle thoracic esophagus","E":"Within the gastric antrum"},"correct":"B","explanation":"B rings lie at the squamocolumnar junction and have gastric cardia mucosa on their undersurface, whereas A rings lie above the gastroesophageal junction and are covered by squamous mucosa. Upper esophageal webs occur in Plummer Vinson syndrome. The cricopharyngeus, midesophagus, and antrum are not ring sites. The cricopharyngeal muscle forms the upper esophageal sphincter, whereas B rings lie in the distal esophagus at the squamocolumnar junction. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 1, esophageal webs and rings); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (Schatzki ring).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Schatzki rings are associated with hiatal hernia and GERD (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, esophageal ring). COMLEX: rings cause intermittent solid food dysphagia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 5 — the lower esophageal sphincter","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"The cricopharyngeus, midesopha","C":"The cricopharyngeal muscle forms the upper esophageal sphinc"}},{"id":"sdl08_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathogenesis, morphologic features, and clinical manifestations of esophageal obstructive and motility disorders, including stenosis, webs & rings, and achalasia, and correlate the underlying structural or neuromuscular abnormality with patterns of dysphagia.","batch":1,"stem":"What dysphagia pattern does progressive esophageal stenosis typically produce?","choices":{"A":"Painful swallowing of liquids","B":"Solids and liquids from the onset","C":"Liquids worse than solids","D":"Solids first and later liquids","E":"Episodic dysphagia for meat"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Stenosis narrows the lumen progressively, so mechanical dysphagia begins with solids and later involves liquids as the caliber narrows, with appetite and weight usually preserved unless the lesion is malignant. Dysphagia to solids and liquids from onset is the motor pattern of achalasia. Episodic meat impaction fits rings. Painful swallowing is odynophagia; the lecture describes esophageal stenosis as progressive mechanical dysphagia, first to solids and later to liquids. Liquids worse than solids is not the stenosis pattern; as the lumen narrows, liquids are affected only later, after solids. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 1, esophageal stenosis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: strictures follow GERD, caustic ingestion, or radiation (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, esophageal stricture). COMLEX: barium swallow shows narrowing at the stricture.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 5 — the lower esophageal sphincter","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Painful swallowing is odynophagia; the lecture describes eso","C":"Liquids worse than solids is not the stenosis pattern; as th"}},{"id":"sdl08_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the major forms of esophageal injury and inflammation-including mucosal lacerations, reflux espophagitis, infectious esophagitis, and eosinophilic esophagitis-based on their etiology, pathogenesis, clinical presentation and characteristic morphologic findings.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what failure during severe retching causes a Mallory Weiss tear?","choices":{"A":"Pylorus fails to close","B":"Vagal tone is lost","C":"Gastric acid is excessive","D":"Diaphragm fails to contract","E":"LES fails to relax first"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Mallory Weiss tears occur when reflex relaxation of the LES fails to precede antiperistaltic gastric expulsion, so the wall near the gastroesophageal junction is overstretched and lacerated. Pyloric closure, vagal tone, excess acid, and diaphragm contraction are not the lecture mechanism. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 2, mucosal lacerations).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Mallory Weiss syndrome is associated with alcohol use and bulimia nervosa (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Mallory Weiss syndrome). COMLEX: these tears account for up to 10% of upper GI bleeding.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — esophageal epithelium and protection","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Pyloric closure, vagal tone, e","C":"Pyloric closure, vagal tone, e"}},{"id":"sdl08_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the major forms of esophageal injury and inflammation-including mucosal lacerations, reflux espophagitis, infectious esophagitis, and eosinophilic esophagitis-based on their etiology, pathogenesis, clinical presentation and characteristic morphologic findings.","batch":1,"stem":"What does Candida esophagitis look like at endoscopy?","choices":{"A":"Punched out volcanic ulcers","B":"Stacked circular mucosal rings","C":"Salmon pink mucosal tongues","D":"Gray white pseudomembranes","E":"Dilated submucosal veins"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Candida forms adherent gray white pseudomembranes of matted hyphae, pseudohyphae, and inflammatory cells over eroded mucosa. Punched out ulcers, stacked rings, salmon pink tongues, and dilated veins are not the Candida appearance. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 2, infectious esophagitis); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (esophageal candidiasis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Candida histology shows pseudohyphae and budding yeast (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, esophageal candidiasis). COMLEX: infectious esophagitis causes prominent odynophagia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — esophageal epithelium and protection","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the major forms of esophageal injury and inflammation-including mucosal lacerations, reflux espophagitis, infectious esophagitis, and eosinophilic esophagitis-based on their etiology, pathogenesis, clinical presentation and characteristic morphologic findings.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what is the minimum eosinophil count on esophageal biopsy that supports eosinophilic esophagitis?","choices":{"A":"At least 2 per HPF","B":"At least 15 per HPF","C":"At least 5 per HPF","D":"At least 10 per HPF","E":"At least 50 per HPF"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Eosinophilic esophagitis shows dense intraepithelial eosinophils, at least 15 per high power field, forming superficial clusters with spongiosis. Reflux esophagitis has only scattered intraepithelial eosinophils, so counts of 2, 5, or 10 per field are below the cutoff, and 50 per field exceeds the minimum. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 2, eosinophilic esophagitis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: eosinophilic esophagitis is driven by Th2 inflammation to food antigens (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, eosinophilic esophagitis). COMLEX: it is associated with atopic conditions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — esophageal epithelium and protection","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Reflux esophagitis has only sc","C":"Reflux esophagitis has only sc"}},{"id":"sdl08_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the pathogenesis and morphologic features of gastroesophageal reflux disease and correlate chronic reflux injury with the development of Barrett esophagus, dysplasia and esophagel adenocarcinoma.","batch":1,"stem":"Which cell on biopsy is the histologic hallmark of Barrett esophagus?","choices":{"A":"Goblet cells","B":"Parietal cells","C":"Chief cells","D":"Paneth cells","E":"Keratinocytes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Barrett esophagus is simple columnar epithelium containing distinctive goblet cells with pale blue mucin vacuoles. Parietal and chief cells belong to gastric glands. Paneth cells are not the lecture hallmark. Keratinocytes would indicate squamous epithelium. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 3, development of Barrett esophagus); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (Barrett esophagus).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in Barrett esophagus the Z line extends proximally (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Barrett esophagus). COMLEX: Barrett mucosa may undergo malignant transformation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 5 — mechanisms preventing reflux","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the pathogenesis and morphologic features of gastroesophageal reflux disease and correlate chronic reflux injury with the development of Barrett esophagus, dysplasia and esophagel adenocarcinoma.","batch":1,"stem":"What process replaces esophageal squamous epithelium in Barrett esophagus?","choices":{"A":"Squamous hyperplasia","B":"Gastric glandular atrophy","C":"Intestinal metaplasia","D":"Fibrous scarring","E":"Epithelial apoptosis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Persistent acid injury reprograms esophageal stem cells toward a columnar fate, so intestinal metaplasia replaces the squamous lining with acid resistant columnar epithelium. Hyperplasia keeps squamous epithelium. Atrophy, scarring, and apoptosis do not produce columnar epithelium. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 3, development of Barrett esophagus).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Barrett esophagus is a complication of chronic GERD (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, GERD). COMLEX: endoscopy shows salmon pink mucosa above the junction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 5 — mechanisms preventing reflux","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the pathogenesis and morphologic features of gastroesophageal reflux disease and correlate chronic reflux injury with the development of Barrett esophagus, dysplasia and esophagel adenocarcinoma.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, mutation of which gene is a key step in progression of Barrett dysplasia to adenocarcinoma?","choices":{"A":"APC","B":"TP53","C":"BRCA1","D":"RB1","E":"VHL"},"correct":"B","explanation":"TP53 mutation is among the genetic alterations that drive Barrett dysplasia to invasive esophageal adenocarcinoma in the lecture. APC, BRCA1, RB1, and VHL are not listed for this progression. APC mutations underlie familial adenomatous polyposis and colorectal neoplasia, not Barrett progression. BRCA1 is a DNA repair gene linked to hereditary breast and ovarian cancer, not to Barrett progression. RB loss is the classic driver of retinoblastoma, not of Barrett dysplasia progression. VHL loss-of-function mutations underlie hereditary and many sporadic renal cell carcinomas, not Barrett progression. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 3, development of esophageal adenocarcinoma; section 5, pathogenesis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: esophageal adenocarcinoma arises in the distal third (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, adenocarcinoma). COMLEX: GERD and obesity are adenocarcinoma risk factors.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 5 — mechanisms preventing reflux","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"APC mutations underlie familial adenomatous polyposis and co","C":"BRCA1 is a DNA repair gene linked to hereditary breast and o","D":"RB loss is the classic driver of retinoblastoma, not of Barr","E":"VHL loss-of-function mutations underlie hereditary and many "}},{"id":"sdl08_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathogenesis and clinical significance of esophageal varices by relating portal hypertension and cirrhosis to the development of portosystemic collateral vessels and the risk of life-threatening upper GI hemorrhage.","batch":1,"stem":"Where do esophageal veins drain the blood shunted to them from the portal circulation?","choices":{"A":"Splenic vein","B":"Inferior mesenteric vein","C":"Hepatic veins","D":"Renal veins","E":"Azygos system"},"correct":"E","explanation":"At the esophagogastric junction, portal blood shunted at the junction enters esophageal veins that drain into the azygos system. The splenic and inferior mesenteric veins feed the portal system. Hepatic veins drain the liver. Renal veins drain the kidneys. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 4, portosystemic collateral vessels).","boardPrep":"Step 1: varices are dilated submucosal veins at a portocaval anastomosis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, esophageal varices). COMLEX: variceal bleeding causes hematemesis and shock.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — layers of the esophageal wall","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathogenesis and clinical significance of esophageal varices by relating portal hypertension and cirrhosis to the development of portosystemic collateral vessels and the risk of life-threatening upper GI hemorrhage.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what share of patients with cirrhosis develop esophageal varices?","choices":{"A":"About 5 to 10 percent","B":"About 15 to 20 percent","C":"About 40 to 60 percent","D":"About 80 to 90 percent","E":"About 1 to 2 percent"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Varices develop in about 40% to 60% of patients with cirrhosis. The lower and higher ranges do not match the lecture. About 5 to 10 percent is too low; the lecture says varices develop in about 40 to 60 percent of patients with cirrhosis. About 15 to 20 percent is too low; the lecture says varices develop in about 40 to 60 percent of patients with cirrhosis. About 80 to 90 percent is too high; the lecture says varices develop in about 40 to 60 percent of patients with cirrhosis. About 1 to 2 percent is far too low; the lecture says varices develop in about 40 to 60 percent of patients with cirrhosis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 4, clinical significance).","boardPrep":"Step 1: portal hypertension dilates submucosal veins (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, esophageal varices). COMLEX: cirrhosis commonly follows alcohol use, viral hepatitis, or steatohepatitis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — layers of the esophageal wall","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"About 5 to 10 percent is too low; the lecture says varices d","B":"About 15 to 20 percent is too low; the lecture says varices ","D":"About 80 to 90 percent is too high; the lecture says varices","E":"About 1 to 2 percent is far too low; the lecture says varice"}},{"id":"sdl08_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathogenesis and clinical significance of esophageal varices by relating portal hypertension and cirrhosis to the development of portosystemic collateral vessels and the risk of life-threatening upper GI hemorrhage.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what is the immediate mortality of each episode of variceal bleeding?","choices":{"A":"About 15 to 30 percent","B":"About 1 to 2 percent","C":"About 3 to 5 percent","D":"About 50 to 60 percent","E":"About 80 to 90 percent"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Each episode of variceal bleeding carries an immediate mortality of 15% to 30%, and survivors face an 80% two year risk of rebleeding. Ranges of 1 to 2 or 3 to 5 percent understate the risk, and 50 to 60 or 80 to 90 percent overstate it; the 80% figure is the two year rebleeding risk in survivors, not immediate mortality. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 4, clinical significance).","boardPrep":"Step 1: variceal bleeding presents abruptly with hematemesis and melena (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, esophageal varices). COMLEX: hemorrhagic shock may follow.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — layers of the esophageal wall","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare esophageal squamous cell carcinoma and adenocarcinoma with respect to epidemology, major risk factors, pathoogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical presentation and precursor lesions.","batch":1,"stem":"Where in the esophagus does squamous cell carcinoma most often arise?","choices":{"A":"Distal third","B":"Gastric cardia","C":"Middle third","D":"Upper sphincter","E":"Abdominal segment"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Squamous cell carcinoma arises mainly in the middle third (about 50%) and upper third, whereas adenocarcinoma lies in the distal third and often invades the gastric cardia. The upper sphincter and abdominal segment are not the usual sites. Adenocarcinoma, not squamous cell carcinoma, is exclusively in the distal third; squamous cell carcinoma occurs primarily in the proximal esophagus, with half in the middle third. Distal-third adenocarcinoma frequently invades the gastric cardia; squamous cell carcinoma occurs primarily in the proximal esophagus, with half in the middle third. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 5, anatomic distribution); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (squamous cell carcinoma: proximal two thirds).","boardPrep":"Step 1: squamous cell carcinoma is the most common esophageal cancer worldwide (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, squamous cell carcinoma). COMLEX: barium swallow may show an apple core lesion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — squamous and columnar epithelium","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Adenocarcinoma, not squamous cell carcinoma, is exclusively ","B":"Distal-third adenocarcinoma frequently invades the gastric c"}},{"id":"sdl08_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare esophageal squamous cell carcinoma and adenocarcinoma with respect to epidemology, major risk factors, pathoogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical presentation and precursor lesions.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which infection is associated with a reduced risk of esophageal adenocarcinoma?","choices":{"A":"Human papillomavirus","B":"Epstein Barr virus","C":"Cytomegalovirus","D":"Helicobacter pylori","E":"Herpes simplex virus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The lecture notes that H. pylori infection is associated with a reduced risk of esophageal adenocarcinoma, and that alcohol is not a risk factor. Human papillomavirus is listed by Bootcamp as a squamous cell carcinoma risk factor. Epstein Barr virus, cytomegalovirus, and herpes simplex virus are not described as protective. Epstein Barr virus is linked to a molecular subtype of intestinal-type gastric adenocarcinoma, not to a reduced risk of esophageal adenocarcinoma. Cytomegalovirus causes shallow esophageal ulcerations with inclusions in endothelial and stromal cells and is not protective against esophageal adenocarcinoma. Herpes simplex virus causes punched-out esophageal ulcers with multinucleate inclusion-bearing cells and is not protective against esophageal adenocarcinoma. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 5, major risk factors); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (squamous cell carcinoma risk factors).","boardPrep":"Step 1: adenocarcinoma is the most common esophageal cancer in the US (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, adenocarcinoma). COMLEX: adenocarcinoma shows a strong male predominance.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — squamous and columnar epithelium","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Epstein Barr virus is linked to a molecular subtype of intes","C":"Cytomegalovirus causes shallow esophageal ulcerations with i","E":"Herpes simplex virus causes punched-out esophageal ulcers wi"}},{"id":"sdl08_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare esophageal squamous cell carcinoma and adenocarcinoma with respect to epidemology, major risk factors, pathoogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical presentation and precursor lesions.","batch":1,"stem":"Which histologic feature identifies esophageal squamous cell carcinoma?","choices":{"A":"Keratin pearls","B":"Goblet cell glands","C":"Signet ring cells","D":"Mucin vacuoles","E":"Cribriform glands"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Squamous cell carcinoma shows nests of malignant squamous cells with keratin pearls and intercellular bridges. Goblet cells, signet ring cells, mucin vacuoles, and cribriform glands are glandular features rather than squamous ones. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 5, morphologic features); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (squamous cell carcinoma histopathology).","boardPrep":"Step 1: squamous cell carcinoma risk factors include smoking, alcohol, and hot beverages (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, squamous cell carcinoma). COMLEX: both esophageal cancers present with progressive dysphagia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — squamous and columnar epithelium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathogenesis, morphologic features, and clinical manifestations of esophageal obstructive and motility disorders, including stenosis, webs & rings, and achalasia, and correlate the underlying structural or neuromuscular abnormality with patterns of dysphagia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 37-year-old woman who grew up in rural Brazil has dysphagia to both solids and liquids, regurgitation of undigested food at night, and a bird beak narrowing on barium swallow. Which agent most likely caused her disease?","choices":{"A":"Trypanosoma cruzi","B":"Candida albicans","C":"Herpes simplex virus","D":"Cytomegalovirus","E":"Helicobacter pylori"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Secondary achalasia arises from Chagas disease, in which Trypanosoma cruzi destroys the myenteric plexus; it can also follow diabetic neuropathy, amyloidosis, sarcoidosis, or malignancy. Candida, herpes simplex virus, and cytomegalovirus cause infectious esophagitis with odynophagia. H. pylori infects the stomach. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 1, achalasia); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (Chagas disease secondary achalasia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: T. cruzi is shed in the feces of the reduviid bug (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Chagas disease). COMLEX: Chagas disease can cause megaesophagus.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 5 — the lower esophageal sphincter","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathogenesis, morphologic features, and clinical manifestations of esophageal obstructive and motility disorders, including stenosis, webs & rings, and achalasia, and correlate the underlying structural or neuromuscular abnormality with patterns of dysphagia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old man has had episodes over several years of a steak bolus sticking low in his chest, each resolving without trouble swallowing liquids. Which lesion best explains this pattern?","choices":{"A":"Primary achalasia","B":"Esophageal adenocarcinoma","C":"Scleroderma dysmotility","D":"Esophageal varices","E":"Distal esophageal ring"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Rings cause episodic, nonprogressive mechanical dysphagia triggered by poorly chewed solid boluses, the steakhouse syndrome. Achalasia causes dysphagia to solids and liquids from onset. Cancer causes progressive dysphagia with weight loss. Scleroderma causes motor dysphagia with reflux. Varices bleed rather than obstruct. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 1, esophageal webs and rings).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Schatzki B rings lie at the squamocolumnar junction (lecture SDL 8). COMLEX: rings present with dysphagia and regurgitation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — esophageal epithelium","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Cancer causes progressive dysp"}},{"id":"sdl08_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathogenesis, morphologic features, and clinical manifestations of esophageal obstructive and motility disorders, including stenosis, webs & rings, and achalasia, and correlate the underlying structural or neuromuscular abnormality with patterns of dysphagia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman has fatigue, a sore smooth tongue, cracks at the corners of her mouth, and dysphagia high in her throat. Her blood count shows a microcytic anemia. Which syndrome is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Pharyngeal Zenker diverticulum","B":"Plummer Vinson syndrome","C":"Mallory Weiss syndrome","D":"Boerhaave syndrome","E":"Barrett esophagus"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Upper esophageal webs with iron deficiency anemia, glossitis, and cheilosis make up Plummer Vinson syndrome. Zenker diverticulum is a pouch with halitosis and regurgitation. Mallory Weiss and Boerhaave syndromes follow vomiting. Barrett esophagus is distal metaplasia. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 1, esophageal webs and rings); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (Plummer Vinson syndrome).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Plummer Vinson syndrome raises the risk of squamous cell carcinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Plummer Vinson syndrome). COMLEX: webs occur most often in women over 40.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — esophageal epithelium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the major forms of esophageal injury and inflammation-including mucosal lacerations, reflux espophagitis, infectious esophagitis, and eosinophilic esophagitis-based on their etiology, pathogenesis, clinical presentation and characteristic morphologic findings.","batch":2,"stem":"A 21-year-old man vomits repeatedly after a night of binge drinking and then vomits bright red blood. His vital signs are stable, and the bleeding stops on its own. Which lesion is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Ruptured submucosal vein","B":"Bleeding gastric ulcer","C":"Longitudinal mucosal tear","D":"Ulcerated squamous carcinoma","E":"Candida pseudomembranes"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A Mallory Weiss tear is a longitudinal mucosal laceration crossing the gastroesophageal junction after severe retching, presenting as painful hematemesis that usually heals quickly. Variceal rupture needs portal hypertension. A gastric ulcer is not linked to the retching sequence. Carcinoma and candidiasis do not fit the sudden onset after vomiting. A ruptured submucosal vein describes variceal bleeding, whereas forceful retching causes longitudinal Mallory-Weiss mucosal tears. Candida esophagitis forms adherent gray-white pseudomembranes of matted fungal hyphae, not bleeding after forceful retching. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 2, mucosal lacerations); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (Mallory Weiss syndrome).","boardPrep":"Step 1: endoscopy shows longitudinal tears near the gastroesophageal junction (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Mallory Weiss syndrome). COMLEX: prolonged coughing is also an association.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 3 — gastric propulsion and retropulsion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Carcinoma and candidiasis do n","A":"A ruptured submucosal vein describes variceal bleeding, wher","E":"Candida esophagitis forms adherent gray-white pseudomembrane"}},{"id":"sdl08_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the major forms of esophageal injury and inflammation-including mucosal lacerations, reflux espophagitis, infectious esophagitis, and eosinophilic esophagitis-based on their etiology, pathogenesis, clinical presentation and characteristic morphologic findings.","batch":2,"stem":"An 18-year-old man who is otherwise healthy has severe pain on swallowing. Endoscopy shows punched out ulcers, and biopsy of the ulcer edge shows multinucleated squamous cells with nuclear molding. Which organism is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Herpes simplex virus","B":"Cytomegalovirus","C":"Candida albicans yeast","D":"Trypanosoma cruzi","E":"Helicobacter pylori"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Herpes simplex virus causes punched out, volcanic ulcers with multinucleated squamous cells, Cowdry A inclusions, and nuclear molding at the ulcer edge, and it can occur in healthy hosts. Cytomegalovirus causes shallow linear ulcers. Candida forms pseudomembranes. T. cruzi causes achalasia. H. pylori infects the stomach. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 2, infectious esophagitis); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (herpes esophagitis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: herpes esophagitis is usually caused by HSV 1 (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, herpes esophagitis). COMLEX: odynophagia is prominent in infectious esophagitis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — esophageal epithelium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the major forms of esophageal injury and inflammation-including mucosal lacerations, reflux espophagitis, infectious esophagitis, and eosinophilic esophagitis-based on their etiology, pathogenesis, clinical presentation and characteristic morphologic findings.","batch":2,"stem":"A 13-year-old boy with asthma and eczema has repeated food impaction. Endoscopy shows stacked circular rings and linear furrows, and his symptoms persist after 8 weeks of a PPI. Which condition is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Acid reflux esophagitis","B":"Eosinophilic esophagitis","C":"Herpes simplex esophagitis","D":"Candida esophagitis","E":"Multiple Schatzki rings"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Eosinophilic esophagitis is a Th2 driven allergic disorder linked to atopy that causes food impaction, a ringed feline esophagus with furrows and exudates, and symptoms that are refractory to PPI therapy alone. Reflux esophagitis responds to acid suppression. Herpes and Candida cause odynophagia with ulcers or plaques. Schatzki rings are distal and nonprogressive and do not cause furrows, atopy, or PPI refractory symptoms. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 2, eosinophilic esophagitis); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (eosinophilic esophagitis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: endoscopy may show trachealization of the esophagus (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, eosinophilic esophagitis). COMLEX: infants present with feeding intolerance and vomiting.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — esophageal epithelium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the pathogenesis and morphologic features of gastroesophageal reflux disease and correlate chronic reflux injury with the development of Barrett esophagus, dysplasia and esophagel adenocarcinoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman has heartburn that worsens when she lies down after meals. Endoscopy shows hyperemia, and biopsy of the lower esophagus shows elongated lamina propria papillae. Which other finding is expected?","choices":{"A":"Goblet cell metaplasia","B":"Keratin pearl formation","C":"Cowdry A inclusions","D":"Basal zone hyperplasia","E":"Fungal pseudohyphae"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Reflux esophagitis shows scattered intraepithelial eosinophils, basal zone hyperplasia above 20% of epithelial thickness, and papillae extending into the upper third of the epithelium. Goblet cells, keratin pearls, Cowdry A inclusions, and pseudohyphae are not features of reflux injury. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 2, reflux esophagitis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GERD risk factors include obesity, pregnancy, hiatal hernia, and smoking (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, GERD). COMLEX: GERD is treated with lifestyle change and a PPI.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 5 — mechanisms preventing reflux","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the pathogenesis and morphologic features of gastroesophageal reflux disease and correlate chronic reflux injury with the development of Barrett esophagus, dysplasia and esophagel adenocarcinoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man with long-standing Barrett esophagus has surveillance biopsies. The glands show rounded hyperchromatic nuclei, loss of nuclear polarity, atypical mitoses, and gland within gland patterns, but the basement membrane is intact. What is the diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Low grade dysplasia","B":"Invasive adenocarcinoma","C":"High grade dysplasia","D":"Reflux esophagitis","E":"Squamous dysplasia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"High grade dysplasia shows marked atypia, rounded hyperchromatic nuclei, loss of polarity, atypical mitoses, and cribriform architecture, while the basement membrane is not breached. Low grade dysplasia has elongated nuclei limited to the basal aspect of glands. Invasion of the lamina propria defines carcinoma. Reflux esophagitis is inflammatory. Squamous dysplasia arises in squamous epithelium. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 3, progression to dysplasia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic reflux causes intestinal metaplasia with simple columnar epithelium (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Barrett esophagus). COMLEX: Barrett esophagus needs endoscopic surveillance.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 3 — precursor epithelial lesions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the pathogenesis and morphologic features of gastroesophageal reflux disease and correlate chronic reflux injury with the development of Barrett esophagus, dysplasia and esophagel adenocarcinoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with Barrett esophagus has a new nodule in the distal esophagus. Biopsy shows atypical glands that have breached the basement membrane and invaded the lamina propria and submucosa. What is the diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"High grade dysplasia","B":"Invasive adenocarcinoma","C":"Squamous cell carcinoma","D":"Gastrointestinal stromal tumor","E":"Leiomyoma of the wall"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Once dysplastic Barrett cells breach the basement membrane and invade the lamina propria and submucosa, the lesion is invasive esophageal adenocarcinoma. High grade dysplasia lacks invasion. Squamous cell carcinoma forms squamous nests. Stromal tumors and leiomyomas are mesenchymal. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 3, development of esophageal adenocarcinoma).","boardPrep":"Step 1: adenocarcinoma histology shows glandular tumor with intestinal differentiation (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, adenocarcinoma). COMLEX: esophageal cancer presents with progressive dysphagia for solids then liquids and rapid weight loss (lecture SDL 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 4 — gastric adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathogenesis and clinical significance of esophageal varices by relating portal hypertension and cirrhosis to the development of portosystemic collateral vessels and the risk of life-threatening upper GI hemorrhage.","batch":2,"stem":"A 54-year-old woman with alcoholic cirrhosis suddenly vomits a large amount of blood and becomes hypotensive. Which lesion is the most likely source?","choices":{"A":"Mallory Weiss mucosal tear","B":"Ulcerated Barrett adenocarcinoma","C":"Ruptured esophageal varices","D":"Candida pseudomembranes","E":"Eosinophilic esophagitis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cirrhosis raises portal pressure and creates varices beneath thin squamous mucosa that rupture with massive, life threatening hematemesis. A Mallory Weiss tear needs prior retching and is usually self limited. Carcinoma, Candida, and eosinophilic esophagitis rarely cause sudden massive bleeding. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 4, clinical significance).","boardPrep":"Step 1: variceal bleeding can cause melena or hematochezia (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, esophageal varices). COMLEX: survivors have a high risk of rebleeding.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 4 — management of variceal hemorrhage","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Carcinoma, Candida, and eosino"}},{"id":"sdl08_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathogenesis and clinical significance of esophageal varices by relating portal hypertension and cirrhosis to the development of portosystemic collateral vessels and the risk of life-threatening upper GI hemorrhage.","batch":2,"stem":"A 59-year-old man has cirrhosis and portal hypertension. Which vein carries portal blood into his esophageal collaterals at the esophagogastric junction?","choices":{"A":"Superior rectal vein","B":"Paraumbilical vein","C":"Inferior vena cava","D":"Right renal vein","E":"Left gastric vein"},"correct":"E","explanation":"At the esophagogastric junction, blood shunts from the left gastric (coronary) vein into the esophageal veins. The superior rectal and paraumbilical veins form other portosystemic collaterals. The inferior vena cava and renal veins are systemic. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 4, portosystemic collateral vessels).","boardPrep":"Step 1: varices form at portocaval anastomoses (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, esophageal varices). COMLEX: varices also form in the proximal stomach.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 4 — management of variceal hemorrhage","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathogenesis and clinical significance of esophageal varices by relating portal hypertension and cirrhosis to the development of portosystemic collateral vessels and the risk of life-threatening upper GI hemorrhage.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old woman with cirrhosis has large varices in her distal esophagus. Which anatomic feature makes these vessels especially prone to rupture?","choices":{"A":"Thick muscular venous walls","B":"Low pressure within the veins","C":"Deep location in the adventitia","D":"Thin overlying squamous mucosa","E":"Dense surrounding fibrosis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Varices lie immediately beneath thin squamous mucosa under high hydrostatic pressure, so they rupture easily and bleed massively. Their walls are thin, pressure is high, and they lie in the subepithelial and submucosal plexuses rather than the adventitia. Fibrosis does not protect them. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 4, development of collaterals and clinical significance).","boardPrep":"Step 1: varices lie in the subepithelial and submucosal venous plexuses of the distal esophagus (lecture SDL 8). COMLEX: varices develop in many patients with cirrhosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 1 — layers of the alimentary canal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Their walls are thin, pressure","E":"Fibrosis does not protect them"}},{"id":"sdl08_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare esophageal squamous cell carcinoma and adenocarcinoma with respect to epidemology, major risk factors, pathoogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical presentation and precursor lesions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old woman who grew up in northern Iran drinking very hot tea has progressive dysphagia and weight loss. Endoscopy shows an ulcerated esophageal mass. Which tumor is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Squamous cell carcinoma","B":"Esophageal adenocarcinoma","C":"Gastrointestinal stromal tumor","D":"Esophageal leiomyoma","E":"Neuroendocrine tumor"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Squamous cell carcinoma is the most common esophageal cancer worldwide, concentrated in belts such as Iran and central China, and linked to very hot beverages. Adenocarcinoma arises from Barrett mucosa. Stromal tumors, leiomyomas, and neuroendocrine tumors are not linked to hot tea. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 5, epidemiology, risk factors, and anatomic distribution).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Zenker diverticulum is listed as a squamous cell carcinoma risk factor (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, squamous cell carcinoma). COMLEX: both cancers have poor five year survival.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — squamous and columnar epithelium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare esophageal squamous cell carcinoma and adenocarcinoma with respect to epidemology, major risk factors, pathoogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical presentation and precursor lesions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old woman with long-standing reflux and obesity has a mass in her distal esophagus that extends into the gastric cardia. Which histology is expected on biopsy?","choices":{"A":"Nests with keratin pearls","B":"Spindle cells with KIT","C":"Small round blue cells","D":"Malignant mucin glands","E":"Sheets of lymphoid cells"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Esophageal adenocarcinoma, linked to GERD, Barrett esophagus, and obesity, arises in the distal third and shows mucin producing malignant glands or signet ring cells. Keratin pearls, spindle cells, small round cells, and lymphoid sheets are not the expected pattern. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 5, morphologic features).","boardPrep":"Step 1: adenocarcinoma risk factors include Barrett esophagus, smoking, GERD, and obesity (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, adenocarcinoma). COMLEX: endoscopy with biopsy confirms the diagnosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 4 — gastric adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare esophageal squamous cell carcinoma and adenocarcinoma with respect to epidemology, major risk factors, pathoogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical presentation and precursor lesions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old woman has a midesophageal lesion in which atypical cells replace the full thickness of the surface lining but do not invade. Which lesion is this?","choices":{"A":"Barrett metaplasia","B":"Basal zone hyperplasia","C":"Reflux esophagitis","D":"Candida esophagitis","E":"Squamous dysplasia"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Squamous cell carcinoma arises from squamous dysplasia, also called carcinoma in situ, driven by cell cycle gene mutations. Barrett metaplasia is columnar. Basal zone hyperplasia and reflux esophagitis are reactive. Candida esophagitis forms pseudomembranes. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 5, pathogenesis and precursor lesions).","boardPrep":"Step 1: tylosis is a squamous cell carcinoma risk factor (lecture SDL 8). COMLEX: early lesions appear as gray white plaques.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — squamous and columnar epithelium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl08_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathogenesis, morphologic features, and clinical manifestations of esophageal obstructive and motility disorders, including stenosis, webs & rings, and achalasia, and correlate the underlying structural or neuromuscular abnormality with patterns of dysphagia.","batch":3,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman swallowed lye as a child and now has slowly worsening dysphagia. Endoscopy shows a localized circumferential narrowing. Which change in the wall causes the narrowing?","choices":{"A":"Myenteric neuron loss","B":"Submucosal fibrosis","C":"Mucosal fold duplication","D":"Muscularis hypertrophy","E":"Venous plexus dilation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Esophageal stenosis is incomplete luminal opening from fibrous thickening of the submucosa with atrophy of the muscularis propria and mucosal damage; acquired forms follow caustic ingestion, GERD, radiation, or systemic sclerosis. Myenteric neuron loss causes achalasia. Fold duplication is not a stenosis mechanism. The muscularis atrophies rather than hypertrophies. Dilated venous plexuses are varices. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 1, esophageal stenosis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: caustic ingestion is a risk factor for esophageal stricture (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, esophageal stricture). COMLEX: caustic strictures also raise squamous cell carcinoma risk (lecture SDL 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 1 — layers of the alimentary canal","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl08_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the major forms of esophageal injury and inflammation-including mucosal lacerations, reflux espophagitis, infectious esophagitis, and eosinophilic esophagitis-based on their etiology, pathogenesis, clinical presentation and characteristic morphologic findings.","batch":3,"stem":"A 45-year-old man has sudden severe chest pain after forceful vomiting. He is tachypneic and hypotensive, with crepitus in his neck and air around the mediastinum on CT. Which injury explains the air?","choices":{"A":"Full thickness wall tear","B":"Superficial mucosal tear","C":"Variceal wall rupture","D":"Candida mucosal erosion","E":"Eosinophilic microabscess"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Boerhaave syndrome is a transmural esophageal rupture from a sudden rise in intraesophageal pressure during vomiting or straining, so air escapes and causes pneumomediastinum, mediastinitis, and shock. A superficial tear is confined to the mucosa and does not perforate the esophageal wall, so air does not enter the mediastinum. Variceal rupture, Candida erosions, and eosinophilic microabscesses do not breach the wall. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 2, mucosal lacerations); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (Boerhaave syndrome: transmural perforation, subcutaneous or mediastinal emphysema).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Boerhaave syndrome usually involves the distal third of the esophagus (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Boerhaave syndrome). COMLEX: barium for contrast esophagography may cause mediastinitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Boerhaave syndrome).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 1 — layers of the alimentary canal","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl08_q03","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the major forms of esophageal injury and inflammation-including mucosal lacerations, reflux espophagitis, infectious esophagitis, and eosinophilic esophagitis-based on their etiology, pathogenesis, clinical presentation and characteristic morphologic findings.","batch":3,"stem":"A 15-year-old girl receiving chemotherapy has odynophagia, and endoscopy shows shallow linear ulcers. Her physician suspects cytomegalovirus. Where should the biopsy target to find viral inclusions?","choices":{"A":"Squamous cells at the margin","B":"Superficial keratin layer","C":"Ulcer base endothelium","D":"Overlying pseudomembrane","E":"Normal mucosa nearby"},"correct":"C","explanation":"CMV inclusions, both nuclear and cytoplasmic, sit within endothelial cells and stromal fibroblasts in the ulcer base, so the base is sampled. Multinucleated squamous cells at the ulcer edge are the herpes finding. A pseudomembrane suggests Candida. The keratin layer and nearby normal mucosa do not hold CMV inclusions. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 2, infectious esophagitis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CMV inclusions are both intranuclear and cytoplasmic (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, CMV esophagitis). COMLEX: infectious esophagitis affects immunocompromised patients.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — esophageal epithelium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl08_q04","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the pathogenesis and morphologic features of gastroesophageal reflux disease and correlate chronic reflux injury with the development of Barrett esophagus, dysplasia and esophagel adenocarcinoma.","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old man has esophageal adenocarcinoma arising in Barrett mucosa, and his tumor is tested for a targetable oncogene that is commonly amplified during Barrett progression. Which gene is it?","choices":{"A":"ERBB2","B":"MYCN","C":"ABL1","D":"PIK3CA","E":"BRAF"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Progression of Barrett dysplasia to adenocarcinoma involves tumor suppressor loss and oncogene amplification such as ERBB2 or KRAS, and ERBB2 (HER2) is a targetable receptor. MYCN, ABL1, PIK3CA, and BRAF are not the oncogenes listed for this progression. MYCN (NMYC) amplification is characteristic of neuroblastoma. ABL1 is activated by the BCR::ABL fusion of chronic myeloid leukemia. Activating BRAF mutations are found in nearly all hairy cell leukemias and about 60% of melanomas. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 3, development of esophageal adenocarcinoma; section 5, pathogenesis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: high grade dysplasia in Barrett mucosa shows loss of nuclear polarity and atypical mitoses (lecture SDL 8). COMLEX: tobacco and obesity raise adenocarcinoma risk.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 4 — gastric adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"MYCN (NMYC) amplification is characteristic of neuroblastoma","C":"ABL1 is activated by the BCR::ABL fusion of chronic myeloid ","E":"Activating BRAF mutations are found in nearly all hairy cell"}},{"id":"trial_sdl08_q05","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathogenesis and clinical significance of esophageal varices by relating portal hypertension and cirrhosis to the development of portosystemic collateral vessels and the risk of life-threatening upper GI hemorrhage.","batch":3,"stem":"A 57-year-old woman has cirrhosis. Besides fixed fibrosis, which active change inside her liver raises resistance to portal flow?","choices":{"A":"Hepatic artery dilation","B":"Lower splanchnic inflow","C":"Reduced endothelin levels","D":"Sinusoidal constriction","E":"Portal vein thrombolysis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Intrahepatic resistance rises from fixed fibrosis and from active sinusoidal vasoconstriction caused by less endothelial NO and more endothelin 1; splanchnic arterial vasodilation then raises portal inflow. Hepatic artery dilation is not the mechanism. Splanchnic inflow rises rather than falls. Endothelin rises rather than falls. Thrombolysis would open rather than block the portal vein. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 4, pathogenesis of portal hypertension).","boardPrep":"Step 1: portal hypertension dilates submucosal veins at portocaval anastomoses (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, esophageal varices). COMLEX: cirrhosis commonly follows alcohol use or viral hepatitis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 4 — vasoactive agents for variceal hemorrhage","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Splanchnic inflow rises rather","C":"Endothelin rises rather than f"}},{"id":"trial_sdl08_q06","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare esophageal squamous cell carcinoma and adenocarcinoma with respect to epidemology, major risk factors, pathoogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical presentation and precursor lesions.","batch":3,"stem":"A 60-year-old man from western Kenya has drunk mursik, a fermented milk, for decades and now has squamous cell carcinoma of the esophagus. Which carcinogen in the drink is implicated?","choices":{"A":"Aflatoxin B1","B":"Vinyl chloride","C":"Benzene vapor","D":"Asbestos fiber","E":"Acetaldehyde"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The lecture lists mursik as a squamous cell carcinoma risk factor because of its acetaldehyde content. Aflatoxin, vinyl chloride, benzene, and asbestos are linked to other cancers. Dietary aflatoxin B1, a mycotoxin from Aspergillus on crops, is a risk factor for hepatocellular carcinoma. Vinyl chloride exposure is linked to hepatic angiosarcoma. Benzene exposure is linked to acute myeloid leukemia. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 5, major risk factors).","boardPrep":"Step 1: tylosis and caustic strictures are squamous cell carcinoma risk factors (lecture SDL 8). COMLEX: squamous cell carcinoma is more common in African American men in Western nations.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — squamous and columnar epithelium","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Dietary aflatoxin B1, a mycotoxin from Aspergillus on crops,","B":"Vinyl chloride exposure is linked to hepatic angiosarcoma.","C":"Benzene exposure is linked to acute myeloid leukemia."}}]},{"sdlNumber":9,"title":"SDL 9 — Pathology of the Stomach","questions":[{"id":"sdl09_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate acute/reactive gastropathy, Helicobacter Pylori gastritis, and autoimmune gastritis based on their etiologies, pathogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical manifestations, and major complications.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which feature of the inflammatory infiltrate typifies acute reactive gastropathy?","choices":{"A":"Few or absent neutrophils","B":"Neutrophilic pit abscesses","C":"Dense plasma cell band","D":"Gland centered lymphocytes","E":"Eosinophil microabscesses"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Reactive gastropathy from NSAIDs, alcohol, bile reflux, or ischemia shows edema, vascular congestion, erosions, and foveolar hyperplasia with a notable paucity or absence of neutrophils. Pit abscesses and a superficial plasma cell rich infiltrate are features of H. pylori gastritis. A deep gland centered lymphocytic infiltrate is autoimmune gastritis. Eosinophil microabscesses are not described. Dense eosinophilic infiltrates define eosinophilic gastritis, often an allergic disorder, not reactive gastropathy. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 1, acute or reactive gastropathy).","boardPrep":"Step 1: NSAIDs, alcohol, smoking, and physiologic stress cause gastritis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastritis general principles). COMLEX: gastritis is evaluated by H. pylori testing and upper endoscopy with biopsy (Bootcamp Gastroenterology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 4 — the gastric mucosal barrier","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Dense eosinophilic infiltrates define eosinophilic gastritis"}},{"id":"sdl09_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate acute/reactive gastropathy, Helicobacter Pylori gastritis, and autoimmune gastritis based on their etiologies, pathogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical manifestations, and major complications.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, where are H. pylori organisms concentrated in a gastric biopsy?","choices":{"A":"Deep oxyntic glands","B":"Submucosal lymphatics","C":"Muscularis propria","D":"Lamina propria capillaries","E":"Superficial mucus layer"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Spiral bacilli of H. pylori are concentrated in the superficial mucus layer and are seen on H&E or silver and immunostains. They do not live in the deep glands, submucosal lymphatics, muscularis propria, or lamina propria vessels. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 1, H. pylori gastritis morphology).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H. pylori is a gram negative motile spiral bacillus that may be seen on silver stain (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, H. pylori infection). COMLEX: chronic H. pylori infection raises the risk of gastric adenocarcinoma and MALT lymphoma (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 4 — the gastric mucosal barrier","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate acute/reactive gastropathy, Helicobacter Pylori gastritis, and autoimmune gastritis based on their etiologies, pathogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical manifestations, and major complications.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, at about what age does autoimmune atrophic gastritis typically present?","choices":{"A":"Around age 20","B":"Around age 35","C":"Around age 60","D":"Around age 45","E":"Around age 80"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Autoimmune atrophic gastritis typically presents around age 60 with manifestations of vitamin B12 deficiency. The other ages do not match the lecture. Around age 20 is too young; autoimmune atrophic gastritis typically presents around age 60 with vitamin B12 deficiency. Around age 35 is too young; autoimmune atrophic gastritis typically presents around age 60 with vitamin B12 deficiency. Around age 45 is too young; autoimmune atrophic gastritis typically presents around age 60 with vitamin B12 deficiency. Around age 80 is too old; autoimmune atrophic gastritis typically presents around age 60 with vitamin B12 deficiency. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 1, autoimmune atrophic gastritis clinical manifestations).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic atrophic gastritis can follow H. pylori or pernicious anemia (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic gastritis). COMLEX: autoimmune gastritis is associated with Hashimoto thyroiditis, type 1 diabetes, and Graves disease (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 4 — the gastric mucosal barrier","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Around age 20 is too young; autoimmune atrophic gastritis ty","B":"Around age 35 is too young; autoimmune atrophic gastritis ty","D":"Around age 45 is too young; autoimmune atrophic gastritis ty","E":"Around age 80 is too old; autoimmune atrophic gastritis typi"}},{"id":"sdl09_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pathogenesis, clinical and morphological features, and complications of gastritis, including the roles of H. pylori, and NSAIDs in PUD, and gastric acid hypersecretion in Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which zone forms the deepest layer of a chronic peptic ulcer base?","choices":{"A":"Fibrinoid necrotic debris","B":"Neutrophilic infiltrate","C":"Granulation tissue","D":"Dense fibrous scar","E":"Foveolar hyperplasia"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A peptic ulcer shows four zones from the surface down: fibrinoid necrotic debris, an active neutrophilic infiltrate, vascularized granulation tissue, and deep dense fibrous scar. Foveolar hyperplasia is a reactive gastropathy feature, not an ulcer zone. Fibrinoid necrotic debris is the most superficial zone of a peptic ulcer, not the deepest. The active neutrophilic infiltrate is the second zone from the surface, above granulation tissue and the scar. Vascularized granulation tissue is the third zone, lying above the deep dense fibrous scar. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 2, morphologic features of PUD).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastric ulcers most often lie on the lesser curvature (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, peptic ulcer disease). COMLEX: gastric ulcers carry a higher malignancy risk than duodenal ulcers (Bootcamp Gastroenterology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 5 — mucosal protection and peptic ulcer pathophysiology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Fibrinoid necrotic debris is the most superficial zone of a ","B":"The active neutrophilic infiltrate is the second zone from t","C":"Vascularized granulation tissue is the third zone, lying abo"}},{"id":"sdl09_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pathogenesis, clinical and morphological features, and complications of gastritis, including the roles of H. pylori, and NSAIDs in PUD, and gastric acid hypersecretion in Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, when does peptic ulcer pain classically occur?","choices":{"A":"Within minutes of swallowing","B":"Mostly on waking each morning","C":"One to three hours after eating","D":"Steady through day and night","E":"Mainly during strenuous exercise"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Peptic ulcer pain is epigastric burning or aching that often occurs 1 to 3 hours after meals or at night and is relieved by food or antacids. Pain within minutes of swallowing suggests an esophageal cause. Pain mainly on waking, steady day and night, or with exertion does not fit, because ulcer pain follows meals by 1 to 3 hours, is worse at night (usually 11 p.m. to 2 a.m.), and is relieved by food or alkali. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 2, clinical features of PUD); Robbins Ch. 17 (peptic ulcer pain timing).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H. pylori infection and NSAIDs are the main causes of peptic ulcer disease (lecture SDL 9). COMLEX: gastric ulcers lead to weight loss and duodenal ulcers to weight gain (Bootcamp Gastroenterology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 5 — mucosal protection and peptic ulcer pathophysiology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Pain mainly on waking, steady "}},{"id":"sdl09_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pathogenesis, clinical and morphological features, and complications of gastritis, including the roles of H. pylori, and NSAIDs in PUD, and gastric acid hypersecretion in Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, how does antral H. pylori infection lead to duodenal ulcer disease?","choices":{"A":"Reduced mucosal prostaglandin synthesis","B":"Autonomous tumor gastrin release","C":"Autoimmune parietal cell loss","D":"Increased duodenal bicarbonate output","E":"Reduced D cell somatostatin release"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In duodenal ulcer disease, antral H. pylori inflammation inhibits D cell somatostatin secretion, removing the brake on G cells; the resulting uninhibited gastrin release causes parietal cell hyperplasia and greater acid delivery to the duodenum, while duodenal bicarbonate secretion falls. Reduced mucosal prostaglandin synthesis is the mechanism of NSAIDs, which inhibit cyclooxygenase and lower mucus, bicarbonate, and blood flow. Autonomous tumor gastrin release is Zollinger-Ellison syndrome from a gastrinoma. Autoimmune parietal cell loss causes atrophic gastritis with low acid rather than duodenal ulcers. Duodenal bicarbonate output falls rather than rises in H. pylori duodenal ulcer disease. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 2, roles of H. pylori and NSAIDs in PUD and ZES mechanism, lines 1714–1752); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (peptic ulcer disease).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists H. pylori in about 90 percent of duodenal ulcers and about 70 percent of gastric ulcers (First Aid 2025 GI, peptic ulcer disease). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes that NSAIDs inhibit cyclooxygenase, lowering PGE2, bicarbonate secretion, and gastric blood flow (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute erosive gastritis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 5 — mucosal protection and peptic ulcer pathophysiology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish the major types of gastric polyps and pecursor epithelial lesions based on their pathogenesis, morphologic features, clinical associations, and risk for malignant transformation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which gastric polyps account for up to 75 percent of all gastric polyps?","choices":{"A":"Fundic gland polyps","B":"Gastric adenomas","C":"Hyperplastic polyps","D":"Hamartomatous polyps","E":"Neuroendocrine polyps"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Inflammatory and hyperplastic polyps account for up to 75% of gastric polyps and arise as reactive responses to chronic mucosal injury, most often chronic H. pylori gastritis. Fundic gland polyps are less common. Adenomas are up to 10% of gastric polyps. Hamartomatous and neuroendocrine polyps are not the majority. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 3, inflammatory and hyperplastic polyps).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic gastritis can cause gastric mucosal atrophy with loss of glands and metaplasia (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic gastritis). COMLEX: hyperplastic polyps peak at ages 50 to 60 (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 1 — cardiac, fundic, and pyloric regions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish the major types of gastric polyps and pecursor epithelial lesions based on their pathogenesis, morphologic features, clinical associations, and risk for malignant transformation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which histologic finding characterizes a fundic gland polyp?","choices":{"A":"Cystic oxyntic glands","B":"Corkscrew foveolar glands","C":"Dysplastic tubular glands","D":"Sheets of signet ring cells","E":"Lymphoepithelial lesions"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Fundic gland polyps are composed of cystically dilated glands lined by parietal, chief, and foveolar cells with minimal inflammation. Elongated corkscrew foveolar glands in an inflamed lamina propria are hyperplastic polyps. Dysplastic glands define adenomas. Signet ring cells are diffuse carcinoma. Lymphoepithelial lesions are MALT lymphoma. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 3, fundic gland polyps).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastric adenocarcinoma is the most common gastric malignancy (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric malignancy). COMLEX: fundic gland polyps lie in the body and fundus (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 1 — cardiac, fundic, and pyloric regions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish the major types of gastric polyps and pecursor epithelial lesions based on their pathogenesis, morphologic features, clinical associations, and risk for malignant transformation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about what risk of invasive adenocarcinoma do gastric adenomas larger than 2 cm carry?","choices":{"A":"Up to 1 percent","B":"Up to 30 percent","C":"Up to 5 percent","D":"Up to 60 percent","E":"Up to 90 percent"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Gastric adenomas carry up to a 30% risk of harboring or progressing to invasive adenocarcinoma when larger than 2 cm. Up to 1 or 5 percent understates the risk, and 60 or 90 percent overstates it for adenomas larger than 2 cm. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 3, gastric adenomas).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H. pylori infection raises the risk of gastric malignancy (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, H. pylori infection). COMLEX: gastric adenomas are most common in men aged 50 to 60 (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 1 — cardiac, fundic, and pyloric regions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Up to 1 or 5 percent understat"}},{"id":"sdl09_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, precursor lesions, morphologic patterns, and clinical features of gastric adenocarcinoma, including the relationship of chronic gastritis, intestinal metaplasia, dysplasia and injerited or acquired molecular alteration to tumor development.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which molecular subtype of intestinal type gastric adenocarcinoma features TP53 mutations and RTK RAS amplifications?","choices":{"A":"Microsatellite instability","B":"Epstein Barr virus type","C":"Genomically stable type","D":"Hypermutated POLE type","E":"Chromosomal instability"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The chromosomal instability subtype is characterized by TP53 mutations, Wnt pathway mutations, SMAD4 mutations, and RTK RAS pathway amplifications such as ERBB2, KRAS, EGFR, and MET. Microsatellite instability and Epstein Barr virus association are separate subtypes. Genomically stable and POLE types are not described in the lecture. Genomically stable tumors correspond mainly to diffuse-type gastric cancer, not intestinal type. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 4, pathogenesis and molecular alterations).","boardPrep":"Step 1: EBV is a risk factor for gastric malignancy (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric malignancy). COMLEX: intestinal type tumors form glands (tubular or papillary) (Bootcamp Gastroenterology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 5 — esophageal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Genomically stable tumors correspond mainly to diffuse-type "}},{"id":"sdl09_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, precursor lesions, morphologic patterns, and clinical features of gastric adenocarcinoma, including the relationship of chronic gastritis, intestinal metaplasia, dysplasia and injerited or acquired molecular alteration to tumor development.","batch":1,"stem":"Spread of gastric adenocarcinoma to the left supraclavicular lymph node produces which named finding?","choices":{"A":"Krukenberg tumor","B":"Virchow node","C":"Sister Mary Joseph nodule","D":"Irish node","E":"Blumer shelf"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The left supraclavicular sentinel node is the Virchow node. A Krukenberg tumor is ovarian metastasis, a Sister Mary Joseph nodule is a periumbilical node, an Irish node is the left axillary node, and a Blumer shelf is a deposit in the pouch of Douglas. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 4, metastatic patterns); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (Virchow node, Sister Mary Joseph nodule, Krukenberg tumor). Irish node and Blumer shelf names are not used in the lecture; their sites are.","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastric malignancy may present with the Leser Trélat sign or acanthosis nigricans (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric malignancy). COMLEX: gastric cancer is difficult to detect early (Bootcamp Gastroenterology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 5 — esophageal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"A Krukenberg tumor is ovarian ","E":"A Krukenberg tumor is ovarian "}},{"id":"sdl09_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, precursor lesions, morphologic patterns, and clinical features of gastric adenocarcinoma, including the relationship of chronic gastritis, intestinal metaplasia, dysplasia and injerited or acquired molecular alteration to tumor development.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, where in the stomach do intestinal type adenocarcinomas mainly arise?","choices":{"A":"Fundus and greater curve","B":"Cardia near the junction","C":"Body along the greater curvature","D":"Antrum or lesser curvature","E":"Pylorus and duodenal bulb"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Intestinal type gastric adenocarcinoma is located predominantly in the antrum or lesser curvature and forms bulky exophytic or ulcerated masses of malignant glands. Diffuse type tumors infiltrate the wall without a discrete mass. Robbins adds that the lesser curvature is involved more often than the greater curvature, so the fundus, greater curve, and cardia are not the main sites, and the duodenal bulb lies outside the stomach. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 4, morphologic patterns); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (intestinal type: lesser curvature); Robbins Ch. 17 (most distal tumors in the antrum, lesser curvature more than greater).","boardPrep":"Step 1: intestinal type gastric adenocarcinoma occurs at an older age than diffuse type (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, intestinal type gastric adenocarcinoma). COMLEX: gastric malignancy can cause gastric outlet obstruction and upper GI bleeding (Bootcamp Gastroenterology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 5 — esophageal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Robbins adds that the lesser c","C":"Robbins adds that the lesser c"}},{"id":"sdl09_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate gastric lymphomas, neuroendocrine tumors, and gastrointestinal stromal tumors based on their pathogenesis, molecular or immunophenotypic features, morphology, clinical associations and principles of diagnosis and management.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which marker pattern fits the cells of gastric MALT lymphoma?","choices":{"A":"CD20 positive CD5 negative","B":"CD5 and CD23 positive","C":"CD10 and BCL6 positive","D":"CD3 positive CD20 negative","E":"CD117 and DOG1 positive"},"correct":"A","explanation":"MALT lymphoma cells express mature B cell markers CD19 and CD20, are negative for CD5 and CD10, are CD43 positive in about 25% of cases, and show light chain restriction. CD5 positivity and CD10 positivity are excluded. CD3 marks T cells. CD117 and DOG1 mark GIST. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 5, gastric lymphomas).","boardPrep":"Step 1: MALT lymphoma occurs at an older age and follows persistent H. pylori inflammation (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, MALT lymphoma). COMLEX: MALT lymphoma is about 5% of gastric malignancies (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — mucosal specializations and MALT","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"CD5 positivity and CD10 positi"}},{"id":"sdl09_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate gastric lymphomas, neuroendocrine tumors, and gastrointestinal stromal tumors based on their pathogenesis, molecular or immunophenotypic features, morphology, clinical associations and principles of diagnosis and management.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, gastrointestinal stromal tumors arise from which cells?","choices":{"A":"Smooth muscle myocytes","B":"Cajal pacemaker cells","C":"Enterochromaffin cells","D":"Peripheral Schwann cells","E":"Stromal myofibroblasts"},"correct":"B","explanation":"GISTs are the most common abdominal mesenchymal tumor and arise from the interstitial cells of Cajal, the pacemaker cells of the gut. Enterochromaffin type cells give rise to neuroendocrine tumors. Smooth muscle cells, Schwann cells, and myofibroblasts are not the described origin. Gastric mesenchymal tumors of smooth muscle origin are leiomyomas or leiomyosarcomas, not GISTs. Tumors of nerve sheath origin in the stomach are schwannomas, not GISTs. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 5, gastrointestinal stromal tumors).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GIST is most common in the stomach but can arise elsewhere in the GI tract (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, GIST). COMLEX: GIST occurs at an older age (Bootcamp Gastroenterology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — gastric pacemaking and propulsion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Gastric mesenchymal tumors of smooth muscle origin are leiom","D":"Tumors of nerve sheath origin in the stomach are schwannomas"}},{"id":"sdl09_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate gastric lymphomas, neuroendocrine tumors, and gastrointestinal stromal tumors based on their pathogenesis, molecular or immunophenotypic features, morphology, clinical associations and principles of diagnosis and management.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which staining result supports the diagnosis of a gastric neuroendocrine tumor?","choices":{"A":"CD117 and DOG1 staining","B":"CD20 and CD19 staining","C":"Desmin and actin staining","D":"Chromogranin A staining","E":"S100 and SOX10 staining"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Gastric neuroendocrine tumors express synaptophysin and chromogranin A, and the diagnosis is made by histology with this positivity. CD117 and DOG1 mark GIST. CD20 and CD19 mark B cell lymphoma. Desmin, actin, S100, and SOX10 are not the described markers. Desmin and actin staining marks smooth muscle tumors such as leiomyoma, not neuroendocrine tumors. S100 and SOX10 staining marks Schwann cell tumors such as schwannoma, not neuroendocrine tumors. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 5, neuroendocrine tumors).","boardPrep":"Step 1: carcinoid syndrome causes watery diarrhea, flushing, and bronchospasm (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, carcinoid tumor). COMLEX: urinary 5 HIAA is used to diagnose carcinoid tumors (Bootcamp Gastroenterology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 5 — pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Desmin and actin staining marks smooth muscle tumors such as","E":"S100 and SOX10 staining marks Schwann cell tumors such as sc"}},{"id":"sdl09_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate acute/reactive gastropathy, Helicobacter Pylori gastritis, and autoimmune gastritis based on their etiologies, pathogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical manifestations, and major complications.","batch":2,"stem":"A 71-year-old man had a partial gastrectomy with a gastrojejunal anastomosis decades ago and has chronic bile reflux. A gastric biopsy shows mucosal edema, vascular congestion, and corkscrew foveolar hyperplasia. Which diagnosis fits best?","choices":{"A":"Helicobacter gastritis","B":"Reactive gastropathy","C":"Autoimmune gastritis","D":"Menetrier disease","E":"MALT lymphoma"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Bile reflux injures the epithelium directly, and the reactive gastropathy pattern is edema, congestion, foveolar hyperplasia with corkscrew profiles, and few or no neutrophils. H. pylori gastritis has neutrophils and plasma cells. Autoimmune gastritis has a deep lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate in the body. Ménétrier disease causes giant folds with protein loss. MALT lymphoma is a dense lymphoid infiltrate. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 1, acute or reactive gastropathy).","boardPrep":"Step 1: previous gastric surgery raises the risk of gastric adenocarcinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric malignancy). COMLEX: management of gastritis includes avoiding NSAIDs, tobacco, and alcohol (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute erosive gastritis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 4 — gastric mucosal barrier and its disruption","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"H. pylori gastritis has neutro"}},{"id":"sdl09_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate acute/reactive gastropathy, Helicobacter Pylori gastritis, and autoimmune gastritis based on their etiologies, pathogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical manifestations, and major complications.","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old man has chronic dyspepsia, and a silver stain of his antral biopsy shows curved rods. Which inflammatory finding marks active disease in this gastritis?","choices":{"A":"Eosinophilic microabscesses","B":"Noncaseating granulomas","C":"Deep lymphoid gland injury","D":"Mast cell infiltrates","E":"Intraepithelial neutrophils"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In H. pylori gastritis, intraepithelial and lamina propria neutrophils form pit abscesses, alongside a superficial infiltrate of plasma cells, lymphocytes, and macrophages. Eosinophilic microabscesses and granulomas are not the described pattern. Deep gland centered lymphocytic injury is autoimmune gastritis. Mast cell infiltrates are not described. Dense eosinophilic infiltrates define eosinophilic gastritis, often an allergic disorder, not active H. pylori gastritis. Granulomatous gastritis is most often caused by Crohn disease, followed by sarcoidosis, and granulomas do not mark H. pylori activity. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 1, H. pylori gastritis morphology).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H. pylori is treated with triple therapy (amoxicillin, clarithromycin, and a PPI) or quadruple therapy (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, H. pylori infection). COMLEX: H. pylori infection usually starts in the antrum and spreads to the body (Bootcamp Gastroenterology).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — H. pylori eradication regimens","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Dense eosinophilic infiltrates define eosinophilic gastritis","B":"Granulomatous gastritis is most often caused by Crohn diseas"}},{"id":"sdl09_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate acute/reactive gastropathy, Helicobacter Pylori gastritis, and autoimmune gastritis based on their etiologies, pathogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical manifestations, and major complications.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old woman with type 1 diabetes has a macrocytic anemia. Biopsy of the gastric body shows a deep gland centered infiltrate of lymphocytes and plasma cells without prominent neutrophils, with goblet and Paneth cells in the mucosa. Which other change is expected in her oxyntic mucosa?","choices":{"A":"Foveolar hyperplasia","B":"Neutrophilic pit abscesses","C":"Spiral surface bacilli","D":"Loss of chief cells","E":"Signet ring cells"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Autoimmune atrophic gastritis shows thinning of the body and fundic mucosa, a deep gland centered lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate, extensive loss of parietal and chief cells, intestinal metaplasia, and neuroendocrine cell hyperplasia. Foveolar hyperplasia is a reactive or Ménétrier change. Pit abscesses and spiral bacilli are H. pylori gastritis. Signet ring cells are diffuse carcinoma. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 1, autoimmune atrophic gastritis morphology).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pernicious anemia raises the risk of gastric adenocarcinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric malignancy). COMLEX: autoimmune gastritis is linked to HLA predisposition (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 3 — consequences of gastric cell loss","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pathogenesis, clinical and morphological features, and complications of gastritis, including the roles of H. pylori, and NSAIDs in PUD, and gastric acid hypersecretion in Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with a known posterior duodenal ulcer now has constant epigastric pain radiating to his back, and his serum lipase is raised. Which complication is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Penetration into pancreas","B":"Free perforation","C":"Gastric outlet obstruction","D":"Acute ulcer hemorrhage","E":"Malignant transformation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Penetration of an ulcer into an adjacent structure such as the pancreas causes constant pain radiating to the back and can raise pancreatic enzymes. Free perforation causes peritonitis and free air. Outlet obstruction causes vomiting. Hemorrhage causes melena or hematemesis. Duodenal ulcers are usually benign. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 2, complications of PUD); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (duodenal ulcers usually benign).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a posterior duodenal ulcer can bleed from the gastroduodenal artery (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, ulcer hemorrhage). COMLEX: H. pylori and gastrinoma cause duodenal ulcers (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, peptic ulcer disease).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — acute and chronic pancreatitis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Duodenal ulcers are usually be"}},{"id":"sdl09_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pathogenesis, clinical and morphological features, and complications of gastritis, including the roles of H. pylori, and NSAIDs in PUD, and gastric acid hypersecretion in Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":2,"stem":"A 49-year-old woman has endoscopy for epigastric pain. A 1.5 cm round gastric lesion has clean, flat, nonelevated borders and a smooth base, and biopsy shows necrotic debris over granulation tissue and scar. Which lesion is this?","choices":{"A":"Intestinal type carcinoma","B":"Ulcerated stromal tumor","C":"Gastric MALT lymphoma","D":"Linitis plastica","E":"Benign peptic ulcer"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A peptic ulcer is classically a solitary, small, round to oval punched out defect with clean nonelevated borders and a smooth flat base, with zones of necrotic debris, neutrophils, granulation tissue, and scar. Intestinal type carcinoma forms bulky or ulcerated masses of malignant glands. A stromal tumor is a submucosal mass. MALT lymphoma is a lymphoid infiltrate. Linitis plastica thickens the whole wall. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 2, morphologic features of PUD; section 4, morphologic patterns).","boardPrep":"Step 1: NSAIDs and H. pylori cause gastric ulcers (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, peptic ulcer disease). COMLEX: PUD is managed with a PPI or H2 antagonist (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute erosive gastritis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — treatment and prevention of peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pathogenesis, clinical and morphological features, and complications of gastritis, including the roles of H. pylori, and NSAIDs in PUD, and gastric acid hypersecretion in Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old woman with years of ulcer disease near the pylorus now vomits undigested food several hours after meals, and a succussion splash is heard. Which change most likely causes her symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Pyloric muscle hypertrophy","B":"Vagal motor neuron loss","C":"Twisting of the stomach","D":"Pyloric channel scarring","E":"Swallowed bezoar formation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Peptic ulcers can cause gastric outlet obstruction from edema or fibrous scarring. Pyloric muscle hypertrophy is a congenital infant disorder. Vagal motor neuron loss, gastric volvulus, and bezoars are not ulcer complications in the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 2, complications of PUD).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastric outlet obstruction is a complication of gastric malignancy (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric malignancy). COMLEX: intractable pain is also a complication of PUD (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 3 — gastric mixing, propulsion, and pyloric resistance","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Vagal motor neuron loss, gastr"}},{"id":"sdl09_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish the major types of gastric polyps and pecursor epithelial lesions based on their pathogenesis, morphologic features, clinical associations, and risk for malignant transformation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 14-year-old girl whose father had colectomy for hundreds of colon adenomas has many small polyps in her gastric body and fundus made of cystically dilated glands. Which gene is most likely mutated in her germline?","choices":{"A":"CDH1","B":"STK11","C":"APC","D":"SMAD4","E":"KRAS"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Syndromic fundic gland polyps arise with germline APC mutations in familial adenomatous polyposis, which is dominant and fits her father; they can develop dysplasia. CDH1 causes hereditary diffuse gastric cancer. STK11 and SMAD4 cause hamartomatous polyposis syndromes. KRAS is a somatic oncogene. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 3, fundic gland polyps). The STK11 and SMAD4 syndromes are not in the lecture; Robbins links STK11 to Peutz-Jeghers syndrome and SMAD4 to juvenile polyposis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: adenomatous polyps are the most common colonic polyps (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, colonic polyps). COMLEX: fundic gland polyps can also arise with MUTYH mutations (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 1 — cardiac, fundic, and pyloric regions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"STK11 and SMAD4 cause hamartom","D":"STK11 and SMAD4 cause hamartom"}},{"id":"sdl09_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish the major types of gastric polyps and pecursor epithelial lesions based on their pathogenesis, morphologic features, clinical associations, and risk for malignant transformation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man has taken omeprazole for ten years. Endoscopy shows several small smooth polyps in the gastric body, and biopsy confirms the polyp type linked to his acid suppression. What is their malignant potential?","choices":{"A":"Negligible cancer risk","B":"Risk similar to adenomas","C":"High risk needing gastrectomy","D":"Risk rising after 1 cm","E":"Frequent HER2 amplification"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Sporadic and PPI associated fundic gland polyps carry no significant risk of malignant transformation, unlike syndromic polyps in FAP. Adenomas carry a high risk. Hyperplastic polyp risk rises in pedunculated polyps over 1 cm. HER2 amplification is a carcinoma change. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 3, fundic gland polyps; hyperplastic polyps; gastric adenomas).","boardPrep":"Step 1: PPIs are used for gastritis, PUD, GERD, and stress ulcer prophylaxis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: sporadic fundic gland polyps have increased with widespread PPI use (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — drug classes for acid suppression","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish the major types of gastric polyps and pecursor epithelial lesions based on their pathogenesis, morphologic features, clinical associations, and risk for malignant transformation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old woman has chronic atrophic gastritis. Endoscopy shows a solitary 2.5 cm velvety antral polyp, and biopsy shows enlarged elongated hyperchromatic nuclei with pseudostratification and crowded glands. Which management is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Repeat biopsy in five years","B":"Complete endoscopic resection","C":"PPI therapy without resection","D":"Reassurance and no follow up","E":"Antibiotics without resection"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This is a gastric adenoma, a true neoplastic polyp with epithelial dysplasia that arises on chronic gastritis with atrophy and metaplasia; its risk of harboring or progressing to invasive carcinoma requires complete endoscopic resection and surveillance. Delayed biopsy, acid suppression, reassurance, or antibiotics leave the dysplastic lesion in place. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 3, gastric adenomas).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastric malignancy is diagnosed by endoscopy (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric malignancy). COMLEX: high grade dysplasia carries a substantial risk of progression to invasive adenocarcinoma (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — Barrett esophagus and dysplasia","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Delayed biopsy, acid suppressi","C":"Delayed biopsy, acid suppressi"}},{"id":"sdl09_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, precursor lesions, morphologic patterns, and clinical features of gastric adenocarcinoma, including the relationship of chronic gastritis, intestinal metaplasia, dysplasia and injerited or acquired molecular alteration to tumor development.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman has early satiety and weight loss. Imaging shows a diffusely thickened, rigid stomach wall without a discrete mass, and biopsy shows single discohesive cells infiltrating the wall. What mainly stiffens the wall?","choices":{"A":"Submucosal edema","B":"Smooth muscle hypertrophy","C":"Amyloid deposition","D":"Desmoplastic stroma","E":"Dense lymphoid infiltrate"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Diffuse type gastric adenocarcinoma infiltrates as individual mucin filled cells and elicits an intense desmoplastic stromal reaction that stiffens and thickens the wall without a mass, giving the leather bottle appearance of linitis plastica. Edema, muscle hypertrophy, amyloid, and lymphoid infiltrates are not the described cause. Lymphoid aggregates in chronic H. pylori gastritis are induced MALT that can transform into lymphoma, a different process from the desmoplastic stroma of diffuse-type carcinoma. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 4, morphologic patterns).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diffuse type gastric adenocarcinoma has obscure borders and poorly cohesive cells (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, diffuse type gastric adenocarcinoma). COMLEX: early satiety is especially common in diffuse type cancer (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 5 — esophageal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Lymphoid aggregates in chronic H. pylori gastritis are induc"}},{"id":"sdl09_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, precursor lesions, morphologic patterns, and clinical features of gastric adenocarcinoma, including the relationship of chronic gastritis, intestinal metaplasia, dysplasia and injerited or acquired molecular alteration to tumor development.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man grew up eating salt preserved and smoked fish and has smoked for decades. He now has a bulky antral adenocarcinoma of the intestinal type. Which dietary carcinogen class is most implicated?","choices":{"A":"Aflatoxin B1","B":"Acrylamide","C":"Nitrosamines","D":"Benzene","E":"Vinyl chloride"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Intestinal type gastric adenocarcinoma risk factors include chronic H. pylori gastritis, a high salt diet, N nitroso compounds or nitrosamines, and smoking. Aflatoxin is linked to liver cancer. Acrylamide, benzene, and vinyl chloride are not the listed gastric risk factors. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 4, risk factors and precursor lesions); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (nitrosamines, smoked and salt preserved foods).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastric cancer metastasizes to the periumbilical node as a Sister Mary Joseph nodule (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric malignancy). COMLEX: tobacco use raises gastric cancer risk (Bootcamp Gastroenterology).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 5 — esophageal cancer risk factors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, precursor lesions, morphologic patterns, and clinical features of gastric adenocarcinoma, including the relationship of chronic gastritis, intestinal metaplasia, dysplasia and injerited or acquired molecular alteration to tumor development.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman has bilateral solid ovarian masses, and the ovarian tissue contains mucin filled cells with nuclei pushed to the periphery. Her stomach wall is thickened. Which term describes the ovarian lesions?","choices":{"A":"Brenner tumor","B":"Krukenberg tumor","C":"Dysgerminoma","D":"Mature teratoma","E":"Granulosa cell tumor"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Metastasis of gastric signet ring cell carcinoma to both ovaries is a Krukenberg tumor. Brenner tumors, dysgerminomas, teratomas, and granulosa cell tumors are primary ovarian tumors. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 4, metastatic patterns); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (Krukenberg tumor). The primary ovarian tumor types are not in the lecture.","boardPrep":"Step 1: diffuse type gastric adenocarcinoma occurs at a younger age (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, diffuse type gastric adenocarcinoma). COMLEX: gastric cancer can spread to the pouch of Douglas (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 5 — esophageal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate gastric lymphomas, neuroendocrine tumors, and gastrointestinal stromal tumors based on their pathogenesis, molecular or immunophenotypic features, morphology, clinical associations and principles of diagnosis and management.","batch":2,"stem":"A 69-year-old woman has early stage gastric marginal zone B cell lymphoma arising in mucosa infected with H. pylori. Which first treatment can produce complete remission?","choices":{"A":"Trastuzumab therapy","B":"Total gastrectomy","C":"Antibiotic therapy","D":"Octreotide therapy","E":"Radiation therapy first"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Early stage H. pylori dependent MALT lymphomas frequently achieve complete remission after antibiotic eradication of H. pylori, because chronic antigen stimulation drives them. Trastuzumab targets HER2. Gastrectomy is not first line. Octreotide is a somatostatin analog for neuroendocrine tumors. Radiation is reserved for resistant tumors. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 5, gastric lymphomas); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (MALT lymphoma: eradication therapy).","boardPrep":"Step 1: quadruple therapy for H. pylori uses metronidazole, tetracycline, bismuth, and a PPI (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, H. pylori infection). COMLEX: MALT lymphoma presents with dyspepsia, epigastric pain, or GI bleeding (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — H. pylori eradication regimens","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate gastric lymphomas, neuroendocrine tumors, and gastrointestinal stromal tumors based on their pathogenesis, molecular or immunophenotypic features, morphology, clinical associations and principles of diagnosis and management.","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old man with autoimmune gastritis has several small yellow submucosal nodules in the gastric body. Biopsy shows uniform cells with salt and pepper chromatin, and staining confirms neuroendocrine origin. Which finding best predicts an indolent course?","choices":{"A":"Low Ki 67 index","B":"TP53 and RB1 loss","C":"High mitotic count","D":"Liver metastases","E":"KIT gene mutation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Well differentiated neuroendocrine tumors have a low Ki 67 proliferative index, and small gastric NETs associated with autoimmune gastritis have an indolent course managed by endoscopic resection. High Ki 67 with TP53 and RB1 mutations marks poorly differentiated carcinoma. Liver metastases signal aggressive disease and carcinoid syndrome. KIT mutations drive GIST. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 5, neuroendocrine tumors).","boardPrep":"Step 1: about one third of carcinoid tumors metastasize, usually to the liver (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, carcinoid tumor). COMLEX: carcinoid tumors are managed with resection or a somatostatin analog such as octreotide (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, carcinoid tumor).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 5 — pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate gastric lymphomas, neuroendocrine tumors, and gastrointestinal stromal tumors based on their pathogenesis, molecular or immunophenotypic features, morphology, clinical associations and principles of diagnosis and management.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man has melena. CT shows a 9 cm gastric submucosal mass with liver lesions, and biopsy shows spindle cells that stain strongly for CD117 and DOG1. Which drug is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Rituximab","B":"Trastuzumab","C":"Octreotide","D":"Cisplatin","E":"Imatinib"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Unresectable or metastatic GISTs with KIT or PDGFRA mutations are treated with tyrosine kinase inhibitors such as imatinib. Rituximab targets CD20 on B cells. Trastuzumab targets HER2. Octreotide treats neuroendocrine tumors. Cisplatin is not the described therapy. Cisplatin is a platinum analog that damages DNA like an alkylating agent, whereas imatinib targets the c-KIT tyrosine kinase expressed by GISTs. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 5, gastrointestinal stromal tumors).","boardPrep":"Step 1: c KIT and PDGFRA mutations cause aberrant receptor tyrosine kinase activity in GIST (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, GIST). COMLEX: GIST malignant risk correlates with size, mitotic count, and location (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 3 — gastric pacemaking and propulsion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Cisplatin is a platinum analog that damages DNA like an alky"}},{"id":"trial_sdl09_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate acute/reactive gastropathy, Helicobacter Pylori gastritis, and autoimmune gastritis based on their etiologies, pathogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical manifestations, and major complications.","batch":3,"stem":"A 44-year-old man has leg edema and a low serum albumin. Endoscopy shows giant cerebriform folds in the gastric body, and biopsy shows marked foveolar hyperplasia with atrophy of the oxyntic glands. Overactivity of which signaling pathway causes this disease?","choices":{"A":"Gastrin via CCK2","B":"Histamine via H2","C":"Hedgehog via Smoothened","D":"Wnt via Frizzled","E":"TGF alpha via EGFR"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Ménétrier disease results from increased TGF alpha binding the EGF receptor, which drives foveolar hyperplasia and mucus overproduction with parietal and chief cell atrophy; the protein losing gastropathy causes hypoalbuminemia and edema. Gastrin and histamine stimulate acid secretion rather than foveolar hyperplasia. Hedgehog and Wnt signaling are not the described pathway. Sources: Bootcamp Gastroenterology (Ménétrier disease: TGF alpha binds EGFR, protein losing gastropathy, foveolar hyperplasia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ménétrier disease shows thick gastric folds resembling cerebral gyri on imaging (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Ménétrier disease). COMLEX: Ménétrier disease raises the risk of gastric malignancy (Bootcamp Gastroenterology).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — gastric epithelial cell types","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Gastrin and histamine stimulat"}},{"id":"trial_sdl09_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pathogenesis, clinical and morphological features, and complications of gastritis, including the roles of H. pylori, and NSAIDs in PUD, and gastric acid hypersecretion in Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":3,"stem":"A 47-year-old man has refractory ulcers, and imaging for a suspected gastrinoma searches his pancreas. Besides the pancreas, where do these tumors commonly arise?","choices":{"A":"Gastric antrum","B":"Gastric fundus","C":"Splenic hilum","D":"Duodenal wall","E":"Distal ileum"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Gastrinomas arise in the non beta cell pancreas, the duodenum, or peripancreatic soft tissue, the region called the gastrinoma triangle. Antral G cells make normal gastrin but are not the described tumor site. The fundus, splenic hilum, and distal ileum are not gastrinoma sites in the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 2, Zollinger-Ellison syndrome pathogenesis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the lecture links Zollinger-Ellison syndrome to MEN1 (lecture SDL 9, neuroendocrine tumors). COMLEX: gastric NETs can arise in the hypergastrinemia of Zollinger-Ellison syndrome (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 5 — insulinoma, gastrinoma, glucagonoma, and VIPoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Antral G cells make normal gas"}},{"id":"trial_sdl09_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish the major types of gastric polyps and pecursor epithelial lesions based on their pathogenesis, morphologic features, clinical associations, and risk for malignant transformation.","batch":3,"stem":"A 57-year-old woman has a gastric polyp that arose on chronic H. pylori gastritis. It shows elongated corkscrew foveolar glands in an edematous, inflamed lamina propria. Which feature most raises its risk of dysplasia?","choices":{"A":"Pedunculated and over 1 cm","B":"Location in the fundus","C":"Many tiny sessile polyps","D":"Patient age under thirty years","E":"Smooth surface mucosa"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Hyperplastic polyps carry a dysplasia risk of 1% to 20%, which rises significantly in pedunculated polyps larger than 1 cm. Fundic location, multiple tiny polyps, young age, and a smooth surface are not the described risk features. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 3, inflammatory and hyperplastic polyps).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H. pylori infection raises the risk of peptic ulcer disease (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, H. pylori infection). COMLEX: hyperplastic polyps are most common in the antrum (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — H. pylori eradication regimens","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl09_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, precursor lesions, morphologic patterns, and clinical features of gastric adenocarcinoma, including the relationship of chronic gastritis, intestinal metaplasia, dysplasia and injerited or acquired molecular alteration to tumor development.","batch":3,"stem":"A 39-year-old woman has a sporadic diffuse type gastric adenocarcinoma that has lost E-cadherin protein, but sequencing finds no mutation in the gene that encodes it. Which change most likely silenced the gene?","choices":{"A":"Gene amplification","B":"Activating point mutation","C":"Promoter hypermethylation","D":"Balanced translocation","E":"Microsatellite expansion"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Diffuse type gastric adenocarcinoma is driven by loss of the E-cadherin gene in about half of sporadic cases, either by loss of function mutation or by promoter hypermethylation, an epigenetic change that silences an intact sequence. Amplification and activating mutations increase gene activity. Balanced translocations and microsatellite expansions are not the described mechanism. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 4, pathogenesis and molecular alterations).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diffuse type gastric adenocarcinoma shows signet ring cells (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, diffuse type gastric adenocarcinoma). COMLEX: hereditary diffuse gastric cancer arises de novo without identified precursor lesions (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — molecular progression in Barrett esophagus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl09_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate gastric lymphomas, neuroendocrine tumors, and gastrointestinal stromal tumors based on their pathogenesis, molecular or immunophenotypic features, morphology, clinical associations and principles of diagnosis and management.","batch":3,"stem":"A 73-year-old man has gastric MALT lymphoma. Which fusion protein is produced by the most characteristic translocation of this tumor?","choices":{"A":"API2 MALT1 fusion","B":"BCR ABL1 fusion","C":"PML RARA fusion","D":"MYC IGH fusion","E":"EWSR1 FLI1 fusion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The t(11;18)(q21;q21) translocation forms the API2 MALT1 fusion protein. BCR ABL1 is chronic myeloid leukemia, PML RARA is acute promyelocytic leukemia, MYC IGH is Burkitt lymphoma, and EWSR1 FLI1 is Ewing sarcoma. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 5, gastric lymphomas). The other fusions are not in the lecture.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MALT lymphoma forms lymphoid follicles within the gastric mucosa (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, MALT lymphoma). COMLEX: MALT lymphoma is diagnosed by endoscopic biopsy with IgH clonality testing (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — mucosal lymphoid tissue","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl09_q06","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate gastric lymphomas, neuroendocrine tumors, and gastrointestinal stromal tumors based on their pathogenesis, molecular or immunophenotypic features, morphology, clinical associations and principles of diagnosis and management.","batch":3,"stem":"A 13-year-old girl has a paraganglioma and a gastric stromal tumor. Tumor testing finds no KIT or PDGFRA mutation. Which defect most likely drives her tumors?","choices":{"A":"CDH1 mutation","B":"SDH deficiency","C":"APC mutation","D":"Menin gene mutation","E":"RET mutation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"GISTs without KIT or PDGFRA mutations can be driven by succinate dehydrogenase complex mutations, which are linked to Carney Stratakis syndrome with paraganglioma. CDH1 causes diffuse gastric cancer. APC causes FAP. MEN1 and RET cause endocrine neoplasia syndromes. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 5, gastrointestinal stromal tumors). The paraganglioma link is from Robbins: germline loss of succinate dehydrogenase function raises the risk of GIST and paraganglioma (Carney-Stratakis syndrome).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GIST mutations in KIT and PDGFRA are mutually exclusive (lecture SDL 9). COMLEX: gastric GISTs are less aggressive than small intestinal GISTs (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 3 — gastric pacemaking and propulsion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"MEN1 and RET cause endocrine n"}}]},{"sdlNumber":10,"title":"SDL 10 — Pharmacology of acid-peptic diseases, antiemetics, and variceal hemorrhage","questions":[{"id":"sdl10_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Compare and contrast the mechanisms of action, pharmacokinetics, adverse effects, and clinical indications of the major drug classes used to treat gastroesophageal reflux disease, and select appropriate therapy based on clinical presentation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, how long does acid suppression from a single proton pump inhibitor dose last despite a plasma half life of about 1.5 hours?","choices":{"A":"About 90 minutes","B":"About 6 hours","C":"About 3 days","D":"About 2 weeks","E":"About 24 hours"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Proton pump inhibitors have a short plasma half life of about 1.5 hours but act for about 24 hours because they bind the H+/K+ ATPase covalently and irreversibly, so acid secretion returns only as new pumps are made. The shorter and longer durations do not match the lecture. About 90 minutes is close to the plasma half life of 1.5 hours, but acid suppression lasts about 24 hours because the PPI binds the pump irreversibly. About 6 hours is too short; irreversible binding to the H+/K+ ATPase means acid secretion returns only as new pumps are made, giving about 24 hours of suppression. About 3 days is too long; a single PPI dose suppresses acid for about 24 hours, until new pumps are made. About 2 weeks is far too long; a single PPI dose suppresses acid for about 24 hours, until new pumps are made. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 1, PPI pharmacokinetics).","boardPrep":"Step 1: proton pump inhibitors irreversibly inhibit the H+/K+ ATPase on parietal cells (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: omeprazole and esomeprazole inhibit CYP2C19 (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 2 — parietal cells and their products","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"About 90 minutes is close to the plasma half life of 1.5 hou","B":"About 6 hours is too short; irreversible binding to the H+/K","C":"About 3 days is too long; a single PPI dose suppresses acid ","D":"About 2 weeks is far too long; a single PPI dose suppresses "}},{"id":"sdl10_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Compare and contrast the mechanisms of action, pharmacokinetics, adverse effects, and clinical indications of the major drug classes used to treat gastroesophageal reflux disease, and select appropriate therapy based on clinical presentation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which antisecretory drug class requires a dose reduction in renal insufficiency?","choices":{"A":"Proton pump inhibitors","B":"Alginate raft formers","C":"H2 receptor antagonists","D":"Calcium carbonate antacids","E":"Dopamine D2 antagonists"},"correct":"C","explanation":"H2 receptor antagonists are cleared by hepatic metabolism and renal secretion, so their dose is reduced in renal insufficiency. Proton pump inhibitors are cleared by hepatic CYP2C19 and CYP3A4 and need no renal adjustment. Alginates and antacids act locally, although magnesium and aluminum accumulate in renal failure. Dopamine D2 antagonists are prokinetics rather than antisecretory drugs. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 1, H2 receptor antagonist pharmacokinetics).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H2 antagonists reversibly inhibit H2 receptors on parietal cells (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: H2 antagonists are used for gastritis, PUD, GERD, and stress ulcer prophylaxis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 2 — parietal cells and their products","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Alginates and antacids act loc"}},{"id":"sdl10_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Compare and contrast the mechanisms of action, pharmacokinetics, adverse effects, and clinical indications of the major drug classes used to treat gastroesophageal reflux disease, and select appropriate therapy based on clinical presentation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, how far apart should antacid doses be spaced from tetracyclines, fluoroquinolones, or iron?","choices":{"A":"Ten minutes","B":"Six hours","C":"Twelve hours","D":"Two hours","E":"One full day"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Antacids bind other drugs such as tetracyclines, fluoroquinolones, and iron, so the lecture calls for a 2 hour separation. Ten minutes falls short of that separation, and six hours, twelve hours, or a full day are longer than the lecture requires. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 1, antacid adverse effects and interactions).","boardPrep":"Step 1: aluminum hydroxide causes constipation and hypophosphatemia, while magnesium hydroxide causes diarrhea (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: calcium carbonate can cause milk alkali syndrome and rebound acidity (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 2 — parietal cells and their products","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pharmacologic principles underlying the treatment and prevention of peptic ulcer disease, including H. pylori eradication, NSAID-associated ulcer treatment and prevention, and pharmacologic strategies to reduce the risk of recurrent bleeding.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, why is bismuth quadruple therapy now recommended as first line H. pylori treatment?","choices":{"A":"Clarithromycin resistance","B":"Amoxicillin allergy rates","C":"Shorter treatment course","D":"Fewer daily pill doses","E":"Metronidazole resistance"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Bismuth quadruple therapy is first line because clarithromycin resistance is rising, and triple therapy is now limited to regions where resistance is below 15 percent. The quadruple regimen lasts 10 to 14 days with several daily doses, so it is not shorter or simpler, and it contains metronidazole. Amoxicillin allergy is not the stated reason, and the bismuth regimen (PPI, bismuth, metronidazole, tetracycline) contains no amoxicillin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 2, H. pylori eradication regimens).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H. pylori quadruple therapy combines metronidazole, tetracycline, bismuth, and a PPI (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, H. pylori). COMLEX: bismuth also treats traveler diarrhea and can darken the stool (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, antidiarrheal medications).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — H. pylori gastritis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"The quadruple regimen lasts 10"}},{"id":"sdl10_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pharmacologic principles underlying the treatment and prevention of peptic ulcer disease, including H. pylori eradication, NSAID-associated ulcer treatment and prevention, and pharmacologic strategies to reduce the risk of recurrent bleeding.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, how long must a proton pump inhibitor be held before a test that confirms H. pylori eradication?","choices":{"A":"Two days","B":"Six hours","C":"Six months","D":"Two weeks","E":"Twelve hours"},"correct":"D","explanation":"PPIs must be held for 2 weeks before a urea breath test, fecal antigen test, or biopsy to avoid false negative results, and testing is done at least 4 weeks after the antibiotics end. Holds of six hours, twelve hours, or two days are too short to avoid false negative results, and six months is far longer than the lecture's 2 weeks. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 2, role of PPI in eradication).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bismuth and sucralfate bind the gastric mucosa and raise bicarbonate secretion (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: sucralfate requires an acidic environment to work (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 2 — peptic ulcer disease and its complications","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Holds of six hours, twelve hou"}},{"id":"sdl10_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pharmacologic principles underlying the treatment and prevention of peptic ulcer disease, including H. pylori eradication, NSAID-associated ulcer treatment and prevention, and pharmacologic strategies to reduce the risk of recurrent bleeding.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what sustained intragastric pH threshold prevents acid inactivation of pepsin and preserves clot formation?","choices":{"A":"Below pH 2","B":"Above pH 6","C":"Near pH 3","D":"Near pH 4","E":"Above pH 10"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Sustained elevation of intragastric pH above 6.0 prevents acid inactivation of pepsin and preserves clotting by optimizing platelet aggregation and coagulation. Lower values still allow clot breakdown, and a pH above 10 is not a lecture target. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 2, reduction of recurrent ulcer bleeding risk).","boardPrep":"Step 1: proton pump inhibitors raise the risk of C. difficile infection and aspiration pneumonia (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: proton pump inhibitors are used for GI bleeding and stress ulcer prophylaxis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 2 — peptic ulcer disease and its complications","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Lower values still allow clot ","C":"Lower values still allow clot ","D":"Lower values still allow clot "}},{"id":"sdl10_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and major adverse effects of antiemetic drug classes, and select appropriate therapy for nausea and vomiting in common clinical settings, including chemotherapy-induced and postoperative nausea and vomiting.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which receptors in the chemoreceptor trigger zone and area postrema do aprepitant and fosaprepitant block?","choices":{"A":"Substance P receptors","B":"Dopamine D2 receptors","C":"Muscarinic M1 receptors","D":"Histamine H1 receptors","E":"Cannabinoid CB1 receptors"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Aprepitant and fosaprepitant cross the blood brain barrier and block central NK1 receptors, the receptors for substance P. Dopamine D2 receptors are blocked by prochlorperazine and metoclopramide. M1 and H1 receptors are blocked by scopolamine and the antihistamines. Dronabinol and nabilone are CB1 agonists. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 3, NK1 receptor antagonists).","boardPrep":"Step 1: NK1 antagonists such as aprepitant act centrally and treat chemotherapy induced emesis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, antiemetic medications). COMLEX: NK1 antagonists are combined with a 5 HT3 antagonist and dexamethasone for highly emetogenic chemotherapy (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 4 — vagal afferents and the vagovagal reflex","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and major adverse effects of antiemetic drug classes, and select appropriate therapy for nausea and vomiting in common clinical settings, including chemotherapy-induced and postoperative nausea and vomiting.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what property makes palonosetron better than older agents for delayed chemotherapy induced emesis?","choices":{"A":"Extra NK1 blockade","B":"Central CB1 agonism","C":"Long 40 hour half life","D":"Added D2 receptor blockade","E":"Peripheral restriction"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Palonosetron is a second generation 5 HT3 antagonist with higher receptor affinity and a 40 hour half life, which makes it superior for delayed CINV. It does not block NK1 or D2 receptors or act at CB1 receptors, and it acts both peripherally and centrally. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 3, serotonin 5 HT3 receptor antagonists).","boardPrep":"Step 1: 5 HT3 antagonists cause QT prolongation, headache, and serotonin syndrome (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, antiemetic medications). COMLEX: 5 HT3 antagonists are used for chemotherapy, postoperative, and radiation induced emesis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, antiemetic medications).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 4 — vagal afferents and the vagovagal reflex","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"It does not block NK1 or D2 re"}},{"id":"sdl10_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and major adverse effects of antiemetic drug classes, and select appropriate therapy for nausea and vomiting in common clinical settings, including chemotherapy-induced and postoperative nausea and vomiting.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, aprepitant inhibits CYP3A4. What change is required in the dexamethasone given with it?","choices":{"A":"Double the dose","B":"Reduce dose by half","C":"Give it intravenously","D":"Stop dexamethasone","E":"Give it at bedtime"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Aprepitant and netupitant inhibit CYP3A4 and raise dexamethasone levels, so the dexamethasone dose is reduced by 50 percent. Raising the dose, changing the route or timing, and stopping the steroid are not what the lecture describes, because dexamethasone potentiates NK1 antagonists. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 3, NK1 adverse effects and interactions).","boardPrep":"Step 1: aprepitant also lowers the warfarin INR (lecture SDL 10). COMLEX: dexamethasone used as an antiemetic can cause insomnia and hyperglycemia (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 4 — vagal afferents and the vagovagal reflex","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Raising the dose, changing the","C":"Raising the dose, changing the","D":"Raising the dose, changing the","E":"Raising the dose, changing the"}},{"id":"sdl10_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the mechanisms of action and clinical roles of vasoactive agents and nonselective β-blockers in the management of esophageal variceal hemorrhage, distinguishing their use in acute and chronic settings.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, how does octreotide reduce portal inflow in acute variceal bleeding?","choices":{"A":"Inhibits glucagon release","B":"Activates V1a receptors","C":"Blocks beta 1 receptors","D":"Releases nitric oxide","E":"Directly constricts arterioles"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Somatostatin and octreotide cause selective splanchnic vasoconstriction by inhibiting the release of glucagon and other vasodilatory peptides, which lowers portal inflow without systemic vasoconstriction. Vasopressin and terlipressin activate V1a receptors. Beta 1 blockade is how nonselective beta blockers reduce cardiac output. Nitric oxide is a splanchnic vasodilator that worsens portal hypertension. Octreotide's vasoconstriction is indirect; direct arteriolar constriction through vascular smooth muscle receptors is the vasopressin and terlipressin mechanism. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 4, somatostatin and octreotide mechanism).","boardPrep":"Step 1: portal hypertension dilates submucosal portocaval veins that form esophageal varices (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, esophageal varices). COMLEX: variceal bleeding begins abruptly with hematemesis or melena and can cause hemorrhagic shock (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, esophageal varices).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 5 — somatostatin actions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the mechanisms of action and clinical roles of vasoactive agents and nonselective β-blockers in the management of esophageal variceal hemorrhage, distinguishing their use in acute and chronic settings.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which drug must be given with vasopressin in variceal bleeding to offset its systemic and coronary effects?","choices":{"A":"Phenylephrine","B":"Nitroglycerin","C":"Propranolol","D":"Terlipressin","E":"Norepinephrine"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Vasopressin causes severe systemic and coronary vasoconstriction with hypertension and myocardial ischemia, so it requires co administration of IV nitroglycerin. Phenylephrine and norepinephrine add vasoconstriction. Propranolol is contraindicated during active bleeding. Terlipressin is a V1 agonist with greater V1 selectivity and fewer side effects, not a drug that offsets vasopressin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 4, vasopressin adverse effects).","boardPrep":"Step 1: vasopressin also causes free water retention and hyponatremia (lecture SDL 10). COMLEX: terlipressin has greater V1 selectivity and fewer side effects than vasopressin (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 4 — esophageal varices and portal hypertension","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the mechanisms of action and clinical roles of vasoactive agents and nonselective β-blockers in the management of esophageal variceal hemorrhage, distinguishing their use in acute and chronic settings.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, to what resting heart rate is propranolol titrated in chronic variceal management?","choices":{"A":"40 to 45 bpm","B":"70 to 75 bpm","C":"80 to 85 bpm","D":"90 to 95 bpm","E":"55 to 60 bpm"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The dose is titrated to reduce the resting heart rate by 25 percent or to 55 to 60 bpm. Lower targets risk symptomatic bradycardia, and higher ones give too little portal pressure reduction. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 4, clinical role in chronic settings).","boardPrep":"Step 1: TIPS is a management option for portal hypertension (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, portal hypertension). COMLEX: lactulose and rifaximin treat hepatic encephalopathy (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, portal hypertension).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 4 — esophageal varices and portal hypertension","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Lower targets risk symptomatic","B":"Lower targets risk symptomatic","C":"Lower targets risk symptomatic","D":"Lower targets risk symptomatic"}},{"id":"sdl10_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Compare and contrast the mechanisms of action, pharmacokinetics, adverse effects, and clinical indications of the major drug classes used to treat gastroesophageal reflux disease, and select appropriate therapy based on clinical presentation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman has heartburn four times a week for six months. Endoscopy shows erosive esophagitis without Barrett mucosa. Which regimen is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Famotidine as needed","B":"Metoclopramide daily","C":"Antacids as needed","D":"Alginate after meals","E":"Daily omeprazole"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Frequent heartburn on 3 or more days a week and erosive esophagitis call for a proton pump inhibitor, which heals 85 to 90 percent of erosive esophagitis when taken 30 to 60 minutes before breakfast. H2 blockers heal less than half and suit mild infrequent symptoms. Metoclopramide is not recommended as monotherapy. Antacids and alginates give only short temporary relief. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 1, PPI and H2 blocker indications).","boardPrep":"Step 1: proton pump inhibitors reduce absorption of vitamin B12, iron, calcium, and magnesium and can cause acute interstitial nephritis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: GERD is managed with lifestyle changes and a proton pump inhibitor (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, esophagus).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — reflux injury and Barrett esophagus","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"H2 blockers heal less than hal","D":"Antacids and alginates give on"}},{"id":"sdl10_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Compare and contrast the mechanisms of action, pharmacokinetics, adverse effects, and clinical indications of the major drug classes used to treat gastroesophageal reflux disease, and select appropriate therapy based on clinical presentation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old man takes an over the counter drug for heartburn. Over three months he develops tender breast enlargement, and his warfarin effect rises. Which drug is he most likely taking?","choices":{"A":"Pantoprazole","B":"Famotidine","C":"Cimetidine","D":"Sucralfate","E":"Nizatidine"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cimetidine inhibits androgen binding and raises prolactin, causing gynecomastia, impotence, and galactorrhea, and it inhibits CYP1A2, 2C9, 2D6, and 3A4, which raises warfarin levels. Famotidine and nizatidine lack these effects. Pantoprazole is a PPI without antiandrogen effects. Sucralfate is a local mucosal protectant. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 1, H2 receptor antagonist adverse effects).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cimetidine causes CYP450 inhibition, antiandrogenic effects, and CNS effects (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: H2 blockers can cause confusion in elderly or ICU patients with renal or hepatic impairment (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — parietal cells and their products","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Compare and contrast the mechanisms of action, pharmacokinetics, adverse effects, and clinical indications of the major drug classes used to treat gastroesophageal reflux disease, and select appropriate therapy based on clinical presentation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman with long standing diabetes has persistent regurgitation and delayed gastric emptying despite a proton pump inhibitor. An adjunct drug is added that blocks central and peripheral dopamine receptors. Which adverse effect most limits its long term use?","choices":{"A":"Osmotic diarrhea","B":"Tardive dyskinesia","C":"Milk alkali syndrome","D":"Interstitial nephritis","E":"Hypomagnesemia"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Metoclopramide is a D2 antagonist that crosses the blood brain barrier, and central D2 blockade causes extrapyramidal effects including dystonia, parkinsonism, and tardive dyskinesia. Osmotic diarrhea is from magnesium antacids. Milk alkali syndrome is from excess calcium. Interstitial nephritis and hypomagnesemia are long term PPI risks. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 1, prokinetic agents).","boardPrep":"Step 1: metoclopramide raises LES tone and gastric contractions and causes hyperprolactinemia (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: metoclopramide is contraindicated in small bowel obstruction (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 5 — the lower esophageal sphincter","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pharmacologic principles underlying the treatment and prevention of peptic ulcer disease, including H. pylori eradication, NSAID-associated ulcer treatment and prevention, and pharmacologic strategies to reduce the risk of recurrent bleeding.","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman who is 12 weeks pregnant has rheumatoid arthritis and a past bleeding ulcer. She must continue an NSAID and needs ulcer prophylaxis. Which drug should be avoided?","choices":{"A":"Omeprazole","B":"Famotidine","C":"Pantoprazole","D":"Misoprostol","E":"Sucralfate"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Misoprostol is a synthetic PGE1 analog that raises mucus and bicarbonate secretion and mucosal blood flow, but it is an abortifacient and is contraindicated in pregnancy, and it also causes dose dependent cramping and diarrhea. A once daily PPI is the alternative cotherapy for high risk NSAID users. Famotidine and sucralfate act on acid or the mucosa without abortifacient effects. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 2, NSAID ulcer prevention in high risk patients).","boardPrep":"Step 1: misoprostol raises bicarbonate and mucus, lowers parietal cell acid secretion, and raises gastric blood flow (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: misoprostol is used for NSAID induced PUD prophylaxis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — prostaglandins in mucosal protection","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A once daily PPI is the altern","C":"A once daily PPI is the altern"}},{"id":"sdl10_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pharmacologic principles underlying the treatment and prevention of peptic ulcer disease, including H. pylori eradication, NSAID-associated ulcer treatment and prevention, and pharmacologic strategies to reduce the risk of recurrent bleeding.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with a duodenal ulcer and a positive urea breath test starts a regimen of two antimicrobials, bismuth, and a twice daily acid suppressant. How does the acid suppressant improve eradication?","choices":{"A":"Antibiotic stability","B":"Thicker protective mucus","C":"Bacterial urease blockade","D":"Faster gastric emptying","E":"Stronger IgA secretion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"PPIs suppress acid and raise gastric pH, which increases antibiotic stability and lowers the minimal inhibitory concentration against H. pylori. They do not thicken mucus, block urease directly, speed emptying, or raise IgA. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 2, role of PPI in eradication).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H. pylori triple therapy combines amoxicillin, clarithromycin, and a PPI (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, H. pylori). COMLEX: after the antibiotics, the PPI is continued once daily for 4 to 6 weeks to heal the ulcer (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — H. pylori and NSAIDs in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pharmacologic principles underlying the treatment and prevention of peptic ulcer disease, including H. pylori eradication, NSAID-associated ulcer treatment and prevention, and pharmacologic strategies to reduce the risk of recurrent bleeding.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man has hematemesis. Endoscopy shows a duodenal ulcer with a visible vessel that is treated endoscopically. Which drug regimen best lowers his risk of rebleeding?","choices":{"A":"Oral famotidine at bedtime","B":"IV PPI bolus and infusion","C":"IV octreotide infusion","D":"Oral sucralfate slurry","E":"IV vasopressin infusion"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ulcers with high risk stigmata such as active bleeding, a visible vessel, or an adherent clot are treated with an IV PPI bolus followed by a continuous infusion for 72 hours, or high dose oral PPI. Bedtime famotidine and sucralfate give too little acid suppression. Octreotide and vasopressin are vasoactive drugs for variceal bleeding. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 2, high dose or IV PPI therapy).","boardPrep":"Step 1: peptic ulcer disease is a cause of upper GI bleeding along with varices and Mallory Weiss tears (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, upper GI bleeding). COMLEX: a duodenal ulcer can erode the gastroduodenal artery (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — complications of peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and major adverse effects of antiemetic drug classes, and select appropriate therapy for nausea and vomiting in common clinical settings, including chemotherapy-induced and postoperative nausea and vomiting.","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman who is 10 weeks pregnant has nausea and vomiting most mornings. Diet changes have not helped. Which treatment is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Meclizine by mouth","B":"Dronabinol by mouth","C":"Prochlorperazine suppository","D":"Aprepitant with dexamethasone","E":"Doxylamine with pyridoxine"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Doxylamine with pyridoxine is the H1 antihistamine combination the lecture names for nausea and vomiting of pregnancy. Meclizine is for motion sickness and vertigo. Dronabinol is second line for refractory CINV. Prochlorperazine is a broad spectrum D2 antagonist with extrapyramidal effects. Aprepitant with dexamethasone is for highly emetogenic CINV. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 3, H1 antihistamines and anticholinergics).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H1 antihistamines and anticholinergics cause sedation, dry mouth, blurred vision, and urinary retention (lecture SDL 10). COMLEX: prochlorperazine blocks D2, muscarinic, and histamine receptors and causes extrapyramidal symptoms (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, antiemetic medications).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 4 — vagal afferents and the vagovagal reflex","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and major adverse effects of antiemetic drug classes, and select appropriate therapy for nausea and vomiting in common clinical settings, including chemotherapy-induced and postoperative nausea and vomiting.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man is leaving on a week long sailing trip and became very nauseated on his last boat trip. Which option best prevents his symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Scopolamine patch","B":"Ondansetron tablets","C":"Aprepitant capsules","D":"Dexamethasone tablets","E":"Dronabinol capsules"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Motion sickness is prevented by blocking M1 and H1 receptors in the vestibular apparatus and solitary nucleus, as with a transdermal scopolamine patch, meclizine, or dimenhydrinate. 5 HT3 antagonists such as ondansetron are ineffective for motion sickness. Aprepitant is for CINV, and dexamethasone potentiates other antiemetics in CINV and PONV. Dronabinol is for refractory CINV and AIDS cachexia. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 3, H1 antihistamines and anticholinergics).","boardPrep":"Step 1: dronabinol and nabilone are CB1 agonists that can cause euphoria, sedation, and orthostatic hypotension (lecture SDL 10). COMLEX: cannabinoids also stimulate appetite in AIDS cachexia (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic innervation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and major adverse effects of antiemetic drug classes, and select appropriate therapy for nausea and vomiting in common clinical settings, including chemotherapy-induced and postoperative nausea and vomiting.","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old man taking sertraline for depression receives repeated doses of ondansetron after surgery. He becomes agitated with tremor, clonus, sweating, and a temperature of 39.2 °C. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Acute dystonia","B":"Serotonin syndrome","C":"Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","D":"Anticholinergic toxicity","E":"Thyroid storm"},"correct":"B","explanation":"5 HT3 antagonists carry a risk of serotonin syndrome when combined with SSRIs or SNRIs, and clonus, tremor, agitation, and fever fit it. Acute dystonia is an extrapyramidal effect of D2 blockers. Neuroleptic malignant syndrome follows dopamine blockade and causes rigidity rather than clonus. Anticholinergic toxicity causes dry skin rather than sweating. Thyroid storm is not linked to these drugs. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 3, 5 HT3 antagonist adverse effects).","boardPrep":"Step 1: metoclopramide blocks D2 and 5 HT3 receptors centrally and stimulates 5 HT4 receptors peripherally (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, antiemetic medications). COMLEX: metoclopramide can cause neuroleptic malignant syndrome (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, antiemetic medications).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 1 — GI hormones, paracrines, and neurocrines","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the mechanisms of action and clinical roles of vasoactive agents and nonselective β-blockers in the management of esophageal variceal hemorrhage, distinguishing their use in acute and chronic settings.","batch":2,"stem":"A 54-year-old man with alcohol related cirrhosis has hematemesis, a pulse of 118/min, and a blood pressure of 88/54 mm Hg. An infusion of octreotide is started before endoscopy. Why is his usual propranolol held?","choices":{"A":"Blunts compensatory reflexes","B":"Raises portal vein inflow","C":"Worsens hepatic encephalopathy","D":"Interacts with octreotide","E":"Constricts coronary arteries"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Nonselective beta blockers are contraindicated in acute active variceal bleeding because they blunt the compensatory cardiovascular reflexes needed during hemorrhagic shock. They lower rather than raise portal inflow. Encephalopathy and an octreotide interaction are not the reason given. Coronary vasoconstriction is a vasopressin effect. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 4, contraindications and adverse effects).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the esophageal portocaval anastomosis joins the left gastric vein to the distal esophageal veins (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, portal hypertension). COMLEX: octreotide is given immediately for suspected variceal bleeding and continued for 3 to 5 days (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 4 — esophageal varices","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the mechanisms of action and clinical roles of vasoactive agents and nonselective β-blockers in the management of esophageal variceal hemorrhage, distinguishing their use in acute and chronic settings.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old woman with cirrhosis and medium varices that have never bled starts nadolol. Besides lowering cardiac output, which effect of the drug reduces portal inflow?","choices":{"A":"Direct V1a agonism","B":"Reduced glucagon release","C":"Unopposed alpha 1 tone","D":"More nitric oxide release","E":"Stellate cell relaxation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Nonselective beta blockers lower portal pressure by beta 1 blockade, which cuts cardiac output, and by beta 2 blockade of vasodilatory receptors, which leaves alpha 1 receptors unopposed to constrict the splanchnic bed. V1a agonism is the vasopressin mechanism. Reduced glucagon release is the octreotide mechanism. Nitric oxide dilates the splanchnic bed. Stellate cell relaxation is not the described mechanism. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 4, nonselective beta blocker mechanism).","boardPrep":"Step 1: carvedilol, nadolol, and propranolol prevent variceal bleeding (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, portal hypertension). COMLEX: beta blockers are contraindicated in severe asthma or COPD because of bronchospasm (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 4 — esophageal varices and portal hypertension","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the mechanisms of action and clinical roles of vasoactive agents and nonselective β-blockers in the management of esophageal variceal hemorrhage, distinguishing their use in acute and chronic settings.","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with cirrhosis survived a variceal bleed two months ago and takes propranolol. What should be combined with it to prevent another bleed?","choices":{"A":"Chronic octreotide infusion","B":"Daily oral metoprolol","C":"Monthly vasopressin doses","D":"Endoscopic band ligation","E":"Balloon tamponade"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Secondary prophylaxis combines a nonselective beta blocker with endoscopic variceal band ligation. Octreotide and vasopressin are short term acute therapies. Metoprolol is cardioselective and far less effective. Balloon tamponade is a temporary rescue measure for active bleeding. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 4, clinical role in chronic settings).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a hepatic venous pressure gradient measurement assesses portal hypertension (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, portal hypertension). COMLEX: primary prophylaxis is for medium or large varices that have not bled (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 4 — portal hypertension and portocaval collaterals","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl10_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Compare and contrast the mechanisms of action, pharmacokinetics, adverse effects, and clinical indications of the major drug classes used to treat gastroesophageal reflux disease, and select appropriate therapy based on clinical presentation.","batch":3,"stem":"A 61-year-old man on hemodialysis takes an antacid several times a day for heartburn and has had loose stools since starting it. He develops weakness, hyporeflexia, and hypotension. Which ion has most likely accumulated?","choices":{"A":"Calcium","B":"Aluminum","C":"Bicarbonate","D":"Phosphate","E":"Magnesium"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Magnesium and aluminum from antacids accumulate in renal failure, and magnesium excess causes hyporeflexia, hypotension, cardiac effects, and respiratory failure. Calcium excess follows calcium carbonate use and can cause milk alkali syndrome. Aluminum also accumulates in renal failure but causes constipation rather than loose stools. Aluminum hydroxide lowers phosphate. Bicarbonate excess follows sodium bicarbonate use. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 1, antacid adverse effects); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (gastric pharmacology, lines 1203–1209).","boardPrep":"Step 1: magnesium hydroxide can cause hyporeflexia, hypotension, cardiac effects, and respiratory failure (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: antacids give rapid relief within 5 to 15 minutes that lasts only 1 to 2 hours (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — parietal cells and their products","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Aluminum also accumulates in r"}},{"id":"trial_sdl10_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Compare and contrast the mechanisms of action, pharmacokinetics, adverse effects, and clinical indications of the major drug classes used to treat gastroesophageal reflux disease, and select appropriate therapy based on clinical presentation.","batch":3,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman has taken a proton pump inhibitor daily for eight years and now has a low trauma hip fracture. Which drug effect best explains her fracture risk?","choices":{"A":"Reduced vitamin D synthesis","B":"Reduced calcium absorption","C":"Increased renal calcium loss","D":"Direct osteoclast activation","E":"Low estrogen production"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Long term PPI use decreases absorption of calcium, non heme iron, and vitamin B12 and raises the risk of osteoporotic hip and wrist fractures. PPIs do not block vitamin D synthesis, raise renal calcium loss, activate osteoclasts directly, or lower estrogen. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 1, PPI adverse effects).","boardPrep":"Step 1: long term PPI use also causes benign fundic gland polyps (lecture SDL 10). COMLEX: PPIs are cleared by hepatic CYP2C19 and CYP3A4 and need no renal dose change (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 3 — consequences of gastric cell loss","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl10_q03","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pharmacologic principles underlying the treatment and prevention of peptic ulcer disease, including H. pylori eradication, NSAID-associated ulcer treatment and prevention, and pharmacologic strategies to reduce the risk of recurrent bleeding.","batch":3,"stem":"A 69-year-old woman with severe hip osteoarthritis had a bleeding gastric ulcer last year. She needs long term anti inflammatory treatment. Which regimen carries the lowest risk of another ulcer?","choices":{"A":"Celecoxib with a PPI","B":"Naproxen with famotidine","C":"Ibuprofen with sucralfate","D":"Celecoxib with antacids","E":"Aspirin with misoprostol"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Selective COX 2 inhibitors combined with a PPI carry the lowest ulcer risk in high risk patients. Famotidine and sucralfate give less protection than a PPI with nonselective NSAIDs. Antacids give only brief relief. Misoprostol protects but aspirin remains a nonselective COX inhibitor, and misoprostol causes cramping and diarrhea. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 2, prevention in high risk patients).","boardPrep":"Step 1: risk factors for NSAID ulcers include prior ulcer or bleeding, advanced age, and anticoagulant, antiplatelet, or corticosteroid use (lecture SDL 10). COMLEX: if NSAIDs are stopped, H2 blockers or PPIs for 4 to 8 weeks heal the ulcer (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — prostaglandins in mucosal protection","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Famotidine and sucralfate give","C":"Famotidine and sucralfate give"}},{"id":"trial_sdl10_q04","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and major adverse effects of antiemetic drug classes, and select appropriate therapy for nausea and vomiting in common clinical settings, including chemotherapy-induced and postoperative nausea and vomiting.","batch":3,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman starting cisplatin receives four drug antiemetic prophylaxis that includes an atypical antipsychotic. Which receptor types does this added drug block?","choices":{"A":"Histamine and muscarinic","B":"Cannabinoid and opioid","C":"Neurokinin and GABA","D":"Adrenergic and glutamate","E":"Dopamine and serotonin"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Olanzapine is part of four drug high risk CINV prophylaxis and blocks D2, 5 HT2C, and 5 HT3 receptors. Histamine and muscarinic blockade is the antihistamine and anticholinergic mechanism. Cannabinoids are CB1 agonists. NK1 antagonists block substance P but not GABA. Adrenergic and glutamate receptors are not the described targets. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 3, dopamine D2 antagonists and olanzapine).","boardPrep":"Step 1: D2 antagonist antiemetics cause extrapyramidal symptoms, sedation, hyperprolactinemia, and QT prolongation (lecture SDL 10). COMLEX: dexamethasone potentiates 5 HT3 and NK1 antagonists in CINV and PONV (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 4 — vagal afferents and the vagovagal reflex","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl10_q05","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the mechanisms of action and clinical roles of vasoactive agents and nonselective β-blockers in the management of esophageal variceal hemorrhage, distinguishing their use in acute and chronic settings.","batch":3,"stem":"A 50-year-old man with cirrhosis has large varices that have never bled. He also has severe asthma with frequent attacks. Which effect makes propranolol a poor choice for him?","choices":{"A":"Bronchospasm","B":"Hyperkalemia","C":"Hepatotoxicity","D":"Hyperglycemia","E":"Tachycardia"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Nonselective beta blockers block bronchial beta 2 receptors and can cause bronchospasm, so they are contraindicated in severe asthma or COPD. Hyperkalemia and hepatotoxicity are not among the lecture-listed adverse effects, they can mask hypoglycemia rather than cause hyperglycemia, and they cause bradycardia rather than tachycardia. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 4, contraindications and adverse effects).","boardPrep":"Step 1: beta 1 blockade lowers cardiac output and portal inflow (lecture SDL 10). COMLEX: cardioselective beta blockers such as atenolol and metoprolol are far less effective for varices (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 4 — esophageal varices","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl10_q06","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the mechanisms of action and clinical roles of vasoactive agents and nonselective β-blockers in the management of esophageal variceal hemorrhage, distinguishing their use in acute and chronic settings.","batch":3,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with cirrhosis is given a vasopressin infusion for variceal bleeding. Two days later he is confused, and his serum sodium is 124 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L). What is the most likely cause?","choices":{"A":"Renal sodium wasting","B":"Adrenal insufficiency","C":"Osmotic diuresis","D":"Free water retention","E":"Gastrointestinal sodium loss"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Vasopressin causes free water retention that produces hyponatremia, along with systemic and coronary vasoconstriction. Renal sodium wasting, adrenal insufficiency, osmotic diuresis, and GI sodium loss are not the described effects of vasopressin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 4, vasopressin adverse effects).","boardPrep":"Step 1: octreotide is given as a 50 mcg bolus then 50 mcg per hour (lecture SDL 10). COMLEX: long term octreotide can cause gallstones (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 4 — portal hypertension and portocaval collaterals","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":11,"title":"SDL 11 — Embryology of the liver, gallbladder and pancreas","questions":[{"id":"sdl11_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand how liver development is triggered by signals from nearby tissues.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which tissue secretes the FGF2 that starts liver bud outgrowth?","choices":{"A":"Septum transversum","B":"Paraxial mesoderm","C":"Cardiac mesoderm","D":"Surface ectoderm","E":"Neural crest"},"correct":"C","explanation":"FGF2 comes from the cardiac mesoderm and nearby vascular endothelial cells at the site of liver bud outgrowth. The septum transversum secretes BMPs, not FGF2. Ectoderm is one of the repressing tissues. Paraxial mesoderm and neural crest are not FGF2 sources for liver induction. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 1, inductive signaling by FGF2 and BMPs, lines 2169–2174); Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 731–735.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the liver parenchyma arises from foregut endoderm, and its connective tissue comes from mesoderm of the septum transversum (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: endoderm forms the primitive gut tube, the GI tract, and the liver (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, embryology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — foregut divisions and the liver bud","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand how liver development is triggered by signals from nearby tissues.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what is the main role of the BMPs secreted by the septum transversum during liver induction?","choices":{"A":"Raising endoderm competence","B":"Repressing hepatic genes","C":"Forming bile ducts directly","D":"Inducing pancreatic buds","E":"Blocking FGF2 signaling"},"correct":"A","explanation":"BMPs from the septum transversum enhance the competence of prospective liver endoderm to respond to FGF2. Hepatic genes are repressed by ectoderm, noncardiac mesoderm, and the notochord. BMPs do not form bile ducts, induce the pancreatic buds, or block FGF2. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 1, BMPs, lines 2175–2177); Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 735–737.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the ventral mesogastrium gives rise to the liver, lesser omentum, and falciform ligament (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, embryology). COMLEX: the liver weighs about 10 percent of body weight at week 10 (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — foregut divisions and the liver bud","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand how liver development is triggered by signals from nearby tissues.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which transcription factors control differentiation of liver bud cells into hepatocytes and biliary cells?","choices":{"A":"PAX4 and PAX6","B":"HNF3 and HNF4","C":"SOX2 and CDXC","D":"PDX1 and SHH","E":"NKX2 and GATA4"},"correct":"B","explanation":"After the hepatic field is specified, liver bud cells become hepatocyte and biliary lineages under control of the hepatocyte nuclear transcription factors HNF3 and HNF4. PAX4 and PAX6 specify pancreatic endocrine cells. SOX2 specifies esophagus and stomach and CDXC the intestine. PDX1 is the pancreatic factor, and SHH must be repressed for pancreas to form. NKX2 and GATA4 are not the described factors. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 1, downstream differentiation, lines 2178–2181); Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 738–740.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SOX2 specifies the esophagus and stomach, PDX1 the duodenum, and CDXC the small and large intestine (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the bare area of the liver touches the central tendon of the diaphragm (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — foregut divisions and the liver bud","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the cause and clinical presentation of extrahepatic biliary atresia.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about what share of extrahepatic biliary atresia cases are the perinatal form?","choices":{"A":"About 10 percent","B":"About 25 percent","C":"About 50 percent","D":"About 95 percent","E":"About 80 percent"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The perinatal form makes up about 80 percent of cases. The tree forms normally and is then destroyed after birth by suspected viral, toxic, or autoimmune injury. The fetal form is less common and reflects abnormal development or failed recanalization. About 10 percent is too low; the perinatal form accounts for about 80 percent of extrahepatic biliary atresia. About 25 percent is too low; the perinatal form accounts for about 80 percent of extrahepatic biliary atresia. About 50 percent is too low; the perinatal form accounts for about 80 percent of extrahepatic biliary atresia. About 95 percent is too high; the perinatal form accounts for about 80 percent, with the less common fetal form making up the rest. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 2, perinatal and fetal forms, lines 2187–2193).","boardPrep":"Step 1: biliary atresia is a fibro obliterative disease of the extrahepatic biliary tree (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, biliary atresia). COMLEX: biliary atresia shows raised AST, ALT, GGT, and ALP (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, biliary atresia).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 4 — large bile duct obstruction","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"About 10 percent is too low; the perinatal form accounts for","B":"About 25 percent is too low; the perinatal form accounts for","C":"About 50 percent is too low; the perinatal form accounts for","D":"About 95 percent is too high; the perinatal form accounts fo"}},{"id":"sdl11_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the cause and clinical presentation of extrahepatic biliary atresia.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, by what age does uncorrected extrahepatic biliary atresia lead to secondary biliary cirrhosis?","choices":{"A":"1 to 2 weeks","B":"2 to 3 years","C":"8 to 10 years","D":"3 to 6 months","E":"12 to 18 months"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Uncorrected obstruction leads to secondary biliary cirrhosis by 3 to 6 months of age, which is why drainage must be restored early. At 1 to 2 weeks physiologic newborn jaundice is still resolving, and 2 to 3 years or 8 to 10 years are far later than the 3 to 6 months by which cirrhosis is already present. 12 to 18 months is later than the 3 to 6 months by which uncorrected obstruction already leads to secondary biliary cirrhosis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 2, morphologic features, lines 2194–2197); Robbins Ch. 18 (physiologic jaundice of the newborn resolves by 2 weeks).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ultrasound in biliary atresia shows an absent or irregular gallbladder or common bile duct (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, biliary atresia). COMLEX: infants present within 2 to 6 weeks of birth with neonatal cholestasis (lecture SDL 11).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 4 — large bile duct obstruction","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"At 1 to 2 weeks physiologic ne","B":"At 1 to 2 weeks physiologic ne","E":"12 to 18 months is later than the 3 to 6 months by which unc"}},{"id":"sdl11_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the cause and clinical presentation of extrahepatic biliary atresia.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, extrahepatic biliary atresia is the leading indication for which procedure in children?","choices":{"A":"Small bowel transplantation","B":"Kidney transplantation","C":"Liver transplantation","D":"Pancreas transplantation","E":"Heart transplantation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Biliary atresia accounts for one third of neonatal cholestasis and 50 to 60 percent of pediatric liver transplants. The other transplants are not linked to it. Biliary atresia is not linked to small bowel transplantation; it is the leading indication for pediatric liver transplantation. Biliary atresia is not linked to kidney transplantation; it accounts for 50 to 60 percent of pediatric liver transplants. Biliary atresia is not linked to pancreas transplantation; it accounts for 50 to 60 percent of pediatric liver transplants. Biliary atresia is not linked to heart transplantation; it accounts for 50 to 60 percent of pediatric liver transplants. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 2, clinical significance, lines 2201–2203).","boardPrep":"Step 1: biliary atresia is the most common reason for pediatric liver transplantation (First Aid 2025 GI biliary atresia). COMLEX: 15 to 20 percent of infants with biliary atresia have patent proximal ducts and a correctable defect (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 4 — large bile duct obstruction","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Biliary atresia is not linked to small bowel transplantation","B":"Biliary atresia is not linked to kidney transplantation; it ","D":"Biliary atresia is not linked to pancreas transplantation; i","E":"Biliary atresia is not linked to heart transplantation; it a"}},{"id":"sdl11_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe how the pancreas forms from two buds and rotates around the duodenum.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which part of the pancreas forms from the ventral bud?","choices":{"A":"Tail of pancreas","B":"Body of pancreas","C":"Neck of pancreas","D":"Uncinate process","E":"Accessory duct"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The ventral bud forms the uncinate process and the inferior and posterior head. The dorsal bud forms the body, tail, and superior and anterior head, and the accessory duct comes from the proximal dorsal duct. The neck is part of the remaining gland, which Langman derives from the dorsal bud. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 3, bud fusion and derivatives, lines 2220–2223); Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 815–817.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the dorsal pancreatic bud lies in the dorsal mesentery and the ventral bud lies next to the bile duct (lecture SDL 11). COMLEX: visceral mesoderm around the pancreatic buds forms the pancreatic connective tissue (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 4 — duodenal development and the major papilla","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe how the pancreas forms from two buds and rotates around the duodenum.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, between which gestational weeks do the parenchyma and ducts of the two pancreatic buds fuse?","choices":{"A":"Weeks 5 to 8","B":"Weeks 2 to 3","C":"Weeks 10 to 12","D":"Weeks 16 to 20","E":"Weeks 30 to 36"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The ventral bud comes to lie below and behind the dorsal bud, and their parenchyma and ducts fuse between weeks 5 and 8. The other intervals do not match the lecture. Weeks 2 to 3 is too early; the pancreatic bud parenchyma and ducts fuse between weeks 5 and 8. Weeks 10 to 12 is too late; the pancreatic bud parenchyma and ducts fuse between weeks 5 and 8. Weeks 16 to 20 is too late; the pancreatic bud parenchyma and ducts fuse between weeks 5 and 8. Weeks 30 to 36 is far too late; the pancreatic bud parenchyma and ducts fuse between weeks 5 and 8. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 3, bud fusion, lines 2217–2219).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in about 10 percent of people the pancreatic duct systems fail to fuse and the double system persists (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: pancreas divisum is usually asymptomatic but raises pancreatitis risk (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic anomalies).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 4 — duodenal development and the major papilla","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Weeks 2 to 3 is too early; the pancreatic bud parenchyma and","C":"Weeks 10 to 12 is too late; the pancreatic bud parenchyma an","D":"Weeks 16 to 20 is too late; the pancreatic bud parenchyma an","E":"Weeks 30 to 36 is far too late; the pancreatic bud parenchym"}},{"id":"sdl11_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe how the pancreas forms from two buds and rotates around the duodenum.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, the accessory pancreatic duct of Santorini derives from which duct segment?","choices":{"A":"Distal dorsal duct","B":"Proximal dorsal duct","C":"Entire ventral duct","D":"Proximal ventral duct","E":"Common hepatic duct"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The accessory duct of Santorini comes from the proximal dorsal duct and either obliterates or drains at the minor papilla. The main duct of Wirsung forms from the distal dorsal duct and the entire ventral duct. The common hepatic duct is biliary. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 3, ductal anatomy, lines 2224–2231); Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 817–822.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the main pancreatic duct and bile duct enter the duodenum together at the major papilla, the ampulla of Vater (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the accessory duct, when present, drains at the minor papilla (lecture SDL 11).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 4 — duodenal development and the major papilla","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify transcription factors involved in the formation of endocrine pancreatic cells.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which structures produce the two signals, one of them FGF2, that permit pancreatic specification of the foregut endoderm?","choices":{"A":"Septum transversum and heart","B":"Neural tube and somites","C":"Liver bud and yolk sac","D":"Ectoderm and allantois","E":"Notochord and dorsal aorta"},"correct":"E","explanation":"FGF2 and activin, a TGF beta family member, come from the notochord and the endothelium of the dorsal aorta. The septum transversum and cardiac mesoderm signal liver induction. The neural tube and somites, the liver bud and yolk sac, and the ectoderm and allantois are not the sources; FGF2 and activin from the notochord and dorsal aorta repress SHH in the endoderm destined to form the dorsal pancreatic bud. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 4, repression of SHH, lines 2235–2237); Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 843–845.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic islets develop by the ninth week and scatter through the gland (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: glucagon and somatostatin cells also arise from pancreatic parenchyma (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 5 — islet cell types and their hormones","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"The neural tube and somites, t"}},{"id":"sdl11_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify transcription factors involved in the formation of endocrine pancreatic cells.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which signal must be repressed in foregut endoderm for the pancreas to be specified?","choices":{"A":"Retinoic acid","B":"Beta catenin","C":"Sonic hedgehog","D":"Notch receptor","E":"Fibronectin"},"correct":"C","explanation":"FGF2 and activin repress sonic hedgehog in the endoderm destined to form the dorsal pancreatic bud, which permits pancreatic specification. Retinoic acid, beta catenin, Notch, and fibronectin are not the described targets. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 4, repression of SHH, lines 2235–2237); Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 843–845.","boardPrep":"Step 1: activin is a member of the TGF beta family (lecture SDL 11). COMLEX: insulin secretion begins at about the 12th week (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 5 — islet cell types and their hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify transcription factors involved in the formation of endocrine pancreatic cells.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Langman, which tissue induces the ventral pancreatic bud?","choices":{"A":"Paraxial mesoderm","B":"Visceral mesoderm","C":"Surface ectoderm","D":"Cardiac mesoderm","E":"Dorsal aorta"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The ventral pancreatic bud is induced by visceral mesoderm, which then upregulates PDX1. Cardiac mesoderm induces the liver. Paraxial mesoderm, surface ectoderm, and the dorsal aorta are not the described inducers of the ventral bud. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 845–846; Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 4, PDX1 upregulated under mesodermal influence, lines 2238–2239).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cells expressing both PAX4 and PAX6 become beta, delta, and gamma cells (lecture SDL 11). COMLEX: cells expressing PAX6 alone become alpha cells (lecture SDL 11).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 5 — islet cell types and their hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize common congenital anomalies of the pancreas, like annular pancreas.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what is the most common congenital anomaly of the pancreas?","choices":{"A":"Annular pancreas","B":"Ectopic pancreas","C":"Pancreatic agenesis","D":"Pancreatic hypoplasia","E":"Pancreas divisum"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Pancreas divisum is the most common congenital pancreatic anomaly, with a 3 to 10 percent incidence. Annular pancreas and ectopic pancreas are less common, and agenesis from PDX1 loss is rare. Hypoplasia is not a listed anomaly. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 5, pancreas divisum, lines 2264–2266).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreas divisum is failed fusion of the dorsal and ventral bud duct networks (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic anomalies). COMLEX: in pancreas divisum the ventral and dorsal parts fail to fuse at 7 weeks (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatic anomalies).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — congenital pancreatic anomalies","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize common congenital anomalies of the pancreas, like annular pancreas.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which part of the duodenum does an annular pancreas encircle?","choices":{"A":"First part","B":"Third part","C":"Fourth part","D":"Second part","E":"Duodenal bulb"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A bifid ventral bud whose left portion migrates the wrong way forms a ring of normal pancreas around the second part of the duodenum. The first part and bulb lie proximal, and the third and fourth parts lie distal. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 5, annular pancreas, lines 2254–2258).","boardPrep":"Step 1: annular pancreas is associated with polyhydramnios and feeding regurgitation (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic anomalies). COMLEX: about one third of annular pancreas cases are asymptomatic (lecture SDL 11).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — congenital pancreatic anomalies","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The first part and bulb lie pr","B":"The first part and bulb lie pr","E":"The first part and bulb lie pr"}},{"id":"sdl11_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize common congenital anomalies of the pancreas, like annular pancreas.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, where is ectopic pancreatic tissue most often found?","choices":{"A":"Stomach and duodenum","B":"Esophagus and colon","C":"Spleen and liver","D":"Ileum and appendix","E":"Gallbladder and lung"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Ectopic pancreas is found in about 2 percent of autopsies, most often in the submucosa or mucosa of the stomach and duodenum, then the jejunum, Meckel diverticulum, and ileum. The esophagus and colon, spleen and liver, and gallbladder and lung are not on the lecture's list of favored sites (stomach, duodenum, jejunum, Meckel diverticulum, ileum). Langman also stresses gastric mucosa and Meckel diverticulum. The ileum is among the listed sites but less common than the stomach and duodenum, and the appendix is not on the lecture's list of favored sites. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 5, ectopic pancreas, lines 2274–2278); Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 873–876.","boardPrep":"Step 1: accessory pancreatic tissue can lie anywhere from the distal esophagus to the tip of the primary intestinal loop (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: ectopic pancreas is found in about 2 percent of autopsies (lecture SDL 11).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — congenital pancreatic anomalies","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The esophagus and colon, splee","C":"The esophagus and colon, splee","D":"The ileum is among the listed sites but less common than the","E":"The esophagus and colon, splee"}},{"id":"sdl11_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand how liver development is triggered by signals from nearby tissues.","batch":2,"stem":"A 33-year-old woman at 7 weeks of gestation asks how her embryo forms a liver. Ectoderm, noncardiac mesoderm, and the notochord all release signals that reach the foregut endoderm. What is their effect on that endoderm?","choices":{"A":"Activation of liver genes","B":"Conversion to pancreas","C":"Induction of bile ducts","D":"Repression of liver genes","E":"Promotion of sinusoids"},"correct":"D","explanation":"All foregut endoderm can express liver genes, but signals from ectoderm, noncardiac mesoderm, and especially the notochord repress them. Liver forms only where FGF2 lifts this repression, so the induction works by removing a repressor. These signals do not activate liver genes, convert endoderm to pancreas, or build ducts or sinusoids. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 1, repressor signals, lines 2163–2168); Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 727–731 and Fig. 15.21 legend.","boardPrep":"Step 1: induction can occur by removal of a repressor signal rather than direct activation (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the liver bud appears in the middle of the third week at the distal foregut (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — foregut, midgut, and hindgut","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"These signals do not activate ","C":"These signals do not activate ","E":"These signals do not activate "}},{"id":"sdl11_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand how liver development is triggered by signals from nearby tissues.","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old woman at 6 weeks of gestation asks why only one region of her embryo's gut forms a liver. Her physician describes an experiment in which endoderm from the future stomach region of the gut tube is placed beside the tissue that normally induces the liver bud, and the transplanted endoderm begins to express liver-specific genes. Which property of the endoderm does this experiment show?","choices":{"A":"Hepatic potential limited to the liver bud","B":"Reprogramming by migrating mesoderm cells","C":"Fusion with septum transversum cells","D":"Hepatic potential acquired from ectoderm","E":"Hepatic potential throughout the foregut"},"correct":"E","explanation":"All foregut endoderm inherently has the potential to express liver-specific genes, so endoderm from another foregut region forms liver once it receives the inductive signal. If hepatic potential were limited to the liver bud, the transplanted stomach-region endoderm could not respond. The hepatocytes arise from the endoderm itself, not from reprogrammed mesoderm or fusion with septum transversum cells, which instead contribute BMPs, connective tissue, and Kupffer cells. Ectoderm releases repressing signals rather than conferring hepatic potential. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 1, foregut endoderm potential and inductive signaling, lines 2162–2177); Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 727–735.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the liver bud arises as an outgrowth of endodermal epithelium at the distal foregut (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: hepatic induction depends on signals from the cardiac mesoderm, nearby endothelium, and septum transversum (lecture SDL 11).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — foregut, midgut, and hindgut","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand how liver development is triggered by signals from nearby tissues.","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman at 8 weeks of gestation asks how her embryo's liver began. Her physician explains that, a few weeks earlier, FGF2 released beside the ventral foregut started liver bud formation in that endoderm. By which mechanism did FGF2 start hepatic gene expression?","choices":{"A":"Inducing sonic hedgehog in endoderm","B":"Blocking repressor signals","C":"Converting nearby mesoderm into hepatocytes","D":"Stimulating PDX1 expression in endoderm","E":"Promoting apoptosis of endoderm"},"correct":"B","explanation":"FGF2 from the cardiac mesoderm and nearby endothelial cells suppresses the action of repressor signals, derepressing the hepatic field so that the foregut endoderm expresses liver-specific genes; BMPs from the septum transversum enhance the competence of the endoderm to respond. Sonic hedgehog induction is not the hepatic mechanism, and repression of SHH is instead a step in pancreatic specification. Hepatocytes come from endoderm, not from converted mesoderm. PDX1 is the master factor for the pancreas rather than the liver. Apoptosis of endoderm creates vacuoles during duodenal recanalization and does not specify liver. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 1, inductive signaling by FGF2 and BMPs, lines 2169–2177; section 4, SHH repression and PDX1, lines 2235–2241); Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 727–735.","boardPrep":"Step 1: in liver induction, BMPs from the septum transversum enhance the competence of the endoderm to respond to FGF2 (lecture SDL 11). COMLEX: HNF3 and HNF4 then direct hepatocyte and biliary differentiation of liver bud cells (lecture SDL 11).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — foregut, midgut, and hindgut","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the cause and clinical presentation of extrahepatic biliary atresia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 4-week-old boy has jaundice, dark urine, clay colored stools, and hepatomegaly. Direct bilirubin and GGT are high. Ultrasound shows no visible gallbladder. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Physiologic jaundice","B":"Breast milk jaundice","C":"Dubin Johnson syndrome","D":"Biliary atresia","E":"Crigler Najjar syndrome"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Persistent conjugated jaundice with acholic stools, dark urine, hepatomegaly, and an absent gallbladder 2 to 6 weeks after birth fits extrahepatic biliary atresia. Physiologic and breast milk jaundice and Crigler Najjar syndrome raise unconjugated bilirubin with normal stools. Dubin Johnson syndrome raises conjugated bilirubin but has normal stools, a visible gallbladder, and a dark liver. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 2, clinical presentation, lines 2198–2200); First Aid 2025 GI (biliary atresia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: biliary atresia often presents after 2 weeks of life with jaundice, dark urine, and acholic stools (First Aid 2025 GI biliary atresia). COMLEX: biliary atresia accounts for one third of neonatal cholestasis (lecture SDL 11).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 3 — conjugated versus unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the cause and clinical presentation of extrahepatic biliary atresia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 6-week-old girl has persistent conjugated jaundice and pale stools. An intraoperative cholangiogram shows fibrotic, obliterated extrahepatic bile ducts. Which treatment should be done now to restore bile drainage?","choices":{"A":"Ursodeoxycholic acid therapy","B":"Exchange transfusion","C":"Cholecystectomy","D":"Phototherapy sessions","E":"Kasai portoenterostomy"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A Kasai portoenterostomy restores bile drainage before irreversible cirrhosis develops. Ursodiol does not open an obliterated tree. Exchange transfusion and phototherapy treat unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia. Cholecystectomy does not bypass the blocked ducts. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 2, clinical significance, lines 2203–2205).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the fetal form of biliary atresia reflects failed ductal recanalization (lecture SDL 11). COMLEX: intrahepatic biliary duct atresia is rare and may follow fetal infection (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — large bile duct obstruction","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Ursodiol does not open an obli"}},{"id":"sdl11_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the cause and clinical presentation of extrahepatic biliary atresia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 5-week-old boy has rising direct bilirubin, acholic stools, and hepatomegaly, and ultrasound shows an irregular gallbladder. A liver biopsy is taken before surgery. Which finding is expected?","choices":{"A":"Periportal ground glass cells","B":"Portal ductular reaction","C":"Centrilobular necrosis","D":"Interface plasma cells","E":"Copper laden hepatocytes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Biopsy in extrahepatic biliary atresia shows portal edema, ductular reactions, and neutrophilic infiltrates, with progressive fibrosis of the extrahepatic ducts. Ground glass hepatocytes suggest hepatitis B. Centrilobular necrosis suggests ischemia or toxins. Interface plasma cells suggest autoimmune hepatitis. Copper laden hepatocytes suggest Wilson disease. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 2, morphologic features, lines 2194–2196).","boardPrep":"Step 1: biliary atresia causes jaundice and scleral icterus in the first weeks of life (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, biliary atresia). COMLEX: biliary atresia is associated with an absent or abnormal gallbladder on ultrasound (First Aid 2025 GI biliary atresia).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 5 — bile flow through canaliculi and ducts","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe how the pancreas forms from two buds and rotates around the duodenum.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man has a pancreaticoduodenectomy, and the surgeon notes that the common bile duct runs behind the duodenum. Which developmental event explains this position?","choices":{"A":"Duodenal rotation","B":"Liver bud regression","C":"Gallbladder descent","D":"Ventral mesentery loss","E":"Splenic migration"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The bile duct first enters the duodenum anteriorly, but as the duodenum rotates to the right and becomes C shaped, its entrance shifts posteriorly, so the bile duct passes behind the duodenum. The ventral pancreatic bud follows the same path. The liver bud does not regress, and neither gallbladder position nor the spleen, which develops in the dorsal mesogastrium, sets where the bile duct enters the duodenum. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 686–689 and 809–814; Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 3, rotation around the duodenum, lines 2213–2215).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the pancreas and most of the duodenum become attached to the posterior abdominal wall (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the hepatoduodenal ligament carries the portal triad (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, liver anatomy).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 3 — stomach rotation and the mesenteries","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"The liver bud does not regress"}},{"id":"sdl11_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe how the pancreas forms from two buds and rotates around the duodenum.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old woman has ERCP, and contrast injected at the major papilla fills the main pancreatic duct to the tail. Which embryonic ducts formed this channel?","choices":{"A":"Ventral and proximal dorsal ducts","B":"Dorsal duct and cystic duct","C":"Ventral duct and bile duct","D":"Ventral and distal dorsal ducts","E":"Proximal and distal dorsal ducts"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The main duct of Wirsung forms from the distal part of the dorsal duct joined to the entire ventral duct, and it enters the duodenum with the bile duct at the major papilla. The proximal dorsal duct becomes the accessory duct. The cystic and bile ducts are biliary. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 3, main pancreatic duct, lines 2225–2228); Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 817–823.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the ventral bud forms the uncinate process and inferior head of the pancreas (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the dorsal bud forms the body, tail, and superior head of the pancreas (lecture SDL 11).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 3 — exocrine pancreas and its ducts","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"The cystic and bile ducts are "}},{"id":"sdl11_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe how the pancreas forms from two buds and rotates around the duodenum.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman has a first trimester ultrasound, and her physician explains how the fetal pancreas forms. After the ventral bud moves around the duodenum, where does it come to lie relative to the dorsal bud?","choices":{"A":"Above and in front","B":"Directly anterior to it","C":"To the left of it","D":"Behind the stomach","E":"Below and behind it"},"correct":"E","explanation":"As the duodenum rotates to the right, the ventral bud moves dorsally and comes to lie immediately below and behind the dorsal bud before the two fuse. Above, in front, anterior, or left positions and a site behind the stomach do not match the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 3, bud fusion, lines 2217–2218); Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 812–815.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the pancreas forms from two endodermal buds of the duodenal lining (lecture SDL 11). COMLEX: the tail of the pancreas lies in the splenorenal ligament with the splenic vessels (First Aid 2025 GI ligaments).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — duodenal development","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify transcription factors involved in the formation of endocrine pancreatic cells.","batch":2,"stem":"A 4-month-old boy has a loss of function variant in a transcription factor gene, leaving only one major islet lineage able to form. Which islet hormone can still be produced normally?","choices":{"A":"Glucagon release","B":"Insulin release","C":"Somatostatin release","D":"Pancreatic polypeptide release","E":"Amylin release"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cells expressing both PAX4 and PAX6 become beta, delta, and gamma cells, while cells expressing PAX6 alone become alpha cells. Without PAX4, alpha cells that make glucagon can still form. Insulin and amylin both come from beta cells, somatostatin from delta cells, and pancreatic polypeptide from gamma or PP cells, and all three lineages need PAX4. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 4, PAX4 and PAX6, lines 2242–2250); Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 846–850.","boardPrep":"Step 1: beta cells make insulin, delta cells somatostatin, and gamma cells pancreatic polypeptide (lecture SDL 11). COMLEX: alpha cells make glucagon (lecture SDL 11).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 5 — islet cell types and their hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify transcription factors involved in the formation of endocrine pancreatic cells.","batch":2,"stem":"A newborn girl of consanguineous parents has neonatal diabetes and fatty stools, and imaging shows no pancreatic tissue. Both alleles of one homeobox gene are inactivated. Which gene is it?","choices":{"A":"PAX6","B":"SOX2","C":"HNF4","D":"CDX2","E":"PDX1"},"correct":"E","explanation":"PDX1 is the master homeobox factor for pancreatic outgrowth and differentiation, and homozygous loss of function causes pancreatic agenesis. PAX6 specifies endocrine cells. SOX2 specifies esophagus and stomach. HNF4 controls hepatocyte and biliary differentiation. CDX2 is an intestinal factor. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 4, PDX1, lines 2238–2241); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 5, pancreatic agenesis, lines 2281–2284).","boardPrep":"Step 1: PDX1 is upregulated in pancreatic endoderm under mesodermal influence (lecture SDL 11). COMLEX: pancreatic agenesis is a rare anomaly with complete failure of pancreatic development (lecture SDL 11).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — congenital pancreatic anomalies","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify transcription factors involved in the formation of endocrine pancreatic cells.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman with type 1 diabetes is 6 weeks pregnant and asks how her embryo's pancreas gets started. Her physician explains that two secreted signals from nearby tissues act on the dorsal foregut endoderm and permit it to become pancreas. One of them is FGF2. Which signal is the other?","choices":{"A":"Noggin","B":"Chordin","C":"Activin","D":"Follistatin","E":"Sonic hedgehog"},"correct":"C","explanation":"FGF2 and activin, a TGF beta family member, from the notochord and dorsal aorta endothelium repress sonic hedgehog in the foregut endoderm and so permit pancreatic specification. Noggin and chordin are BMP antagonists and are not the signals the lecture gives for pancreatic specification. Follistatin binds and neutralizes activin rather than acting with FGF2. Sonic hedgehog is the signal that must be repressed for the pancreas to form, so it is the target rather than the partner signal. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 4, repression of sonic hedgehog by FGF2 and activin, lines 2235–2237); Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 826–850.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PDX1 is the master pancreatic homeobox factor, and homozygous loss causes pancreatic agenesis (lecture SDL 11). COMLEX: PAX6 without PAX4 yields alpha cells, the glucagon-producing islet cells (lecture SDL 11).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 5 — islet cell types and their hormones","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Follistatin binds and neutrali","E":"Sonic hedgehog is the signal t"}},{"id":"sdl11_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize common congenital anomalies of the pancreas, like annular pancreas.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man has recurrent pancreatitis without alcohol use or gallstones. MRCP shows most of the gland draining through a narrow minor papilla, and a small duct drains only part of the head. Which developmental error explains this?","choices":{"A":"Abnormal ventral bud rotation","B":"Failed ductal fusion","C":"Ectopic pancreatic rests","D":"Absent ventral bud","E":"Duodenal recanalization failure"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pancreas divisum results from failure of the dorsal and ventral duct systems to fuse, so the dorsal bulk drains through the small minor papilla via the duct of Santorini, and about 5 percent develop chronic pancreatitis. Abnormal ventral bud rotation causes annular pancreas. Ectopic rests are misplaced tissue. An absent ventral bud and failed duodenal recanalization are not the described cause. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 5, pancreas divisum, lines 2264–2272).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreas divisum raises the risk of pancreatitis and abdominal pain (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic anomalies). COMLEX: most cases of pancreas divisum are asymptomatic (lecture SDL 11).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — acute and chronic pancreatitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize common congenital anomalies of the pancreas, like annular pancreas.","batch":2,"stem":"A 2-day-old girl vomits with every feed. Imaging shows a double bubble, and surgery finds a ring of normal pancreatic tissue around the duodenum. Which developmental error explains this?","choices":{"A":"Failed ductal fusion","B":"Failed duodenal recanalization","C":"Split ventral bud rotation","D":"Pyloric muscle hypertrophy","E":"Incomplete midgut rotation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Annular pancreas forms when a bifid ventral bud splits and its left portion migrates opposite to the right, encircling the duodenum. Failed ductal fusion is pancreas divisum. Failed recanalization causes duodenal atresia without a pancreatic ring. Pyloric hypertrophy and midgut malrotation are not ring lesions. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 5, annular pancreas, lines 2254–2262); Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 856–862.","boardPrep":"Step 1: annular pancreas is associated with trisomy 21 (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic anomalies). COMLEX: annular pancreas can cause duodenal stenosis and complete obstruction (lecture SDL 11).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — congenital duodenal obstruction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize common congenital anomalies of the pancreas, like annular pancreas.","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman has a small submucosal nodule in the gastric antrum with central umbilication. Biopsy shows normal pancreatic acini, ducts, and islets. Which complication can this lesion cause?","choices":{"A":"Mucosal bleeding","B":"Duodenal atresia","C":"Biliary cirrhosis","D":"Neonatal diabetes","E":"Hepatic abscess"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Ectopic pancreas is usually incidental but can cause local pain, inflammation, or mucosal bleeding. Duodenal atresia is a recanalization defect. Biliary cirrhosis follows biliary obstruction. Neonatal diabetes fits pancreatic agenesis. Hepatic abscess is not a described complication. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 5, ectopic pancreas, lines 2276–2280).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ectopic pancreatic nests may show all the histologic features of the pancreas (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: ectopic pancreas can cause localized abdominal pain and inflammation (lecture SDL 11).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 5 — normal gastric histology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl11_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand how liver development is triggered by signals from nearby tissues.","batch":3,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman is 6 weeks pregnant by her last menstrual period, so her embryo is about 4 weeks old. In her embryo, which cells beside the gut tube at the liver bud site secrete FGF2?","choices":{"A":"Notochord sheath cells","B":"Surface ectoderm cells","C":"Endothelial cells","D":"Neural crest cells","E":"Somitic mesoderm cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"FGF2 that lifts repression of hepatic genes comes from cardiac mesoderm and from blood vessel forming endothelial cells next to the gut tube at the liver bud site. The notochord and ectoderm release repressing signals. Neural crest and somitic mesoderm are not described FGF2 sources. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 1, inductive signaling by FGF2 and BMPs, lines 2170–2174); Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 731–735.","boardPrep":"Step 1: cardiac mesoderm and nearby endothelium instruct gut endoderm to express liver genes by inhibiting an inhibitor (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: liver cords intermingle with the vitelline and umbilical veins, which form the hepatic sinusoids (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — foregut, midgut, and hindgut","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl11_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the cause and clinical presentation of extrahepatic biliary atresia.","batch":3,"stem":"A 7-week-old girl has conjugated jaundice and acholic stools, and surgery finds fibrotic, obliterated extrahepatic ducts. Among infants with this defect, about what share have patent proximal ducts and a correctable lesion?","choices":{"A":"15 to 20 percent","B":"1 to 2 percent","C":"40 to 50 percent","D":"70 to 80 percent","E":"95 to 99 percent"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Langman states that 15 to 20 percent of infants with extrahepatic biliary atresia have patent proximal ducts and a correctable defect, while the rest usually need a liver transplant. The other shares do not match. 1 to 2 percent is too low; Langman states 15 to 20 percent of infants with extrahepatic biliary atresia have patent proximal ducts and a correctable defect. 40 to 50 percent is too high; Langman states 15 to 20 percent have patent proximal ducts and a correctable defect. 70 to 80 percent is too high; Langman states only 15 to 20 percent have patent proximal ducts and a correctable defect, and the rest usually need a liver transplant. 95 to 99 percent is far too high; Langman states only 15 to 20 percent have patent proximal ducts and a correctable defect. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 782–785.","boardPrep":"Step 1: extrahepatic biliary atresia occurs in about 1 in 15,000 live births (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: biliary atresia shows jaundice, dark urine, acholic stools, and hepatomegaly (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, biliary atresia).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — large bile duct obstruction","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"1 to 2 percent is too low; Langman states 15 to 20 percent o","C":"40 to 50 percent is too high; Langman states 15 to 20 percen","D":"70 to 80 percent is too high; Langman states only 15 to 20 p","E":"95 to 99 percent is far too high; Langman states only 15 to "}},{"id":"trial_sdl11_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe how the pancreas forms from two buds and rotates around the duodenum.","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman has a mass in the tail of the pancreas on CT. Which embryonic structure formed this part of the gland?","choices":{"A":"Ventral pancreatic bud","B":"Hepatic diverticulum","C":"Cystic duct outgrowth","D":"Septum transversum","E":"Dorsal pancreatic bud"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The dorsal bud forms the body, tail, and superior and anterior head of the pancreas. The ventral bud forms the uncinate process and inferior head. The hepatic diverticulum forms the liver, the cystic outgrowth forms the gallbladder, and the septum transversum gives mesoderm for the liver. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 3, dorsal bud derivatives, lines 2222–2223).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the tail of the pancreas comes to lie against the posterior body wall in the dorsal mesogastrium (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the ventral bud lies close to the bile duct outgrowth (lecture SDL 11).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 4 — pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl11_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify transcription factors involved in the formation of endocrine pancreatic cells.","batch":3,"stem":"Which two transcription factor genes must be expressed in pancreatic parenchymal precursors to specify the islet endocrine lineage?","choices":{"A":"HNF3 and HNF4","B":"PAX4 and PAX6","C":"SOX2 and CDXC","D":"PDX1 and SHH","E":"NKX2 and GATA4"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Expression of the paired box genes PAX4 and PAX6 specifies the endocrine lineage. HNF3 and HNF4 control hepatocyte and biliary lineages. SOX2 and CDXC pattern the gut tube. PDX1 drives overall pancreatic outgrowth, and SHH must be repressed for pancreas to form. NKX2 and GATA4 are not the described factors. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 4, endocrine lineage specification, lines 2242–2245).","boardPrep":"Step 1: homozygous PDX1 mutations cause pancreatic agenesis (lecture SDL 11). COMLEX: islet cells scatter throughout the pancreas after forming from parenchymal tissue (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 5 — islet cell types and their hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl11_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize common congenital anomalies of the pancreas, like annular pancreas.","batch":3,"stem":"A 40-year-old woman learns that MRCP shows pancreas divisum. She asks how often people with this anomaly develop chronic pancreatitis. About what share do?","choices":{"A":"About 5 percent","B":"About 25 percent","C":"About 50 percent","D":"About 75 percent","E":"About 95 percent"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Most cases of pancreas divisum are asymptomatic, but about 5 percent develop chronic pancreatitis from inadequate drainage through the minor papilla. The higher shares do not match the lecture. About 25 percent is too high; most pancreas divisum is asymptomatic and about 5 percent develop chronic pancreatitis. About 50 percent is too high; most pancreas divisum is asymptomatic and about 5 percent develop chronic pancreatitis. About 75 percent is too high; most pancreas divisum is asymptomatic and about 5 percent develop chronic pancreatitis. About 95 percent is far too high; most pancreas divisum is asymptomatic and about 5 percent develop chronic pancreatitis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 5, pancreas divisum, lines 2270–2272).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in pancreas divisum the duct of Wirsung drains only a small portion of the head (lecture SDL 11). COMLEX: pancreas divisum has a 3 to 10 percent incidence (lecture SDL 11).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — congenital pancreatic anomalies","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"About 25 percent is too high; most pancreas divisum is asymp","C":"About 50 percent is too high; most pancreas divisum is asymp","D":"About 75 percent is too high; most pancreas divisum is asymp","E":"About 95 percent is far too high; most pancreas divisum is a"}},{"id":"trial_sdl11_q06","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize common congenital anomalies of the pancreas, like annular pancreas.","batch":3,"stem":"A 50-year-old man has a CT for kidney stones that incidentally shows a ring of pancreatic tissue around the duodenum. About what share of people with this anomaly have no symptoms?","choices":{"A":"About one tenth","B":"About one half","C":"About two thirds","D":"About one third","E":"About nine tenths"},"correct":"D","explanation":"About one third of annular pancreas cases are asymptomatic, while others develop duodenal stenosis or complete obstruction with vomiting in neonates or adults. The other shares do not match the lecture. About one tenth is too low; the lecture says about one third of annular pancreas cases are asymptomatic. About one half is too high; the lecture says about one third of annular pancreas cases are asymptomatic. About two thirds is too high; the lecture says about one third of annular pancreas cases are asymptomatic, and the rest develop duodenal stenosis or obstruction. About nine tenths is far too high; the lecture says about one third of annular pancreas cases are asymptomatic. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 5, annular pancreas, lines 2259–2262).","boardPrep":"Step 1: an annular pancreas forms a ring of normal pancreatic tissue around the duodenum (lecture SDL 11). COMLEX: annular pancreas can present with vomiting in neonates or adults (lecture SDL 11).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — congenital duodenal obstruction","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"About one tenth is too low; the lecture says about one third","B":"About one half is too high; the lecture says about one third","C":"About two thirds is too high; the lecture says about one thi","E":"About nine tenths is far too high; the lecture says about on"}}]},{"sdlNumber":12,"title":"SDL 12 — Histology of the Liver","questions":[{"id":"sdl12_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the organization of the classic hepatic lobule and trace hepatic blood flow, including the portal triad, hepatic sinusoids, hepatocyte plates, and central vein.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about what share of total hepatic blood flow does the portal vein supply?","choices":{"A":"About 70 to 75 percent","B":"About 25 to 30 percent","C":"About 10 to 15 percent","D":"About 45 to 50 percent","E":"About 90 to 95 percent"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The portal vein supplies about 70 to 75 percent of hepatic blood flow as deoxygenated, nutrient rich blood from the GI tract and spleen, and the hepatic artery supplies the other 25 to 30 percent as oxygen rich blood. About 25 to 30 percent is the hepatic artery's share of hepatic blood flow, not the portal vein's. About 10 to 15 percent is too low; the portal vein supplies 70 to 75 percent of hepatic blood flow. About 45 to 50 percent is too low; the portal vein supplies most of hepatic blood flow, 70 to 75 percent. About 90 to 95 percent is too high; the hepatic artery supplies the other 25 to 30 percent of hepatic blood flow. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 1, hepatic blood flow pathway, lines 2299–2302).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the liver has a dual blood supply from the portal vein and the proper hepatic artery (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, liver anatomy). COMLEX: the hepatoduodenal ligament carries the hepatic portal vein, proper hepatic artery, and common bile duct (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, liver anatomy).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 1 — liver development","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"About 25 to 30 percent is the hepatic artery's share of hepa","C":"About 10 to 15 percent is too low; the portal vein supplies ","D":"About 45 to 50 percent is too low; the portal vein supplies ","E":"About 90 to 95 percent is too high; the hepatic artery suppl"}},{"id":"sdl12_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the organization of the classic hepatic lobule and trace hepatic blood flow, including the portal triad, hepatic sinusoids, hepatocyte plates, and central vein.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, how thick are normal adult hepatocyte plates?","choices":{"A":"Two cells thick","B":"Three cells thick","C":"Five cells thick","D":"Ten cells thick","E":"One cell thick"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Adult hepatocytes form branching, anastomosing plates that are normally one cell thick and radiate from the central vein, with sinusoids between neighboring plates. Thicker plates are not the adult pattern. Adult hepatocyte plates are one cell thick; two-cell-thick plates are not the adult pattern. Adult hepatocyte plates are one cell thick; three-cell-thick plates are not the adult pattern. Adult hepatocyte plates are one cell thick; five-cell-thick plates are not the adult pattern. Adult hepatocyte plates are one cell thick, with sinusoids between them; ten-cell-thick plates do not occur normally. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 1, hepatocyte plates and sinusoids, lines 2294–2297).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hepatocytes have a central round nucleus with prominent nucleoli and stain PAS positive from glycogen (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic microarchitecture). COMLEX: hepatocytes carry out metabolism, detoxification, and protein synthesis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic microarchitecture).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 1 — liver development","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Adult hepatocyte plates are one cell thick; two-cell-thick p","B":"Adult hepatocyte plates are one cell thick; three-cell-thick","C":"Adult hepatocyte plates are one cell thick; five-cell-thick ","D":"Adult hepatocyte plates are one cell thick, with sinusoids b"}},{"id":"sdl12_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the organization of the classic hepatic lobule and trace hepatic blood flow, including the portal triad, hepatic sinusoids, hepatocyte plates, and central vein.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, into which vessels does the central vein drain first?","choices":{"A":"Hepatic veins","B":"Portal venules","C":"Interlobular veins","D":"Sublobular veins","E":"Splenic veins"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The central vein empties into sublobular veins, which join the hepatic veins and then the inferior vena cava. Portal and interlobular veins bring blood into the lobule rather than draining it, and the splenic vein feeds the portal vein. Hepatic veins receive blood from the sublobular veins; the central vein drains first into sublobular veins. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 1, centripetal flow and venous drainage, lines 2303–2308).","boardPrep":"Step 1: portal vein blood passes through interlobular veins and sinusoids to the central vein and hepatic vein, a summary that skips the sublobular veins (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, flow of blood and bile). COMLEX: Budd Chiari syndrome causes ischemic zone 3 injury (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic zones).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 1 — liver development","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Hepatic veins receive blood from the sublobular veins; the c"}},{"id":"sdl12_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare zones 1, 2, and 3 of the liver acinus based on blood supply and oxygenation, and predict their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury, including centrilobular (zone 3) injury.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what forms the short axis of the liver acinus?","choices":{"A":"Line joining two central veins","B":"Sublobular vein branches","C":"Interlobular bile ducts","D":"Bile canaliculi network","E":"Distributing portal vessels"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The short axis of the acinus is formed by the penetrating distributing vessels from portal triads between two classic lobules, and the long axis runs between the two nearest central veins. Sublobular veins drain the lobule, and bile ducts and canaliculi carry bile. The line joining two central veins is the long axis of the acinus; the short axis is formed by the distributing portal vessels. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 2, structure of the liver acinus, lines 2312–2316).","boardPrep":"Step 1: zone 1 is the first zone to meet incoming blood and has the highest oxygen and metabolic activity (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic zones). COMLEX: zone 1 is affected first by viral hepatitis and ingested toxins such as cocaine (First Aid 2025 GI liver tissue architecture).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 1 — circulatory disorders of the liver","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The line joining two central veins is the long axis of the a"}},{"id":"sdl12_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare zones 1, 2, and 3 of the liver acinus based on blood supply and oxygenation, and predict their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury, including centrilobular (zone 3) injury.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what gross appearance results from chronic passive congestion of the liver?","choices":{"A":"Hobnail liver","B":"Nutmeg liver","C":"Bronze liver","D":"Green liver","E":"Zahn infarct"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ischemic or congestive injury causes centrilobular necrosis and a mottled gross appearance termed nutmeg liver. A hobnail surface fits cirrhosis, bronze color fits iron overload, green color fits cholestasis, and a Zahn infarct is not the described pattern. Lines of Zahn are laminations of thrombi formed in flowing blood, not a feature of hepatic congestion. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 2, ischemic susceptibility, lines 2327–2333).","boardPrep":"Step 1: zone 3 is affected first by ischemia because it is least oxygenated, as in congestive hepatopathy (First Aid 2025 GI liver tissue architecture). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists Budd Chiari syndrome and chronic alcohol use as zone 3 injuries (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic zones).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 1 — circulatory disorders of the liver","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Lines of Zahn are laminations of thrombi formed in flowing b"}},{"id":"sdl12_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare zones 1, 2, and 3 of the liver acinus based on blood supply and oxygenation, and predict their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury, including centrilobular (zone 3) injury.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what overall shape does the liver acinus have?","choices":{"A":"Diamond shaped","B":"Hexagonal prism","C":"Spherical cluster","D":"Cylindrical tube","E":"Rectangular block"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The liver acinus is a diamond or lozenge shaped functional unit defined along microvascular lines. The hexagonal prism is the classic lobule. Spherical, cylindrical, and rectangular shapes are not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 2, structure of the liver acinus, line 2312).","boardPrep":"Step 1: zone 2 has intermediate oxygen tension and is the zone affected by yellow fever (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic zones). COMLEX: the classic lobule is a hexagon with portal tracts at its six angles (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 1 — circulatory disorders of the liver","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the structure of hepatic sinusoids and the space of Disse and explain how the discontinuous sinusoidal endothelium facilitates exchange between plasma and hepatocytes.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, up to what diameter are the fenestrae of hepatic sinusoidal endothelium?","choices":{"A":"About 10 nm","B":"About 10 µm","C":"About 0.1 nm","D":"About 1 µm","E":"About 100 µm"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Sinusoidal endothelial cells have large fenestrae up to about 1 µm without diaphragms, wide gaps between cells, and no continuous basal lamina. The other sizes do not match the lecture. About 10 nm is too small; sinusoidal endothelial fenestrae are large, up to about 1 µm, and lack diaphragms. About 10 µm is ten times too large; sinusoidal endothelial fenestrae reach up to about 1 µm. About 0.1 nm is vastly too small; sinusoidal endothelial fenestrae reach up to about 1 µm. About 100 µm is far too large; sinusoidal endothelial fenestrae reach up to about 1 µm. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 3, discontinuous sinusoidal endothelium, lines 2345–2349).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sinusoids are lined by a single permeable, poorly selective layer of endothelial cells (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic microarchitecture). COMLEX: the apical surface of hepatocytes faces bile canaliculi and the basolateral surface faces sinusoids (First Aid 2025 GI liver tissue architecture).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — epithelial types and mucosal specializations","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"About 10 nm is too small; sinusoidal endothelial fenestrae a","B":"About 10 µm is ten times too large; sinusoidal endothelial f","C":"About 0.1 nm is vastly too small; sinusoidal endothelial fen","E":"About 100 µm is far too large; sinusoidal endothelial fenest"}},{"id":"sdl12_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the structure of hepatic sinusoids and the space of Disse and explain how the discontinuous sinusoidal endothelium facilitates exchange between plasma and hepatocytes.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, hepatocyte microvilli in the space of Disse raise the surface area for exchange by up to how much?","choices":{"A":"Twofold","B":"Tenfold","C":"Sixfold","D":"Fiftyfold","E":"Hundredfold"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Numerous hepatocyte microvilli project into the space of Disse and increase the surface area for metabolic exchange up to sixfold. Twofold understates the gain, and tenfold, fiftyfold, or hundredfold overstate it. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 3, space of Disse, lines 2350–2354).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hepatic stellate cells lie in the perisinusoidal space of Disse (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic microarchitecture). COMLEX: the space of Disse lies between the sinusoidal endothelium and the hepatocytes (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — epithelial types and mucosal specializations","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the structure of hepatic sinusoids and the space of Disse and explain how the discontinuous sinusoidal endothelium facilitates exchange between plasma and hepatocytes.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, excess fluid in the space of Disse drains into which space to form hepatic lymph?","choices":{"A":"Space of Mall","B":"Space of Retzius","C":"Bile canaliculi","D":"Central vein lumen","E":"Canals of Hering"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Excess fluid in the space of Disse drains into the periportal spaces of Mall and forms hepatic lymph. The space of Retzius is retropubic. Bile canaliculi and the canals of Hering carry bile. The central vein carries blood. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 3, plasma hepatocyte exchange, lines 2362–2363).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hepatocytes release albumin, clotting factors, and lipoproteins into the space of Disse (lecture SDL 12). COMLEX: hepatocytes take up glucose, amino acids, lipids, and toxins from plasma in the space of Disse (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — epithelial types and mucosal specializations","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the major functions of Kupffer cells and hepatic stellate (Ito) cells, including their roles in phagocytosis, iron recycling, vitamin A storage, and collagen deposition during hepatic fibrosis.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about what share of total liver cells are Kupffer cells?","choices":{"A":"About 2 percent","B":"About 15 percent","C":"About 40 percent","D":"About 60 percent","E":"About 85 percent"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Kupffer cells make up about 15 percent of liver cells and 80 to 90 percent of the tissue macrophages in the body. The other shares do not match the lecture. About 2 percent is too low; Kupffer cells make up about 15 percent of liver cells. About 40 percent is too high; Kupffer cells make up about 15 percent of liver cells. About 60 percent is too high; Kupffer cells make up about 15 percent of liver cells, though they are 80 to 90 percent of body tissue macrophages. About 85 percent is too high; Kupffer cells make up about 15 percent of liver cells, while 80 to 90 percent refers to their share of tissue macrophages. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 4, Kupffer cell location and nature, lines 2366–2370).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Kupffer cells are hepatic sinusoidal macrophages that carry out phagocytosis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic microarchitecture). COMLEX: Kupffer cells clear bacteria and damaged or senescent red cells (First Aid 2025 GI liver tissue architecture).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — alcohol associated and steatotic liver disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"About 2 percent is too low; Kupffer cells make up about 15 p","C":"About 40 percent is too high; Kupffer cells make up about 15","D":"About 60 percent is too high; Kupffer cells make up about 15","E":"About 85 percent is too high; Kupffer cells make up about 15"}},{"id":"sdl12_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the major functions of Kupffer cells and hepatic stellate (Ito) cells, including their roles in phagocytosis, iron recycling, vitamin A storage, and collagen deposition during hepatic fibrosis.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, in what chemical form is dietary vitamin A stored in the liver?","choices":{"A":"Retinoic acid","B":"Free retinol","C":"Beta carotene","D":"Retinyl esters","E":"Retinal aldehyde"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Quiescent hepatic stellate cells take up and esterify dietary vitamin A and store it as retinyl esters in cytoplasmic lipid droplets. Retinol is released bound to retinol binding protein when blood levels fall. Retinal acts in the retina, where 11-cis-retinal binds opsin, and neither it nor beta carotene is the esterified form held in stellate cell lipid droplets. Retinoic acid is the active vitamin A form that regulates gene transcription through nuclear retinoid receptors, not the stored form. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 4, vitamin A storage, lines 2384–2388); Pawlina Ch. 18 (hepatic stellate cells: retinyl esters; 11-cis-retinal binds opsin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: stellate cells store vitamin A when quiescent and produce extracellular matrix when activated (First Aid 2025 GI liver tissue architecture). COMLEX: activated stellate cells are responsible for hepatic fibrosis (First Aid 2025 GI liver tissue architecture).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — alcohol associated and steatotic liver disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Retinoic acid is the active vitamin A form that regulates ge"}},{"id":"sdl12_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the major functions of Kupffer cells and hepatic stellate (Ito) cells, including their roles in phagocytosis, iron recycling, vitamin A storage, and collagen deposition during hepatic fibrosis.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which two cytokines drive fibrogenic activation in chronic liver injury?","choices":{"A":"IL 2 and IL 4","B":"IFN gamma and IL 12","C":"TGF beta and PDGF","D":"IL 5 and IL 13","E":"GM CSF and IL 3"},"correct":"C","explanation":"TGF beta and PDGF released by activated Kupffer cells and injured hepatocytes turn stellate cells into proliferative, fibrogenic myofibroblasts. The other pairs are lymphocyte or hematopoietic cytokines not described here. The lecture names TGF beta and PDGF, released by activated Kupffer cells and injured hepatocytes, as the cytokines that turn stellate cells into fibrogenic myofibroblasts; IL 2 and IL 4 are not named. The lecture names TGF beta and PDGF, released by activated Kupffer cells and injured hepatocytes, as the cytokines that turn stellate cells into fibrogenic myofibroblasts; IFN gamma and IL 12 are not named. The lecture names TGF beta and PDGF, released by activated Kupffer cells and injured hepatocytes, as the cytokines that turn stellate cells into fibrogenic myofibroblasts; IL 5 and IL 13 are not named. The lecture names TGF beta and PDGF, released by activated Kupffer cells and injured hepatocytes, as the cytokines that turn stellate cells into fibrogenic myofibroblasts; GM CSF and IL 3 are not named. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 4, activation and collagen deposition, lines 2389–2393).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Kupffer cells release IL 1, IL 6, TNF alpha, and IL 8 in alcohol induced hepatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, alcohol induced hepatitis). COMLEX: zone 3 is usually affected first in alcohol induced hepatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, alcohol induced hepatitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — alcohol associated and steatotic liver disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The lecture names TGF beta and PDGF, released by activated Kupffer cells and injured hepatocytes, as the cytokines that turn stellate cells into fibrogenic myofibroblasts; IL 2 and","B":"The lecture names TGF beta and PDGF, released by activated Kupffer cells and injured hepatocytes, as the cytokines that turn stellate cells into fibrogenic myofibroblasts; IFN gamm","D":"The lecture names TGF beta and PDGF, released by activated Kupffer cells and injured hepatocytes, as the cytokines that turn stellate cells into fibrogenic myofibroblasts; IL 5 and","E":"The lecture names TGF beta and PDGF, released by activated Kupffer cells and injured hepatocytes, as the cytokines that turn stellate cells into fibrogenic myofibroblasts; GM CSF a"}},{"id":"sdl12_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Trace bile flow from hepatocytes through bile canaliculi and the intrahepatic bile ducts, and contrast the direction of bile flow with hepatic blood flow.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about what diameter are bile canaliculi?","choices":{"A":"About 5 to 10 µm","B":"About 0.5 to 1 µm","C":"About 50 to 100 µm","D":"About 1 to 2 mm","E":"About 10 to 20 nm"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Bile canaliculi are tiny intercellular channels about 0.5 to 1 µm across, formed by apposed grooves between hepatocytes. The other sizes do not match the lecture. About 5 to 10 µm is too wide; bile canaliculi are about 0.5 to 1 µm across. About 50 to 100 µm is far too wide; bile canaliculi are about 0.5 to 1 µm across. About 1 to 2 mm is far too wide; bile canaliculi are tiny intercellular channels about 0.5 to 1 µm across. About 10 to 20 nm is too narrow; bile canaliculi are about 0.5 to 1 µm across. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 5, bile synthesis and canaliculi, lines 2403–2407).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bile flows from canaliculi to bile ductules, the hepatic duct, and the common bile duct (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, flow of blood and bile). COMLEX: hepatocytes secrete bile across their apical membranes (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — formation and composition of bile","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"About 5 to 10 µm is too wide; bile canaliculi are about 0.5 ","C":"About 50 to 100 µm is far too wide; bile canaliculi are abou","D":"About 1 to 2 mm is far too wide; bile canaliculi are tiny in","E":"About 10 to 20 nm is too narrow; bile canaliculi are about 0"}},{"id":"sdl12_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Trace bile flow from hepatocytes through bile canaliculi and the intrahepatic bile ducts, and contrast the direction of bile flow with hepatic blood flow.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which short channels at the lobule margin are lined by both hepatocytes and cuboidal cholangiocytes?","choices":{"A":"Bile canaliculi","B":"Interlobular ducts","C":"Canals of Hering","D":"Spaces of Mall","E":"Sublobular veins"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Canaliculi empty into canals of Hering at the lobule margin, which are uniquely lined by both hepatocytes and cholangiocytes and have contractile activity. Canaliculi are lined only by hepatocyte membranes. Interlobular ducts are fully cholangiocyte lined. The spaces of Mall hold lymph, and sublobular veins carry blood. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 5, intrahepatic biliary pathway, lines 2409–2412).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bile ductules collect bile from the canals of Hering and are lined completely by cholangiocytes (lecture SDL 12). COMLEX: ductules pass into the periportal space before joining interlobular ducts (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — formation and composition of bile","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Trace bile flow from hepatocytes through bile canaliculi and the intrahepatic bile ducts, and contrast the direction of bile flow with hepatic blood flow.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, where do the right and left hepatic ducts unite to form the common hepatic duct?","choices":{"A":"Ampulla of Vater","B":"Gallbladder neck","C":"Minor papilla","D":"Caudate lobe","E":"Porta hepatis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The right and left hepatic ducts unite at the porta hepatis to form the common hepatic duct. The ampulla of Vater and minor papilla open into the duodenum, the gallbladder neck leads to the cystic duct, and the caudate lobe is a liver lobe. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 5, major hepatic ducts, lines 2417–2420).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the portal triad of the lobule holds a hepatic arteriole, an interlobular vein, and a bile ductule (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic microarchitecture). COMLEX: tight junctions on hepatocytes prevent mixing of bile with sinusoidal blood (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic microarchitecture).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — formation and composition of bile","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the organization of the classic hepatic lobule and trace hepatic blood flow, including the portal triad, hepatic sinusoids, hepatocyte plates, and central vein.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man has a liver biopsy. At one angle of a hexagonal lobule, the pathologist identifies branches of the portal vein and hepatic artery next to an interlobular bile duct. Which other structures normally run with these?","choices":{"A":"Sinusoids and Kupffer cells","B":"Lymphatics and nerves","C":"Lymph nodes and ganglia","D":"Hepatic venules","E":"Smooth muscle bundles"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A portal triad holds terminal branches of the portal vein and hepatic artery and an interlobular bile duct, along with lymphatic vessels and nerve fibers. Sinusoids and Kupffer cells lie between the plates. Lymph nodes and ganglia are not in portal tracts. Hepatic venules lie at the lobule center. Smooth muscle bundles are not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 1, portal triad components, lines 2291–2293).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists the portal vein, hepatic artery, bile ducts, and lymphatics in the portal triad (First Aid 2025 GI liver tissue architecture). COMLEX: the classic lobule is about 1 to 2 mm across (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 1 — liver development","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the organization of the classic hepatic lobule and trace hepatic blood flow, including the portal triad, hepatic sinusoids, hepatocyte plates, and central vein.","batch":2,"stem":"A 37-year-old woman has a liver transplant, and her hepatic artery thromboses while portal flow continues. Which feature of hepatic inflow has she mainly lost?","choices":{"A":"Nutrient rich blood","B":"Gut derived toxins","C":"Splenic venous return","D":"Oxygen rich blood","E":"Most of total flow"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The hepatic artery brings oxygen rich blood and 25 to 30 percent of flow, while the portal vein brings most of the flow as deoxygenated, nutrient rich blood from the GI tract and spleen. Losing the artery removes mainly the oxygen rich supply. Gut derived blood and splenic venous return both travel in the portal vein, which still flows. The portal vein, which still flows, carries most of total flow (70 to 75 percent); the hepatic artery supplies only 25 to 30 percent. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 1, hepatic blood flow pathway, lines 2299–2303).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hepatic artery blood passes through hepatic arterioles and sinusoids to the central vein (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, flow of blood and bile). COMLEX: blood from both inflow vessels opens into sinusoids at the lobule periphery (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 1 — circulatory disorders of the liver","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Gut derived blood and splenic ","E":"The portal vein, which still flows, carries most of total fl"}},{"id":"sdl12_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the organization of the classic hepatic lobule and trace hepatic blood flow, including the portal triad, hepatic sinusoids, hepatocyte plates, and central vein.","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old man has contrast injected into a small portal vein branch during angiography. Where does the contrast first enter the hepatic sinusoids?","choices":{"A":"Lobule center","B":"Space of Disse","C":"Lobule periphery","D":"Bile canaliculi","E":"Sublobular veins"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Terminal branches of the portal vein and hepatic artery open into the sinusoids at the periphery of the lobule, and blood then flows centripetally toward the central vein. The center is where blood leaves. The space of Disse holds plasma outside the sinusoid, canaliculi carry bile, and sublobular veins drain the lobule. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 1, hepatic blood flow and centripetal flow, lines 2302–2306).","boardPrep":"Step 1: mixed blood flows centripetally from the portal triads toward the central vein (lecture SDL 12). COMLEX: the hepatic veins drain into the inferior vena cava (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 4 — portal hypertension and portocaval collaterals","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare zones 1, 2, and 3 of the liver acinus based on blood supply and oxygenation, and predict their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury, including centrilobular (zone 3) injury.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man has cardiogenic shock for six hours. Two days later his aminotransferases are markedly raised. A liver biopsy is taken. Which pattern is expected?","choices":{"A":"Periportal necrosis","B":"Midzonal necrosis","C":"Portal tract fibrosis","D":"Interface hepatitis","E":"Pericentral necrosis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Zone 3 hepatocytes receive blood already depleted of oxygen, so shock and hypotension cause centrilobular or pericentral necrosis. Periportal injury fits direct toxins or viral hepatitis. Midzonal necrosis fits yellow fever. Fibrosis and interface hepatitis are chronic patterns. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 2, ischemic susceptibility, lines 2327–2331).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists ischemic injury such as Budd Chiari syndrome among zone 3 lesions (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic zones). COMLEX: ischemic hepatitis follows hypotension, shock, or heart failure (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 1 — hepatic venous outflow and ischemic injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare zones 1, 2, and 3 of the liver acinus based on blood supply and oxygenation, and predict their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury, including centrilobular (zone 3) injury.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old man has acute viral hepatitis, and a liver biopsy shows the most intense inflammation in one part of the acinus. Which part is it most likely?","choices":{"A":"Subcapsular region","B":"Midzonal zone 2","C":"Central vein wall","D":"Periportal zone 1","E":"Sublobular septa"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Direct acting toxins and viral hepatitis often injure the periportal zone 1, which meets incoming blood first. Zone 2 is the yellow fever pattern. The subcapsular region, the central vein wall, and sublobular septa are not described sites. The pericentral zone 3 around the central vein is the least oxygenated region and the classic site of ischemic and acetaminophen necrosis, not viral periportal injury. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 2, toxic susceptibility, lines 2338–2340).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists autoimmune hepatitis and alpha 1 antitrypsin deficiency among zone 1 lesions (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic zones). COMLEX: zone 1 is best oxygenated and most resistant to circulatory compromise (First Aid 2025 GI liver tissue architecture).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 2 — hepatotropic viruses","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Zone 2 is the yellow fever pat","C":"The pericentral zone 3 around the central vein is the least "}},{"id":"sdl12_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare zones 1, 2, and 3 of the liver acinus based on blood supply and oxygenation, and predict their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury, including centrilobular (zone 3) injury.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man is exposed to carbon tetrachloride at work, and a liver biopsy shows necrosis of the hepatocytes around the central veins. Which property of these hepatocytes best explains their injury?","choices":{"A":"High P450 enzyme content","B":"Highest oxygen tension","C":"Absent Kupffer cells","D":"Thin sinusoidal flow","E":"Densest bile canaliculi network"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Zone 3 has high concentrations of cytochrome P450 enzymes, so toxins that need bioactivation, such as carbon tetrachloride and acetaminophen, form reactive metabolites there and cause centrilobular necrosis. Zone 3 has the lowest, not highest, oxygen. Kupffer cells, sinusoidal flow, and canaliculi do not explain the zone. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 2, toxic susceptibility, lines 2334–2338).","boardPrep":"Step 1: zone 3 is the main site of drug detoxification by CYP450 (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic zones). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists halothane, CCl4, and rifampin as zone 3 toxins (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic zones).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 5 — drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Kupffer cells, sinusoidal flow"}},{"id":"sdl12_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the structure of hepatic sinusoids and the space of Disse and explain how the discontinuous sinusoidal endothelium facilitates exchange between plasma and hepatocytes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man has normal liver function, and his hepatocytes make albumin continuously. By which route does newly made albumin reach the blood?","choices":{"A":"Bile canaliculi to ducts","B":"Lymph to thoracic duct","C":"Canals of Hering to hepatic veins","D":"Space of Disse to sinusoid","E":"Central vein directly"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Hepatocytes release albumin, clotting factors, and lipoproteins into the space of Disse, and these enter sinusoidal blood through the fenestrated, gapped endothelium. Canaliculi and canals of Hering carry bile, lymph carries only excess Disse fluid, and hepatocytes do not secrete straight into the central vein. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 3, plasma hepatocyte exchange, lines 2355–2362).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the basolateral surface of hepatocytes faces the sinusoids (First Aid 2025 GI liver tissue architecture). COMLEX: large macromolecules and plasma proteins pass freely from the sinusoid into the space of Disse (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — epithelial types and mucosal specializations","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the structure of hepatic sinusoids and the space of Disse and explain how the discontinuous sinusoidal endothelium facilitates exchange between plasma and hepatocytes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman has a research liver study showing that large lipoproteins pass freely between sinusoidal plasma and hepatocytes. Besides large fenestrae and wide cell gaps, which feature allows this?","choices":{"A":"Tight endothelial junctions","B":"Thick glycocalyx layer","C":"Diaphragm covered pores","D":"Pericyte wrapped capillaries","E":"No continuous basal lamina"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The sinusoidal endothelium has large fenestrae without diaphragms, wide intercellular gaps, and no continuous basal lamina, so even large macromolecules pass freely into the space of Disse. Tight junctions, a thick glycocalyx, diaphragms, and pericyte coverage would restrict exchange. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 3, discontinuous endothelium and exchange, lines 2345–2358).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hepatic sinusoids are discontinuous capillaries (lecture SDL 12). COMLEX: hepatocyte microvilli project into the space of Disse (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — epithelial types and mucosal specializations","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Tight junctions, a thick glyco"}},{"id":"sdl12_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the structure of hepatic sinusoids and the space of Disse and explain how the discontinuous sinusoidal endothelium facilitates exchange between plasma and hepatocytes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old man with cirrhosis has a biopsy showing dense collagen in the perisinusoidal space and sinusoids that have lost their fenestrae. Which hepatocyte function is most directly impaired by this change?","choices":{"A":"Bile acid conjugation","B":"Plasma exchange","C":"Glycogen synthesis","D":"Drug glucuronidation","E":"Ammonia to urea"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Collagen in the space of Disse with loss of fenestrae, called sinusoidal capillarization, blocks the free passage of plasma between sinusoids and hepatocytes. Conjugation, glycogen synthesis, glucuronidation, and the urea cycle are intracellular functions, not the exchange step this structure provides. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 3, plasma hepatocyte exchange, lines 2355–2358); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 4, sinusoidal capillarization, lines 2394–2396).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cirrhosis features bridging fibrosis from activated stellate cells and regenerative nodules (First Aid 2025 GI cirrhosis). COMLEX: activated stellate cells contract sinusoids and contribute to portal hypertension (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 1 — fibrosis in chronic liver disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Conjugation, glycogen synthesi","E":"Conjugation, glycogen synthesi"}},{"id":"sdl12_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the major functions of Kupffer cells and hepatic stellate (Ito) cells, including their roles in phagocytosis, iron recycling, vitamin A storage, and collagen deposition during hepatic fibrosis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man has a gut barrier defect that lets bacteria and endotoxin into his portal blood. Which liver cells are the first line of defense against them?","choices":{"A":"Stellate cells","B":"Cholangiocytes","C":"Endothelial cells","D":"Pit cells","E":"Kupffer cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Kupffer cells are resident sinusoidal macrophages that act as the first line of defense against gut bacteria, endotoxin, and particles in portal blood, recognizing PAMPs with pattern recognition receptors. Stellate cells store vitamin A and make collagen. Cholangiocytes line ducts. Endothelial cells line sinusoids. Pit cells are not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 4, phagocytosis and innate defense, lines 2371–2375).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Kupffer cells make up 80 to 90 percent of the tissue macrophages in the body (lecture SDL 12). COMLEX: Kupffer cells recognize endotoxin with pattern recognition receptors (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 3 — bacterial and parasitic liver infections","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the major functions of Kupffer cells and hepatic stellate (Ito) cells, including their roles in phagocytosis, iron recycling, vitamin A storage, and collagen deposition during hepatic fibrosis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old man with chronic hepatitis has a biopsy in which perisinusoidal cells have lost their lipid droplets, express smooth muscle actin, and lay down type I and type III collagen. Which cell type is this?","choices":{"A":"Kupffer cells","B":"Cholangiocytes","C":"Stellate cells","D":"Endothelial cells","E":"Pit cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Activated hepatic stellate cells lose their vitamin A lipid droplets, become myofibroblasts with alpha smooth muscle actin, and deposit type I and type III collagen in the space of Disse, causing fibrosis. Kupffer cells release the activating cytokines but do not make this matrix. Cholangiocytes, endothelial cells, and pit cells are not the fibrogenic cell. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 4, activation and collagen deposition, lines 2389–2398).","boardPrep":"Step 1: stellate cells sit in the space of Disse between hepatocytes and endothelial cells (lecture SDL 12). COMLEX: chronic liver injury, reactive oxygen species, and cytokines activate stellate cells (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 1 — alcohol associated and steatotic liver disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the major functions of Kupffer cells and hepatic stellate (Ito) cells, including their roles in phagocytosis, iron recycling, vitamin A storage, and collagen deposition during hepatic fibrosis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman has chronic hemolysis, and her Kupffer cells take up many senescent red cells. After hemoglobin breakdown, how is the released iron sent back to the blood?","choices":{"A":"Through ferroportin","B":"Through DMT1 channels","C":"Through hepcidin pores","D":"Through hemopexin","E":"Through haptoglobin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Kupffer cells break down hemoglobin and store iron in ferritin or export it through ferroportin to bind transferrin for erythropoiesis. DMT1 is an apical iron importer, hepcidin regulates ferroportin rather than forming pores, and hemopexin and haptoglobin bind heme and hemoglobin in plasma. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 4, iron recycling, lines 2376–2380).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Kupffer cells store iron safely in ferritin (lecture SDL 12). COMLEX: exported iron binds transferrin for systemic erythropoiesis (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 2 — hemochromatosis and iron overload","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"DMT1 is an apical iron importe","C":"DMT1 is an apical iron importe","D":"DMT1 is an apical iron importe","E":"DMT1 is an apical iron importe"}},{"id":"sdl12_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Trace bile flow from hepatocytes through bile canaliculi and the intrahepatic bile ducts, and contrast the direction of bile flow with hepatic blood flow.","batch":2,"stem":"A 39-year-old woman has a liver biopsy, and the histology instructor traces bile flow within a classic lobule. In which direction does bile move?","choices":{"A":"Toward central veins","B":"Into sinusoids","C":"Toward portal triads","D":"Into space of Disse","E":"Into sublobular veins"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Bile flows centrifugally, starting near the central vein and moving outward to the portal triads, opposite to the centripetal flow of blood toward the central vein. Bile does not enter sinusoids, the space of Disse, or sublobular veins. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 5, directional contrast, lines 2421–2425).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bile and blood flow in opposite directions in the lobule (lecture SDL 12). COMLEX: interlobular ducts converge into larger intrahepatic ducts (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — formation and composition of bile","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Trace bile flow from hepatocytes through bile canaliculi and the intrahepatic bile ducts, and contrast the direction of bile flow with hepatic blood flow.","batch":2,"stem":"A 56-year-old woman has an autoimmune disease that destroys the small interlobular bile ducts. Where are these ducts located in the liver?","choices":{"A":"Around central veins","B":"Within portal triads","C":"In space of Disse","D":"Between hepatocyte plates","E":"In sublobular septa"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Bile ductules join interlobular bile ducts within the portal triads of the portal canals. The central veins carry blood, the space of Disse holds plasma, canaliculi rather than ducts run between hepatocytes, and sublobular septa are not described. Central veins drain sinusoidal blood at the lobule center and do not carry interlobular bile ducts. The space of Disse lies between hepatocytes and sinusoidal endothelium and contains no bile ducts. Between hepatocyte plates run bile canaliculi, not interlobular bile ducts. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 5, interlobular bile ducts, lines 2415–2416).","boardPrep":"Step 1: interlobular bile ducts run in the portal canals with the portal vein and hepatic artery branches (lecture SDL 12). COMLEX: intrahepatic bile ductules are lined completely by cholangiocytes (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 5 — primary biliary cholangitis and sclerosing cholangitis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Central veins drain sinusoidal blood at the lobule center an","C":"The space of Disse lies between hepatocytes and sinusoidal e","D":"Between hepatocyte plates run bile canaliculi, not interlobu"}},{"id":"sdl12_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Trace bile flow from hepatocytes through bile canaliculi and the intrahepatic bile ducts, and contrast the direction of bile flow with hepatic blood flow.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old man has a cholestatic drug injury in which bile leaks from canaliculi into the blood. Which structures normally seal bile canaliculi from the vascular space?","choices":{"A":"Tight junctions","B":"Desmosomes","C":"Gap junctions","D":"Hemidesmosomes","E":"Adherens junctions"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Bile canaliculi are formed by apposed grooves between hepatocytes and are sealed from the vascular space by tight junctions. Desmosomes and adherens junctions anchor cells, gap junctions allow small molecule passage, and hemidesmosomes anchor cells to the basal lamina. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 5, bile synthesis and canaliculi, lines 2403–2407).","boardPrep":"Step 1: canals of Hering have contractile activity that propels bile (lecture SDL 12). COMLEX: canaliculi are formed by apposed grooves between adjacent hepatocytes (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 5 — drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl12_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the organization of the classic hepatic lobule and trace hepatic blood flow, including the portal triad, hepatic sinusoids, hepatocyte plates, and central vein.","batch":3,"stem":"A 51-year-old woman has hepatic venous outflow obstruction. Normally, into which vessel do the hepatic veins empty?","choices":{"A":"Inferior vena cava","B":"Superior vena cava","C":"Hemiazygos vein","D":"Left renal vein","E":"Superior mesenteric vein"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Blood drains from the sinusoids into the central vein, then sublobular veins, then hepatic veins, and finally the inferior vena cava. The superior vena cava, hemiazygos vein, and left renal vein do not receive hepatic venous blood, and the superior mesenteric vein feeds the portal vein. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 1, centripetal flow and venous drainage, lines 2306–2308).","boardPrep":"Step 1: portal vein blood passes through sinusoids to the central vein and on to the hepatic vein (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, flow of blood and bile). COMLEX: hepatic venous outflow obstruction injures zone 3 first (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic zones).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 1 — hepatic venous outflow obstruction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl12_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare zones 1, 2, and 3 of the liver acinus based on blood supply and oxygenation, and predict their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury, including centrilobular (zone 3) injury.","batch":3,"stem":"A 33-year-old man returns from South America with fever, jaundice, and bleeding, and is diagnosed with yellow fever. Which acinar zone is classically injured?","choices":{"A":"Periportal zone 1","B":"Pericentral zone 3","C":"Panlobular injury","D":"Midzonal zone 2","E":"Zone 1 and 3 together"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Yellow fever classically causes midzonal, zone 2 injury. Zone 1 is injured by viral hepatitis and ingested toxins such as cocaine. Zone 3 is injured by ischemia and bioactivated toxins. Yellow fever injury is midzonal rather than panlobular or split between zones 1 and 3. Sources: Bootcamp Gastroenterology (hepatic zones, lines 1527–1528); First Aid 2025 GI (liver tissue architecture, fa_gastro_cache lines 594–595). The lecture describes zone 2 as intermediate in oxygen and substrates (SDL 12, section 2, lines 2321–2322).","boardPrep":"Step 1: zone 2 has intermediate oxygen tension and metabolic activity (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic zones). COMLEX: zone 3 has the highest concentration of cytochrome P450 (First Aid 2025 GI liver tissue architecture).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 1 — hepatic injury, scarring, repair, and liver failure","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Zone 1 is injured by viral hep","B":"Zone 3 is injured by ischemia ","E":"Yellow fever injury is midzona"}},{"id":"trial_sdl12_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the structure of hepatic sinusoids and the space of Disse and explain how the discontinuous sinusoidal endothelium facilitates exchange between plasma and hepatocytes.","batch":3,"stem":"A 47-year-old woman has a liver biopsy examined by electron microscopy. The sinusoidal endothelium shows large pores without diaphragms and wide gaps between cells. Which capillary type is this?","choices":{"A":"Continuous type","B":"Diaphragmed fenestrated","C":"Blood brain barrier type","D":"Glomerular type","E":"Discontinuous type"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Hepatic sinusoids have discontinuous endothelium with large fenestrae up to 1 µm that lack diaphragms, wide intercellular gaps, and no continuous basal lamina. Continuous and blood brain barrier capillaries have tight junctions and no pores. Diaphragmed fenestrated capillaries line gut mucosa and endocrine glands. Glomerular capillaries have fenestrae on a thick basement membrane. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 3, discontinuous sinusoidal endothelium, lines 2345–2349).","boardPrep":"Step 1: excess Disse fluid drains to the periportal spaces and forms hepatic lymph (lecture SDL 12). COMLEX: discontinuous sinusoids allow plasma proteins to reach hepatocytes without obstruction (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — epithelial types and mucosal specializations","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Diaphragmed fenestrated capill"}},{"id":"trial_sdl12_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the major functions of Kupffer cells and hepatic stellate (Ito) cells, including their roles in phagocytosis, iron recycling, vitamin A storage, and collagen deposition during hepatic fibrosis.","batch":3,"stem":"A 9-year-old boy has a low dietary intake of vitamin A for months. When his blood level falls, the liver releases stored vitamin A into the blood bound to which carrier?","choices":{"A":"Ceruloplasmin","B":"Thyroxine binding globulin","C":"Retinol binding protein","D":"Vitamin D binding protein","E":"Cortisol binding globulin"},"correct":"C","explanation":"When systemic vitamin A drops, stellate cells release retinol bound to retinol binding protein into the blood. Ceruloplasmin carries copper, and the binding globulins and vitamin D binding protein carry thyroxine, cortisol, and vitamin D. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 4, vitamin A storage, lines 2384–2388).","boardPrep":"Step 1: stellate cells hold vitamin A in cytoplasmic lipid droplets (lecture SDL 12). COMLEX: stellate cells lose their lipid droplets when they become myofibroblasts (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — bile salt micelles needed to absorb fat-soluble vitamins such as A and D","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Ceruloplasmin carries copper, "}},{"id":"trial_sdl12_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Trace bile flow from hepatocytes through bile canaliculi and the intrahepatic bile ducts, and contrast the direction of bile flow with hepatic blood flow.","batch":3,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman studies liver histology with her tutor, who asks how the walls of a bile canaliculus are formed. What forms them?","choices":{"A":"Apposed hepatocyte grooves","B":"Cholangiocyte lined tubes","C":"Endothelial lined channels","D":"Kupffer cell processes","E":"Stellate cell processes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Bile canaliculi are intercellular channels formed by apposed grooves in the membranes of adjacent hepatocytes and sealed by tight junctions. Cholangiocytes line ductules and ducts, endothelium lines sinusoids, and Kupffer and stellate cells do not form canaliculi. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 5, bile synthesis and canaliculi, lines 2403–2407).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bile canaliculi empty into canals of Hering at the lobule margin (lecture SDL 12). COMLEX: the right and left hepatic ducts form the common hepatic duct (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — formation and composition of bile","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl12_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the organization of the classic hepatic lobule and trace hepatic blood flow, including the portal triad, hepatic sinusoids, hepatocyte plates, and central vein.","batch":3,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman has a needle liver biopsy, and the pathologist measures one classic hexagonal lobule. About what size is a classic lobule across?","choices":{"A":"10 to 20 µm","B":"1 to 2 mm","C":"50 to 100 µm","D":"1 to 2 cm","E":"5 to 10 cm"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The classic hepatic lobule is a roughly hexagonal prism about 1 to 2 mm across, centered on a central vein with portal tracts at its six angles. The micrometer sizes are cell to tissue scale, far below a lobule, since a hepatocyte is only about 20 to 30 µm, and the centimeter sizes match whole lobes or masses. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 1, classic hepatic lobule structure, lines 2287–2290).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the classic lobule centers on a terminal hepatic venule (lecture SDL 12). COMLEX: First Aid describes the lobule as a central vein with portal triads on its edges (First Aid 2025 GI liver tissue architecture).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 1 — liver development","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"The micrometer sizes are cell ","D":"The micrometer sizes are cell ","E":"The micrometer sizes are cell "}}]},{"sdlNumber":13,"title":"SDL 13 — Histology of the Gallbladder, and pancreas","questions":[{"id":"sdl13_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the key histologic features of the gallbladder wall, including its simple columnar epithelium, mucosal folds, muscularis externa, absence of a muscularis mucosae and submucosa, and Rokitansky- Aschoff sinuses.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what type of epithelium lines the gallbladder mucosa?","choices":{"A":"Simple cuboidal epithelium","B":"Stratified squamous epithelium","C":"Pseudostratified ciliated epithelium","D":"Simple columnar epithelium","E":"Transitional epithelium"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The gallbladder is lined by a single layer of tall columnar absorptive cells with short apical microvilli, apical tight junctions, and abundant mitochondria. Cuboidal, stratified squamous, pseudostratified ciliated, and transitional linings are not described. Nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium lines the esophagus and ends at the esophagogastric junction, not the gallbladder. Pseudostratified ciliated epithelium lines the trachea and bronchi, not the gallbladder. Transitional epithelium (urothelium) lines the urinary passages from the calyces to the bladder, not the gallbladder. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 1, simple columnar epithelium, lines 2428–2431).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gallbladder epithelial cells have apical tight junctions that seal the lumen (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: the gallbladder has a fundus, body, infundibulum, and neck (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, biliary anatomy).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 3 — acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium lines the esop","C":"Pseudostratified ciliated epithelium lines the trachea and b","E":"Transitional epithelium (urothelium) lines the urinary passa"}},{"id":"sdl13_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the key histologic features of the gallbladder wall, including its simple columnar epithelium, mucosal folds, muscularis externa, absence of a muscularis mucosae and submucosa, and Rokitansky- Aschoff sinuses.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which two layers are missing from the gallbladder wall?","choices":{"A":"Muscularis externa and serosa","B":"Muscularis mucosae and submucosa","C":"Lamina propria and adventitia","D":"Epithelium and lamina propria","E":"Adventitia and muscularis externa"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The gallbladder lacks both a muscularis mucosae and a submucosa, so the lamina propria rests directly on the muscularis externa. It has an epithelium, a lamina propria, a muscularis externa, and an adventitia on the liver side with serosa on the free surface. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 1, absence of muscularis mucosae and submucosa, lines 2438–2440).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the lamina propria of the gallbladder rests directly upon the muscularis externa (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: the free surface of the gallbladder is covered by visceral peritoneum (lecture SDL 13).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 3 — acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the key histologic features of the gallbladder wall, including its simple columnar epithelium, mucosal folds, muscularis externa, absence of a muscularis mucosae and submucosa, and Rokitansky- Aschoff sinuses.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what does the gallbladder lamina propria noticeably lack?","choices":{"A":"Lymphatic vessels","B":"Fenestrated capillaries","C":"Plasma cells","D":"Resident lymphocytes","E":"Small venules"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The gallbladder lamina propria is cellular loose connective tissue rich in fenestrated capillaries, small venules, lymphocytes, and plasma cells, but it noticeably lacks lymphatic vessels. Fenestrated capillaries are present in the gallbladder lamina propria; what it lacks is lymphatic vessels. Plasma cells are present in the gallbladder lamina propria; what it lacks is lymphatic vessels. Lymphocytes are present in the gallbladder lamina propria; what it lacks is lymphatic vessels. Small venules are present in the gallbladder lamina propria; what it lacks is lymphatic vessels. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 1, lamina propria, lines 2435–2437).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the empty gallbladder mucosa forms deep temporary folds that flatten as the organ fills (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: calculous cholecystitis follows gallstone impaction in the cystic duct and thickens the gallbladder wall (First Aid 2025 GI biliary pathology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 3 — acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Fenestrated capillaries are present in the gallbladder lamin","C":"Plasma cells are present in the gallbladder lamina propria; ","D":"Lymphocytes are present in the gallbladder lamina propria; w","E":"Small venules are present in the gallbladder lamina propria;"}},{"id":"sdl13_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how gallbladder epithelial cells concentrate bile through active electrolyte transport and passive, aquaporin-mediated water absorption.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which pump on the lateral membranes of gallbladder epithelial cells drives active electrolyte transport?","choices":{"A":"Hydrogen potassium ATPase","B":"Calcium ATPase pumps","C":"Sodium glucose cotransporter","D":"Chloride bicarbonate exchanger","E":"Sodium potassium ATPase"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Lateral membrane Na+/K+ ATPase pumps drive transport of Na+, Cl- and HCO3- into the lateral intercellular space. The hydrogen potassium ATPase is the gastric proton pump. Calcium pumps, sodium glucose cotransport, and a chloride bicarbonate exchanger are not named as the driver. The Na+-glucose cotransporter SGLT1 absorbs glucose across the small intestinal apical membrane and is not the gallbladder electrolyte driver. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 2, active electrolyte transport, lines 2456–2459).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gallbladder cells move Na+, Cl- and HCO3- into the lateral intercellular compartment (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: solute build up in the narrow lateral space sets up an osmotic gradient with the lumen (lecture SDL 13).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 5 — gallbladder function and enterohepatic circulation","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"The Na+-glucose cotransporter SGLT1 absorbs glucose across t"}},{"id":"sdl13_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how gallbladder epithelial cells concentrate bile through active electrolyte transport and passive, aquaporin-mediated water absorption.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which membrane water channels carry water across gallbladder epithelial cells?","choices":{"A":"Aquaporins 1 and 8","B":"Aquaporins 2 and 3","C":"Aquaporins 4 and 5","D":"Aquaporins 0 and 7","E":"Connexin channels"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Water crosses the apical and basolateral membranes of gallbladder epithelial cells through aquaporin 1 and aquaporin 8. The other aquaporin pairs are not described for the gallbladder, and connexins form gap junctions. Aquaporins 2 and 3 are not the gallbladder pair; water crosses its epithelium through aquaporins 1 and 8. Aquaporins 4 and 5 are not the gallbladder pair; water crosses its epithelium through aquaporins 1 and 8. Aquaporins 0 and 7 are not the gallbladder pair; water crosses its epithelium through aquaporins 1 and 8. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 2, aquaporin mediated water absorption, lines 2464–2467).","boardPrep":"Step 1: water movement in the gallbladder is passive and follows the osmotic gradient (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: bile is stored within the gallbladder between meals (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, biliary physiology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 5 — gallbladder function and enterohepatic circulation","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Aquaporins 2 and 3 are not the gallbladder pair; water cross","C":"Aquaporins 4 and 5 are not the gallbladder pair; water cross","D":"Aquaporins 0 and 7 are not the gallbladder pair; water cross"}},{"id":"sdl13_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how gallbladder epithelial cells concentrate bile through active electrolyte transport and passive, aquaporin-mediated water absorption.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about what share of the water in incoming hepatic bile does the gallbladder remove?","choices":{"A":"About 50 percent","B":"About 90 percent","C":"About 25 percent","D":"About 10 percent","E":"About 70 percent"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Coupled electrolyte and water reabsorption removes about 90 percent of the water from hepatic bile. The lower figures understate the concentrating power of the gallbladder. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 2, net bile concentration, lines 2472–2474).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gallbladder concentration raises bile salts, cholesterol, and bilirubin together (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: gallbladder stasis promotes sludge and gallstone formation (First Aid 2025 GI biliary pathology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 5 — gallbladder function and enterohepatic circulation","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The lower figures understate t","C":"The lower figures understate t","D":"The lower figures understate t","E":"The lower figures understate t"}},{"id":"sdl13_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the organization of the exocrine pancreas and distinguish acinar, centroacinar, and ductal cells by their histologic features and secretory functions.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, how is the exocrine pancreas classified as a gland?","choices":{"A":"Compound tubuloacinar serous gland","B":"Simple branched tubular mucous gland","C":"Compound tubular mixed mucous gland","D":"Simple coiled tubular gland","E":"Compound alveolar seromucous gland"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The exocrine pancreas is a compound tubuloacinar serous gland divided into ill defined lobules by thin septa from its capsule. Simple branched, simple coiled, mucous, and seromucous patterns describe other glands. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 3, overall organization, lines 2478–2479).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the exocrine pancreas makes up the majority of pancreatic mass (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, exocrine pancreas). COMLEX: thin septa from the capsule divide the pancreas into ill defined lobules (lecture SDL 13).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — pancreatic digestive enzymes and autodigestion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Simple branched, simple coiled"}},{"id":"sdl13_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the organization of the exocrine pancreas and distinguish acinar, centroacinar, and ductal cells by their histologic features and secretory functions.","batch":1,"stem":"A pathologist must decide whether a section of serous gland came from the pancreas or the parotid. Which feature found within the acini identifies the pancreas?","choices":{"A":"Basophilic basal cytoplasm","B":"Apical zymogen granules","C":"Pale centroacinar cells","D":"Serous demilunes on mucous cells","E":"Pyramidal secretory cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Pale, flattened centroacinar cells at the center of the acinus are the intra-acinar start of the intercalated ducts, a feature unique to the pancreas. Basophilic basal cytoplasm, apical zymogen granules, and pyramidal secretory cells are shared by serous acini such as those of the parotid. Serous demilunes capping mucous acini belong to mixed glands such as the submandibular gland. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 3, centroacinar cells are a unique feature of the pancreas, p71); Pawlina (serous demilunes of the submandibular gland).","boardPrep":"Step 1: centroacinar cells lack zymogen granules and secrete an isotonic sodium bicarbonate fluid (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: secretin acts on duct and centroacinar cells through cAMP (lecture SDL 13).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 2 — duct and centroacinar bicarbonate secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the organization of the exocrine pancreas and distinguish acinar, centroacinar, and ductal cells by their histologic features and secretory functions.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, pale centroacinar cells mark the intra-acinar start of which ducts?","choices":{"A":"Interlobular ducts","B":"Striated ducts","C":"Main pancreatic duct","D":"Intercalated ducts","E":"Intralobular ducts"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Centroacinar cells are the intra-acinar origin of the intercalated ducts, a feature unique to the pancreas. Intralobular and interlobular ducts lie farther downstream, striated ducts belong to salivary glands, and the main duct is the final collecting channel. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 3, centroacinar cells, lines 2487–2491).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic secretions pass from acini to centroacinar cells, intercalated ducts, and pancreatic ducts (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, exocrine pancreas). COMLEX: centroacinar cells lack zymogen granules and ergastoplasm and stain faintly (lecture SDL 13).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — pancreatic digestive enzymes and autodigestion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of pancreatic exocrine secretion by secretin and CCK, relating each hormone to its target pancreatic cell type and secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which cells release secretin when acid enters the small bowel?","choices":{"A":"Duodenal I cells","B":"Duodenal S cells","C":"Jejunal K cells","D":"Gastric G cells","E":"Ileal L cells"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Secretin is released by duodenal S cells when gastric acid lowers duodenal pH below about 4.5. I cells release CCK, K cells release GIP, G cells release gastrin, and L cells release GLP 1 and PYY. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 4, secretin source and trigger, lines 2507–2508).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secretin increases pancreatic bicarbonate secretion and bile secretion (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, GI hormones). COMLEX: secretin also decreases parietal cell H+ secretion and gastric motility (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, GI hormones).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — GI hormones","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"I cells release CCK, K cells r","C":"I cells release CCK, K cells r","E":"I cells release CCK, K cells r"}},{"id":"sdl13_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of pancreatic exocrine secretion by secretin and CCK, relating each hormone to its target pancreatic cell type and secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, CCK1 receptors on the pancreatic target cells of CCK activate which signaling pathway?","choices":{"A":"Cyclic AMP","B":"Cyclic GMP","C":"IP3 and calcium","D":"JAK STAT signaling","E":"Tyrosine kinase"},"correct":"C","explanation":"CCK binds CCK1 receptors on acinar cells and activates the IP3 and intracellular Ca2+ pathway, causing zymogen granule exocytosis. Cyclic AMP is the secretin pathway in ductal cells. Cyclic GMP, JAK STAT, and tyrosine kinase signaling are not described. Cyclic GMP is generated by guanylyl cyclase, the mechanism of atrial natriuretic peptide and nitric oxide, not of the CCK1 receptor. JAK-STAT signaling follows hormone binding to tyrosine kinase-associated receptors such as the growth hormone receptor, not the CCK1 receptor. Receptor tyrosine kinase signaling is the mechanism of insulin and insulin-like growth factors, not of CCK at CCK1 receptors. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 4, CCK target cell and pathway, lines 2519–2520).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CCK increases gallbladder contraction and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, GI hormones). COMLEX: CCK also slows gastric emptying (First Aid 2025 GI GI hormones).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — GI hormones","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Cyclic GMP is generated by guanylyl cyclase, the mechanism o","D":"JAK-STAT signaling follows hormone binding to tyrosine kinas","E":"Receptor tyrosine kinase signaling is the mechanism of insul"}},{"id":"sdl13_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of pancreatic exocrine secretion by secretin and CCK, relating each hormone to its target pancreatic cell type and secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which phase accounts for about 80 percent of total pancreatic secretion?","choices":{"A":"Cephalic phase","B":"Gastric phase","C":"Interdigestive phase","D":"Oral phase","E":"Intestinal phase"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Secretin, CCK, and vagal ACh act together during the intestinal phase, which accounts for about 80 percent of total pancreatic secretion. The cephalic and gastric phases contribute less, and the interdigestive and oral phases are not the main period. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 4, coordinated action, lines 2524–2527).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secretin and CCK act synergistically with vagal ACh to deliver an alkaline enzyme rich juice (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: somatostatin inhibits the exocrine pancreas (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, exocrine pancreas).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — GI hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the endocrine and exocrine pancreas histologically and identify the major islet cell types and their hormone products","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about what share of the pancreas is endocrine tissue?","choices":{"A":"80 to 85 percent","B":"10 to 15 percent","C":"1 to 2 percent","D":"25 to 30 percent","E":"5 to 8 percent"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The endocrine pancreas makes up only 1 to 2 percent of the organ as scattered islets, while the exocrine pancreas makes up 80 to 85 percent. The other figures are not described. 80 to 85 percent is the exocrine pancreas's share; the endocrine islets make up only 1 to 2 percent. 10 to 15 percent is too high; the endocrine pancreas is only 1 to 2 percent of the organ as scattered islets. 25 to 30 percent is too high; the endocrine pancreas is only 1 to 2 percent of the organ as scattered islets. 5 to 8 percent is too high; the endocrine pancreas is only 1 to 2 percent of the organ as scattered islets. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 5, histologic differentiation, lines 2533–2536).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic endocrine function is glucose regulation by insulin, glucagon, and somatostatin (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreas physiology). COMLEX: islets are most concentrated in the tail of the pancreas (lecture SDL 13).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 5 — pancreatic neuroendocrine neoplasms","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"80 to 85 percent is the exocrine pancreas's share; the endoc","B":"10 to 15 percent is too high; the endocrine pancreas is only","D":"25 to 30 percent is too high; the endocrine pancreas is only","E":"5 to 8 percent is too high; the endocrine pancreas is only 1"}},{"id":"sdl13_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the endocrine and exocrine pancreas histologically and identify the major islet cell types and their hormone products","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, where in a human islet are beta cells mainly located?","choices":{"A":"Islet periphery","B":"Capsule surface","C":"Acinar lumen","D":"Central core","E":"Duct walls"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Beta cells, about 60 to 70 percent of islet cells, lie mainly in the central core of the islet. Alpha and delta cells lie mainly at the periphery. Capsules, acinar lumens, and duct walls are not islet locations. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 5, beta cells, lines 2540–2541).","boardPrep":"Step 1: beta cells make up about 60 to 70 percent of islet cells and produce insulin (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: islet cells form irregular cords invested by fenestrated capillaries (lecture SDL 13).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 5 — pancreatic neuroendocrine neoplasms","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the endocrine and exocrine pancreas histologically and identify the major islet cell types and their hormone products","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which electron microscopic feature marks the 325 nm granules of islet delta cells?","choices":{"A":"Crystalline polyhedral core","B":"Round eccentric core","C":"Clear peripheral halo","D":"Lamellar body","E":"Homogeneous matrix"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Delta cell granules are about 325 nm with a homogeneous matrix. Beta cell granules have a crystalline polyhedral core with a clear halo, and alpha cell granules have a dense round eccentric core. Lamellar bodies are not islet granules. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 5, delta cells, lines 2548–2551).","boardPrep":"Step 1: delta cells make up about 5 to 10 percent of islet cells and sit at the islet periphery (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: PP cells make pancreatic polypeptide, which inhibits intestinal motility and pancreatic secretion (lecture SDL 13).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 5 — pancreatic neuroendocrine neoplasms","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the key histologic features of the gallbladder wall, including its simple columnar epithelium, mucosal folds, muscularis externa, absence of a muscularis mucosae and submucosa, and Rokitansky- Aschoff sinuses.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with gallstones and chronic cholecystitis has a cholecystectomy. Histology shows deep mucosal outpouchings that extend through the lamina propria into the muscularis externa. What are these structures?","choices":{"A":"Intestinal crypts of Lieberkuhn","B":"Rokitansky Aschoff sinuses","C":"Submucosal Brunner glands","D":"Periportal canals of Hering","E":"Pancreatic acinar rests"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Rokitansky Aschoff sinuses are deep mucosal invaginations that extend into or through the muscularis externa. They arise from epithelial hyperplasia and herniation under luminal pressure, and trapped bile and bacteria promote chronic inflammation and stones. Crypts of Lieberkuhn and Brunner glands belong to the intestine, canals of Hering belong to the liver, and acinar rests are not described. Brunner glands are submucosal glands of the duodenum, not gallbladder structures. Canals of Hering lie at the periphery of hepatic lobules, not in the gallbladder wall. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 1, Rokitansky Aschoff sinuses, lines 2448–2452).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bile stagnation in mucosal outpouchings promotes chronic gallbladder inflammation and gallstones (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: a porcelain gallbladder is a calcified wall from chronic cholecystitis (First Aid 2025 GI biliary pathology).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 4 — chronic cholecystitis and porcelain gallbladder","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Brunner glands are submucosal glands of the duodenum, not ga","D":"Canals of Hering lie at the periphery of hepatic lobules, no"}},{"id":"sdl13_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the key histologic features of the gallbladder wall, including its simple columnar epithelium, mucosal folds, muscularis externa, absence of a muscularis mucosae and submucosa, and Rokitansky- Aschoff sinuses.","batch":2,"stem":"A 42-year-old woman has a cholecystectomy, and the surgeon dissects the gallbladder off the liver bed. Which layer covers the surface that was attached to the liver?","choices":{"A":"Visceral serosa","B":"Submucosal layer","C":"Muscularis mucosae","D":"Fibrous adventitia","E":"Parietal peritoneum"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The attached surface is covered by a connective tissue adventitia carrying large vessels, lymphatics, and nerves, while the free surface has a smooth serosa of visceral peritoneum. The gallbladder lacks a submucosa and a muscularis mucosae, and parietal peritoneum lines the body wall. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 1, adventitia versus serosa, lines 2444–2447).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the adventitia on the liver side carries large blood vessels, lymphatics, and nerves (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: acalculous cholecystitis is due to stasis, hypoperfusion, or infection in critically ill patients (First Aid 2025 GI biliary pathology).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 3 — acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the key histologic features of the gallbladder wall, including its simple columnar epithelium, mucosal folds, muscularis externa, absence of a muscularis mucosae and submucosa, and Rokitansky- Aschoff sinuses.","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old man eats a fatty meal, and his gallbladder squeezes bile into the cystic duct. Which wall component produces this contraction?","choices":{"A":"Interlacing smooth muscle","B":"Circular skeletal muscle","C":"Muscularis mucosae","D":"Myoepithelial cells","E":"Longitudinal skeletal muscle"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The muscularis externa is made of randomly oriented interlacing smooth muscle bundles with elastic and collagen fibers, and its contraction reduces luminal volume to expel bile. The gallbladder has no skeletal muscle or muscularis mucosae, and myoepithelial cells belong to glands such as salivary glands. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 1, muscularis externa, lines 2441–2443).","boardPrep":"Step 1: after a fatty meal, CCK triggers gallbladder contraction that can force a stone into the cystic duct (First Aid 2025 GI biliary pathology). COMLEX: biliary colic worsens after meals because CCK drives gallbladder contraction (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, biliary colic).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 5 — gallbladder function and enterohepatic circulation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how gallbladder epithelial cells concentrate bile through active electrolyte transport and passive, aquaporin-mediated water absorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman with gallstones asks how her gallbladder changes the bile it stores. By about how much are bile salts concentrated during storage?","choices":{"A":"2 to 3 times","B":"100 to 200 times","C":"4 to 5 times","D":"50 to 60 times","E":"10 to 20 times"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Removal of about 90 percent of the water concentrates bile salts, cholesterol, and bilirubin up to 10 to 20 times. The smaller figures understate, and the larger figures overstate, the effect. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 2, net bile concentration, lines 2472–2474).","boardPrep":"Step 1: most gallstones form within the gallbladder (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis). COMLEX: raised cholesterol, reduced bile salts, and gallbladder stasis all cause sludge or stones (First Aid 2025 GI biliary pathology).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 2 — formation and composition of gallstones","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The smaller figures understate","B":"The smaller figures understate","C":"The smaller figures understate","D":"The smaller figures understate"}},{"id":"sdl13_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how gallbladder epithelial cells concentrate bile through active electrolyte transport and passive, aquaporin-mediated water absorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 39-year-old man has a cholecystectomy, and the pathologist explains how the gallbladder absorbs fluid. After isotonic fluid crosses the basal lamina into the lamina propria, where is it taken up?","choices":{"A":"Lymphatic capillaries","B":"Fenestrated capillaries","C":"Lumen of the cystic duct","D":"Bile in the gallbladder lumen","E":"Muscularis externa"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hydrostatic pressure in the lateral spaces pushes isotonic fluid across the basal lamina, where fenestrated capillaries rapidly absorb it. The lamina propria noticeably lacks lymphatics, fluid does not return to the lumen or cystic duct, and the muscularis does not absorb it. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 2, hydrostatic fluid movement, lines 2468–2471).","boardPrep":"Step 1: water build up distends the lateral intercellular space and raises local hydrostatic pressure (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: the lamina propria of the gallbladder has fenestrated capillaries and small venules (lecture SDL 13).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 5 — gallbladder function and enterohepatic circulation","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"The lamina propria noticeably "}},{"id":"sdl13_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how gallbladder epithelial cells concentrate bile through active electrolyte transport and passive, aquaporin-mediated water absorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old woman has a cholecystectomy, and her surgeon explains how a living gallbladder concentrates bile. Water moves from the lumen through membrane water channels into the lateral intercellular space. What drives this water movement?","choices":{"A":"Active water pumping","B":"Bile salt uptake","C":"Osmotic gradient","D":"Vesicular transport","E":"Ciliary beating"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Actively pumped electrolytes collect in the lateral space and create an osmotic gradient, and water follows passively through aquaporins. Water is not pumped actively, bile salts are concentrated rather than absorbed, and vesicles and cilia are not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 2, osmotic gradient and water absorption, lines 2460–2467).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gallbladder epithelial cells have abundant apical and basal mitochondria for active transport (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: the striated border of gallbladder cells is formed by short apical microvilli (lecture SDL 13).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 5 — gallbladder function and enterohepatic circulation","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"Water is not pumped actively, ","E":"Water is not pumped actively, "}},{"id":"sdl13_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the organization of the exocrine pancreas and distinguish acinar, centroacinar, and ductal cells by their histologic features and secretory functions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 6-year-old boy has recurrent pneumonia, poor weight gain, greasy stools, and a raised sweat chloride. Which apical protein of his pancreatic duct cells is most likely defective?","choices":{"A":"ENaC sodium channel","B":"Aquaporin water channel","C":"Voltage gated K channel","D":"Chloride bicarbonate exchanger","E":"CFTR chloride channel"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Ductal cells secrete Na+ and HCO3- rich fluid through CFTR Cl- channels working with apical Cl-/HCO3- exchangers. A defective CFTR reduces this watery output. The Cl-/HCO3- exchanger is the partner of CFTR but is not the defective protein in cystic fibrosis. ENaC, aquaporins, and potassium channels are not the described apical secretory step. ENaC absorbs luminal sodium and is normally inhibited by CFTR, so in cystic fibrosis its activity rises rather than being the defective protein. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 3, ductal cell secretory function, lines 2500–2503).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ductal secretion brings duodenal pH to about 8.0 for pancreatic enzyme activity (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: pancreatic fluid is isotonic with water, bicarbonate, and other electrolytes (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, exocrine pancreas).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — acute and chronic pancreatitis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"ENaC, aquaporins, and potassiu","D":"The Cl-/HCO3- exchanger is the","A":"ENaC absorbs luminal sodium and is normally inhibited by CFT"}},{"id":"sdl13_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the organization of the exocrine pancreas and distinguish acinar, centroacinar, and ductal cells by their histologic features and secretory functions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man has a pancreatic biopsy. In H&E sections, pale flattened cells sit in the center of each acinus and lack zymogen granules. What do these cells mainly secrete?","choices":{"A":"Sodium bicarbonate fluid","B":"Trypsinogen and proelastase","C":"Insulin and pancreatic polypeptide","D":"Mucin and lysozyme","E":"Pancreatic amylase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Centroacinar cells secrete the initial isotonic aqueous fluid rich in sodium and bicarbonate. Acinar cells secrete proenzymes and amylase, islet cells secrete insulin and pancreatic polypeptide, and mucin and lysozyme are not described. Insulin and pancreatic polypeptide come from islet beta and PP cells, not duct-type cells. Lysozyme is an antimicrobial product of Paneth cells, not pancreatic duct cells. Pancreatic amylase is stored in zymogen granules of acinar cells, not secreted by pale duct-type cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 3, centroacinar cells, lines 2487–2493).","boardPrep":"Step 1: intercalated ducts secrete bicarbonate (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, exocrine pancreas). COMLEX: at high postprandial flow, the bicarbonate to chloride ratio of pancreatic fluid rises (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, exocrine pancreas).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — pancreatic bicarbonate secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Acinar cells secrete proenzyme","C":"Insulin and pancreatic polypeptide come from islet beta and ","D":"Lysozyme is an antimicrobial product of Paneth cells, not pa","E":"Pancreatic amylase is stored in zymogen granules of acinar c"}},{"id":"sdl13_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the organization of the exocrine pancreas and distinguish acinar, centroacinar, and ductal cells by their histologic features and secretory functions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman has acute pancreatitis with raised serum enzymes. Which pancreatic enzymes do acinar cells secrete in active form rather than as proenzymes?","choices":{"A":"Trypsin and elastase","B":"Chymotrypsin and trypsin","C":"Carboxypeptidase and elastase","D":"Amylase and lipase","E":"Enteropeptidase"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Acinar cells secrete proteases as proenzymes such as trypsinogen, chymotrypsinogen, procarboxypeptidase, and proelastase, but release pancreatic amylase and lipases as active enzymes. Enteropeptidase is a brush border enzyme rather than an acinar product. Trypsin and elastase are secreted as the proenzymes trypsinogen and proelastase, not in active form. Carboxypeptidase and elastase are secreted as the proenzymes procarboxypeptidase and proelastase, not in active form. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 3, acinar cell secretory function, lines 2484–2486).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic fluid contains zymogens such as trypsinogen and chymotrypsinogen (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, exocrine pancreas). COMLEX: pancreatic proteases are secreted as proenzymes also called zymogens (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatic secretions).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — pancreatic digestive enzymes and autodigestion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Trypsin and elastase are secreted as the proenzymes trypsino","C":"Carboxypeptidase and elastase are secreted as the proenzymes"}},{"id":"sdl13_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of pancreatic exocrine secretion by secretin and CCK, relating each hormone to its target pancreatic cell type and secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old man eats a meal, and acidic chyme drops his duodenal pH below 4.5. Which pancreatic secretion does the resulting hormone mainly stimulate?","choices":{"A":"Low volume enzyme rich juice","B":"Mucus rich interlobular secretion","C":"Insulin rich islet output","D":"Chloride rich low flow fluid","E":"High volume bicarbonate fluid"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Acid releases secretin from S cells, which acts on ductal and centroacinar cells to produce a copious watery fluid rich in sodium bicarbonate with little enzyme. Low volume enzyme rich juice is the CCK response, and chloride rich fluid is the low flow basal pattern. Interlobular goblet cells add little mucus, and islet insulin is not an exocrine output of secretin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 4, secretin, lines 2506–2514).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secretin is released by acid and fatty acids in the duodenal lumen (First Aid 2025 GI GI hormones). COMLEX: S cells that release secretin are found in the duodenum (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, enteroendocrine cells).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — GI hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of pancreatic exocrine secretion by secretin and CCK, relating each hormone to its target pancreatic cell type and secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 53-year-old woman eats a meal rich in fat and protein, and fatty acids and phenylalanine stimulate duodenal I cells. Which pancreatic cells are the main target of the released hormone?","choices":{"A":"Ductal cells","B":"Centroacinar cells","C":"Acinar cells","D":"Beta cells","E":"Stellate cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"I cells release CCK, which binds CCK1 receptors on acinar cells and triggers zymogen granule exocytosis for a low volume enzyme rich secretion. Ductal and centroacinar cells answer mainly to secretin, beta cells are endocrine, and stellate cells are not the target. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 4, cholecystokinin, lines 2515–2523).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CCK stimulates zymogen release from acinar cells (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, exocrine pancreas). COMLEX: First Aid places CCK producing I cells in the duodenum and jejunum (First Aid 2025 GI GI hormones).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — GI hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of pancreatic exocrine secretion by secretin and CCK, relating each hormone to its target pancreatic cell type and secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with suspected gastrinoma has a secretin stimulation test. In the pancreas, secretin binds ductal cell receptors that raise which intracellular messenger?","choices":{"A":"IP3 and calcium","B":"Cyclic GMP","C":"Nitric oxide","D":"Cyclic AMP","E":"Diacylglycerol"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Secretin binds receptors on ductal and centroacinar cells and stimulates adenylyl cyclase to raise intracellular cAMP. IP3 and calcium are the CCK pathway in acinar cells. Cyclic GMP, nitric oxide, and diacylglycerol are not described. Cyclic GMP is generated by guanylyl cyclase, the mechanism of atrial natriuretic peptide and nitric oxide, not of secretin in ductal cells. Nitric oxide from endothelial cells acts through cGMP to cause vasodilation and is not the second messenger of secretin. Diacylglycerol is released with IP3 by phospholipase C and, with Ca2+, activates protein kinase C, the muscarinic ACh pathway rather than the cAMP pathway of secretin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 4, secretin target cell and pathway, lines 2509–2511).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Zollinger Ellison workup uses fasting gastrin and gastric pH, then a secretin stimulation test (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastrinoma). COMLEX: secretin stimulates bicarbonate release from ductal cells (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, exocrine pancreas).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — pancreatic bicarbonate secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Cyclic GMP is generated by guanylyl cyclase, the mechanism o","C":"Nitric oxide from endothelial cells acts through cGMP to cau","E":"Diacylglycerol is released with IP3 by phospholipase C and, "}},{"id":"sdl13_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the endocrine and exocrine pancreas histologically and identify the major islet cell types and their hormone products","batch":2,"stem":"A 12-year-old girl has new type 1 diabetes from autoimmune loss of insulin producing islet cells. On electron microscopy, which granule appearance marked the destroyed cells?","choices":{"A":"Round eccentric core","B":"Polyhedral core with halo","C":"Homogeneous granule matrix","D":"Large zymogen type granule","E":"Lamellated concentric layers"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Beta cell granules are about 300 nm with a dense crystalline polyhedral core surrounded by a clear halo. A round eccentric core marks alpha cells, a homogeneous matrix marks delta cells, large zymogen granules belong to acinar cells, and lamellated layers are not described. Large zymogen granules belong to pancreatic acinar cells, not insulin-producing beta cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 5, beta cells, lines 2540–2543).","boardPrep":"Step 1: islets are pale staining, highly vascular clusters scattered among dark serous acini (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: the pancreas holds about 1 to 3 million islets of Langerhans (lecture SDL 13).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 5 — insulinoma, the beta cell tumor, contrasted with beta cell loss in type 1 diabetes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Large zymogen granules belong to pancreatic acinar cells, no"}},{"id":"sdl13_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the endocrine and exocrine pancreas histologically and identify the major islet cell types and their hormone products","batch":2,"stem":"A 57-year-old man has a pancreatic islet tumor that secretes excess glucagon. In a normal human islet, where do the cells that make this hormone mainly sit?","choices":{"A":"Islet periphery","B":"Central islet core","C":"Duct epithelium","D":"Acinar lumen","E":"Capsular septa"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Alpha cells, about 15 to 20 percent of islet cells, sit primarily at the islet periphery in humans and make glucagon, which stimulates glycogenolysis and raises blood glucose. Beta cells occupy the central core. Ducts, acinar lumens, and septa are not islet sites. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 5, alpha cells, lines 2544–2547).","boardPrep":"Step 1: alpha cell granules are about 250 nm with a dense round eccentric core (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: glucagon raises blood glucose by stimulating glycogenolysis (lecture SDL 13).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 5 — pancreatic neuroendocrine neoplasms","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the endocrine and exocrine pancreas histologically and identify the major islet cell types and their hormone products","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman with a delta cell tumor of the pancreas has new hyperglycemia and fatty stools. Inhibition of which outputs by the excess hormone explains both findings?","choices":{"A":"Glucagon and bicarbonate output","B":"Amylin and mucin secretion","C":"Insulin and enzyme secretion","D":"Pancreatic polypeptide output","E":"Gastrin and secretin release"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Delta cells make somatostatin, which suppresses insulin and glucagon release as well as exocrine secretion. Less insulin raises glucose, and less enzyme output causes fatty stools. Less glucagon would lower rather than raise glucose, and amylin, mucin, pancreatic polypeptide, gastrin, and secretin do not explain both findings. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 5, delta cells, lines 2548–2551).","boardPrep":"Step 1: somatostatinomas arise from delta cells with autonomous somatostatin release (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, somatostatinoma). COMLEX: somatostatin decreases insulin, glucagon, CCK, and secretin release (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, somatostatin).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 5 — somatostatin actions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Less glucagon would lower rath"}},{"id":"trial_sdl13_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the key histologic features of the gallbladder wall, including its simple columnar epithelium, mucosal folds, muscularis externa, absence of a muscularis mucosae and submucosa, and Rokitansky- Aschoff sinuses.","batch":3,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman has a fasting ultrasound, and her empty gallbladder mucosa shows many deep folds. What happens to these folds as the gallbladder fills with bile?","choices":{"A":"They flatten out","B":"They deepen further","C":"They form villi","D":"They calcify","E":"They become polyps"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In the empty or partly filled state the gallbladder mucosa forms many deep temporary folds, which flatten out as the organ distends with bile. The folds do not deepen, form villi, calcify, or become polyps. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 1, mucosal folds, lines 2432–2434).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the gallbladder lies in the right upper quadrant beneath the liver (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, biliary anatomy). COMLEX: the gallbladder gets sensory innervation from the phrenic nerve (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, biliary anatomy).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 5 — gallbladder storage and concentration","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl13_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how gallbladder epithelial cells concentrate bile through active electrolyte transport and passive, aquaporin-mediated water absorption.","batch":3,"stem":"A 64-year-old man has gallbladder bile that is far more concentrated than his hepatic bile. Which solutes do his gallbladder epithelial cells actively pump into the lateral intercellular spaces?","choices":{"A":"Potassium and hydrogen ions","B":"Calcium and magnesium ions","C":"Conjugated bile salts and bilirubin","D":"Glucose and free amino acids","E":"Sodium chloride and bicarbonate"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Gallbladder epithelial cells actively transport Na+, Cl- and HCO3- across their lateral membranes into the intercellular space, and water follows. Potassium and hydrogen, calcium and magnesium, and nutrients are not the described solutes, and bile salts and bilirubin stay in the lumen and become concentrated. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 2, active electrolyte transport, lines 2456–2459).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the cystic duct joins the common hepatic duct to form the common bile duct (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, biliary anatomy). COMLEX: the gallbladder is supplied by the cystic artery (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, biliary anatomy).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 5 — gallbladder function and enterohepatic circulation","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"Potassium and hydrogen, calciu"}},{"id":"trial_sdl13_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the organization of the exocrine pancreas and distinguish acinar, centroacinar, and ductal cells by their histologic features and secretory functions.","batch":3,"stem":"A 36-year-old woman has a biopsy of a large interlobular pancreatic duct. Besides simple columnar lining cells, which occasional cells are expected in its epithelium?","choices":{"A":"Paneth and intestinal tuft cells","B":"Parietal and gastric chief cells","C":"Goblet and enteroendocrine cells","D":"Ciliated and respiratory basal cells","E":"Hepatic Kupffer and stellate cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Larger interlobular ducts are lined by simple columnar epithelium with occasional enteroendocrine and goblet cells, while intercalated ducts have low cuboidal cells continuous with centroacinar cells. Paneth, parietal, chief, ciliated, Kupffer, and stellate cells belong to other organs. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 3, ductal cells, lines 2494–2499).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the main pancreatic duct empties into the major duodenal papilla (First Aid 2025 GI pancreas embryology figure, fa_gastro_cache lines 198–204; also Bootcamp merged_with_ocr line 2961). COMLEX: the accessory pancreatic duct opens at the minor papilla (First Aid 2025 GI pancreas embryology figure, fa_gastro_cache lines 198–204; also Bootcamp merged_with_ocr line 2962).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 4 — pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl13_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of pancreatic exocrine secretion by secretin and CCK, relating each hormone to its target pancreatic cell type and secretion.","batch":3,"stem":"A 27-year-old man drinks a protein shake, and duodenal I cells release their hormone in response to specific amino acids. Besides phenylalanine and methionine, which amino acid is an especially strong trigger?","choices":{"A":"Glycine","B":"Tryptophan","C":"Glutamine","D":"Asparagine","E":"Hydroxyproline"},"correct":"B","explanation":"I cells release CCK in response to fatty acids, monoglycerides, peptides, and amino acids, especially phenylalanine, methionine, and tryptophan. Glycine, glutamine, asparagine, and hydroxyproline are not named. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 4, CCK source and trigger, lines 2516–2518).","boardPrep":"Step 1: I cells release CCK in the duodenum and jejunum (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, enteroendocrine cells). COMLEX: CCK is released in response to fatty acids and amino acids (First Aid 2025 GI hormones).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — GI hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl13_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the endocrine and exocrine pancreas histologically and identify the major islet cell types and their hormone products","batch":3,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman dies of heart failure, and at autopsy the pathologist counts pancreatic islets region by region. In which region are islets most concentrated?","choices":{"A":"Pancreatic head","B":"Pancreatic neck","C":"Uncinate process","D":"Pancreatic tail","E":"Pancreatic body"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Islets of Langerhans are scattered among the exocrine acini and are most concentrated in the tail. The head, neck, uncinate process, and body hold fewer islets. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 5, histologic differentiation, lines 2535–2538).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic veins drain into the portal circulation (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic vasculature, merged_with_ocr line 2966). COMLEX: the celiac trunk and superior mesenteric artery give rise to the pancreaticoduodenal arteries (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic vasculature, merged_with_ocr line 2965).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 5 — pancreatic neuroendocrine neoplasms","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl13_q06","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the endocrine and exocrine pancreas histologically and identify the major islet cell types and their hormone products","batch":3,"stem":"A 23-year-old man has a biopsy of the pancreas. A rare islet cell type makes up a small minority of islet cells and is also scattered in the exocrine tissue. Its hormone slows intestinal motility. Which hormone is it?","choices":{"A":"Pancreatic polypeptide","B":"Vasoactive intestinal peptide","C":"Gastric inhibitory peptide","D":"Glucagon like peptide 1","E":"Somatostatin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"PP or F cells make up 1 to 2 percent of islet cells, are also scattered in the exocrine pancreas, and make pancreatic polypeptide, which inhibits intestinal motility and pancreatic enzyme and bicarbonate secretion. VIP, GIP, and GLP 1 come from other cells, and somatostatin comes from delta cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 5, PP cells, lines 2552–2554).","boardPrep":"Step 1: L cells in the ileum release GLP 1, GLP 2, and PYY (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, enteroendocrine cells). COMLEX: K cells in the duodenum and jejunum release GIP (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, enteroendocrine cells).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 5 — pancreatic neuroendocrine neoplasms","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"VIP, GIP, and GLP 1 come from ","C":"VIP, GIP, and GLP 1 come from ","D":"VIP, GIP, and GLP 1 come from "}}]},{"sdlNumber":14,"title":"SDL 14 — Physiology - Pancreas, Biliary Secretion, and Liver","questions":[{"id":"sdl14_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the secretion, activation, and functions of pancreatic digestive enzymes and the mechanisms that prevent pancreatic autodigestion, including zymogen secretion, enteropeptidase-mediated trypsin activation, and activation of other pancreatic proteases by trypsin.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which brush border enzyme of duodenal epithelial cells first converts trypsinogen to trypsin?","choices":{"A":"Carboxypeptidase B","B":"Enteropeptidase","C":"Phospholipase A2","D":"Chymotrypsin","E":"Aminopeptidase"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Enteropeptidase, also called enterokinase, sits in the brush border glycocalyx of duodenal cells and cleaves a hexapeptide from trypsinogen to form trypsin. Carboxypeptidase B, phospholipase A2, and chymotrypsin are activated by trypsin afterward, and aminopeptidase is not the trigger. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 1, enteropeptidase mediated trypsin activation, lines 2565–2569).","boardPrep":"Step 1: enteropeptidase is a brush border enzyme on duodenal and jejunal mucosa (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatic enzymes). COMLEX: activation of pancreatic proteases begins in the duodenal lumen rather than in the gland (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 2 — acute and chronic pancreatitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the secretion, activation, and functions of pancreatic digestive enzymes and the mechanisms that prevent pancreatic autodigestion, including zymogen secretion, enteropeptidase-mediated trypsin activation, and activation of other pancreatic proteases by trypsin.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which pancreatic enzyme group is secreted already active rather than as a zymogen?","choices":{"A":"Phospholipase A2","B":"Carboxypeptidase A","C":"Elastase","D":"Chymotrypsin","E":"Nucleases"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Pancreatic proteases are secreted only as inactive proenzymes, while alpha amylase, lipases, cholesterol ester hydrolase, and nucleases are secreted active. Phospholipase A2, carboxypeptidase A, elastase, and chymotrypsin act only after their zymogens are cleaved by trypsin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 1, synthesis and secretion of pancreatic enzymes, lines 2558–2564).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic alpha amylase is secreted in active form (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatic enzymes). COMLEX: pancreatic enzymes are made on rough ER, pass through the Golgi, and are stored in apical zymogen granules (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 2 — acute and chronic pancreatitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the secretion, activation, and functions of pancreatic digestive enzymes and the mechanisms that prevent pancreatic autodigestion, including zymogen secretion, enteropeptidase-mediated trypsin activation, and activation of other pancreatic proteases by trypsin.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which trypsin inhibitor do acinar and ductal cells package directly into zymogen granules?","choices":{"A":"SPINK1","B":"PRSS1","C":"CTRC","D":"SERPINA1","E":"MRP2"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Acinar and ductal cells co-package serine protease inhibitor Kazal type 1, SPINK1, into zymogen granules, where it inactivates up to 20 percent of prematurely formed trypsin. PRSS1 is listed with SPINK1 as a pancreatitis gene but is not the co-packaged inhibitor, SERPINA1 is not described here, CTRC aids trypsin self degradation, and MRP2 moves conjugated bilirubin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 1, trypsin inhibitors, lines 2600–2603).","boardPrep":"Step 1: PRSS1 and SPINK1 mutations are causes of chronic pancreatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic pancreatitis). COMLEX: chronic pancreatitis is linked to alcohol use, tobacco use, cystic fibrosis, and hypertriglyceridemia (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 2 — acute and chronic pancreatitis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"PRSS1 is listed with SPINK1 as","C":"PRSS1 is listed with SPINK1 as","D":"PRSS1 is listed with SPINK1 as","E":"PRSS1 is listed with SPINK1 as"}},{"id":"sdl14_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the mechanism and regulation of pancreatic bicarbonate-rich secretion, including the roles of secretin, carbonic anhydrase, CFTR, and Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ exchange.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, up to about what bicarbonate concentration can stimulated pancreatic juice reach?","choices":{"A":"About 24 mEq/L","B":"About 70 mEq/L","C":"About 145 mEq/L","D":"About 300 mEq/L","E":"About 10 mEq/L"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Centroacinar and intercalated duct cells make an isotonic juice with bicarbonate up to about 140 to 145 mEq/L when stimulated. Plasma bicarbonate is about 24 mEq/L, and the other values are not described. About 70 mEq/L is too low; stimulated pancreatic juice bicarbonate can reach about 140 to 145 mEq/L. About 300 mEq/L is too high; stimulated pancreatic juice is isotonic, with bicarbonate up to about 140 to 145 mEq/L. About 10 mEq/L is too low; stimulated pancreatic juice bicarbonate reaches up to about 145 mEq/L. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 2, cellular mechanism of ductal HCO3 secretion and flow rate, lines 2612–2614, 2640–2643).","boardPrep":"Step 1: stimulated pancreatic juice is isotonic and alkaline (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: at high flow, pancreatic juice has a reciprocal fall in chloride as bicarbonate rises (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 4 — secretin acting on ductal cells to drive bicarbonate-rich pancreatic secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"About 70 mEq/L is too low; stimulated pancreatic juice bicar","D":"About 300 mEq/L is too high; stimulated pancreatic juice is ","E":"About 10 mEq/L is too low; stimulated pancreatic juice bicar"}},{"id":"sdl14_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the mechanism and regulation of pancreatic bicarbonate-rich secretion, including the roles of secretin, carbonic anhydrase, CFTR, and Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ exchange.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, pancreatic duct cells move HCO3- across their apical membrane into the duct lumen. By which route do Na+ and water follow it to make an isotonic juice?","choices":{"A":"Through apical CFTR chloride channels","B":"Between cells through tight junctions","C":"Through apical sodium-hydrogen exchangers","D":"By apical sodium-potassium pumping","E":"By transcytotic vesicle transport"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Na+ and water follow HCO3- paracellularly across the tight junctions, down electrical and osmotic gradients, which makes pancreatic juice isotonic and alkaline. Apical CFTR channels carry Cl- back to the lumen to keep the Cl-/HCO3- exchanger running. The Na+/H+ exchanger and the Na+/K+-ATPase sit on the basolateral membrane, where they extrude H+ and acidify pancreatic venous blood. Transcytotic vesicle transport is not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 2, basolateral H+ extrusion and paracellular fluid secretion, p75).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Na+ and K+ concentrations in pancreatic juice stay equal to plasma at all flow rates (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: at high flow, HCO3- rises toward 145 mEq/L while Cl- falls (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 3 — centroacinar and duct cells of the exocrine pancreas","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"The Na+/H+ exchanger and the N","D":"The Na+/H+ exchanger and the N"}},{"id":"sdl14_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the mechanism and regulation of pancreatic bicarbonate-rich secretion, including the roles of secretin, carbonic anhydrase, CFTR, and Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ exchange.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, how do Na+ and K+ concentrations in pancreatic juice behave as flow rate changes?","choices":{"A":"Rise with flow","B":"Halve at low flow","C":"Exceed plasma twofold","D":"Stay equal to plasma","E":"Track chloride levels"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Na+ and K+ in pancreatic juice stay constant and equal to plasma at all flow rates, while HCO3- and Cl- change reciprocally. They do not rise, halve, double, or follow chloride. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 2, flow rate and ionic composition, lines 2635–2639).","boardPrep":"Step 1: at low basal flow, pancreatic juice is lower in HCO3 and higher in Cl- (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: Bootcamp states that low flow between meals lowers the HCO3 to Cl- ratio (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, exocrine pancreas).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 3 — ductal and centroacinar cells as the source of the bicarbonate-rich fluid","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"They do not rise, halve, doubl"}},{"id":"sdl14_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the phases of pancreatic secretion and their primary neural and hormonal stimuli.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about what share of the postprandial pancreatic enzyme response comes from the cephalic phase?","choices":{"A":"About 5 percent","B":"About 50 percent","C":"About 80 percent","D":"About 35 percent","E":"About 20 percent"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The cephalic phase gives about 20 percent of the postprandial enzyme response, the gastric phase about 5 to 10 percent, and the intestinal phase about 70 to 80 percent. About 5 percent is too low for the cephalic phase; 5 to 10 percent is the gastric phase's share of the enzyme response. About 50 percent is too high; the cephalic phase gives about 20 percent of the postprandial enzyme response. About 35 percent is too high; the cephalic phase gives about 20 percent of the postprandial enzyme response. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 3, phases of pancreatic secretion, lines 2658, 2668, 2674).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cephalic stimuli include sight, smell, taste, chewing, swallowing, and conditioned reflexes (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: the intestinal phase gives about 70 to 80 percent of the total pancreatic response (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — GI hormones","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"About 5 percent is too low for the cephalic phase; 5 to 10 p","B":"About 50 percent is too high; the cephalic phase gives about","D":"About 35 percent is too high; the cephalic phase gives about"}},{"id":"sdl14_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the phases of pancreatic secretion and their primary neural and hormonal stimuli.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what is the primary stimulus of the gastric phase of pancreatic secretion?","choices":{"A":"Sight and smell of food","B":"Acidic duodenal chyme","C":"Stomach wall distention","D":"Duodenal fatty acids","E":"Rising blood glucose"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Mechanical distention of the stomach wall by food drives the gastric phase through long vagovagal reflexes and enteric circuits. Sight and smell drive the cephalic phase, acidic chyme and fatty acids drive the intestinal phase, and blood glucose is not described. During the cephalic phase, the brain signals that drive gastric secretion also release ACh from vagal endings in the pancreas, so sight and smell of food drive the cephalic, not the gastric, phase. Acid in the small intestine stimulates secretin release from duodenal S cells, an intestinal phase stimulus. Monoglycerides and fatty acids in the duodenum stimulate CCK release from I cells, an intestinal phase stimulus. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 3, gastric phase, lines 2668–2673).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the gastric phase maintains a low volume, enzyme rich acinar secretion (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: gastric phase reflexes release ACh onto acinar cells (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — GI hormones","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"During the cephalic phase, the brain signals that drive gast","B":"Acid in the small intestine stimulates secretin release from","D":"Monoglycerides and fatty acids in the duodenum stimulate CCK"}},{"id":"sdl14_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the phases of pancreatic secretion and their primary neural and hormonal stimuli.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what mediator acts directly on acinar cells during the cephalic phase of pancreatic secretion?","choices":{"A":"Vagal acetylcholine","B":"Sympathetic norepinephrine","C":"Circulating secretin","D":"Circulating CCK","E":"Enteric VIP release"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In the cephalic phase, central impulses travel in the vagus nerve and release ACh from postganglionic vagal endings directly onto acinar cells. Secretin and CCK are released later in the intestinal phase, and norepinephrine and VIP are not described here. Sympathetic stimulation usually inhibits gastrointestinal tract activity, so norepinephrine does not mediate cephalic pancreatic enzyme secretion. CCK is secreted by duodenal and jejunal I cells in response to fatty acids and amino acids once food reaches the intestine, so it is not the cephalic phase mediator. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 3, cephalic phase pathways, lines 2661–2663).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cephalic stimulation yields a moderate enzyme rich secretion with very little water (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: CCK acts on acinar CCK1 receptors and through vagovagal reflexes in the intestinal phase (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — GI hormones","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"Secretin and CCK are released ","B":"Sympathetic stimulation usually inhibits gastrointestinal tr","D":"CCK is secreted by duodenal and jejunal I cells in response "}},{"id":"sdl14_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the formation, composition, and digestive functions of bile, including bile acid synthesis and conjugation and the role of bile salts in lipid emulsification and micelle formation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about what share of the solutes in bile are bile salts?","choices":{"A":"About 4 percent","B":"About 2 percent","C":"About 50 percent","D":"About 40 percent","E":"About 90 percent"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Bile salts make up about 50 percent of bile solutes, phospholipids mainly lecithin about 40 percent, cholesterol about 4 percent, and bile pigments about 2 percent. About 4 percent is the share of cholesterol in bile solutes, not bile salts, which make up about 50 percent. About 2 percent is the share of bile pigments in bile solutes, not bile salts, which make up about 50 percent. About 40 percent is the share of phospholipids (mainly lecithin) in bile solutes, not bile salts, which make up about 50 percent. About 90 percent is too high; bile salts make up about 50 percent of bile solutes. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 4, bile formation and composition, lines 2695–2697).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hepatocytes produce bile continuously, about 0.5 to 1.0 L per day (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: bile is composed of bile salts, phospholipids, cholesterol, bilirubin, water, and ions (First Aid 2025 GI bile).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 2 — the bile salt, phospholipid, and cholesterol balance behind cholesterol gallstones","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"About 4 percent is the share of cholesterol in bile solutes,","B":"About 2 percent is the share of bile pigments in bile solute","D":"About 40 percent is the share of phospholipids (mainly lecit","E":"About 90 percent is too high; bile salts make up about 50 pe"}},{"id":"sdl14_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the formation, composition, and digestive functions of bile, including bile acid synthesis and conjugation and the role of bile salts in lipid emulsification and micelle formation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which hepatocyte enzyme converts cholesterol into the primary bile acids?","choices":{"A":"HMG CoA reductase","B":"Heme oxygenase","C":"Lipoprotein lipase","D":"7alpha hydroxylase","E":"Acyl CoA synthetase"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Hepatocytes make cholic and chenodeoxycholic acid from cholesterol through 7alpha hydroxylase, the rate limiting enzyme. HMG CoA reductase makes cholesterol, heme oxygenase breaks down heme, lipoprotein lipase acts on plasma triglycerides, and acyl CoA synthetase activates fatty acids. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 4, primary bile acids, lines 2702–2703).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cholesterol 7alpha hydroxylase catalyzes the rate limiting step of bile acid synthesis (First Aid 2025 GI bile). COMLEX: the primary bile acids are cholic acid and chenodeoxycholic acid (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, bile acids).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 → Obj 2 — bile acid sequestrants and cholesterol conversion into bile acids","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the formation, composition, and digestive functions of bile, including bile acid synthesis and conjugation and the role of bile salts in lipid emulsification and micelle formation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what change do intestinal bacteria make to primary bile acids to form secondary bile acids?","choices":{"A":"Dehydroxylation at C7","B":"Conjugation with glycine","C":"Glucuronidation at C3","D":"Oxidation at C12","E":"Sulfation at C24"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Intestinal bacteria dehydroxylate primary bile acids at C7, turning cholic acid into deoxycholic acid and chenodeoxycholic acid into lithocholic acid. Conjugation with glycine or taurine happens in the liver. Glucuronidation is how the liver conjugates bilirubin, and glucuronidation, C12 oxidation, and C24 sulfation would all add groups, whereas deoxycholic and lithocholic acid each lack the C7 hydroxyl of their parent acid. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 4, secondary bile acids, lines 2704–2706).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the secondary bile acids are deoxycholic acid and lithocholic acid (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, bile acids). COMLEX: bacteria in the ileum and colon deconjugate and modify bile salts to form secondary bile acids (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, bile acids).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 → Obj 2 — ursodiol, a secondary bile acid used as a bile acid agent","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain gallbladder function and the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts, including bile storage and concentration, CCK-mediated gallbladder contraction and sphincter of Oddi relaxation, terminal ileal reabsorption, hepatic recycling, and regulation of bile acid synthesis.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about what share of secreted bile salts pass on to the terminal ileum after lipid absorption?","choices":{"A":"About 50 percent","B":"About 20 percent","C":"About 95 percent","D":"About 75 percent","E":"About 5 percent"},"correct":"C","explanation":"After lipid digestion and absorption in the jejunum, about 95 percent of secreted bile salts pass down to the terminal ileum for active reabsorption. The other figures are not described. About 50 percent is too low; about 95 percent of secreted bile salts reach the terminal ileum for active reabsorption. About 20 percent is too low; about 95 percent of secreted bile salts reach the terminal ileum for active reabsorption. About 75 percent is too low; about 95 percent of secreted bile salts reach the terminal ileum for active reabsorption. About 5 percent is far too low; about 95 percent of secreted bile salts reach the terminal ileum for active reabsorption. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 5, terminal ileal reabsorption, lines 2758–2759).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the terminal ileum is the site of bile salt reabsorption and vitamin B12 absorption (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, small intestine). COMLEX: reabsorbed bile salts return to the liver in portal blood for re-secretion into bile (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 → Obj 2 — bile acid sequestrants interrupting the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"About 50 percent is too low; about 95 percent of secreted bi","B":"About 20 percent is too low; about 95 percent of secreted bi","D":"About 75 percent is too low; about 95 percent of secreted bi","E":"About 5 percent is far too low; about 95 percent of secreted"}},{"id":"sdl14_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain gallbladder function and the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts, including bile storage and concentration, CCK-mediated gallbladder contraction and sphincter of Oddi relaxation, terminal ileal reabsorption, hepatic recycling, and regulation of bile acid synthesis.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which carrier moves bile salts from the ileal lumen into enterocytes?","choices":{"A":"Sodium bile salt cotransporter","B":"Sodium glucose cotransporter","C":"Proton peptide cotransporter","D":"Chloride bicarbonate exchanger","E":"Multidrug resistance protein 2"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Ileal enterocytes take up bile salts actively through Na+ bile salt cotransporters, called ASBT. Sodium glucose and proton peptide cotransport carry sugars and peptides, the chloride bicarbonate exchanger moves anions, and MRP2 exports conjugated bilirubin into bile. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 5, terminal ileal reabsorption, lines 2760–2761).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp traces reuptake at the terminal ileum into the portal circulation and back to the liver (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, bile acids). COMLEX: hepatocytes extract returning bile salts efficiently through basolateral carriers (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 → Obj 2 — bile acid sequestrants interrupting the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Sodium glucose and proton pept"}},{"id":"sdl14_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain gallbladder function and the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts, including bile storage and concentration, CCK-mediated gallbladder contraction and sphincter of Oddi relaxation, terminal ileal reabsorption, hepatic recycling, and regulation of bile acid synthesis.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about how large is the total bile salt pool?","choices":{"A":"About 25 to 30 g","B":"About 2.5 to 3 g","C":"About 0.2 to 0.3 g","D":"About 12 to 15 g","E":"About 100 g"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The bile salt pool is about 2.5 to 3.0 g and recirculates several times per meal, for a daily flux of 12 to 30 g. The 25 to 30 g and 12 to 15 g values fall within the daily flux range rather than the pool, 100 g is far too large, and 0.2 to 0.3 g is too small. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 5, regulation of bile acid synthesis, lines 2766–2767).","boardPrep":"Step 1: returning bile salts inhibit the rate limiting enzyme of bile acid synthesis (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: bile acid sequestrants bind bile acids in the gut lumen to prevent bile acid diarrhea (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, bile acid sequestrants).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 → Obj 2 — bile acid sequestrants interrupting the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Trace bilirubin production, transport, hepatic conjugation, intestinal metabolism, and excretion, and predict changes in bilirubin and its metabolites in major causes of jaundice.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about what share of bilirubin comes from the breakdown of aging red blood cells?","choices":{"A":"About 15 percent","B":"About 50 percent","C":"About 30 percent","D":"About 65 percent","E":"About 85 percent"},"correct":"E","explanation":"About 85 percent of bilirubin comes from aging red cells broken down by macrophages in the spleen, liver, and bone marrow, and about 15 percent from hepatic hemoproteins such as P450 cytochromes. About 15 percent is the share of bilirubin from hepatic hemoproteins such as P450 cytochromes, not from aging red cells. About 50 percent is too low; about 85 percent of bilirubin comes from aging red cells broken down by macrophages. About 30 percent is too low; about 85 percent of bilirubin comes from aging red cells broken down by macrophages. About 65 percent is too low; about 85 percent of bilirubin comes from aging red cells broken down by macrophages. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 6, bilirubin production, lines 2780–2783).","boardPrep":"Step 1: RBC destruction releases hemoglobin, which yields heme for bilirubin production (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, bilirubin metabolism). COMLEX: unconjugated bilirubin is lipid soluble and travels to the liver bound to albumin (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, bilirubin metabolism).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 3 — hereditary hyperbilirubinemias","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"About 15 percent is the share of bilirubin from hepatic hemo","B":"About 50 percent is too low; about 85 percent of bilirubin c","C":"About 30 percent is too low; about 85 percent of bilirubin c","D":"About 65 percent is too low; about 85 percent of bilirubin c"}},{"id":"sdl14_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Trace bilirubin production, transport, hepatic conjugation, intestinal metabolism, and excretion, and predict changes in bilirubin and its metabolites in major causes of jaundice.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which macrophage enzyme converts heme to biliverdin?","choices":{"A":"Biliverdin reductase","B":"Heme oxygenase","C":"Glucuronyl transferase","D":"Beta glucuronidase","E":"Ferrochelatase"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Macrophage heme oxygenase turns heme into biliverdin, which biliverdin reductase then reduces to unconjugated bilirubin. Glucuronyl transferase conjugates bilirubin in hepatocytes, bacterial beta glucuronidase deconjugates it in the gut, and ferrochelatase builds heme. Biliverdin reductase acts after heme oxygenase, reducing biliverdin to unconjugated bilirubin; it does not convert heme itself. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 6, bilirubin production, lines 2784–2786).","boardPrep":"Step 1: biliverdin is green and bilirubin is yellow brown (First Aid 2025 GI bilirubin). COMLEX: indirect bilirubin is unconjugated and water insoluble (First Aid 2025 GI bilirubin).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 3 — hereditary hyperbilirubinemias","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Biliverdin reductase acts after heme oxygenase, reducing bil"}},{"id":"sdl14_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Trace bilirubin production, transport, hepatic conjugation, intestinal metabolism, and excretion, and predict changes in bilirubin and its metabolites in major causes of jaundice.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which transporter moves conjugated bilirubin across the canalicular membrane into bile?","choices":{"A":"MRP2","B":"ASBT","C":"OATP","D":"CFTR","E":"NTCP"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Conjugated bilirubin is actively pumped across the canalicular membrane by MRP2, multidrug resistant protein 2. ASBT takes up bile salts in the ileum, CFTR is a chloride channel, and OATP and NTCP are basolateral uptake carriers not described for this step. ASBT absorbs bile salts across ileal enterocytes, not bilirubin into canaliculi. OATP1B1 and OATP1B3 mediate hepatic uptake from blood, not canalicular export. NTCP is the sinusoidal sodium taurocholate cotransporter (also the HBV entry receptor), not a canalicular bilirubin exporter. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 6, hepatic conjugation and excretion, lines 2797–2799).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Dubin Johnson syndrome is a defective MRP2 transporter with reduced excretion of conjugated bilirubin (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hereditary hyperbilirubinemias). COMLEX: Dubin Johnson and Rotor syndromes are excretion defects (First Aid 2025 GI jaundice).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 3 — hereditary hyperbilirubinemias","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"ASBT absorbs bile salts across ileal enterocytes, not biliru","C":"OATP1B1 and OATP1B3 mediate hepatic uptake from blood, not c","E":"NTCP is the sinusoidal sodium taurocholate cotransporter (al"}},{"id":"sdl14_q19","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the secretion, activation, and functions of pancreatic digestive enzymes and the mechanisms that prevent pancreatic autodigestion, including zymogen secretion, enteropeptidase-mediated trypsin activation, and activation of other pancreatic proteases by trypsin.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with heavy alcohol use has acute pancreatitis after a small amount of trypsin is activated inside the gland. Which built in property of trypsin normally limits this damage?","choices":{"A":"Autolysis aided by CTRC","B":"Acinar cell apoptosis","C":"Bicarbonate neutralization","D":"Lysosomal colocalization","E":"Bile salt inhibition"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Trypsinogen and trypsin carry cleavage sites that allow autocatalytic self degradation, helped by chymotrypsin C or caldecrin, if trypsin is activated early. Apoptosis, bicarbonate, and bile salts do not inactivate trypsin, and lysosomal colocalization is not described as protective. Aberrant delivery of zymogens to lysosomes lets lysosomal hydrolases activate pancreatic proenzymes, so colocalization drives pancreatitis rather than limiting it. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 1, trypsin self inactivation, lines 2604–2607).","boardPrep":"Step 1: zymogens stay unactivated until they leave the ducts and meet enteropeptidase in the small intestine (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: chronic alcohol use and tobacco use are listed causes of chronic pancreatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic pancreatitis etiology).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — acute and chronic pancreatitis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Aberrant delivery of zymogens to lysosomes lets lysosomal hy"}},{"id":"sdl14_q20","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the secretion, activation, and functions of pancreatic digestive enzymes and the mechanisms that prevent pancreatic autodigestion, including zymogen secretion, enteropeptidase-mediated trypsin activation, and activation of other pancreatic proteases by trypsin.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman with chronic pancreatitis asks why her enzyme capsules need more than lipase. Her physician explains that duodenal bile salts inhibit lipase on its own. Which cofactor, activated by trypsin, binds lipase so it can still work?","choices":{"A":"Elastase","B":"Lecithin","C":"Albumin","D":"Colipase","E":"Secretin"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Trypsin converts procolipase to colipase, which binds pancreatic lipase and lets it digest lipid in the presence of inhibitory bile salts. Elastase is a protease, lecithin is a phospholipid, albumin carries bilirubin in blood, and secretin is a hormone. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 1, cascade activation by trypsin, lines 2578–2580).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic lipase hydrolyzes triglycerides into 2 monoglycerides and free fatty acids (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: trypsin also activates prophospholipase A2 (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 1 — pancrelipase replacing lipase and other enzymes in exocrine pancreatic insufficiency","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q21","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the secretion, activation, and functions of pancreatic digestive enzymes and the mechanisms that prevent pancreatic autodigestion, including zymogen secretion, enteropeptidase-mediated trypsin activation, and activation of other pancreatic proteases by trypsin.","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old man with chronic pancreatitis takes enzyme capsules with meals. Which capsule protease removes single amino acids from the C terminal end of peptides?","choices":{"A":"Pancreatic trypsin","B":"Pancreatic chymotrypsin","C":"Pancreatic elastase","D":"Brush border aminopeptidase","E":"Carboxypeptidase B"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Carboxypeptidases A and B are exopeptidases that remove single amino acids one at a time from the C terminal end. Trypsin, chymotrypsin, and elastase are endopeptidases that cut internal bonds, and aminopeptidase is a brush border enzyme rather than a pancreatic capsule protease. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 1, digestive functions of activated enzymes, lines 2582–2585).","boardPrep":"Step 1: trypsin also cleaves more trypsinogen into active trypsin in a positive feedback loop (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatic enzymes). COMLEX: pancreatic alpha amylase produces disaccharides and trisaccharides from starch and glycogen (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 1 — pancrelipase for exocrine insufficiency","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q22","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the mechanism and regulation of pancreatic bicarbonate-rich secretion, including the roles of secretin, carbonic anhydrase, CFTR, and Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ exchange.","batch":2,"stem":"A 7-year-old girl with cystic fibrosis has poor pancreatic bicarbonate output. Normally, how does her defective apical channel support bicarbonate secretion?","choices":{"A":"Pumping bicarbonate into blood","B":"Secreting enzymes into acini","C":"Absorbing sodium from the lumen","D":"Recycling chloride to the lumen","E":"Generating carbonic acid"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The apical CFTR Cl- channel lets Cl- recycle back into the lumen, keeping luminal Cl- high enough for continuous Cl-/HCO3- exchange. CFTR does not pump bicarbonate into blood, secrete enzymes, absorb sodium, or make carbonic acid. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 2, role of CFTR Cl- channels, lines 2623–2627).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cystic fibrosis is a listed cause of chronic pancreatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic pancreatitis etiology). COMLEX: secretin raises duct cell cAMP to open CFTR and drive Cl-/HCO3 exchange (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — cystic fibrosis as a cause of chronic pancreatitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q23","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the mechanism and regulation of pancreatic bicarbonate-rich secretion, including the roles of secretin, carbonic anhydrase, CFTR, and Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ exchange.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man with suspected chronic pancreatitis has a secretin stimulation test. As his duct cells secrete bicarbonate, how does the venous blood leaving his pancreas change?","choices":{"A":"It becomes more alkaline","B":"It stays unchanged","C":"It loses chloride","D":"It gains bile salts","E":"It becomes more acidic"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The H+ formed with each HCO3- is pumped across the basolateral membrane by Na+/H+ exchange into capillaries, acidifying pancreatic venous blood. It does not become alkaline or stay unchanged, and chloride and bile salts are not the described change. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 2, basolateral H+ extrusion, lines 2628–2631).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sodium and water follow HCO3 paracellularly across tight junctions into the duct lumen (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: Bootcamp describes pancreatic fluid as isotonic, with water, bicarbonate, and electrolytes (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic fluid).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 4 — secretin and CCK regulation of the exocrine pancreas","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q24","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the mechanism and regulation of pancreatic bicarbonate-rich secretion, including the roles of secretin, carbonic anhydrase, CFTR, and Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ exchange.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman has pancreatic juice collected during a secretin test, and her flow rises sharply. How does the anion content of the juice change?","choices":{"A":"Chloride up and bicarbonate down","B":"Bicarbonate up and chloride down","C":"Both anions rise together","D":"Neither anion changes","E":"Bicarbonate down and chloride flat"},"correct":"B","explanation":"At high flow, centroacinar and ductal cells secrete large volumes with bicarbonate rising to about 140 to 145 mEq/L and a reciprocal fall in chloride. Low flow shows the opposite pattern, and the two anions change in opposite directions rather than together, not at all, or with one held flat. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 2, flow rate and ionic composition, lines 2635–2644).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secretin is released when duodenal pH falls below 4.5 (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: ACh and CCK potentiate secretin driven bicarbonate secretion through IP3 and Ca2+ (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — GI hormones","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Low flow shows the opposite pa","D":"Low flow shows the opposite pa"}},{"id":"sdl14_q25","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the phases of pancreatic secretion and their primary neural and hormonal stimuli.","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man with a prior truncal vagotomy joins a pancreatic function study. A balloon distends his stomach while no chyme is allowed to enter the duodenum, and his pancreatic enzyme output still rises slightly. Which pathway most likely carries this signal?","choices":{"A":"Vagal efferent fibers to acinar cells","B":"Secretin from duodenal S cells","C":"Local enteric neural circuits","D":"Sympathetic splanchnic fibers","E":"Cortical conditioned reflexes"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Gastric distension drives the gastric phase of pancreatic secretion through two routes: long vagovagal reflexes and local enteric neural circuits, both of which release ACh onto acinar cells. With the vagal trunks divided, the local enteric circuits are the route left to carry the signal. Vagal efferent fibers were cut by the vagotomy. Secretin release needs acid chyme below pH 4.5 in the duodenum, which was prevented. Sympathetic fibers do not drive acinar enzyme secretion. Cortical conditioned reflexes belong to the cephalic phase and travel in the vagus. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 3, gastric phase pathways, p76).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the gastric phase makes a low-volume, enzyme-rich juice and provides about 5 to 10 percent of the enzyme response (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: sympathetic input generally inhibits GI secretion (lecture SDL 4).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 4 — vagovagal reflexes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_q26","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the phases of pancreatic secretion and their primary neural and hormonal stimuli.","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old woman smells lunch cooking, and her pancreas secretes enzymes that mostly stay in the duct system until later. Why do they stay there?","choices":{"A":"Inhibition by somatostatin","B":"Very low fluid volume","C":"Ductal reabsorption of enzymes","D":"Absent zymogen granules","E":"Low enzyme synthesis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The cephalic response is a moderate enzyme rich secretion with very little water or electrolyte volume, so most enzymes stay stored in the ducts until fluid flow increases later. Somatostatin, ductal enzyme reabsorption, absent granules, and low synthesis are not the described reason. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 3, cephalic phase secretory response, lines 2664–2667).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in the cephalic phase, ACh is released from postganglionic vagal endings onto acinar cells (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: somatostatin inhibits secretin and CCK release (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, somatostatin).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — vagal parasympathetic innervation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_q27","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the phases of pancreatic secretion and their primary neural and hormonal stimuli.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man eats a large meal, and chyme distends his duodenum and excites stretch and chemical receptors in the duodenal wall. Which pathway then amplifies the effects of secretin and CCK?","choices":{"A":"Vagovagal reflexes","B":"Enterogastric reflex","C":"Gastrocolic reflex","D":"Sympathetic reflexes","E":"Myenteric peristalsis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In the intestinal phase, distention and chemical stimuli excite vagal afferents and trigger efferent ACh release, a vagovagal reflex that potentiates both secretin and CCK. The enterogastric and gastrocolic reflexes, sympathetic reflexes, and peristalsis are not described here. Enterogastric reflexes from the duodenal wall inhibit gastric emptying rather than drive pancreatic secretion. The gastrocolic reflex is triggered by gastric distention and acts on the colon, not the pancreas. Sympathetic stimulation usually inhibits gastrointestinal tract activity, so it does not amplify secretin and CCK. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 3, intestinal phase mechanisms, lines 2686–2688).","boardPrep":"Step 1: intestinal phase stimuli include acidic chyme and fat and protein digestion products (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: CCK stimulates massive zymogen exocytosis from acinar cells in the intestinal phase (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 4 — vagovagal reflexes","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Enterogastric reflexes from the duodenal wall inhibit gastri","C":"The gastrocolic reflex is triggered by gastric distention an","D":"Sympathetic stimulation usually inhibits gastrointestinal tr"}},{"id":"sdl14_q28","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the formation, composition, and digestive functions of bile, including bile acid synthesis and conjugation and the role of bile salts in lipid emulsification and micelle formation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman with gallstones asks how her bile salts can work in the duodenum. They stay ionized and water soluble at duodenal pH. Which feature explains this?","choices":{"A":"Bacterial dehydroxylation","B":"Albumin binding in bile","C":"Low pKa after conjugation","D":"Micelle core packing","E":"Bicarbonate buffering"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Conjugation with glycine or taurine lowers the pKa from about 7 to between 1 and 4, so bile salts are fully ionized and water soluble at duodenal pH (3 to 5 in the lecture). Bacterial dehydroxylation makes secondary bile acids, albumin carries bilirubin in blood, and micelles and buffering do not set the pKa. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 4, conjugation into bile salts, lines 2707–2711).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bile acids are conjugated in the liver with glycine or taurine to form amphipathic bile salts (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, bile acids). COMLEX: conjugation with glycine or taurine makes bile acids water soluble (First Aid 2025 GI bile).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 2 — gallbladder concentration of bile before it reaches the duodenum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q29","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the formation, composition, and digestive functions of bile, including bile acid synthesis and conjugation and the role of bile salts in lipid emulsification and micelle formation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 36-year-old man eats a fatty meal, and his bile salts break large fat globules into tiny emulsified droplets. By up to about how much does the lipid surface area increase?","choices":{"A":"Up to 10 times","B":"Up to 2 times","C":"Up to 100 times","D":"Up to 1 million times","E":"Up to 1000 times"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Bile salts lower interfacial tension, and intestinal mixing breaks fat into droplets under 1 µm, raising surface area up to 1000 fold for pancreatic lipase. Their negative charges repel and keep the droplets from merging. Two, 10, or 100 times understates the gain, and 1 million times overstates it. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 4, role of bile salts in emulsification, lines 2716–2721).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp states that emulsification raises surface area for pancreatic enzymes and increases fat digestion and absorption (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, bile acid function). COMLEX: bile salts have a hydrophobic steroid nucleus and hydrophilic negatively charged groups (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 1 — pancrelipase restoring fat digestion when lipase is deficient","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Two, 10, or 100 times understa"}},{"id":"sdl14_q30","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the formation, composition, and digestive functions of bile, including bile acid synthesis and conjugation and the role of bile salts in lipid emulsification and micelle formation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old woman digests a fatty meal, and mixed micelles carry lipids across the unstirred water layer to her enterocytes. Where do the bile salts end up once the lipids enter the cells?","choices":{"A":"In the gut lumen","B":"Inside the enterocyte","C":"In the portal blood","D":"In the lymph lacteals","E":"In the brush border"},"correct":"A","explanation":"At the brush border, monoglycerides and fatty acids leave the micelle and diffuse into enterocytes, while bile salts are left behind in the lumen for later ileal reabsorption. They do not enter the jejunal enterocyte, portal blood, lacteals, or brush border with the lipids. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 4, role of bile salts in micelle formation, lines 2730–2733).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bile functions include absorption of lipids and fat soluble vitamins (First Aid 2025 GI bile). COMLEX: bile is the main route for eliminating cholesterol and bilirubin (First Aid 2025 GI bile).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation returns bile salts from the terminal ileum","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"They do not enter the jejunal ","E":"They do not enter the jejunal "}},{"id":"sdl14_q31","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain gallbladder function and the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts, including bile storage and concentration, CCK-mediated gallbladder contraction and sphincter of Oddi relaxation, terminal ileal reabsorption, hepatic recycling, and regulation of bile acid synthesis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man with Crohn disease has his terminal ileum resected and develops steatorrhea even though his liver now makes more bile acids. Why does steatorrhea occur?","choices":{"A":"Synthesis is shut off","B":"Synthesis cannot match losses","C":"Lipase is destroyed in bile","D":"Micelles form too easily","E":"Gallbladder stops storing bile"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Less bile salt returns to the liver, which relieves inhibition and raises synthesis, but even maximal synthesis falls behind heavy fecal loss. The pool shrinks, lipid solubilization fails, and steatorrhea follows. Synthesis rises rather than stopping; micelles form too poorly, not too easily, and the cause is the shrunken bile salt pool rather than lipase destruction or loss of gallbladder storage. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 5, regulation of bile acid synthesis, lines 2770–2774).","boardPrep":"Step 1: malabsorption of bile salts at the distal ileum, as in Crohn disease, prevents normal fat absorption (First Aid 2025 GI bile). COMLEX: ileal bile salt loss lets dietary calcium bind fat, so free oxalate is absorbed and oxalate stones form (First Aid 2025 GI bile).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 2 — bile acid sequestrants interrupting the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Synthesis rises rather than st"}},{"id":"sdl14_q32","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain gallbladder function and the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts, including bile storage and concentration, CCK-mediated gallbladder contraction and sphincter of Oddi relaxation, terminal ileal reabsorption, hepatic recycling, and regulation of bile acid synthesis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman fasts overnight, and her liver bile flows into her gallbladder for storage. What are the states of her sphincter of Oddi and gallbladder?","choices":{"A":"Open with gallbladder contracted","B":"Open with gallbladder relaxed","C":"Closed with gallbladder relaxed","D":"Closed with gallbladder contracted","E":"Spasming with ductal reflux"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Between meals the sphincter of Oddi is closed and the gallbladder is relaxed, so continuously made bile flows into the gallbladder. After a meal CCK reverses both, contracting the gallbladder and relaxing the sphincter. Spasm with ductal reflux is not the fasting state; the closed sphincter simply diverts bile into the relaxed gallbladder. Between meals the sphincter of Oddi is closed, not open; an open sphincter would not divert bile into the gallbladder for storage. The sphincter is closed, but the gallbladder is relaxed, not contracted; CCK after a meal contracts the gallbladder and relaxes the sphincter. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 5, gallbladder storage, lines 2738–2740).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CCK contracts the gallbladder and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi so bile is ejected in pulsatile spurts (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: vagal ACh adds to CCK in stimulating gallbladder emptying (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 2 — gallbladder concentration of bile","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"After a meal CCK reverses both","B":"Between meals the sphincter of Oddi is closed, not open; an ","D":"The sphincter is closed, but the gallbladder is relaxed, not"}},{"id":"sdl14_q33","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain gallbladder function and the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts, including bile storage and concentration, CCK-mediated gallbladder contraction and sphincter of Oddi relaxation, terminal ileal reabsorption, hepatic recycling, and regulation of bile acid synthesis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old man eats three meals a day. About how many times does his bile salt pool recirculate with each meal?","choices":{"A":"2 to 3 times","B":"10 to 12 times","C":"Once per day","D":"5 to 6 times","E":"20 to 30 times"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The bile salt pool of about 2.5 to 3.0 g recirculates 2 to 3 times per meal, for 12 to 30 g per day. Once per day is too few, and the larger counts are too many. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 5, regulation of bile acid synthesis, lines 2766–2767).","boardPrep":"Step 1: about 95 percent of bile salts reach the terminal ileum for reuptake (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: portal blood carries returning bile salts back to hepatocytes (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 2 — bile acid sequestrants interrupting the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Once per day is too few, and t","D":"Once per day is too few, and t","E":"Once per day is too few, and t"}},{"id":"sdl14_q34","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Trace bilirubin production, transport, hepatic conjugation, intestinal metabolism, and excretion, and predict changes in bilirubin and its metabolites in major causes of jaundice.","batch":2,"stem":"A 25-year-old man with sickle cell disease has jaundice during a hemolytic crisis. Which finding is expected?","choices":{"A":"Strongly positive urine bilirubin","B":"Absent urine urobilinogen","C":"Pale clay colored stools","D":"Negative urine bilirubin","E":"Raised conjugated bilirubin"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Hemolytic jaundice raises mainly unconjugated bilirubin, which is albumin bound and is not filtered by glomeruli, so urine bilirubin is negative. Urine urobilinogen rises and stools are dark. Strong urine bilirubin, absent urobilinogen, and pale stools fit obstruction, and conjugated bilirubin stays normal. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 6, prehepatic jaundice, lines 2813–2823).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hemolysis is a cause of unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia (First Aid 2025 GI jaundice). COMLEX: jaundice appears when total serum bilirubin exceeds about 2.0 to 2.5 mg/dL (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 2 — black pigment gallstones","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Urine urobilinogen rises and s"}},{"id":"sdl14_q35","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Trace bilirubin production, transport, hepatic conjugation, intestinal metabolism, and excretion, and predict changes in bilirubin and its metabolites in major causes of jaundice.","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman has a pancreatic head mass that completely blocks her common bile duct. Which stool and urine pattern is expected?","choices":{"A":"Dark stools with clear urine","B":"Dark stools and raised urobilinogen","C":"Pale stools and dark urine","D":"Normal stools and normal urine","E":"Green stools and red urine"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Complete obstruction keeps bile out of the gut, so stools are pale and urine urobilinogen is absent, while water soluble conjugated bilirubin spills into urine and makes it dark and tea colored. Dark stools and raised urobilinogen fit hemolysis. Complete bile duct obstruction keeps bile pigment out of the gut, so stools are pale, not dark, and conjugated bilirubin spills into urine to darken it. Complete obstruction keeps bile out of the gut and spills water-soluble conjugated bilirubin into urine, so stools are pale and urine dark, not normal. Complete obstruction gives pale stools and dark, tea-colored urine, not green stools and red urine. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 6, posthepatic jaundice, lines 2835–2846).","boardPrep":"Step 1: obstructive jaundice with a palpable nontender gallbladder is the Courvoisier sign (First Aid 2025 GI biliary structures). COMLEX: Bootcamp states that about 80 percent of urobilinogen becomes stercobilin for fecal excretion (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, bilirubin metabolism).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 4 — pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Complete bile duct obstruction keeps bile pigment out of the","D":"Complete obstruction keeps bile out of the gut and spills wa","E":"Complete obstruction gives pale stools and dark, tea-colored"}},{"id":"sdl14_q36","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Trace bilirubin production, transport, hepatic conjugation, intestinal metabolism, and excretion, and predict changes in bilirubin and its metabolites in major causes of jaundice.","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old man has mild unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia that appears during fasting. Which hepatocyte enzyme of the smooth ER is mildly deficient?","choices":{"A":"HMOX1","B":"UGT1A1","C":"BLVRA","D":"CYP7A1","E":"CYP2E1"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Gilbert syndrome is an inherited mild UGT1A1 deficiency that lowers bilirubin glucuronidation in the smooth ER and raises unconjugated bilirubin. HMOX1 makes biliverdin, BLVRA makes bilirubin, CYP7A1 is the rate limiting 7 alpha hydroxylase of bile acid synthesis, and CYP2E1 is induced by alcohol, so none of them conjugates bilirubin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 6, hepatic conjugation and hepatic jaundice, lines 2793–2796, 2824–2827).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Crigler Najjar type I has severely reduced UGT1A1 activity and type II less severe loss (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hereditary hyperbilirubinemias). COMLEX: phenobarbital raises UGT1A1 expression and may help Crigler Najjar type II (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hereditary hyperbilirubinemias).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 3 — hereditary hyperbilirubinemias","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl14_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the secretion, activation, and functions of pancreatic digestive enzymes and the mechanisms that prevent pancreatic autodigestion, including zymogen secretion, enteropeptidase-mediated trypsin activation, and activation of other pancreatic proteases by trypsin.","batch":3,"stem":"A 49-year-old woman with chronic pancreatitis takes enzyme capsules with a fatty meal. When phospholipase A2, activated by trypsin, cleaves a fatty acid from dietary phospholipid, what product remains?","choices":{"A":"Monoglycerides","B":"Free cholesterol","C":"Lecithin","D":"Lysolecithin","E":"Glycerol"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Phospholipase A2 cleaves fatty acids from phospholipids to yield lysolecithin. Monoglycerides come from lipase acting on triglycerides, free cholesterol comes from cholesterol ester hydrolase, lecithin is the starting phospholipid, and glycerol is not the described product. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 1, lipolytic enzymes, lines 2588–2592).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cholesterol ester hydrolase converts cholesterol esters to free cholesterol and fatty acids (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: endopeptidases such as trypsin, chymotrypsin, and elastase cleave internal peptide bonds (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 1 — pancrelipase replacing lipase and other enzymes in exocrine pancreatic insufficiency","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl14_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the mechanism and regulation of pancreatic bicarbonate-rich secretion, including the roles of secretin, carbonic anhydrase, CFTR, and Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ exchange.","batch":3,"stem":"A 37-year-old man with chronic pancreatitis has a secretin stimulation test. Besides making it inside the cell, by which basolateral route do his duct cells take up extra bicarbonate from blood?","choices":{"A":"Sodium bicarbonate symport","B":"Chloride bicarbonate exchange","C":"Sodium proton exchange","D":"Paracellular diffusion","E":"Aquaporin water channels"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Extra HCO3- enters the ductal cell across the basolateral membrane by Na+/HCO3- cotransport. The Cl-/HCO3- exchanger sits on the apical side, Na+/H+ exchange removes H+ basolaterally, sodium and water move paracellularly into the lumen, and aquaporins carry water. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 2, carbonic anhydrase activity, lines 2618–2619).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the H+ made with HCO3 leaves duct cells by basolateral Na+/H+ exchange and Na+/K+ ATPase (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: secretin is the primary stimulant of aqueous HCO3 secretion (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 4 — secretin acting on ductal cells to drive bicarbonate-rich pancreatic secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl14_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the phases of pancreatic secretion and their primary neural and hormonal stimuli.","batch":3,"stem":"A 42-year-old woman eats lunch. During the gastric phase of pancreatic secretion, what kind of secretion do her acinar cells make?","choices":{"A":"High volume bicarbonate fluid","B":"Low volume enzyme juice","C":"Pure electrolyte secretion","D":"Mucus rich ductal secretion","E":"Bile rich duodenal reflux"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The gastric phase uses long vagovagal reflexes and enteric circuits that release ACh onto acinar cells and keep up a low volume, enzyme rich secretion. High volume bicarbonate fluid is the secretin response of the intestinal phase. The other choices are not described. Acinar cells in the gastric phase release an enzyme-rich secretion under ACh; pure electrolyte secretion is not described for this phase. Acinar cells make an enzyme-rich secretion under ACh in the gastric phase; mucus-rich ductal secretion is not described for it. Bile-rich duodenal reflux is not a pancreatic acinar secretion; the gastric phase keeps up a low-volume, enzyme-rich secretion through ACh. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 3, gastric phase, lines 2668–2673).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the gastric phase gives about 5 to 10 percent of the postprandial enzyme response (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: intestinal phase amino acid triggers include phenylalanine, methionine, and tryptophan (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 4 — vagovagal reflexes","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"Acinar cells in the gastric phase release an enzyme-rich sec","D":"Acinar cells make an enzyme-rich secretion under ACh in the ","E":"Bile-rich duodenal reflux is not a pancreatic acinar secreti"}},{"id":"trial_sdl14_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the formation, composition, and digestive functions of bile, including bile acid synthesis and conjugation and the role of bile salts in lipid emulsification and micelle formation.","batch":3,"stem":"A 59-year-old man recovering from a cholecystectomy absorbs fat normally after a fatty meal. About what size are the mixed micelles that carry his lipid digestion products?","choices":{"A":"30 to 60 nm","B":"3 to 6 µm","C":"3 to 6 nm","D":"300 to 600 nm","E":"0.3 to 0.6 mm"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Mixed micelles are cylindrical complexes about 3 to 6 nm across, made of bile salts with lecithin, fatty acids, monoglycerides, cholesterol, and lysolecithin. Emulsified droplets are larger, under 1 µm, and the other sizes are not described. 30 to 60 nm is ten times too large; mixed micelles are about 3 to 6 nm across. 300 to 600 nm is far too large; mixed micelles are about 3 to 6 nm across, and even emulsified droplets are under 1 µm. 0.3 to 0.6 mm is vastly too large; mixed micelles are about 3 to 6 nm across. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 4, role of bile salts in micelle formation, lines 2723–2726).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in a micelle, hydrophobic lipids sit inside and the charged polar groups of bile salts face outward (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: negative charges on surface bile salts keep emulsified droplets from merging again (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 2 — gallbladder concentration of bile before it reaches the duodenum","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"30 to 60 nm is ten times too large; mixed micelles are about","D":"300 to 600 nm is far too large; mixed micelles are about 3 t","E":"0.3 to 0.6 mm is vastly too large; mixed micelles are about "}},{"id":"trial_sdl14_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain gallbladder function and the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts, including bile storage and concentration, CCK-mediated gallbladder contraction and sphincter of Oddi relaxation, terminal ileal reabsorption, hepatic recycling, and regulation of bile acid synthesis.","batch":3,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman has gallstones, and her biliary pain begins after a fatty meal when duodenal I cells release their hormone. Besides contracting her gallbladder, what else does this hormone do?","choices":{"A":"Sphincter of Oddi relaxation","B":"Sphincter of Oddi contraction","C":"Pyloric sphincter relaxation","D":"Ileocecal valve closure","E":"Gallbladder wall relaxation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"CCK from I cells contracts the gallbladder wall and at the same time relaxes the sphincter of Oddi, so concentrated bile spurts into the duodenum. It does not contract the sphincter of Oddi or relax the gallbladder wall, and the pylorus and ileocecal valve are not the described targets. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 5, CCK mediated gallbladder ejection, lines 2748–2756).","boardPrep":"Step 1: biliary colic worsens after meals because CCK drives gallbladder contraction against an obstructing stone (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, biliary colic). COMLEX: CCK is released when fatty acids, monoglycerides, and amino acids enter the proximal duodenum (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 3 — acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"It does not contract the sphin","C":"It does not contract the sphin","D":"It does not contract the sphin"}},{"id":"trial_sdl14_q06","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Trace bilirubin production, transport, hepatic conjugation, intestinal metabolism, and excretion, and predict changes in bilirubin and its metabolites in major causes of jaundice.","batch":3,"stem":"A 72-year-old man on long courses of broad spectrum antibiotics notices lighter stools. Normally, which bacterial product gives stool its brown color?","choices":{"A":"Biliverdin","B":"Bilirubin","C":"Urobilinogen","D":"Hemosiderin","E":"Stercobilin"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Colonic bacteria oxidize most urobilinogen to stercobilin and urobilin, which give feces their normal dark brown color. Biliverdin is the green heme breakdown product, bilirubin is deconjugated and reduced in the gut, urobilinogen is colorless, and hemosiderin is a tissue iron store. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 6, intestinal metabolism and excretion, lines 2800–2805).","boardPrep":"Step 1: about 20 percent of urobilinogen is reabsorbed and a small share reaches the urine as yellow urobilin (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: bacterial beta glucuronidases deconjugate bilirubin in the terminal ileum and colon (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — large bile duct obstruction","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":15,"title":"SDL 15 — Pathology - Infectious and Inflammatory Liver Diseases","questions":[{"id":"sdl15_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathophysiology of hepatic injury, scarring, and repair and identify the clinical features of acute (hepatic encephalopathy, coagulopathy, portal hypertension) and chronic (pruritus) liver failure.","batch":1,"stem":"In acute and chronic hepatitis, what does Robbins call the pyknotic eosinophilic remnants of apoptotic hepatocytes?","choices":{"A":"Mallory hyaline","B":"Acidophil bodies","C":"Russell bodies","D":"Ground glass cells","E":"Psammoma bodies"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Irreversible hepatocyte injury by apoptosis leaves pyknotic, eosinophilic acidophil bodies ringed by mononuclear cells. Robbins notes that these were first described in yellow fever and named Councilman bodies, and by convention keeps that eponym for yellow fever; in other hepatitis the term is acidophil bodies. Mallory hyaline is clumped intermediate filament in ballooned cells, ground glass cells mark chronic hepatitis B, and Russell and psammoma bodies are not liver apoptosis findings. Sources: Robbins ch 18, hepatocyte apoptosis (SDL 15–19 course reading, p. 6); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 1, hepatocyte responses to injury, lines 2850–2856).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp and the lecture use Councilman bodies more loosely, for the eosinophilic inclusions of acute viral hepatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute viral hepatitis); expect either name. COMLEX: confluent necrosis is zonal loss of contiguous hepatocytes, such as zone 3 perivenular necrosis (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 4 — Kupffer and stellate cells","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathophysiology of hepatic injury, scarring, and repair and identify the clinical features of acute (hepatic encephalopathy, coagulopathy, portal hypertension) and chronic (pruritus) liver failure.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which cell is mainly responsible for collagen deposition and scar formation in the injured liver?","choices":{"A":"Hepatic stellate cells","B":"Resident Kupffer cells","C":"Portal tract fibroblasts","D":"Ductal cholangiocytes","E":"Sinusoidal endothelium"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Hepatic stellate or Ito cells are the main collagen depositing cells. Cytokines such as TNF alpha and TGF beta from Kupffer cells and injured hepatocytes activate them, and they become myofibroblasts that lay down type I and III collagen in the space of Disse. Kupffer cells signal but do not make the scar. Portal fibroblasts may also contribute in some disorders, but stellate cells are the principal source; sinusoidal endothelium loses its fenestrations as the collagen is laid down rather than making it, and cholangiocytes are not named as scar forming cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 1, scar formation and hepatic stellate cells, lines 2857–2865); Robbins Ch. 18 (portal fibroblasts may also play a role in scarring).","boardPrep":"Step 1: collagen in the space of Disse causes loss of endothelial fenestrations, called sinusoidal capillarization (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: PDGF drives proliferation of activated stellate cells (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 4 — Kupffer and stellate cells","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Kupffer cells signal but do no","C":"Portal fibroblasts may also co","D":"Portal fibroblasts may also co"}},{"id":"sdl15_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathophysiology of hepatic injury, scarring, and repair and identify the clinical features of acute (hepatic encephalopathy, coagulopathy, portal hypertension) and chronic (pruritus) liver failure.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, acute liver failure is encephalopathy and coagulopathy arising within what time of the first injury?","choices":{"A":"Within 2 weeks","B":"Within 52 weeks","C":"Within 6 weeks","D":"Within 26 weeks","E":"Within 5 years"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Acute liver failure is acute liver illness with encephalopathy and coagulopathy within 26 weeks of the first injury in a patient without preexisting liver disease, and it shows massive necrosis with a small shrunken liver. The other windows are not the lecture definition. Within 2 weeks is not the lecture definition; acute liver failure is encephalopathy and coagulopathy within 26 weeks of the first injury. Within 52 weeks is not the lecture definition; acute liver failure is encephalopathy and coagulopathy within 26 weeks of the first injury. Within 6 weeks is not the lecture definition; acute liver failure is encephalopathy and coagulopathy within 26 weeks of the first injury. Within 5 years is far too long for acute liver failure, which is defined as encephalopathy and coagulopathy within 26 weeks of the first injury. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 1, acute liver failure, lines 2873–2876).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in acute liver failure, portal hypertension develops over days to weeks and shows as ascites (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: First Aid lists drug injury such as acetaminophen, ischemic hepatitis, viral hepatitis, and autoimmune hepatitis as causes of very high aminotransferases (First Aid 2025 GI liver markers).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 4 — Kupffer and stellate cells","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Within 2 weeks is not the lecture definition; acute liver fa","B":"Within 52 weeks is not the lecture definition; acute liver f","C":"Within 6 weeks is not the lecture definition; acute liver fa","E":"Within 5 years is far too long for acute liver failure, whic"}},{"id":"sdl15_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the serological markers and their timing, viral proteins and genome types (RNA vs DNA, positive vs negative sense, envelope), and clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses (Hepatitis A, B, C, D, and E).","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what genome and structure does hepatitis A virus have?","choices":{"A":"Enveloped partially double stranded DNA","B":"Enveloped positive sense RNA","C":"Nonenveloped positive sense RNA","D":"Enveloped circular defective RNA","E":"Nonenveloped negative sense RNA"},"correct":"C","explanation":"HAV is a small nonenveloped single stranded positive sense RNA picornavirus. Partially double stranded DNA is HBV, enveloped positive sense RNA is HCV, enveloped circular defective RNA is HDV, and no nonenveloped hepatotropic virus is negative sense. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 2, hepatitis A virus, lines 2898–2901).","boardPrep":"Step 1: IgG anti HAV persists for life and confers long term immunity (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: HAV spreads by the fecal oral route and does not cause chronic liver disease (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the serological markers and their timing, viral proteins and genome types (RNA vs DNA, positive vs negative sense, envelope), and clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses (Hepatitis A, B, C, D, and E).","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about how long after exposure do anti-HCV antibodies emerge in hepatitis C infection?","choices":{"A":"3 to 6 days","B":"3 to 6 weeks","C":"1 to 3 weeks","D":"3 to 6 months","E":"6 to 12 months"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In hepatitis C, HCV RNA becomes detectable within 1 to 3 weeks of infection along with elevated aminotransferases, and anti-HCV antibodies emerge 3 to 6 weeks after exposure. One to 3 weeks is the timing of HCV RNA, not antibody. Three to 6 days is too early for either marker. Three to 6 months and 6 to 12 months are too late; persistent HCV RNA beyond 6 months instead defines chronic infection. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 2, hepatitis C serological markers and timing, lines 2943–2946).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists HBV and HCV as the viral hepatitides classically associated with hepatocellular carcinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatocellular carcinoma). COMLEX: First Aid lists HCV among the causes of cirrhosis linked to hepatocellular carcinoma (First Aid 2025 GI, hepatocellular carcinoma).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"One to 3 weeks is the timing o"}},{"id":"sdl15_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the serological markers and their timing, viral proteins and genome types (RNA vs DNA, positive vs negative sense, envelope), and clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses (Hepatitis A, B, C, D, and E).","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which marker is crucial for diagnosing acute hepatitis B after HBsAg clears but before anti HBs appears?","choices":{"A":"IgG anti HBs","B":"IgG anti HBc","C":"IgM anti HAV","D":"IgG anti HDV","E":"IgM anti HBc"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In the window period after HBsAg clears and before anti HBs appears, IgM anti HBc identifies acute infection. IgG anti HBc and anti HBs mark past or resolved infection, and the HAV and HDV antibodies belong to other viruses. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 2, hepatitis B serological markers, lines 2921–2926).","boardPrep":"Step 1: HBeAg and HBV DNA indicate active replication and high infectivity (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: anti HBs rises after resolution and is the basis of the hepatitis B vaccine (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"IgG anti HBc and anti HBs mark"}},{"id":"sdl15_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the complications (liver abscess, sepsis, cholangiocarcinoma, hydatid cysts), risk factors (biliary obstruction), clinical manifestations, and underlying causes (ascending cholangitis, vectors) related to bacterial and parasitic liver infections.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which organisms most commonly ascend the biliary tree to cause cholangitis and pyogenic liver abscess?","choices":{"A":"Enteric coliforms","B":"Staphylococcus aureus","C":"Group A streptococci","D":"Anaerobic clostridia","E":"Pseudomonas species"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Enteric bacteria, most commonly coliforms such as E. coli along with enterococci, ascend the obstructed biliary tree and can extend into the liver to form pyogenic abscesses. Staphylococcus aureus, group A streptococci, clostridia, and Pseudomonas are not the gut organisms the lecture names (E. coli, enterococci, Klebsiella) as ascending the obstructed tree. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 3, ascending cholangitis pathogenesis, lines 2989–2992).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the Charcot triad of cholangitis is jaundice, fever, and right upper quadrant pain (First Aid 2025 GI acute cholangitis). COMLEX: the Reynolds pentad adds altered mental status and shock to the Charcot triad (First Aid 2025 GI acute cholangitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 4 — large bile duct obstruction and cholangitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the complications (liver abscess, sepsis, cholangiocarcinoma, hydatid cysts), risk factors (biliary obstruction), clinical manifestations, and underlying causes (ascending cholangitis, vectors) related to bacterial and parasitic liver infections.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what morphologic finding characterizes cholestasis of sepsis?","choices":{"A":"Zone 3 necrosis","B":"Interface hepatitis","C":"Ground glass hepatocytes","D":"Ductular bile plugs","E":"Onion skin fibrosis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Cholestasis of sepsis shows ductular or cholangiolar cholestasis, with dark bile plugs in dilated canals of Hering and ductules at the portal parenchymal interface and little inflammation. Zone 3 necrosis, interface hepatitis, ground glass cells, and onion skin fibrosis are other patterns. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 3, sepsis and cholestasis of sepsis, lines 2998–3005).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sepsis injures the liver by direct bacterial action, hypoperfusion, or the host response to circulating endotoxin (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: complications of ascending cholangitis include sepsis and intrahepatic abscess (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 4 — large bile duct obstruction and cholangitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the complications (liver abscess, sepsis, cholangiocarcinoma, hydatid cysts), risk factors (biliary obstruction), clinical manifestations, and underlying causes (ascending cholangitis, vectors) related to bacterial and parasitic liver infections.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which imaging feature is characteristic of hydatid cysts in the liver?","choices":{"A":"Air fluid level","B":"Central stellate scar","C":"Peripheral target sign","D":"Septated hemorrhage","E":"Calcified cyst wall"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Echinococcus larval tapeworms form large hydatid cysts that characteristically show calcification of the cyst wall on imaging. A central stellate scar suggests focal nodular hyperplasia. An air fluid level, a peripheral target sign, and septated hemorrhage are not described for hydatid disease; Robbins notes the wall calcification is what may enable radiologic diagnosis, and First Aid calls it eggshell calcification. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 3, echinococcosis, lines 3016–3019); Robbins Ch. 18 (hydatid cyst wall calcification); First Aid 2025 Microbiology (Echinococcus: eggshell calcification).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hydatid cysts carry a risk of rupture, anaphylaxis, and daughter cyst spread (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: liver flukes inhabit intrahepatic bile ducts and cause hepatolithiasis (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 4 — large bile duct obstruction and cholangitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, and characteristic serological markers of autoimmune hepatitis type 1 and 2.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which autoantibodies characterize type 1 autoimmune hepatitis?","choices":{"A":"LKM1 and LC1","B":"AMA and gp210","C":"ANA and ASMA","D":"ANCA and MPO","E":"Anti CCP and RF"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Type 1 autoimmune hepatitis shows antinuclear and anti smooth muscle actin antibodies, sometimes with anti SLA or LPA, and high IgG. LKM1 and LC1 mark type 2. AMA points to primary biliary cholangitis, and ANCA and anti CCP fit other diseases. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 4, type 1 autoimmune hepatitis, lines 3048–3052).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists hypergammaglobulinemia with raised IgG in autoimmune hepatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, autoimmune hepatitis). COMLEX: type 1 autoimmune hepatitis is linked to thyroiditis, type 1 diabetes, and celiac disease (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 5 — primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, and characteristic serological markers of autoimmune hepatitis type 1 and 2.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which enzyme is the target of LKM1 antibodies in type 2 autoimmune hepatitis?","choices":{"A":"CYP2D6","B":"CYP2E1","C":"CYP3A4","D":"CYP1A2","E":"CYP2C9"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Anti LKM1 antibodies in type 2 autoimmune hepatitis are directed against cytochrome P450 CYP2D6. CYP2E1 makes NAPQI from acetaminophen, and CYP3A4, CYP1A2, and CYP2C9 are not described as autoantigens here. CYP2E1 converts acetaminophen to its reactive toxic metabolite and is not the LKM1 target. CYP3A4 is the most abundant hepatic P450, about 30% of liver P450 content, and is a major drug-metabolizing enzyme, not the LKM1 autoantigen. CYP1A2 is a drug-metabolizing isoform whose substrates include caffeine and acetaminophen, and it is not the LKM1 autoantigen. CYP2C9 is the isoform behind most pharmacokinetic warfarin interactions, not the LKM1 autoantigen. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 4, type 2 autoimmune hepatitis, lines 3056–3058).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes that autoimmune hepatitis is often positive for anti smooth muscle or anti LKM1 antibodies (First Aid 2025 GI autoimmune hepatitis). COMLEX: type 2 autoimmune hepatitis mainly affects children and adolescents (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 5 — primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"CYP2E1 converts acetaminophen to its reactive toxic metaboli","C":"CYP3A4 is the most abundant hepatic P450, about 30% of liver","D":"CYP1A2 is a drug-metabolizing isoform whose substrates inclu","E":"CYP2C9 is the isoform behind most pharmacokinetic warfarin i"}},{"id":"sdl15_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, and characteristic serological markers of autoimmune hepatitis type 1 and 2.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about what share of autoimmune hepatitis patients are female?","choices":{"A":"About 25 percent","B":"About 50 percent","C":"About 95 percent","D":"About 78 percent","E":"About 10 percent"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Autoimmune hepatitis occurs mainly in females, about 78 percent, with a genetic tie to HLA alleles such as HLA DR3 in Europeans. The other figures are not described. About 25 percent is too low; autoimmune hepatitis occurs mainly in females, about 78 percent. About 50 percent is too low; autoimmune hepatitis occurs mainly in females, about 78 percent. About 95 percent is too high; the lecture gives about 78 percent of autoimmune hepatitis patients as female. About 10 percent is far too low; autoimmune hepatitis occurs mainly in females, about 78 percent. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 4, pathogenesis and triggers, lines 3027–3031).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp describes dense lymphoplasmacytic infiltration with periportal piecemeal necrosis in autoimmune hepatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, autoimmune hepatitis). COMLEX: autoimmune hepatitis can progress to acute liver failure, cirrhosis, or hepatocellular carcinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, autoimmune hepatitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 5 — primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"About 25 percent is too low; autoimmune hepatitis occurs mai","B":"About 50 percent is too low; autoimmune hepatitis occurs mai","C":"About 95 percent is too high; the lecture gives about 78 per","E":"About 10 percent is far too low; autoimmune hepatitis occurs"}},{"id":"sdl15_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the morphological finding, clinical manifestation, associated agent, and idiosyncratic genetic susceptibility of drug and toxin-induced liver injuries.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which cytochrome converts excess acetaminophen into its toxic metabolite?","choices":{"A":"CYP3A4","B":"CYP2D6","C":"CYP2E1","D":"CYP2C9","E":"CYP1A2"},"correct":"C","explanation":"After an overdose saturates glucuronidation and sulfation, acetaminophen is shunted to CYP2E1, which forms NAPQI. CYP3A4, CYP2D6, CYP2C9, and CYP1A2 are not the isoform the lecture names for NAPQI formation. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 5, acute necrosis pathogenesis, lines 3083–3086).","boardPrep":"Step 1: induction of CYP2E1 by chronic alcohol or anticonvulsants increases acetaminophen susceptibility (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists zone 3 centrilobular necrosis in acetaminophen toxicity (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acetaminophen toxicity).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 2 — acinar zones and their injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the morphological finding, clinical manifestation, associated agent, and idiosyncratic genetic susceptibility of drug and toxin-induced liver injuries.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which feature describes idiosyncratic drug induced hepatotoxicity?","choices":{"A":"Highly predictable","B":"Not dose dependent","C":"Reproducible in animals","D":"Immediate after exposure","E":"Seen in most users"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Idiosyncratic hepatotoxicity is unpredictable, not dose dependent, occurs in only a small minority of exposed people, and has variable latency. Predictable, reproducible, rapid injury is direct intrinsic toxicity. Idiosyncratic hepatotoxicity occurs in only a small minority of exposed people; injury seen in most users would be direct intrinsic toxicity. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 5, general pathogenesis and categories, lines 3067–3074).","boardPrep":"Step 1: idiosyncratic susceptibility comes from polymorphisms in drug metabolizing enzymes, transporters, or HLA alleles (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: direct hepatotoxicity is predictable, dose dependent, and occurs shortly after exposure (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 2 — acinar zones and their injury","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Predictable, reproducible, rap","E":"Idiosyncratic hepatotoxicity occurs in only a small minority"}},{"id":"sdl15_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the morphological finding, clinical manifestation, associated agent, and idiosyncratic genetic susceptibility of drug and toxin-induced liver injuries.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which drug is listed as a cause of microvesicular steatosis from mitochondrial toxicity?","choices":{"A":"Amiodarone","B":"Methotrexate","C":"Isoniazid","D":"Oxaliplatin","E":"Valproate"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Valproate, tetracycline, high dose aspirin, and nucleoside analogs such as zidovudine cause microvesicular steatosis. Amiodarone causes steatohepatitis, methotrexate fibrosis, isoniazid acute hepatitis, and oxaliplatin sinusoidal obstruction. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 5, microvesicular steatosis, lines 3117–3120).","boardPrep":"Step 1: microvesicular steatosis presents with liver dysfunction, hypoglycemia, lactic acidosis, and encephalopathy without overt necrosis (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: methotrexate, retinoids, and enalapril are listed causes of periportal or pericellular fibrosis (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 2 — acinar zones and their injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathophysiology of hepatic injury, scarring, and repair and identify the clinical features of acute (hepatic encephalopathy, coagulopathy, portal hypertension) and chronic (pruritus) liver failure.","batch":2,"stem":"A 54-year-old man with cirrhosis has new confusion and a nonrhythmic flapping of his outstretched wrists. Which mechanism best explains these signs?","choices":{"A":"Low serum albumin","B":"Raised blood ammonia","C":"High serum bilirubin","D":"Low platelet count","E":"High portal pressure"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hepatic encephalopathy, with confusion and asterixis, comes from raised blood ammonia when hepatic urea cycle detoxification fails. Low albumin, high bilirubin, low platelets, and portal pressure do not cause asterixis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 1, hepatic encephalopathy, lines 2877–2881).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists hepatic encephalopathy and asterixis as neurologic signs of liver disease (First Aid 2025 GI cirrhosis signs). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists raised ammonia with high PT or INR and low albumin in cirrhosis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cirrhosis diagnostics).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 1 — alcohol associated and steatotic liver disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathophysiology of hepatic injury, scarring, and repair and identify the clinical features of acute (hepatic encephalopathy, coagulopathy, portal hypertension) and chronic (pruritus) liver failure.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old woman with chronic liver failure from long standing cholestasis has severe, constant itching. What most likely causes her itching?","choices":{"A":"Histamine from mast cells","B":"High serum ammonia","C":"Excess estrogen","D":"Low clotting factors","E":"Retained bile salts"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Persistent cholestasis in chronic liver failure causes profound pruritus from systemic buildup of retained bile salts and solutes. Ammonia causes encephalopathy, excess estrogen causes spider angiomas and gynecomastia, low clotting factors cause bleeding, and mast cell histamine is not the described cause. Elevated ammonia, produced mainly in the gut, is believed to cause hepatic encephalopathy, not cholestatic pruritus. Hyperestrogenemia from impaired estrogen metabolism causes palmar erythema and spider angiomas, not pruritus. Loss of hepatic clotting factor synthesis causes coagulopathy with easy bruising and bleeding, not itching. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 1, chronic liver failure and pruritus, lines 2889–2892).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists jaundice, conjunctival icterus, pruritus, and fetor hepaticus in cirrhosis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cirrhosis presentation). COMLEX: First Aid lists jaundice among the integumentary signs of liver disease (First Aid 2025 GI cirrhosis signs).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 5 — primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Elevated ammonia, produced mainly in the gut, is believed to","C":"Hyperestrogenemia from impaired estrogen metabolism causes p","D":"Loss of hepatic clotting factor synthesis causes coagulopath"}},{"id":"sdl15_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathophysiology of hepatic injury, scarring, and repair and identify the clinical features of acute (hepatic encephalopathy, coagulopathy, portal hypertension) and chronic (pruritus) liver failure.","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old man with cirrhosis has gynecomastia, palmar erythema, and spider angiomas on his chest. Which change best explains these findings?","choices":{"A":"Excess testosterone output","B":"Raised serum ammonia levels","C":"Impaired estrogen clearance","D":"Portal venous congestion","E":"Reduced clotting factors"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hyperestrogenemia from impaired hepatic estrogen clearance causes palmar erythema, spider angiomas, hypogonadism, and gynecomastia. Testosterone is not raised, ammonia causes encephalopathy, portal congestion causes ascites and varices, and low clotting factors cause bleeding. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 1, chronic liver failure, lines 2892–2894).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists gynecomastia, hypogonadism, anovulation, and palmar erythema in cirrhosis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cirrhosis presentation). COMLEX: First Aid lists spider angiomas and palmar erythema among the integumentary signs of liver disease (First Aid 2025 GI cirrhosis signs).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 1 — alcohol associated and steatotic liver disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the serological markers and their timing, viral proteins and genome types (RNA vs DNA, positive vs negative sense, envelope), and clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses (Hepatitis A, B, C, D, and E).","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old man still has detectable HBsAg 8 months after an acute hepatitis illness. Which state does this finding define?","choices":{"A":"Hepatitis window period","B":"Resolved infection","C":"Acute HDV coinfection","D":"Chronic hepatitis B","E":"Prior HBV vaccination"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Persistence of HBsAg beyond 6 months defines chronic HBV infection. The window period has no HBsAg, resolved infection and vaccination show anti HBs without HBsAg, and HDV coinfection is usually a self limited acute illness. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 2, hepatitis B serological markers, lines 2927–2929).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in adults, about 5 to 10 percent of HBV infections progress to chronic hepatitis (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: perinatal HBV transmission leads to chronic infection in about 90 percent of infants (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the serological markers and their timing, viral proteins and genome types (RNA vs DNA, positive vs negative sense, envelope), and clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses (Hepatitis A, B, C, D, and E).","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman who is 30 weeks pregnant develops severe acute hepatitis during a waterborne outbreak in South Asia. Which virus is the most likely cause?","choices":{"A":"Hepatitis E","B":"Hepatitis A","C":"Hepatitis B","D":"Hepatitis C","E":"Hepatitis D"},"correct":"A","explanation":"HEV spreads by the fecal oral route in waterborne epidemics, and acute HEV in pregnant women carries a mortality approaching 20 percent. HAV is also fecal oral, but the lecture ties this pregnancy mortality to HEV, and HBV, HCV, and HDV are parenteral. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 2, hepatitis E virus, lines 2975–2978).","boardPrep":"Step 1: HEV is a nonenveloped positive sense RNA virus in the Hepeviridae family (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: chronic HEV occurs only in immunocompromised hosts such as organ transplant recipients (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 2 — liver disease in pregnancy","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"HAV is also fecal oral, but th","C":"HAV is also fecal oral, but th","D":"HAV is also fecal oral, but th","E":"HAV is also fecal oral, but th"}},{"id":"sdl15_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the serological markers and their timing, viral proteins and genome types (RNA vs DNA, positive vs negative sense, envelope), and clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses (Hepatitis A, B, C, D, and E).","batch":2,"stem":"A 39-year-old man who is a long standing HBsAg carrier and injects drugs develops a new acute hepatitis, and IgM anti HDV is positive. What outcome is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Rapid HBsAg clearance","B":"Lifelong HDV immunity","C":"Mild self limited illness","D":"Clearance of both viruses","E":"Chronic HDV infection"},"correct":"E","explanation":"HDV superinfection of a chronic HBsAg carrier leads to chronic HDV in more than 80 percent of cases, with rapid progression to severe hepatitis, cirrhosis, and HCC. Coinfection with HBV and HDV at the same time is the usually self limited pattern. Lifelong HDV immunity and clearance of both viruses are unlikely, because the carrier's persistent HBsAg supplies the envelope HDV needs, and anti HDV and HBsAg persist in chronic delta hepatitis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 2, hepatitis D virus, lines 2960–2964).","boardPrep":"Step 1: HDV encodes a single internal polypeptide, the delta antigen (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: anti HDV and HBsAg persist in chronic delta hepatitis (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Lifelong HDV immunity and clea"}},{"id":"sdl15_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the complications (liver abscess, sepsis, cholangiocarcinoma, hydatid cysts), risk factors (biliary obstruction), clinical manifestations, and underlying causes (ascending cholangitis, vectors) related to bacterial and parasitic liver infections.","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old woman has fever, right upper quadrant pain, and jaundice, and enteric bacteria grow from her bile. Which condition sets the stage for this infection?","choices":{"A":"Portal vein thrombosis","B":"Biliary obstruction","C":"Right heart failure","D":"Alcohol overuse","E":"Hepatic artery spasm"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Extrahepatic or intrahepatic biliary obstruction from stones, strictures, or tumors creates stagnant bile that lets bacteria multiply and ascend, causing ascending cholangitis. Portal thrombosis, heart failure, alcohol, and arterial spasm are not the described setup. Portal vein thrombosis obstructs portal inflow and causes portal hypertension with congestive splenomegaly, not infection of stagnant bile. Right heart failure causes passive hepatic congestion with centrilobular necrosis, not ascending bacterial infection of bile. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 3, ascending cholangitis cause and risk factors, lines 2985–2988).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp explains that biliary obstruction impairs the mechanisms that keep bacteria out of the biliary tree (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute cholangitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists conjunctival icterus, pale stools, and dark urine in acute cholangitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute cholangitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — large bile duct obstruction and cholangitis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Portal vein thrombosis obstructs portal inflow and causes po","C":"Right heart failure causes passive hepatic congestion with c"}},{"id":"sdl15_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the complications (liver abscess, sepsis, cholangiocarcinoma, hydatid cysts), risk factors (biliary obstruction), clinical manifestations, and underlying causes (ascending cholangitis, vectors) related to bacterial and parasitic liver infections.","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old man from Southeast Asia often eats undercooked freshwater fish and has flukes in his intrahepatic bile ducts. Which tumor is he at greatly increased risk for?","choices":{"A":"Hepatocellular carcinoma","B":"Gallbladder adenoma","C":"Hepatic angiosarcoma","D":"Pancreatic cystadenoma","E":"Cholangiocarcinoma"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Opisthorchis and Clonorchis flukes live in intrahepatic bile ducts and cause chronic biliary inflammation, hepatolithiasis, and a dramatically increased risk of cholangiocarcinoma. Flukes raise the risk of bile duct cancer rather than hepatocellular carcinoma, gallbladder adenoma, or pancreatic cystadenoma, and hepatic angiosarcoma is linked to vinyl chloride, arsenic, and Thorotrast. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 3, liver flukes, lines 3012–3015); Robbins Ch. 18 (angiosarcoma: vinyl chloride, arsenic, Thorotrast).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists Clonorchis sinensis infection as a risk factor for cholangiocarcinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholangiocarcinoma). COMLEX: First Aid lists liver fluke infection such as Clonorchis as a cholangiocarcinoma risk factor (First Aid 2025 GI biliary tumors).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 5 — HCC versus other hepatic malignancies","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the complications (liver abscess, sepsis, cholangiocarcinoma, hydatid cysts), risk factors (biliary obstruction), clinical manifestations, and underlying causes (ascending cholangitis, vectors) related to bacterial and parasitic liver infections.","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old man has bloody dysentery and weeks later a liver abscess that drains thick anchovy paste fluid. Which organism most likely caused it?","choices":{"A":"Entamoeba histolytica","B":"Echinococcus granulosus","C":"Schistosoma mansoni","D":"Clonorchis sinensis","E":"Giardia lamblia"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Entamoeba histolytica trophozoites breach the colonic mucosa, causing amebic dysentery, and travel by portal blood to form liver abscesses full of necrotic anchovy paste debris. Echinococcus forms hydatid cysts, Schistosoma causes portal fibrosis, Clonorchis lives in bile ducts, and Giardia does not invade. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 3, amebiasis, lines 3020–3023).","boardPrep":"Step 1: amebiasis spreads by the fecal oral route (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: bacteria can also reach the liver by portal hematogenous spread or direct extension (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 1 — portal venous flow that carries gut organisms to the liver","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, and characteristic serological markers of autoimmune hepatitis type 1 and 2.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman with thyroiditis has chronic liver inflammation with high IgG and circulating autoantibodies. Which histologic feature is most characteristic of her liver disease?","choices":{"A":"Onion skin fibrosis","B":"Florid duct lesion","C":"Ground glass hepatocytes","D":"Interface hepatitis","E":"Zone 3 necrosis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Autoimmune hepatitis shows prominent interface hepatitis, dense lymphoplasmacytic infiltrates with plasma cell clusters, emperipolesis, and hepatocyte rosettes. Onion skin fibrosis fits PSC, the florid duct lesion fits PBC, ground glass cells fit chronic HBV, and zone 3 necrosis fits toxic or ischemic injury. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 4, morphologic features, lines 3036–3042).","boardPrep":"Step 1: emperipolesis is intact lymphocytes or plasma cells engulfed within hepatocytes (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: First Aid describes a portal and periportal lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate in autoimmune hepatitis (First Aid 2025 GI autoimmune hepatitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 5 — primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, and characteristic serological markers of autoimmune hepatitis type 1 and 2.","batch":2,"stem":"A 12-year-old girl has autoimmune hepatitis with anti LKM1 antibodies and no ANA. Which other autoantibody may also be present?","choices":{"A":"Anti mitochondrial","B":"Anti liver cytosol 1","C":"Anti smooth muscle","D":"Anti neutrophil cytoplasm","E":"Anti tissue transglutaminase"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Type 2 autoimmune hepatitis shows anti LKM1 and or anti liver cytosol 1 antibodies, with ANA and smooth muscle antibodies typically absent. Anti mitochondrial antibodies fit PBC, anti smooth muscle fits type 1, and the other antibodies fit vasculitis and celiac disease. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 4, type 2 autoimmune hepatitis, lines 3054–3059).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists ANA and anti smooth muscle in type 1 and anti LKM1 in type 2 autoimmune hepatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, autoimmune hepatitis). COMLEX: serum IgG is elevated in both types of autoimmune hepatitis (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 5 — primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Anti mitochondrial antibodies ","E":"Anti mitochondrial antibodies "}},{"id":"sdl15_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, and characteristic serological markers of autoimmune hepatitis type 1 and 2.","batch":2,"stem":"A 42-year-old woman of European descent develops autoimmune hepatitis after a course of minocycline. Which HLA allele is most linked to her disease?","choices":{"A":"HLA B27","B":"HLA DQ2","C":"HLA DR3","D":"HLA B51","E":"HLA DR2"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Autoimmune hepatitis is linked to HLA DR3 in Europeans, HLA DR4 in Japanese, and HLA DRB1 in South Americans, and drugs such as minocycline are proposed triggers. HLA B27, DQ2, B51, and DR2 are tied to other diseases. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 4, pathogenesis and triggers, lines 3029–3033).","boardPrep":"Step 1: proposed triggers of autoimmune hepatitis include viral infections, drugs, and vaccinations (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: CD4 helper T cells drive B cells to make autoantibodies in autoimmune hepatitis (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 5 — associated conditions and characteristic serum antibodies of primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the morphological finding, clinical manifestation, associated agent, and idiosyncratic genetic susceptibility of drug and toxin-induced liver injuries.","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old woman takes a large acetaminophen overdose and two days later has very high aminotransferases. Depletion of which protective molecule allows the toxic metabolite to injure her hepatocytes?","choices":{"A":"Glutathione","B":"Glucuronic acid","C":"Sulfate stores","D":"Cytochrome P450","E":"Bilirubin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Excess NAPQI depletes glutathione and then binds cellular proteins, damaging mitochondria and killing zone 3 hepatocytes. Glucuronidation and sulfation are the normal pathways that are saturated, cytochrome P450 makes NAPQI, and bilirubin is not protective here. Most acetaminophen is excreted as glucuronate or sulfate conjugates; injury follows depletion of glutathione, which normally conjugates NAPQI. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 5, acute necrosis pathogenesis, lines 3083–3089).","boardPrep":"Step 1: acetaminophen is the most common cause of acute liver failure requiring transplantation in the United States (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: Bootcamp states that depleted glutathione raises NAPQI and causes hepatocyte necrosis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acetaminophen toxicity).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 2 — acinar zones and their injury","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Glucuronidation and sulfation ","B":"Most acetaminophen is excreted as glucuronate or sulfate con"}},{"id":"sdl15_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the morphological finding, clinical manifestation, associated agent, and idiosyncratic genetic susceptibility of drug and toxin-induced liver injuries.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man had high dose chemotherapy for a bone marrow transplant and now has tender hepatomegaly, weight gain, ascites, and rising bilirubin. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Budd Chiari syndrome","B":"Portal vein thrombosis","C":"Veno occlusive disease","D":"Acute viral hepatitis","E":"Drug induced cholestasis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Sinusoidal obstruction syndrome, or veno occlusive disease, follows high dose cytotoxic drugs, with toxic sinusoidal endothelial injury and fibrous obliteration of terminal hepatic venules. Budd Chiari involves the large hepatic veins. Portal vein thrombosis mainly causes abdominal pain and portal hypertension with varices, and drug induced bland cholestasis presents with pruritus, jaundice, and a high alkaline phosphatase rather than tender hepatomegaly, weight gain, and ascites after transplant. Acute viral hepatitis does not follow high dose chemotherapy; this picture is sinusoidal obstruction syndrome from toxic sinusoidal endothelial injury with fibrous obliteration of terminal hepatic venules. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 5, sinusoidal obstruction syndrome, lines 3137–3146); Robbins Ch. 18 (portal vein obstruction).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pyrrolizidine alkaloids in bush tea also cause sinusoidal obstruction syndrome (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: azathioprine, oxaliplatin, and cyclophosphamide are listed causes of sinusoidal obstruction syndrome (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 1 — circulatory disorders of the liver","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Acute viral hepatitis does not follow high dose chemotherapy"}},{"id":"sdl15_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the morphological finding, clinical manifestation, associated agent, and idiosyncratic genetic susceptibility of drug and toxin-induced liver injuries.","batch":2,"stem":"A 33-year-old man taking isoniazid for latent tuberculosis develops fever, rash, eosinophilia, and jaundice with ALT more than 5 times normal. Which injury pattern is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Microvesicular steatosis","B":"Zone 3 confluent necrosis","C":"Bland canalicular cholestasis","D":"Acute inflammatory hepatitis","E":"Sinusoidal endothelial injury"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Isoniazid, nitrofurantoin, phenytoin, methyldopa, and checkpoint inhibitors cause idiosyncratic acute inflammatory hepatitis with lymphoplasmacytic inflammation, fever, rash, eosinophilia, and ALT at least 5 times normal. Microvesicular steatosis follows valproate, tetracycline, high dose aspirin, or zidovudine; bland canalicular cholestasis follows oral contraceptives and anabolic steroids; sinusoidal endothelial injury follows high dose cytotoxic chemotherapy; and zone 3 confluent necrosis is the acetaminophen pattern. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 5, acute inflammatory hepatitis, lines 3108–3116).","boardPrep":"Step 1: immune checkpoint inhibitors are listed causes of idiosyncratic acute inflammatory hepatitis (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes eosinophilia with halothane induced liver injury (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, inhaled anesthetic toxicity).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 2 — zones of the liver acinus and their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl15_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathophysiology of hepatic injury, scarring, and repair and identify the clinical features of acute (hepatic encephalopathy, coagulopathy, portal hypertension) and chronic (pruritus) liver failure.","batch":3,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman with acute liver failure has easy bruising and a prolonged prothrombin time. What is the main cause of her bleeding tendency?","choices":{"A":"Low clotting factor output","B":"Platelet antibody destruction","C":"Dietary vitamin C deficiency","D":"Excess heparin release","E":"Bone marrow aplasia"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Coagulopathy in liver failure comes from defective hepatic synthesis of clotting factors II, V, VII, IX, X, XI, XII, and fibrinogen and from failure to clear activated factors. Platelet antibodies, vitamin C deficiency, heparin, and marrow aplasia are not the described cause. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 1, coagulopathy, lines 2882–2885).","boardPrep":"Step 1: liver failure coagulopathy predisposes to easy bruising, severe bleeding, and DIC (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists raised PT or INR and low platelets in cirrhosis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cirrhosis diagnostics).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 1 — complications of alcohol-associated liver disease and MASLD","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl15_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the serological markers and their timing, viral proteins and genome types (RNA vs DNA, positive vs negative sense, envelope), and clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses (Hepatitis A, B, C, D, and E).","batch":3,"stem":"A 23-year-old man who injects drugs has chronic hepatitis C and starts a drug aimed at the viral RNA dependent RNA polymerase. Which viral protein does it target?","choices":{"A":"NS3","B":"NS5A","C":"NS5B","D":"NS4A","E":"Core"},"correct":"C","explanation":"NS5B is the HCV RNA dependent RNA polymerase, and its high error rate creates genotypes and quasispecies. NS3 and NS4A form the protease, NS5A is part of the replication complex, and core is structural. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 2, hepatitis C viral proteins, lines 2938–2942).","boardPrep":"Step 1: 80 to 90 percent of HCV infections become chronic, and about 20 percent progress to cirrhosis over 20 to 30 years (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: anti HCV antibodies appear 3 to 6 weeks after exposure, and HCV RNA appears within 1 to 3 weeks (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl15_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the complications (liver abscess, sepsis, cholangiocarcinoma, hydatid cysts), risk factors (biliary obstruction), clinical manifestations, and underlying causes (ascending cholangitis, vectors) related to bacterial and parasitic liver infections.","batch":3,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman who swam in freshwater lakes in Africa as a child now has portal hypertension and portal fibrosis without cirrhosis. Which infection is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Clonorchiasis","B":"Echinococcosis","C":"Amebic abscess","D":"Schistosomiasis","E":"Hepatic fascioliasis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Schistosoma spreads through freshwater with snail vectors. Adult worms live in mesenteric veins, and eggs lodge in portal venules, causing granulomas, portal fibrosis, and noncirrhotic portal hypertension. Clonorchiasis affects bile ducts, echinococcosis forms cysts, amebiasis forms abscesses, and fascioliasis is not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 3, schistosomiasis, lines 3007–3011).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists cirrhosis and schistosomiasis as separate intrahepatic causes of portal hypertension (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, portal hypertension). COMLEX: First Aid lists schistosomiasis among causes of portal hypertension (First Aid 2025 GI portal hypertension).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 4 — portal hypertension and the portosystemic collaterals that form varices","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl15_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, and characteristic serological markers of autoimmune hepatitis type 1 and 2.","batch":3,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman has autoimmune hepatitis, and her biopsy shows a dense lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate. Which cells work with CD4 helper T cells to kill the hepatocytes?","choices":{"A":"Neutrophil granulocytes","B":"Activated mast cells","C":"Resident Kupffer cells","D":"Natural killer cells","E":"CD8 cytotoxic T cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In autoimmune hepatitis, CD4 helper T cells activate B cells to make autoantibodies, while CD8 cytotoxic T cells work with CD4 cells to attack hepatocytes at the interface. Neutrophils, mast cells, Kupffer cells, and NK cells are not the described effectors. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 4, pathogenesis and triggers, lines 3033–3035).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hepatocyte rosettes are regenerating hepatocytes arranged around dilated canaliculi (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: First Aid notes autoimmune hepatitis may present with fatigue, nausea, or pruritus (First Aid 2025 GI autoimmune hepatitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 5 — primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Neutrophils, mast cells, Kupff"}},{"id":"trial_sdl15_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the morphological finding, clinical manifestation, associated agent, and idiosyncratic genetic susceptibility of drug and toxin-induced liver injuries.","batch":3,"stem":"A 7-year-old boy is given aspirin for influenza and days later has vomiting, hypoglycemia, and encephalopathy. Which liver finding is expected?","choices":{"A":"Macrovesicular steatosis","B":"Microvesicular steatosis","C":"Zone 3 confluent necrosis","D":"Portal interface hepatitis","E":"Pericellular bridging fibrosis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"High dose aspirin in children causes Reye syndrome, a mitochondrial toxicity with hepatocytes packed with tiny fat droplets around a central nucleus. Macrovesicular fat pushes the nucleus aside, zone 3 necrosis fits acetaminophen, interface hepatitis fits autoimmune disease, and bridging fibrosis is chronic. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 5, microvesicular steatosis, lines 3117–3126).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp describes Reye syndrome as mitochondrial injury with hyperammonemia, encephalopathy, and cerebral edema (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Reye syndrome). COMLEX: First Aid links Reye syndrome to aspirin given for viral infections such as VZV and influenza (First Aid 2025 GI Reye syndrome).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 1 — alcohol associated and steatotic liver disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl15_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathophysiology of hepatic injury, scarring, and repair and identify the clinical features of acute (hepatic encephalopathy, coagulopathy, portal hypertension) and chronic (pruritus) liver failure.","batch":3,"stem":"A 63-year-old man with long standing hepatitis C has a liver biopsy showing fibrous septa that encircle nodules of surviving hepatocytes. What does this pattern define?","choices":{"A":"Cirrhosis","B":"Steatosis","C":"Cholestasis","D":"Hemosiderosis","E":"Amyloidosis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Chronic persistent injury causes ongoing parenchymal loss and fibrous septa that encircle surviving hepatocytes, forming the regenerative nodules that define cirrhosis. Steatosis is fat, cholestasis is bile buildup, and hemosiderosis and amyloidosis are deposition diseases. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 1, regeneration versus cirrhosis, lines 2866–2870).","boardPrep":"Step 1: after acute uncomplicated injury or partial hepatectomy, hepatocytes regenerate without scarring (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: if the injury stops, metalloproteinases can partly degrade fibrous bands (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":16,"title":"SDL 16 — Pathology - Steatotic, Inherited, and Cholestatic Liver Diseases","questions":[{"id":"sdl16_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate alcohol-associated liver disease from metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD) based on risk factors, pathogenesis, histologic findings (chicken wire, Mallory hyaline), complications, and AST:ALT ratio.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about how much daily alcohol intake for 10 to 20 years is the usual threshold for alcohol associated liver disease?","choices":{"A":"At least 8 g per day","B":"At least 20 g per day","C":"At least 200 g per day","D":"At least 80 g per day","E":"At least 40 g per day"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The lecture gives 80 g or more of ethanol per day for 10 to 20 years as the usual threshold for alcohol associated liver disease, with women more susceptible at lower intake. Robbins (SDL 15-19 reading) puts it slightly differently: risk of severe injury becomes significant at 80 g or more per day, and 160 g or more per day for 10 to 20 years is frequently associated with severe injury. At least 8 g per day is far below the threshold; the lecture gives 80 g or more of ethanol per day for 10 to 20 years. At least 20 g per day is below the lecture's threshold of 80 g or more of ethanol per day for 10 to 20 years. At least 200 g per day is above the lecture's usual threshold of 80 g or more of ethanol per day for 10 to 20 years. At least 40 g per day is below the lecture's threshold of 80 g or more of ethanol per day for 10 to 20 years. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 1, risk factors, lines 3149–3154).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists macrovesicular steatosis, neutrophils, ballooning, and Mallory Denk bodies in alcohol induced hepatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, alcohol induced hepatitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp adds pericellular and sinusoidal fibrosis in advanced alcohol induced hepatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, alcohol induced hepatitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"At least 8 g per day is far below the threshold; the lecture","B":"At least 20 g per day is below the lecture's threshold of 80","C":"At least 200 g per day is above the lecture's usual threshol","E":"At least 40 g per day is below the lecture's threshold of 80"}},{"id":"sdl16_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate alcohol-associated liver disease from metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD) based on risk factors, pathogenesis, histologic findings (chicken wire, Mallory hyaline), complications, and AST:ALT ratio.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which proteins make up the Mallory hyaline seen in ballooned hepatocytes?","choices":{"A":"Keratin 8 and 18","B":"Desmin and vimentin","C":"Actin and myosin","D":"Tau and amyloid beta","E":"Collagen types I and III"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Mallory hyaline, or Mallory Denk bodies, are tangled clumps of the intermediate filaments keratin 8 and 18 in ballooned hepatocytes. Desmin, vimentin, actin, myosin, tau, amyloid beta, and collagen are not its components. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 1, histologic findings, lines 3163–3171).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp gives an AST to ALT ratio above 1.5 to 1 with values under 500 U/L in alcohol induced hepatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, alcohol induced hepatitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists raised GGT, bilirubin, and ferritin with leukocytosis in alcohol induced hepatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, alcohol induced hepatitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate alcohol-associated liver disease from metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD) based on risk factors, pathogenesis, histologic findings (chicken wire, Mallory hyaline), complications, and AST:ALT ratio.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what AST to ALT ratio is typical of metabolic dysfunction associated steatotic liver disease?","choices":{"A":"2 or greater","B":"About 1.5","C":"Less than 1","D":"Above 5","E":"Between 3 and 4"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In MASLD the ALT usually exceeds the AST, so the AST to ALT ratio is less than 1. A ratio of 2 or more points to alcohol associated disease. Ratios of about 1.5, 3 to 4, or above 5 all exceed 1, and the last two fall in the alcohol range. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 1, AST to ALT ratio, lines 3176–3181).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp gives MASLD aminotransferases under 500 U/L with an AST to ALT ratio below 1 (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, MASLD). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists central obesity, type 2 diabetes, hypertension, high triglycerides, and low HDL as MASLD causes (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, MASLD).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A ratio of 2 or more points to","B":"Ratios of about 1.5, 3 to 4, o"}},{"id":"sdl16_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the genetic mutations, pathogenesis, morphological hallmarks, and clinical features of inherited metabolic liver diseases (hemochromatosis, Wilson disease, and α-antitrypsin deficiency).","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which HFE mutation is the most frequent cause of hereditary hemochromatosis?","choices":{"A":"H63D","B":"C282Y","C":"G551D","D":"F508del","E":"V600E"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hereditary hemochromatosis is most often an autosomal recessive HFE mutation on chromosome 6, most frequently the C282Y cysteine to tyrosine change. H63D is a less frequent HFE variant, and the others are CFTR and BRAF mutations. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 2, hereditary hemochromatosis, lines 3185–3190).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists Vibrio vulnificus, Yersinia enterocolitica, and Listeria as infections favored by iron overload (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hereditary hemochromatosis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists therapeutic phlebotomy, deferoxamine, deferasirox, and deferiprone as treatment (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hereditary hemochromatosis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"H63D is a less frequent HFE va","D":"H63D is a less frequent HFE va","E":"H63D is a less frequent HFE va"}},{"id":"sdl16_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the genetic mutations, pathogenesis, morphological hallmarks, and clinical features of inherited metabolic liver diseases (hemochromatosis, Wilson disease, and α-antitrypsin deficiency).","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which gene and chromosome are mutated in Wilson disease?","choices":{"A":"HFE on chromosome 6","B":"ATP7A on the X chromosome","C":"SERPINA1 on chromosome 14","D":"HAMP on chromosome 19","E":"ATP7B on chromosome 13"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Wilson disease is an autosomal recessive loss of function mutation in ATP7B on chromosome 13, a hepatocyte copper transporting ATPase. HFE causes hemochromatosis, SERPINA1 causes AAT deficiency, HAMP encodes hepcidin, and ATP7A is a different copper transporter. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 2, Wilson disease, lines 3213–3217).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists Fanconi syndrome as a complication of Wilson disease (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Wilson disease). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists a low copper diet, penicillamine, trientine, and zinc salts as management (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Wilson disease).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the genetic mutations, pathogenesis, morphological hallmarks, and clinical features of inherited metabolic liver diseases (hemochromatosis, Wilson disease, and α-antitrypsin deficiency).","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what circulating AAT level do PiZZ homozygotes have?","choices":{"A":"About 10 percent of normal","B":"About 50 percent of normal","C":"About 30 percent of normal","D":"About 1 percent of normal","E":"About 75 percent of normal"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AAT deficiency is an autosomal codominant SERPINA1 disorder, and PiZZ homozygotes have circulating AAT levels only about 10 percent of normal. About 50 percent is too high; PiZZ homozygotes have circulating AAT of only about 10 percent of normal. About 30 percent is too high; PiZZ homozygotes have circulating AAT of only about 10 percent of normal. About 1 percent is too low; PiZZ homozygotes have circulating AAT of about 10 percent of normal. About 75 percent is too high; PiZZ homozygotes have circulating AAT of only about 10 percent of normal. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 2, AAT deficiency, lines 3237–3240).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp describes PAS positive, diastase resistant inclusions in periportal zone 1 hepatocytes in AAT deficiency (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, AAT deficiency). COMLEX: the lecture calls AAT deficiency the most common inherited liver disease diagnosed in infants and children (lecture SDL 16).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"About 50 percent is too high; PiZZ homozygotes have circulat","C":"About 30 percent is too high; PiZZ homozygotes have circulat","D":"About 1 percent is too low; PiZZ homozygotes have circulatin","E":"About 75 percent is too high; PiZZ homozygotes have circulat"}},{"id":"sdl16_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate hereditary unconjugated and conjugated hyperbilirubinemias based on the underlying defect, bilirubin pattern, and characteristic clinical features (Crigler-Najjar, Dubin-Johnson, and Rotor syndromes).","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what serum bilirubin level is typical of Crigler Najjar type I?","choices":{"A":"Below 3 mg/dL","B":"Above 20 mg/dL","C":"About 5 mg/dL","D":"Below 1 mg/dL","E":"About 10 mg/dL"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Crigler Najjar type I causes severe unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia, typically above 20 mg/dL. Gilbert syndrome is usually below 3 mg/dL, and type II is usually below 20 mg/dL. Values of about 5 or 10 mg/dL fit the moderate, usually below 20 mg/dL range of type II. Below 1 mg/dL is not hyperbilirubinemia; Crigler Najjar type I causes severe unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia, typically above 20 mg/dL. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 3, Crigler Najjar type I, lines 3258–3266).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp notes that phototherapy converts unconjugated bilirubin into a water soluble form excreted in urine and bile (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Crigler Najjar syndrome). COMLEX: Bootcamp states that liver transplant is curative in Crigler Najjar syndrome (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Crigler Najjar syndrome).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 6 — bilirubin production, conjugation, and causes of jaundice","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Below 1 mg/dL is not hyperbilirubinemia; Crigler Najjar type"}},{"id":"sdl16_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate hereditary unconjugated and conjugated hyperbilirubinemias based on the underlying defect, bilirubin pattern, and characteristic clinical features (Crigler-Najjar, Dubin-Johnson, and Rotor syndromes).","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about how much normal UGT1A1 activity remains in Gilbert syndrome?","choices":{"A":"About 3 percent","B":"About 10 percent","C":"About 30 percent","D":"About 70 percent","E":"About 90 percent"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Gilbert syndrome reduces UGT1A1 expression to about 30 percent of normal activity, most often from a TATA box promoter mutation. Crigler Najjar type II keeps under 10 percent, and type I has none. About 3 percent is too low; Gilbert syndrome keeps about 30 percent of UGT1A1 activity, whereas type I Crigler Najjar has none. About 70 percent is too high; Gilbert syndrome reduces UGT1A1 to about 30 percent of normal activity. About 90 percent is too high; Gilbert syndrome reduces UGT1A1 to about 30 percent of normal activity. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 3, Gilbert syndrome, lines 3275–3283).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp calls Gilbert syndrome the most common inherited hyperbilirubinemia syndrome (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Gilbert syndrome). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes that Gilbert syndrome usually presents in adolescence with a mild course (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Gilbert syndrome).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 6 — bilirubin production, conjugation, and causes of jaundice","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"About 3 percent is too low; Gilbert syndrome keeps about 30 ","D":"About 70 percent is too high; Gilbert syndrome reduces UGT1A","E":"About 90 percent is too high; Gilbert syndrome reduces UGT1A"}},{"id":"sdl16_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate hereditary unconjugated and conjugated hyperbilirubinemias based on the underlying defect, bilirubin pattern, and characteristic clinical features (Crigler-Najjar, Dubin-Johnson, and Rotor syndromes).","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which transporters are deficient in Rotor syndrome?","choices":{"A":"MRP2 transporter","B":"UGT1A1 enzyme","C":"ATP7B transporter","D":"BSEP transporter","E":"OATP1B1 and 1B3"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Rotor syndrome is an autosomal recessive deficiency of OATP1B1 and OATP1B3, impairing hepatic reuptake and storage of conjugated bilirubin. MRP2 is defective in Dubin Johnson syndrome, and UGT1A1 in Gilbert and Crigler Najjar syndromes. ATP7B is the Wilson disease copper transporter, and BSEP exports bile salts rather than bilirubin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 3, Rotor syndrome, lines 3294–3302); Robbins Ch. 18 (canalicular transporters: BSEP for bile salts).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists raised total urinary coproporphyrin in Rotor syndrome (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Rotor syndrome). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes that some conjugated bilirubin returns to the sinusoids and is taken up by downstream hepatocytes via OATP1B1 and 1B3 (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hereditary hyperbilirubinemias).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 6 — bilirubin production, conjugation, and causes of jaundice","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the etiologies, clinical features, and complications (choledocholithiasis and ascending cholangitis) of large bile duct obstructions.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which laboratory pattern accompanies large bile duct obstruction?","choices":{"A":"Isolated raised AST","B":"Raised ALP and GGT","C":"Raised lipase and amylase","D":"Raised indirect bilirubin","E":"Raised CK and aldolase"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Large bile duct obstruction raises serum alkaline phosphatase, GGT, and conjugated bilirubin, with jaundice, pruritus, dark urine, and pale stools. Indirect bilirubin, isolated AST, lipase, and muscle enzymes do not define it. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 4, clinical features, lines 3313–3318).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp notes choledocholithiasis pain usually lasts more than 4 hours (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, choledocholithiasis). COMLEX: Bootcamp adds that the pain may radiate to the back or right scapula with nausea and diaphoresis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, choledocholithiasis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 3 — ascending cholangitis, liver abscess, and sepsis in bacterial liver infections","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Indirect bilirubin, isolated A"}},{"id":"sdl16_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the etiologies, clinical features, and complications (choledocholithiasis and ascending cholangitis) of large bile duct obstructions.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which nodule pattern marks biliary cirrhosis after uncorrected choledocholithiasis?","choices":{"A":"Garland shaped nodules","B":"Hobnail surface nodules","C":"Nutmeg mottled pattern","D":"Jigsaw puzzle nodules","E":"Onion skin layering"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Uncorrected choledocholithiasis causes progressive periductal fibrosis and biliary cirrhosis with irregular jigsaw puzzle shaped regenerative nodules. Garland shaped cirrhosis is linked to PBC in the lecture, nutmeg liver is passive congestion, onion skin fibrosis is PSC, and a hobnail surface is not the lecture pattern. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 4, choledocholithiasis, lines 3320–3324).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the lecture lists surgical or ischemic strictures and PSC as adult causes of large duct obstruction (lecture SDL 16). COMLEX: the lecture lists pancreatic head adenocarcinoma and cholangiocarcinoma as malignant adult causes (lecture SDL 16).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 3 — ascending cholangitis, liver abscess, and sepsis in bacterial liver infections","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the etiologies, clinical features, and complications (choledocholithiasis and ascending cholangitis) of large bile duct obstructions.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what is the morphologic hallmark of ascending cholangitis?","choices":{"A":"Granulomas around ducts","B":"Concentric periductal fibrosis","C":"Plasma cell interface hepatitis","D":"Eosinophilic duct infiltrates","E":"Neutrophils in duct epithelium"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Ascending cholangitis shows neutrophilic infiltration within bile duct epithelium and lumens. Granulomatous duct injury fits PBC, concentric fibrosis fits PSC, and plasma cell interface hepatitis fits autoimmune hepatitis. Eosinophilic duct infiltrates are not the hallmark of ascending cholangitis, which shows neutrophils in the duct epithelium and lumens. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 4, ascending cholangitis, lines 3325–3331).","boardPrep":"Step 1: severe ascending cholangitis can progress to pyogenic liver abscesses and Gram negative sepsis (lecture SDL 16). COMLEX: the lecture lists acute on chronic liver failure and death as outcomes of severe cases (lecture SDL 16).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 3 — ascending cholangitis, liver abscess, and sepsis in bacterial liver infections","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Eosinophilic duct infiltrates are not the hallmark of ascend"}},{"id":"sdl16_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated conditions, morphology (portal inflammation, intralobular bile duct destruction, beading, onion-skin fibrosis), characteristic serum antibodies, and clinical presentations of primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about what fraction of PBC patients are women?","choices":{"A":"About 50 percent","B":"About 30 percent","C":"About 70 percent","D":"About 90 percent","E":"About 10 percent"},"correct":"D","explanation":"PBC has a striking female predilection, about 90 percent women at a 9 to 1 ratio, peaking at 40 to 50 years. PSC instead is about 70 percent male. About 50 percent is too low; PBC has a striking female predilection of about 90 percent, a 9 to 1 ratio. About 30 percent is far too low; PBC is about 90 percent women, a 9 to 1 ratio. About 10 percent would make PBC male predominant; it is about 90 percent women. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 5, associated conditions and demographics, lines 3336–3344).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists ursodeoxycholic acid and cholestyramine, then liver transplant, for PBC (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, primary biliary cholangitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists hyperpigmentation, xanthomas, and hepatomegaly in PBC (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, primary biliary cholangitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"About 50 percent is too low; PBC has a striking female predi","B":"About 30 percent is far too low; PBC is about 90 percent wom","E":"About 10 percent would make PBC male predominant; it is abou"}},{"id":"sdl16_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated conditions, morphology (portal inflammation, intralobular bile duct destruction, beading, onion-skin fibrosis), characteristic serum antibodies, and clinical presentations of primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which antigen do the antimitochondrial antibodies of PBC target?","choices":{"A":"PDC E2 subunit","B":"CYP2D6 enzyme","C":"Thyroid peroxidase","D":"Tissue transglutaminase","E":"Proteinase 3"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AMA, present in 95 percent of PBC, targets the PDC E2 subunit of pyruvate dehydrogenase. CYP2D6 is the LKM1 target, and thyroid peroxidase, tissue transglutaminase, and proteinase 3 belong to other autoimmune diseases. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 5, characteristic serum antibodies, lines 3358–3363).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists antimitochondrial antibodies with hypergammaglobulinemia in PBC (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, primary biliary cholangitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp describes a dense lymphocytic portal infiltrate with granulomas on PBC biopsy (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, primary biliary cholangitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated conditions, morphology (portal inflammation, intralobular bile duct destruction, beading, onion-skin fibrosis), characteristic serum antibodies, and clinical presentations of primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what is the lifetime risk of cholangiocarcinoma in primary sclerosing cholangitis?","choices":{"A":"About 2 percent","B":"About 50 percent","C":"About 20 percent","D":"About 80 percent","E":"About 5 percent"},"correct":"C","explanation":"PSC uniquely carries a high lifetime risk of cholangiocarcinoma of about 20 percent. About 2 percent is too low; PSC uniquely carries a high lifetime cholangiocarcinoma risk of about 20 percent. About 50 percent is too high; the lifetime cholangiocarcinoma risk in PSC is about 20 percent. About 80 percent is far too high; the lifetime cholangiocarcinoma risk in PSC is about 20 percent. About 5 percent is too low; PSC uniquely carries a lifetime cholangiocarcinoma risk of about 20 percent. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 5, clinical presentations and risks, lines 3368–3372).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp links PSC to ulcerative colitis, HLA B8, and HLA DR3 (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, primary sclerosing cholangitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists supportive care then liver transplant as PSC management (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, primary sclerosing cholangitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"About 2 percent is too low; PSC uniquely carries a high life","B":"About 50 percent is too high; the lifetime cholangiocarcinom","D":"About 80 percent is far too high; the lifetime cholangiocarc","E":"About 5 percent is too low; PSC uniquely carries a lifetime "}},{"id":"sdl16_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate alcohol-associated liver disease from metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD) based on risk factors, pathogenesis, histologic findings (chicken wire, Mallory hyaline), complications, and AST:ALT ratio.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man who drinks a fifth of whiskey daily has AST 240 U/L (reference 12–38 U/L) and ALT 100 U/L (reference 10–40 U/L), and his CK is normal. Which mechanism best explains why his AST exceeds his ALT?","choices":{"A":"Reduced renal AST clearance","B":"Bile duct leakage of ALT","C":"Selective hepatic ALT degradation","D":"Low hepatic pyridoxal phosphate","E":"Skeletal muscle AST release"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In alcohol associated disease the AST to ALT ratio is usually 2 or more because ethanol depletes hepatic pyridoxal 5 phosphate, and because ethanol injures hepatocyte mitochondria. Renal clearance, ductal leak, selective ALT degradation, and muscle release, which a normal CK argues against, are not the lecture mechanism. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 1, AST to ALT ratio, lines 3176–3181).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists raised PT or INR, low albumin, and low platelets in advanced alcohol induced hepatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, alcohol induced hepatitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists acute liver failure, cirrhosis, and HCC as complications (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, alcohol induced hepatitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 5 — drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Renal clearance, ductal leak, "}},{"id":"sdl16_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate alcohol-associated liver disease from metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD) based on risk factors, pathogenesis, histologic findings (chicken wire, Mallory hyaline), complications, and AST:ALT ratio.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman with central obesity and type 2 diabetes who does not drink has hepatomegaly and fatty liver on imaging. Which mechanism best explains the fat in her hepatocytes?","choices":{"A":"Increased VLDL export","B":"More peripheral lipolysis","C":"Excess NADH from ethanol","D":"Reduced insulin resistance","E":"Copper driven radicals"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In MASLD, insulin resistance increases lipolysis in adipose tissue, raising free fatty acid delivery to the liver, where triglyceride synthesis causes steatosis. VLDL export removes fat, ethanol derived NADH is the alcohol mechanism, and copper radicals fit Wilson disease. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 1, pathogenesis, lines 3155–3162).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp describes triglyceride and free fatty acid buildup causing ROS, mitochondrial dysfunction, and inflammation that progress to MASH (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, MASLD). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes that MASLD is often asymptomatic apart from hepatomegaly (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, MASLD).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate alcohol-associated liver disease from metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD) based on risk factors, pathogenesis, histologic findings (chicken wire, Mallory hyaline), complications, and AST:ALT ratio.","batch":2,"stem":"A 57-year-old man with heavy alcohol use has a liver biopsy compared with that of a patient with MASH. Which finding is more prominent in his biopsy?","choices":{"A":"Mainly microvesicular fat","B":"Granulomatous duct injury","C":"Periportal iron granules","D":"Lymphoplasmacytic rosettes","E":"Abundant Mallory hyaline"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Both diseases show steatosis, ballooning, and chicken wire pericellular fibrosis, but Mallory hyaline and neutrophilic infiltrates are more prominent in alcoholic hepatitis than in MASH. Fat is mainly macrovesicular, and the other findings belong to PBC, hemochromatosis, and autoimmune hepatitis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 1, histologic findings, lines 3163–3171).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists jaundice, tender hepatomegaly, anorexia, and fever in alcohol induced hepatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, alcohol induced hepatitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists hepatic encephalopathy in severe alcohol induced hepatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, alcohol induced hepatitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Fat is mainly macrovesicular, ","C":"Fat is mainly macrovesicular, ","D":"Fat is mainly macrovesicular, "}},{"id":"sdl16_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the genetic mutations, pathogenesis, morphological hallmarks, and clinical features of inherited metabolic liver diseases (hemochromatosis, Wilson disease, and α-antitrypsin deficiency).","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man has diabetes, slate gray skin, cardiomyopathy, and cirrhosis. His transferrin saturation is very high. Which mechanism best explains his disease?","choices":{"A":"Excess dietary heme iron","B":"Defective ceruloplasmin","C":"Blocked biliary iron excretion","D":"Low hepcidin synthesis","E":"Reduced ferroportin activity"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In hereditary hemochromatosis, HFE mutations impair hepatic hepcidin synthesis. Low hepcidin leaves ferroportin uninhibited in enterocytes and macrophages, so iron is hyperabsorbed. Reduced ferroportin activity would lower absorption, and ceruloplasmin defects fit Wilson disease. Hemochromatosis is not caused by excess dietary heme iron; HFE mutations lower hepcidin, leaving ferroportin uninhibited so iron is hyperabsorbed. Iron overload in hemochromatosis comes from increased absorption due to low hepcidin, not blocked biliary iron excretion. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 2, hemochromatosis pathogenesis, lines 3191–3196).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists raised iron, ferritin, and transferrin saturation with low TIBC in hereditary hemochromatosis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hereditary hemochromatosis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists chronic transfusions and chronic liver disease as causes of secondary hemochromatosis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hereditary hemochromatosis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Hemochromatosis is not caused by excess dietary heme iron; H","C":"Iron overload in hemochromatosis comes from increased absorp"}},{"id":"sdl16_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the genetic mutations, pathogenesis, morphological hallmarks, and clinical features of inherited metabolic liver diseases (hemochromatosis, Wilson disease, and α-antitrypsin deficiency).","batch":2,"stem":"A 16-year-old boy has a new tremor, depression, and Coombs negative hemolytic anemia. His serum ceruloplasmin is low. Which eye finding is most likely on slit lamp exam?","choices":{"A":"Kayser Fleischer rings","B":"Cherry red macular spot","C":"Upward lens dislocation","D":"Blue tinted sclerae","E":"Conjunctival Bitot spots"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Wilson disease leaks free copper into the blood, which deposits in Descemet membrane at the corneal limbus as green to brown Kayser Fleischer rings. Cherry red spots, lens dislocation, blue sclerae, and Bitot spots fit other disorders. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 2, Wilson disease, lines 3223–3236).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists low haptoglobin and raised indirect bilirubin with a negative direct Coombs test in Wilson disease (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Wilson disease). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists low ceruloplasmin, raised 24 hour urine copper, and raised free serum copper (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Wilson disease).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 1 — clinical features of acute liver failure","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the genetic mutations, pathogenesis, morphological hallmarks, and clinical features of inherited metabolic liver diseases (hemochromatosis, Wilson disease, and α-antitrypsin deficiency).","batch":2,"stem":"A 2-month-old boy has cholestatic hepatitis. His biopsy shows round cytoplasmic globules in periportal hepatocytes that stay pink after diastase digestion with PAS. Which mechanism best explains his liver injury?","choices":{"A":"Unchecked elastase in liver","B":"Copper buildup in cells","C":"Iron overload of cells","D":"Viral infection of cells","E":"Misfolded protein in ER"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In AAT deficiency the PiZ protein misfolds and is retained in the endoplasmic reticulum, a toxic gain of function that triggers ER stress and hepatocyte death. Unchecked elastase injures the lungs, not the liver. Copper and iron fit Wilson disease and hemochromatosis. Viral infection does not produce PAS positive, diastase resistant globules; those are retained AAT. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 2, AAT deficiency, lines 3241–3254).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp links abnormal AAT in hepatocytes to cell stress, inflammation, cirrhosis, and HCC (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, AAT deficiency). COMLEX: the lecture gives a 2 to 3 percent HCC risk in adult AAT deficiency (lecture SDL 16).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate hereditary unconjugated and conjugated hyperbilirubinemias based on the underlying defect, bilirubin pattern, and characteristic clinical features (Crigler-Najjar, Dubin-Johnson, and Rotor syndromes).","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-week-old girl has unconjugated bilirubin of 28 mg/dL (reference 0.1–1.0 mg/dL total) that persists despite phototherapy. Her hemoglobin and reticulocyte count are normal, and her direct Coombs test is negative. Which defect is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Absent UGT1A1 activity","B":"Mildly reduced UGT1A1","C":"Defective MRP2 export","D":"Absent OATP1B1 reuptake","E":"Excess heme oxygenase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Profound unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia above 20 mg/dL with hemolysis excluded fits Crigler Najjar type I, a complete loss of UGT1A1 activity that risks kernicterus. Type II has residual activity that phenobarbital can induce. MRP2 and OATP defects raise conjugated bilirubin. Overproduction of bilirubin from heme, as in hemolysis, is excluded by the normal hemoglobin and reticulocyte count and the negative Coombs test. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 3, Crigler Najjar syndromes, lines 3258–3274).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp describes persistent neonatal jaundice progressing to kernicterus in type I disease (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Crigler Najjar syndrome). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes type II is less likely to cause neonatal jaundice (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Crigler Najjar syndrome).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 6 — bilirubin production, conjugation, and causes of jaundice","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Type II has residual activity ","D":"MRP2 and OATP defects raise co"}},{"id":"sdl16_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate hereditary unconjugated and conjugated hyperbilirubinemias based on the underlying defect, bilirubin pattern, and characteristic clinical features (Crigler-Najjar, Dubin-Johnson, and Rotor syndromes).","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old woman has recurrent mild jaundice with conjugated bilirubin in her urine. Her liver biopsy shows coarse dark pigment within hepatocyte lysosomes. Which hepatocyte transporter is most likely defective?","choices":{"A":"Sinusoidal OATP1B1","B":"Basolateral NTCP","C":"Canalicular MRP2","D":"Canalicular BSEP","E":"Hepatocyte ATP7B"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Benign conjugated hyperbilirubinemia with dark lysosomal pigment and a grossly black liver is Dubin Johnson syndrome, a loss of function of the canalicular MRP2 transporter. OATP1B1 loss causes Rotor syndrome with a normal looking liver, BSEP exports bile salts, NTCP takes up bile salts, and ATP7B defects cause Wilson disease. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 3, Dubin Johnson syndrome, lines 3284–3302).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists over 50 percent direct bilirubin with a mild course in Dubin Johnson syndrome (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Dubin Johnson syndrome). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes dark granular pigment in hepatic lysosomes on biopsy (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Dubin Johnson syndrome).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 6 — bilirubin production, conjugation, and causes of jaundice","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate hereditary unconjugated and conjugated hyperbilirubinemias based on the underlying defect, bilirubin pattern, and characteristic clinical features (Crigler-Najjar, Dubin-Johnson, and Rotor syndromes).","batch":2,"stem":"A 17-year-old boy notices mild yellow eyes after fasting and exam stress. His indirect bilirubin is 2.4 mg/dL (reference 0.1–1.0 mg/dL total) and other tests are normal. Which genetic change most likely explains this?","choices":{"A":"Coding region frameshift","B":"Complete UGT1A1 deletion","C":"MRP2 nonsense mutation","D":"TATA box promoter mutation","E":"Biallelic OATP1B3 deletion"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Gilbert syndrome causes mild, fluctuating unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia under 3 mg/dL with stress or fasting, and is most often due to a TATA box promoter mutation that lowers UGT1A1 expression. Complete loss fits Crigler Najjar type I, and MRP2 or OATP changes raise conjugated bilirubin. A coding region frameshift that destroys the enzyme would fit complete loss of activity rather than Gilbert's reduced expression. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 3, Gilbert syndrome, lines 3275–3283).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp notes some Gilbert syndrome variants are exceptions to autosomal recessive inheritance (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hereditary hyperbilirubinemias). COMLEX: the lecture gives Gilbert syndrome a normal life expectancy (lecture SDL 16).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 6 — bilirubin production, conjugation, and causes of jaundice","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Complete loss fits Crigler Naj","E":"Complete loss fits Crigler Naj"}},{"id":"sdl16_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the etiologies, clinical features, and complications (choledocholithiasis and ascending cholangitis) of large bile duct obstructions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 49-year-old woman has jaundice, itching, tea colored urine, and clay colored stools from a blocked common bile duct. What best explains the color of her stools?","choices":{"A":"Raised fecal urobilinogen","B":"Excess biliverdin in gut","C":"Colonic bile salt deficiency","D":"Bacterial overgrowth in colon","E":"Absent intestinal stercobilin"},"correct":"E","explanation":"With large duct obstruction, conjugated bilirubin fails to reach the gut, so no stercobilin forms and stools turn pale or acholic, while water soluble conjugated bilirubin darkens the urine. Fecal urobilinogen falls rather than rises, biliverdin is reduced to bilirubin in macrophages long before bile, and the brown color comes from bacterial stercobilin, so neither bile salt lack nor bacterial overgrowth explains pale stools. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 4, clinical features, lines 3313–3318).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists jaundice, icterus, pale stools, dark urine, and pruritus in choledocholithiasis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, choledocholithiasis). COMLEX: the lecture explains that pruritus comes from systemic retention of bile salts (lecture SDL 16).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 6 — bilirubin production, conjugation, and causes of jaundice","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the etiologies, clinical features, and complications (choledocholithiasis and ascending cholangitis) of large bile duct obstructions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old woman with a stone in her common bile duct develops epigastric pain radiating to the back and a lipase five times normal. Which complication is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Acute cholecystitis","B":"Acute pancreatitis","C":"Perforated duodenal ulcer","D":"Hepatic abscess","E":"Ascending cholangitis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Impacted ductal stones can cause secondary acute pancreatitis, shown here by back radiating epigastric pain and a high lipase. Cholangitis, abscess, cholecystitis, and ulcer perforation do not explain the lipase. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 4, choledocholithiasis, lines 3320–3324).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the lecture lists pressure necrosis and biliary colic from impacted ductal stones (lecture SDL 16). COMLEX: Bootcamp describes choledocholithiasis as common bile duct obstruction by a gallstone with severe RUQ or epigastric pain (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, choledocholithiasis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 3 — acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the etiologies, clinical features, and complications (choledocholithiasis and ascending cholangitis) of large bile duct obstructions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman with known gallstones has fever, chills, right upper quadrant pain, and jaundice. Which named sign cluster does she show?","choices":{"A":"Beck triad","B":"Virchow triad","C":"Charcot triad","D":"Whipple triad","E":"Reynolds pentad"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Fever with chills, right upper quadrant pain, and jaundice form the Charcot triad of ascending cholangitis, usually from enteric coliforms such as E coli, Enterococcus, and Klebsiella. Reynolds pentad adds altered mental status and shock, which she lacks. Beck triad (hypotension, distended neck veins, distant heart sounds) is cardiac tamponade, not fever with RUQ pain and jaundice. Virchow triad (stasis, endothelial injury, hypercoagulability) predicts thrombosis, not fever, RUQ pain, and jaundice. Whipple triad (episodic hypoglycemia relieved by glucose) is a pancreatic islet tumor presentation, not fever with RUQ pain and jaundice. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 4, ascending cholangitis, lines 3325–3331); First Aid 2025 GI acute cholangitis (fa_gastro_cache 2267–2268).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the lecture names E coli, Enterococcus, and Klebsiella as the usual organisms of ascending cholangitis (lecture SDL 16). COMLEX: the lecture describes cholangitis as infection of a stagnant, obstructed biliary tree (lecture SDL 16).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 3 — ascending cholangitis, liver abscess, and sepsis in bacterial liver infections","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Beck triad (hypotension, distended neck veins, distant heart","B":"Virchow triad (stasis, endothelial injury, hypercoagulabilit","D":"Whipple triad (episodic hypoglycemia relieved by glucose) is"}},{"id":"sdl16_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated conditions, morphology (portal inflammation, intralobular bile duct destruction, beading, onion-skin fibrosis), characteristic serum antibodies, and clinical presentations of primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old woman with autoimmune thyroiditis has fatigue, intense itching, and a high ALP. Her antimitochondrial antibody is positive. Which finding is most likely on liver biopsy?","choices":{"A":"Florid duct lesion","B":"Onion skin fibrosis","C":"Interface hepatitis","D":"Bile duct beading","E":"Ground glass cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"PBC is T cell mediated destruction of small and medium intrahepatic bile ducts, with the florid duct lesion of granulomatous, lymphoplasmacytic duct destruction. Onion skin fibrosis and beading fit PSC, interface hepatitis fits autoimmune hepatitis (lecture SDL 15, line 3036), and ground glass cells are not a PBC finding. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 5, morphology and duct lesions, lines 3345–3351).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists raised cholesterol and triglycerides with markedly raised ALP in PBC (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, primary biliary cholangitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp links PBC to autoimmune conditions such as Hashimoto thyroiditis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, primary biliary cholangitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated conditions, morphology (portal inflammation, intralobular bile duct destruction, beading, onion-skin fibrosis), characteristic serum antibodies, and clinical presentations of primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man with ulcerative colitis has a raised ALP on routine labs, and MRCP shows strictures alternating with dilations of his bile ducts. Which antibody is most likely positive?","choices":{"A":"Positive AMA","B":"Positive pANCA","C":"Positive ASMA","D":"Positive anti LKM1","E":"Positive anti dsDNA"},"correct":"B","explanation":"PSC, linked to ulcerative colitis and showing a beaded biliary tree, is pANCA positive in about 65 percent, while AMA is characteristically negative. Anti smooth muscle and anti LKM1 fit autoimmune hepatitis, and anti dsDNA fits lupus. AMA is characteristically negative in PSC; a positive AMA marks PBC. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 5, characteristic serum antibodies, lines 3358–3363).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp describes concentric periductal onion skin fibrosis on PSC histopathology (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, primary sclerosing cholangitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes that ERCP shows similar findings to MRCP and allows stent placement (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, primary sclerosing cholangitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"AMA is characteristically negative in PSC; a positive AMA ma","C":"Anti smooth muscle and anti LK"}},{"id":"sdl16_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated conditions, morphology (portal inflammation, intralobular bile duct destruction, beading, onion-skin fibrosis), characteristic serum antibodies, and clinical presentations of primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman with PBC for many years now has high serum cholesterol. Which skin finding is she most likely to develop?","choices":{"A":"Pretibial myxedema","B":"Malar rash","C":"Xanthelasmas","D":"Erythema nodosum","E":"Heliotrope rash"},"correct":"C","explanation":"PBC progresses over years to hypercholesterolemia, xanthelasmas, jaundice, steatorrhea, osteomalacia, and biliary cirrhosis. Pretibial myxedema fits Graves disease, the malar rash fits lupus, erythema nodosum fits sarcoidosis or IBD, and the heliotrope rash fits dermatomyositis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 5, clinical presentations and risks, lines 3364–3367).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the lecture lists osteomalacia and steatorrhea late in PBC (lecture SDL 16). COMLEX: the lecture describes PSC as often found by a raised ALP during ulcerative colitis screening (lecture SDL 16).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl16_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate alcohol-associated liver disease from metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD) based on risk factors, pathogenesis, histologic findings (chicken wire, Mallory hyaline), complications, and AST:ALT ratio.","batch":3,"stem":"A 61-year-old man with obesity, hypertension, and type 2 diabetes has biopsy proven steatohepatitis without alcohol use. Beyond liver failure and cancer, which outcome is his disease additionally tied to?","choices":{"A":"Cardiovascular death","B":"Pulmonary embolism death","C":"Renal failure death","D":"Hemorrhagic stroke death","E":"Sepsis death"},"correct":"A","explanation":"MASLD and MASH progress like alcohol associated disease to fibrosis, cirrhosis, and HCC, and are additionally associated with high cardiovascular mortality from the underlying metabolic syndrome. The lecture ties no other extrahepatic cause of death to MASLD. The lecture ties MASLD to high cardiovascular mortality from the metabolic syndrome, not to pulmonary embolism death. The lecture ties MASLD to cardiovascular mortality, not to renal failure death. The lecture ties MASLD to cardiovascular mortality, not to hemorrhagic stroke death. The lecture ties MASLD to cardiovascular mortality, not to sepsis death. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 1, complications, lines 3172–3175).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp names the inflammatory stage of MASLD metabolic dysfunction associated steatohepatitis, or MASH (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, MASLD). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists raised GGT, bilirubin, and ferritin in MASH (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, MASLD).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The lecture ties MASLD to high cardiovascular mortality from","C":"The lecture ties MASLD to cardiovascular mortality, not to r","D":"The lecture ties MASLD to cardiovascular mortality, not to h","E":"The lecture ties MASLD to cardiovascular mortality, not to s"}},{"id":"trial_sdl16_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the genetic mutations, pathogenesis, morphological hallmarks, and clinical features of inherited metabolic liver diseases (hemochromatosis, Wilson disease, and α-antitrypsin deficiency).","batch":3,"stem":"A 47-year-old man with diabetes, bronze skin, and cirrhosis has a liver biopsy showing golden yellow granules in periportal hepatocytes. Which stain best highlights this pigment?","choices":{"A":"PAS with diastase","B":"Rhodanine copper","C":"Congo red","D":"Oil red O","E":"Prussian blue"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Hemosiderin in hereditary hemochromatosis starts in periportal hepatocytes and is highlighted intensely by Prussian blue iron stain, with no inflammation. PAS with diastase marks AAT globules, copper stains fit Wilson disease, Congo red marks amyloid, and oil red O marks fat. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 2, hemochromatosis morphology, lines 3197–3203).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists MCP 2 and 3 arthropathy and pseudogout in hereditary hemochromatosis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hereditary hemochromatosis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists amenorrhea, hypogonadism, and loss of libido from iron deposition (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hereditary hemochromatosis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl16_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate hereditary unconjugated and conjugated hyperbilirubinemias based on the underlying defect, bilirubin pattern, and characteristic clinical features (Crigler-Najjar, Dubin-Johnson, and Rotor syndromes).","batch":3,"stem":"A 9-year-old girl has lifelong unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia near 12 mg/dL (reference 0.1–1.0 mg/dL total) and residual UGT1A1 activity under 10 percent of normal. Which drug most likely lowers her bilirubin?","choices":{"A":"Penicillamine","B":"Cholestyramine","C":"Phenobarbital","D":"Phlebotomy","E":"Ursodeoxycholic acid"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Crigler Najjar type II keeps residual UGT1A1 activity, and its jaundice responds to phenobarbital, which induces the residual enzyme. Penicillamine treats Wilson disease, phlebotomy treats hemochromatosis, and Bootcamp lists cholestyramine and ursodeoxycholic acid as PBC treatment (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, primary biliary cholangitis). Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 3, Crigler Najjar type II, lines 3267–3274).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp notes conjugated bilirubin enters the bile canaliculi through MRP2 (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hereditary hyperbilirubinemias). COMLEX: the lecture gives type II a milder course with low kernicterus risk (lecture SDL 16).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 6 — bilirubin production, conjugation, and causes of jaundice","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl16_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the etiologies, clinical features, and complications (choledocholithiasis and ascending cholangitis) of large bile duct obstructions.","batch":3,"stem":"A 3-year-old girl has had three episodes of jaundice with right upper quadrant pain over the past year. Examination shows scleral icterus and a palpable right upper quadrant mass. Laboratory studies show a total bilirubin of 4.6 mg/dL (reference range: 0.1–1.0 mg/dL) and direct bilirubin of 3.5 mg/dL (reference range: 0.0–0.3 mg/dL). Ultrasound shows a normal gallbladder and a large fusiform cystic dilation of the common bile duct. Which condition is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Biliary atresia","B":"Choledochal cyst","C":"Cystic fibrosis","D":"Pancreatic pseudocyst","E":"Crigler Najjar syndrome"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The lecture lists extrahepatic biliary atresia, choledochal cysts, and cystic fibrosis as the pediatric causes of large bile duct obstruction. A choledochal cyst is a congenital dilation of the common bile duct that usually presents before age 10 with jaundice, recurrent abdominal pain, and sometimes a mass, and a fusiform dilation of the duct with a normal gallbladder fits it. Biliary atresia presents in the first weeks of life with an absent or irregular gallbladder and progresses rather than coming and going. Cystic fibrosis obstructs ducts with thick secretions and does not form a cystic dilation of the common bile duct. A pancreatic pseudocyst follows pancreatitis and lies beside the pancreas rather than forming the duct wall. Crigler Najjar syndrome raises unconjugated bilirubin without duct dilation. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 4, pediatric etiologies and clinical features, lines 3306–3318); Robbins Ch. 18 (choledochal cyst); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (biliary atresia ultrasound).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists biliary tract obstruction among the causes of conjugated hyperbilirubinemia (First Aid 2025 GI, conjugated hyperbilirubinemia). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes that ultrasound in biliary atresia shows an absent or irregularly shaped gallbladder (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, biliary atresia).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 6 — bilirubin production, conjugation, and causes of jaundice","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl16_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated conditions, morphology (portal inflammation, intralobular bile duct destruction, beading, onion-skin fibrosis), characteristic serum antibodies, and clinical presentations of primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis.","batch":3,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman has fatigue, itching, a high ALP, and a positive antimitochondrial antibody. Which associated autoimmune condition is most common in her disease?","choices":{"A":"Sjögren syndrome","B":"Ulcerative colitis","C":"Crohn disease","D":"Ankylosing spondylitis","E":"Psoriatic arthritis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"PBC is linked to Sjögren syndrome in about 70 percent, along with autoimmune thyroiditis, scleroderma, rheumatoid arthritis, Raynaud phenomenon, and celiac disease. Ulcerative colitis goes with PSC. Ankylosing spondylitis is an HLA B27 disease, not one of PBC's autoimmune associations. Crohn disease is not among PBC's listed associations (Sjögren, autoimmune thyroiditis, scleroderma, rheumatoid arthritis, Raynaud, celiac disease). Psoriatic arthritis is not among PBC's listed autoimmune associations; Sjögren syndrome is found in about 70 percent. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 5, associated conditions, lines 3336–3341); First Aid 2025 Immunology (HLA-B27: ankylosing spondylitis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the lecture notes that ANAs are present in 40 to 50 percent of PBC (lecture SDL 16). COMLEX: the lecture states PBC spares large intrahepatic and extrahepatic ducts (lecture SDL 16).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Crohn disease is not among PBC's listed associations (Sjögre","E":"Psoriatic arthritis is not among PBC's listed autoimmune ass"}},{"id":"trial_sdl16_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the genetic mutations, pathogenesis, morphological hallmarks, and clinical features of inherited metabolic liver diseases (hemochromatosis, Wilson disease, and α-antitrypsin deficiency).","batch":3,"stem":"A 35-year-old nonsmoking man has panacinar emphysema and cirrhosis. Both parents carry one abnormal copy of his mutated gene and have intermediate serum levels of its protein. Which term best describes how his alleles are expressed in serum protein levels?","choices":{"A":"Autosomal dominant","B":"X linked dominant","C":"X linked recessive","D":"Autosomal codominant","E":"Maternal mitochondrial"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AAT deficiency is an autosomal codominant SERPINA1 disorder, so each allele contributes protein and heterozygotes have intermediate levels. Robbins calls the disease autosomal recessive because clinical disease needs two Z alleles, but describes allele expression as codominant. Low circulating AAT lets elastase injure the lung, and retained PiZ protein injures the liver. Because SERPINA1 is autosomal and both parents carry it, X linked dominant and maternal mitochondrial inheritance do not fit. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 2, AAT deficiency, lines 3237–3246).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp links SERPINA1 mutation to raised elastase activity with lung tissue destruction (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, AAT deficiency). COMLEX: the lecture states PAS positive globules start in periportal hepatocytes (lecture SDL 16).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Because SERPINA1 is autosomal "}}]},{"sdlNumber":17,"title":"SDL 17 — Pathology - Circulatory, Pregnancy-Related, and Neoplastic Liver Diseases","questions":[{"id":"sdl17_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the morphology and underlying causes of circulatory disorders of the liver, including hepatic venous outflow obstruction (Budd-Chiari syndrome) and passive congestion (cardiac failure).","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, Budd Chiari syndrome is caused by thrombosis or obstruction of which veins?","choices":{"A":"One minor hepatic vein","B":"A single left hepatic vein","C":"The main portal vein","D":"Two or more major veins","E":"Scattered sublobular veins"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Budd Chiari syndrome is hepatic venous outflow obstruction from thrombosis or obstruction of two or more major hepatic veins. Portal vein thrombosis is a prehepatic problem, and a single small vein does not produce the syndrome. Budd Chiari involves two or more major hepatic veins; scattered sublobular veins and a single small vein do not produce the syndrome. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 1, Budd Chiari syndrome, lines 3376–3383).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes Budd Chiari syndrome causes congestive liver disease with hepatomegaly, ascites, varices, and abdominal pain but no JVD (First Aid 2025 GI Budd-Chiari syndrome). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists Budd Chiari syndrome and cardiac causes as posthepatic causes of portal hypertension (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, portal hypertension).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 2 — zones of the liver acinus and their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Portal vein thrombosis is a pr","E":"Budd Chiari involves two or more major hepatic veins; scatte"}},{"id":"sdl17_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the morphology and underlying causes of circulatory disorders of the liver, including hepatic venous outflow obstruction (Budd-Chiari syndrome) and passive congestion (cardiac failure).","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which disorder is a primary cause of hepatic vein thrombosis?","choices":{"A":"Hemophilia A","B":"Polycythemia vera","C":"Von Willebrand disease","D":"Immune thrombocytopenia","E":"Vitamin K deficiency"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Primary causes of Budd Chiari syndrome include myeloproliferative neoplasms such as polycythemia vera, inherited thrombophilias, antiphospholipid syndrome, PNH, HCC invading hepatic veins, pregnancy, and oral contraceptives. The other choices are bleeding disorders, not thrombotic ones. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 1, Budd Chiari syndrome, lines 3376–3383).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid links Budd Chiari syndrome to hypercoagulable states, the postpartum state, and HCC (First Aid 2025 GI Budd-Chiari syndrome). COMLEX: the lecture notes a swollen red purple liver with a tense capsule in Budd Chiari syndrome (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 2 — zones of the liver acinus and their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The other choices are bleeding","C":"The other choices are bleeding","D":"The other choices are bleeding","E":"The other choices are bleeding"}},{"id":"sdl17_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the morphology and underlying causes of circulatory disorders of the liver, including hepatic venous outflow obstruction (Budd-Chiari syndrome) and passive congestion (cardiac failure).","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what is the pericellular centrilobular fibrosis of long standing right heart failure called?","choices":{"A":"Cardiac sclerosis","B":"Bridging necrosis","C":"Hepatoportal sclerosis","D":"Chicken wire fibrosis","E":"Onion skin fibrosis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Long standing right heart failure leads to pericellular centrilobular fibrosis termed cardiac sclerosis. Bridging necrosis links lobules in severe hepatitis, hepatoportal sclerosis is a portal venous lesion, chicken wire fibrosis is the perisinusoidal pattern of steatohepatitis, and onion skin fibrosis surrounds ducts in PSC. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 1, passive congestion, lines 3389–3400).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists congestive hepatopathy and nutmeg liver with centrilobular necrosis as posthepatic findings (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, portal hypertension). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists nonselective beta blockers such as carvedilol, nadolol, and propranolol to prevent variceal bleeding (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, portal hypertension).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 2 — zones of the liver acinus and their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the pathology, clinical presentation, and morphology of preeclampsia, eclampsia, and acute fatty liver of pregnancy.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, eclampsia is preeclampsia plus hyperreflexia and which other feature?","choices":{"A":"Jaundice","B":"Seizures","C":"Hypoglycemia","D":"Hematuria","E":"Hypothermia"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Preeclampsia is maternal hypertension, proteinuria, edema, and coagulation defects, and eclampsia is preeclampsia plus hyperreflexia and seizures. Hypoglycemia is a feature of acute fatty liver of pregnancy. Mild jaundice can occur in preeclampsia but does not define eclampsia, and hematuria and hypothermia are not listed features. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 2, preeclampsia and eclampsia, lines 3405–3411).","boardPrep":"Step 1: preeclampsia can present as HELLP syndrome, with hemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, and low platelets (lecture SDL 17). COMLEX: the lecture explains preeclamptic liver injury by endothelial dysfunction, microvascular thrombosis, and vasospasm (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — morphological findings of drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the pathology, clinical presentation, and morphology of preeclampsia, eclampsia, and acute fatty liver of pregnancy.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what is the characteristic histology of acute fatty liver of pregnancy?","choices":{"A":"Periportal fibrin deposits","B":"Macrovesicular fat with ballooning","C":"Centrilobular hemorrhagic necrosis","D":"Portal lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate","E":"Diffuse microvesicular steatosis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"AFLP shows diffuse microvesicular steatosis, with hepatocytes packed with tiny fat droplets around a central nucleus that is not displaced. Periportal fibrin deposits fit preeclampsia, and ballooning with macrovesicular fat fits steatohepatitis. Centrilobular hemorrhagic necrosis is the ischemic or congestive zone 3 pattern, and a portal lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate fits idiosyncratic drug induced hepatitis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 2, acute fatty liver of pregnancy, lines 3428–3431).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the lecture gives AFLP an incidence of about 1 in 16,000 pregnancies (lecture SDL 17). COMLEX: severe AFLP shows lobular disarray, hepatocyte dropout, and cholestasis (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — morphological findings of drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the pathology, clinical presentation, and morphology of preeclampsia, eclampsia, and acute fatty liver of pregnancy.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which fetal enzyme defect underlies a subset of acute fatty liver of pregnancy?","choices":{"A":"LCHAD deficiency","B":"MCAD deficiency","C":"G6PD deficiency","D":"HGPRT deficiency","E":"CPT2 deficiency"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In a subset of AFLP cases, homozygous fetal long chain 3 hydroxyacyl CoA dehydrogenase deficiency lets toxic fatty acid intermediates cross into the mother and overwhelm maternal mitochondrial beta oxidation. The other enzyme defects are not the described cause. The lecture ties a subset of acute fatty liver of pregnancy to homozygous fetal LCHAD deficiency, not to MCAD deficiency. The lecture ties a subset of acute fatty liver of pregnancy to homozygous fetal LCHAD deficiency, not to G6PD deficiency. The lecture ties a subset of acute fatty liver of pregnancy to homozygous fetal LCHAD deficiency, not to HGPRT deficiency. The lecture ties a subset of acute fatty liver of pregnancy to homozygous fetal LCHAD deficiency, not to CPT2 deficiency. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 2, acute fatty liver of pregnancy, lines 3421–3427).","boardPrep":"Step 1: AFLP requires urgent termination of pregnancy (lecture SDL 17). COMLEX: AFLP presents late in pregnancy with bleeding, nausea, vomiting, jaundice, hypoglycemia, and encephalopathy (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — morphological findings of drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The lecture ties a subset of acute fatty liver of pregnancy to homozygous fetal LCHAD deficiency, not to MCAD deficiency","C":"The lecture ties a subset of acute fatty liver of pregnancy to homozygous fetal LCHAD deficiency, not to G6PD deficiency","D":"The lecture ties a subset of acute fatty liver of pregnancy to homozygous fetal LCHAD deficiency, not to HGPRT deficienc","E":"The lecture ties a subset of acute fatty liver of pregnancy to homozygous fetal LCHAD deficiency, not to CPT2 deficiency"}},{"id":"sdl17_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the complications, risk factors, underlying causes, and morphology of nonneoplastic mass lesions (focal nodular hyperplasia) and benign neoplasms of the liver (hepatocellular adenoma).","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which immunostaining pattern is pathognomonic of focal nodular hyperplasia?","choices":{"A":"Nuclear beta catenin","B":"Loss of LFABP staining","C":"Map like GS staining","D":"CRP and SAA staining","E":"Membranous CD31 staining"},"correct":"C","explanation":"FNH shows a pathognomonic map like pattern of glutamine synthetase, or GS, staining. Nuclear beta catenin, LFABP loss, and CRP or SAA overexpression mark hepatocellular adenoma subtypes, and CD31 marks endothelium. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 3, focal nodular hyperplasia, lines 3444–3450).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid calls FNH the second most common benign liver tumor, mostly in women aged 35 to 50 years (First Aid 2025 GI liver tumors). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes FNH becomes isodense to the surrounding liver in the portal venous phase after arterial hyperenhancement (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, additional liver masses).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — risk factors of metabolic dysfunction associated steatotic liver disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the complications, risk factors, underlying causes, and morphology of nonneoplastic mass lesions (focal nodular hyperplasia) and benign neoplasms of the liver (hepatocellular adenoma).","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which exposure is most strongly associated with hepatocellular adenoma?","choices":{"A":"Vinyl chloride exposure","B":"Chronic hepatitis B","C":"Aflatoxin ingestion","D":"Liver fluke infection","E":"Oral contraceptive use"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Hepatocellular adenoma is a benign epithelial neoplasm strongly associated with oral contraceptives, anabolic steroids, and metabolic syndrome or obesity. Vinyl chloride fits angiosarcoma, HBV and aflatoxin fit HCC, and liver flukes fit cholangiocarcinoma. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 3, hepatocellular adenoma, lines 3453–3456).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes a hepatic adenoma may regress spontaneously or rupture with abdominal pain and shock (First Aid 2025 GI liver tumors). COMLEX: Bootcamp ties adenoma rupture to a rich arterial supply and lack of stroma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, additional liver masses).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — risk factors of metabolic dysfunction associated steatotic liver disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Vinyl chloride fits angiosarco","D":"Vinyl chloride fits angiosarco"}},{"id":"sdl17_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the complications, risk factors, underlying causes, and morphology of nonneoplastic mass lesions (focal nodular hyperplasia) and benign neoplasms of the liver (hepatocellular adenoma).","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which hepatocellular adenoma subtype carries a high risk of transformation to HCC?","choices":{"A":"HNF1A inactivated","B":"Inflammatory subtype","C":"Sonic hedgehog subtype","D":"Beta catenin activated","E":"Fibrolamellar subtype"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Beta catenin activated adenomas carry activating CTNNB1 exon 3 mutations, are common in males and anabolic steroid users, and have a high risk of HCC. HNF1A inactivated adenomas have minimal risk, inflammatory adenomas mild risk, and sonic hedgehog adenomas a high bleeding risk. Fibrolamellar is an HCC variant. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 3, adenoma subtypes, lines 3457–3469).","boardPrep":"Step 1: surgical resection is indicated for adenomas in males, beta catenin mutants, or tumors 5 cm or larger (lecture SDL 17). COMLEX: sonic hedgehog adenomas carry GLI1 fusions and a high risk of intraperitoneal hemorrhage (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — risk factors of metabolic dysfunction associated steatotic liver disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the risk factors, morphology, characteristic image finding, and tumor markers associated with hepatocellular carcinoma.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which precursor lesions give rise to HCC in cirrhotic livers?","choices":{"A":"Mallory bodies","B":"Dysplastic nodules","C":"Councilman bodies","D":"Hyperplastic polyps","E":"Florid duct lesions"},"correct":"B","explanation":"HCC arises from precursor lesions such as small cell change, large cell change, and dysplastic nodules in cirrhotic livers. Mallory and Councilman bodies are injury findings, florid duct lesions fit PBC, and hyperplastic polyps are colonic. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 4, morphology and precursor lesions, lines 3482–3485).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid calls HCC the most common primary malignant liver tumor in adults and notes hematogenous spread (First Aid 2025 GI liver tumors). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists tumor seeding during liver biopsy as an HCC complication (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatocellular carcinoma).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 2 — clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the risk factors, morphology, characteristic image finding, and tumor markers associated with hepatocellular carcinoma.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which gene fusion drives fibrolamellar HCC?","choices":{"A":"TMPRSS2 ERG","B":"KIAA1549 BRAF","C":"DNAJB1 PRKACA","D":"ETV6 NTRK3","E":"RUNX1 RUNX1T1"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The fibrolamellar variant occurs in young adults without cirrhosis, is driven by a DNAJB1 PRKACA fusion, and shows oncocytic cells in dense lamellar collagen. The other fusions drive prostate cancer, pilocytic astrocytoma, infantile fibrosarcoma, and acute myeloid leukemia. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 4, fibrolamellar variant, lines 3487–3489).","boardPrep":"Step 1: HCC has a strong propensity for vascular invasion into the portal and hepatic veins (lecture SDL 17). COMLEX: HCC grossly may be green from cholestasis or yellow from fat (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 2 — clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The other fusions drive prosta","B":"The other fusions drive prosta","D":"The other fusions drive prosta","E":"The other fusions drive prosta"}},{"id":"sdl17_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the risk factors, morphology, characteristic image finding, and tumor markers associated with hepatocellular carcinoma.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which serum tumor marker is raised in advanced HCC?","choices":{"A":"Carcinoembryonic antigen","B":"Cancer antigen 125","C":"Prostate specific antigen","D":"Chromogranin A","E":"Alpha fetoprotein"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Serum alpha fetoprotein is raised in advanced HCC, although it is unhelpful for early screening and negative in fibrolamellar HCC. CEA, CA 125, PSA, and chromogranin A mark other tumors. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 4, tumor markers, lines 3495–3497).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes that HCC recurrence is monitored with serum AFP (First Aid 2025 GI liver tumors). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists weight loss, hepatomegaly, and abdominal pain in HCC (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatocellular carcinoma).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 2 — clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"CEA, CA 125, PSA, and chromogr","C":"CEA, CA 125, PSA, and chromogr"}},{"id":"sdl17_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate hepatocellular carcinoma from other hepatic malignancies (cholangiocarcinoma, hepatic angiosarcoma) by morphology, exposure associations, and associated conditions.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which exposure is historically associated with hepatic angiosarcoma?","choices":{"A":"Aflatoxin B1","B":"Liver flukes","C":"Oral contraceptives","D":"Vinyl chloride","E":"Asbestos fibers"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Hepatic angiosarcoma is historically associated with occupational vinyl chloride monomer, arsenic, and Thorotrast. Aflatoxin fits HCC, liver flukes fit cholangiocarcinoma, and oral contraceptives fit adenoma. Asbestos is linked to bronchogenic carcinoma and mesothelioma rather than liver tumors. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 5, hepatic angiosarcoma, lines 3527–3529); First Aid 2025 Pathology (carcinogens: asbestos, lung).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp notes hepatic angiosarcoma is often metastatic at diagnosis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, additional liver masses). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists PECAM 1, or CD31, positivity in angiosarcoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, additional liver masses).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 5 — associated conditions of primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate hepatocellular carcinoma from other hepatic malignancies (cholangiocarcinoma, hepatic angiosarcoma) by morphology, exposure associations, and associated conditions.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which immunostains are positive in intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma?","choices":{"A":"CK7 and CK19","B":"HepPar1 and AFP","C":"CD31 and CD34","D":"S100 and HMB45","E":"Desmin and actin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma is positive for mucin and CK7 and CK19, and negative for AFP and HepPar1. CD31 and CD34 mark angiosarcoma, S100 and HMB45 mark melanoma, and desmin and actin mark muscle. HepPar1 and AFP are negative in intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma, which is positive for mucin, CK7, and CK19. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 5, intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma, lines 3519–3520).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists raised CA 19 9 and CEA in cholangiocarcinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholangiocarcinoma). COMLEX: First Aid places the most common cholangiocarcinoma at the convergence of the right and left hepatic ducts (First Aid 2025 GI cholangiocarcinoma).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 5 — associated conditions of primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"HepPar1 and AFP are negative in intrahepatic cholangiocarcin"}},{"id":"sdl17_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate hepatocellular carcinoma from other hepatic malignancies (cholangiocarcinoma, hepatic angiosarcoma) by morphology, exposure associations, and associated conditions.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, from which cells does hepatic angiosarcoma arise?","choices":{"A":"Intrahepatic biliary epithelium","B":"Perisinusoidal stellate cells","C":"Sinusoidal endothelial cells","D":"Polygonal hepatocytes","E":"Resident Kupffer macrophages"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hepatic angiosarcoma is a rare, aggressive mesenchymal tumor of sinusoidal endothelial cells that forms hemorrhagic red purple masses. Biliary epithelium gives cholangiocarcinoma, and hepatocytes give HCC. Kupffer macrophages are not the cell of origin; the tumor stains for endothelial markers such as CD31, CD34, and ERG. Hepatic angiosarcoma arises from sinusoidal endothelial cells, not perisinusoidal stellate cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 5, hepatic angiosarcoma, lines 3521–3526).","boardPrep":"Step 1: angiosarcoma cells line and dissect along preexisting sinusoidal spaces (lecture SDL 17). COMLEX: angiosarcoma stains for CD34, ERG, and Factor VIII related antigen (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 5 — associated conditions of primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Hepatic angiosarcoma arises from sinusoidal endothelial cell"}},{"id":"sdl17_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the morphology and underlying causes of circulatory disorders of the liver, including hepatic venous outflow obstruction (Budd-Chiari syndrome) and passive congestion (cardiac failure).","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman with antiphospholipid syndrome has abdominal pain, tender hepatomegaly, and new ascites. Doppler shows thrombi in three major hepatic veins. Which liver finding is most likely on biopsy?","choices":{"A":"Periportal fibrin deposits","B":"Portal egg granulomas","C":"Microvesicular steatosis","D":"Periductal onion skin","E":"Pericentral congestion"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Budd Chiari syndrome causes marked pericentral or centrilobular sinusoidal congestion with hepatocellular necrosis, and chronic cases develop pericentral sinusoidal fibrosis. Periportal fibrin fits preeclampsia, egg granulomas fit schistosomiasis, microvesicular fat fits AFLP, and onion skin fibrosis fits PSC. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 1, Budd Chiari morphology, lines 3384–3388).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes Budd Chiari syndrome can produce a mottled nutmeg liver (First Aid 2025 GI Budd-Chiari syndrome). COMLEX: Bootcamp links posthepatic obstruction to raised sinusoidal hydrostatic pressure (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, portal hypertension).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 2 — zones of the liver acinus and their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the morphology and underlying causes of circulatory disorders of the liver, including hepatic venous outflow obstruction (Budd-Chiari syndrome) and passive congestion (cardiac failure).","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with long standing right sided heart failure dies, and his liver is examined at autopsy. Which gross appearance is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Chocolate brown liver","B":"Diffusely green liver","C":"Nutmeg mottled liver","D":"Diffusely yellow liver","E":"Jigsaw nodular liver"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Passive congestion from right heart failure gives a nutmeg liver, a mottled red tan pattern of centrilobular congestion alternating with lighter periportal tissue. Chocolate brown fits hemochromatosis, green fits cholestasis, yellow fits steatosis, and jigsaw nodules fit biliary cirrhosis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 1, passive congestion, lines 3389–3398).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in passive congestion, raised central venous pressure is transmitted back through the IVC and hepatic veins (lecture SDL 17). COMLEX: centrilobular sinusoids are dilated and filled with red cells (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 2 — zones of the liver acinus and their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the morphology and underlying causes of circulatory disorders of the liver, including hepatic venous outflow obstruction (Budd-Chiari syndrome) and passive congestion (cardiac failure).","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old woman with severe cardiac decompensation has centrilobular hepatocyte necrosis on biopsy. Which hepatocytes are most likely to remain intact?","choices":{"A":"Zones 1 and 2","B":"Central zone 3","C":"Zones 2 and 3","D":"Zones 1 and 3","E":"Midzonal zone 2"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In passive congestion, centrilobular zone 3 hepatocytes undergo ischemic atrophy and necrosis while periportal zone 1 and 2 hepatocytes remain intact. Zone 3 is the necrotic zone, so any choice that includes zone 3 is wrong. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 1, passive congestion, lines 3395–3398).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists paracentesis with a raised serum ascites albumin gradient in portal hypertension (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, portal hypertension). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists TIPS as a portal hypertension treatment (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, portal hypertension).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 2 — zones of the liver acinus and their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Zone 3 is the necrotic zone, s","C":"Zone 3 is the necrotic zone, s","D":"Zone 3 is the necrotic zone, s"}},{"id":"sdl17_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the pathology, clinical presentation, and morphology of preeclampsia, eclampsia, and acute fatty liver of pregnancy.","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman at 36 weeks of gestation has blood pressure of 162/110 mm Hg, proteinuria, edema, and right upper quadrant pain. Her AST is 310 U/L (reference 12–38 U/L). Which liver finding is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Diffuse microvesicular fat","B":"Pericentral congestion","C":"Portal plasma cell infiltrate","D":"Periportal fibrin deposits","E":"Bridging fibrous septa"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In preeclampsia, periportal sinusoids contain fibrin deposits with hemorrhage into the space of Disse, leading to periportal coagulative necrosis. Microvesicular fat fits AFLP, pericentral congestion fits venous outflow problems, plasma cells fit autoimmune hepatitis, and septa fit chronic fibrosis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 2, preeclampsia morphology, lines 3412–3414).","boardPrep":"Step 1: patients with preeclamptic liver injury have raised transaminases and mild jaundice (lecture SDL 17). COMLEX: severe coagulopathy marks advanced disease that needs prompt delivery (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 5 — morphological findings of drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the pathology, clinical presentation, and morphology of preeclampsia, eclampsia, and acute fatty liver of pregnancy.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman at 37 weeks of gestation with eclampsia suddenly develops severe right upper quadrant pain and hypotension. Which complication is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Hepatic vein thrombosis","B":"Ruptured liver hematoma","C":"Acute cholecystitis","D":"Perforated peptic ulcer","E":"Ruptured ectopic pregnancy"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In eclampsia, high pressure bleeding forms intrahepatic hematomas that can dissect beneath Glisson capsule into a subcapsular hematoma with a risk of catastrophic rupture. An ectopic pregnancy is not possible at 37 weeks of an intrauterine pregnancy. Hepatic vein thrombosis produces liver enlargement, pain, and ascites, acute cholecystitis begins with progressive pain lasting more than 6 hours with mild fever, and a perforated ulcer causes peritonitis with free air, so none fits sudden pain with shock in eclampsia. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 2, preeclampsia morphology, lines 3414–3417); Robbins Ch. 18 (Budd-Chiari syndrome; acute calculous cholecystitis clinical features); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 2, perforation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the lecture defines HELLP as hemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, and low platelets (lecture SDL 17). COMLEX: preeclampsia combines maternal hypertension, proteinuria, edema, and coagulation defects (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 5 — morphological findings of drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the pathology, clinical presentation, and morphology of preeclampsia, eclampsia, and acute fatty liver of pregnancy.","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman at 35 weeks of gestation has nausea, vomiting, jaundice, and confusion. Her glucose is 45 mg/dL (reference 70–100 mg/dL), and viral serologies are negative. Which mechanism best explains her liver disease?","choices":{"A":"Maternal autoimmune hepatitis","B":"Acute hepatitis A infection","C":"Hepatic microvascular thrombosis","D":"Canalicular bile salt retention","E":"Overwhelmed beta oxidation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Acute fatty liver of pregnancy presents in the third trimester with incipient liver failure, hypoglycemia, and encephalopathy. In a subset, fetal LCHAD deficiency sends toxic fatty acid intermediates into the mother, overwhelming maternal mitochondrial beta oxidation. Microvascular thrombosis is the preeclampsia mechanism. Canalicular bile salt retention is cholestasis, which brings pruritus and a high alkaline phosphatase rather than hypoglycemia and encephalopathy. Autoimmune hepatitis is not the cause of third-trimester liver failure with hypoglycemia; AFLP results from overwhelmed maternal mitochondrial beta oxidation, in a subset from fetal LCHAD deficiency. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 2, acute fatty liver of pregnancy, lines 3421–3435).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in AFLP, tiny fat droplets surround a central nucleus without displacing it (lecture SDL 17). COMLEX: AFLP is a rare, life threatening metabolic emergency of the third trimester (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 5 — morphological findings of drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Autoimmune hepatitis is not the cause of third-trimester liv"}},{"id":"sdl17_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the complications, risk factors, underlying causes, and morphology of nonneoplastic mass lesions (focal nodular hyperplasia) and benign neoplasms of the liver (hepatocellular adenoma).","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman has a 4 cm liver mass found incidentally on imaging. It is unencapsulated with a central stellate scar and radiating fibrous septa. Which approach is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Urgent surgical resection","B":"Conservative management","C":"Hepatic artery embolization","D":"Systemic chemotherapy","E":"Liver transplantation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A circumscribed unencapsulated nodule with a central stellate scar is focal nodular hyperplasia, a benign hyperplastic lesion with no risk of malignant transformation or spontaneous rupture, so it is usually managed conservatively. Resection, embolization, chemotherapy, and transplant are reserved for lesions such as adenomas 5 cm or larger or malignancy. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 3, focal nodular hyperplasia, lines 3440–3452).","boardPrep":"Step 1: FNH septa contain thick walled dystrophic arteries with a ductular reaction but no interlobular bile ducts (lecture SDL 17). COMLEX: First Aid notes FNH is usually asymptomatic and found incidentally (First Aid 2025 GI liver tumors).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 1 — organization of the classic hepatic lobule and hepatic blood flow","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the complications, risk factors, underlying causes, and morphology of nonneoplastic mass lesions (focal nodular hyperplasia) and benign neoplasms of the liver (hepatocellular adenoma).","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman taking oral contraceptives has a liver adenoma. Its biopsy shows marked steatosis and loss of LFABP expression. Which molecular change is most likely?","choices":{"A":"CTNNB1 exon 3 activation","B":"IL6ST activation","C":"HNF1A inactivation","D":"GLI1 gene fusion","E":"DNAJB1 PRKACA fusion"},"correct":"C","explanation":"HNF1A inactivated adenomas show loss of LFABP expression, marked steatosis, and minimal malignant risk. CTNNB1 mutations define beta catenin adenomas, gp130 activation defines inflammatory adenomas, GLI1 fusions define sonic hedgehog adenomas, and DNAJB1 PRKACA drives fibrolamellar HCC. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 3, adenoma subtypes, lines 3457–3469).","boardPrep":"Step 1: inflammatory adenomas overexpress CRP and SAA and are associated with obesity (lecture SDL 17). COMLEX: adenomas are sheets of normal appearing hepatocytes with arterial supply but no portal tracts or bile ducts (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 1 — risk factors of metabolic dysfunction associated steatotic liver disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"CTNNB1 mutations define beta c"}},{"id":"sdl17_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the complications, risk factors, underlying causes, and morphology of nonneoplastic mass lesions (focal nodular hyperplasia) and benign neoplasms of the liver (hepatocellular adenoma).","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old woman who takes oral contraceptives has sudden right upper quadrant pain and hypotension. CT shows hemoperitoneum from a bleeding liver mass. Which lesion is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Hepatocellular adenoma","B":"Focal nodular hyperplasia","C":"Simple hepatic cyst","D":"Fibrolamellar carcinoma","E":"Amebic liver abscess"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Hepatocellular adenoma is linked to oral contraceptives and is prone to spontaneous intraperitoneal hemorrhage or rupture, a surgical emergency. FNH carries no rupture risk, fibrolamellar HCC is malignant without this exposure, a simple cyst does not bleed in this way, and an abscess would bring fever. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 3, hepatocellular adenoma, lines 3453–3474).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp notes hepatocellular adenoma carries a raised risk of malignant transformation (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, additional liver masses). COMLEX: transformation to HCC is most likely with the beta catenin subtype or a size of 5 cm or more (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 1 — organization of the classic hepatic lobule and hepatic blood flow","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"FNH carries no rupture risk, f"}},{"id":"sdl17_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the risk factors, morphology, characteristic image finding, and tumor markers associated with hepatocellular carcinoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old man with chronic hepatitis B and cirrhosis has weight loss and a liver mass invading the portal vein. Biopsy shows polygonal cells in thick trabeculae with bile plugs. Which diagnosis is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Focal nodular hyperplasia","B":"Cavernous hemangioma","C":"Cholangiocarcinoma","D":"Colorectal metastasis","E":"Hepatocellular carcinoma"},"correct":"E","explanation":"HCC cells resemble hepatocytes growing in thickened trabeculae or pseudoacinar structures with bile plugs, and HCC invades the portal and hepatic veins. Cholangiocarcinoma does not produce bile, FNH and hemangioma are benign, and metastases, though the most common malignant liver tumors, are rarely solitary and would not fit HBV cirrhosis with bile plugs as well. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 4, morphology, lines 3478–3490).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp describes aflatoxin B1 forming DNA adducts that cause a TP53 G to T transversion (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatocellular carcinoma). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes metastases are the most common malignant liver tumors overall (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatocellular carcinoma).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 2 — clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Cholangiocarcinoma does not pr"}},{"id":"sdl17_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the risk factors, morphology, characteristic image finding, and tumor markers associated with hepatocellular carcinoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 23-year-old man without liver disease has a large liver mass. His serum tumor markers are unremarkable, and biopsy shows large oncocytic cells in dense lamellar collagen bundles. Which tumor is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Hepatocellular adenoma","B":"Fibrolamellar carcinoma","C":"Focal nodular hyperplasia","D":"Intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma","E":"Hepatic angiosarcoma"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Fibrolamellar HCC arises in young adults without cirrhosis, is AFP negative, and shows oncocytic cells in dense lamellar collagen. FNH has a central stellate scar rather than lamellar collagen around oncocytic cells, adenoma lacks stroma, cholangiocarcinoma forms glands in desmoplastic stroma, and angiosarcoma is vascular. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 4, fibrolamellar variant, lines 3487–3497).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes contrast CT or MRI confirms HCC after ultrasound screening (First Aid 2025 GI liver tumors). COMLEX: Bootcamp describes HCC on ultrasound as a heterogeneous, hypervascular lesion with irregular borders (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatocellular carcinoma).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 1 — pathophysiology of hepatic injury, scarring, and repair","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"FNH has a central stellate sca"}},{"id":"sdl17_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the risk factors, morphology, characteristic image finding, and tumor markers associated with hepatocellular carcinoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old farmer from rural West Africa without cirrhosis develops HCC. He ate mold contaminated stored peanuts for decades. Which molecular change most likely links this exposure to his tumor?","choices":{"A":"KRAS activating mutation","B":"APC truncating mutation","C":"BRAF V600E mutation","D":"TP53 point mutation","E":"MYCN amplification"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The dietary mycotoxin from Aspergillus on stored crops forms DNA adducts that cause a TP53 inactivating G to T transversion, lowering p53 function. Robbins notes aflatoxin acts synergistically with HBV. KRAS, APC, BRAF, and MYCN changes are not the described route. BRAF mutations are described in bile duct lesions and craniopharyngiomas, not as the aflatoxin signature in HCC. MYCN amplification is a prognostic marker in neuroblastoma, not a feature of aflatoxin-driven HCC. APC loss is the lineage-specific driver of colon carcinoma, whereas aflatoxin leaves a codon 249 TP53 signature mutation in HCC. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 4, risk factors, lines 3478–3481); Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatocellular carcinoma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists HBV with or without cirrhosis, and all other causes of cirrhosis, as HCC associations (First Aid 2025 GI liver tumors). COMLEX: HCC risk factors include cirrhosis from hemochromatosis and AAT deficiency (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 2 — clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"BRAF mutations are described in bile duct lesions and cranio","E":"MYCN amplification is a prognostic marker in neuroblastoma, ","B":"APC loss is the lineage-specific driver of colon carcinoma, "}},{"id":"sdl17_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate hepatocellular carcinoma from other hepatic malignancies (cholangiocarcinoma, hepatic angiosarcoma) by morphology, exposure associations, and associated conditions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with primary sclerosing cholangitis and ulcerative colitis has a firm gray white sclerotic liver mass. Which finding best separates it from HCC?","choices":{"A":"Mucin positive glands","B":"Bile plugs in tumor","C":"AFP positive cells","D":"HepPar1 positive cells","E":"CD31 positive channels"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma, linked to PSC, forms malignant glands in desmoplastic stroma that are mucin positive and do not make bile. Bile plugs, AFP, and HepPar1 favor HCC, and CD31 marks angiosarcoma. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 5, intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma, lines 3510–3520).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes cholangiocarcinoma usually presents late with fatigue, weight loss, abdominal pain, and jaundice (First Aid 2025 GI cholangiocarcinoma). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists the Courvoisier sign in cholangiocarcinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholangiocarcinoma).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 5 — associated conditions of primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate hepatocellular carcinoma from other hepatic malignancies (cholangiocarcinoma, hepatic angiosarcoma) by morphology, exposure associations, and associated conditions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man who worked for decades at a PVC plastics plant has an ill defined hemorrhagic red purple liver mass. Which immunostains are most likely positive?","choices":{"A":"Glypican 3 and AFP","B":"CK7 and CK19","C":"CD31 and ERG","D":"Chromogranin and CD56","E":"CD117 and DOG1"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Vinyl chloride exposure and a hemorrhagic red purple mass point to angiosarcoma, which is positive for the endothelial markers CD31, CD34, ERG, and Factor VIII related antigen. Glypican 3 and AFP fit HCC, CK7 and CK19 fit cholangiocarcinoma, and the others fit neuroendocrine and stromal tumors. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 5, hepatic angiosarcoma, lines 3521–3531).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid links hepatic angiosarcoma to arsenic and vinyl chloride (First Aid 2025 GI liver tumors). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists thorium dioxide exposure for angiosarcoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, additional liver masses).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 5 — morphological findings of drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Glypican 3 and AFP fit HCC, CK","E":"Glypican 3 and AFP fit HCC, CK"}},{"id":"sdl17_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate hepatocellular carcinoma from other hepatic malignancies (cholangiocarcinoma, hepatic angiosarcoma) by morphology, exposure associations, and associated conditions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man with chronic hepatitis C has a liver mass. Biopsy shows polygonal tumor cells in trabeculae with bile production. Which stain is most likely positive?","choices":{"A":"Mucin positive","B":"ERG positive","C":"Chromogranin positive","D":"HepPar1 positive","E":"Desmin positive"},"correct":"D","explanation":"HCC is positive for AFP, HepPar1, and glypican 3 and negative for mucin. Mucin fits cholangiocarcinoma, ERG fits angiosarcoma, and chromogranin and desmin fit neuroendocrine and muscle tumors. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 5, HCC diagnostic markers, lines 3502–3509).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists cellular atypia, pleomorphic hyperchromatic nuclei, and a trabecular pattern in HCC (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatocellular carcinoma). COMLEX: the lecture ties HCC to chronic HBV, HCV, cirrhosis, alcohol, MASLD, and aflatoxin (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 2 — clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl17_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the morphology and underlying causes of circulatory disorders of the liver, including hepatic venous outflow obstruction (Budd-Chiari syndrome) and passive congestion (cardiac failure).","batch":3,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman has had slowly developing hepatic vein obstruction from a hypercoagulable state for several years. Her liver biopsy is reviewed for chronic changes. Which finding is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Pericentral fibrosis","B":"Periportal fibrin deposits","C":"Concentric periductal fibrosis","D":"Bridging portal fibrosis","E":"Diffuse microvesicular fat"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In chronic or slowly developing Budd Chiari syndrome, pericentral sinusoidal fibrosis develops and the major hepatic veins show organizing thrombi. Periportal fibrin fits preeclampsia, periductal fibrosis fits PSC, bridging portal fibrosis links portal tracts in chronic hepatitis rather than centering on veins, and microvesicular fat fits AFLP. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 1, Budd Chiari morphology, lines 3384–3388).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists portal vein and splenic vein thrombosis as prehepatic causes of portal hypertension (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, portal hypertension). COMLEX: First Aid notes portal vein thrombosis causes no hepatomegaly unless liver disease preexists (First Aid 2025 GI Budd-Chiari syndrome).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 2 — zones of the liver acinus and their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl17_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the pathology, clinical presentation, and morphology of preeclampsia, eclampsia, and acute fatty liver of pregnancy.","batch":3,"stem":"A 33-year-old woman at 34 weeks of gestation with preeclampsia has rising transaminases, and her INR climbs to 2.1 (reference 0.8–1.2). Which step is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Plasmapheresis","B":"Liver transplant","C":"Oral corticosteroids","D":"Prompt delivery","E":"Heparin infusion"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In preeclamptic liver injury, severe coagulopathy indicates advanced disease requiring prompt delivery or termination of pregnancy. Plasmapheresis, steroids, and transplant do not remove the placental cause, and heparin would worsen bleeding with an INR of 2.1. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 2, preeclampsia clinical presentation, lines 3418–3420).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in eclampsia, intrahepatic hematomas can dissect beneath Glisson capsule (lecture SDL 17). COMLEX: periportal hemorrhage into the space of Disse leads to periportal coagulative necrosis in preeclampsia (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 1 — clinical features of acute liver failure","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Plasmapheresis, steroids, and "}},{"id":"trial_sdl17_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the complications, risk factors, underlying causes, and morphology of nonneoplastic mass lesions (focal nodular hyperplasia) and benign neoplasms of the liver (hepatocellular adenoma).","batch":3,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman has an incidental liver nodule with a central stellate scar and radiating fibrous septa. What underlies the formation of this lesion?","choices":{"A":"Portal vein thrombosis","B":"Clonal CTNNB1 mutation","C":"Chronic HBV infection","D":"Dietary aflatoxin exposure","E":"Aberrant local arteries"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Focal nodular hyperplasia is a nonneoplastic hyperplastic reaction of hepatocytes to local vascular malformations or altered arterial flow. Portal vein thrombosis is a prehepatic inflow obstruction, CTNNB1 mutations fit adenoma, and HBV and aflatoxin fit HCC. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 3, focal nodular hyperplasia, lines 3440–3443).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid describes FNH as a hyperplastic reaction of hepatocytes to an aberrant dystrophic artery (First Aid 2025 GI liver tumors). COMLEX: Bootcamp describes radiating fibrous bands from the central scar containing abnormal vessels (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, additional liver masses).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 1 — organization of the classic hepatic lobule and hepatic blood flow","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl17_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the risk factors, morphology, characteristic image finding, and tumor markers associated with hepatocellular carcinoma.","batch":3,"stem":"A 64-year-old man with cirrhosis has a liver mass that enhances brightly in the early contrast phase and washes out in the delayed phase on triphasic CT. What explains this enhancement pattern?","choices":{"A":"Portal venous supply","B":"Venous outflow block","C":"Arterial tumor supply","D":"Intratumoral fat content","E":"Central fibrous scar"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Arterial phase hyperenhancement with venous or delayed washout is the hallmark of HCC and reflects a predominant arterial blood supply from tumor neovascularization. The tumor is fed by arteries rather than the portal vein, so contrast arrives early and leaves early, and a central scar fits FNH. Intratumoral fat does not explain early arterial enhancement with washout. Venous outflow block does not explain early arterial hyperenhancement; HCC enhances brightly because of arterial blood supply from tumor neovascularization. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 4, characteristic image finding, lines 3491–3494).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp describes multiphase imaging with early arterial hyperenhancement and rapid washout in portal and delayed phases (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatocellular carcinoma). COMLEX: HCC grossly may be a unifocal mass, multifocal nodules, or a diffusely infiltrative tumor (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 2 — clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Venous outflow block does not explain early arterial hyperen"}},{"id":"trial_sdl17_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate hepatocellular carcinoma from other hepatic malignancies (cholangiocarcinoma, hepatic angiosarcoma) by morphology, exposure associations, and associated conditions.","batch":3,"stem":"A 57-year-old woman with recurrent intrahepatic duct stones has weight loss and dull right upper quadrant pain. CT shows a solitary firm mass in the left hepatic lobe with dilated ducts upstream of it. Needle biopsy shows malignant glands embedded in abundant desmoplastic stroma. Which additional histologic finding is most likely in this tumor?","choices":{"A":"Bile production by tumor cells","B":"Prominent perineural invasion","C":"Tumor cells dissecting sinusoids","D":"Polygonal cells in trabeculae","E":"Ovarian type stroma"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hepatolithiasis is a risk factor for intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma, a firm, gray-white, sclerotic adenocarcinoma of biliary epithelium with malignant glands in abundant desmoplastic stroma that shows prominent perineural and lymphovascular invasion. Bile production by tumor cells is a feature of hepatocellular carcinoma; cholangiocarcinoma does not produce bile. Malignant endothelial cells dissecting along sinusoids are the pattern of hepatic angiosarcoma. Polygonal cells in trabeculae are the growth pattern of hepatocellular carcinoma. Ovarian-type stroma defines mucinous cystic neoplasms (mostly pancreatic, rarely hepatobiliary), which are cystic lesions rather than an infiltrating desmoplastic adenocarcinoma; Robbins lists hepatobiliary mucinous cystic neoplasms only as a less common precursor of cholangiocarcinoma. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 5, intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma, HCC, and angiosarcoma morphology, lines 3501–3528); First Aid 2025 GI (cholangiocarcinoma histology); Robbins Ch. 18 (mucinous cystic neoplasms as less common precursors, lines 4511–4512).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid describes cholangiocarcinoma histology as infiltrating neoplastic glands with desmoplastic stroma (First Aid 2025 GI, cholangiocarcinoma). COMLEX: First Aid notes that cholangiocarcinoma usually presents late with fatigue, weight loss, abdominal pain, and jaundice (First Aid 2025 GI, cholangiocarcinoma).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 3 — underlying causes and vectors of bacterial and parasitic liver infections","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl17_q06","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the risk factors, morphology, characteristic image finding, and tumor markers associated with hepatocellular carcinoma.","batch":3,"stem":"A 50-year-old man reviews his medical records with his physician, which list several past liver diagnoses. Which of these places him at risk for hepatocellular carcinoma?","choices":{"A":"Hemochromatosis","B":"Gilbert syndrome","C":"Rotor syndrome","D":"Cavernous hemangioma","E":"Focal nodular hyperplasia"},"correct":"A","explanation":"HCC risk factors include chronic HBV and HCV, cirrhosis of any cause including hereditary hemochromatosis and AAT deficiency, and aflatoxin. Gilbert and Rotor syndromes are benign, FNH has no malignant potential, and First Aid describes hemangioma as a benign venous malformation. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 4, risk factors, lines 3478–3481).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid links HCC to Wilson disease and autoimmune disease among the causes of cirrhosis (First Aid 2025 GI liver tumors). COMLEX: Bootcamp calls HCC the most common primary liver malignancy in adults (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatocellular carcinoma).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 2 — inherited metabolic liver diseases and their clinical features","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Gilbert and Rotor syndromes ar"}}]},{"sdlNumber":18,"title":"SDL 18 — Pathology of the Gallbladder","questions":[{"id":"sdl18_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the distinct epidemiological risk factors (4 Fs), associated conditions, and morphology of cholesterol and pigment gallstones.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which genetic variants explain a cholesterol gallstone prevalence above 75 percent in Pima, Hopi, and Navajo groups?","choices":{"A":"ABCG8 variants","B":"HFE variants","C":"PKD1 variants","D":"CFTR variants","E":"ATP7B variants"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Native American groups such as the Pima, Hopi, and Navajo exceed 75 percent prevalence of cholesterol gallstones because of ABCG8 transporter variants. HFE and ATP7B variants cause hemochromatosis and Wilson disease (lecture SDL 16), CFTR variants cause cystic fibrosis (lecture SDL 19), and PKD1 variants cause polycystic kidney disease (Robbins Ch 20, library/text/Robbins Patho/Ch.Robbins20.pdf.txt lines 4457–4462). Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 1, ethnicity and genetics, lines 3544–3547).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists the 8 Fs of gallstones, including fair, feeds (TPN), fasting, and fibrates (First Aid 2025 GI cholelithiasis). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes cholesterol stones are the majority of gallstones and are generally radiolucent (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — bile acid synthesis and conjugation and the role of bile salts in micelle formation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the distinct epidemiological risk factors (4 Fs), associated conditions, and morphology of cholesterol and pigment gallstones.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what is cholesterolosis, with foam cells filling the lamina propria, also called?","choices":{"A":"Porcelain gallbladder","B":"Strawberry gallbladder","C":"Hydrops of gallbladder","D":"Courvoisier gallbladder","E":"Phrygian cap gallbladder"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cholesterol stones are associated with cholesterolosis, or strawberry gallbladder, in which the lamina propria contains abundant cholesterol laden foam cells. Porcelain gallbladder is wall calcification, and hydrops is a dilated gallbladder of clear mucoid fluid. Courvoisier sign is a palpably enlarged, nontender gallbladder with painless jaundice, not cholesterolosis. A phrygian cap is a congenital fold of the gallbladder fundus, not cholesterol-laden foam cells in the lamina propria. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 1, cholesterol gallstone morphology, lines 3551–3557).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pure cholesterol stones are pale yellow with a radiating crystalline palisade on cross section (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: fibrate therapy and multiparity are conditions associated with cholesterol stones (lecture SDL 18).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — bile acid synthesis and conjugation and the role of bile salts in micelle formation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Courvoisier sign is a palpably enlarged, nontender gallbladd","E":"A phrygian cap is a congenital fold of the gallbladder fundu"}},{"id":"sdl18_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the distinct epidemiological risk factors (4 Fs), associated conditions, and morphology of cholesterol and pigment gallstones.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, where do brown pigment gallstones form?","choices":{"A":"Sterile gallbladder bile","B":"Main pancreatic duct lumen","C":"Hepatocyte bile canaliculi","D":"Gallbladder mucosal crypts","E":"Infected large bile ducts"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Brown pigment stones form in infected large bile ducts and are soft, laminated, and greasy. Black pigment stones form in sterile gallbladder bile. The main pancreatic duct, hepatocyte canaliculi, and mucosal crypts are not where they form, because brown stones need infection of the large bile ducts. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 1, black versus brown stones, lines 3567–3577).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp links brown pigment stones to Clonorchis sinensis and E coli infection of the biliary system (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis). COMLEX: First Aid lists black pigment stones as radiopaque and brown pigment stones as radiolucent (First Aid 2025 GI cholelithiasis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 6 — bilirubin production, conjugation, and causes of jaundice","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The main pancreatic duct, hepa","C":"The main pancreatic duct, hepa","D":"The main pancreatic duct, hepa"}},{"id":"sdl18_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the underlying biochemical pathogenesis of the formation and composition of cholesterol and black pigment gallstones.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what keeps cholesterol in colloidal solution in bile?","choices":{"A":"Albumin and globulins","B":"Mucin and calcium","C":"Bilirubin and mucin","D":"Bile salts and lecithin","E":"Bicarbonate and chloride"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Water insoluble cholesterol must be incorporated into mixed micelles with bile salts and phospholipids such as lecithin to stay in colloidal solution. Mucin and calcium instead help stones grow. Albumin and globulins and the electrolytes bicarbonate and chloride cannot form micelles; that takes amphipathic bile salts with lecithin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 2, solubilization requirement, lines 3583–3585).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists high biliary cholesterol, low bile acids or lecithin, and biliary stasis as general causes of gallstones (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis). COMLEX: supersaturation occurs when hepatocytes hypersecrete cholesterol or bile salt and lecithin secretion drops (lecture SDL 18).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — bile acid synthesis and the role of bile salts in micelle formation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Albumin and globulins and the ","C":"Mucin and calcium instead help","E":"Albumin and globulins and the "}},{"id":"sdl18_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the underlying biochemical pathogenesis of the formation and composition of cholesterol and black pigment gallstones.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, how much of a cholesterol gallstone is crystalline cholesterol monohydrate?","choices":{"A":"Less than 10 percent","B":"Close to 40 percent","C":"Close to 20 percent","D":"Close to 30 percent","E":"More than 50 percent"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Cholesterol stones are more than 50 percent crystalline cholesterol monohydrate, with variable calcium salts and a mucin glycoprotein scaffold. Less than 10 percent is too low; cholesterol stones are more than 50 percent crystalline cholesterol monohydrate. Close to 40 percent is too low; cholesterol stones are more than 50 percent crystalline cholesterol monohydrate. Close to 20 percent is too low; cholesterol stones are more than 50 percent crystalline cholesterol monohydrate. Close to 30 percent is too low; cholesterol stones are more than 50 percent crystalline cholesterol monohydrate. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 2, composition, lines 3596–3597).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid states cholesterol stones make up about 80 percent of stones (First Aid 2025 GI cholelithiasis). COMLEX: Bootcamp describes cholesterol stones as mixed, with more cholesterol than calcium (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — bile acid synthesis and the role of bile salts in micelle formation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Less than 10 percent is too low; cholesterol stones are more","B":"Close to 40 percent is too low; cholesterol stones are more ","C":"Close to 20 percent is too low; cholesterol stones are more ","D":"Close to 30 percent is too low; cholesterol stones are more "}},{"id":"sdl18_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the underlying biochemical pathogenesis of the formation and composition of cholesterol and black pigment gallstones.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about what share of conjugated bilirubin in bile undergoes deconjugation?","choices":{"A":"About 25 percent","B":"About 50 percent","C":"About 1 percent","D":"About 10 percent","E":"About 90 percent"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Although most bilirubin in bile is conjugated, about 1 percent undergoes spontaneous or enzymatic deconjugation. Chronic hypersecretion of conjugated bilirubin therefore yields high absolute levels of free unconjugated bilirubin. About 25 percent is too high; only about 1 percent of conjugated bilirubin in bile undergoes deconjugation. About 50 percent is too high; only about 1 percent of conjugated bilirubin in bile undergoes deconjugation. About 10 percent is too high; only about 1 percent of conjugated bilirubin in bile undergoes deconjugation. About 90 percent is far too high; most bilirubin in bile stays conjugated, and only about 1 percent is deconjugated. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 2, deconjugation, lines 3604–3607).","boardPrep":"Step 1: unconjugated bilirubin binds free calcium in sterile bile to form insoluble calcium bilirubinate (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: Bootcamp states black pigment stones consist mainly of calcium bilirubinate (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — bile acid synthesis and the role of bile salts in micelle formation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"About 25 percent is too high; only about 1 percent of conjug","B":"About 50 percent is too high; only about 1 percent of conjug","D":"About 10 percent is too high; only about 1 percent of conjug","E":"About 90 percent is far too high; most bilirubin in bile sta"}},{"id":"sdl18_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the pathology, morphology, and risk factors of acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what share of acute cholecystitis cases are calculous?","choices":{"A":"About 50 percent","B":"About 10 percent","C":"About 30 percent","D":"About 90 percent","E":"About 60 percent"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Acute calculous cholecystitis accounts for about 90 percent of cases and follows obstruction of the gallbladder neck or cystic duct by an impacted stone. Acalculous disease accounts for about 10 percent. About 50 percent is too low; acute calculous cholecystitis accounts for about 90 percent of cases. About 30 percent is too low; acute calculous cholecystitis accounts for about 90 percent of cases. About 60 percent is too low; acute calculous cholecystitis accounts for about 90 percent of cases. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 3, acute calculous cholecystitis, lines 3618–3621).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid calls cholecystitis the most common complication of gallstones (First Aid 2025 GI cholelithiasis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists IV antibiotics and laparoscopic cholecystectomy for acute cholecystitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute cholecystitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 4 — complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"About 50 percent is too low; acute calculous cholecystitis a","C":"About 30 percent is too low; acute calculous cholecystitis a","E":"About 60 percent is too low; acute calculous cholecystitis a"}},{"id":"sdl18_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the pathology, morphology, and risk factors of acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, into which toxic product do mucosal phospholipases convert luminal lecithin in acute calculous cholecystitis?","choices":{"A":"Sphingomyelin","B":"Lysolecithin","C":"Phosphatidylserine","D":"Cholesteryl ester","E":"Ceramide"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Mucosal phospholipases hydrolyze luminal lecithin into toxic lysolecithin, which breaks down the protective mucus and exposes the epithelium to bile salt detergent action. Sphingomyelin, phosphatidylserine, cholesteryl esters, and ceramide are not what phospholipase hydrolysis of lecithin produces. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 3, acute calculous cholecystitis, lines 3621–3623).","boardPrep":"Step 1: distention compresses mucosal blood flow and causes ischemia with prostaglandin mediated inflammation (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: E coli and Enterococcus may superinfect the inflamed gallbladder (lecture SDL 18).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 4 — complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Sphingomyelin, phosphatidylser"}},{"id":"sdl18_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the pathology, morphology, and risk factors of acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what is the primary driver of acute acalculous cholecystitis?","choices":{"A":"Impacted gallstone","B":"Clonorchis infestation","C":"Cystic artery ischemia","D":"Sphincter of Oddi spasm","E":"Biliary atresia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Acalculous cholecystitis is driven mainly by ischemia of the cystic artery, an end artery without collaterals, compounded by stasis, viscous bile, and sludge. An impacted stone defines calculous disease. Clonorchis is a risk for pigment stones and cholangiocarcinoma, sphincter of Oddi spasm is not a listed driver, and biliary atresia is an infant duct disease rather than a complication of critical illness. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 3, acute acalculous cholecystitis, lines 3637–3642).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes acalculous cholecystitis is seen in critically ill patients and can follow CMV infection (First Aid 2025 GI cholecystitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp attributes acalculous cholecystitis to reduced perfusion, biliary stasis, and infection (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute cholecystitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 4 — complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the associated conditions, morphology (porcelain gallbladder), and complications of chronic cholecystitis.","batch":1,"stem":"A 64-year-old woman has a cholecystectomy for long-standing gallstones. The resected wall shows dense, uniform fibrous replacement of the wall and mucosa, with very little epithelium left. Which variant is this?","choices":{"A":"Porcelain gallbladder","B":"Xanthogranulomatous cholecystitis","C":"Hydrops of the gallbladder","D":"Cholesterolosis of the mucosa","E":"Hyalinizing cholecystitis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Hyalinizing cholecystitis is a variant of chronic cholecystitis with dense, uniform fibrous replacement of the wall and mucosa and minimal remaining epithelium; it carries a markedly increased risk of gallbladder adenocarcinoma. Porcelain gallbladder is extensive dystrophic calcification of the wall. Xanthogranulomatous cholecystitis is a shrunken, nodular wall full of foamy macrophages from ruptured Rokitansky-Aschoff sinuses. Hydrops is a dilated gallbladder filled with clear mucoid fluid. Cholesterolosis is lipid-laden macrophages in the lamina propria, the strawberry gallbladder. Sources: course SDL 18 reading (Robbins, Liver and gallbladder, chronic cholecystitis variants); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 4, morphology and variants, p103).","boardPrep":"Step 1: porcelain gallbladder, hyalinizing cholecystitis, and long-standing gallstones raise the risk of gallbladder adenocarcinoma (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: gallstones are present in more than 90 percent of chronic cholecystitis (lecture SDL 18).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 5 — risk factors for gallbladder carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the associated conditions, morphology (porcelain gallbladder), and complications of chronic cholecystitis.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what defines porcelain gallbladder?","choices":{"A":"Dystrophic calcification","B":"Foamy macrophage infiltrate","C":"Dense hyaline fibrosis","D":"Clear mucoid distention","E":"Transmural wall necrosis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Porcelain gallbladder is a rare form of chronic cholecystitis with extensive dystrophic intramural calcification of the wall. Foamy macrophages fit xanthogranulomatous disease, hyaline fibrosis fits hyalinizing cholecystitis, and mucoid distention is hydrops. Porcelain gallbladder is extensive dystrophic intramural calcification of the wall, not necrosis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 4, porcelain gallbladder, lines 3667–3668).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes porcelain gallbladder is usually found incidentally on imaging (First Aid 2025 GI porcelain gallbladder). COMLEX: First Aid generally recommends prophylactic cholecystectomy because of cancer risk (First Aid 2025 GI porcelain gallbladder).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 4 — etiologies, clinical features, and complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Porcelain gallbladder is extensive dystrophic intramural cal"}},{"id":"sdl18_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the associated conditions, morphology (porcelain gallbladder), and complications of chronic cholecystitis.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, where does gallstone ileus usually obstruct the bowel?","choices":{"A":"Ligament of Treitz","B":"Sigmoid colon","C":"Splenic flexure","D":"Ileocecal valve","E":"Rectosigmoid junction"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A large stone eroding through a cholecystenteric fistula can cause gallstone ileus, with obstruction at the ileocecal valve. First Aid adds that the ileocecal valve is the narrowest point, so the stone lodges there rather than at the ligament of Treitz or in the colon. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 4, complications, lines 3684–3686); First Aid 2025 GI (gallstone ileus: ileocecal valve, narrowest point).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid describes the Rigler triad of pneumobilia, small bowel obstruction, and an ectopic gallstone (First Aid 2025 GI gallstone ileus). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists large gallstones, acute cholecystitis, and malignancy as fistula risk factors (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gallstone ileus).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 4 — etiologies, clinical features, and complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"First Aid adds that the ileoce","B":"First Aid adds that the ileoce","C":"First Aid adds that the ileoce","E":"First Aid adds that the ileoce"}},{"id":"sdl18_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the risk factors, underlying pathogenesis, and clinical presentation of gallbladder carcinoma.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, in about what share of gallbladder carcinoma cases are gallstones present?","choices":{"A":"About 50 percent","B":"About 25 percent","C":"About 10 percent","D":"About 75 percent","E":"About 95 percent"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Gallstones are present in about 95 percent of gallbladder carcinomas and are the single most important risk factor. About 50 percent is too low; gallstones are present in about 95 percent of gallbladder carcinomas. About 25 percent is too low; gallstones are present in about 95 percent of gallbladder carcinomas. About 10 percent is far too low; gallstones are present in about 95 percent of gallbladder carcinomas. About 75 percent is too low; gallstones are present in about 95 percent of gallbladder carcinomas. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 5, risk factors, lines 3691–3693).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists raised CA 19 9 and CEA in gallbladder adenocarcinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gallbladder adenocarcinoma). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists the Courvoisier sign in gallbladder adenocarcinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gallbladder adenocarcinoma).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"About 50 percent is too low; gallstones are present in about","B":"About 25 percent is too low; gallstones are present in about","C":"About 10 percent is far too low; gallstones are present in a","D":"About 75 percent is too low; gallstones are present in about"}},{"id":"sdl18_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the risk factors, underlying pathogenesis, and clinical presentation of gallbladder carcinoma.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which lesion is a precursor of gallbladder carcinoma?","choices":{"A":"Hyperplastic polyp","B":"Flat dysplasia","C":"Adenomyomatosis","D":"Cholesterol polyp","E":"Rokitansky sinus"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Gallbladder carcinoma progresses through flat dysplasia or in situ lesions, intracholecystic papillary tubular neoplasms, and intestinal metaplasia. Hyperplastic and cholesterol polyps, adenomyomatosis, and Rokitansky Aschoff sinuses are not on the lecture's precursor list; Rokitansky Aschoff sinuses are the mucosal outpouchings of chronic cholecystitis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 5, precursor lesions, lines 3704–3705).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic mucosal irritation drives persistent epithelial injury and regeneration in gallbladder carcinoma (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: gain of function mutations in EGF receptor family members, including HER2, occur in gallbladder carcinoma (lecture SDL 18).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the risk factors, underlying pathogenesis, and clinical presentation of gallbladder carcinoma.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what is the 5 year survival of gallbladder carcinoma?","choices":{"A":"Under 10 percent","B":"Above 30 percent","C":"Above 50 percent","D":"Above 70 percent","E":"Above 90 percent"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Gallbladder carcinoma has an extremely poor prognosis, with most tumors found at an advanced, unresectable stage and a 5 year survival under 10 percent. Above 30 percent is far too high; gallbladder carcinoma is usually found at an advanced stage, with 5 year survival under 10 percent. Above 50 percent is far too high; gallbladder carcinoma is usually advanced and unresectable, with 5 year survival under 10 percent. Above 70 percent is far too high; gallbladder carcinoma has an extremely poor prognosis, with 5 year survival under 10 percent. Above 90 percent is far too high; gallbladder carcinoma has an extremely poor prognosis, with 5 year survival under 10 percent. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 5, prognosis, lines 3721–3723).","boardPrep":"Step 1: preoperative diagnosis of gallbladder carcinoma occurs in under 20 percent of cases (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: the tumor appears as an exophytic papillary mass or diffuse firm thickening of the fundic wall (lecture SDL 18).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Above 30 percent is far too high; gallbladder carcinoma is u","C":"Above 50 percent is far too high; gallbladder carcinoma is u","D":"Above 70 percent is far too high; gallbladder carcinoma has ","E":"Above 90 percent is far too high; gallbladder carcinoma has "}},{"id":"sdl18_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the distinct epidemiological risk factors (4 Fs), associated conditions, and morphology of cholesterol and pigment gallstones.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman with obesity, three prior pregnancies, and oral contraceptive use has gallstones on ultrasound that do not show on a plain radiograph. What are her stones most likely made of?","choices":{"A":"Calcium bilirubinate polymer","B":"Calcium carbonate salts","C":"Calcium oxalate crystals","D":"Cholesterol monohydrate","E":"Uric acid crystals"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Female sex hormones, multiparity, oral contraceptives, and obesity raise cholesterol secretion into bile, and cholesterol stones are typically radiolucent, with only 10 to 20 percent opaque. Calcium bilirubinate stones are black pigment stones, about half or more of which are radiopaque. Uric acid is not among the gallstone types the lecture describes (cholesterol, black pigment, brown pigment). Calcium carbonate is only a component of black pigment stones; her risk factors (obesity, multiparity, oral contraceptives) and radiolucent stones fit cholesterol stones, of which only 10 to 20 percent are opaque. Calcium oxalate is not a gallstone class in the lecture (cholesterol, black pigment, brown pigment); her risk factors fit cholesterol stones. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 1, cholesterol gallstones, lines 3535–3557).","boardPrep":"Step 1: estrogen raises hepatic LDL receptor expression and HMG CoA reductase activity (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: biliary cholesterol saturation increases progressively with age (lecture SDL 18).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — bile acid synthesis and the role of bile salts in micelle formation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Calcium carbonate is only a component of black pigment stone","C":"Calcium oxalate is not a gallstone class in the lecture (cho"}},{"id":"sdl18_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the distinct epidemiological risk factors (4 Fs), associated conditions, and morphology of cholesterol and pigment gallstones.","batch":2,"stem":"A 9-year-old boy with hereditary spherocytosis has small, friable, dark, spiculated stones in his gallbladder. Which stone type is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Brown pigment stones","B":"Black pigment stones","C":"Cholesterol stones","D":"Calcium carbonate stones","E":"Calcium oxalate stones"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Chronic hemolytic anemias such as hereditary spherocytosis and sickle cell anemia cause black pigment stones, which are small, multiple, friable, and dark with molded or spiculated contours. Brown stones form in infected ducts. Cholesterol stones follow female sex hormones, obesity, and multiparity; chronic hemolysis such as hereditary spherocytosis produces black pigment stones. Calcium carbonate is only a component of black pigment stones; chronic hemolysis such as hereditary spherocytosis causes black pigment stones. Calcium oxalate is not a gallstone class in the lecture; chronic hemolysis such as hereditary spherocytosis causes black pigment stones. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 1, pigment gallstones, lines 3558–3573).","boardPrep":"Step 1: mechanical prosthetic heart valves are also associated with pigment stones (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists chronic hemolysis and liver disease as risk factors for black pigment stones (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 6 — bilirubin production, conjugation, and causes of jaundice","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Cholesterol stones follow female sex hormones, obesity, and ","D":"Calcium carbonate is only a component of black pigment stone","E":"Calcium oxalate is not a gallstone class in the lecture; chr"}},{"id":"sdl18_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the distinct epidemiological risk factors (4 Fs), associated conditions, and morphology of cholesterol and pigment gallstones.","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old man from rural Southeast Asia with Clonorchis infection has soft, laminated, greasy stones in his large bile ducts. What are these stones mainly composed of?","choices":{"A":"Pure cholesterol monohydrate","B":"Calcium phosphate and mucin gel","C":"Calcium oxalate and uric acid","D":"Cystine and xanthine crystals","E":"Calcium soaps and bilirubinate"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Brown pigment stones form in infected large ducts and are made of calcium salts of unconjugated bilirubin, cholesterol, and calcium soaps of palmitate and stearate. They are characteristically radiolucent. Cystine and xanthine are not gallstone components; First Aid places cystine stones in the urinary tract. Pure cholesterol monohydrate makes cholesterol stones; stones in infected large ducts are brown pigment stones made of calcium salts of unconjugated bilirubin and calcium soaps. Calcium phosphate and mucin are components of black pigment stones; stones from infected large ducts are brown pigment stones of calcium bilirubinate, cholesterol, and calcium soaps. Calcium oxalate and uric acid are not gallstone components in the lecture; infected-duct stones are brown pigment stones of calcium bilirubinate and calcium soaps. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 1, brown pigment stones, lines 3574–3577); First Aid 2025 Biochemistry (cystinuria: cystine stones).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp states brown pigment stones usually form outside the gallbladder (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis). COMLEX: pigment stones are more common in rural populations and non Western countries (lecture SDL 18).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 3 — underlying causes and vectors of bacterial and parasitic liver infections","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Pure cholesterol monohydrate makes cholesterol stones; stone","B":"Calcium phosphate and mucin are components of black pigment ","C":"Calcium oxalate and uric acid are not gallstone components i"}},{"id":"sdl18_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the underlying biochemical pathogenesis of the formation and composition of cholesterol and black pigment gallstones.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman with sickle cell anemia forms black gallstones. Her hepatic bilirubin conjugation is normal. Why does free unconjugated bilirubin rise in her bile?","choices":{"A":"Absent hepatic conjugation","B":"Bacterial infection of the bile","C":"Lower biliary calcium levels","D":"Deconjugation of excess output","E":"Cholesterol supersaturated bile"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In chronic hemolysis the liver secretes excessive conjugated bilirubin into bile. About 1 percent of it deconjugates, so chronic hypersecretion yields high absolute levels of free unconjugated bilirubin that binds calcium. Infection drives brown stones, and supersaturation drives cholesterol stones. Her hepatic conjugation is normal; the free bilirubin comes from the liver secreting excessive conjugated bilirubin, about 1 percent of which deconjugates in bile. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 2, pathogenesis of black pigment stones, lines 3598–3610).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid links pigment stones to Crohn disease, chronic hemolysis, alcoholic cirrhosis, and TPN (First Aid 2025 GI cholelithiasis). COMLEX: black stones contain calcium bilirubinate, carbonate, phosphate, mucin, and little cholesterol (lecture SDL 18).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 6 — bilirubin production, conjugation, and causes of jaundice","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Her hepatic conjugation is normal; the free bilirubin comes "}},{"id":"sdl18_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the underlying biochemical pathogenesis of the formation and composition of cholesterol and black pigment gallstones.","batch":2,"stem":"A 39-year-old woman loses 30 kg in the 6 months after bariatric surgery and develops gallstones. Which change most directly lets cholesterol crystals accumulate into stones?","choices":{"A":"Biliary bacterial infection","B":"Excess bilirubin secretion","C":"Gallbladder hypomotility","D":"Low cholesterol secretion","E":"Increased lecithin output"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Rapid weight loss and gallbladder stasis are major risk factors. Supersaturated cholesterol precipitates as monohydrate crystals, and gallbladder hypomotility or stasis lets the crystals accumulate. More lecithin and less cholesterol would protect against stones. Biliary infection gives brown pigment stones in the ducts, and excess bilirubin secretion, as in hemolysis, gives black pigment stones. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 1 and 2, risk factors and nucleation and stasis, lines 3547, 3589–3592).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists fasting or rapid weight loss among the 8 Fs of gallstones (First Aid 2025 GI cholelithiasis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists biliary stasis among the general causes of gallstones (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — bile acid synthesis and the role of bile salts in micelle formation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"More lecithin and less cholest"}},{"id":"sdl18_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the underlying biochemical pathogenesis of the formation and composition of cholesterol and black pigment gallstones.","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman with obesity has cholesterol supersaturated bile containing crystals. What lets the nucleated crystals grow into macroscopic stones?","choices":{"A":"Mucin glycoprotein gel","B":"Calcium phosphate shell","C":"Fibrin clot matrix","D":"Collagen fiber scaffold","E":"Bacterial biofilm"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Epithelial hypersecretion of mucin glycoproteins forms a gel matrix that traps nucleated crystals and allows progressive accretion into macroscopic stones. A calcium phosphate shell, fibrin clot, collagen scaffold, or bacterial biofilm is not the matrix; cholesterol stones have a mucin glycoprotein scaffold, and calcium phosphate belongs to black pigment stones. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 2, mucus secretion and accretion, lines 3593–3595).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cholesterol is water insoluble and needs mixed micelles with bile salts and lecithin (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists malabsorption syndromes among the classic risk factors for cholesterol stones (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — bile acid synthesis and the role of bile salts in micelle formation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"A calcium phosphate shell, fib"}},{"id":"sdl18_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the pathology, morphology, and risk factors of acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old woman has 8 hours of right upper quadrant pain, fever, and inspiratory arrest on palpation. Ultrasound shows a stone in the gallbladder neck. Which histologic change is most likely early on?","choices":{"A":"Rokitansky Aschoff sinuses","B":"Edema and neutrophils","C":"Dense hyaline fibrosis","D":"Foamy xanthoma cells","E":"Dystrophic wall calcium"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Early acute calculous cholecystitis shows edema, congestion, mucosal erosions, and early neutrophilic infiltration. Rokitansky Aschoff sinuses, hyaline fibrosis, xanthoma cells, and calcification are features of chronic disease. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 3 and 4, acute calculous morphology and chronic variants, lines 3629–3636, 3664–3674).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid describes the Murphy sign as inspiratory arrest on RUQ palpation, with pain that may radiate to the right shoulder (First Aid 2025 GI cholecystitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists gallbladder wall thickening, edema, and pericholecystic fluid on ultrasound (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute cholecystitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Rokitansky Aschoff sinuses, hy"}},{"id":"sdl18_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the pathology, morphology, and risk factors of acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old man in the ICU after severe burns develops fever and right upper quadrant tenderness. Ultrasound shows a distended gallbladder with sludge and no stones. Which outcome is more frequent in his condition than in the stone related form?","choices":{"A":"Porcelain wall calcification","B":"Common duct stone impaction","C":"Gallbladder adenocarcinoma","D":"Cholecystenteric fistula ileus","E":"Gangrene and perforation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Acalculous cholecystitis in critically ill patients is recognized late, so gangrene and perforation occur at a significantly higher frequency than in calculous disease. Porcelain calcification and adenocarcinoma follow long standing chronic cholecystitis, and common duct impaction and fistula ileus need stones. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 3, acute acalculous cholecystitis, lines 3643–3650).","boardPrep":"Step 1: risk factors for acalculous disease include sepsis with hypotension, trauma, burns, cardiac surgery, diabetes, and vasculitis (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists raised CRP and leukocytosis in acute cholecystitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute cholecystitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the pathology, morphology, and risk factors of acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man with diabetes has acute gallbladder inflammation, and CT shows gas within the gallbladder wall. Which form of the disease is this?","choices":{"A":"Emphysematous cholecystitis","B":"Gangrenous cholecystitis","C":"Gallbladder empyema","D":"Xanthogranulomatous cholecystitis","E":"Hyalinizing cholecystitis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Severe acute cholecystitis can show gas forming bacterial infection, called emphysematous cholecystitis. Gangrenous disease is transmural necrosis, empyema is a gallbladder full of pus, and xanthogranulomatous and hyalinizing forms are chronic. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 3, acute calculous morphology, lines 3629–3636).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp notes cholecystitis pain usually lasts more than 4 hours with localized guarding (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute cholecystitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp states a HIDA scan is positive when the gallbladder is not seen on delayed or morphine augmented imaging (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute cholecystitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the associated conditions, morphology (porcelain gallbladder), and complications of chronic cholecystitis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman with long standing gallstones has a porcelain gallbladder on imaging. Which malignancy of that organ is she at greatest risk of?","choices":{"A":"Angiosarcoma","B":"MALT lymphoma","C":"Carcinoid tumor","D":"Hepatoblastoma","E":"Adenocarcinoma"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Chronic cholecystitis carries a markedly increased risk of gallbladder adenocarcinoma, particularly with porcelain gallbladder, hyalinizing cholecystitis, or long standing stones. The lecture names adenocarcinoma as the usual gallbladder cancer; angiosarcoma, MALT lymphoma, and carcinoid tumor are not linked to porcelain gallbladder, and hepatoblastoma is a rare childhood liver tumor. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 4, complications, lines 3677–3680); Robbins Ch. 18 (hepatoblastoma occurs in children).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp traces chronic cholecystitis with porcelain gallbladder to gallbladder adenocarcinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute cholecystitis). COMLEX: First Aid notes the cancer risk is mostly adenocarcinoma (First Aid 2025 GI porcelain gallbladder).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The lecture names adenocarcino","D":"The lecture names adenocarcino"}},{"id":"sdl18_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the associated conditions, morphology (porcelain gallbladder), and complications of chronic cholecystitis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old woman with recurrent cholecystitis has vomiting and abdominal distention. Radiographs show air in the biliary tree, dilated small bowel, and an ectopic calcified stone in the bowel. Which mechanism explains her presentation?","choices":{"A":"Emphysematous infection","B":"Choledochal cyst rupture","C":"Cholecystenteric fistula","D":"Duodenal ulcer perforation","E":"Mesenteric ischemia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A large stone can erode from the gallbladder into the duodenum or colon through a cholecystenteric fistula, letting air and bacteria into the biliary tree and causing gallstone ileus. Emphysematous infection is a gas forming infection of the gallbladder itself, not a stone in the bowel. Choledochal cyst rupture, duodenal ulcer perforation, and mesenteric ischemia would not put air in the biliary tree with a calcified stone in the bowel. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 4, biliary enteric fistula, lines 3684–3686).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp notes gallstone ileus raises the risk of acute cholangitis and often needs surgery (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gallstone ileus). COMLEX: Bootcamp describes episodic small bowel obstruction with intermittent diffuse pain in gallstone ileus (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gallstone ileus).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the associated conditions, morphology (porcelain gallbladder), and complications of chronic cholecystitis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with chronic cholecystitis has a shrunken, nodular, markedly thickened gallbladder wall filled with lipid laden foamy macrophages. What process most likely produced these cells?","choices":{"A":"Dystrophic wall calcification","B":"Cystic artery thrombosis","C":"Clostridial gas infection","D":"Ruptured Rokitansky sinuses","E":"Hemolytic bile pigment"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Xanthogranulomatous cholecystitis forms when ruptured Rokitansky Aschoff sinuses spill bile into the wall, drawing abundant foamy, lipid laden xanthoma cells. Calcification makes porcelain gallbladder, and gas infection is emphysematous disease. Cystic artery ischemia is the driver of acute acalculous cholecystitis, not of xanthoma cells. Hemolytic bile pigment is the basis of black pigment stones, not of xanthoma cells, which come from bile spilled by ruptured Rokitansky Aschoff sinuses. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 4, xanthogranulomatous cholecystitis, lines 3671–3674).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hyalinizing cholecystitis is dense, uniform fibrous replacement of the wall with little epithelium (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: hydrops is a chronically obstructed, dilated gallbladder of clear mucoid fluid (lecture SDL 18).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Hemolytic bile pigment is the basis of black pigment stones,"}},{"id":"sdl18_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the risk factors, underlying pathogenesis, and clinical presentation of gallbladder carcinoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman with long standing gallstones has right upper quadrant pain, weight loss, and a firm thickened fundic wall. She then becomes jaundiced. What does her jaundice most likely indicate?","choices":{"A":"Early localized disease","B":"Porta hepatis invasion","C":"Benign stone passage","D":"Paraneoplastic hemolysis","E":"Drug induced cholestasis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In gallbladder carcinoma, jaundice indicates direct invasion or metastasis into the porta hepatis or common bile duct, an advanced stage. Jaundice therefore does not mean early localized disease, and benign stone passage, paraneoplastic hemolysis, and drug cholestasis do not fit a firm, infiltrating fundic mass with weight loss. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 5, jaundice and advanced stage, lines 3716–3717).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gallbladder carcinoma has a female to male ratio above 2 to 1 and high incidence in Native American, Hispanic, Chilean, Bolivian, and Northern Indian groups (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists PSC and Clonorchis infection as gallbladder adenocarcinoma risk factors (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gallbladder adenocarcinoma).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the risk factors, underlying pathogenesis, and clinical presentation of gallbladder carcinoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old woman has a resected gallbladder carcinoma with marked aneuploidy. Which tumor suppressor change is found in about half of these tumors?","choices":{"A":"TP53 loss of function","B":"APC loss of function","C":"VHL loss of function","D":"SMAD4 loss of function","E":"BRCA1 loss of function"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Loss of function TP53 mutations occur in about 50 percent of gallbladder carcinomas and cause marked aneuploidy, alongside gain of function changes in EGF receptor family members and RAS signaling. APC, VHL, SMAD4, and BRCA1 are not among the drivers the lecture lists for this tumor. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 5, molecular driver alterations, lines 3706–3710).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic S typhi carriage and parasitic infection are chronic inflammatory risk factors (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: intracholecystic papillary tubular neoplasms are precursor lesions (lecture SDL 18).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the risk factors, underlying pathogenesis, and clinical presentation of gallbladder carcinoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman with symptoms like chronic cholecystitis is found to have gallbladder carcinoma with direct liver invasion. What most explains the poor outlook of this cancer?","choices":{"A":"Chemotherapy induced toxicity","B":"Frequent bone metastasis","C":"Advanced stage at diagnosis","D":"Hormone driven growth","E":"Early brain metastasis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Symptoms mimic gallstones or chronic cholecystitis, so most tumors present at an advanced, unresectable stage with liver invasion, peritoneal spread, or nodal metastasis. Chemotherapy toxicity and hormone driven growth are not the reason, and the spread is to liver, peritoneum, and nodes rather than bone or brain; fewer than 20% are diagnosed before surgery, and 5 year survival is under 10%. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 5, clinical presentation and prognosis, lines 3711–3723).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp notes gallbladder adenocarcinoma is initially asymptomatic before an RUQ mass and pain appear (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gallbladder adenocarcinoma). COMLEX: right upper quadrant pain, nausea, vomiting, anorexia, and weight loss are the presenting symptoms (lecture SDL 18).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl18_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the distinct epidemiological risk factors (4 Fs), associated conditions, and morphology of cholesterol and pigment gallstones.","batch":3,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman has cholesterol gallstones on ultrasound, but her plain abdominal radiograph looks normal. About what share of cholesterol stones contain enough calcium to be radiopaque?","choices":{"A":"10 to 20 percent","B":"50 to 75 percent","C":"90 to 95 percent","D":"1 to 2 percent","E":"30 to 40 percent"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cholesterol stones are typically radiolucent, and only 10 to 20 percent contain enough calcium carbonate to be radiopaque. About 50 to 75 percent of black pigment stones are radiopaque. 90 to 95 percent is far too high; cholesterol stones are typically radiolucent, with only 10 to 20 percent radiopaque. 1 to 2 percent is too low; 10 to 20 percent of cholesterol stones contain enough calcium to be radiopaque. 30 to 40 percent is too high; only 10 to 20 percent of cholesterol stones contain enough calcium to be radiopaque. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 1, cholesterol and black stone morphology, lines 3551–3573).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists Crohn disease, advanced age, estrogen therapy, and rapid weight loss among cholesterol stone associations (First Aid 2025 GI cholelithiasis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists progesterone excess and malabsorption among classic cholesterol stone risk factors (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — bile acid synthesis and the role of bile salts in micelle formation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"90 to 95 percent is far too high; cholesterol stones are typ","D":"1 to 2 percent is too low; 10 to 20 percent of cholesterol s","E":"30 to 40 percent is too high; only 10 to 20 percent of chole"}},{"id":"trial_sdl18_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the underlying biochemical pathogenesis of the formation and composition of cholesterol and black pigment gallstones.","batch":3,"stem":"A 35-year-old man with Crohn disease had his terminal ileum resected and now has black gallstones. How does his ileal disease promote these stones?","choices":{"A":"Excess cholesterol absorption","B":"Biliary bacterial growth","C":"Reduced bilirubin output","D":"Lost bile salt reabsorption","E":"Increased lecithin output"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In ileal disease, impaired bile salt reabsorption leads the liver to secrete excess conjugated bilirubin into bile, and the deconjugated fraction precipitates with calcium as black stones. Bacterial growth drives brown stones. More lecithin would help keep cholesterol in solution and plays no part in black stones, which come from excess bilirubin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 2, hemolysis and ileal disease, lines 3601–3603).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ileal resection or bypass is an associated condition of pigment stones (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: First Aid lists Crohn disease among pigment stone associations (First Aid 2025 GI cholelithiasis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl18_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the pathology, morphology, and risk factors of acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis.","batch":3,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman with acute calculous cholecystitis develops spiking fevers from bacterial superinfection of her gallbladder. Which organism is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Streptococcus pyogenes","B":"Escherichia coli","C":"Staphylococcus aureus","D":"Candida albicans","E":"Neisseria gonorrhoeae"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Bacterial superinfection of acute calculous cholecystitis is usually from enteric organisms such as E coli and Enterococcus. Streptococcus pyogenes, Staphylococcus aureus, Candida, and Neisseria gonorrhoeae are not the enteric bacteria the lecture names. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 3, acute calculous cholecystitis, lines 3624–3626).","boardPrep":"Step 1: if the gallbladder is filled with pure pus, the condition is termed empyema (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists fever, malaise, and fatigue in acute cholecystitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute cholecystitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl18_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the associated conditions, morphology (porcelain gallbladder), and complications of chronic cholecystitis.","batch":3,"stem":"A 60-year-old woman has a palpable, nontender, distended gallbladder from a long standing obstruction. At surgery it is filled with clear mucoid fluid. Which condition is this?","choices":{"A":"Hydrops of gallbladder","B":"Gallbladder empyema","C":"Porcelain gallbladder","D":"Strawberry gallbladder","E":"Emphysematous gallbladder"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Hydrops is a chronically obstructed, dilated gallbladder filled with clear mucoid fluid. Empyema is filled with pus, porcelain gallbladder is calcified, strawberry gallbladder is cholesterolosis, and emphysematous disease has gas. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 4, hydrops of the gallbladder, lines 3675–3676).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic cholecystitis can be complicated by pericholecystic abscess or rupture with peritonitis (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: chronic cholecystitis can lead to ascending cholangitis and sepsis (lecture SDL 18).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl18_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the risk factors, underlying pathogenesis, and clinical presentation of gallbladder carcinoma.","batch":3,"stem":"A 64-year-old woman from Bolivia has a gallbladder carcinoma resected after 20 years of gallstones. Her oncologist orders tumor profiling to look for a targetable driver. Which alteration is most likely to be found?","choices":{"A":"APC tumor suppressor loss","B":"VHL tumor suppressor loss","C":"EGFR family activation","D":"BCR ABL kinase fusion","E":"JAK2 V617F activation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The lecture lists gain of function mutations in EGF receptor family members, including HER2, and in downstream RAS signaling, alongside TP53 loss, as the molecular drivers of gallbladder carcinoma. APC loss is the classic colorectal driver, VHL loss drives clear cell renal carcinoma, BCR ABL defines CML, and JAK2 V617F drives myeloproliferative neoplasms; none is among the drivers the lecture lists. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 5, molecular driver alterations, lines 3706–3710).","boardPrep":"Step 1: preoperative diagnosis of gallbladder carcinoma occurs in less than 20 percent of cases (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes gallbladder adenocarcinoma arises from glandular tissue of the gallbladder (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gallbladder adenocarcinoma).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl18_q06","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the pathology, morphology, and risk factors of acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis.","batch":3,"stem":"A 57-year-old man with acute cholecystitis worsens, and at surgery his gallbladder lumen is filled entirely with pus. What is this complication called?","choices":{"A":"Gallbladder hydrops","B":"Porcelain gallbladder","C":"Gangrenous cholecystitis","D":"Biliary sludge","E":"Gallbladder empyema"},"correct":"E","explanation":"When the lumen of an acutely inflamed gallbladder fills with pure pus, it is termed gallbladder empyema. Gangrenous disease is transmural necrosis, hydrops holds clear mucoid fluid, and porcelain gallbladder is calcified. Biliary sludge is viscous bile with microcrystals, a feature of acalculous disease, not pus. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 3, acute calculous morphology, lines 3629–3632).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the inflamed gallbladder is tense and red to green black from subserosal hemorrhage (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes a sonographic Murphy sign in acute cholecystitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute cholecystitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":19,"title":"SDL 19 — Pathology of the Pancreas and Pancreatic Neuroendocrine Tumors","questions":[{"id":"sdl19_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the presentation and pathogenesis of congenital pancreatic anomalies (pancreas divisum, annular pancreas, and ectopic pancreas)","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what is the incidence of pancreas divisum, the most common congenital pancreatic anomaly?","choices":{"A":"20 to 30 percent","B":"40 to 50 percent","C":"3 to 10 percent","D":"0.1 to 0.5 percent","E":"60 to 70 percent"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Pancreas divisum is the most common congenital pancreatic anomaly, with an incidence of 3 to 10 percent, and about 95 percent of affected people have no symptoms. 20 to 30 percent is too high; pancreas divisum has an incidence of 3 to 10 percent. 40 to 50 percent is too high; pancreas divisum has an incidence of 3 to 10 percent. 0.1 to 0.5 percent is too low for the most common congenital pancreatic anomaly, whose incidence is 3 to 10 percent. 60 to 70 percent is far too high; pancreas divisum has an incidence of 3 to 10 percent. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 1, pancreas divisum, lines 3726–3738).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes the ventral and dorsal parts fail to fuse at 7 weeks in pancreas divisum (First Aid 2025 GI pancreas embryology). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists a raised risk of pancreatitis and abdominal pain in pancreas divisum (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreas divisum).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 5 — common congenital anomalies of the pancreas, like annular pancreas","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"20 to 30 percent is too high; pancreas divisum has an incide","B":"40 to 50 percent is too high; pancreas divisum has an incide","D":"0.1 to 0.5 percent is too low for the most common congenital","E":"60 to 70 percent is far too high; pancreas divisum has an in"}},{"id":"sdl19_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the presentation and pathogenesis of congenital pancreatic anomalies (pancreas divisum, annular pancreas, and ectopic pancreas)","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, through which route does the bulk of the gland drain in pancreas divisum?","choices":{"A":"Major papilla via Wirsung","B":"Ampulla via common bile duct","C":"Minor papilla via Wirsung","D":"Major papilla via Santorini","E":"Minor papilla via Santorini"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In pancreas divisum the dorsal and ventral duct systems fail to fuse, so the bulk of the gland, from the dorsal primordium, drains through the small minor papilla via the duct of Santorini. The duct of Wirsung drains only part of the head through the major papilla. In pancreas divisum the bulk of the gland drains through the small minor papilla via the duct of Santorini, not the ampulla, while the duct of Wirsung drains only a small part of the head. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 1, pancreas divisum, lines 3726–3734).","boardPrep":"Step 1: about 5 percent of people with pancreas divisum develop recurrent acute or chronic pancreatitis (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: the fetal duct systems normally fuse between the 5th and 8th gestational weeks (lecture SDL 19).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 5 — common congenital anomalies of the pancreas, like annular pancreas","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"In pancreas divisum the bulk of the gland drains through the"}},{"id":"sdl19_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the presentation and pathogenesis of congenital pancreatic anomalies (pancreas divisum, annular pancreas, and ectopic pancreas)","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, in about what share of postmortem examinations is ectopic pancreatic tissue found?","choices":{"A":"About 0.1 percent","B":"About 2 percent","C":"About 15 percent","D":"About 30 percent","E":"About 50 percent"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ectopic pancreas, aberrantly situated pancreatic tissue derived from gut endoderm, is present in about 2 percent of postmortem examinations, usually as an incidental subepithelial rest. About 15, 30, and 50 percent overstate how often it is found, and 0.1 percent understates it. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 1, ectopic pancreas, lines 3749–3758); Robbins Ch. 19 (ectopic pancreas in about 2 percent of careful routine postmortem examinations).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins notes ectopic rests may cause pain from localized inflammation or incite mucosal bleeding (Robbins Ch. 19). COMLEX: ectopic pancreatic nests are usually incidental subepithelial findings (lecture SDL 19).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 5 — common congenital anomalies of the pancreas, like annular pancreas","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate acute and chronic pancreatitis based on etiology (cystic fibrosis, alcoholism), diagnostic criteria (Ranson criteria concept), morphology, serum markers, and complications.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which event initiates acute pancreatitis?","choices":{"A":"Islet beta cell destruction","B":"Stellate cell collagen deposition","C":"IgG4 plasma cell infiltration","D":"Trypsin activation in gland","E":"Biliary epithelial apoptosis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Acute pancreatitis begins with inappropriate intrapancreatic activation of trypsinogen to trypsin, which activates other zymogens and drives autodigestion and fat necrosis. Stellate cell collagen deposition drives chronic pancreatitis. Islet loss is a late chronic change, IgG4 plasma cells mark autoimmune type 1 chronic pancreatitis, and biliary epithelial apoptosis is not the initiating event. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 2, pathogenesis, lines 3778–3782).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists didanosine, stavudine, furosemide, hydrochlorothiazide, glucocorticoids, and GLP 1 agonists among drug causes of acute pancreatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute pancreatitis). COMLEX: First Aid gives the I GET SMASHED mnemonic for causes of acute pancreatitis (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — mechanisms that prevent pancreatic autodigestion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate acute and chronic pancreatitis based on etiology (cystic fibrosis, alcoholism), diagnostic criteria (Ranson criteria concept), morphology, serum markers, and complications.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which serum marker is the most sensitive and specific for acute pancreatitis?","choices":{"A":"Serum lipase","B":"Serum amylase","C":"Serum ALT","D":"Serum calcium","E":"Serum CRP"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Serum lipase is the most sensitive and specific marker. It rises within 4 to 12 hours and stays raised for 8 to 14 days, whereas amylase rises quickly but returns to baseline within 3 to 5 days. The lecture names serum lipase, not ALT, as the most sensitive and specific marker for acute pancreatitis. The lecture names serum lipase, not serum calcium, as the most sensitive and specific marker for acute pancreatitis. The lecture names serum lipase, not CRP, as the most sensitive and specific marker for acute pancreatitis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 2, serum markers, lines 3804–3808).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid diagnoses acute pancreatitis by 2 of 3 criteria, including enzymes at 3 times the upper limit of normal (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists raised ESR, CRP, and LDH in pancreatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — mechanisms that prevent pancreatic autodigestion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"The lecture names serum lipase, not ALT, as the most sensiti","D":"The lecture names serum lipase, not serum calcium, as the mo","E":"The lecture names serum lipase, not CRP, as the most sensiti"}},{"id":"sdl19_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate acute and chronic pancreatitis based on etiology (cystic fibrosis, alcoholism), diagnostic criteria (Ranson criteria concept), morphology, serum markers, and complications.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which cells deposit collagen in chronic pancreatitis after activation by TGF beta and PDGF?","choices":{"A":"Pancreatic islet alpha cells","B":"Centroacinar duct cells","C":"Pancreatic stellate cells","D":"Resident tissue mast cells","E":"Ductal epithelial cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Repeated acinar injury stimulates local TGF beta and PDGF, which activate pancreatic stellate cells, the periacinar myofibroblasts, to deposit collagen and cause progressive fibrosis and acinar loss. Islet alpha cells are glucagon-secreting endocrine cells; collagen is deposited by activated pancreatic stellate cells in chronic pancreatitis. Centroacinar cells are duct cells that secrete bicarbonate; collagen is deposited by activated pancreatic stellate cells, the periacinar myofibroblasts. Mast cells are not the collagen-depositing cells; TGF beta and PDGF activate pancreatic stellate cells to deposit collagen. Ductal epithelial cells are not the cells activated by TGF beta and PDGF; pancreatic stellate cells deposit the collagen. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 2, pathogenesis, lines 3782–3785).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists dystrophic calcifications and parenchymal atrophy in chronic pancreatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic pancreatitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists low fecal elastase 1 in chronic pancreatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — mechanisms that prevent pancreatic autodigestion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Islet alpha cells are glucagon-secreting endocrine cells; co","B":"Centroacinar cells are duct cells that secrete bicarbonate; ","D":"Mast cells are not the collagen-depositing cells; TGF beta a","E":"Ductal epithelial cells are not the cells activated by TGF b"}},{"id":"sdl19_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate nonneoplastic cysts (pseudocysts) from cystic neoplasms (serous, mucinous, and intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms) by their histology, underlying cause, and complications.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what makes up the wall of a pancreatic pseudocyst?","choices":{"A":"Fibrous granulation tissue","B":"Glycogen rich cuboidal cells","C":"Tall mucinous columnar cells","D":"Dense ovarian type stroma","E":"Papillary ductal epithelium"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A pseudocyst lacks a true epithelial lining. Its wall is fibrous tissue, granulation tissue, and necrotic debris with saponified fat. Glycogen rich cuboidal cells line serous cystadenomas, and mucinous epithelium with ovarian stroma lines MCNs. Papillary ductal epithelium would be a true epithelial lining; a pseudocyst lacks one, and its wall is fibrous and granulation tissue. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 3, pseudocysts, lines 3826–3829).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pseudocysts account for about 75 percent of all pancreatic cysts (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: Bootcamp describes a pseudocyst as a fibrous capsule surrounding pancreatic fluid (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic pseudocyst).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 3 — organization of the exocrine pancreas","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Papillary ductal epithelium would be a true epithelial linin"}},{"id":"sdl19_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate nonneoplastic cysts (pseudocysts) from cystic neoplasms (serous, mucinous, and intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms) by their histology, underlying cause, and complications.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which gene and chromosome are tied to serous cystic neoplasms?","choices":{"A":"KRAS on 12p","B":"VHL on 3p","C":"GNAS on 20q","D":"RNF43 on 17q","E":"SMAD4 on 18q"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Serous cystadenomas are strongly associated with loss of function of the VHL tumor suppressor on chromosome 3p. KRAS and GNAS mark mucinous lesions, and SMAD4 marks invasive progression. RNF43 loss marks mucinous cystic neoplasms and IPMNs. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 3, serous cystic neoplasms, lines 3841–3845).","boardPrep":"Step 1: serous cystadenomas occur twice as often in females, peak in the 6th to 7th decades, and favor the tail (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: serous cystadenomas carry virtually no risk of malignant transformation (lecture SDL 19).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 3 — organization of the exocrine pancreas","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate nonneoplastic cysts (pseudocysts) from cystic neoplasms (serous, mucinous, and intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms) by their histology, underlying cause, and complications.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which feature is pathognomonic of a mucinous cystic neoplasm?","choices":{"A":"Glycogen rich lining","B":"Main duct papillae","C":"Granulation tissue wall","D":"Honeycomb microcysts","E":"Ovarian type stroma"},"correct":"E","explanation":"MCNs are lined by tall mucin producing columnar epithelium backed by a pathognomonic dense ovarian type stroma that expresses estrogen and progesterone receptors and inhibin. Main duct papillae fit IPMN, and honeycomb microcysts with glycogen rich cells fit serous cystadenoma. A wall of granulation and fibrous tissue without epithelium is a pseudocyst. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 3, mucinous cystic neoplasms, lines 3849–3853).","boardPrep":"Step 1: MCNs are driven by KRAS mutations in about 50 percent and RNF43 loss in about 33 percent (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: up to one third of MCNs harbor an invasive adenocarcinoma (lecture SDL 19).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 3 — organization of the exocrine pancreas","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe location-dependent clinical presentation (Courvoisier and Trousseau sign), markers (CA19-9), and mutations of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about what share of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinomas arise in the head?","choices":{"A":"About 15 percent","B":"About 60 percent","C":"About 5 percent","D":"About 30 percent","E":"About 90 percent"},"correct":"B","explanation":"About 60 percent of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinomas arise in the head, about 15 percent in the body, and about 5 percent in the tail. About 15 percent is the share arising in the body, not the head; about 60 percent arise in the head. About 5 percent is the share arising in the tail, not the head; about 60 percent arise in the head. About 30 percent is too low; about 60 percent of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinomas arise in the head. About 90 percent is too high; about 60 percent of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinomas arise in the head, 15 percent in the body, and 5 percent in the tail. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 4, location dependent presentation, lines 3878–3884).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes pancreatic adenocarcinoma is often metastatic at presentation, with average survival of about 1 year (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatic adenocarcinoma). COMLEX: First Aid names tobacco smoking the strongest risk factor (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatic adenocarcinoma).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 4 — clinical features of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"About 15 percent is the share arising in the body, not the h","C":"About 5 percent is the share arising in the tail, not the he","D":"About 30 percent is too low; about 60 percent of pancreatic ","E":"About 90 percent is too high; about 60 percent of pancreatic"}},{"id":"sdl19_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe location-dependent clinical presentation (Courvoisier and Trousseau sign), markers (CA19-9), and mutations of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which alteration is the earliest driver in low grade PanIN 1 lesions?","choices":{"A":"TP53 inactivation","B":"SMAD4 inactivation","C":"CDKN2A inactivation","D":"KRAS activation","E":"BRCA2 inactivation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Activating KRAS point mutations on 12p occur in more than 90 percent of cases and are the earliest driver in PanIN 1. CDKN2A loss appears at PanIN 2, TP53 and SMAD4 loss at PanIN 3, and BRCA2 is a germline predisposition gene. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 4, key mutations, lines 3894–3907).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CDKN2A is inactivated in about 30 percent by mutation and up to 90 percent with promoter hypermethylation (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: First Aid names a KRAS activating mutation as the most common genomic abnormality (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatic adenocarcinoma).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 3 — organization of the exocrine pancreas","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe location-dependent clinical presentation (Courvoisier and Trousseau sign), markers (CA19-9), and mutations of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what causes the Trousseau sign in pancreatic cancer?","choices":{"A":"Tumor procoagulant mucins","B":"Platelet antibody consumption","C":"Antithrombin gene mutation","D":"Factor V Leiden variant","E":"Estrogen driven clotting"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The Trousseau sign, migratory thrombophlebitis or recurrent venous thrombosis, is caused by tumor derived procoagulant mucins. Platelet antibodies, antithrombin or factor V Leiden variants, and estrogen are not the mechanism, because the clotting comes from the tumor itself. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 4, systemic presentations, lines 3885–3888).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid describes migratory thrombophlebitis as redness and tenderness on palpation of the extremities (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatic adenocarcinoma). COMLEX: Bootcamp links a hypercoagulable state to superficial thrombophlebitis in pancreatic adenocarcinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic adenocarcinoma).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 3 — organization of the exocrine pancreas","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the morphology, associated conditions, serum markers, and clinical presentations of insulinomas, gastrinomas, glucagonomas, and VIPomas.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, from which protein is the amyloid in insulinomas derived?","choices":{"A":"Proinsulin","B":"Calcitonin","C":"Light chains","D":"Transthyretin","E":"Islet amylin"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Insulinomas show monotonous cords of well differentiated beta cells with characteristic amyloid derived from amylin. Calcitonin amyloid fits medullary thyroid carcinoma, light chains fit AL amyloid, and transthyretin fits senile amyloid. First Aid also lists islet amyloid polypeptide (amylin), not proinsulin, as the islet amyloid protein. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 5, insulinoma morphology, lines 3911–3915); First Aid 2025 Pathology (islet amyloid polypeptide from amylin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: about 90 percent of insulinomas are benign, solitary, encapsulated nodules under 2 cm (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: Bootcamp states insulinomas arise from beta cells with autonomous insulin production (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, insulinoma).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 5 — major islet cell types and their hormone products","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"First Aid also lists islet amy"}},{"id":"sdl19_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the morphology, associated conditions, serum markers, and clinical presentations of insulinomas, gastrinomas, glucagonomas, and VIPomas.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about what share of gastrinomas occur with MEN1 syndrome?","choices":{"A":"About 5 percent","B":"About 50 percent","C":"About 25 percent","D":"About 75 percent","E":"About 95 percent"},"correct":"C","explanation":"About 25 percent of gastrinomas occur with MEN1, often multifocal and duodenal, whereas sporadic gastrinomas are usually single. About 10 percent of insulinomas are MEN1 related. About 5 percent is too low; about 25 percent of gastrinomas occur with MEN1. About 50 percent is too high; about 25 percent of gastrinomas occur with MEN1. About 75 percent is too high; about 25 percent of gastrinomas occur with MEN1, and most are sporadic. About 95 percent is far too high; about 25 percent of gastrinomas occur with MEN1. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 5, gastrinomas, lines 3926–3932).","boardPrep":"Step 1: more than 50 percent of gastrinomas are locally invasive or metastatic at diagnosis (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: Bootcamp describes autonomous gastrin release driving parietal cell acid secretion (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastrinoma).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 5 — major islet cell types and their hormone products","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"About 5 percent is too low; about 25 percent of gastrinomas ","B":"About 50 percent is too high; about 25 percent of gastrinoma","D":"About 75 percent is too high; about 25 percent of gastrinoma","E":"About 95 percent is far too high; about 25 percent of gastri"}},{"id":"sdl19_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the morphology, associated conditions, serum markers, and clinical presentations of insulinomas, gastrinomas, glucagonomas, and VIPomas.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which skin rash is characteristic of a glucagonoma?","choices":{"A":"Painful erythema nodosum","B":"Violaceous livedo reticularis","C":"Velvety acanthosis nigricans","D":"Necrolytic migratory erythema","E":"Itchy dermatitis herpetiformis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Glucagonoma causes mild diabetes, necrolytic migratory erythema, anemia, glossitis, and deep vein thrombosis. The other rashes fit inflammatory bowel disease, vasculopathy, insulin resistance, and celiac disease. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 5, glucagonomas, lines 3939–3947).","boardPrep":"Step 1: glucagonomas are most common in perimenopausal or postmenopausal women (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists weight loss and thrombosis in glucagonoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, glucagonoma).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 5 — major islet cell types and their hormone products","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The other rashes fit inflammat","B":"The other rashes fit inflammat","C":"The other rashes fit inflammat","E":"The other rashes fit inflammat"}},{"id":"sdl19_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the presentation and pathogenesis of congenital pancreatic anomalies (pancreas divisum, annular pancreas, and ectopic pancreas)","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old man who does not drink alcohol has had four episodes of acute pancreatitis since age 17. He takes no medications, ultrasound shows no gallstones, and his serum calcium and triglycerides are normal. His younger sister has also had pancreatitis, and neither parent has. MRCP shows that his dorsal and ventral pancreatic ducts never joined. Genetic testing is most likely to show a variant in which gene?","choices":{"A":"ATP7B","B":"UGT1A1","C":"ABCB11","D":"CFTR","E":"HNF1A"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Most people with pancreas divisum have no symptoms, and only about 5 percent develop pancreatitis from inadequate drainage through the narrow minor papilla, particularly when a genetic defect that increases susceptibility to pancreatitis is also present. Recurrent pancreatitis from adolescence with an affected sibling and unaffected parents suggests a recessive susceptibility gene, and CFTR is one of the genes Robbins names in hereditary pancreatitis, along with PRSS1 and SPINK1. ATP7B mutations cause Wilson disease with hepatic and neurologic copper overload. ABCB11 encodes the canalicular bile salt export pump, and its defects cause familial intrahepatic cholestasis rather than pancreatitis. HNF1A variants cause a monogenic form of diabetes and hepatocellular adenomas, not recurrent pancreatitis. UGT1A1 variants cause Gilbert or Crigler Najjar syndrome with unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 1, pancreas divisum and predisposing genetic defects, lines 3726–3738); Robbins Ch. 19 (pancreas divisum; PRSS1, SPINK1, and CFTR in hereditary pancreatitis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes that pancreas divisum is a common anomaly, mostly asymptomatic, that may cause chronic abdominal pain or pancreatitis (First Aid 2025 GI, pancreas and spleen embryology). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists an increased risk of pancreatitis and abdominal pain with pancreas divisum (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic anomalies).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — cystic fibrosis as an etiology of pancreatitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the presentation and pathogenesis of congenital pancreatic anomalies (pancreas divisum, annular pancreas, and ectopic pancreas)","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old woman has had 8 months of postprandial epigastric fullness and intermittent vomiting. She does not use NSAIDs, and upper endoscopy shows no ulcer or mass. CT shows a band of tissue with the same density and enhancement as the pancreatic head wrapped around the duodenum, with a dilated stomach and duodenal bulb proximal to it. Which process best explains her symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Hypertrophy of pyloric muscle","B":"Peptic scarring of the pylorus","C":"Extrinsic duodenal constriction","D":"Intraluminal duodenal mucosal web","E":"Failure of gastric smooth muscle"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A ring of pancreatic tissue around the duodenum is an annular pancreas, formed when a split ventral bud migrates around the duodenal C loop. About one third of cases are incidental, but in symptomatic patients the ring constricts the duodenum from outside and causes stenosis or obstruction, presenting in neonates or in adults with persistent vomiting and distention. Pyloric muscle hypertrophy is an infant lesion at the pylorus and would not dilate the duodenal bulb. Peptic scarring of the pylorus requires ulcer disease, and endoscopy showed none. An intraluminal duodenal web is a recanalization defect inside the lumen, not an encircling band of pancreas. Gastric smooth muscle failure would leave the duodenal bulb undilated and would show no encircling tissue. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 1, annular pancreas, lines 3739–3748); Robbins Ch. 19 (annular pancreas); First Aid 2025 GI (annular pancreas).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes that annular pancreas may cause duodenal narrowing and vomiting and is associated with Down syndrome (First Aid 2025 GI, pancreas and spleen embryology). COMLEX: an annular pancreas can first cause symptoms in adulthood (lecture SDL 19).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — congenital duodenal obstruction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the presentation and pathogenesis of congenital pancreatic anomalies (pancreas divisum, annular pancreas, and ectopic pancreas)","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man has epigastric pain and occult blood in his stool. Endoscopy shows a small submucosal gastric nodule, and biopsy shows normal appearing acini, ducts, and islets. Which diagnosis is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Gastric carcinoid","B":"Gastric lipoma","C":"Gastric leiomyoma","D":"Gastric lymphoma","E":"Ectopic pancreas"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Ectopic pancreas forms subepithelial rests of normal acini, ducts, and sometimes islets, most often in the stomach and duodenum. Inflammation can cause epigastric pain, and ulceration can cause occult bleeding. A carcinoid, lipoma, leiomyoma, or lymphoma would not show normal appearing acini, ducts, and islets on biopsy. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 1, ectopic pancreas, lines 3749–3759).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ectopic pancreas is found in about 2 percent of postmortem examinations (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: ectopic pancreas in a Meckel diverticulum can cause peptic injury (lecture SDL 19).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 3 — how the pancreas forms from two buds and rotates around the duodenum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate acute and chronic pancreatitis based on etiology (cystic fibrosis, alcoholism), diagnostic criteria (Ranson criteria concept), morphology, serum markers, and complications.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with heavy alcohol use has epigastric pain radiating to his back, and CT shows peripancreatic fat necrosis. His serum calcium is 7.2 mg/dL (reference 8.4–10.2 mg/dL). Which mechanism explains his low calcium?","choices":{"A":"Fat saponification","B":"Renal calcium wasting","C":"PTH gland failure","D":"Calcitonin surge","E":"Bone mineral uptake"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In acute pancreatitis, released lipases break down fat, and the fatty acids bind calcium to form calcium soaps, so fat saponification causes hypocalcemia. Renal wasting, parathyroid failure, a calcitonin surge, and bone uptake are not the mechanism; calcium is consumed as soaps in the necrotic fat. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 2, morphology and complications, lines 3794–3797, 3812–3816).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid attributes hypocalcemia in acute pancreatitis to precipitation of calcium soaps (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes acute pancreatitis pain radiates to the back and improves with leaning forward (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — mechanisms that prevent pancreatic autodigestion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate acute and chronic pancreatitis based on etiology (cystic fibrosis, alcoholism), diagnostic criteria (Ranson criteria concept), morphology, serum markers, and complications.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman is admitted with acute pancreatitis. Which admission finding counts toward her Ranson score?","choices":{"A":"AST 150 U/L","B":"Age 45 years","C":"Glucose 120 mg/dL","D":"LDH 200 U/L","E":"LDH 400 U/L"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Ranson admission criteria include age over 55, WBC over 16,000 per microliter, glucose over 200 mg/dL, AST over 250 U/L, and LDH over 350 U/L. Only an LDH of 400 U/L meets a threshold here. An AST of 150 U/L is below the Ranson admission threshold of over 250 U/L. Age 45 is below the Ranson admission threshold of over 55 years. A glucose of 120 mg/dL is below the Ranson admission threshold of over 200 mg/dL. An LDH of 200 U/L is below the Ranson admission threshold of over 350 U/L. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 2, Ranson criteria concept, lines 3786–3792).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ranson criteria at 48 hours include a hematocrit fall over 10 percent and a BUN rise over 5 mg/dL (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: the 48 hour criteria include calcium under 8 mg/dL and PaO2 under 60 mm Hg (lecture SDL 19).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — secretion, activation, and functions of pancreatic digestive enzymes and the mechanisms that prevent pancreatic autodigestion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"An AST of 150 U/L is below the Ranson admission threshold of","B":"Age 45 is below the Ranson admission threshold of over 55 ye","C":"A glucose of 120 mg/dL is below the Ranson admission thresho","D":"An LDH of 200 U/L is below the Ranson admission threshold of"}},{"id":"sdl19_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate acute and chronic pancreatitis based on etiology (cystic fibrosis, alcoholism), diagnostic criteria (Ranson criteria concept), morphology, serum markers, and complications.","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old man with long standing alcohol use has steatorrhea, weight loss, and pancreatic calcifications on CT. During a painful attack his pancreatic enzymes are barely raised. Which explains this?","choices":{"A":"Rapid renal enzyme loss","B":"Enzymes bound by albumin","C":"Lab assay interference","D":"Acinar cell dropout","E":"Islet cell destruction"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In chronic pancreatitis, amylase and lipase may be normal or only mildly raised during attacks because extensive acinar dropout leaves too little functional tissue to release enzymes. Islets are preserved until late and do not make these enzymes. The low values reflect real loss of enzyme producing tissue rather than assay interference. Albumin binding is not the explanation; the low values reflect real loss of enzyme-producing tissue from acinar dropout. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 2, serum markers, lines 3809–3811).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists fat soluble vitamin A, D, E, and K deficiency in chronic pancreatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic pancreatitis). COMLEX: First Aid notes pancreatic insufficiency typically appears below 10 percent function (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 1 — clinical use of pancrelipase in exocrine pancreatic insufficiency","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Albumin binding is not the explanation; the low values refle"}},{"id":"sdl19_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate nonneoplastic cysts (pseudocysts) from cystic neoplasms (serous, mucinous, and intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms) by their histology, underlying cause, and complications.","batch":2,"stem":"A 49-year-old man with chronic alcoholic pancreatitis had an acute attack 6 weeks ago and now has a walled off peripancreatic fluid collection with debris. Which complication is this lesion prone to?","choices":{"A":"Malignant transformation","B":"Islet amyloid deposits","C":"Abscess formation","D":"GNAS driven carcinoma","E":"Tuberculous granulomas"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A pseudocyst arises after acute pancreatitis, especially on chronic alcoholic disease, when walled off fat necrosis liquefies. Its complications are secondary infection forming an abscess, erosion into vessels or viscera, and rupture. It is not a neoplasm, so malignant change and GNAS carcinoma do not apply, islet amyloid belongs to type 2 diabetes, and tuberculous granulomas are not among its listed complications; its infectious risk is abscess. Amyloid deposition in islets is a pathologic feature of type 2 diabetes, not a pseudocyst complication. GNAS mutations are present in about two-thirds of IPMNs but not in other pancreatic cysts, so they do not arise in a non-neoplastic pseudocyst. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 3, pseudocysts, lines 3830–3836).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp notes pseudocysts raise the risk of extrinsic compression of local structures (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic pseudocyst). COMLEX: First Aid describes a pseudocyst as lined by granulation tissue, not epithelium (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 3 — organization of the exocrine pancreas","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"It is not a neoplasm, so malig","B":"Amyloid deposition in islets is a pathologic feature of type","D":"GNAS mutations are present in about two-thirds of IPMNs but "}},{"id":"sdl19_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate nonneoplastic cysts (pseudocysts) from cystic neoplasms (serous, mucinous, and intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms) by their histology, underlying cause, and complications.","batch":2,"stem":"A 42-year-old woman has a slow growing, painless, 6 cm multiloculated cyst in her pancreatic tail filled with thick, tenacious mucin. Which management is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Endoscopic drainage","B":"Percutaneous drainage","C":"Antibiotic therapy","D":"Early resection","E":"Somatostatin analog"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A mucin filled multiloculated tail cyst in a woman is a mucinous cystic neoplasm, a precancerous lesion in which up to one third harbor invasive adenocarcinoma, so early surgical resection is mandatory. Drainage suits pseudocysts, not neoplasms. Antibiotics treat an infected pseudocyst or abscess, not a neoplasm, and a somatostatin analog does not remove a precancerous cyst. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 3, mucinous cystic neoplasms, lines 3849–3861).","boardPrep":"Step 1: About 95 percent of MCNs arise in women (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: TP53 and SMAD4 mutations mark the transition of MCN to invasive cancer (lecture SDL 19).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 3 — organization of the exocrine pancreas","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate nonneoplastic cysts (pseudocysts) from cystic neoplasms (serous, mucinous, and intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms) by their histology, underlying cause, and complications.","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old man has a cystic lesion of the pancreatic head that arises within a dilated main duct and contains mucin producing papillae without ovarian stroma. Which mutation best distinguishes this lesion from other pancreatic cysts?","choices":{"A":"VHL inactivation","B":"GNAS activation","C":"BRAF activation","D":"HNF1A inactivation","E":"CTNNB1 activation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"An IPMN arises in the larger ducts, favors the head in men, and lacks ovarian stroma. Its oncogenic GNAS mutations, in about two thirds, are unique among pancreatic cysts. VHL marks serous cystadenoma. BRAF, HNF1A, and CTNNB1 are not among the cyst mutations the lecture lists (KRAS, RNF43, GNAS, VHL). CTNNB1 activating mutations are found in nearly all solid pseudopapillary neoplasms, not intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms. BRAF alterations occur in only about 10% of IPMNs, so they do not distinguish this lesion the way GNAS mutations do. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 3, IPMNs, lines 3862–3873).","boardPrep":"Step 1: up to 20 percent of IPMNs are multifocal (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: IPMNs also carry KRAS mutations in about 80 percent and RNF43 mutations in about 50 percent (lecture SDL 19).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 3 — organization of the exocrine pancreas","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"BRAF, HNF1A, and CTNNB1 are no","E":"CTNNB1 activating mutations are found in nearly all solid ps","C":"BRAF alterations occur in only about 10% of IPMNs, so they d"}},{"id":"sdl19_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe location-dependent clinical presentation (Courvoisier and Trousseau sign), markers (CA19-9), and mutations of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old man has mild, progressive painless jaundice, dark urine, clay colored stools, and a palpable nontender gallbladder. Where is his tumor most likely located?","choices":{"A":"Pancreatic head","B":"Pancreatic body","C":"Pancreatic tail","D":"Gallbladder fundus","E":"Proximal jejunum"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Tumors of the pancreatic head obstruct the distal common bile duct and cause obstructive jaundice with the Courvoisier sign, a palpable nontender gallbladder. Body and tail tumors present late with pain and weight loss. A jejunal mass does not obstruct the distal common bile duct, and a gallbladder fundus cancer forms a firm, thickened wall rather than a distended, nontender gallbladder. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 4, location dependent presentation, lines 3877–3884).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp describes a double duct sign on imaging in pancreatic adenocarcinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic adenocarcinoma). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists endoscopic ultrasound with tissue sampling for diagnosis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic adenocarcinoma).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — clinical features of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"A jejunal mass does not obstru"}},{"id":"sdl19_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe location-dependent clinical presentation (Courvoisier and Trousseau sign), markers (CA19-9), and mutations of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old woman developed diabetes a year ago and now has back pain, weight loss, and recurrent superficial vein clots. CT shows a mass in her pancreatic body. Which serum marker is most useful to track her response to treatment?","choices":{"A":"Cancer antigen 125 level","B":"Carbohydrate antigen 19 9","C":"Prostate specific antigen","D":"Neuron specific enolase","E":"Serum alpha fetoprotein"},"correct":"B","explanation":"CA 19 9 and CEA are often raised in pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma. They lack the sensitivity and specificity for screening but are useful to track response to treatment and detect recurrence. CA 125, PSA, neuron specific enolase, and alpha fetoprotein, an HCC marker, are not the pancreatic adenocarcinoma markers the lecture names. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 4, tumor markers, lines 3889–3893).","boardPrep":"Step 1: new onset diabetes appears in up to 50 percent of patients up to 2 years before diagnosis (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists metastasis to the liver as a complication (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic adenocarcinoma).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — etiologies, clinical features, and complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"CA 125, PSA, neuron specific e","C":"CA 125, PSA, neuron specific e"}},{"id":"sdl19_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe location-dependent clinical presentation (Courvoisier and Trousseau sign), markers (CA19-9), and mutations of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man has pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma, and several relatives have had melanoma and atypical moles. Which germline gene is most likely mutated in his family?","choices":{"A":"BRCA2 gene","B":"PALB2 gene","C":"PRSS1 gene","D":"SERPINA1 gene","E":"CDKN2A gene"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Germline CDKN2A mutation causes FAMMM syndrome, with familial atypical moles, melanoma, and pancreatic cancer. BRCA2, PALB2, and PRSS1 are other pancreatic cancer genes without this melanoma pattern, and SERPINA1 causes AAT deficiency. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 4, germline predisposition, lines 3905–3907).","boardPrep":"Step 1: about 10 percent of patients carry germline mutations in cancer genes (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists HNPCC, neurofibromatosis type 1, and hereditary breast and ovarian cancer syndrome as inherited risks (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic adenocarcinoma).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 3 — organization of the exocrine pancreas","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the morphology, associated conditions, serum markers, and clinical presentations of insulinomas, gastrinomas, glucagonomas, and VIPomas.","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman has episodes of confusion during fasting that resolve when she eats. During an episode her glucose is 40 mg/dL (reference 70–100 mg/dL), with high insulin and C peptide. Which histology is most likely in her tumor?","choices":{"A":"Ovarian type stroma","B":"Cords with amyloid","C":"Glycogen rich clear cells","D":"Signet ring cells","E":"Desmoplastic glands"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Fasting hypoglycemia relieved by food with high insulin and C peptide is the Whipple triad of insulinoma, which shows monotonous cords of well differentiated beta cells with amylin derived amyloid. Ovarian type stroma marks mucinous cystic neoplasms, glycogen rich clear cells mark serous cystadenoma, desmoplastic glands mark ductal adenocarcinoma, and signet ring cells mark diffuse gastric cancer. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 5, insulinomas, lines 3911–3925); Robbins Ch. 19 (ductal adenocarcinoma desmoplasia); Robbins Ch. 17 (diffuse gastric carcinoma signet ring cells).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp defines the Whipple triad as hypoglycemic symptoms, hypoglycemia during the episode, and relief with glucose (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, insulinoma). COMLEX: congenital hyperinsulinism occurs in infants of diabetic mothers and Beckwith Wiedemann syndrome (lecture SDL 19).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 5 — major islet cell types and their hormone products","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the morphology, associated conditions, serum markers, and clinical presentations of insulinomas, gastrinomas, glucagonomas, and VIPomas.","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man with MEN1 has severe, refractory ulcers in his duodenum and jejunum and chronic diarrhea. Where are his gastrinomas most likely found?","choices":{"A":"Single pancreatic tail mass","B":"Single pancreatic head mass","C":"Multiple duodenal tumors","D":"Gastric antral polyps","E":"Primary hepatic tumor"},"correct":"C","explanation":"MEN1 associated gastrinomas are frequently multifocal and located in the duodenum, whereas sporadic gastrinomas are typically single. Gastrinomas arise in the pancreas or the duodenal gastrinoma triangle, not as primary liver tumors. Sporadic gastrinomas are typically single; MEN1 gastrinomas are frequently multifocal and duodenal. Sporadic gastrinomas are typically single; MEN1 gastrinomas are frequently multifocal and duodenal, not a single pancreatic head mass. Gastrinomas arise in the pancreas or duodenal gastrinoma triangle; MEN1 tumors are frequently multifocal and duodenal, not antral polyps. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 5, gastrinomas, lines 3926–3938).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diarrhea in Zollinger Ellison syndrome comes from acid inactivation of pancreatic enzymes (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists refractory peptic ulcers, diarrhea, steatorrhea, and weight loss in gastrinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastrinoma).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — regulation of gastric acid secretion by gastrin","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Sporadic gastrinomas are typically single; MEN1 gastrinomas are frequently multifocal and duodenal.","B":"Sporadic gastrinomas are typically single; MEN1 gastrinomas are frequently multifocal and duodenal, ","D":"Gastrinomas arise in the pancreas or duodenal gastrinoma tri"}},{"id":"sdl19_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the morphology, associated conditions, serum markers, and clinical presentations of insulinomas, gastrinomas, glucagonomas, and VIPomas.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man has profuse watery diarrhea of 4 liters a day, potassium of 2.6 mEq/L (reference 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), and achlorhydria. CT shows a large mass in his pancreatic tail. Which mechanism explains his diarrhea?","choices":{"A":"Excess VIP secretion","B":"Excess gastrin secretion","C":"Excess serotonin secretion","D":"Excess glucagon secretion","E":"Excess calcitonin secretion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A VIPoma, typically a large solitary tail tumor, causes WDHA or Verner Morrison syndrome: watery diarrhea, hypokalemia, and achlorhydria. Gastrin excess raises acid, the opposite of achlorhydria. A VIPoma, a large solitary pancreatic tail tumor, causes WDHA syndrome (watery diarrhea, hypokalemia, achlorhydria) through VIP; serotonin is not its product. Excess glucagon (glucagonoma) gives mild diabetes, migrating rash, anemia, and glossitis, not watery diarrhea with hypokalemia and achlorhydria. A VIPoma, a large solitary pancreatic tail tumor, causes WDHA syndrome (watery diarrhea, hypokalemia, achlorhydria) through VIP, not calcitonin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 5, VIPomas, lines 3948–3957).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp describes VIP driving bicarbonate and chloride secretion by intestinal epithelial cells (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, VIPoma). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists weight loss in VIPoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, VIPoma).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 5 — major islet cell types and their hormone products","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"A VIPoma, a large solitary pancreatic tail tumor, causes WDHA syndrome (watery diarrhea, hypokalemia, achlorhydria) through VIP; serotonin i","D":"Excess glucagon (glucagonoma) gives mild diabetes, migrating","E":"A VIPoma, a large solitary pancreatic tail tumor, causes WDHA syndrome (watery diarrhea, hypokalemia, achlorhydria) through VIP, not calcito"}},{"id":"trial_sdl19_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the presentation and pathogenesis of congenital pancreatic anomalies (pancreas divisum, annular pancreas, and ectopic pancreas)","batch":3,"stem":"A 1-day-old boy whose mother had polyhydramnios vomits with every feed. Imaging shows a ring of pancreatic tissue around the second part of his duodenum. Which condition is classically associated with his anomaly?","choices":{"A":"Trisomy 18","B":"Turner syndrome","C":"Trisomy 13","D":"Trisomy 21","E":"Klinefelter syndrome"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Annular pancreas forms a ring of pancreatic tissue around the second part of the duodenum and is associated with trisomy 21, or Down syndrome. Trisomy 18 is not the associated condition; annular pancreas is associated with trisomy 21 (Down syndrome). Turner syndrome is not the associated condition; annular pancreas is associated with trisomy 21 (Down syndrome). Trisomy 13 is not the associated condition; annular pancreas is associated with trisomy 21 (Down syndrome). Klinefelter syndrome is not the associated condition; annular pancreas is associated with trisomy 21 (Down syndrome). Sources: Bootcamp Gastroenterology, annular pancreas (merged_with_ocr line 146); First Aid 2025 GI pancreas embryology (fa_gastro_cache line 191); ring anatomy from Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 1, annular pancreas, lines 3739–3748).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes annular pancreas may narrow the duodenum and cause vomiting (First Aid 2025 GI pancreas embryology). COMLEX: Bootcamp describes a distended upper and scaphoid lower abdomen in annular pancreas (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, annular pancreas).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 5 — common congenital anomalies of the pancreas, like annular pancreas","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Trisomy 18 is not the associated condition; annular pancreas","B":"Turner syndrome is not the associated condition; annular pan","C":"Trisomy 13 is not the associated condition; annular pancreas","E":"Klinefelter syndrome is not the associated condition; annula"}},{"id":"trial_sdl19_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate acute and chronic pancreatitis based on etiology (cystic fibrosis, alcoholism), diagnostic criteria (Ranson criteria concept), morphology, serum markers, and complications.","batch":3,"stem":"A 34-year-old man with Crohn disease develops acute epigastric pain radiating to his back a few weeks after starting a new maintenance drug. He does not drink and has no gallstones. Which drug most likely caused his pancreatitis?","choices":{"A":"Azathioprine","B":"Amoxicillin","C":"Metformin","D":"Acetaminophen","E":"Levothyroxine"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The lecture lists azathioprine, statins, and GLP 1 agonists among drug causes of acute pancreatitis, beside gallstones, alcohol, hypertriglyceridemia, hypercalcemia, trauma, ERCP, infections, and genetic mutations. Amoxicillin, metformin, acetaminophen, and levothyroxine are not among the drug causes it lists. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 2, etiology, lines 3763–3769).","boardPrep":"Step 1: mumps and coxsackievirus are infectious causes of acute pancreatitis (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists post ERCP injury among traumatic or iatrogenic causes (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — mechanisms that prevent pancreatic autodigestion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl19_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate nonneoplastic cysts (pseudocysts) from cystic neoplasms (serous, mucinous, and intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms) by their histology, underlying cause, and complications.","batch":3,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman has an incidental cystic mass in her pancreatic tail. Resection shows small cysts lined by glycogen rich cuboidal cells and filled with clear, thin fluid. Which gross appearance fits this lesion?","choices":{"A":"Thick mucin cysts","B":"Honeycomb microcysts","C":"Main duct dilation","D":"Necrotic debris cavity","E":"Solid papillary mass"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Serous cystadenomas have a microcystic, honeycomb appearance with 1 to 3 mm cysts lined by glycogen rich cuboidal cells and filled with clear, thin, straw colored fluid. Thick mucin fits MCN, main duct dilation fits IPMN, and a debris cavity fits a pseudocyst. A solid papillary mass fits the solid pseudopapillary neoplasm, which has solid and cystic components. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 3, serous cystic neoplasms, lines 3837–3848); Robbins Ch. 19 (solid pseudopapillary neoplasm).","boardPrep":"Step 1: surgical resection of a serous cystadenoma is curative if it causes symptoms from mass effect (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: IPMNs lack the dense ovarian stroma of MCNs (lecture SDL 19).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 3 — organization of the exocrine pancreas","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl19_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe location-dependent clinical presentation (Courvoisier and Trousseau sign), markers (CA19-9), and mutations of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma.","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old man has a resected pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma. Molecular testing shows the tumor has lost TGF beta signaling. Which gene is most likely inactivated?","choices":{"A":"KRAS","B":"TP53","C":"CDKN2A","D":"BRCA2","E":"SMAD4"},"correct":"E","explanation":"SMAD4 on 18q is inactivated in 55 percent of pancreatic adenocarcinomas at the PanIN 3 stage and abolishes TGF beta signaling. KRAS is activated rather than lost, TP53 loss impairs the DNA damage response, and CDKN2A loss disrupts the cell cycle. BRCA2 is a germline predisposition gene, not the TGF beta pathway gene. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 4, key mutations, lines 3894–3904).","boardPrep":"Step 1: TP53 on 17p is inactivated in 70 to 75 percent of cases (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: First Aid lists chronic pancreatitis, especially beyond 20 years, as a risk factor (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatic adenocarcinoma).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 3 — organization of the exocrine pancreas","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl19_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the morphology, associated conditions, serum markers, and clinical presentations of insulinomas, gastrinomas, glucagonomas, and VIPomas.","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old postmenopausal woman has mild diabetes, glossitis, anemia, a deep vein thrombosis, and a painful, migrating, blistering red rash. Which cells most likely gave rise to her tumor?","choices":{"A":"Islet beta cells","B":"Islet delta cells","C":"Islet alpha cells","D":"Duodenal G cells","E":"Islet PP cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Mild diabetes, a migrating blistering rash, anemia, glossitis, and DVT in a postmenopausal woman fit a glucagonoma, an alpha cell tumor with extremely high plasma glucagon. Beta cells form insulinomas and G cells form gastrinomas. Mild diabetes, necrolytic migratory erythema, anemia, glossitis, and DVT describe a glucagonoma, which the lecture calls an alpha cell tumor, not a delta cell tumor. Mild diabetes, necrolytic migratory erythema, anemia, glossitis, and DVT describe a glucagonoma, which the lecture calls an alpha cell tumor, not a PP cell tumor. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 5, glucagonomas, lines 3939–3947).","boardPrep":"Step 1: glucagonomas show monotonous cells with salt and pepper chromatin, and a high proportion are malignant (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: Bootcamp describes glucagonomas as arising from alpha cells with autonomous glucagon release (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, glucagonoma).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 5 — major islet cell types and their hormone products","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Mild diabetes, necrolytic migratory erythema, anemia, glossitis, and DVT describe a glucagonoma, which the lecture calls an alpha cell tumor, not a delta cell t","D":"Beta cells form insulinomas an","E":"Mild diabetes, necrolytic migratory erythema, anemia, glossitis, and DVT describe a glucagonoma, which the lecture calls an alpha cell tumor, not a PP cell tumo"}},{"id":"trial_sdl19_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate acute and chronic pancreatitis based on etiology (cystic fibrosis, alcoholism), diagnostic criteria (Ranson criteria concept), morphology, serum markers, and complications.","batch":3,"stem":"A 56-year-old man with decades of chronic pancreatitis has already developed steatorrhea, and now his fasting glucose begins to rise. Which complication is he developing?","choices":{"A":"Secondary diabetes","B":"Fat soluble vitamin loss","C":"Chronic steatorrhea","D":"Pseudocyst formation","E":"Protein azotorrhea"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Chronic pancreatitis destroys exocrine tissue first, while the islets are preserved until late, so secondary diabetes from endocrine failure develops after exocrine insufficiency. Vitamin loss, steatorrhea, and azotorrhea are exocrine, and pseudocysts are structural. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 2, morphology and complications, lines 3798–3801, 3817–3822).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic pancreatitis raises the risk of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma, up to 40 percent lifetime in PRSS1 hereditary pancreatitis (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: autoimmune type 1 pancreatitis shows storiform fibrosis, obliterative phlebitis, and IgG4 positive plasma cells (lecture SDL 19).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 1 — clinical use of pancrelipase in exocrine pancreatic insufficiency","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Vitamin loss, steatorrhea, and"}}]},{"sdlNumber":20,"title":"SDL 20 — Pharmacology of bile acid agents and pancreatic enzyme supplements","questions":[{"id":"sdl20_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the therapeutic rationale and clinical use of pancrelipase in the treatment of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, below what level of normal enzyme secretion does exocrine pancreatic insufficiency occur?","choices":{"A":"Below 10 percent","B":"Below 50 percent","C":"Below 30 percent","D":"Below 75 percent","E":"Below 90 percent"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Exocrine pancreatic insufficiency occurs when pancreatic enzyme secretion falls below 10 percent of normal. It is most often caused by cystic fibrosis, chronic pancreatitis, or surgical resection. Below 50 percent is too high a threshold; exocrine pancreatic insufficiency occurs when enzyme secretion falls below 10 percent of normal. Below 30 percent is too high a threshold; exocrine pancreatic insufficiency occurs when enzyme secretion falls below 10 percent of normal. Below 75 percent is far too high; exocrine pancreatic insufficiency occurs when enzyme secretion falls below 10 percent of normal. Below 90 percent is far too high; exocrine pancreatic insufficiency occurs when enzyme secretion falls below 10 percent of normal. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 1, etiology and pathophysiology of EPI, lines 3960–3963).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes pancreatic insufficiency typically appears at less than 10 percent pancreatic function (First Aid 2025 GI chronic pancreatitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists decreased fecal elastase 1 among chronic pancreatitis diagnostics (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 2 — differentiate acute and chronic pancreatitis based on etiology (cystic fibrosis, alcoholism)","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Below 50 percent is too high a threshold; exocrine pancreati","C":"Below 30 percent is too high a threshold; exocrine pancreati","D":"Below 75 percent is far too high; exocrine pancreatic insuff","E":"Below 90 percent is far too high; exocrine pancreatic insuff"}},{"id":"sdl20_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the therapeutic rationale and clinical use of pancrelipase in the treatment of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what is the source of the enzyme extracts used in pancrelipase?","choices":{"A":"Recombinant yeast","B":"Porcine pancreas","C":"Bovine stomach","D":"Fungal fermentation","E":"Human donor plasma"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Replacement therapy uses animal derived porcine pancreatic extracts containing lipase, amylase, and proteases. Pancrelipase gives about 12 times the lipolytic activity of older pancreatin extracts. Recombinant yeast, bovine stomach, fungal fermentation, and human donor plasma are not the source the lecture gives. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 1, therapeutic rationale, lines 3967–3972).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancrelipase gives more than 4 times the proteolytic activity per weight of pancreatin (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists steatorrhea and fat soluble vitamin deficiency in chronic pancreatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 2 — differentiate acute and chronic pancreatitis based on etiology (cystic fibrosis, alcoholism)","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the therapeutic rationale and clinical use of pancrelipase in the treatment of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, when should pancrelipase be taken?","choices":{"A":"Once daily at bedtime","B":"Before breakfast on waking","C":"With each meal and snack","D":"Twice daily between meals","E":"Weekly with a fatty meal"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Pancrelipase is given orally with every meal and snack, with the dose individualized by body weight, severity of insufficiency, and dietary fat intake. Once daily, on waking, between meal, or weekly dosing would leave most meals without enzyme, so they do not match the every meal and snack schedule. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 1, clinical uses and dosing, lines 3984–3989).","boardPrep":"Step 1: therapy starts at 60,000 to 90,000 USP units of lipase per meal (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists cystic fibrosis among causes of chronic pancreatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 2 — differentiate acute and chronic pancreatitis based on etiology (cystic fibrosis, alcoholism)","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Once daily, on waking, between","B":"Once daily, on waking, between","D":"Once daily, on waking, between","E":"Once daily, on waking, between"}},{"id":"sdl20_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the pharmacodynamics, mechanisms of action, and clinical uses of bile acid agents in the management of gastrointestinal and hepatobiliary disorders.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which receptor does obeticholic acid activate?","choices":{"A":"Farnesoid X receptor","B":"Pregnane X receptor","C":"Liver X receptor alpha","D":"PPAR gamma receptor","E":"Vitamin D receptor"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Obeticholic acid is a synthetic chenodeoxycholate derivative and a potent agonist of the nuclear farnesoid X receptor, or FXR, which downregulates hepatic bile acid synthesis. Obeticholic acid is a potent agonist of the nuclear farnesoid X receptor, not the pregnane X receptor. Obeticholic acid is a potent agonist of the nuclear farnesoid X receptor, not the liver X receptor alpha. Obeticholic acid is a potent agonist of the nuclear farnesoid X receptor, not PPAR gamma. Obeticholic acid is a potent agonist of the nuclear farnesoid X receptor, not the vitamin D receptor. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 2, obeticholic acid mechanism, lines 4029–4035).","boardPrep":"Step 1: obeticholic acid is given at 5 to 10 mg per day for PBC (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: First Aid lists ursodiol as the treatment of primary biliary cholangitis (First Aid 2025 GI primary biliary cholangitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and regulation of bile acid synthesis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Obeticholic acid is a potent agonist of the nuclear farnesoid X receptor, not the pregnane X recepto","C":"Obeticholic acid is a potent agonist of the nuclear farnesoid X receptor, not the liver X receptor a","D":"Obeticholic acid is a potent agonist of the nuclear farnesoid X receptor, not PP","E":"Obeticholic acid is a potent agonist of the nuclear farnesoid X receptor, not the vitamin D receptor"}},{"id":"sdl20_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the pharmacodynamics, mechanisms of action, and clinical uses of bile acid agents in the management of gastrointestinal and hepatobiliary disorders.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about how much of the circulating bile acid pool does long term ursodiol become?","choices":{"A":"Less than 5 percent","B":"About 10 percent","C":"80 to 90 percent","D":"Nearly 100 percent","E":"30 to 50 percent"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Ursodiol is normally less than 5 percent of the bile salt pool, but daily long term dosing expands it to 30 to 50 percent of the circulating bile acid pool. Less than 5 percent is ursodiol's normal share of the bile salt pool, not its share after long term dosing, which is 30 to 50 percent. About 10 percent is too low; daily long term ursodiol dosing expands it to 30 to 50 percent of the circulating bile acid pool. 80 to 90 percent is too high; long term ursodiol becomes 30 to 50 percent of the circulating bile acid pool. Nearly 100 percent is too high; long term ursodiol becomes 30 to 50 percent of the circulating bile acid pool. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 2, ursodiol pharmacokinetics, lines 4001–4007).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ursodiol is conjugated with glycine or taurine and has a serum half life of about 100 hours (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists ursodeoxycholic acid in PBC management (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, primary biliary cholangitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and regulation of bile acid synthesis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Less than 5 percent is ursodiol's normal share of the bile s","B":"About 10 percent is too low; daily long term ursodiol dosing","C":"80 to 90 percent is too high; long term ursodiol becomes 30 ","D":"Nearly 100 percent is too high; long term ursodiol becomes 3"}},{"id":"sdl20_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the pharmacodynamics, mechanisms of action, and clinical uses of bile acid agents in the management of gastrointestinal and hepatobiliary disorders.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which adverse effect of obeticholic acid can lead to stopping treatment?","choices":{"A":"Severe hepatotoxicity","B":"Uric acid kidney stones","C":"Oropharyngeal mucositis","D":"Dose dependent pruritus","E":"Fibrosing colonopathy"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Obeticholic acid causes severe, dose dependent pruritus in up to 25 percent of patients, leading to stopping in up to 10 percent. Uric acid stones, mucositis, and colonopathy are pancrelipase effects, and hepatotoxicity is the problem the lecture ties to the older agent chenodeoxycholate. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 2, obeticholic acid adverse effects, lines 4043–4045; ursodiol adverse effects, lines 4027–4028).","boardPrep":"Step 1: obeticholic acid has been studied in MASLD, with improved liver fibrosis (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists cholestyramine with ursodeoxycholic acid in PBC management (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, primary biliary cholangitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and regulation of bile acid synthesis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q07","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the therapeutic rationale and clinical use of pancrelipase in the treatment of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with alcohol related chronic pancreatitis takes non enteric coated pancrelipase tablets with meals, but his greasy stools persist. Which drug should be added to improve his response?","choices":{"A":"Cholestyramine resin","B":"Loperamide with meals","C":"Metronidazole course","D":"Ondansetron before meals","E":"Proton pump inhibitor"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Gastric acid rapidly and permanently inactivates pancrelipase, so non enteric coated tablets such as Viokace must be given with acid suppression, a PPI or H2 blocker. Cholestyramine binds bile acids, loperamide only slows transit, metronidazole is a nitroimidazole with potent activity against anaerobes, and ondansetron is a 5 HT3 antagonist antiemetic; none protects the enzymes from acid. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 1, pharmacodynamics and formulations, lines 3973–3983); Katzung Ch 50, metronidazole, lines 22–27; Katzung Ch 62, serotonin 5 HT3 antagonists, lines 1802–1806.","boardPrep":"Step 1: enteric coated capsules dissolve in alkaline duodenal pH, so they need no routine acid suppression (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists chronic alcohol use and tobacco use as causes of chronic pancreatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — secretion, activation, and functions of pancreatic digestive enzymes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q08","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the therapeutic rationale and clinical use of pancrelipase in the treatment of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency.","batch":2,"stem":"A 9-year-old girl with cystic fibrosis has taken high dose pancrelipase for years. She now has crampy abdominal pain and distension, and imaging shows a long stricture of her ascending colon. Which complication best explains this?","choices":{"A":"Meconium plug ileus","B":"Ileocolic intussusception","C":"Sigmoid colon volvulus","D":"Fibrosing colonopathy","E":"Toxic colonic dilation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"High doses with high lipase activity were linked to fibrosing colonopathy, with colonic strictures, in patients with cystic fibrosis. Meconium ileus affects newborns, volvulus and intussusception cause acute obstruction without a long stricture, and toxic dilation follows severe colitis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 1, adverse effects and precautions, lines 3990–3996).","boardPrep":"Step 1: excessive pancrelipase doses can cause diarrhea and abdominal pain (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: First Aid lists cystic fibrosis among genetic risk factors for chronic pancreatitis (First Aid 2025 GI chronic pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — mechanism and regulation of pancreatic bicarbonate rich secretion, including the role of CFTR","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q09","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the therapeutic rationale and clinical use of pancrelipase in the treatment of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man on high dose pancrelipase after a Whipple resection develops flank pain and hematuria. CT shows a renal stone that was not seen on plain film. Which property of his therapy best explains this?","choices":{"A":"High purine content","B":"High oxalate content","C":"High calcium content","D":"High cystine content","E":"High phosphate load"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Pancreatic extracts contain high purine levels, so high doses can cause hyperuricosuria and renal uric acid stones, which are radiolucent, unlike radiopaque calcium stones such as calcium oxalate. Phosphate is not a constituent the lecture flags in the extracts, and cystine stones come from the inborn error cystinuria. Pancreatic extracts contain high purine levels, so high doses cause hyperuricosuria and renal uric acid stones; high oxalate content is not described. Pancreatic extracts contain high purine levels, so high doses cause hyperuricosuria and renal uric acid stones; high calcium content is not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 1, adverse effects and precautions, lines 3990–3996); Robbins Ch 20, library/text/Robbins Patho/Ch.Robbins20.pdf.txt, lines 4907–4908 (cystinuria) and 4953 (radiolucent uric acid stones).","boardPrep":"Step 1: capsules must be swallowed whole, since chewing them can cause oropharyngeal mucositis (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: First Aid notes chronic pancreatitis can cause diabetes mellitus from pancreatic insufficiency (First Aid 2025 GI chronic pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — secretion, activation, and functions of pancreatic digestive enzymes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Pancreatic extracts contain high purine levels, so high doses cause hyperuricosuria and renal uric acid stones; high oxa","C":"Pancreatic extracts contain high purine levels, so high doses cause hyperuricosuria and renal uric acid stones; high cal"}},{"id":"sdl20_q10","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the pharmacodynamics, mechanisms of action, and clinical uses of bile acid agents in the management of gastrointestinal and hepatobiliary disorders.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old woman with symptomatic gallstones refuses surgery. Ultrasound shows several 4 mm stones, and plain film shows no calcified stones. Which mechanism explains how the drug chosen for her will dissolve them?","choices":{"A":"Binding bile acids in the gut","B":"Lower biliary cholesterol secretion","C":"Activating hepatic FXR signaling","D":"Raising bilirubin conjugation rate","E":"Stimulating gallbladder contraction"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ursodiol dissolves small, radiolucent, noncalcified cholesterol stones by suppressing hepatic cholesterol secretion and lowering the cholesterol content of bile, with dissolution in up to 50 percent over 12 to 24 months. Sequestrants bind bile acids, and FXR agonism is obeticholic acid. Raising bilirubin conjugation and stimulating gallbladder contraction are not ursodiol actions. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 2, ursodiol mechanism and uses, lines 4008–4018).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ursodiol also prevents gallstones in obese patients during rapid weight loss (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists cholesterol supersaturation of bile among causes of cholelithiasis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 1 — epidemiological risk factors, associated conditions, and morphology of cholesterol and pigment gallstones","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Sequestrants bind bile acids, "}},{"id":"sdl20_q11","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the pharmacodynamics, mechanisms of action, and clinical uses of bile acid agents in the management of gastrointestinal and hepatobiliary disorders.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman with antimitochondrial antibodies has had a high alkaline phosphatase for a year despite full dose ursodiol. Which drug should be added?","choices":{"A":"Cholestyramine","B":"Chenodeoxycholate","C":"Obeticholic acid","D":"Oral prednisone","E":"Oral pancrelipase"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Obeticholic acid is approved for PBC at 5 to 10 mg per day with ursodiol when the response to ursodiol alone is inadequate, or alone if ursodiol is not tolerated. Chenodeoxycholate is hepatotoxic, and pancrelipase treats exocrine insufficiency. Cholestyramine, a bile acid binding resin, can ease cholestatic pruritus but does not treat the disease, and prednisone is not among the lecture's PBC drugs. Cholestyramine is a bile acid-binding resin that removes bile acids from the gut, not a second-line PBC therapy for persistent alkaline phosphatase. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 2, obeticholic acid clinical uses, lines 4037–4042; ursodiol adverse effects, lines 4027–4028); Katzung Ch. 35 (bile acid binding resins relieve pruritus in cholestasis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ursodiol at 13 to 15 mg/kg/day is first line for early PBC and delays transplant (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists liver transplant in PBC management (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, primary biliary cholangitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and regulation of bile acid synthesis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Cholestyramine is a bile acid-binding resin that removes bil"}},{"id":"sdl20_q12","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the pharmacodynamics, mechanisms of action, and clinical uses of bile acid agents in the management of gastrointestinal and hepatobiliary disorders.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man with primary biliary cholangitis stopped his first-line bile acid therapy after 3 months because he could not tolerate it. His serum alkaline phosphatase is 410 U/L (reference range: 25–100 U/L) and total bilirubin is 1.1 mg/dL (reference range: 0.1–1.0 mg/dL). He starts once-daily oral monotherapy with a synthetic derivative of chenodeoxycholic acid. Which hepatic effect of this drug most directly protects his hepatocytes and cholangiocytes?","choices":{"A":"Reduced bile acid synthesis","B":"Reduced biliary cholesterol secretion","C":"Binding of bile acids in the gut","D":"Blocked ileal bile acid uptake","E":"Inhibited hepatic cholesterol synthesis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A synthetic chenodeoxycholate derivative given as monotherapy for primary biliary cholangitis when the first-line agent is not tolerated is obeticholic acid. It is a potent agonist of the nuclear farnesoid X receptor, and FXR activation downregulates hepatic bile acid synthesis; the lower intracellular concentration of toxic endogenous bile acids protects hepatocytes and cholangiocytes, and the drug also modulates inflammation and reduces fibrosis. Reduced biliary cholesterol secretion is the action of ursodiol, which lowers the cholesterol content of bile and dissolves small cholesterol stones. Binding bile acids in the gut lumen describes the nonabsorbed bile acid sequestrants, which do not act on the hepatocyte nucleus. Blocking ileal bile acid uptake interrupts enterohepatic recirculation in the gut rather than acting through a hepatic nuclear receptor. Inhibiting hepatic cholesterol synthesis is the action of statins, not of a bile acid agent. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 2, obeticholic acid mechanism and clinical uses, lines 4029–4046; ursodiol mechanism, lines 4008–4013); First Aid 2025 GI (bile, rate-limiting step of bile acid synthesis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cholesterol 7α-hydroxylase catalyzes the rate-limiting step of bile acid synthesis (First Aid 2025 GI, bile). COMLEX: First Aid gives ursodiol as the treatment of primary biliary cholangitis, the setting for add-on or substitute bile acid agents (First Aid 2025 GI, primary biliary cholangitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — bile acid synthesis and the composition of bile","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl20_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the therapeutic rationale and clinical use of pancrelipase in the treatment of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency.","batch":3,"stem":"A 7-year-old boy with cystic fibrosis takes enteric coated pancrelipase capsules, but he has been chewing them because they are hard to swallow. He now has painful sores in his mouth. Which adverse effect is he showing?","choices":{"A":"Herpetic gingivostomatitis","B":"Oral candidiasis","C":"Aphthous Crohn ulcers","D":"Oropharyngeal mucositis","E":"Vitamin B12 glossitis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Pancrelipase capsules must be swallowed whole and not chewed, because contact with active enzymes can cause oropharyngeal mucositis. Herpetic gingivostomatitis, candidiasis, aphthous Crohn ulcers, and B12 glossitis are not tied to chewing the capsules. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 1, adverse effects and precautions, lines 3990–3993).","boardPrep":"Step 1: high doses with high lipase activity were linked to fibrosing colonopathy in cystic fibrosis (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists PRSS1 and SPINK1 mutations among causes of chronic pancreatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — differentiate acute and chronic pancreatitis based on etiology (cystic fibrosis, alcoholism)","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl20_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the pharmacodynamics, mechanisms of action, and clinical uses of bile acid agents in the management of gastrointestinal and hepatobiliary disorders.","batch":3,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman with a BMI of 44 is starting a very low calorie diet for rapid weight loss before bariatric surgery. Her physician wants to lower her risk of forming gallstones during this period. Which drug fits?","choices":{"A":"Cholestyramine","B":"Obeticholic acid","C":"Orlistat","D":"Gemfibrozil","E":"Ursodiol"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Ursodiol prevents gallstone formation in obese patients undergoing rapid weight loss by lowering the cholesterol content of bile. Cholestyramine binds bile acids, fibrates raise gallstone risk, orlistat is a GI lipase inhibitor that reduces lipid absorption for weight loss rather than a stone preventive, and obeticholic acid is a second line PBC drug. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 2, ursodiol clinical uses, lines 4008–4020); Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis; Katzung Ch 16, anti obesity drugs, lines 1781–1785.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ursodiol is first line for early PBC at 13 to 15 mg/kg/day (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists obesity, female sex, fibrates, and rapid weight loss as gallstone risk factors (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 1 — epidemiological risk factors, associated conditions, and morphology of cholesterol and pigment gallstones","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Cholestyramine binds bile acid"}},{"id":"trial_sdl20_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the therapeutic rationale and clinical use of pancrelipase in the treatment of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency.","batch":3,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman with cystic fibrosis takes delayed release pancrelipase capsules with meals and no acid suppression. Her steatorrhea is well controlled. Which feature of her capsules explains why no PPI is needed?","choices":{"A":"Coat dissolves at duodenal pH","B":"Acid stable form of lipase","C":"Gastric lipase is activated","D":"Bile salts shield the enzymes","E":"Jejunal uptake of enzymes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Enteric coated, delayed release capsules such as Creon have acid resistant coatings that protect the enzymes from gastric acid and dissolve in the alkaline duodenum, so routine acid suppression is not needed. The enzymes themselves are inactivated by acid. Gastric lipase is not what protects the capsules; the enzymes themselves are inactivated by acid, so the acid-resistant coating protects them until the duodenum. Bile salts shielding the enzymes is not described; the enzymes are protected by an acid-resistant enteric coating that dissolves in the alkaline duodenum. Pancrelipase enzymes act in the intestinal lumen rather than being taken up by the jejunum; their enteric coating is what protects them from gastric acid. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 1, pharmacodynamics and formulations, lines 3973–3983).","boardPrep":"Step 1: non enteric coated tablets such as Viokace need a PPI or H2 blocker (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: First Aid notes pancreatic insufficiency causes steatorrhea and fat soluble vitamin deficiency (First Aid 2025 GI chronic pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — mechanism and regulation of pancreatic bicarbonate rich secretion, including the role of CFTR","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The enzymes themselves are ina","C":"Gastric lipase is not what protects the capsules; the enzyme","D":"Bile salts shielding the enzymes is not described; the enzym","E":"Pancrelipase enzymes act in the intestinal lumen rather than"}},{"id":"trial_sdl20_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the pharmacodynamics, mechanisms of action, and clinical uses of bile acid agents in the management of gastrointestinal and hepatobiliary disorders.","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with primary biliary cholangitis starts ursodiol. Which mechanism best explains how it protects her hepatocytes?","choices":{"A":"Binds bile acids in the gut lumen","B":"Blocks hepatic bile acid uptake","C":"Displaces hydrophobic bile acids","D":"Acts as a potent FXR agonist","E":"Raises cholesterol secretion"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Ursodiol stabilizes canalicular membranes and protects hepatocytes by replacing toxic hydrophobic bile acids with nontoxic hydrophilic ursodiol, with cytoprotective and anti inflammatory effects. It lowers rather than raises biliary cholesterol secretion, it does not bind gut bile acids or block their uptake, and potent FXR agonism is the action of obeticholic acid. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 2, ursodiol mechanism, lines 4008–4014).","boardPrep":"Step 1: obeticholic acid is added for PBC with an inadequate response to ursodiol (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: Bootcamp describes PBC as T cell mediated destruction of small and medium intrahepatic ducts (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, primary biliary cholangitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — formation, composition, and digestive functions of bile, including bile acid synthesis and conjugation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl20_q05","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the therapeutic rationale and clinical use of pancrelipase in the treatment of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency.","batch":3,"stem":"A 55-year-old man has steatorrhea and weight loss after a total pancreatectomy. Which starting lipase dose per meal, in USP units, is standard?","choices":{"A":"60,000 to 90,000 units","B":"9,000 to 12,000 units","C":"5,000 to 8,000 units","D":"120,000 to 180,000 units","E":"2,000 to 4,000 units"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Pancrelipase therapy starts at 60,000 to 90,000 USP units, or 20,000 to 30,000 IU, of lipase per meal, which reduces steatorrhea to insignificant levels in most patients. Excess doses cause diarrhea and pain. 9,000 to 12,000 units is far below the standard start; pancrelipase begins at 60,000 to 90,000 USP units of lipase per meal. 5,000 to 8,000 units is far below the standard start; pancrelipase begins at 60,000 to 90,000 USP units of lipase per meal. 120,000 to 180,000 units is above the standard start of 60,000 to 90,000 USP units per meal; excess doses cause diarrhea and pain. 2,000 to 4,000 units is far below the standard start; pancrelipase begins at 60,000 to 90,000 USP units of lipase per meal. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 1, clinical uses and dosing, lines 3984–3992).","boardPrep":"Step 1: surgical pancreatic resection is a common cause of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists a positive Sudan III stain among chronic pancreatitis diagnostics (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — secretion, activation, and functions of pancreatic digestive enzymes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"9,000 to 12,000 units is far below the standard start; pancr","C":"5,000 to 8,000 units is far below the standard start; pancre","D":"120,000 to 180,000 units is above the standard start of 60,0","E":"2,000 to 4,000 units is far below the standard start; pancre"}},{"id":"trial_sdl20_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the pharmacodynamics, mechanisms of action, and clinical uses of bile acid agents in the management of gastrointestinal and hepatobiliary disorders.","batch":3,"stem":"A 74-year-old man with heart failure has symptomatic small radiolucent gallstones and is not a surgical candidate. His physician chooses ursodiol over the older agent chenodeoxycholate. Which advantage supports this choice?","choices":{"A":"Faster dissolution","B":"Lacks hepatotoxicity","C":"Clears calcified stones","D":"Once weekly dosing","E":"Clears pigment stones"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ursodiol is remarkably safe and virtually free of serious toxicity, and unlike chenodeoxycholate it is not hepatotoxic. It works only on small, noncalcified cholesterol stones and takes 12 to 24 months. Dissolution is not faster, since it takes 12 to 24 months, and dosing is daily, not weekly. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 2, ursodiol clinical uses and adverse effects, lines 4015–4028).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bile salt induced diarrhea is uncommon with ursodiol (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes cholesterol stones are generally radiolucent on X ray (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 2 — biochemical pathogenesis of the formation and composition of cholesterol and black pigment gallstones","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"It works only on small, noncal","E":"It works only on small, noncal"}}]}]},{"examNumber":2,"sdls":[{"sdlNumber":21,"title":"SDL 21 — Embryology of the Small Intestine","questions":[{"id":"sdl21_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply normal midgut rotation and intestinal positioning to distinguish incomplete rotation from reversed rotation, including timeline and explain their potential consequences.","batch":1,"stem":"Viewed from the front, how far does the primary intestinal loop normally rotate in total?","choices":{"A":"About 90 degrees clockwise","B":"About 180 degrees clockwise","C":"About 90 degrees counterclockwise","D":"About 270 degrees counterclockwise","E":"About 360 degrees counterclockwise"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The loop rotates about 270 degrees counterclockwise: about 90 degrees during herniation and the remaining 180 degrees during return to the abdomen. Ninety degrees counterclockwise is only the herniation phase, and clockwise rotation is abnormal. A full 360 degrees overshoots normal rotation, which totals 270 degrees counterclockwise: 90 during herniation plus 180 during return. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Rotation of the midgut; Retraction of herniated loops; Gut rotation defects, Figs. 15.24–15.27, 15.33). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the stomach rotates 90 degrees clockwise during the 4th to 6th weeks, so the left vagus comes to lie anteriorly (First Aid Gastrointestinal, normal embryology). COMLEX: the jejunum and ileum coil during rotation, whereas the large intestine lengthens without coiling (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — stomach rotation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"A full 360 degrees overshoots normal rotation, which totals "}},{"id":"sdl21_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply normal midgut rotation and intestinal positioning to distinguish incomplete rotation from reversed rotation, including timeline and explain their potential consequences.","batch":1,"stem":"Which structure forms the axis of rotation of the primary intestinal loop?","choices":{"A":"Celiac arterial trunk","B":"Vitelline duct and yolk stalk","C":"Superior mesenteric artery","D":"Inferior mesenteric artery","E":"Left umbilical vein remnant"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The superior mesenteric artery forms the axis about which the primary intestinal loop rotates. The celiac trunk and inferior mesenteric artery supply the foregut and hindgut, and the vitelline duct connects the loop apex to the yolk sac. The umbilical vein runs in the cord to the liver and is not the pivot of the midgut loop; the superior mesenteric artery is the central axis. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Rotation of the midgut; Retraction of herniated loops; Gut rotation defects, Figs. 15.24–15.27, 15.33). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the cephalic limb of the primary loop forms the distal duodenum, jejunum, and part of the ileum (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the dorsal mesentery twists around the origin of this artery as the caudal limb moves to the right (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — stomach rotation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"The umbilical vein runs in the cord to the liver and is not "}},{"id":"sdl21_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply normal midgut rotation and intestinal positioning to distinguish incomplete rotation from reversed rotation, including timeline and explain their potential consequences.","batch":1,"stem":"During which week do the herniated intestinal loops begin to return to the abdominal cavity?","choices":{"A":"Fourth week","B":"Sixth week","C":"Eighth week","D":"Sixteenth week","E":"Tenth week"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Herniated loops begin to return during the 10th week, aided by regression of the mesonephros, slower liver growth, and abdominal cavity expansion. Herniation begins in the 6th week, and the return is finished by about the 12th week. In the fourth week the loops have not yet herniated; physiologic herniation does not begin until the 6th week. In the eighth week the loops are still herniated in the cord; retraction into the abdomen starts at the 10th week. By the sixteenth week the loops have long since returned, since retraction is complete by the 10th to 12th week. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Rotation of the midgut; Retraction of herniated loops; Gut rotation defects, Figs. 15.24–15.27, 15.33). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the proximal jejunum returns first and settles on the left side (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the cecal bud returns last and then descends into the right iliac fossa (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — stomach rotation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"In the fourth week the loops have not yet herniated; physiol","C":"In the eighth week the loops are still herniated in the cord","D":"By the sixteenth week the loops have long since returned, si"}},{"id":"sdl21_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate physiologic umbilical herniation, omphalocele, and gastroschisis using developmental timing, defect location, coverings of the viscera, and associated abnormalities.","batch":1,"stem":"Which covering surrounds the herniated viscera in an omphalocele?","choices":{"A":"Skin","B":"Dura","C":"Amnion","D":"Chorion","E":"Pleura"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In omphalocele, the viscera herniate through an enlarged umbilical ring and are covered by a membrane of amnion and peritoneum separated by Wharton's jelly, which gives the sac a smooth, spherical look on ultrasound. Skin covers an umbilical hernia, and gastroschisis has no covering membrane. Dura is a meningeal covering of the central nervous system and plays no part in the omphalocele sac, which is amnion and peritoneum with Wharton's jelly. Chorion is not part of the omphalocele sac; the membrane is amnion and peritoneum separated by Wharton's jelly. Pleura lines the thoracic cavity, whereas the omphalocele membrane is amnion and peritoneum separated by Wharton's jelly. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Physiologic herniation; Body wall defects, Figs. 15.26, 15.31). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid describes the omphalocele sac as peritoneum plus amnion, light gray and shiny (First Aid Gastrointestinal, ventral wall defects). COMLEX: omphalocele occurs in about 2.5 per 10,000 births with about 25% mortality (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Dura is a meningeal covering of the central nervous system a","D":"Chorion is not part of the omphalocele sac; the membrane is ","E":"Pleura lines the thoracic cavity, whereas the omphalocele me"}},{"id":"sdl21_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate physiologic umbilical herniation, omphalocele, and gastroschisis using developmental timing, defect location, coverings of the viscera, and associated abnormalities.","batch":1,"stem":"During which week do midgut loops normally enter the umbilical cord as physiologic herniation?","choices":{"A":"Third week","B":"Fourth week","C":"Twelfth week","D":"Sixth week","E":"Twentieth week"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Rapid elongation of the loop and growth of the liver make the abdomen temporarily too small, so loops enter the extraembryonic cavity of the cord during the 6th week. They return beginning in the 10th week. The third week is too early; herniation into the cord is driven by midgut elongation and liver growth beginning in the 6th week. The fourth week precedes physiologic herniation, which begins in the 6th week. By the twelfth week the loops have finished returning to the abdomen rather than entering the cord. By the twentieth week bowel in the cord is abnormal, because loops normally return by the 10th to 12th week. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Physiologic herniation; Body wall defects, Figs. 15.26, 15.31). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: coiling of the small intestinal loops and formation of the cecum occur while the gut is herniated (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: physiologic herniation persists until about the 10th week, so bowel in the cord base before then is normal (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The third week is too early; herniation into the cord is dri","B":"The fourth week precedes physiologic herniation, which begin","C":"By the twelfth week the loops have finished returning to the","E":"By the twentieth week bowel in the cord is abnormal, because"}},{"id":"sdl21_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate physiologic umbilical herniation, omphalocele, and gastroschisis using developmental timing, defect location, coverings of the viscera, and associated abnormalities.","batch":1,"stem":"Where is the abdominal wall defect of gastroschisis usually located?","choices":{"A":"Through the enlarged umbilical ring itself","B":"Lateral to the umbilicus on the right","C":"Lateral to the umbilicus on the left side","D":"In the upper epigastric midline above it","E":"Just above the pubic symphysis in midline"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Gastroschisis occurs lateral to the umbilicus, usually on the right, from abnormal body wall closure around the connecting stalk. Herniation through the umbilical ring itself is omphalocele. A suprapubic defect suggests bladder exstrophy. Gastroschisis is lateral to the umbilicus but usually on the right, not the left. Gastroschisis is a defect beside the umbilicus, not an upper epigastric midline defect. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Physiologic herniation; Body wall defects, Figs. 15.26, 15.31). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastroschisis is a lateral fold closure defect, while bladder exstrophy is a caudal fold defect (First Aid Gastrointestinal, ventral wall defects). COMLEX: gastroschisis is increasing in frequency, especially in babies of mothers under 20 (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Gastroschisis is lateral to the umbilicus but usually on the","D":"Gastroschisis is a defect beside the umbilicus, not an upper"}},{"id":"sdl21_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate the persistence of different portions of the vitelline duct to Meckel diverticulum, vitelline cysts, and vitelline fistulas and explain their associated bleeding or obstruction.","batch":1,"stem":"Meckel diverticulum results from persistence of which embryonic structure?","choices":{"A":"Urachal remnant","B":"Allantoic stalk","C":"Vitelline duct","D":"Cloacal membrane","E":"Umbilical vein"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A small persisting portion of the vitelline duct forms an outpocketing of the ileum, Meckel diverticulum. A persisting urachus comes from the allantois and drains urine. The cloacal membrane and umbilical vein are not related to the ileum. The allantois gives rise to the urachus and bladder region, not to an ileal outpouching. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Vitelline duct abnormalities, Fig. 15.32). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Meckel diverticulum is the most common congenital anomaly of the GI tract (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Meckel diverticulum). COMLEX: the diverticulum usually causes no symptoms in adults (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — parietal cell HCl secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The allantois gives rise to the urachus and bladder region, "}},{"id":"sdl21_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate the persistence of different portions of the vitelline duct to Meckel diverticulum, vitelline cysts, and vitelline fistulas and explain their associated bleeding or obstruction.","batch":1,"stem":"Along which border of the ileum does Meckel diverticulum arise?","choices":{"A":"Antimesenteric border","B":"Mesenteric border","C":"Duodenojejunal border","D":"Retroperitoneal border","E":"Ileocecal valve border"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Meckel diverticulum lies on the antimesenteric border of the ileum, about 40 to 60 cm from the ileocecal valve. It does not arise from the mesenteric border, the duodenojejunal flexure, or the ileocecal valve itself. The ileum bearing a Meckel diverticulum is intraperitoneal on its mesentery; the diverticulum arises on the antimesenteric border, not a retroperitoneal one. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Vitelline duct abnormalities, Fig. 15.32). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the rule of 2s lists 2 feet from the ileocecal valve and 2 inches long (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Meckel diverticulum). COMLEX: Meckel diverticulum is twice as likely in males (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Meckel diverticulum).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — parietal cell HCl secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"The ileum bearing a Meckel diverticulum is intraperitoneal o"}},{"id":"sdl21_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate the persistence of different portions of the vitelline duct to Meckel diverticulum, vitelline cysts, and vitelline fistulas and explain their associated bleeding or obstruction.","batch":1,"stem":"What anomaly results when the omphalomesenteric duct stays patent along its entire length?","choices":{"A":"Vitelline cyst","B":"Urachal sinus tract","C":"Duplication cyst","D":"Omphalocele sac","E":"Vitelline fistula"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A duct that stays patent throughout forms a vitelline, or umbilical, fistula connecting the ileal lumen with the umbilicus, so feces may drain at the umbilicus. A vitelline cyst forms when only the middle part persists between two fibrous cords. A urachal sinus is an allantoic remnant draining toward the bladder, not a patent omphalomesenteric duct reaching the ileum. Duplication cysts are abnormal proliferations of gut parenchyma, most often near the ileum, unrelated to a patent vitelline duct. An omphalocele sac is failed return of herniated loops through an enlarged umbilical ring, not a patent vitelline duct. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Vitelline duct abnormalities, Fig. 15.32). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: umbilical drainage of feces points to a patent omphalomesenteric duct, whereas urine drainage points to a patent urachus (Langman Ch. 16). COMLEX: the vitelline duct connects the apex of the primary loop with the yolk sac (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — parietal cell HCl secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"A urachal sinus is an allantoic remnant draining toward the ","C":"Duplication cysts are abnormal proliferations of gut parench","D":"An omphalocele sac is failed return of herniated loops throu"}},{"id":"sdl21_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish intestinal obstruction caused by lack of recanalization from atresia following vascular compromise, and recognize abnormal gut differentiation as another mechanism of atresia.","batch":1,"stem":"In which intestinal segment do most atresias and stenoses occur?","choices":{"A":"Jejunum","B":"Distal ileum","C":"Appendix","D":"Duodenum","E":"Colon"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Most atresias and stenoses occur in the duodenum, the fewest occur in the colon, and the jejunum and ileum have equal numbers (about 1 in 1500 births). The appendix is not a usual atresia site. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Gut atresias and stenoses, Figs. 15.34–15.35). Textbook enrichment (Langman) — detail not in SDL 21 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: duodenal atresia is associated with Down syndrome and shows a double bubble on x-ray (First Aid Gastrointestinal, intestinal atresia). COMLEX: atresias account for 95% of these defects and stenoses for only 5% (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 4 — duodenal recanalization","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Langman"},{"id":"sdl21_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish intestinal obstruction caused by lack of recanalization from atresia following vascular compromise, and recognize abnormal gut differentiation as another mechanism of atresia.","batch":1,"stem":"Which mechanism most likely causes atresia of the upper duodenum, just distal to the stomach?","choices":{"A":"Lack of recanalization","B":"Vascular accident","C":"Midgut volvulus","D":"Amniotic fluid injury","E":"Persistent vitelline cord"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Upper duodenal atresias are probably due to failure of the solid duodenal lumen to recanalize. Distal to the duodenum, atresias have been attributed to vascular accidents, which can follow volvulus, malrotation, or abdominal wall defects. Exposure of bowel to amniotic fluid injures bare loops in gastroschisis but does not cause upper duodenal atresia. A persistent vitelline cord causes obstruction by loops twisting around it, not upper duodenal atresia. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Gut atresias and stenoses, Figs. 15.34–15.35). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: intestinal atresia presents with bilious vomiting and distension within the first 1 to 2 days of life (First Aid Gastrointestinal, intestinal atresia). COMLEX: the duodenal lumen is temporarily obliterated by epithelial proliferation before recanalizing (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 4 — duodenal recanalization","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Exposure of bowel to amniotic fluid injures bare loops in ga","E":"A persistent vitelline cord causes obstruction by loops twis"}},{"id":"sdl21_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish intestinal obstruction caused by lack of recanalization from atresia following vascular compromise, and recognize abnormal gut differentiation as another mechanism of atresia.","batch":1,"stem":"Misexpression of which gene families supports a non-vascular, differentiation-based cause of intestinal atresia?","choices":{"A":"RET and GDNF family receptor genes","B":"HOX genes and FGF family genes","C":"SHH and patched receptor genes","D":"PAX and SOX transcription factor genes","E":"WNT and BMP signaling pathway genes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Misexpression of HOX genes and of FGF family ligands and receptors disrupts epithelial-mesenchymal signaling and causes gut atresias, showing that faulty gut differentiation, not only vascular accidents, can produce these defects. RET mutations cause aganglionic megacolon. SHH from endoderm induces HOX expression in mesoderm for craniocaudal patterning; the notes tie atresia specifically to HOX and FGF misexpression. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Gut atresias and stenoses, Figs. 15.34–15.35). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: RET mutations impair neural crest migration and cause Hirschsprung disease (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: in 50% of atresias a region of bowel is lost, and in 20% a fibrous cord remains (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 4 — duodenal recanalization","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"SHH from endoderm induces HOX expression in mesoderm for cra"}},{"id":"sdl21_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply developmental origins and arterial supply of the small intestine to distinguish its epithelial, muscular and vascular components.","batch":1,"stem":"Which germ layer forms the epithelium lining the small intestine?","choices":{"A":"Ectoderm","B":"Neural crest","C":"Somatic layer","D":"Paraxial layer","E":"Endoderm"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The epithelium of the digestive system and the parenchyma of its derivatives originate in endoderm. Connective tissue, muscle, and peritoneal components come from mesoderm. Ectoderm forms only the caudal anal canal. Neural crest forms enteric ganglia, not the mucosal epithelium, which derives from gut tube endoderm. Somatic mesoderm forms the body wall; gut wall mesoderm is the splanchnic layer, and the epithelium itself is endoderm. Paraxial mesoderm forms somites, not the intestinal epithelium, which is endodermal. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Summary; Table 15.1). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the caudal third of the anal canal is ectodermal and is supplied by the inferior rectal artery (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: gut differentiation depends on reciprocal epithelial-mesenchymal interactions (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Neural crest forms enteric ganglia, not the mucosal epitheli","C":"Somatic mesoderm forms the body wall; gut wall mesoderm is t","D":"Paraxial mesoderm forms somites, not the intestinal epitheli"}},{"id":"sdl21_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply developmental origins and arterial supply of the small intestine to distinguish its epithelial, muscular and vascular components.","batch":1,"stem":"Which embryonic tissue forms the smooth muscle of the small intestinal wall?","choices":{"A":"Splanchnic mesoderm","B":"Gut tube endoderm","C":"Surface ectoderm","D":"Paraxial mesoderm","E":"Somatic body wall mesoderm"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The muscular and connective tissue components of the gut come from mesoderm, specifically the splanchnic (visceral) layer surrounding the endodermal tube. Endoderm forms the epithelium. Somatic and paraxial mesoderm form body wall and somite derivatives. Surface ectoderm forms the skin epidermis and only the caudal anal canal, not intestinal smooth muscle. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Summary; Table 15.1). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: endodermal sonic hedgehog induces HOX gene expression in the surrounding gut mesoderm (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: visceral peritoneum covers the gut while parietal peritoneum lines the body wall (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Surface ectoderm forms the skin epidermis and only the cauda"}},{"id":"sdl21_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply developmental origins and arterial supply of the small intestine to distinguish its epithelial, muscular and vascular components.","batch":1,"stem":"Which structure is the most distal derivative of the midgut?","choices":{"A":"Distal third of the transverse colon and flexure","B":"Proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon","C":"Descending colon at the splenic flexure region","D":"Terminal ileum at the ileocecal valve junction","E":"Upper anal canal above the pectinate line"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The midgut ends at the junction of the proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon with the distal third. The distal third of the transverse colon, the descending colon, and the upper anal canal are hindgut. The terminal ileum is proximal to the end. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Summary; Table 15.1). Textbook enrichment (Langman) — detail not in SDL 21 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the midgut receives vagal parasympathetic innervation and lies at the L1 vertebral level of its artery (First Aid Gastrointestinal, blood supply and innervation). COMLEX: the caudal limb of the primary loop forms the lower ileum, cecum, appendix, and ascending colon (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Langman"},{"id":"sdl21_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply normal midgut rotation and intestinal positioning to distinguish incomplete rotation from reversed rotation, including timeline and explain their potential consequences.","batch":2,"stem":"A 4-day-old boy has sudden bilious vomiting. An upper GI series shows the small intestinal loops lying in the right abdomen, and a contrast enema places the cecum and colon in the left abdomen. The ileum enters the cecum from the right. Which developmental error best explains this anatomy?","choices":{"A":"Clockwise rotation of the primary loop","B":"Complete rotation with failed colonic fixation","C":"Failure of the loops to leave the abdomen","D":"Persistent attachment of the omphalomesenteric duct","E":"Rotation that stopped near 90 degrees"},"correct":"E","explanation":"When rotation amounts to only 90 degrees, the colon and cecum return first and settle on the left, and later loops move to the right, giving a left-sided colon. Clockwise (reversed) rotation places the transverse colon behind the duodenum. Failed fixation leaves a mobile colon in its normal place. If loops never left the abdomen there would be no physiologic herniation, and this would not explain a left-sided colon with ileum entering from the right. A persistent omphalomesenteric duct causes a Meckel diverticulum or fistula, not a left-sided colon. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Rotation of the midgut; Retraction of herniated loops; Gut rotation defects, Figs. 15.24–15.27, 15.33). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: malrotation leaves the small bowel clumped on the right and the colon on the left, with fibrous Ladd bands (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malrotation). COMLEX: malrotation predisposes to volvulus with compromised blood supply (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 3 — stomach rotation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"If loops never left the abdomen there would be no physiologi","D":"A persistent omphalomesenteric duct causes a Meckel divertic"}},{"id":"sdl21_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply normal midgut rotation and intestinal positioning to distinguish incomplete rotation from reversed rotation, including timeline and explain their potential consequences.","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-week-old girl with known intestinal malrotation abruptly develops bilious vomiting, abdominal distension, and bloody stools. Heart rate is 190/min, and capillary refill is 4 seconds. Which complication most likely threatens her bowel?","choices":{"A":"Telescoping of ileum into the cecum","B":"Twisting that compromises blood flow","C":"Spasm of an aganglionic colonic segment","D":"Ulceration of ectopic gastric mucosa","E":"Plugging of the ileum by thick meconium"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Malrotation may result in twisting of the intestine (volvulus) and a compromised blood supply, which explains bloody stools and shock. Intussusception, aganglionosis, Meckel ulceration, and meconium ileus cause obstruction or bleeding by other mechanisms and are not the typical malrotation emergency. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Rotation of the midgut; Retraction of herniated loops; Gut rotation defects, Figs. 15.24–15.27, 15.33). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: midgut volvulus is more common in infants, whereas sigmoid volvulus is more common in older adults (First Aid Gastrointestinal, volvulus). COMLEX: in gastroschisis, volvulus with compromised blood supply may kill large regions of bowel (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply normal midgut rotation and intestinal positioning to distinguish incomplete rotation from reversed rotation, including timeline and explain their potential consequences.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman undergoes CT for flank pain. The radiologist notes that her transverse colon passes posterior to the duodenum and lies behind the superior mesenteric artery. Which embryologic event best explains this finding?","choices":{"A":"Clockwise rotation of about 90 degrees","B":"Counterclockwise rotation of about 90 degrees","C":"Failed posterior attachment of the ascending colon","D":"Arrested descent of the cecal bud from the liver","E":"Return of the colon before the proximal jejunum"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Reversed rotation occurs when the primary loop rotates 90 degrees clockwise, so the transverse colon passes behind the duodenum and the superior mesenteric artery. Counterclockwise rotation of only 90 degrees produces a left-sided colon. Failed attachment produces a mobile colon. The cecal bud descends from the right upper quadrant, and arrested descent leaves a high cecum, not a colon behind the duodenum and SMA. Colon returning first, before the small bowel, is the pattern of 90-degree incomplete rotation giving a left-sided colon. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Rotation of the midgut; Retraction of herniated loops; Gut rotation defects, Figs. 15.24–15.27, 15.33). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: after normal rotation, the transverse colon passes in front of the duodenum (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the cecal bud lies temporarily in the right upper quadrant below the liver before descending (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 3 — stomach rotation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"The cecal bud descends from the right upper quadrant, and ar","E":"Colon returning first, before the small bowel, is the patter"}},{"id":"sdl21_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate physiologic umbilical herniation, omphalocele, and gastroschisis using developmental timing, defect location, coverings of the viscera, and associated abnormalities.","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman has a routine ultrasound at 20 weeks of gestation. It shows a smooth, membrane-bound midline sac containing bowel and liver at the cord insertion. Which associated finding should next be sought?","choices":{"A":"Renal agenesis","B":"Cardiac anomaly","C":"Hip dysplasia","D":"Cleft palate","E":"Clubfoot deformity"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This is omphalocele, which is associated with cardiac anomalies in about 50%, neural tube defects in about 40%, and chromosomal abnormalities in about 15% of live-born infants. The other anomalies are not the classic associations. Renal agenesis is not a listed omphalocele association; cardiac (50%), neural tube (40%), and chromosomal (15%) defects are. Hip dysplasia is not among omphalocele's associated defects, which are cardiac, neural tube, and chromosomal anomalies. Cleft palate is not a classic omphalocele association; cardiac anomalies are the most common at about 50%. Clubfoot is not among omphalocele's associated defects, which are cardiac, neural tube, and chromosomal anomalies. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Physiologic herniation; Body wall defects, Figs. 15.26, 15.31). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: omphalocele is associated with trisomies 13 and 18 and Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome (First Aid Gastrointestinal, ventral wall defects). COMLEX: omphalocele arises when bowel fails to return from physiologic herniation during weeks 6 to 10 (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Renal agenesis is not a listed omphalocele association; card","C":"Hip dysplasia is not among omphalocele's associated defects,","D":"Cleft palate is not a classic omphalocele association; cardi","E":"Clubfoot is not among omphalocele's associated defects, whic"}},{"id":"sdl21_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate physiologic umbilical herniation, omphalocele, and gastroschisis using developmental timing, defect location, coverings of the viscera, and associated abnormalities.","batch":2,"stem":"A newborn boy is delivered to an 18-year-old mother. Thickened, uncovered bowel loops protrude through a small defect just to the right of a normal umbilical cord. No other anomalies are found. Which developmental error most likely caused this defect?","choices":{"A":"Failure of loops to return from the umbilical cord","B":"Persistence of a patent omphalomesenteric duct","C":"Delayed closure of the umbilical ring after birth","D":"Abnormal closure of the body wall around the stalk","E":"Failed recanalization of the solid gut lumen"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Gastroschisis is most likely due to abnormal closure of the body wall around the connecting stalk. The bowel is exposed to amniotic fluid and may be damaged. Failed return of herniated loops causes omphalocele, and delayed umbilical ring closure causes an umbilical hernia covered by skin. A patent omphalomesenteric duct forms a vitelline fistula draining feces at the umbilicus, not bare loops beside a normal cord. Failed recanalization causes upper duodenal atresia, not a full-thickness abdominal wall defect. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Physiologic herniation; Body wall defects, Figs. 15.26, 15.31). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastroschisis is not commonly associated with chromosomal abnormalities and has a good prognosis (First Aid Gastrointestinal, ventral wall defects). COMLEX: gastroschisis occurs in about 1 per 10,000 births (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"A patent omphalomesenteric duct forms a vitelline fistula dr","E":"Failed recanalization causes upper duodenal atresia, not a f"}},{"id":"sdl21_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate physiologic umbilical herniation, omphalocele, and gastroschisis using developmental timing, defect location, coverings of the viscera, and associated abnormalities.","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman has an early ultrasound at 9 weeks of gestation. Several bowel loops are seen within the base of the umbilical cord. The fetal heart, spine, and limbs appear normal. Which interpretation is most appropriate for this normal-appearing fetus?","choices":{"A":"Omphalocele with amnion covering","B":"Gastroschisis with uncovered loops","C":"Physiologic midgut herniation","D":"Congenital umbilical hernia","E":"Vitelline cyst at the umbilicus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Midgut loops normally herniate into the cord from the 6th week and begin returning in the 10th week, so bowel in the cord at 9 weeks is expected. Omphalocele and gastroschisis are diagnosed later in gestation. A congenital umbilical hernia is skin-covered and pathologic, whereas bowel in the cord at 9 weeks is the normal physiologic herniation. A vitelline cyst is a dilated central duct segment between fibrous cords, not loops of bowel in the cord base. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Physiologic herniation; Body wall defects, Figs. 15.26, 15.31). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a congenital umbilical hernia is covered by skin and often closes spontaneously (First Aid Gastrointestinal, congenital umbilical hernia). COMLEX: herniation occurs because liver growth makes the abdomen temporarily too small (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"A congenital umbilical hernia is skin-covered and pathologic","E":"A vitelline cyst is a dilated central duct segment between f"}},{"id":"sdl21_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate the persistence of different portions of the vitelline duct to Meckel diverticulum, vitelline cysts, and vitelline fistulas and explain their associated bleeding or obstruction.","batch":2,"stem":"A 2-year-old boy has painless passage of a large amount of maroon blood per rectum. Hemoglobin is 8.9 g/dL (reference range: 10.5–13.5 g/dL). A technetium-99m pertechnetate scan shows a focus of uptake in the right lower quadrant. What most likely caused his bleeding?","choices":{"A":"Acid secretion by ectopic gastric mucosa","B":"Ischemia from an ileocolic intussusception","C":"Angiodysplasia within the cecal mucosal wall","D":"Hyperplasia of the ileal lymphoid follicles","E":"Erosion by a swallowed coin or button battery"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The scan detects heterotopic gastric mucosa in an ileal diverticulum. Acid from this mucosa ulcerates adjacent ileal mucosa, causing bleeding or perforation. Intussusception causes pain, angiodysplasia is a disease of older adults, and lymphoid hyperplasia does not take up pertechnetate. A swallowed foreign body would not concentrate pertechnetate, which localizes to ectopic gastric mucosa in a Meckel diverticulum. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Vitelline duct abnormalities, Fig. 15.32). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the pertechnetate scan is called a Meckel scan because it detects heterotopic gastric mucosa (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Meckel diverticulum). COMLEX: heterotopic pancreatic tissue in the diverticulum can also cause ulceration (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 3 — parietal cell HCl secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"A swallowed foreign body would not concentrate pertechnetate"}},{"id":"sdl21_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate the persistence of different portions of the vitelline duct to Meckel diverticulum, vitelline cysts, and vitelline fistulas and explain their associated bleeding or obstruction.","batch":2,"stem":"A 6-year-old girl has colicky pain, bilious vomiting, and distension. At surgery, a cyst lies between the ileum and the umbilicus, attached at both ends by fibrous cords, and a loop of ileum is wrapped around one cord. Which mechanism caused her obstruction?","choices":{"A":"Mucosal ulceration with free perforation","B":"Volvulus of loops on the vitelline ligaments","C":"Telescoping of the ileum into the ascending colon","D":"Aganglionic spasm of a distal colonic segment","E":"Inspissated meconium filling the ileal lumen"},"correct":"B","explanation":"When both ends of the duct become fibrous cords and the middle forms a vitelline cyst, the cords cross the peritoneal cavity, so loops may twist around them, causing obstruction, strangulation, or volvulus. Ulceration is the bleeding mechanism of ectopic mucosa. Telescoping of ileum into colon is intussusception, for which Meckel acts as a lead point, but here the loop is wrapped around a vitelline cord. Aganglionic spasm is Hirschsprung disease of the colon and does not involve vitelline cords. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Vitelline duct abnormalities, Fig. 15.32). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Meckel diverticulum can cause intussusception, volvulus, or obstruction near the terminal ileum (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Meckel diverticulum). COMLEX: a vitelline cyst is also called an enterocystoma (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — parietal cell HCl secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Telescoping of ileum into colon is intussusception, for whic","D":"Aganglionic spasm is Hirschsprung disease of the colon and d"}},{"id":"sdl21_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate the persistence of different portions of the vitelline duct to Meckel diverticulum, vitelline cysts, and vitelline fistulas and explain their associated bleeding or obstruction.","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old man has right lower quadrant pain. At laparoscopy, the appendix is normal, but an inflamed 5-cm outpouching arises from the ileum about 50 cm from the ileocecal valve. Pathology shows mucosa, submucosa, and muscularis propria in its wall. How is this outpouching best classified?","choices":{"A":"Duplication cyst","B":"False diverticulum","C":"Pulsion pseudodiverticulum","D":"Pancreatic rest","E":"True diverticulum"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A persistent vitelline duct remnant contains all layers of the bowel wall, so it is a true diverticulum. False diverticula contain only mucosa and submucosa. Duplication cysts are separate tubular or cystic structures, most often near the ileum. A pulsion pseudodiverticulum lacks the muscularis propria, which this lesion contains. A pancreatic rest is ectopic tissue within a wall, often inside a Meckel diverticulum, not a full-thickness outpouching itself. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Vitelline duct abnormalities, Fig. 15.32); First Aid Gastrointestinal (Meckel diverticulum). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Meckel diverticulum commonly presents in the first 2 years of life (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Meckel diverticulum). COMLEX: about 2% of people have a Meckel diverticulum (lecture notes; Langman gives 2% to 4%).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"A pulsion pseudodiverticulum lacks the muscularis propria, w","D":"A pancreatic rest is ectopic tissue within a wall, often ins"}},{"id":"sdl21_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish intestinal obstruction caused by lack of recanalization from atresia following vascular compromise, and recognize abnormal gut differentiation as another mechanism of atresia.","batch":2,"stem":"A newborn girl has bilious vomiting and distension on day 1. Her mother used cocaine during pregnancy. Abdominal x-ray shows several dilated proximal loops and a gasless colon. At surgery, the proximal jejunum ends blindly, separated from distal bowel by a V-shaped mesenteric gap. Which mechanism best explains this lesion?","choices":{"A":"Ischemic necrosis of a fetal intestinal segment","B":"Failure of solid-cord recanalization of the lumen","C":"Traction by a persistent vitelline fibrous cord","D":"Exposure of loops to amniotic fluid through a defect","E":"Rotation that stopped near 90 degrees of turning"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Atresias distal to the duodenum have been attributed to vascular accidents that compromise blood flow and cause necrosis and loss of a bowel segment. Vasoconstrictive drugs raise this risk. Failure of recanalization is the mechanism for upper duodenal atresia. A persistent vitelline cord obstructs by loops twisting around it, not by leaving a blind jejunum with a V-shaped mesenteric gap. Amniotic exposure damages bare loops in gastroschisis, but no abdominal wall defect is described here. Rotation stopping at 90 degrees gives a left-sided colon and volvulus risk, not a blind-ended jejunum with a mesenteric gap. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Gut atresias and stenoses, Figs. 15.34–15.35); First Aid Gastrointestinal (intestinal atresia). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: jejunal and ileal atresia are associated with cystic fibrosis and gastroschisis (First Aid Gastrointestinal, intestinal atresia). COMLEX: maternal tobacco smoking is a risk factor for jejunal and ileal atresia (First Aid Gastrointestinal, intestinal atresia).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — duodenal recanalization","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"A persistent vitelline cord obstructs by loops twisting arou","D":"Amniotic exposure damages bare loops in gastroschisis, but n","E":"Rotation stopping at 90 degrees gives a left-sided colon and"}},{"id":"sdl21_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish intestinal obstruction caused by lack of recanalization from atresia following vascular compromise, and recognize abnormal gut differentiation as another mechanism of atresia.","batch":2,"stem":"A preterm newborn with low birth weight has bilious vomiting. At surgery, the proximal jejunum ends blindly, and the short distal small intestine is coiled around a thin mesenteric remnant. Which defect is present?","choices":{"A":"Membranous duodenal web","B":"Annular pancreas","C":"Apple peel atresia","D":"Multiple segmental atresias","E":"Midgut duplication cyst"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Apple peel atresia accounts for about 10% of atresias. It occurs in the proximal jejunum, and the shortened distal intestine is coiled around a remnant of the mesentery. Membranous webs and annular pancreas obstruct the duodenum. Multiple atresias are separate segmental gaps, not one short distal bowel coiled helically around a mesenteric remnant. Duplication cysts are separate tubular or cystic structures, most often near the ileum, not a coiled distal bowel after jejunal atresia. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Gut atresias and stenoses, Figs. 15.34–15.35). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: effects of an atresia depend on how much bowel is damaged and where it lies (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: a thin diaphragm separating larger and smaller pieces of bowel accounts for about 20% of atresias (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — duodenal recanalization","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Multiple atresias are separate segmental gaps, not one short","E":"Duplication cysts are separate tubular or cystic structures,"}},{"id":"sdl21_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish intestinal obstruction caused by lack of recanalization from atresia following vascular compromise, and recognize abnormal gut differentiation as another mechanism of atresia.","batch":2,"stem":"A newborn boy has several separate ileal atresias found at surgery. The mesenteric vessels are intact, there was no malrotation or volvulus, and an older sister had a similar defect. Which mechanism most likely explains his atresias?","choices":{"A":"Intrauterine volvulus with segment infarction","B":"Failed recanalization of the solid lumen","C":"Maternal vasoconstrictive drug exposure","D":"Faulty gene-directed gut differentiation","E":"Amniotic injury through an abdominal wall defect"},"correct":"D","explanation":"New evidence suggests that problems with gut differentiation, including misexpression of developmental genes and growth factor receptors, can cause atresias without a vascular accident. Intact vessels and no volvulus argue against ischemia, and recanalization failure applies to the upper duodenum. Maternal vasoconstrictive drugs act through vascular accidents, which intact vessels here argue against. Amniotic injury requires an abdominal wall defect like gastroschisis, which is not described. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Gut atresias and stenoses, Figs. 15.34–15.35). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: stenoses and multiple atresias each account for about 5% of these defects (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: about 33% of intestinal duplications are associated with other defects such as atresias (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — duodenal recanalization","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Maternal vasoconstrictive drugs act through vascular acciden","E":"Amniotic injury requires an abdominal wall defect like gastr"}},{"id":"sdl21_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply developmental origins and arterial supply of the small intestine to distinguish its epithelial, muscular and vascular components.","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old man with atrial fibrillation has sudden severe periumbilical pain. CT angiography shows an embolus occluding the superior mesenteric artery just beyond its origin. Which bowel segment is most likely to be spared from ischemia?","choices":{"A":"Proximal jejunum","B":"Terminal ileum","C":"Descending colon","D":"Cecum and appendix","E":"Ascending colon"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The descending colon is a hindgut derivative supplied by the inferior mesenteric artery. The jejunum, ileum, cecum, and ascending colon are midgut derivatives supplied by the superior mesenteric artery. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Summary; Table 15.1). Textbook enrichment (Langman) — detail not in SDL 21 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the hindgut extends from the distal third of the transverse colon to the anal canal above the pectinate line (First Aid Gastrointestinal, normal embryology). COMLEX: the left third of the transverse colon is a hindgut derivative (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Langman"},{"id":"sdl21_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply developmental origins and arterial supply of the small intestine to distinguish its epithelial, muscular and vascular components.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old woman has severe celiac trunk stenosis, but her distal duodenum stays well perfused. Which landmark marks the embryologic boundary between foregut and midgut territory in her duodenum?","choices":{"A":"Pyloric sphincter","B":"Minor duodenal papilla","C":"Duodenojejunal flexure","D":"First-part duodenal bulb","E":"Major duodenal papilla"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The foregut includes the duodenum to the level of the liver and ventral pancreatic buds (major papilla), and the midgut begins just distal to the entrance of the bile duct. The duodenum caudal to the major papilla is therefore midgut. The pylorus is proximal to the major papilla and so lies in foregut territory. The minor papilla is not the boundary; the foregut-midgut transition is just distal to the major papilla where the bile duct enters. The duodenojejunal flexure lies well within midgut territory, distal to the boundary at the major papilla. The duodenal bulb is proximal duodenum supplied by the celiac trunk, not the boundary. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Summary; Table 15.1). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the foregut extends from the esophagus to the duodenum at the level of the ampulla of Vater (First Aid Gastrointestinal, normal embryology). COMLEX: the liver, pancreas, and biliary apparatus grow out of the upper duodenal endoderm (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The pylorus is proximal to the major papilla and so lies in ","B":"The minor papilla is not the boundary; the foregut-midgut tr","C":"The duodenojejunal flexure lies well within midgut territory","D":"The duodenal bulb is proximal duodenum supplied by the celia"}},{"id":"sdl21_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply developmental origins and arterial supply of the small intestine to distinguish its epithelial, muscular and vascular components.","batch":2,"stem":"A newborn with holoprosencephaly has a loss-of-function mutation in a secreted signaling gene, and imaging shows gut malformations as well. In the developing gut, which signal from the endoderm patterns the surrounding mesoderm along the craniocaudal axis?","choices":{"A":"Retinoic acid","B":"Wnt signaling","C":"Bone morphogenetic protein 4","D":"Sonic hedgehog","E":"Fibroblast growth factor 10"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Gut endoderm secretes sonic hedgehog, which induces HOX genes in the surrounding mesoderm that then regulate the craniocaudal organization of the gut. A retinoic acid gradient specifies gut regions but is not the endodermal signal to mesoderm. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Summary; Table 15.1). Textbook enrichment (Langman) — detail not in SDL 21 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: HOX genes in gut mesoderm, induced by endodermal signaling, regulate craniocaudal organization of the gut and its derivatives (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: an RA gradient causes region-specific transcription factors along the gut tube (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 1 — signals triggering liver development","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Langman"},{"id":"trial_sdl21_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply normal midgut rotation and intestinal positioning to distinguish incomplete rotation from reversed rotation, including timeline and explain their potential consequences.","batch":3,"stem":"A 27-year-old woman is at 11 weeks of gestation, and her fetus is undergoing normal midgut return. Where does the cecal bud lie just after it reenters the abdomen?","choices":{"A":"Right upper quadrant","B":"Left upper quadrant","C":"Right lower quadrant","D":"Left lower quadrant","E":"Pelvic cavity"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The cecal bud is the last part to reenter and lies temporarily in the right upper quadrant below the right lobe of the liver. It then descends into the right iliac fossa. A left-sided colon results only from incomplete midgut rotation, not from normal return of the midgut. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Retraction of herniated loops, Fig. 15.27). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in about 50% of people the appendix is retrocecal or retrocolic because it develops during colonic descent (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the appendix forms from the distal end of the cecal bud (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 3 — stomach rotation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"A left-sided colon results only from incomplete midgut rotat"}},{"id":"trial_sdl21_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate physiologic umbilical herniation, omphalocele, and gastroschisis using developmental timing, defect location, coverings of the viscera, and associated abnormalities.","batch":3,"stem":"A newborn has a midline membrane-covered umbilical sac, macroglossia, and hypoglycemia. Which syndrome is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Down syndrome","B":"Turner syndrome","C":"Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome","D":"DiGeorge syndrome","E":"Marfan syndrome"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Omphalocele is associated with Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome and trisomies 13 and 18. Macroglossia and neonatal hypoglycemia point to Beckwith-Wiedemann. Sources: First Aid Gastrointestinal (ventral wall defects); Langman Ch. 15 (Body wall defects). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: about 15% of live-born infants with omphalocele have chromosomal abnormalities (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: omphalocele reflects failed return of physiologically herniated loops, whereas gastroschisis reflects abnormal body wall closure around the connecting stalk (lecture notes; Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl21_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate the persistence of different portions of the vitelline duct to Meckel diverticulum, vitelline cysts, and vitelline fistulas and explain their associated bleeding or obstruction.","batch":3,"stem":"A 35-year-old man asks how common his incidentally found ileal diverticulum is. What fraction of people have one?","choices":{"A":"About 0.1%","B":"About 10%","C":"About 25%","D":"About 40%","E":"About 2%"},"correct":"E","explanation":"About 2% of people have a persistent proximal vitelline duct forming an ileal diverticulum, matching First Aid's rule of 2s; Langman gives a range of 2% to 4%. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Vitelline duct abnormalities). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Meckel diverticulum may contain two types of ectopic epithelium, gastric and pancreatic (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Meckel diverticulum). COMLEX: Meckel diverticulum can present with painless hematochezia or melena (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Meckel diverticulum).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl21_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish intestinal obstruction caused by lack of recanalization from atresia following vascular compromise, and recognize abnormal gut differentiation as another mechanism of atresia.","batch":3,"stem":"A 1-day-old girl has bilious vomiting from jejunal atresia. Which finding is expected on her abdominal x-ray?","choices":{"A":"Triple bubble sign","B":"Double bubble sign","C":"String sign","D":"Bird beak sign","E":"Apple core lesion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Jejunal and ileal atresia may show a triple bubble (dilated stomach, duodenum, and proximal jejunum) with a gasless colon. A double bubble suggests duodenal atresia. A string sign reflects the narrowed lumen of Crohn disease with transmural thickening, not neonatal atresia. A bird-beak sign on barium swallow is the classic finding of achalasia at the lower esophageal sphincter. An apple core lesion is a stenosing malignancy seen on barium study, as in colorectal or esophageal cancer. Sources: First Aid Gastrointestinal (intestinal atresia); Langman Ch. 15 (Gut atresias and stenoses). Textbook enrichment (Langman) — detail not in SDL 21 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: in jejunal and ileal atresia, ischemic necrosis leads to segmental resorption and discontinuous bowel (First Aid Gastrointestinal, intestinal atresia). COMLEX: a narrowing with a thin diaphragm is one of the common atresia patterns (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — duodenal recanalization","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Langman","whyNot":{"C":"A string sign reflects the narrowed lumen of Crohn disease w","D":"A bird-beak sign on barium swallow is the classic finding of","E":"An apple core lesion is a stenosing malignancy seen on bariu"}},{"id":"trial_sdl21_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply developmental origins and arterial supply of the small intestine to distinguish its epithelial, muscular and vascular components.","batch":3,"stem":"A newborn has gut segments with abnormal regional identity. Which signal specifies gut regions along the craniocaudal axis before epithelial-mesenchymal interactions?","choices":{"A":"Sonic hedgehog gradient","B":"Retinoic acid gradient","C":"BMP4 gradient","D":"FGF10 gradient","E":"Indian hedgehog gradient"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Langman states that regions of the gut tube such as the esophagus, stomach, and duodenum are specified by a retinoic acid gradient that induces region-specific transcription factors. Sonic hedgehog, BMP4, FGF10, and Indian hedgehog act later in reciprocal epithelial-mesenchymal signaling rather than setting craniocaudal regional identity. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Summary). Textbook enrichment (Langman) — detail not in SDL 21 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: differentiation of gut derivatives depends on reciprocal endoderm-mesoderm interactions (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the gut tube extends from the oropharyngeal membrane to the cloacal membrane (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 1 — signals triggering liver development","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Langman"},{"id":"trial_sdl21_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply normal midgut rotation and intestinal positioning to distinguish incomplete rotation from reversed rotation, including timeline and explain their potential consequences.","batch":3,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman has recurrent right-sided abdominal pain. CT shows small bowel loops trapped behind a poorly attached ascending colon. Which defect is present?","choices":{"A":"Paraduodenal hernia","B":"Umbilical hernia","C":"Hiatal hernia","D":"Retrocolic hernia","E":"Inguinal hernia"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Incomplete attachment of the ascending colon to the posterior body wall may leave retrocolic pockets, and small intestine trapped behind the ascending colon forms a retrocolic hernia. A paraduodenal hernia traps intestine in the fossa to the left of the ascending duodenum near the duodenojejunal flexure, not behind the ascending colon. A congenital umbilical hernia is a skin-covered defect from delayed closure of the umbilical ring, not entrapment behind the colon. A sliding hiatal hernia moves the gastroesophageal junction and cardia into the posterior mediastinum. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Abnormalities of the mesenteries). Textbook enrichment (Langman) — detail not in SDL 21 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a mobile ascending colon that fails to attach may twist, causing volvulus of the cecum and colon (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: Toldt fascia lies between peritoneal layers where colon attaches to the posterior wall (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 3 — stomach rotation","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Langman","whyNot":{"A":"A paraduodenal hernia traps intestine in the fossa to the le","B":"A congenital umbilical hernia is a skin-covered defect from ","C":"A sliding hiatal hernia moves the gastroesophageal junction "}}]},{"sdlNumber":22,"title":"SDL 22 — Embryology of the Large Intestine","questions":[{"id":"sdl22_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Relate the midgut and hindgut contributions to the large intestine to their boundaries, epithelial origin and arterial supply.","batch":1,"stem":"Which artery supplies the cecum and ascending colon?","choices":{"A":"Celiac arterial trunk","B":"Internal iliac artery","C":"Superior mesenteric artery","D":"Median sacral artery","E":"Splenic artery"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The cecum and ascending colon are midgut derivatives and are supplied by the superior mesenteric artery through its ileocolic and right colic branches. The celiac trunk and splenic artery supply foregut structures, and the internal iliac and median sacral arteries supply pelvic structures rather than midgut colon. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (midgut and hindgut contributions); Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Hindgut; Table 15.1). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the midgut ends where the proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon meet the distal third (First Aid Gastrointestinal, normal embryology). COMLEX: the ileocolic, right colic, and middle colic branches serve the colonic part of this territory (SDL 22 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Relate the midgut and hindgut contributions to the large intestine to their boundaries, epithelial origin and arterial supply.","batch":1,"stem":"Where along the transverse colon does midgut territory end and hindgut territory begin?","choices":{"A":"Junction of its proximal two-thirds and distal third","B":"Junction of its proximal third and distal two-thirds","C":"Midpoint between the two colic flexures","D":"Hepatic flexure at the right colic angle","E":"Splenic flexure at the left colic angle"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The midgut extends to the junction of the proximal two-thirds and distal third of the transverse colon; the distal third belongs to the hindgut. The proximal-third split reverses the proportions, the midpoint is not the boundary, the hepatic flexure lies well within midgut territory, and the boundary lies just before the splenic flexure rather than at it. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (midgut and hindgut contributions); Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Hindgut; Table 15.1). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: this boundary is why the splenic flexure region is a watershed between superior and inferior mesenteric supply (First Aid Gastrointestinal, GI blood supply). COMLEX: hindgut derivatives continue caudally to the pectinate line of the anal canal (SDL 22 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Relate the midgut and hindgut contributions to the large intestine to their boundaries, epithelial origin and arterial supply.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following large intestine structures develops from the midgut?","choices":{"A":"Sigmoid colon","B":"Vermiform appendix","C":"Descending colon","D":"Rectum","E":"Upper anal canal"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The midgut forms the cecum, vermiform appendix, ascending colon, and proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon. The sigmoid colon, descending colon, rectum, and upper anal canal all come from the hindgut and receive inferior mesenteric supply. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (midgut and hindgut contributions); Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Hindgut; Table 15.1). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the appendix forms from the distal end of the cecal bud (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: about half of appendices lie retrocecal or retrocolic because the appendix grows during colonic descent (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply normal cecal descent and mesenteric attachment to explain abnormal colonic positioning, increased mobility, and intestinal entrapment.","batch":1,"stem":"Which connective tissue layer anchors the ascending and descending colon to the posterior abdominal wall?","choices":{"A":"Scarpa fascia","B":"Camper fascia","C":"Toldt fascia","D":"Denonvilliers fascia","E":"Transversalis fascia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"As the ascending and descending colon settle against the posterior wall, their dorsal mesenteries fuse with the parietal peritoneum through Toldt fascia. Scarpa and Camper fascia are layers of the anterior abdominal subcutaneous tissue, Denonvilliers fascia lies between rectum and prostate, and transversalis fascia lines the inner abdominal wall. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (cecal descent and mesenteric attachment); Langman Ch. 15 (Retraction of herniated loops; Mesenteries of the intestinal loops; Abnormalities of the mesenteries). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: surgeons mobilize the colon along the white line of Toldt during colectomy (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the mesentery of the sigmoid colon persists, so this segment stays intraperitoneal (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 1 — midgut rotation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply normal cecal descent and mesenteric attachment to explain abnormal colonic positioning, increased mobility, and intestinal entrapment.","batch":1,"stem":"Once their dorsal mesenteries fuse with the parietal peritoneum, the ascending and descending colon are classified as which type of structure?","choices":{"A":"Primarily retroperitoneal","B":"Intraperitoneal on a mesentery","C":"Infraperitoneal in the pelvis","D":"Extraperitoneal in the wall","E":"Secondarily retroperitoneal"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The colon first hangs on a dorsal mesentery and becomes secondarily retroperitoneal after fusion with the posterior wall. Primary retroperitoneal organs, such as the kidneys, develop without a mesentery; intraperitoneal describes segments that keep a mesentery, such as the transverse and sigmoid colon; infraperitoneal and extraperitoneal describe pelvic or body wall positions. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (cecal descent and mesenteric attachment); Langman Ch. 15 (Retraction of herniated loops; Mesenteries of the intestinal loops; Abnormalities of the mesenteries). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the duodenum except its first part and the pancreas except its tail are also secondarily retroperitoneal (First Aid Gastrointestinal, retroperitoneal structures). COMLEX: the transverse mesocolon fuses with the greater omentum rather than the posterior wall (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 1 — midgut rotation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply normal cecal descent and mesenteric attachment to explain abnormal colonic positioning, increased mobility, and intestinal entrapment.","batch":1,"stem":"After midgut rotation, which process carries the cecum from the right upper quadrant to the right lower quadrant?","choices":{"A":"Rotation of the stomach","B":"Elongation of the colon","C":"Enlargement of the liver","D":"Regression of the mesonephros","E":"Fusion of the mesocolon"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The cecal bud lies first in the right upper quadrant below the liver, and lengthening of the colon carries the cecum and appendix caudally into the right iliac fossa. Gastric rotation shapes the foregut, liver growth and mesonephric regression influence the earlier return of herniated loops, and mesocolic fusion fixes the colon after it is in place. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (cecal descent and mesenteric attachment); Langman Ch. 15 (Retraction of herniated loops; Mesenteries of the intestinal loops; Abnormalities of the mesenteries). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the cecal bud is the last part of the herniated loop to reenter the abdomen (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: a cecum that stays high produces a subhepatic appendix with atypical right upper quadrant pain (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 1 — midgut rotation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish anorectal malformations caused by abnormal cloacal or urorectal septum development from imperforate anus caused by failure of anal membrane breakdown.","batch":1,"stem":"Which structure forms between the anal and urogenital openings after the cloaca is divided?","choices":{"A":"Genital tubercle","B":"Urogenital fold","C":"Anal column","D":"Perineal body","E":"Labioscrotal swelling"},"correct":"D","explanation":"When the cloacal membrane ruptures, the tip of the dividing mesodermal wedge remains between the anal and urogenital openings as the perineal body (Langman-only detail; not in SDL 22 lecture notes). The genital tubercle, urogenital folds, and labioscrotal swellings are external genital primordia, and anal columns are mucosal folds of the upper anal canal. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut; tip of urorectal septum → perineal body, Figs. 15.36–15.37); SDL 22 lecture notes (urorectal septum context). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the perineal body anchors the perineal muscles and can be torn during vaginal delivery (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the allantois enters the anterior cloaca and the hindgut enters the posterior cloaca (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — foregut partitioning by a septum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish anorectal malformations caused by abnormal cloacal or urorectal septum development from imperforate anus caused by failure of anal membrane breakdown.","batch":1,"stem":"At the end of which week does the anal membrane (posterior cloacal membrane) break down?","choices":{"A":"Fourth week","B":"Seventh week","C":"Fifth week","D":"Tenth week","E":"Twelfth week"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The anal membrane (the posterior portion of the cloacal membrane) degenerates at the end of the seventh week, opening communication between the anal pit and the hindgut. The fourth and fifth weeks are too early because the cloaca is still being partitioned, and the tenth and twelfth weeks correspond to return of the herniated midgut. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (imperforate anus / anal membrane); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: rectourethral and rectovaginal fistulas occur in about 1 in 5,000 live births (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: these defects likely reflect misexpression of genes during epithelial–mesenchymal signaling (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — foregut partitioning by a septum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish anorectal malformations caused by abnormal cloacal or urorectal septum development from imperforate anus caused by failure of anal membrane breakdown.","batch":1,"stem":"If the urorectal septum fails to extend far enough caudally, how is the hindgut opening displaced?","choices":{"A":"Posterior shift of the hindgut opening","B":"Lateral shift of the hindgut opening","C":"Cranial shift of the hindgut opening","D":"Duplication of the hindgut opening","E":"Anterior shift of the hindgut opening"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A small cloaca or a urorectal septum that stops short causes the hindgut opening to shift anteriorly, so it can empty into the urethra, vagina, or perineum. Posterior, lateral, and cranial displacement are not described, and duplication is a separate anomaly unrelated to septal growth. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (anorectal malformations vs imperforate anus); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut; Hindgut abnormalities, Figs. 15.36–15.37). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: an anterior opening explains meconium in the urine of a boy with a rectourethral fistula (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: some variants end in rectal atresia or a fibrous cord to the perineum (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — foregut partitioning by a septum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the dual embryologic origin of the anal canal to explain the epithelial transition at the pectinate line and the arterial supplies above and below it.","batch":1,"stem":"Which epithelium lines the anal canal just below the pectinate line?","choices":{"A":"Pseudostratified columnar epithelium","B":"Transitional epithelium","C":"Stratified squamous epithelium","D":"Simple cuboidal epithelium","E":"Stratified cuboidal epithelium"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Below the pectinate line the ectodermal mucosa is stratified squamous epithelium that continues to the perianal skin. Pseudostratified columnar epithelium lines the airways, transitional epithelium lines the urinary tract, simple cuboidal epithelium lines ducts and tubules, and stratified cuboidal epithelium lines larger gland ducts. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (dual origin of the anal canal); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut, Fig. 15.36). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: tumors below the line are typically squamous cell carcinomas (First Aid Gastrointestinal, pectinate line). COMLEX: an anal transitional zone lies at the junction of the two epithelial types (SDL 22 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 5 — hemorrhoids and anal canal tumors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the dual embryologic origin of the anal canal to explain the epithelial transition at the pectinate line and the arterial supplies above and below it.","batch":1,"stem":"The superior rectal artery is the terminal continuation of which vessel?","choices":{"A":"Inferior mesenteric artery","B":"Internal iliac artery","C":"Internal pudendal artery","D":"Median sacral artery","E":"Left colic artery"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The superior rectal artery continues the inferior mesenteric artery, the artery of the hindgut, and supplies the anal canal above the pectinate line. The internal iliac and internal pudendal arteries supply the region below the line, the median sacral artery is a small midline branch of the aorta, and the left colic artery is a separate, more proximal branch. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (dual origin of the anal canal); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut, Fig. 15.36). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: internal hemorrhoids above the line drain to the superior rectal vein and portal system (First Aid Gastrointestinal, pectinate line). COMLEX: sigmoidal branches arise from the same parent vessel between the left colic and terminal branch (SDL 22 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 5 — hemorrhoids and anal canal tumors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the dual embryologic origin of the anal canal to explain the epithelial transition at the pectinate line and the arterial supplies above and below it.","batch":1,"stem":"Which mucosal landmark lies immediately above the pectinate line?","choices":{"A":"Anal verge","B":"Perianal skin","C":"Intersphincteric groove","D":"Anal columns","E":"Levator ani"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The pectinate line, which marks the junction of endodermal and ectodermal parts of the anal canal, lies just below the anal columns. The anal verge and perianal skin lie below it at the outer margin, the intersphincteric groove lies between the sphincters below the line, and the levator ani is pelvic floor muscle rather than a mucosal landmark. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (dual origin of the anal canal); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut, Fig. 15.36). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: visceral pain fibers serve the canal above the line, and somatic fibers serve it below (First Aid Gastrointestinal, pectinate line). COMLEX: the cranial two-thirds of the anal canal come from the primitive anorectal canal (SDL 22 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 5 — hemorrhoids and anal canal tumors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Relate neural crest cell migration and RET function to the development of intestinal parasympathetic ganglia and congenital megacolon.","batch":1,"stem":"Parasympathetic ganglion cells of the enteric nervous system derive from which embryonic cells?","choices":{"A":"Splanchnic mesoderm cells","B":"Paraxial mesoderm cells","C":"Surface ectoderm cells","D":"Neural tube cells","E":"Neural crest cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Enteric parasympathetic ganglion cells come from neural crest cells that migrate from the neural folds into the bowel wall. Splanchnic mesoderm forms the gut muscle and connective tissue, paraxial mesoderm forms somites, surface ectoderm forms epidermis, and the neural tube forms the central nervous system. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (neural crest, RET, congenital megacolon); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut abnormalities). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: neural crest derivatives also include adrenal medulla, melanocytes, and Schwann cells (First Aid Embryology, germ layer derivatives). COMLEX: the endothelin-3 and EDNRB pathway also supports these migrating precursors (SDL 22 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Relate neural crest cell migration and RET function to the development of intestinal parasympathetic ganglia and congenital megacolon.","batch":1,"stem":"The RET gene that is mutated in congenital megacolon encodes which type of protein?","choices":{"A":"Ligand-gated ion channel","B":"G protein-coupled receptor","C":"Nuclear hormone receptor","D":"Receptor tyrosine kinase","E":"Serine threonine kinase"},"correct":"D","explanation":"RET encodes a receptor tyrosine kinase required for proliferation, survival, and directed migration of enteric neural crest precursors. Ligand-gated channels, G protein-coupled receptors, nuclear hormone receptors, and serine threonine kinases are other signaling classes not encoded by RET. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (neural crest, RET, congenital megacolon); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut abnormalities). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gain-of-function RET mutations cause MEN 2A and 2B, the opposite of the loss of function in megacolon (First Aid Endocrinology, MEN syndromes). COMLEX: RET is discussed further with neural crest development in Langman Ch. 18.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Relate neural crest cell migration and RET function to the development of intestinal parasympathetic ganglia and congenital megacolon.","batch":1,"stem":"Which bowel segment is involved in nearly every case of aganglionic megacolon?","choices":{"A":"Rectum","B":"Cecum","C":"Appendix","D":"Jejunum","E":"Ileum"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Aganglionosis begins distally and extends proximally for a variable distance, so the rectum is involved in nearly all cases. The cecum and appendix are affected only in long-segment or total colonic disease, and the jejunum and ileum only in rare extended forms. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (neural crest, RET, congenital megacolon); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut abnormalities). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: suction rectal biopsy is the diagnostic test (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Hirschsprung disease). COMLEX: in about 80% the defect extends to the midpoint of the sigmoid, and in about 3% the whole colon is affected (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Relate the midgut and hindgut contributions to the large intestine to their boundaries, epithelial origin and arterial supply.","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old man with atrial fibrillation has sudden severe periumbilical pain out of proportion to a soft abdomen. His pulse is 112/min and blood pressure is 104/66 mm Hg. Serum lactate is 4.8 mmol/L (reference range: 0.5–2.2 mmol/L). CT angiography shows an embolus occluding the first anterior branch of the aorta below the celiac trunk, just past its origin. Which colonic segment is at greatest risk of infarction?","choices":{"A":"Descending colon","B":"Sigmoid colon","C":"Ascending colon","D":"Rectum","E":"Distal transverse colon"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The occluded vessel is the superior mesenteric artery, which supplies midgut colon: cecum, appendix, ascending colon, and proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon. The descending colon, sigmoid colon, rectum, and distal third of the transverse colon are hindgut and receive inferior mesenteric supply. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (midgut and hindgut contributions); Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Hindgut; Table 15.1). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: acute mesenteric ischemia classically follows embolism from atrial fibrillation (First Aid Gastrointestinal, acute mesenteric ischemia). COMLEX: pain out of proportion to examination is the early bedside clue (First Aid Gastrointestinal).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 2 — ischemic bowel disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Relate the midgut and hindgut contributions to the large intestine to their boundaries, epithelial origin and arterial supply.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman undergoes open aortic aneurysm repair during which the vessel arising from the anterior aorta at the level of L3 is ligated. Two days later she has left lower quadrant pain and bloody diarrhea. Her pulse is 98/min and blood pressure is 128/74 mm Hg. Colonoscopy shows ischemic mucosa from just beyond the left colic flexure to the rectosigmoid. Which other structure normally depends on the same arterial territory?","choices":{"A":"Lower third of the anal canal","B":"Cecum and vermiform appendix","C":"Proximal two-thirds of transverse colon","D":"Upper two-thirds of the anal canal","E":"Terminal ileum and ileocecal valve"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The ligated vessel is the inferior mesenteric artery, the artery of the hindgut, which supplies the distal transverse colon, descending and sigmoid colon, rectum, and upper two-thirds of the anal canal. The lower third of the anal canal is ectodermal and supplied by inferior rectal branches, while the cecum, appendix, proximal transverse colon, and terminal ileum are midgut. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (midgut and hindgut contributions); Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Hindgut; Table 15.1). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colonic ischemia after aortic surgery reflects loss of inferior mesenteric inflow (First Aid Gastrointestinal, colonic ischemia). COMLEX: the left colic, sigmoidal, and superior rectal arteries are the named branches of this vessel (SDL 22 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Relate the midgut and hindgut contributions to the large intestine to their boundaries, epithelial origin and arterial supply.","batch":2,"stem":"A 78-year-old man is admitted with septic shock, and his blood pressure falls to 78/42 mm Hg for several hours. Two days later he has crampy left upper quadrant pain and passes maroon stool. His pulse is 104/min. Serum lactate is 3.1 mmol/L (reference range: 0.5–2.2 mmol/L). Colonoscopy shows segmental ischemia centered near the left colic flexure. The ischemic region lies at the watershed between the terminal branches of which arteries?","choices":{"A":"Ileocolic and right colic arteries","B":"Middle colic and left colic arteries","C":"Right colic and middle colic arteries","D":"Left colic and sigmoidal arteries","E":"Sigmoidal and superior rectal arteries"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The left colic flexure region lies near the midgut–hindgut boundary, where terminal middle colic branches of the superior mesenteric artery meet left colic branches of the inferior mesenteric artery. The ileocolic, right colic, and middle colic pairs lie within midgut territory, and the left colic, sigmoidal, and superior rectal pairs lie within hindgut territory. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (midgut and hindgut contributions); Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Hindgut; Table 15.1). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the splenic flexure and rectosigmoid junction are the classic watershed sites for colonic ischemia (First Aid Gastrointestinal, colonic ischemia). COMLEX: hypotension rather than occlusion is the usual cause of this pattern (First Aid Gastrointestinal).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 2 — ischemic bowel disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply normal cecal descent and mesenteric attachment to explain abnormal colonic positioning, increased mobility, and intestinal entrapment.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman has 12 hours of colicky abdominal pain, distension, and vomiting. She has had two similar milder episodes. Her pulse is 108/min and blood pressure is 118/72 mm Hg. CT shows a dilated cecum displaced into the left upper quadrant with a whirl of mesenteric vessels at its base. Which anatomic feature of her colon most likely allowed this twist?","choices":{"A":"Elongated mesentery of the sigmoid colon","B":"Narrowed lumen at the ileocecal valve","C":"Persistent free mesentery of the ascending colon","D":"Absent ganglion cells in the rectum","E":"Thickened teniae coli of the cecum"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Failure of the ascending colon to fuse with the posterior wall leaves a free mesentery, so the mobile cecum and colon can twist around their vascular pedicle and obstruct. An elongated sigmoid mesentery predisposes to sigmoid twisting rather than a displaced cecum, a narrow ileocecal valve does not cause rotation, rectal aganglionosis causes functional obstruction, and teniae do not create mobility. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (cecal descent and mesenteric attachment); Langman Ch. 15 (Retraction of herniated loops; Mesenteries of the intestinal loops; Abnormalities of the mesenteries). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: volvulus of the cecum and colon can progress to ischemic gangrene (SDL 22 notes). COMLEX: volvulus is one of the four major causes of intestinal obstruction, most often in the sigmoid colon (First Aid Gastrointestinal, volvulus).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 1 — intestinal obstruction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply normal cecal descent and mesenteric attachment to explain abnormal colonic positioning, increased mobility, and intestinal entrapment.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old man with no prior abdominal surgery has intermittent cramping pain and bilious vomiting for 2 days. His pulse is 96/min and blood pressure is 132/80 mm Hg. CT shows a cluster of dilated small bowel loops lying behind the right colon within an abnormal peritoneal recess. Which developmental error most likely produced this recess?","choices":{"A":"Incomplete fusion of the ascending mesocolon","B":"Persistent vitelline duct at the umbilicus","C":"Failed return of the herniated midgut","D":"Reversed rotation of the midgut loop","E":"Arrested descent of the sigmoid colon"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Incomplete or irregular fusion of the ascending mesocolon with the posterior wall leaves retrocolic pockets in which small intestine can become trapped and obstruct. A vitelline duct remnant causes a diverticulum or fistula, failed return of the midgut produces an umbilical defect, reversed rotation places the duodenum anterior to the transverse colon, and the sigmoid does not descend in the same way. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (cecal descent and mesenteric attachment); Langman Ch. 15 (Retraction of herniated loops; Mesenteries of the intestinal loops; Abnormalities of the mesenteries). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hernias and adhesions are leading causes of small bowel obstruction (First Aid Gastrointestinal, intestinal obstruction). COMLEX: internal hernias can occur without surgical history when mesenteric fusion is abnormal (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 1 — midgut rotation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply normal cecal descent and mesenteric attachment to explain abnormal colonic positioning, increased mobility, and intestinal entrapment.","batch":2,"stem":"A 9-year-old boy has 18 hours of fever, anorexia, and right upper quadrant pain. His temperature is 38.4°C (101.1°F) and pulse is 110/min. Leukocyte count is 15,800/mm3 (reference range: 4,500–11,000/mm3). Ultrasonography shows a normal gallbladder and an inflamed appendix lying beneath the right lobe of the liver at the end of a high-riding colon. Which developmental process most likely failed?","choices":{"A":"Counterclockwise rotation of the midgut","B":"Caudal descent of the cecal bud","C":"Fusion of the descending mesocolon","D":"Recanalization of the appendiceal lumen","E":"Return of loops from the umbilical cord"},"correct":"B","explanation":"After rotation the cecum lies in the right upper quadrant and normally descends into the right iliac fossa; when it fails to descend, the cecum and appendix stay subhepatic and appendicitis presents with right upper quadrant pain. Failed rotation places the colon on the left, descending mesocolic fusion affects the left side, the appendix does not undergo recanalization, and failed return causes an umbilical defect. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (cecal descent and mesenteric attachment); Langman Ch. 15 (Retraction of herniated loops; Mesenteries of the intestinal loops; Abnormalities of the mesenteries). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: periumbilical pain migrating to McBurney point is the classic presentation when the appendix is in its usual position (First Aid Gastrointestinal, appendicitis). COMLEX: atypical appendix positions delay diagnosis and raise perforation risk (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 5 — appendicitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish anorectal malformations caused by abnormal cloacal or urorectal septum development from imperforate anus caused by failure of anal membrane breakdown.","batch":2,"stem":"A 1-day-old boy has a flat perineum with no anal opening. His vital signs are normal. Over the next day, greenish particulate material appears in his urine. Imaging confirms a rectourinary fistula. Which embryologic error most likely caused this malformation?","choices":{"A":"Arrested caudal growth of the urorectal septum","B":"Failed proliferation of proctodeal ectoderm","C":"Failed recanalization of the rectal lumen","D":"Absent neural crest cells in the rectum","E":"Persistent vitelline duct at the umbilicus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"When the cloaca is too small or the urorectal septum does not extend far enough caudally, the hindgut opening shifts anteriorly into the urethra, producing a rectourethral fistula. Proctodeal ectoderm forms the anal pit, the rectum is not described as recanalizing, neural crest loss causes megacolon without a fistula, and a vitelline duct remnant opens at the umbilicus. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (anorectal malformations vs imperforate anus); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut; Hindgut abnormalities, Figs. 15.36–15.37). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: meconium in the urine of a newborn boy signals a rectourinary tract communication (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: anorectal malformations often occur with vertebral, cardiac, renal, and limb defects in VACTERL association (First Aid Genetics, VACTERL).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 2 — partitioning by a septum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish anorectal malformations caused by abnormal cloacal or urorectal septum development from imperforate anus caused by failure of anal membrane breakdown.","batch":2,"stem":"A 1-day-old girl has no anal opening in its normal position. On examination, meconium is seen passing from the vaginal introitus. Her vital signs are normal and the abdomen is mildly distended. Abnormal partitioning between which two embryonic compartments most likely caused this finding?","choices":{"A":"Allantoic duct and yolk sac","B":"Midgut and hindgut loops","C":"Urogenital sinus and anorectal canal","D":"Mesonephric and paramesonephric ducts","E":"Bladder and the urachus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The urorectal septum normally divides the cloaca into the urogenital sinus anteriorly and the primitive anorectal canal posteriorly; incomplete separation leaves a rectovaginal fistula. The allantois and yolk sac are extraembryonic, the midgut–hindgut junction is in the transverse colon, the genital ducts form internal reproductive organs, and a urachal defect drains urine at the umbilicus. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (anorectal malformations vs imperforate anus); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut; Hindgut abnormalities, Figs. 15.36–15.37). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: rectovaginal fistula is the female counterpart of the rectourethral fistula (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the cloaca is endoderm-lined and bounded ventrally by surface ectoderm (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 2 — partitioning by a septum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish anorectal malformations caused by abnormal cloacal or urorectal septum development from imperforate anus caused by failure of anal membrane breakdown.","batch":2,"stem":"A 1-day-old boy has not passed meconium, and his abdomen is distended. A normally located anal dimple is present, and a thin bluish bulging membrane is seen at its center. No meconium appears in the urine or on the perineum, and imaging shows no fistula. The persistent tissue separates the hindgut from which structure?","choices":{"A":"Allantois","B":"Urachus","C":"Phallus","D":"Bladder","E":"Anal pit"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Imperforate anus occurs when the anal membrane fails to break down, so the hindgut stays closed off from the anal pit, the ectodermal depression of the proctodeum. The allantois and its remnant, the urachus, run from the bladder toward the umbilicus, the phallus forms from the genital tubercle, and the bladder forms from the urogenital sinus anterior to the septum. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (anorectal malformations vs imperforate anus); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut; Hindgut abnormalities, Figs. 15.36–15.37). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: an isolated membrane without a fistula distinguishes this lesion from septal defects (SDL 22 notes). COMLEX: the anal membrane is the posterior portion of the cloacal membrane (SDL 22 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 4 — intestinal atresia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the dual embryologic origin of the anal canal to explain the epithelial transition at the pectinate line and the arterial supplies above and below it.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man has a painful, bleeding perianal lesion for 3 months. He has a history of HIV infection. His vital signs are normal. Anoscopy shows an ulcerated mass entirely below the pectinate line, and biopsy shows squamous cell carcinoma. Which artery is the main arterial supply to the region of this tumor?","choices":{"A":"Superior rectal artery","B":"Middle colic artery","C":"Sigmoidal artery","D":"Inferior rectal artery","E":"Median sacral artery"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Below the pectinate line the anal canal is ectodermal and supplied by the inferior rectal arteries from the internal pudendal arteries. The superior rectal artery supplies the endodermal canal above the line, and the middle colic, sigmoidal, and median sacral arteries supply other territories. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (dual origin of the anal canal); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut, Fig. 15.36). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: tumors below the line drain to the superficial inguinal nodes (First Aid Gastrointestinal, pectinate line). COMLEX: HIV and HPV infection raise the risk of anal squamous cancer (general knowledge; no First Aid anal-cancer entry verified).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 5 — anal canal tumors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the dual embryologic origin of the anal canal to explain the epithelial transition at the pectinate line and the arterial supplies above and below it.","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman has painless bright red blood coating her stool for 2 weeks. Her vital signs are normal. Hemoglobin is 13.2 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–16.0 g/dL). Anoscopy shows engorged vascular cushions arising above the pectinate line. Biopsy of the mucosa overlying these cushions would most likely show which epithelium?","choices":{"A":"Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium","B":"Simple columnar epithelium with goblet cells","C":"Transitional epithelium with umbrella cells","D":"Simple cuboidal epithelium with microvilli","E":"Stratified cuboidal epithelium with ducts"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Above the pectinate line the mucosa is endodermal and lined by simple columnar epithelium with goblet cells and intestinal glands. Pseudostratified ciliated epithelium lines the airways, transitional epithelium lines the urinary tract, simple cuboidal epithelium lines tubules, and stratified cuboidal epithelium lines larger gland ducts. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (dual origin of the anal canal); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut, Fig. 15.36). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: internal hemorrhoids are painless because the region above the line has visceral innervation (First Aid Gastrointestinal, hemorrhoids). COMLEX: tumors above the line are typically adenocarcinomas (First Aid Gastrointestinal, pectinate line).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 5 — hemorrhoids","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the dual embryologic origin of the anal canal to explain the epithelial transition at the pectinate line and the arterial supplies above and below it.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old man has sharp anal pain with each bowel movement and streaks of blood on the toilet paper for 3 weeks. His vital signs are normal. Examination shows a posterior midline tear of the anoderm extending from the outer margin to just below the pectinate line. The epithelium at the site of this tear derives from which embryonic tissue?","choices":{"A":"Endoderm of the hindgut","B":"Mesoderm of the urorectal septum","C":"Endoderm of the allantois","D":"Ectoderm of the neural tube","E":"Ectoderm of the anal pit"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The lower third of the anal canal, below the pectinate line, derives from surface ectoderm of the proctodeum, the anal pit, which is why it is squamous and somatically sensitive. Hindgut endoderm forms the canal above the line, the septal mesoderm forms the perineal body, allantoic endoderm relates to the bladder, and neural tube ectoderm forms the central nervous system. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (dual origin of the anal canal); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut, Fig. 15.36). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: anal fissures are painful because they lie below the line in somatically innervated tissue (First Aid Gastrointestinal, anal fissure). COMLEX: most fissures occur in the posterior midline (First Aid Gastrointestinal).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 5 — anal canal disorders","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Relate neural crest cell migration and RET function to the development of intestinal parasympathetic ganglia and congenital megacolon.","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-day-old boy has not passed meconium since birth and has progressive abdominal distension and bilious vomiting. His temperature is 37.0°C (98.6°F) and pulse is 150/min. Rectal examination produces an explosive release of stool. Contrast enema shows a narrow rectosigmoid with a markedly dilated colon proximal to it. Which mechanism best explains the dilation of the proximal colon?","choices":{"A":"Tonic contraction of the distal aganglionic segment","B":"Absent ganglia in the dilated proximal segment","C":"Mechanical obstruction by inspissated meconium","D":"Excess ganglion cells in the distal segment","E":"Fibrotic stricture from ischemic injury"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Lost enteric ganglion cells normally signal relaxation, so the distal aganglionic segment stays hypercontracted, peristalsis fails to pass, and content backs up into the normally innervated proximal colon, which dilates. The dilated segment has ganglia, inspissated meconium causes meconium ileus, excess ganglia do not cause this picture, and ischemic strictures are acquired. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (neural crest, RET, congenital megacolon); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut abnormalities). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the narrow segment is the diseased one, and the dilated bowel above it is normal (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Hirschsprung disease). COMLEX: Hirschsprung disease is associated with Down syndrome (First Aid Gastrointestinal).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 5 — defecation reflex","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Relate neural crest cell migration and RET function to the development of intestinal parasympathetic ganglia and congenital megacolon.","batch":2,"stem":"A 2-week-old girl has chronic constipation and abdominal distension, and her older brother required colonic surgery in infancy. Her vital signs are normal. Genetic testing shows a heterozygous loss-of-function variant in RET. Which developmental process was most likely impaired?","choices":{"A":"Recanalization of the colonic lumen","B":"Partitioning of the cloaca","C":"Differentiation of colonic smooth muscle","D":"Rotation of the primary intestinal loop","E":"Craniocaudal migration of enteric precursors"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Loss of RET signaling arrests the proximal-to-distal migration of neural crest precursors along the bowel or causes their premature death, leaving a distal aganglionic segment. Recanalization defects cause atresia or stenosis, cloacal partitioning defects cause fistulas, smooth muscle differentiation is a mesodermal process, and rotation defects cause malrotation. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (neural crest, RET, congenital megacolon); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut abnormalities). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the most distal bowel is affected because it is reached last by migrating precursors (SDL 22 notes). COMLEX: in about 80% of cases the aganglionic segment extends to the midpoint of the sigmoid (Langman Ch. 15); EDNRB–EDN3 also supports enteric crest migration (SDL 22 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — enteric nervous system","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Relate neural crest cell migration and RET function to the development of intestinal parasympathetic ganglia and congenital megacolon.","batch":2,"stem":"A 5-day-old boy has delayed passage of meconium and abdominal distension. His vital signs are normal. A suction biopsy is taken from the rectum 2 cm above the pectinate line. Absence of ganglion cells in which layers of the bowel wall would confirm the suspected diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Mucosal crypts and lamina propria","B":"Muscularis mucosae and serosa","C":"Serosa and mesenteric fat","D":"Submucosal and myenteric plexuses","E":"Celiac and mesenteric ganglia"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Neural crest cells form the enteric ganglia of the submucosal and myenteric plexuses, and their absence in a rectal biopsy confirms aganglionic megacolon. The mucosa, lamina propria, muscularis mucosae, serosa, and mesenteric fat do not house enteric ganglia, and the celiac and mesenteric ganglia are prevertebral sympathetic ganglia outside the wall. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (neural crest, RET, congenital megacolon); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut abnormalities). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diagnosed by absence of ganglion cells on rectal suction biopsy; RET mutation in the REcTum (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Hirschsprung disease). COMLEX: biopsies are taken ≥2 cm above the pectinate line to avoid the normal hypoganglionic zone (general knowledge).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 1 — GI wall layers","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl22_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Relate the midgut and hindgut contributions to the large intestine to their boundaries, epithelial origin and arterial supply.","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old man has ischemia limited to the sigmoid colon. Which arteries most directly supply this segment?","choices":{"A":"Middle colic arteries","B":"Right colic arteries","C":"Ileocolic arteries","D":"Sigmoidal arteries","E":"Inferior rectal arteries"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The sigmoid colon is hindgut and is supplied by the sigmoidal branches of the inferior mesenteric artery. The middle colic, right colic, and ileocolic arteries supply midgut colon, and the inferior rectal arteries supply the anal canal below the pectinate line. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (midgut and hindgut contributions); Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Hindgut; Table 15.1). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the rectosigmoid junction is a watershed site in colonic ischemia (First Aid Gastrointestinal, colonic ischemia). COMLEX: the left colic artery supplies the descending colon (SDL 22 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 2 — colonic ischemia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl22_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply normal cecal descent and mesenteric attachment to explain abnormal colonic positioning, increased mobility, and intestinal entrapment.","batch":3,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman is found to have an abnormally mobile cecum and ascending colon on imaging. Which acute complication is she predisposed to?","choices":{"A":"Cecal volvulus","B":"Sigmoid volvulus","C":"Intussusception","D":"Diverticulitis","E":"Angiodysplasia"},"correct":"A","explanation":"An ascending colon that keeps a free mesentery is mobile and can twist around its vascular pedicle, causing volvulus of the cecum and colon. Sigmoid volvulus relates to a long sigmoid mesentery, intussusception involves telescoping bowel, diverticulitis follows acquired outpouchings, and angiodysplasia is a vascular malformation. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (cecal descent and mesenteric attachment); Langman Ch. 15 (Retraction of herniated loops; Mesenteries of the intestinal loops; Abnormalities of the mesenteries). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: untreated volvulus can progress to ischemic gangrene (SDL 22 notes). COMLEX: cecal twisting appears on imaging as a dilated cecum displaced from the right lower quadrant (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 1 — volvulus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl22_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish anorectal malformations caused by abnormal cloacal or urorectal septum development from imperforate anus caused by failure of anal membrane breakdown.","batch":3,"stem":"A 1-day-old boy has no anal opening and meconium in his urine. Into which structure does the abnormal tract most likely open?","choices":{"A":"Umbilicus","B":"Ureter","C":"Seminal vesicle","D":"Sacral skin","E":"Urethra"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A urorectal septum that stops short shifts the hindgut opening anteriorly, and in boys it commonly opens into the urethra as a rectourethral fistula. The umbilicus relates to vitelline or urachal remnants, and the ureter, seminal vesicle, and sacral skin are not typical sites. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (anorectal malformations vs imperforate anus); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut; Hindgut abnormalities, Figs. 15.36–15.37). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the female counterpart opens into the vagina (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: these fistulas occur in about 1 in 5,000 live births (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 2 — partitioning by a septum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl22_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the dual embryologic origin of the anal canal to explain the epithelial transition at the pectinate line and the arterial supplies above and below it.","batch":3,"stem":"A 50-year-old man has an adenocarcinoma of the anal canal just above the pectinate line. Which artery mainly supplies this site?","choices":{"A":"Inferior rectal artery","B":"Superior rectal artery","C":"Middle colic artery","D":"Ileocolic artery","E":"Obturator artery"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The canal above the pectinate line is endodermal hindgut and is supplied by the superior rectal artery, the continuation of the inferior mesenteric artery. The inferior rectal artery supplies the canal below the line, the middle colic and ileocolic arteries supply midgut colon, and the obturator artery supplies the thigh. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (dual origin of the anal canal); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut, Fig. 15.36). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: tumors above the line drain to internal iliac nodes (First Aid Gastrointestinal, pectinate line). COMLEX: venous drainage above the line reaches the portal system (First Aid Gastrointestinal).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 5 — anal canal tumors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl22_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Relate neural crest cell migration and RET function to the development of intestinal parasympathetic ganglia and congenital megacolon.","batch":3,"stem":"A newborn with aganglionic megacolon has normal RET. Which other receptor pathway supports enteric neural crest migration?","choices":{"A":"Wnt and Frizzled","B":"Sonic hedgehog and Patched","C":"Endothelin 3 and EDNRB","D":"Notch and Delta","E":"BMP and SMAD"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Besides RET, the endothelin-3 and EDNRB pathway supports proliferation and migration of enteric neural crest precursors, and defects in it also cause aganglionosis. Wnt, hedgehog, Notch, and BMP pathways pattern other parts of the gut and embryo. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (neural crest, RET, congenital megacolon); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut abnormalities). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: enteric ganglia lie in the submucosal and myenteric plexuses (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: aganglionosis begins distally and extends proximally (SDL 22 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — enteric nervous system","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl22_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Relate the midgut and hindgut contributions to the large intestine to their boundaries, epithelial origin and arterial supply.","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman has a polyp in the descending colon. Its epithelium arose from which embryonic tissue?","choices":{"A":"Midgut endoderm","B":"Splanchnic mesoderm","C":"Surface ectoderm","D":"Neural crest","E":"Hindgut endoderm"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The descending colon is a hindgut derivative, and its epithelium comes from hindgut endoderm. Midgut endoderm lines the cecum through proximal transverse colon, splanchnic mesoderm forms muscle and connective tissue, surface ectoderm forms the lower anal canal, and neural crest forms enteric ganglia. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (midgut and hindgut contributions); Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Hindgut; Table 15.1). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gut epithelium and glandular parenchyma are endodermal throughout the tract (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: hindgut endoderm also lines the bladder and urethra (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 5 — origins of gut components","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":23,"title":"SDL 23 — Histology of the Small Intestine","questions":[{"id":"sdl23_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum based on their characteristic histologic features, including Brunner glands, plicae circulares, villi, and Peyer patches.","batch":1,"stem":"In which part of the small intestine are goblet cells most numerous?","choices":{"A":"Proximal duodenum","B":"Distal duodenum","C":"Proximal jejunum","D":"Terminal ileum","E":"Middle jejunum"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Goblet cells increase in number from the duodenum to the terminal ileum. The duodenum and jejunum have fewer of them. The proximal duodenum is marked by Brunner glands, not peak goblet cell density, which is reached toward the distal ileum. The distal duodenum still has relatively few goblet cells; their density rises steadily to its highest in the ileum. The proximal jejunum is specialized for absorption with tall plicae and villi, not for maximal goblet cell density, which is ileal. The middle jejunum has fewer goblet cells than the ileum, where mucus-secreting cells reach their highest density. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Goblet, Paneth, enteroendocrine and M cells, Figs. 17.22–17.23); First Aid Gastrointestinal (GI histology). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the colon has crypts with abundant goblet cells but no villi (First Aid Gastrointestinal, GI histology). COMLEX: on routine H&E, the mucinogen-filled apex of a goblet cell looks empty because the mucinogen is lost during preparation (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — gross and mucosal features of the large intestine","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The proximal duodenum is marked by Brunner glands, not peak ","B":"The distal duodenum still has relatively few goblet cells; t","C":"The proximal jejunum is specialized for absorption with tall","E":"The middle jejunum has fewer goblet cells than the ileum, wh"}},{"id":"sdl23_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum based on their characteristic histologic features, including Brunner glands, plicae circulares, villi, and Peyer patches.","batch":1,"stem":"What portion of the small intestine does the jejunum make up?","choices":{"A":"Lower three-fifths of the small intestine","B":"Middle one-fifth of the small intestine","C":"Upper two-fifths of the small intestine","D":"Upper one-tenth of the small intestine","E":"Lower one-fifth of the small intestine"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The jejunum is about 2.5 m long and makes up the upper two-fifths of the small intestine. The ileum is the lower three-fifths, and the duodenum is about 25 cm long. The jejunum is not one-fifth of the small intestine; it is the upper two-fifths, about 2.5 m long. One-tenth better approximates the short duodenum (about 25 cm), not the 2.5-m jejunum. The lower part of the small intestine is the ileum, which is three-fifths, not a one-fifth jejunal segment. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Small intestine). Textbook enrichment (Pawlina) — detail not in SDL 23 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the small intestine is the longest part of the digestive tract, at more than 6 m (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the ileum ends at the ileocecal junction, where the distal ileum joins the cecum (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — gross and mucosal features of the large intestine","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina","whyNot":{"B":"The jejunum is not one-fifth of the small intestine; it is t","D":"One-tenth better approximates the short duodenum (about 25 c","E":"The lower part of the small intestine is the ileum, which is"}},{"id":"sdl23_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum based on their characteristic histologic features, including Brunner glands, plicae circulares, villi, and Peyer patches.","batch":1,"stem":"Where are plicae circulares most numerous?","choices":{"A":"Proximal duodenum next to the pylorus","B":"Middle and distal ileum segments","C":"Terminal ileum at the ileocecal valve","D":"Cecum and proximal ascending colon","E":"Distal duodenum and proximal jejunum"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Plicae circulares are most numerous in the distal duodenum and the beginning of the jejunum. They become smaller and fewer by the middle of the ileum; the lecture notes likewise describe the jejunum as having the tallest, most numerous plicae and the distal ileum as markedly reduced. Plicae circulares only begin about 5 to 6 cm distal to the pylorus, so the pylorus-adjacent duodenum lacks them. At the terminal ileum plicae are markedly reduced in height and frequency. The cecum and colon lack plicae circulares and villi altogether; these folds belong to the small intestine. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Small intestine; Mucosa, Figs. 17.17–17.19). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: on oral contrast, the jejunum has a feathered look because its plicae circulares are taller and more numerous than the ileum's (First Aid Gastrointestinal, GI histology). COMLEX: these circular folds are permanent ridges that extend one-half to two-thirds of the way around the lumen (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — gross and mucosal features of the large intestine","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Plicae circulares only begin about 5 to 6 cm distal to the p","C":"At the terminal ileum plicae are markedly reduced in height ","D":"The cecum and colon lack plicae circulares and villi altoget"}},{"id":"sdl23_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the structural specializations of the small intestine that facilitate nutrient absorption, including plicae circulares, villi, enterocyte microvilli, and lacteals, and explain their roles in absorptive surface area and lipid transport.","batch":1,"stem":"Which structure provides the major amplification of the small intestinal luminal surface?","choices":{"A":"Plicae circulares folds","B":"Mucosal intestinal villi","C":"Crypts of Lieberkühn","D":"Enterocyte microvilli","E":"Lateral cell plications"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Microvilli of the enterocytes provide the largest amplification of the luminal surface (20- to 600-fold), with several thousand per cell. Plicae and villi amplify it less, and lateral plications enlarge the lateral membrane. Crypts of Lieberkühn are glands housing stem cells and Paneth cells, not the main surface amplifier, which is the microvillus brush border. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Small intestine; Mucosa, Figs. 17.17–17.19); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Enterocytes, Fig. 17.21). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in a malabsorption workup, steatorrhea can be screened for with a Sudan stain for fecal fat (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption syndromes). COMLEX: under the light microscope, the closely packed microvilli form a striated border on the apex of the enterocyte (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — lipid absorption and chylomicron transport","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Crypts of Lieberkühn are glands housing stem cells and Panet"}},{"id":"sdl23_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the structural specializations of the small intestine that facilitate nutrient absorption, including plicae circulares, villi, enterocyte microvilli, and lacteals, and explain their roles in absorptive surface area and lipid transport.","batch":1,"stem":"By roughly how much do microvilli increase the apical surface area of an enterocyte?","choices":{"A":"Up to about 600-fold","B":"Up to about 6-fold","C":"Up to about 60-fold","D":"Up to about 6,000-fold","E":"Up to about 60,000-fold"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Microvilli increase the apical surface area of an enterocyte by as much as 600 times. The other values are one or more orders of magnitude off. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Enterocytes, Fig. 17.21). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: villi project 0.5–1.5 mm into the lumen and cover the whole small intestine, which gives it a velvety look (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: plicae circulares first appear about 5–6 cm beyond the pylorus (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — lipid absorption and chylomicron transport","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the structural specializations of the small intestine that facilitate nutrient absorption, including plicae circulares, villi, enterocyte microvilli, and lacteals, and explain their roles in absorptive surface area and lipid transport.","batch":1,"stem":"Which protein anchors the actin core at the tip of each microvillus?","choices":{"A":"Myosin I","B":"Spectrin","C":"Villin","D":"Dystrophin","E":"Titin"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The actin microfilaments of the microvillus core are anchored to villin at the tip. Myosin I attaches them to the plasma membrane along the sides. Spectrin is a terminal web component, not the protein anchoring actin at the microvillus tip. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Enterocytes, Fig. 17.21). Textbook enrichment (Pawlina) — detail not in SDL 23 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: tight junctions in the ileum are relatively impermeable, so solutes must be moved across by active transport (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: elongated mitochondria cluster in the apical enterocyte cytoplasm, between the terminal web and the nucleus (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — lipid absorption and chylomicron transport","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina","whyNot":{"B":"Spectrin is a terminal web component, not the protein anchor"}},{"id":"sdl23_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the major epithelial cell types of the small intestine and explain their histologic features and functions, including enterocytes, goblet cells, Paneth cells, enteroendocrine cells, and M cells.","batch":1,"stem":"Which antibacterial products are found in Paneth cell secretory vesicles?","choices":{"A":"Lysozyme and α-defensins","B":"Mucinogen and trefoil factors","C":"Pepsinogen and gastric lipase","D":"Secretin and cholecystokinin","E":"Intrinsic factor and gastrin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Paneth cell vesicles contain lysozyme, α-defensins, other glycoproteins, an arginine-rich protein, and zinc. Mucinogen comes from goblet cells, and the hormones come from enteroendocrine cells. Pepsinogen and gastric lipase are gastric chief cell products, not Paneth cell contents. Intrinsic factor and gastrin are gastric parietal cell and G-cell products, not Paneth cell antimicrobial granules. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Goblet, Paneth, enteroendocrine and M cells, Figs. 17.22–17.23). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: lysozyme digests the cell walls of certain groups of bacteria (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: Paneth cells may occasionally be found in small numbers in the normal colon, and their number may rise in some diseases (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — the four GI hormones","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Pepsinogen and gastric lipase are gastric chief cell product","E":"Intrinsic factor and gastrin are gastric parietal cell and G"}},{"id":"sdl23_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the major epithelial cell types of the small intestine and explain their histologic features and functions, including enterocytes, goblet cells, Paneth cells, enteroendocrine cells, and M cells.","batch":1,"stem":"Why is the basal portion of a goblet cell intensely basophilic?","choices":{"A":"Abundant smooth ER with lipid droplets","B":"Dense packing of acidic mucinogen granules","C":"Stacked apical mitochondria with actin","D":"Large glycogen stores with many lysosomes","E":"Heterochromatic nucleus with extensive rER"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The narrow basal stem holds a heterochromatic nucleus, extensive rER, and free ribosomes, which make it intensely basophilic. The mucinogen granules sit at the apex. Smooth ER with lipid droplets characterizes lipid-processing enterocytes, not the basophilic goblet cell stem. Mucinogen granules fill the apical cup, not the basal stem, and are lost on routine H&E. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Goblet, Paneth, enteroendocrine and M cells, Figs. 17.22–17.23). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a large Golgi apparatus forms a wide cup around newly formed mucinogen granules above the goblet cell nucleus (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: microvilli are more obvious on immature goblet cells in the deep half of the intestinal gland (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — the four GI hormones","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Smooth ER with lipid droplets characterizes lipid-processing","B":"Mucinogen granules fill the apical cup, not the basal stem, "}},{"id":"sdl23_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the major epithelial cell types of the small intestine and explain their histologic features and functions, including enterocytes, goblet cells, Paneth cells, enteroendocrine cells, and M cells.","batch":1,"stem":"What activates the apical receptors of open-type intestinal enteroendocrine cells?","choices":{"A":"Stretch of the muscularis","B":"Taste receptor stimulation","C":"Vagal acetylcholine release","D":"Circulating thyroid hormone","E":"Lamina propria histamine"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Open enteroendocrine cells have taste receptors on their apical membrane, and activating them triggers a G-protein cascade and peptide release. Closed cells lie mainly in the lower gland. Muscularis stretch is a mechanical stimulus to the enteric nervous system, not the activator of apical receptors on open enteroendocrine cells. Vagal acetylcholine acts from the basal side, whereas open-type cells sense lumen contents through apical taste receptors. Lamina propria histamine acts from below, not on apical luminal receptors of open-type cells. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Goblet, Paneth, enteroendocrine and M cells, Figs. 17.22–17.23). Textbook enrichment (Pawlina) — detail not in SDL 23 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GIP released in the small intestine stimulates insulin release from the pancreas (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: somatostatin and histamine from enteroendocrine cells act as paracrine hormones that do not circulate (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — the four GI hormones","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina","whyNot":{"A":"Muscularis stretch is a mechanical stimulus to the enteric n","C":"Vagal acetylcholine acts from the basal side, whereas open-t","E":"Lamina propria histamine acts from below, not on apical lumi"}},{"id":"sdl23_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Outline the process of epithelial renewal in the small intestine, including intestinal crypt stem cells, differentiation pathways, and migration patterns of absorptive and secretory cells.","batch":1,"stem":"Where is the small intestinal stem cell niche located?","choices":{"A":"Tips of the intestinal villi","B":"Middle third of each villus surface","C":"Within the muscularis mucosae layer","D":"Lower half of the intestinal gland","E":"Within the duodenal submucosal glands"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Stem cells sit at the base of the intestinal gland, and the niche is restricted to the lower half of the gland. Cells then migrate upward onto the villus and are shed at the tip. Villus tips are where aged cells undergo apoptosis and are shed, not where stem cells live. The mid-villus holds migrating differentiated cells; proliferation is confined to the crypt. The muscularis mucosae is a smooth muscle layer, not an epithelial stem cell niche. Duodenal submucosal (Brunner) glands secrete alkaline mucus and do not host the intestinal stem cell niche. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Epithelial cell renewal in the small intestine). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in celiac disease, histology shows villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia, and intraepithelial lymphocytosis (First Aid Gastrointestinal, celiac disease). COMLEX: the niche is also called the zone of cell replication (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — large-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Villus tips are where aged cells undergo apoptosis and are s","B":"The mid-villus holds migrating differentiated cells; prolife","C":"The muscularis mucosae is a smooth muscle layer, not an epit","E":"Duodenal submucosal (Brunner) glands secrete alkaline mucus "}},{"id":"sdl23_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Outline the process of epithelial renewal in the small intestine, including intestinal crypt stem cells, differentiation pathways, and migration patterns of absorptive and secretory cells.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the renewal time for absorptive and goblet cells in the human small intestine?","choices":{"A":"4–6 days","B":"4–6 hours","C":"4–6 weeks","D":"4–6 months","E":"1–2 years"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Autoradiographic studies show that absorptive and goblet cells in the human small intestine are renewed every 4–6 days. Paneth cells live much longer, about 4 weeks. Four to 6 hours is far too fast; renewal of absorptive and goblet cells takes 4 to 6 days. Four to 6 months is far too slow for this rapidly renewing epithelium, which turns over in days. One to 2 years would not fit an epithelium renewed every 4 to 6 days. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Epithelial cell renewal in the small intestine). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: aging epithelial cells undergo apoptosis at the villus tip and slough into the lumen (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: contraction of the terminal web may help close the gaps left when aging cells exfoliate (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — large-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Four to 6 hours is far too fast; renewal of absorptive and g","D":"Four to 6 months is far too slow for this rapidly renewing e","E":"One to 2 years would not fit an epithelium renewed every 4 t"}},{"id":"sdl23_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Outline the process of epithelial renewal in the small intestine, including intestinal crypt stem cells, differentiation pathways, and migration patterns of absorptive and secretory cells.","batch":1,"stem":"Increased expression of which transcription factor commits crypt progenitor cells to the secretory lineage?","choices":{"A":"Notch1","B":"Math1","C":"Cdx2","D":"Pdx1","E":"Ptf1a"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cells committed to the secretory lineage (goblet, enteroendocrine, and Paneth cells) have increased Math1 expression. When Math1 is inhibited, cells follow the default pathway and become enterocytes. Pdx1 (pancreatic and duodenal homeobox 1) specifies the duodenum during gut tube patterning and is upregulated in the developing pancreatic buds; it does not choose the crypt secretory lineage. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Epithelial cell renewal in the small intestine). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: enteroendocrine cells appear to divide only once before differentiating (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: enteroendocrine cells migrate with absorptive and goblet cells, but more slowly (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — large-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Pdx1 (pancreatic and duodenal homeobox 1) specifies the duod"}},{"id":"sdl23_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the structure and immune functions of intestinal gut-associated lymphoid tissue (GALT), including Peyer patches, M-cell antigen transport, and the production and epithelial transport of secretory IgA.","batch":1,"stem":"Where are Peyer patches preferentially located in the ileal wall?","choices":{"A":"Along the mesenteric border itself","B":"Within the inner muscularis externa","C":"Opposite the mesenteric attachment","D":"Beneath the serosal mesothelium","E":"Within the myenteric nerve plexus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Peyer patches are large and numerous in the ileum and lie preferentially on the side of the intestine opposite the mesenteric attachment. They sit in the lamina propria and often extend into the submucosa. Peyer patches favor the antimesenteric wall, not the mesenteric border. Peyer patches sit in the lamina propria and submucosa, not within the muscularis externa. Peyer patches lie in the lamina propria and submucosa, not beneath the serosa. The myenteric plexus is a nerve network between muscle layers, not the site of Peyer patches. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Small intestine; Mucosa, Figs. 17.17–17.19). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Peyer patches are unencapsulated lymphoid tissue in the lamina propria and submucosa of the ileum (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Peyer patches). COMLEX: in gross specimens, these aggregated nodules look like clusters of white specks (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 4 — lamina propria and GALT of the large intestine","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Peyer patches favor the antimesenteric wall, not the mesente","B":"Peyer patches sit in the lamina propria and submucosa, not w","D":"Peyer patches lie in the lamina propria and submucosa, not b","E":"The myenteric plexus is a nerve network between muscle layer"}},{"id":"sdl23_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the structure and immune functions of intestinal gut-associated lymphoid tissue (GALT), including Peyer patches, M-cell antigen transport, and the production and epithelial transport of secretory IgA.","batch":1,"stem":"From what is the secretory component of secretory IgA derived?","choices":{"A":"Plasma cell J chain peptide","B":"Cleaved polymeric Ig receptor","C":"Goblet cell mucin glycoprotein","D":"Paneth cell defensin precursor","E":"Hepatocyte bile acid carrier"},"correct":"B","explanation":"After the receptor-dIgA complex reaches the apical surface, the polymeric immunoglobulin receptor is cleaved. Its bound extracellular portion becomes the secretory component. The J chain comes from the plasma cell and links the IgA dimer. Goblet cell mucin forms the protective mucus gel, not the secretory component of sIgA. Paneth cell defensins are innate antimicrobial peptides, unrelated to the secretory component. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (GALT; Immunoglobulin-mediated responses, Fig. 17.24; Folder 17.5). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the polymeric Ig receptor carries dIgA across the enterocyte by transcytosis; only at the apical surface is it cleaved, and the retained fragment is the secretory component (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the receptor is a 75-kDa transmembrane glycoprotein synthesized by enterocytes (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 4 — lamina propria and GALT of the large intestine","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Goblet cell mucin forms the protective mucus gel, not the se","D":"Paneth cell defensins are innate antimicrobial peptides, unr"}},{"id":"sdl23_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the structure and immune functions of intestinal gut-associated lymphoid tissue (GALT), including Peyer patches, M-cell antigen transport, and the production and epithelial transport of secretory IgA.","batch":1,"stem":"Which antibody do most plasma cells in the intestinal lamina propria secrete?","choices":{"A":"Monomeric IgG","B":"Pentameric IgM","C":"Monomeric IgE","D":"Monomeric IgD","E":"Dimeric IgA"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Most lamina propria plasma cells secrete dimeric IgA rather than IgG. Some make pentameric IgM or IgE, and IgM can use a similar transcytotic route. IgE is made by only some lamina propria plasma cells; most make dimeric IgA. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (GALT; Immunoglobulin-mediated responses, Fig. 17.24; Folder 17.5). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: B cells stimulated in Peyer patch germinal centers become IgA-secreting plasma cells that settle in the lamina propria (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Peyer patches). COMLEX: some IgE binds lamina propria mast cells and sensitizes them to luminal antigens (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 4 — lamina propria and GALT of the large intestine","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"IgE is made by only some lamina propria plasma cells; most m"}},{"id":"sdl23_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum based on their characteristic histologic features, including Brunner glands, plicae circulares, villi, and Peyer patches.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old man with dyspepsia has an endoscopic biopsy taken just distal to the pylorus. It shows branched tubular submucosal glands whose secretion is highly alkaline. Besides protecting the mucosa, what does this secretion do?","choices":{"A":"Secretes lysozyme and defensins into the crypt lumens","B":"Brings chyme near the pancreatic enzyme pH optimum","C":"Absorbs conjugated bile acids from the passing chyme","D":"Produces intrinsic factor needed for vitamin B12 uptake","E":"Concentrates dimeric IgA for transport to the lumen"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Brunner gland secretion contains bicarbonate and neutral and alkaline glycoproteins. It neutralizes acid chyme and brings the intestinal contents close to the optimal pH for activating pancreatic enzymes. Lysozyme and defensins come from Paneth cells at the crypt base, not from Brunner glands. Brunner glands secrete; they do not absorb bile acids, which is an ileal function. Intrinsic factor is a gastric parietal cell product, not a Brunner gland secretion. Dimeric IgA is transported by enterocytes via pIgR, not concentrated by Brunner glands. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Small intestine; Submucosa, Fig. 17.25; Serosa). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bicarbonate from Brunner glands neutralizes acid, and pancreatic and biliary bicarbonate secretion is stimulated by secretin (First Aid Gastrointestinal, GI secretory products). COMLEX: the duct of each Brunner gland opens into the lumen of an intestinal crypt (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 2 — pancreatic bicarbonate secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Lysozyme and defensins come from Paneth cells at the crypt b","C":"Brunner glands secrete; they do not absorb bile acids, which","D":"Intrinsic factor is a gastric parietal cell product, not a B","E":"Dimeric IgA is transported by enterocytes via pIgR, not conc"}},{"id":"sdl23_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum based on their characteristic histologic features, including Brunner glands, plicae circulares, villi, and Peyer patches.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman has a small bowel resection for trauma. The specimen shows tall villi and tall, closely spaced circular folds. It has no submucosal glands and no large lymphoid aggregates. Which segment was removed?","choices":{"A":"Duodenum","B":"Ileum","C":"Jejunum","D":"Appendix","E":"Rectum"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Prominent, numerous plicae and tall villi without Brunner glands or Peyer patches identify the jejunum. Submucosal glands mark the duodenum, Peyer patches mark the ileum, and the colon lacks villi. The appendix is large bowel with no villi or tall plicae, so it does not fit this specimen. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Small intestine; Mucosa, Figs. 17.17–17.19); First Aid Gastrointestinal (GI histology). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the ileum contains Peyer patches, and its plicae circulares are limited to its proximal part (First Aid Gastrointestinal, GI histology). COMLEX: the jejunum gradually changes its morphology as it becomes the ileum, with no sharp boundary (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — gross and mucosal features of the large intestine","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"The appendix is large bowel with no villi or tall plicae, so"}},{"id":"sdl23_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum based on their characteristic histologic features, including Brunner glands, plicae circulares, villi, and Peyer patches.","batch":2,"stem":"A 39-year-old man has a stab wound that injures the retroperitoneal part of his duodenum. Which layer forms the outer covering of this segment?","choices":{"A":"Serosa","B":"Mesothelium","C":"Submucosa","D":"Muscularis","E":"Adventitia"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Parts of the duodenum in the retroperitoneum contact the posterior abdominal wall and have an adventitia. The intraperitoneal small intestine is covered by serosa, a mesothelium with underlying connective tissue. Submucosa is an internal layer beneath the mucosa, not the outer covering. The muscularis externa lies inside the outer covering rather than forming it. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Small intestine; Submucosa, Fig. 17.25; Serosa). Textbook enrichment (Pawlina) — detail not in SDL 23 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the duodenum is about 25 cm long and is the first, shortest, and widest part of the small intestine (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the myenteric plexus between the two muscularis externa layers contains postsynaptic parasympathetic neuron cell bodies (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina","whyNot":{"C":"Submucosa is an internal layer beneath the mucosa, not the o","D":"The muscularis externa lies inside the outer covering rather"}},{"id":"sdl23_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the structural specializations of the small intestine that facilitate nutrient absorption, including plicae circulares, villi, enterocyte microvilli, and lacteals, and explain their roles in absorptive surface area and lipid transport.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman has chronic diarrhea and iron-deficiency anemia. Duodenal biopsy shows a flat mucosa with crypt hyperplasia and intraepithelial lymphocytes. Which surface-amplifying structure is lost?","choices":{"A":"Plicae","B":"Villi","C":"Crypts","D":"Teniae","E":"Rugae"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Villi are finger- or leaf-like mucosal projections with a lamina propria core, and celiac disease flattens them. Plicae have a submucosal core and persist. Microvilli are cell-surface structures. Crypts are hyperplastic, not lost, in this flat mucosa. Teniae coli are bands of colonic longitudinal muscle, not a small-intestinal surface amplifier. Rugae are gastric folds, not a duodenal surface structure. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Small intestine; Mucosa, Figs. 17.17–17.19); First Aid Gastrointestinal (celiac disease). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: celiac disease mainly affects the distal duodenum and/or proximal jejunum and is linked to HLA-DQ2 and HLA-DQ8 (First Aid Gastrointestinal, celiac disease). COMLEX: celiac serology includes IgA anti-tissue transglutaminase and anti-endomysial antibodies (First Aid Gastrointestinal, celiac disease).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 1 — carbohydrate digestion and lactase deficiency","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Crypts are hyperplastic, not lost, in this flat mucosa.","D":"Teniae coli are bands of colonic longitudinal muscle, not a ","E":"Rugae are gastric folds, not a duodenal surface structure."}},{"id":"sdl23_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the structural specializations of the small intestine that facilitate nutrient absorption, including plicae circulares, villi, enterocyte microvilli, and lacteals, and explain their roles in absorptive surface area and lipid transport.","batch":2,"stem":"A 6-year-old boy has edema and fatty stools. Biopsy shows dilated central lymphatic capillaries in his villi. Why do absorbed lipoprotein particles normally enter these vessels and not the villus blood capillaries?","choices":{"A":"Villus blood capillaries lack endothelial fenestrations","B":"Lymphatic capillaries actively pump in triglyceride","C":"Portal venous blood rapidly degrades fat particles","D":"Lipoproteins cross the tight junctions into lymph","E":"Particles are too large for the blood capillaries"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Sugars, amino acids, and other solutes diffuse into the fenestrated capillaries under the epithelium. Lipoprotein particles are too large to enter blood vessels, so they enter the lacteal. Villus blood capillaries are fenestrated, yet chylomicrons are still too large to pass those fenestrations. Lacteal flow is driven by intermittent contraction of muscularis mucosae fibers, not active triglyceride pumping into lymphatics. Chylomicrons leave enterocytes by basolateral exocytosis and cross between endothelial cells into the lacteal, not through epithelial tight junctions. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Small intestine; Mucosa, Figs. 17.17–17.19); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Enterocytes, Fig. 17.21). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: an enterocyte packages resynthesized lipid as chylomicrons and discharges it into the intercellular space (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: smooth muscle from the muscularis mucosae runs alongside each lacteal and may squeeze lymph out of it (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — lipid absorption and chylomicron transport","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Villus blood capillaries are fenestrated, yet chylomicrons a","B":"Lacteal flow is driven by intermittent contraction of muscul","D":"Chylomicrons leave enterocytes by basolateral exocytosis and"}},{"id":"sdl23_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the structural specializations of the small intestine that facilitate nutrient absorption, including plicae circulares, villi, enterocyte microvilli, and lacteals, and explain their roles in absorptive surface area and lipid transport.","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old man in a nutrition study has a jejunal biopsy during active absorption. Electron microscopy shows widened spaces between the lateral surfaces of adjacent enterocytes. What structural change explains this?","choices":{"A":"Rupture of the apical tight junction protein strands","B":"Loss of the underlying enterocyte basal lamina sheet","C":"Separation of interdigitating cell-surface folds","D":"Shedding of aged enterocytes at the villus tips","E":"Fusion of mucinogen granules at the cell apex"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Enterocytes have flattened lateral processes (plicae) that interdigitate with those of neighboring cells. During active absorption these separate, enlarging the intercellular compartment and driving flow into the lamina propria. Shedding at villus tips is normal turnover, not a cause of widened lateral intercellular spaces. Mucinogen exocytosis is a goblet cell apical event, unrelated to enterocyte lateral spaces. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Enterocytes, Fig. 17.21). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in the duodenum and jejunum, hypotonic contents allow water and small solutes to cross leaky tight junctions by solvent drag (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: contraction of the terminal web spreads the microvilli apart and exposes more surface area (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 1 — intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Shedding at villus tips is normal turnover, not a cause of w","E":"Mucinogen exocytosis is a goblet cell apical event, unrelate"}},{"id":"sdl23_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the major epithelial cell types of the small intestine and explain their histologic features and functions, including enterocytes, goblet cells, Paneth cells, enteroendocrine cells, and M cells.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman with chronic diarrhea has a jejunal biopsy. At the base of the intestinal glands, H&E shows cells with basophilic basal cytoplasm and large, intensely eosinophilic, refractile apical vesicles. What probably causes the eosinophilia?","choices":{"A":"Heme-rich cytochromes","B":"Glycogen granule stores","C":"Neutral mucin polymer","D":"Arginine-rich protein","E":"Iron-ferritin deposits"},"correct":"D","explanation":"These are Paneth cells. An arginine-rich protein in their secretory vesicles is probably responsible for the intense acidophilia. Mucin is lost on routine sections, and glycogen and ferritin do not explain the staining. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Goblet, Paneth, enteroendocrine and M cells, Figs. 17.22–17.23). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Paneth cells in the crypts of Lieberkühn secrete defensins, lysozyme, and TNF (First Aid Gastrointestinal, GI histology). COMLEX: zinc is also stored in these refractile apical vesicles (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — epithelial types and mucosal specializations","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the major epithelial cell types of the small intestine and explain their histologic features and functions, including enterocytes, goblet cells, Paneth cells, enteroendocrine cells, and M cells.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man has an ileal biopsy for chronic diarrhea. Routine H&E shows many epithelial cells with pale, empty-looking apical cups and narrow basal stems. Why does the apex of these cells look empty?","choices":{"A":"Lipid droplets dissolve in alcohol","B":"Mucinogen is lost during preparation","C":"Glycogen leaches out during fixation","D":"Apical vacuoles are filled with water","E":"Dying cells lose their organelles"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Goblet cell mucinogen is water soluble and is lost during routine H&E preparation, so the mucinogen-filled apex looks empty. TEM shows a large mass of mucinogen granules there. Goblet cell apices hold mucinogen, not lipid droplets. The apical cup holds mucinogen granules, not glycogen. The apex is packed with membrane-bound mucinogen granules, not water-filled vacuoles. These are viable mucin-secreting cells with rER-rich basal stems, not dying cells. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Goblet, Paneth, enteroendocrine and M cells, Figs. 17.22–17.23). Textbook enrichment (Pawlina) — detail not in SDL 23 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: in Barrett esophagus, the squamous epithelium is replaced by nonciliated columnar epithelium with goblet cells (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Barrett esophagus). COMLEX: the apical mucinogen mass in a goblet cell pushes on and distorts the shape of neighboring cells (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 2 — large-intestine absorptive and goblet cells","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina","whyNot":{"A":"Goblet cell apices hold mucinogen, not lipid droplets.","C":"The apical cup holds mucinogen granules, not glycogen.","D":"The apex is packed with membrane-bound mucinogen granules, n","E":"These are viable mucin-secreting cells with rER-rich basal s"}},{"id":"sdl23_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the major epithelial cell types of the small intestine and explain their histologic features and functions, including enterocytes, goblet cells, Paneth cells, enteroendocrine cells, and M cells.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old woman has a well-differentiated neuroendocrine tumor in her duodenum. From which epithelial cell type does this tumor most likely arise?","choices":{"A":"Enteroendocrine cells","B":"Absorptive enterocytes","C":"Intermediate goblet cells","D":"Microfold epithelial cells","E":"Myoepithelial basket cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Enteroendocrine cells make paracrine and endocrine peptides, including CCK, secretin, GIP, and motilin, and are the epithelial source of neuroendocrine tumors. Enterocytes absorb, and M cells transport antigen. Goblet cells secrete mucin and are not the source of hormone-producing neuroendocrine tumors. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Goblet, Paneth, enteroendocrine and M cells, Figs. 17.22–17.23). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: motilin from the small intestine initiates gastric and intestinal motility (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: VIP, bombesin, and enkephalins are neurocrine peptides secreted by submucosal and myenteric nerve cells (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — the four GI hormones","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Goblet cells secrete mucin and are not the source of hormone"}},{"id":"sdl23_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Outline the process of epithelial renewal in the small intestine, including intestinal crypt stem cells, differentiation pathways, and migration patterns of absorptive and secretory cells.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman has watery diarrhea 5 days after starting cytotoxic chemotherapy. Biopsy shows short, denuded villi. Why is the small intestinal epithelium damaged so quickly?","choices":{"A":"Paneth cells migrating upward onto the villi","B":"Slow turnover of the mature villus enterocyte layer","C":"Continuous replacement from dividing crypt cells","D":"Stem cells concentrated near the outer villus tips","E":"Mature enterocytes dividing on the villus tips"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Small intestinal absorptive and goblet cells are replaced every 4–6 days from a highly proliferative niche in the lower crypt. When chemotherapy blocks division, losses at the villus tip are not replaced. Paneth cells migrate downward to the crypt base, not upward onto villi. Villus enterocyte turnover is fast, about 4 to 6 days, not slow. Stem cells are in the lower crypt, not near villus tips. Mature villus enterocytes do not divide; they are shed at the tip and replaced from the crypt. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Epithelial cell renewal in the small intestine). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: intermediate cells in the niche usually undergo one or two divisions before committing to absorptive or goblet fates (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: cells shed at the villus tip are replaced by cells migrating upward from the intestinal gland (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — large-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Paneth cells migrate downward to the crypt base, not upward ","B":"Villus enterocyte turnover is fast, about 4 to 6 days, not s","D":"Stem cells are in the lower crypt, not near villus tips.","E":"Mature villus enterocytes do not divide; they are shed at th"}},{"id":"sdl23_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Outline the process of epithelial renewal in the small intestine, including intestinal crypt stem cells, differentiation pathways, and migration patterns of absorptive and secretory cells.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man in a research study has radiolabeled crypt cells. Days later, most labeled cells are on the villi. One labeled population has migrated downward to the gland base, lives about 4 weeks, and no longer divides. Which cells are these?","choices":{"A":"Paneth cells","B":"Goblet cells","C":"Enterocytes","D":"Stem cells","E":"Plasma cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Paneth cells come from the crypt stem cells, migrate downward to the bottom of the gland, live about 4 weeks, and no longer divide once recognizable. Absorptive and goblet cells migrate upward. Enterocytes migrate upward to the villus tip and turn over in 4 to 6 days. Stem cells keep dividing in the crypt niche, unlike the nondividing labeled cells here. Plasma cells are lamina propria immune cells, not epithelial cells derived from crypt stem cells. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Epithelial cell renewal in the small intestine). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: dead Paneth cells are replaced by differentiation of a nearby committed cell in the intestinal gland (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: stem cells in the crypt base give rise to every mature cell type of the intestinal epithelium (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — large-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Enterocytes migrate upward to the villus tip and turn over i","D":"Stem cells keep dividing in the crypt niche, unlike the nond","E":"Plasma cells are lamina propria immune cells, not epithelial"}},{"id":"sdl23_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Outline the process of epithelial renewal in the small intestine, including intestinal crypt stem cells, differentiation pathways, and migration patterns of absorptive and secretory cells.","batch":2,"stem":"A 9-year-old boy has a genetic variant that blocks the transcription factor driving secretory fate in his intestinal stem cell niche. Which cells would differentiating crypt cells mainly become?","choices":{"A":"Mucus-secreting goblet cells","B":"Antimicrobial Paneth cells","C":"Hormone-secreting endocrine cells","D":"Absorptive enterocytes","E":"Antigen-sampling microfold cells"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Inhibiting the secretory transcription factor sends cells down the default pathway into absorptive enterocytes. Goblet, Paneth, and enteroendocrine cells form the secretory lineage. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Epithelial cell renewal in the small intestine). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: intermediate cells committed to the absorptive fate lose their secretory granules and gather mitochondria, rER, and ribosomes (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: intermediate cells committed to the goblet fate develop a small rounded cluster of granules under the apical membrane (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — large-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the structure and immune functions of intestinal gut-associated lymphoid tissue (GALT), including Peyer patches, M-cell antigen transport, and the production and epithelial transport of secretory IgA.","batch":2,"stem":"A 23-year-old man develops typhoid fever after travel. The bacteria crossed his ileal epithelium where it covers large lymphatic nodules. Which route did they most likely use?","choices":{"A":"Microfold cell transcytosis","B":"Paracellular tight junctions","C":"Goblet cell mucus exocytosis","D":"Paneth cell phagocytosis","E":"Lacteal lymphatic uptake"},"correct":"A","explanation":"M cells over Peyer patches bind macromolecules and Gram-negative bacteria to GP2 receptors. They endocytose them and release them into a basolateral pocket holding immune cells. Typhoid bacteria crossing follicle-associated epithelium use M-cell transcytosis, not tight junctions between enterocytes. Goblet cell exocytosis releases mucus outward and is not an entry route for bacteria. Paneth cells secrete antimicrobial granules and do not phagocytose and transport bacteria. Lacteals take up chylomicrons from the villus core, not bacteria across the epithelium. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Goblet, Paneth, enteroendocrine and M cells, Figs. 17.22–17.23); Pawlina Ch. 17 (GALT; Immunoglobulin-mediated responses, Fig. 17.24; Folder 17.5). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Peyer patches contain microfold cells that sample antigens and present them to immune cells (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Peyer patches). COMLEX: M cells have microfolds and a thin glycocalyx on their apical surface, not microvilli (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 4 — lamina propria and GALT of the large intestine","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Typhoid bacteria crossing follicle-associated epithelium use","C":"Goblet cell exocytosis releases mucus outward and is not an ","D":"Paneth cells secrete antimicrobial granules and do not phago","E":"Lacteals take up chylomicrons from the villus core, not bact"}},{"id":"sdl23_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the structure and immune functions of intestinal gut-associated lymphoid tissue (GALT), including Peyer patches, M-cell antigen transport, and the production and epithelial transport of secretory IgA.","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old woman has recurrent giardiasis. Serum IgA is undetectable (reference range 70–400 mg/dL), and IgG and IgM are normal. Which mucosal defense is mainly reduced?","choices":{"A":"Lysis of luminal bacteria by Paneth cell enzymes","B":"Sensitization of the lamina propria mast cells","C":"Opsonization of invading bacteria for neutrophils","D":"Blocking of microbial adhesion to the epithelium","E":"Activation of the classical complement pathway"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Luminal secretory IgA binds antigens, toxins, and microbes. It blocks their attachment and invasion by inhibiting motility, causing aggregation, or masking adhesion sites. Mast cell sensitization uses IgE, and opsonization is mainly an IgG role. Paneth cell lysozyme is innate and independent of IgA, so it is not what is lost. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (GALT; Immunoglobulin-mediated responses, Fig. 17.24; Folder 17.5). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secretory IgA binds an HIV envelope glycoprotein and prevents viral attachment and internalization (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the GALT works as an immunologic barrier along the whole length of the gastrointestinal tract (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 4 — lamina propria and GALT of the large intestine","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Paneth cell lysozyme is innate and independent of IgA, so it"}},{"id":"sdl23_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the structure and immune functions of intestinal gut-associated lymphoid tissue (GALT), including Peyer patches, M-cell antigen transport, and the production and epithelial transport of secretory IgA.","batch":2,"stem":"A 7-year-old girl has recurrent enteric infections. Her lamina propria plasma cells secrete normal J-chain-linked IgA dimers, but her intestinal secretions lack IgA. Which step most likely fails?","choices":{"A":"Class switching of mucosal B cells to the IgA isotype","B":"Linking of IgA monomers together by the J chain","C":"Homing of IgA plasma cells to the lamina propria","D":"Antigen uptake by the Peyer patch dendritic cells","E":"Receptor-mediated transcytosis across enterocytes"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Dimeric IgA binds a receptor on the basal enterocyte membrane and is endocytosed. It is carried across the cell to the apical surface and released into the lumen. Normal dimers with absent luminal IgA point to failed epithelial transport. Class switching is intact because plasma cells already make IgA dimers. J-chain linking is intact, since the dimers are described as normal. Homing is intact because IgA plasma cells are present in the lamina propria. Dendritic cell antigen uptake precedes B-cell activation; IgA is already being made here. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (GALT; Immunoglobulin-mediated responses, Fig. 17.24; Folder 17.5). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: IgM molecules use a similar receptor-mediated transcytosis pathway to reach the mucosal surface (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: dendritic cells and lymphocytes in the M-cell recess sample luminal antigen and can stimulate specific antibody production (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 4 — lamina propria and GALT of the large intestine","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Class switching is intact because plasma cells already make ","B":"J-chain linking is intact, since the dimers are described as","C":"Homing is intact because IgA plasma cells are present in the","D":"Dendritic cell antigen uptake precedes B-cell activation; Ig"}},{"id":"trial_sdl23_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum based on their characteristic histologic features, including Brunner glands, plicae circulares, villi, and Peyer patches.","batch":3,"stem":"A 50-year-old man is having a pancreaticoduodenectomy. How long is the first, shortest, and widest part of his small intestine?","choices":{"A":"About 5 cm","B":"About 2.5 m","C":"About 3.5 m","D":"About 25 cm","E":"About 60 cm"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The duodenum is about 25 cm long. The jejunum is about 2.5 m and the ileum about 3.5 m long. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Small intestine). Textbook enrichment (Pawlina) — detail not in SDL 23 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the duodenum ends at the duodenojejunal junction, where the jejunum begins (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the whole small intestine measures more than 6 m (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — gross and mucosal features of the large intestine","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina"},{"id":"trial_sdl23_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the structural specializations of the small intestine that facilitate nutrient absorption, including plicae circulares, villi, enterocyte microvilli, and lacteals, and explain their roles in absorptive surface area and lipid transport.","batch":3,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman has a normal duodenal biopsy during an anemia workup. How far do normal small intestinal villi project into the lumen?","choices":{"A":"About 0.5–1.5 mm","B":"About 5–15 µm","C":"About 50–150 µm","D":"About 5–15 mm","E":"About 5–15 cm"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Villi extend 0.5–1.5 mm from the mucosal surface into the lumen and give it a velvety look. Microvilli are micrometer-scale, and plicae are larger folds. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Small intestine; Mucosa, Fig. 17.18). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in lactose intolerance the villi look normal unless lactase loss is secondary to injury at the villus tips (First Aid Gastrointestinal, lactose intolerance). COMLEX: the core of each villus is an extension of the lamina propria containing fenestrated capillaries (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 1 — carbohydrate digestion and lactase deficiency","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl23_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the major epithelial cell types of the small intestine and explain their histologic features and functions, including enterocytes, goblet cells, Paneth cells, enteroendocrine cells, and M cells.","batch":3,"stem":"A 48-year-old man has an ileal biopsy examined by electron microscopy. In goblet cells, what is the thin rim of apical cytoplasm bearing microvilli around the mucinogen granules called?","choices":{"A":"Calyx","B":"Cuticle","C":"Theca","D":"Capsule","E":"Mantle"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In goblet cells, microvilli are restricted to a thin rim of cytoplasm called the theca. It surrounds the apical and lateral parts of the mucinogen mass. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Goblet cells, Fig. 17.22). Textbook enrichment (Pawlina) — detail not in SDL 23 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: mitochondria in goblet cells are concentrated in the basal cytoplasm (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the shape of the goblet cell, with a narrow basal stem under an apical granule mass, gives it its name (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 2 — large-intestine absorptive and goblet cells","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina"},{"id":"trial_sdl23_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Outline the process of epithelial renewal in the small intestine, including intestinal crypt stem cells, differentiation pathways, and migration patterns of absorptive and secretory cells.","batch":3,"stem":"A 37-year-old woman has a jejunal biopsy taken for research. In the transit-amplifying compartment of the crypt, how many divisions do intermediate cells usually undergo before committing to an absorptive or goblet fate?","choices":{"A":"About ten divisions","B":"No further divisions","C":"Thirty to forty divisions","D":"Hundreds of divisions","E":"One or two divisions"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Intermediate cells form the amplifying compartment of the niche. They usually undergo one or two divisions before committing to the absorptive or goblet fate. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Intermediate cells; Epithelial cell renewal). Textbook enrichment (Pawlina) — detail not in SDL 23 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: intermediate cells have short, irregular microvilli with long core filaments and many desmosomal junctions (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: small mucin-like granules form a central column in the supranuclear cytoplasm of intermediate cells (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — large-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina"},{"id":"trial_sdl23_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the structure and immune functions of intestinal gut-associated lymphoid tissue (GALT), including Peyer patches, M-cell antigen transport, and the production and epithelial transport of secretory IgA.","batch":3,"stem":"A 28-year-old man has a jejunal biopsy for evaluation of diarrhea. Roughly how much of the normal intestinal mucosa is made up of loosely organized lymphatic tissue?","choices":{"A":"About one-fourth","B":"About one-tenth","C":"About one-half","D":"About two-thirds","E":"About nine-tenths"},"correct":"A","explanation":"About one-fourth of the gut mucosa is a loosely organized layer of lymphatic nodules, lymphocytes, macrophages, plasma cells, and eosinophils in the lamina propria. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (GALT is prominent in the lamina propria). Textbook enrichment (Pawlina) — detail not in SDL 23 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: lymphocytes in the gut are also found between epithelial cells as well as in the lamina propria (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: activated antigen-presenting cells migrate to lamina propria nodules, where new plasma cells arise (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 4 — lamina propria and GALT of the large intestine","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina"},{"id":"trial_sdl23_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum based on their characteristic histologic features, including Brunner glands, plicae circulares, villi, and Peyer patches.","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman has upper endoscopy for anemia. How far beyond the pylorus do the plicae circulares begin to appear?","choices":{"A":"About 5–6 mm","B":"About 5–6 cm","C":"About 50–60 cm","D":"About 1–2 m","E":"About 3–4 m"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Plicae circulares begin to appear about 5–6 cm beyond the pylorus. They are most numerous in the distal duodenum and proximal jejunum. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Small intestine; Mucosa, Fig. 17.17). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: plicae circulares are also called the valves of Kerckring (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: each circular fold contains a core of submucosa, unlike a villus (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":24,"title":"SDL 24 — Histology of the Large Intestine","questions":[{"id":"sdl24_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the distinctive gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli, haustra coli, omental appendices, and the smooth mucosal surface without villi or plicae circulares.","batch":1,"stem":"How many teniae coli run along the cecum and colon?","choices":{"A":"Two opposing bands","B":"Four paired bands","C":"Five irregular bands","D":"Three equal bands","E":"Six spiral bands"},"correct":"D","explanation":"There are three narrowed, thickened, equally spaced teniae coli, formed from the outer longitudinal layer of the muscularis externa. Two bands is too few; the outer longitudinal muscle condenses into three equally spaced teniae. There are three teniae coli, not four paired bands. The teniae are three equally spaced bands, not five irregular ones. The teniae are three straight, equally spaced bands, not six spiral bands. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; gross features, Fig. 17.28; Muscularis externa). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: between the teniae, the outer longitudinal layer of the colon is an extremely thin sheet (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: omental appendices are small fat-filled peritoneal projections on the outer colonic surface (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 4 — colonic motility and haustral contractions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Two bands is too few; the outer longitudinal muscle condense","B":"There are three teniae coli, not four paired bands.","C":"The teniae are three equally spaced bands, not five irregula","E":"The teniae are three straight, equally spaced bands, not six"}},{"id":"sdl24_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the distinctive gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli, haustra coli, omental appendices, and the smooth mucosal surface without villi or plicae circulares.","batch":1,"stem":"In which parts of the large intestine are teniae coli absent?","choices":{"A":"Cecum, ascending, and transverse colon","B":"Transverse, descending, and sigmoid colon","C":"Ascending colon, cecum, and appendix","D":"Descending colon, sigmoid, and rectum","E":"Rectum, anal canal, and appendix"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Teniae coli are seen mainly in the cecum and colon. In the rectum, anal canal, and vermiform appendix, the outer longitudinal muscle is a uniformly thick layer, as in the small intestine. The cecum and ascending and transverse colon are exactly where teniae coli are seen. The transverse, descending, and sigmoid colon all carry teniae coli. The cecum and ascending colon have teniae; only the appendix of this group lacks them. The descending and sigmoid colon have teniae; only the rectum of this group lacks them. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; gross features, Fig. 17.28; Muscularis externa). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the appendix differs from the cecum by having a uniform layer of longitudinal muscle in its muscularis externa (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the anal canal averages 4 cm and runs from the upper pelvic diaphragm to the anus (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 4 — colonic motility and haustral contractions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The cecum and ascending and transverse colon are exactly whe","B":"The transverse, descending, and sigmoid colon all carry teni","C":"The cecum and ascending colon have teniae; only the appendix","D":"The descending and sigmoid colon have teniae; only the rectu"}},{"id":"sdl24_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the distinctive gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli, haustra coli, omental appendices, and the smooth mucosal surface without villi or plicae circulares.","batch":1,"stem":"What are omental appendices of the colon?","choices":{"A":"Fatty projections of the serosa","B":"Mucosal folds over lymphoid nodules","C":"Submucosal outpouchings of the wall","D":"Bands of longitudinal smooth muscle","E":"Sacculations between muscle bands"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Omental appendices are small fatty projections of the serosa on the outer colonic surface. Teniae are muscle bands, and haustra are sacculations between them. Mucosal folds over lymphoid nodules are internal features, whereas omental appendices hang from the outer serosal surface. Submucosal outpouchings describe diverticula, not the fatty serosal tags called omental appendices. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; gross features, Fig. 17.28; Muscularis externa). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: haustra coli are the visible sacculations between the teniae on the external surface of the cecum and colon (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the colon is subdivided into ascending, transverse, descending, and sigmoid parts by anatomic location (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 4 — colonic motility and haustral contractions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Mucosal folds over lymphoid nodules are internal features, w","C":"Submucosal outpouchings describe diverticula, not the fatty "}},{"id":"sdl24_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the main epithelial cell types of the large intestine (columnar absorptive cells and goblet cells) and explain their roles in water/electrolyte reabsorption and mucus secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the primary function of colonic columnar absorptive cells?","choices":{"A":"Absorbing most dietary amino acids","B":"Secreting brush-border disaccharidases","C":"Reabsorbing water and electrolytes","D":"Transporting dimeric IgA to the lumen","E":"Producing antimicrobial defensins"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The primary function of colonic columnar absorptive cells is reabsorbing water and electrolytes. They use the same Na+/K+-ATPase-driven system as small intestinal enterocytes. Most dietary amino acids are absorbed in the small intestine, not by colonocytes, whose role is water and electrolyte reabsorption. Colonocytes do not express digestive enzymes in their glycocalyx, unlike small intestinal enterocytes. Defensins come from Paneth cells, which are normally absent from the human large intestine. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Mucosa, Figs. 17.29–17.30). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colonic absorptive cells are essentially identical in morphology to small intestinal enterocytes (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the principal functions of the large intestine are electrolyte and water reabsorption and elimination of undigested waste (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 2 — electrolyte absorption in jejunum, ileum, and colon","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Most dietary amino acids are absorbed in the small intestine","B":"Colonocytes do not express digestive enzymes in their glycoc","E":"Defensins come from Paneth cells, which are normally absent "}},{"id":"sdl24_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the main epithelial cell types of the large intestine (columnar absorptive cells and goblet cells) and explain their roles in water/electrolyte reabsorption and mucus secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the ratio of columnar absorptive cells to goblet cells in most of the colon?","choices":{"A":"About 4:1","B":"About 1:1","C":"About 1:3","D":"About 20:1","E":"About 100:1"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Columnar absorptive cells predominate about 4:1 over goblet cells in most of the colon. The ratio approaches 1:1 near the rectum, where goblet cells increase. A 1:1 ratio is found only near the rectum, not in most of the colon. Goblet cells never outnumber absorptive cells 3:1; at most the ratio reaches 1:1 near the rectum. Twenty to one far overstates absorptive cell predominance, which is about 4:1. One hundred to one would leave the colon nearly goblet-free, yet goblet cells are more numerous than in the small intestine. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Mucosa, Figs. 17.29–17.30). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: as absorptive cells are traced into the colonic glands they become fewer, while goblet cells increase (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the 4:1 predominance of absorptive cells is not readily apparent in histologic sections (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 2 — electrolyte absorption in jejunum, ileum, and colon","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"A 1:1 ratio is found only near the rectum, not in most of th","C":"Goblet cells never outnumber absorptive cells 3:1; at most t","D":"Twenty to one far overstates absorptive cell predominance, w","E":"One hundred to one would leave the colon nearly goblet-free,"}},{"id":"sdl24_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the main epithelial cell types of the large intestine (columnar absorptive cells and goblet cells) and explain their roles in water/electrolyte reabsorption and mucus secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the turnover time of the glycocalyx secreted by colonic absorptive cells?","choices":{"A":"16–24 minutes","B":"2–3 hours","C":"4–6 days","D":"3–4 weeks","E":"16–24 hours"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Colonic absorptive cells secrete glycocalyx rapidly, with a turnover time of 16–24 hours in humans. Unlike in the small intestine, it has not been shown to contain digestive enzymes. Sixteen to 24 minutes is too fast; glycocalyx turnover is 16 to 24 hours. Two to 3 hours is too short for the 16–24-hour glycocalyx turnover. About 6 days is the turnover of the cells themselves, not of their glycocalyx. Three to 4 weeks matches enteroendocrine cell lifespan, not glycocalyx turnover. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Mucosa, Figs. 17.29–17.30). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the colonic glycocalyx has not been shown to contain digestive enzymes (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: Na+/K+-ATPase is abundant in the lateral plasma membranes of colonic absorptive cells (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 2 — electrolyte absorption in jejunum, ileum, and colon","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Sixteen to 24 minutes is too fast; glycocalyx turnover is 16","B":"Two to 3 hours is too short for the 16–24-hour glycocalyx tu","C":"About 6 days is the turnover of the cells themselves, not of","D":"Three to 4 weeks matches enteroendocrine cell lifespan, not "}},{"id":"sdl24_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Outline the process of epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine, including the stem cell location at the base of intestinal glands and turnover times for absorptive and goblet cells.","batch":1,"stem":"What part of the colonic intestinal gland forms the stem cell niche?","choices":{"A":"Upper third near the surface","B":"Lower third of the gland","C":"Middle third of the gland","D":"Entire length of the gland","E":"Surface cuff between glands"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Stem cells sit at the bottom of the intestinal gland, and the lower third of the gland is the stem cell niche. New cells then migrate up to the luminal surface. The upper gland near the surface is where cells migrate toward shedding, not the niche. The middle third holds migrating cells; the niche and amplifying zone are in the lower third. Stem cells are restricted to the gland base, not spread along its whole length. The surface cuff between glands is where senile cells are shed, not where they are generated. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: all mucosal epithelial cells of the large intestine come from stem cells at the bottom of the intestinal gland (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the intermediate cell types in the lower third of the colonic gland are identical to those in the small intestine (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — small-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The upper gland near the surface is where cells migrate towa","C":"The middle third holds migrating cells; the niche and amplif","D":"Stem cells are restricted to the gland base, not spread alon","E":"The surface cuff between glands is where senile cells are sh"}},{"id":"sdl24_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Outline the process of epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine, including the stem cell location at the base of intestinal glands and turnover times for absorptive and goblet cells.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the turnover time of colonic enteroendocrine cells?","choices":{"A":"About 6 hours","B":"About 24 hours","C":"About 2 days","D":"About 1 year","E":"Up to 4 weeks"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Colonic absorptive and goblet cells turn over in about 6 days, whereas enteroendocrine cells last up to 4 weeks. Six hours is far shorter than the up-to-4-week life of colonic enteroendocrine cells. Twenty-four hours is too short for enteroendocrine cells, which live up to 4 weeks. Two days is shorter even than the 6-day turnover of absorptive and goblet cells. One year greatly exceeds the up-to-4-week lifespan of colonic enteroendocrine cells. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colonic epithelial turnover times are similar to those of the small intestine (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: in the stomach, cells migrating downward to become parietal, chief, and enteroendocrine cells turn over in about 1 year (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — small-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Six hours is far shorter than the up-to-4-week life of colon","B":"Twenty-four hours is too short for enteroendocrine cells, wh","C":"Two days is shorter even than the 6-day turnover of absorpti","D":"One year greatly exceeds the up-to-4-week lifespan of coloni"}},{"id":"sdl24_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Outline the process of epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine, including the stem cell location at the base of intestinal glands and turnover times for absorptive and goblet cells.","batch":1,"stem":"Where on the colonic surface are senile epithelial cells shed?","choices":{"A":"Base of each intestinal gland","B":"Rim of each gland opening","C":"Crests of the semilunar folds","D":"Between two adjacent glands","E":"Tips of the colonic villi"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Senile colonic epithelial cells that reach the surface undergo apoptosis and are shed at the midpoint between two adjacent intestinal glands. The colon has no villi. The gland base is the stem cell niche, where cells are made rather than shed. Cells are shed midway between gland openings, not at the rim of each opening. Semilunar folds are contraction-dependent folds between haustra, not the defined shedding site. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: on scanning EM, the colonic surface is divided by clefts into territories of gland openings (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: colonic gland openings on the luminal surface are arranged in an orderly pattern (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — small-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The gland base is the stem cell niche, where cells are made ","B":"Cells are shed midway between gland openings, not at the rim","C":"Semilunar folds are contraction-dependent folds between haus"}},{"id":"sdl24_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Summarize the specializations of the lamina propria in the large intestine, including the collagen table, pericryptal fibroblast sheath, and the development of GALT.","batch":1,"stem":"Where does the subepithelial collagen–proteoglycan layer of the colon lie?","choices":{"A":"Between muscularis mucosae and submucosa","B":"Between the teniae and the circular muscle","C":"Under the epithelial basal lamina","D":"Within the core of each lymphatic nodule","E":"Beneath the serosal mesothelium layer"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The collagen table is a thick layer of collagen and proteoglycans between the epithelial basal lamina and that of the fenestrated absorptive venous capillaries. The collagen table lies directly under the surface epithelium, not deep at the muscularis mucosae. The teniae and circular muscle are muscularis externa, far from the subepithelial collagen table. Lymphatic nodule cores are GALT, not the site of the subepithelial collagen–proteoglycan layer. The collagen table is subepithelial in the mucosa, not beneath the serosa. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Lamina propria; Cecum and appendix). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the collagen table participates in regulating water and electrolyte transport from the epithelium to the vascular compartment (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the absorptive venous capillaries under the colonic epithelium are fenestrated (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The collagen table lies directly under the surface epitheliu","B":"The teniae and circular muscle are muscularis externa, far f","D":"Lymphatic nodule cores are GALT, not the site of the subepit","E":"The collagen table is subepithelial in the mucosa, not benea"}},{"id":"sdl24_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Summarize the specializations of the lamina propria in the large intestine, including the collagen table, pericryptal fibroblast sheath, and the development of GALT.","batch":1,"stem":"How thick is the subepithelial collagen–proteoglycan layer in the normal human colon?","choices":{"A":"Up to about 5 µm","B":"Up to about 0.5 µm","C":"Up to about 50 µm","D":"Up to about 0.5 mm","E":"Up to about 5 mm"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The collagen table is about 5 µm thick in the normal colon and can be up to 3 times thicker in hyperplastic colonic polyps. Half a micrometer is ten times too thin for the ~5-µm collagen table. Fifty micrometers is ten times the normal ~5-µm thickness, beyond even hyperplastic polyps. Half a millimeter is a hundredfold too thick for the ~5-µm collagen table. Five millimeters is a gross-scale thickness, far beyond the ~5-µm collagen table. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Lamina propria; Cecum and appendix). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hyperplastic polyps are generally benign, not neoplastic (First Aid Gastrointestinal, colonic polyps). COMLEX: the colonic lamina propria shares the basic components of the rest of the gut but has extra specializations (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Half a micrometer is ten times too thin for the ~5-µm collag","C":"Fifty micrometers is ten times the normal ~5-µm thickness, b","D":"Half a millimeter is a hundredfold too thick for the ~5-µm c","E":"Five millimeters is a gross-scale thickness, far beyond the "}},{"id":"sdl24_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Summarize the specializations of the lamina propria in the large intestine, including the collagen table, pericryptal fibroblast sheath, and the development of GALT.","batch":1,"stem":"From what may macrophages in the core of the colonic lamina propria arise?","choices":{"A":"Circulating neutrophils","B":"Pericryptal fibroblasts","C":"Exhausted goblet cells","D":"Epithelial niche cells","E":"Muscularis mucosae cells"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pericryptal fibroblasts divide beneath the gland base and migrate upward with the epithelium. Near the surface most take on macrophage features, and lamina propria macrophages may be their terminal stage. Exhausted goblet cells are epithelial cells shed at the surface, not macrophage precursors. Epithelial niche cells generate epithelium, whereas lamina propria macrophages come from pericryptal fibroblasts. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Lamina propria; Cecum and appendix). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the pericryptal fibroblast sheath exists in both the large and small intestines (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: pericryptal fibroblasts divide adjacent to the epithelial stem cells at the bottom of the gland (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Exhausted goblet cells are epithelial cells shed at the surf","D":"Epithelial niche cells generate epithelium, whereas lamina p"}},{"id":"sdl24_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the lymphatic drainage pattern of the large intestine and explain how the absence of lamina propria lymphatic vessels impacts disease processes such as adenomatous polyp progression and ulcerative colitis.","batch":1,"stem":"Where do colonic lymphatics from the gland bases first drain?","choices":{"A":"Lacteals in the lamina propria core","B":"Vessels reaching the luminal surface","C":"Plexus beneath the serosal surface","D":"Network within the muscularis mucosae","E":"Sinusoids of the regional lymph nodes"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Lymphatics are generally absent from the lamina propria core, but occasional small vessels at the gland bases drain into a network in the muscularis mucosae. Lymph then passes to submucosal and muscularis externa plexuses and on to regional nodes. The colon has no villi and no lacteals; lamina propria lymphatics are absent near the surface. Lymphatics do not reach the luminal surface; they are confined to gland bases. Gland-base lymphatics drain first to the muscularis mucosae, not directly to a subserosal plexus. Regional nodes come last, after the muscularis mucosae, submucosal, and muscularis externa plexuses. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Lamina propria; Cecum and appendix); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Lamina propria lymphatic vessels; Folder 17.6). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: no lymphatic vessels extend toward the luminal surface of the large intestine (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: lymph leaves the colonic wall only after passing through plexuses in the submucosa and muscularis externa (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The colon has no villi and no lacteals; lamina propria lymph","B":"Lymphatics do not reach the luminal surface; they are confin","C":"Gland-base lymphatics drain first to the muscularis mucosae,","E":"Regional nodes come last, after the muscularis mucosae, subm"}},{"id":"sdl24_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the lymphatic drainage pattern of the large intestine and explain how the absence of lamina propria lymphatic vessels impacts disease processes such as adenomatous polyp progression and ulcerative colitis.","batch":1,"stem":"Which antibody marker is used to identify lymphatic endothelium in colonic lamina propria?","choices":{"A":"CD117","B":"CD34","C":"D2-40","D":"CK20","E":"S-100"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The monoclonal antibody D2-40 reacts with a 40-kDa O-linked sialoglycoprotein on lymphatic endothelium and is used to map lamina propria lymphatics. CD117 (KIT) marks normal interstitial cells of Cajal and most gastrointestinal stromal tumors, not lymphatic endothelium. CD34 is a marker of hematopoietic progenitors such as myeloid blasts, not a specific lymphatic endothelial marker. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Lamina propria lymphatic vessels; Folder 17.6). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the absence of lamina propria lymphatics was first found with light and electron microscopy of biopsy samples (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: D2-40 recognizes an O-linked sialoglycoprotein of about 40 kDa (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"CD117 (KIT) marks normal interstitial cells of Cajal and mos","B":"CD34 is a marker of hematopoietic progenitors such as myeloi"}},{"id":"sdl24_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the lymphatic drainage pattern of the large intestine and explain how the absence of lamina propria lymphatic vessels impacts disease processes such as adenomatous polyp progression and ulcerative colitis.","batch":1,"stem":"About what fraction of adenomatous polyps of the large intestine lie in the rectum and sigmoid colon?","choices":{"A":"About a tenth","B":"Almost half","C":"About a quarter","D":"About three-quarters","E":"About nine-tenths"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Almost 50% of adenomatous polyps of the large intestine are in the rectum and sigmoid colon, so rectosigmoidoscopy can detect them. A tenth understates it; almost 50% of adenomatous polyps lie in the rectum and sigmoid colon. A quarter understates the rectosigmoid share, which is almost half of adenomatous polyps. Three-quarters overstates the rectosigmoid share, which is almost half. Nine-tenths overstates it; only about half of adenomatous polyps are rectosigmoid. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Lamina propria lymphatic vessels; Folder 17.6). Textbook enrichment (Pawlina) — detail not in SDL 24 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: tubular adenomas have less malignant potential than villous adenomas (First Aid Gastrointestinal, colonic polyps). COMLEX: an adenomatous polyp is an intraepithelial neoplasm on a tissue mass protruding into the lumen (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina","whyNot":{"A":"A tenth understates it; almost 50% of adenomatous polyps lie","C":"A quarter understates the rectosigmoid share, which is almos","D":"Three-quarters overstates the rectosigmoid share, which is a","E":"Nine-tenths overstates it; only about half of adenomatous po"}},{"id":"sdl24_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the distinctive gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli, haustra coli, omental appendices, and the smooth mucosal surface without villi or plicae circulares.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man has a screening colonoscopy, and a transverse colon biopsy is normal. Compared with an ileal biopsy, which mucosal feature distinguishes it?","choices":{"A":"Glands lined by simple columnar cells","B":"Lamina propria rich in lymphocytes","C":"Muscularis mucosae deep to the glands","D":"Smooth surface without villi","E":"Goblet cells among absorptive cells"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The large intestinal mucosa has a smooth surface with neither villi nor plicae circulares, and its straight tubular glands span the full mucosa. The other features are shared with the ileum. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Mucosa, Figs. 17.29–17.30); First Aid Gastrointestinal (GI histology). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the colon has crypts of Lieberkühn with abundant goblet cells but no villi (First Aid Gastrointestinal, GI histology). COMLEX: the smooth colonic mucosa shows semilunar folds formed by contractions of the muscularis externa (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the distinctive gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli, haustra coli, omental appendices, and the smooth mucosal surface without villi or plicae circulares.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman with long-standing ulcerative colitis has a barium study showing a lead-pipe colon. Which normal arrangement produces the sacculations she has lost?","choices":{"A":"Plicae circulares anchored in the submucosa","B":"Teniae bundles crossing circular muscle","C":"Fat lobules pushing out the serosal surface","D":"Lymphoid nodules distorting the gland rows","E":"Uniform longitudinal muscle around the wall"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Muscle bundles from the teniae penetrate the inner circular layer at irregular intervals. These discontinuities let colonic segments contract independently and form the haustra. Plicae circulares are small intestinal submucosal folds and are absent in the colon. Fat lobules form omental appendices on the serosa, not haustral sacculations. Lymphoid nodules distort gland spacing in the mucosa but do not create haustra. A uniform longitudinal layer, as in the rectum and appendix, is exactly what lacks haustra. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; gross features, Fig. 17.28; Muscularis externa); First Aid Gastrointestinal (inflammatory bowel diseases). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ulcerative colitis is continuous colonic inflammation that involves the rectum (First Aid Gastrointestinal, inflammatory bowel diseases). COMLEX: ulcerative colitis causes friable mucosa with superficial or deep ulcerations and crypt abscesses (First Aid Gastrointestinal, inflammatory bowel diseases).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 4 — colonic motility and haustral contractions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Plicae circulares are small intestinal submucosal folds and ","C":"Fat lobules form omental appendices on the serosa, not haust","D":"Lymphoid nodules distort gland spacing in the mucosa but do ","E":"A uniform longitudinal layer, as in the rectum and appendix,"}},{"id":"sdl24_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the distinctive gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli, haustra coli, omental appendices, and the smooth mucosal surface without villi or plicae circulares.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man has a flexible sigmoidoscopy. In the upper rectum, the endoscopist sees several shelf-like mucosal folds. Which structures are these?","choices":{"A":"Colonic semilunar folds","B":"Longitudinal anal columns","C":"Transverse rectal folds","D":"Plicae circulares folds","E":"Gastric rugae-type folds"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The upper rectum is distinguished from the rest of the large intestine by transverse rectal folds. Anal columns are longitudinal folds of the upper anal canal, and semilunar folds lie in the colon. Plicae circulares belong to the small intestine; the large intestine lacks them. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Rectum and anal canal). Textbook enrichment (Pawlina) — detail not in SDL 24 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the pectinate line forms where hindgut endoderm meets ectoderm (First Aid Gastrointestinal, pectinate line). COMLEX: the depressions between the anal columns are called anal sinuses (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 5 — defecation reflex and the anal sphincters","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina","whyNot":{"D":"Plicae circulares belong to the small intestine; the large i"}},{"id":"sdl24_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the main epithelial cell types of the large intestine (columnar absorptive cells and goblet cells) and explain their roles in water/electrolyte reabsorption and mucus secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman has a colonic biopsy. At the luminal surface, many tall, thin goblet cells hold only a few mucinogen granules in the central apical cytoplasm. What explains their appearance?","choices":{"A":"Secretion outpacing mucin synthesis","B":"Mucin loss during tissue processing","C":"Immature cells still in the niche","D":"Apoptosis of aging goblet cells","E":"Conversion into absorptive cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Goblet cells secrete mucus continuously as they migrate. At the surface the secretion rate exceeds the synthesis rate, so exhausted goblet cells appear. Processing loss empties goblet cells throughout, whereas these surface cells are exhausted from secretion. Immature niche cells lie deep in the gland base, not at the luminal surface. Apoptotic shedding occurs between gland openings, but the depleted granules reflect exhaustion, not cell death. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Mucosa, Figs. 17.29–17.30). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: caveolated tuft cells described in the colonic epithelium may be a form of exhausted goblet cell (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: colonic goblet cells may mature deep in the gland, even within the replicative zone (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 3 — small-intestine epithelial cell types","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Processing loss empties goblet cells throughout, whereas the","C":"Immature niche cells lie deep in the gland base, not at the ","D":"Apoptotic shedding occurs between gland openings, but the de"}},{"id":"sdl24_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the main epithelial cell types of the large intestine (columnar absorptive cells and goblet cells) and explain their roles in water/electrolyte reabsorption and mucus secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 57-year-old man has biopsies at several colonoscopy sites. One site has absorptive and goblet cells in a ratio close to 1:1. Where was it most likely taken?","choices":{"A":"Proximal cecum","B":"Lower rectum","C":"Ascending colon","D":"Transverse colon","E":"Descending colon"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Absorptive cells outnumber goblet cells about 4:1 in most of the colon, but the ratio approaches 1:1 near the rectum, where goblet cells increase. The cecum has the usual ~4:1 absorptive-to-goblet ratio, not 1:1. The ascending colon has the typical 4:1 ratio rather than 1:1. The transverse colon has the typical 4:1 ratio rather than 1:1. The descending colon still has the typical 4:1 ratio; 1:1 is reached only near the rectum. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Mucosa, Figs. 17.29–17.30); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Rectum and anal canal). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: rectal mucosa resembles distal colonic mucosa, with straight tubular glands rich in goblet cells (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the rectum is the dilated distal portion of the alimentary canal (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 5 — defecation reflex and the anal sphincters","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The cecum has the usual ~4:1 absorptive-to-goblet ratio, not","C":"The ascending colon has the typical 4:1 ratio rather than 1:","D":"The transverse colon has the typical 4:1 ratio rather than 1","E":"The descending colon still has the typical 4:1 ratio; 1:1 is"}},{"id":"sdl24_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the main epithelial cell types of the large intestine (columnar absorptive cells and goblet cells) and explain their roles in water/electrolyte reabsorption and mucus secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman with chronic constipation has a colonic biopsy showing abundant goblet cells in the intestinal glands. What is the main role of their continuous secretion?","choices":{"A":"Neutralizing gastric acid in chyme","B":"Digesting the residual disaccharides","C":"Killing bacteria with lysozyme","D":"Absorbing short-chain fatty acids","E":"Easing the passage of firm contents"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Colonic goblet cells, more numerous than in the small intestine, secrete mucin continuously to lubricate the bowel. This eases passage of increasingly solid contents. Colonic cells do not express digestive enzymes, so goblet secretion is not digestive. Lysozyme is a Paneth cell product, and Paneth cells are normally absent from the colon. Goblet cells secrete mucin; absorption is the job of columnar absorptive cells. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Mucosa, Figs. 17.29–17.30). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diverticulosis is associated with low-fiber diets and focal weakness in the colonic wall (First Aid Gastrointestinal, diverticulosis). COMLEX: elimination of semisolid to solid waste is aided by the large amount of mucus in the colonic glands (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — epithelial types and mucosal specializations","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Colonic cells do not express digestive enzymes, so goblet se","C":"Lysozyme is a Paneth cell product, and Paneth cells are norm","D":"Goblet cells secrete mucin; absorption is the job of columna"}},{"id":"sdl24_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Outline the process of epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine, including the stem cell location at the base of intestinal glands and turnover times for absorptive and goblet cells.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old man in a research study has newly generated colonic crypt cells labeled. About how long after they begin migrating are these cells shed at the luminal surface?","choices":{"A":"About 5 hours","B":"About 5 weeks","C":"About 5 days","D":"About 5 months","E":"About 1 year"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Newly generated colonic cells undergo a few more divisions as they migrate up to the surface, where they are shed about 5 days later. Five hours is far too fast; shedding occurs about 5 days after generation. Five weeks is longer even than enteroendocrine lifespan; absorptive cells shed in about 5 days. Five months greatly exceeds the ~6-day colonic epithelial turnover. One year is irrelevant to an epithelium that turns over in about 6 days. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: as new colonic cells begin their upward migration, they continue dividing (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: in the stomach, surface and pit mucous cells turn over in 3–5 days (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — small-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Five hours is far too fast; shedding occurs about 5 days aft","B":"Five weeks is longer even than enteroendocrine lifespan; abs","D":"Five months greatly exceeds the ~6-day colonic epithelial tu","E":"One year is irrelevant to an epithelium that turns over in a"}},{"id":"sdl24_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Outline the process of epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine, including the stem cell location at the base of intestinal glands and turnover times for absorptive and goblet cells.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old woman has a colonic biopsy examined by electron microscopy. Deep in a gland, two cells with apical mucinogen granules are in mitosis. What does this show?","choices":{"A":"Mature goblet cells can still divide","B":"Mucinogen granules form during mitosis","C":"Exhausted surface cells reenter the cycle","D":"Paneth cells give rise to new goblets","E":"Absorptive cells convert into goblets"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Some intestinal cells keep dividing after they differentiate. Colonic goblet cells can mature deep in the gland, even in the replicative zone, and still divide. Exhausted cells are at the luminal surface awaiting shedding, not deep in the gland. Paneth cells are normally absent from the human large intestine. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Mucosa, Figs. 17.29–17.30); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine). Textbook enrichment (Pawlina) — detail not in SDL 24 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: dividing intestinal cells typically move away from the basal lamina toward the gland lumen (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: chromosomes of dividing goblet cells lack a surrounding nuclear membrane on EM (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — small-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina","whyNot":{"C":"Exhausted cells are at the luminal surface awaiting shedding","D":"Paneth cells are normally absent from the human large intest"}},{"id":"sdl24_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Outline the process of epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine, including the stem cell location at the base of intestinal glands and turnover times for absorptive and goblet cells.","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old man has hundreds of colonic adenomatous polyps, and his father had colectomy at age 35. Which initial epithelial event leads to small polyp formation in his colon?","choices":{"A":"Mutation of the KRAS proto-oncogene","B":"Deletion of the TP53 suppressor gene","C":"Methylation of the MLH1 repair gene","D":"Loss of the APC suppressor gene","E":"Gain of the RET tyrosine kinase gene"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In the chromosomal instability pathway, colonic epithelial cells that lose the APC tumor suppressor gene develop small polyps. KRAS mutation then makes a benign adenoma, and p53 and DCC loss lead to carcinoma. MLH1 promoter hypermethylation defines the CpG island methylator (CIMP) pathway of microsatellite-unstable cancers, not the APC-initiated polyposis sequence. Heterozygous loss-of-function RET mutations underlie Hirschsprung disease; RET is not part of the APC-driven adenoma sequence. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Folder 17.7, colorectal cancer); First Aid Gastrointestinal (familial adenomatous polyposis). Textbook enrichment (Pawlina) — detail not in SDL 24 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: familial adenomatous polyposis is an autosomal dominant APC mutation on chromosome 5q21-q22 (First Aid Gastrointestinal, familial adenomatous polyposis). COMLEX: about 98% of colorectal cancers are adenocarcinomas arising from glandular epithelium (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — small-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina","whyNot":{"C":"MLH1 promoter hypermethylation defines the CpG island methyl","E":"Heterozygous loss-of-function RET mutations underlie Hirschs"}},{"id":"sdl24_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Summarize the specializations of the lamina propria in the large intestine, including the collagen table, pericryptal fibroblast sheath, and the development of GALT.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man has a small hyperplastic colonic polyp removed. Under the surface epithelium, a layer of fibrillar protein and proteoglycans is about 15 µm thick. Which structure is thickened?","choices":{"A":"Basal lamina","B":"Muscularis mucosae","C":"Pericryptal sheath","D":"Submucosa","E":"Collagen table"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The collagen table lies between the epithelial and capillary basal laminae and can be up to 3 times its normal thickness in hyperplastic colonic polyps. The basal lamina is a thin layer bounding the epithelium; the collagen table lies between basal laminae. The muscularis mucosae is smooth muscle at the mucosal base, not a subepithelial collagen layer. The pericryptal sheath is replicating fibroblasts around gland bases, not a fibrillar layer under surface epithelium. The submucosa lies below the muscularis mucosae, not directly under the surface epithelium. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Lamina propria; Cecum and appendix). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: mucosal polyps are usually small, look like normal mucosa, and are clinically insignificant (First Aid Gastrointestinal, colonic polyps). COMLEX: submucosal polyps may include lipomas, leiomyomas, and fibromas (First Aid Gastrointestinal, colonic polyps).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 2 — electrolyte absorption in jejunum, ileum, and colon","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The basal lamina is a thin layer bounding the epithelium; th","B":"The muscularis mucosae is smooth muscle at the mucosal base,","C":"The pericryptal sheath is replicating fibroblasts around gla","D":"The submucosa lies below the muscularis mucosae, not directl"}},{"id":"sdl24_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Summarize the specializations of the lamina propria in the large intestine, including the collagen table, pericryptal fibroblast sheath, and the development of GALT.","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old woman has a colonic biopsy. Large lymphatic nodules distort the regular spacing of the glands and extend into the submucosa. What best explains this extensive lymphoid development?","choices":{"A":"Absorption of dietary antigens","B":"Thin goblet-cell mucus layer","C":"Absence of epithelial renewal","D":"Abundant luminal microbes","E":"Drainage of lacteal lymph flow"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Colonic GALT is continuous with that of the terminal ileum and is more extensively developed. This probably reflects the large number and variety of microorganisms and noxious metabolic products in the colonic lumen. Colonic goblet cells are more numerous than in the small intestine, so a thin mucus layer is not the explanation. The colonic epithelium renews continuously about every 6 days. The colon has no villi and so no lacteal lymph flow. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Lamina propria; Cecum and appendix). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Crohn disease microscopically shows noncaseating granulomas and lymphoid aggregates (First Aid Gastrointestinal, inflammatory bowel diseases). COMLEX: large colonic lymphatic nodules extend from the lamina propria into the submucosa (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Colonic goblet cells are more numerous than in the small int","C":"The colonic epithelium renews continuously about every 6 day","E":"The colon has no villi and so no lacteal lymph flow."}},{"id":"sdl24_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Summarize the specializations of the lamina propria in the large intestine, including the collagen table, pericryptal fibroblast sheath, and the development of GALT.","batch":2,"stem":"A 9-year-old boy has acute appendicitis without a fecalith. The resected appendix shows markedly enlarged lymphatic nodules with germinal centers. What is the most likely cause of his obstruction?","choices":{"A":"Fibrous scar tissue","B":"Lymphoid hyperplasia","C":"Carcinoid tumor mass","D":"Inspissated mucus plug","E":"Spasm of the teniae"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The most conspicuous feature of the appendix is its many lymphatic nodules extending into the submucosa. In children, appendicitis can be caused by lymphoid hyperplasia, whereas fecaliths are typical in adults. The appendix lacks teniae coli, so teniae spasm cannot obstruct it. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Lamina propria; Cecum and appendix); First Aid Gastrointestinal (appendicitis). Textbook enrichment (Pawlina) — detail not in SDL 24 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: appendiceal obstruction causes early diffuse periumbilical pain through T8-T10 visceral afferents (First Aid Gastrointestinal, appendicitis). COMLEX: the appendix is a common site of carcinoid, a tumor of enteroendocrine cells (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina","whyNot":{"E":"The appendix lacks teniae coli, so teniae spasm cannot obstr"}},{"id":"sdl24_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the lymphatic drainage pattern of the large intestine and explain how the absence of lamina propria lymphatic vessels impacts disease processes such as adenomatous polyp progression and ulcerative colitis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old man has a pedunculated sigmoid polyp removed. It contains carcinoma confined to the mucosa above the muscularis mucosae. Why is endoscopic removal considered adequate?","choices":{"A":"Polyp stalks contain no blood vessels","B":"Basal lamina halts carcinoma cell spread","C":"Serosa blocks spread to the regional nodes","D":"Mucosal cancer cells stop dividing early","E":"No lymphatics in the lamina propria"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The colonic lamina propria normally has no lymphatic vessels, so cancer may grow within the epithelium and lamina propria before it reaches lymphatics at the muscularis mucosae. While a lesion is confined to the mucosa, endoscopic removal is adequate, pending microscopic review. The key barrier is absent lamina propria lymphatics, not the basal lamina, which the carcinoma has already crossed into lamina propria. The serosa is irrelevant here; mucosal lesions cannot reach nodes because lamina propria lacks lymphatics. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Lamina propria lymphatic vessels; Folder 17.6). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: villous adenomas have more malignant potential than tubular ones ('villous histology is villainous') (First Aid Gastrointestinal, colonic polyps). COMLEX: the polyp stalk is continuous with the colonic submucosa (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The key barrier is absent lamina propria lymphatics, not the","C":"The serosa is irrelevant here; mucosal lesions cannot reach "}},{"id":"sdl24_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the lymphatic drainage pattern of the large intestine and explain how the absence of lamina propria lymphatic vessels impacts disease processes such as adenomatous polyp progression and ulcerative colitis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman with ulcerative colitis has a colonic biopsy. Lymphatic vessels are numerous in the lamina propria between the glands. What does this finding indicate?","choices":{"A":"Active inflammation","B":"Healed remission phase","C":"Normal colonic mucosa","D":"Early adenoma growth","E":"Ischemic mucosal injury"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Lamina propria lymphatics are normally absent. In ulcerative colitis, granulation tissue brings new blood and lymphatic vessels, so more of them signals active inflammation, and their disappearance tracks response to treatment. In remission, lamina propria lymphatics disappear, so numerous vessels argue against healing. Normal colonic lamina propria lacks lymphatics between glands. Lymphatic vessels in the colonic lamina propria proliferate with granulation tissue in active ulcerative colitis and disappear with treatment, so their number tracks inflammation, not adenoma growth. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Lamina propria lymphatic vessels; Folder 17.6). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ulcerative colitis complications include toxic megacolon and colorectal cancer, with risk rising in pancolitis (First Aid Gastrointestinal, inflammatory bowel diseases). COMLEX: treatment progress in ulcerative colitis can be monitored by biopsies showing lymphatics disappearing (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"In remission, lamina propria lymphatics disappear, so numero","C":"Normal colonic lamina propria lacks lymphatics between gland","D":"Lymphatic vessels in the colonic lamina propria proliferate "}},{"id":"sdl24_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the lymphatic drainage pattern of the large intestine and explain how the absence of lamina propria lymphatic vessels impacts disease processes such as adenomatous polyp progression and ulcerative colitis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old man with ulcerative colitis has biopsies showing new lymphatic vessels forming in the lamina propria. Expression of which factor is linked to this lymphangiogenesis?","choices":{"A":"Transforming growth factor alpha","B":"Hepatocyte growth factor and c-Met","C":"Vascular endothelial growth factor","D":"Fibroblast growth factor 10 signaling","E":"Insulin-like growth factor 1 axis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Lymphangiogenesis in ulcerative colitis is linked to the expression of vascular endothelial growth factors (VEGFs). Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Lamina propria lymphatic vessels; Folder 17.6). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ulcerative colitis is associated with primary sclerosing cholangitis and MPO-ANCA (First Aid Gastrointestinal, inflammatory bowel diseases). COMLEX: in ulcerative colitis, granulation tissue forms with proliferating blood and lymphatic vessels (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl24_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the distinctive gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli, haustra coli, omental appendices, and the smooth mucosal surface without villi or plicae circulares.","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman has a colonoscopy. Crescent-shaped folds project into the colonic lumen. What forms these folds?","choices":{"A":"Submucosal plicae circulares","B":"Lymphoid nodules in the mucosa","C":"Fat in the omental appendices","D":"Muscularis externa contraction","E":"Pressure from the luminal feces"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The smooth colonic mucosa has no plicae or villi. Its semilunar folds are formed by contraction of the muscularis externa. Colonic lymphatic nodules distort gland spacing and extend into the submucosa rather than forming crescentic folds. Omental appendices are fatty projections of the outer serosa and do not project into the lumen. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Muscularis externa). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: haustra form because teniae bundles penetrate the circular layer, so colonic segments contract independently (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: mass peristaltic movements of the colon usually occur about once a day (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 4 — colonic motility and haustral contractions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Colonic lymphatic nodules distort gland spacing and extend i","C":"Omental appendices are fatty projections of the outer serosa"}},{"id":"trial_sdl24_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the main epithelial cell types of the large intestine (columnar absorptive cells and goblet cells) and explain their roles in water/electrolyte reabsorption and mucus secretion.","batch":3,"stem":"A 45-year-old man has a colon biopsy. Absorptive cells show dilated intercellular spaces. Which lateral membrane pump drives their fluid uptake?","choices":{"A":"Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase","B":"H⁺/K⁺-ATPase","C":"Ca²⁺-ATPase","D":"Proton V-ATPase","E":"Cu²⁺-ATPase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Colonic absorptive cells transport water and electrolytes with the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase system in their lateral membranes, so the intercellular space is often dilated. H⁺/K⁺-ATPase is the parietal cell proton pump of the stomach, not the colonic absorptive pump. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Mucosa). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colonic absorptive cells are morphologically like small intestinal enterocytes (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the dilated intercellular space reflects active fluid transport (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 2 — electrolyte absorption in jejunum, ileum, and colon","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"H⁺/K⁺-ATPase is the parietal cell proton pump of the stomach"}},{"id":"trial_sdl24_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Outline the process of epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine, including the stem cell location at the base of intestinal glands and turnover times for absorptive and goblet cells.","batch":3,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman has a research colon biopsy. After leaving the stem cell niche, how many more divisions do new colonic cells undergo?","choices":{"A":"Five to six","B":"Ten to twelve","C":"Two to three","D":"Twenty or more","E":"Thirty to forty"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Newly generated colonic cells undergo two or three more divisions as they migrate up toward the luminal surface. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colonic absorptive and goblet cells turn over in about 6 days (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: colonic stem cells reside at the bottom of the intestinal gland (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — small-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl24_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Summarize the specializations of the lamina propria in the large intestine, including the collagen table, pericryptal fibroblast sheath, and the development of GALT.","batch":3,"stem":"A 50-year-old man has a right hemicolectomy. With which region's lymphoid tissue is his cecal GALT continuous?","choices":{"A":"Gastric antrum","B":"Terminal ileum","C":"Distal duodenum","D":"Upper jejunum","E":"Anal canal"},"correct":"B","explanation":"GALT of the large intestine is continuous with that of the terminal ileum and is more extensively developed. The jejunum lacks aggregated lymphatic nodules, which are characteristic of the ileum (Peyer patches). Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Lamina propria). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colonic lymphatic nodules can distort the regular spacing of the glands (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the appendix has many lymphatic nodules that extend into the submucosa (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"The jejunum lacks aggregated lymphatic nodules, which are ch"}},{"id":"trial_sdl24_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the lymphatic drainage pattern of the large intestine and explain how the absence of lamina propria lymphatic vessels impacts disease processes such as adenomatous polyp progression and ulcerative colitis.","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman has a sigmoid colectomy. Lymph leaves the network in the deepest layer of the colonic mucosa. Where does it go next?","choices":{"A":"Submucosal and muscular plexuses","B":"Lacteals in the lamina propria","C":"Vessels near the luminal surface","D":"Portal vein radicles in the liver","E":"Thoracic duct directly from mucosa"},"correct":"A","explanation":"From the network in the muscularis mucosae, lymphatics drain to plexuses in the submucosa and muscularis externa, then to regional lymph nodes. The colonic lamina propria lacks lymphatic capillaries in its core near the luminal surface; small lymphatics are found only at the gland bases. Colonic lamina propria lacks lymphatic capillaries in its core near the luminal surface; small lymphatics are found only at the gland bases. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Lamina propria lymphatic vessels). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the colonic lamina propria core between glands normally lacks lymphatic vessels (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: lymphatic endothelium can be labeled with the D2-40 antibody (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The colonic lamina propria lacks lymphatic capillaries in it","C":"Colonic lamina propria lacks lymphatic capillaries in its co"}},{"id":"trial_sdl24_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the distinctive gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli, haustra coli, omental appendices, and the smooth mucosal surface without villi or plicae circulares.","batch":3,"stem":"A 42-year-old man's colon mucosa is imaged by scanning EM. Clefts divide the surface into territories. About how many gland openings does each hold?","choices":{"A":"1 to 2","B":"3 to 5","C":"5 to 10","D":"500 to 1000","E":"25 to 100"},"correct":"E","explanation":"On scanning EM, the colonic surface is divided by clefts into territories that each contain 25 to 100 gland openings. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Mucosa, Fig. 17.29). Textbook enrichment (Pawlina) — detail not in SDL 24 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: colonic glands are straight tubular glands that extend through the full thickness of the mucosa (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the colonic mucosa lacks villi and plicae circulares, so its luminal surface is flat (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina"}]},{"sdlNumber":25,"title":"SDL 25 — Physiology - Small & Large Intestinal Motility","questions":[{"id":"sdl25_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the relationship between slow waves and intestinal smooth muscle contraction and the effects of parasympathetic and sympathetic input on intestinal motility.","batch":1,"stem":"Slow waves in intestinal smooth muscle originate in which cells?","choices":{"A":"Interstitial cells of Cajal","B":"Myenteric ganglion neurons","C":"Submucosal plexus neurons","D":"Enterochromaffin-like cells","E":"Longitudinal smooth muscle cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Costanzo describes the interstitial cells of Cajal, abundant in the myenteric plexus, as the pacemaker of gastrointestinal smooth muscle; their spontaneous depolarizations spread to smooth muscle through gap junctions. Myenteric and submucosal neurons modulate action potentials and force but do not set slow wave rhythm. Enterochromaffin-like cells are gastric histamine cells; it is mucosal enterochromaffin (EC) cells that release serotonin to start the peristaltic reflex. Smooth muscle cells follow the pacemaker rather than generate it. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Slow waves, origin of slow waves); Guyton Ch. 63 (slow waves). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: interstitial cells of Cajal act like the sinoatrial node of the gut and couple to smooth muscle by gap junctions (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: unitary smooth muscle lets action potentials spread rapidly from cell to cell (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic effects on GI function","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the relationship between slow waves and intestinal smooth muscle contraction and the effects of parasympathetic and sympathetic input on intestinal motility.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the intrinsic slow wave frequency of the duodenum?","choices":{"A":"3 per minute","B":"6 per minute","C":"9 per minute","D":"12 per minute","E":"20 per minute"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Costanzo gives slow wave frequencies from 3 to 12 per minute along the tract, with the duodenum highest at 12 per minute. Three per minute is the stomach, the lowest rate. Nine per minute is the ileum. Six per minute is not a characteristic value given, and 20 per minute exceeds the stated range. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Frequency of slow waves; Small intestinal motility); Guyton Ch. 64 (segmentation maximum near 12 per minute in duodenum, 8 to 9 in terminal ileum). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: slow wave frequency sets the maximum contraction frequency, so segmentation in the proximal small intestine reaches at most about 12 per minute (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: slow wave frequency falls from duodenum to terminal ileum (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic effects on GI function","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the relationship between slow waves and intestinal smooth muscle contraction and the effects of parasympathetic and sympathetic input on intestinal motility.","batch":1,"stem":"How does parasympathetic stimulation change the electrical activity of intestinal smooth muscle?","choices":{"A":"Higher slow wave frequency","B":"More spikes per slow wave","C":"Larger slow wave amplitude","D":"Shorter slow wave plateau","E":"Longer repolarization phase"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Neural and hormonal inputs do not alter the intrinsic slow wave frequency; parasympathetic stimulation increases the number of action potentials on the slow wave plateau and so strengthens contraction. A higher slow wave frequency is the classic trap because that rhythm is set by the pacemaker. Slow wave amplitude, plateau, and repolarization are set by pacemaker calcium and potassium currents and are not described as vagal targets; sympathetic input instead decreases spikes. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Frequency of slow waves; gastric and small intestinal motility). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the more action potentials ride on a slow wave, the larger the phasic contraction (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: acetylcholine depolarizes gut smooth muscle and norepinephrine hyperpolarizes it (Guyton Ch. 63).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic effects on GI function","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate segmentation from peristalsis based on their mechanisms and roles in mixing versus propulsion of intestinal contents.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the main role of segmentation contractions in the small intestine?","choices":{"A":"Propelling chyme toward the cecum","B":"Clearing residue during fasting","C":"Emptying stomach into duodenum","D":"Moving feces into the rectum","E":"Mixing chyme with secretions"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Segmentation splits a bolus orad and caudad and then lets it recombine, mixing chyme with pancreatic enzymes and exposing it to the mucosa with no forward propulsion. Propulsion toward the cecum is peristalsis. Fasting clearance is the migrating complex. Gastric emptying and rectal filling by mass movements are other patterns. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Segmentation contractions, Fig. 8.12). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: segmentation mixes and peristalsis propels, both coordinated by the enteric nervous system (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: segmentation movements may still travel about 1 cm anally, so the two patterns overlap (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system control of motility","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate segmentation from peristalsis based on their mechanisms and roles in mixing versus propulsion of intestinal contents.","batch":1,"stem":"During small intestinal peristalsis, what happens to the segment orad to the bolus?","choices":{"A":"It narrows and lengthens","B":"It widens and shortens","C":"It narrows and shortens","D":"It widens and lengthens","E":"It keeps a fixed diameter"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Behind the bolus, circular muscle contracts while longitudinal muscle relaxes, so the segment narrows and lengthens. Widening and shortening is the caudad segment, where circular muscle relaxes and longitudinal muscle contracts. Narrowing with shortening or widening with lengthening would need both layers to contract or relax together, which reciprocal innervation prevents. A fixed diameter does not propel the bolus. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Peristaltic contractions, Fig. 8.12). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: excitatory transmitters such as ACh and substance P act behind the bolus, inhibitory VIP and NO ahead of it (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: contraction of circular muscle narrows a segment and longitudinal contraction shortens it (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system control of motility","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate segmentation from peristalsis based on their mechanisms and roles in mixing versus propulsion of intestinal contents.","batch":1,"stem":"Which mucosal mediator activates intrinsic primary afferent neurons to start the peristaltic reflex?","choices":{"A":"Somatostatin","B":"Serotonin","C":"Histamine","D":"Glucagon","E":"Secretin"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Costanzo states that the bolus is sensed by mucosal enterochromaffin (EC) cells, which release serotonin onto intrinsic primary afferent neurons to start the peristaltic reflex. Somatostatin is an inhibitory paracrine. Histamine is a gastric acid secretagogue. Glucagon and secretin inhibit small intestinal motility. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Peristaltic contractions). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: 5-HT from mucosal cells activates IPANs that initiate peristalsis (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: gastrin, CCK, insulin, motilin, and serotonin enhance small intestinal motility, while secretin and glucagon inhibit it (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system control of motility","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the migrating motor complex and explain its role in small-intestinal motility during fasting.","batch":1,"stem":"At what interval do migrating myoelectric complexes recur during fasting?","choices":{"A":"At 5 minute intervals","B":"At 30 minute intervals","C":"At 90 minute intervals","D":"At 4 hour intervals","E":"At 12 hour intervals"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Costanzo states that migrating myoelectric complexes recur about every 90 minutes during fasting to clear residual chyme from the stomach and small intestine. The shorter and longer intervals do not match the textbook value. Every 5 minutes is far too frequent; fasting migrating complexes recur about every 90 minutes. A 30-minute interval is too short for the roughly 90-minute fasting cycle. A 4-hour interval is too long; the complexes recur about every 90 minutes. A 12-hour interval would allow only one or two complexes per fast, not the regular 90-minute cycle. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (gastric motility; Small intestinal motility; motilin). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the interdigestive complex sweeps from stomach through small intestine every 90 minutes while fasting (Guyton Ch. 63). COMLEX: fasting complexes clear the stomach of residual chyme from the previous meal (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — gastric mixing and propulsion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Every 5 minutes is far too frequent; fasting migrating compl","B":"A 30-minute interval is too short for the roughly 90-minute ","D":"A 4-hour interval is too long; the complexes recur about eve","E":"A 12-hour interval would allow only one or two complexes per"}},{"id":"sdl25_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the migrating motor complex and explain its role in small-intestinal motility during fasting.","batch":1,"stem":"Which hormone initiates the interdigestive migrating complexes?","choices":{"A":"Cholecystokinin","B":"Secretin","C":"Ghrelin","D":"Motilin","E":"Peptide YY"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Motilin, secreted from the upper duodenum during fasting, initiates the interdigestive myoelectric complexes. CCK and secretin are fed-state hormones that slow gastric emptying. Ghrelin is a gastric hunger hormone, not the initiator of fasting complexes. Peptide YY from ileum and colon inhibits secretion and appetite. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (candidate hormones, motilin). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: motilin is a 22 amino acid candidate hormone outside the gastrin-CCK and secretin families (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: motilin receptor agonists such as erythromycin are used as prokinetics (First Aid Gastrointestinal, GI hormones).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — gastric mixing and propulsion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the migrating motor complex and explain its role in small-intestinal motility during fasting.","batch":1,"stem":"How does eating a meal affect motilin secretion?","choices":{"A":"It inhibits secretion","B":"It stimulates secretion","C":"It doubles the cycle length","D":"It shifts release to the ileum","E":"It has no measurable effect"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Guyton states that motilin is released cyclically during fasting and that its secretion is inhibited after ingestion of food, which is why the fasting complexes stop in the fed state. Stimulation is the opposite effect. Cycle length doubling and an ileal shift are not described; motilin comes from stomach and upper duodenum. Sources: Guyton Ch. 63 (motilin); Costanzo Ch. 8 (motilin secreted during fasting). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: feeding replaces fasting complexes with segmentation and peristalsis (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: motilin is secreted by the stomach and upper duodenum, not the ileum (Guyton Ch. 63).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — gastric mixing and propulsion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain colonic motility, including haustral contractions, mass movements, and the gastrocolic reflex.","batch":1,"stem":"How often do colonic mass movements occur?","choices":{"A":"Ten to twelve times per minute","B":"Three to five times per hour","C":"Twenty to thirty times per day","D":"Once or twice per week","E":"One to three times per day"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Costanzo states that mass movements move colonic contents long distances, such as transverse to sigmoid colon, one to three times per day. Ten to twelve per minute resembles duodenal slow wave rhythm. The hourly and frequent daily options are too often, and once or twice per week too rare. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Mass movements); Guyton Ch. 64 (series of mass movements lasting 10 to 30 minutes). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a final mass movement propels feces into the rectum and produces the urge to defecate (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: during a mass movement the colon loses haustrations and contracts as a unit (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — teniae coli and haustra coli","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain colonic motility, including haustral contractions, mass movements, and the gastrocolic reflex.","batch":1,"stem":"The efferent limb of the gastrocolic reflex is mediated by which hormones?","choices":{"A":"Secretin and GIP","B":"Motilin and ghrelin","C":"CCK and gastrin","D":"Somatostatin and PYY","E":"VIP and nitric oxide"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Costanzo describes gastric distention as the afferent limb, carried by the parasympathetic nervous system, and CCK and gastrin as the efferent limb that increases colonic motility and mass movements. Secretin and GIP decrease contractions. Motilin drives fasting complexes. Somatostatin and peptide YY are inhibitory. VIP and NO are inhibitory neurocrines. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Gastrocolic reflex). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the gastrocolic reflex explains the urge to defecate soon after a meal (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: gastrocolic and duodenocolic reflexes nearly disappear when extrinsic autonomic nerves to the colon are removed (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — teniae coli and haustra coli","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain colonic motility, including haustral contractions, mass movements, and the gastrocolic reflex.","batch":1,"stem":"Which combined contraction forms colonic haustrations?","choices":{"A":"Circular and teniae coli relaxation","B":"Muscularis mucosae fold contraction","C":"External anal sphincter contraction","D":"Circular and teniae coli contraction","E":"Ileocecal sphincter tonic contraction"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Guyton describes about 2.5 cm of circular muscle constricting while the teniae coli, the three longitudinal strips, also contract, so the unstimulated wall bulges into baglike haustrations. Relaxation of both layers would not form sacs. The muscularis mucosae folds the mucosa, and the anal and ileocecal sphincters are not involved. Sources: Guyton Ch. 64 (Mixing movements, haustrations); Costanzo Ch. 8 (segmentation in the cecum and proximal colon). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: haustral contractions peak in about 30 seconds and fade over the next 60 seconds (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: slow haustral mixing takes 8 to 15 hours to move chyme from the ileocecal valve through the colon (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — teniae coli and haustra coli","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the defecation reflex and differentiate the neural control and functions of the internal and external anal sphincters.","batch":1,"stem":"What does rectal distention cause in the rectosphincteric reflex?","choices":{"A":"Relaxation of the external anal sphincter","B":"Contraction of the internal anal sphincter","C":"Relaxation of the internal anal sphincter","D":"Relaxation of the ileocecal sphincter","E":"Contraction of the ileocecal sphincter"},"correct":"C","explanation":"As the rectum fills, its smooth muscle contracts and the internal anal sphincter relaxes in the rectosphincteric reflex. The external sphincter stays tonically contracted until it is relaxed voluntarily. Internal sphincter contraction is the opposite response. Ileocecal sphincter contraction follows entry of chyme into the cecum and is unrelated to rectal filling. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Defecation). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: defecation is delayed after internal sphincter relaxation because the striated external sphincter remains contracted (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the internal anal sphincter is a tonic smooth muscle sphincter (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — vagus and pelvic nerve distribution","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the defecation reflex and differentiate the neural control and functions of the internal and external anal sphincters.","batch":1,"stem":"At what degree of rectal filling is the urge to defecate felt?","choices":{"A":"About 5 percent of capacity","B":"About 25 percent of capacity","C":"About 50 percent of capacity","D":"About 75 percent of capacity","E":"About 90 percent of capacity"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Costanzo states that the urge to defecate arises once the rectum fills to 25 percent of its capacity. The other values do not match the textbook. Five percent filling is too little; the urge arises at about 25 percent of rectal capacity. Fifty percent overshoots the threshold, since the urge begins at about 25 percent filling. Seventy-five percent is far beyond the ~25 percent filling that triggers the urge. Ninety percent is near full capacity, well past the ~25 percent urge threshold. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Defecation). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the Valsalva maneuver raises intraabdominal pressure to aid defecation (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: a weak functional sphincter at the rectosigmoid junction keeps the rectum empty most of the time (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — vagus and pelvic nerve distribution","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Five percent filling is too little; the urge arises at about","C":"Fifty percent overshoots the threshold, since the urge begin","D":"Seventy-five percent is far beyond the ~25 percent filling t","E":"Ninety percent is near full capacity, well past the ~25 perc"}},{"id":"sdl25_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the defecation reflex and differentiate the neural control and functions of the internal and external anal sphincters.","batch":1,"stem":"Which nerve controls the external anal sphincter?","choices":{"A":"Pelvic splanchnic nerves","B":"Hypogastric nerves","C":"Vagus nerve","D":"Inferior mesenteric nerves","E":"Pudendal nerve"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Guyton states that the external anal sphincter, striated voluntary muscle, is controlled by the somatic pudendal nerve. Pelvic splanchnic nerves carry sacral parasympathetic fibers that reinforce the defecation reflex. Hypogastric and inferior mesenteric nerves are sympathetic. The vagus does not reach the anal canal. Sources: Guyton Ch. 64 (Defecation); Costanzo Ch. 8 (external anal sphincter is striated). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the external anal sphincter is striated voluntary muscle, unlike the smooth muscle internal sphincter (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: sacral parasympathetic outflow comes from cord segments S2 to S4 (Guyton Ch. 63).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — vagus and pelvic nerve distribution","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the relationship between slow waves and intestinal smooth muscle contraction and the effects of parasympathetic and sympathetic input on intestinal motility.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man has abdominal distention and no flatus 2 days after an open colectomy. Temperature is 37.2°C, pulse is 96/min, and blood pressure is 134/82 mm Hg. Bowel sounds are absent. Serum potassium is 4.1 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). Heightened sympathetic outflow to the gut is suspected. Which change in his intestinal smooth muscle electrical activity is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Faster pacemaker slow wave rhythm","B":"Slower pacemaker slow wave rhythm","C":"Fewer spikes on each slow wave","D":"More spikes on each slow wave","E":"Loss of gap junction coupling"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Sympathetic activity and norepinephrine hyperpolarize gut smooth muscle and decrease the number of action potentials on each slow wave, weakening contractions. Slow wave frequency is intrinsic to the pacemaker and is not changed by neural input, so faster or slower rhythms are traps. More spikes is the parasympathetic effect. Gap junction coupling is not altered by sympathetic input. The normal potassium excludes hypokalemia as a cause of the ileus. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (frequency of slow waves; small intestinal motility, sympathetic activity decreases contraction); Guyton Ch. 63 (norepinephrine hyperpolarizes; peritoneointestinal reflex in Ch. 64). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: peritoneal irritation inhibits excitatory enteric nerves and can cause intestinal paralysis (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: sympathetic fibers to the small intestine arise from the celiac and superior mesenteric ganglia (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — sympathetic and parasympathetic effects on GI function","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the relationship between slow waves and intestinal smooth muscle contraction and the effects of parasympathetic and sympathetic input on intestinal motility.","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old woman develops constipation and dry mouth 3 weeks after starting a drug for urge incontinence that blocks muscarinic receptors. She has no abdominal pain. Abdominal examination shows mild distention with hypoactive bowel sounds. Serum calcium is 9.4 mg/dL (reference range: 8.4–10.2 mg/dL). Reduced action of which transmitter on intestinal smooth muscle best explains her constipation?","choices":{"A":"Norepinephrine","B":"Vasoactive intestinal peptide","C":"Nitric oxide","D":"Somatostatin","E":"Acetylcholine"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Muscarinic blockade removes the excitatory action of acetylcholine released by parasympathetic and enteric neurons, so fewer action potentials ride on each slow wave and contractions weaken. Norepinephrine, VIP, nitric oxide, and somatostatin are inhibitory, so reducing their action would increase motility. Normal calcium excludes hypercalcemia. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (parasympathetic stimulation increases contraction); Guyton Ch. 63 (acetylcholine excites; norepinephrine inhibits); Guyton Ch. 64 (atropine weakens segmentation). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: anticholinergic adverse effects include constipation, dry mouth, urinary retention, and mydriasis (First Aid Pharmacology, muscarinic antagonists). COMLEX: postganglionic parasympathetic neurons sit in the myenteric and submucosal plexuses (Guyton Ch. 63).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — parasympathetic neurotransmitters and effects on GI function","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the relationship between slow waves and intestinal smooth muscle contraction and the effects of parasympathetic and sympathetic input on intestinal motility.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman has 2 years of bloating, vomiting, and abdominal distention without mechanical obstruction on imaging. Full-thickness jejunal biopsy shows normal ganglion cells but a marked loss of c-KIT positive cells around the myenteric plexus. Serum TSH is 2.1 μU/mL (reference range: 0.5–5.0 μU/mL). Loss of which function most likely explains her symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Mucosal serotonin release","B":"Pacemaker slow wave generation","C":"Inhibitory nitrergic signaling","D":"Sympathetic ganglion output","E":"Somatic sphincter control"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The c-KIT positive cells around the myenteric plexus are interstitial cells of Cajal, which generate slow waves and pass them to smooth muscle through gap junctions; without pacemaker activity, coordinated contractions fail and pseudo-obstruction follows. Ganglion cells are normal, so nitrergic inhibition and enteric circuits are intact. Serotonin release is a mucosal enterochromaffin (EC) cell function. Sympathetic and somatic outputs are extrinsic. Normal TSH excludes hypothyroidism. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (origin of slow waves); Guyton Ch. 63 (slow waves). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: slow waves are not action potentials but oscillations in membrane potential set by pacemaker cells (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: slow wave depolarization is due to inward calcium current and repolarization to outward potassium current (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — enteric nervous system regulation of motility","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate segmentation from peristalsis based on their mechanisms and roles in mixing versus propulsion of intestinal contents.","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old man develops profuse watery diarrhea and cramping 12 hours after eating undercooked chicken at a picnic. Temperature is 38.3°C and pulse is 104/min. Barium given earlier for another reason traveled from the jejunum to the colon within minutes. Which motility pattern best explains this rapid transit?","choices":{"A":"Peristaltic rush","B":"Segmentation contractions","C":"Migrating motor complex","D":"Haustral contractions","E":"Gastroileal reflex"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Intense mucosal irritation, as in severe infectious diarrhea, triggers powerful rapid peristalsis, the peristaltic rush, that sweeps contents into the colon within minutes to relieve the small intestine. Segmentation mixes without much propulsion. The migrating complex is a fasting pattern that travels slowly. Haustral contractions are colonic mixing movements. The gastroileal reflex intensifies ileal peristalsis after a meal but does not cause minute-scale transit. Sources: Guyton Ch. 64 (Peristaltic rush; peristalsis); Costanzo Ch. 8 (peristaltic contractions). Textbook enrichment (Guyton) — detail not in SDL 25 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: normal small intestinal peristalsis moves chyme only about 1 cm/min, taking 3 to 5 hours from pylorus to ileocecal valve (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: peristaltic rush is driven by autonomic and brainstem reflexes plus intrinsic myenteric reflexes (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Guyton"},{"id":"sdl25_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate segmentation from peristalsis based on their mechanisms and roles in mixing versus propulsion of intestinal contents.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman in a drug trial receives an agent that inhibits neuronal nitric oxide synthase in the enteric plexuses. Over the next week she reports cramping and slowed bowel transit. Pulse is 72/min and blood pressure is 128/80 mm Hg. Abdominal examination shows mild diffuse tenderness. Failure of which step of the peristaltic reflex best explains her slowed transit?","choices":{"A":"Contraction of circular muscle behind the bolus","B":"Contraction of longitudinal muscle ahead of the bolus","C":"Relaxation of circular muscle ahead of the bolus","D":"Release of serotonin from mucosal cells","E":"Slow wave generation in pacemaker cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In front of the bolus, inhibitory pathways that release VIP and nitric oxide relax circular muscle, so the segment widens and receives the bolus. Blocking NO synthesis leaves that segment narrow, so propulsion stalls and cramping follows. Circular contraction behind the bolus and longitudinal contraction ahead of it use excitatory transmitters. Serotonin release and pacemaker slow waves do not depend on neuronal NO. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Peristaltic contractions, Fig. 8.12). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: VIP and NO are inhibitory enteric neurocrines that relax gut smooth muscle (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: reciprocal innervation keeps circular and longitudinal layers from contracting together (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 2 — esophageal peristalsis and its neural control","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate segmentation from peristalsis based on their mechanisms and roles in mixing versus propulsion of intestinal contents.","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old man with abdominal cramping takes a drug that blocks muscarinic receptors. Jejunal manometry before the dose shows ring contractions at about 11 per minute that split and recombine chyme. After the dose, contractions at the same rhythm become very weak, and the slow wave frequency is unchanged. Serum magnesium is 2.0 mg/dL (reference range: 1.5–2.5 mg/dL). This change shows that his mixing contractions depend on which input?","choices":{"A":"Circulating motilin from the duodenum","B":"Excitation from the myenteric plexus","C":"Sympathetic outflow from the celiac ganglion","D":"Voluntary outflow from the sacral cord","E":"Stretch afferents of the colonic wall"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Guyton notes that segmentation becomes very weak when atropine blocks enteric excitation; slow waves set the rhythm but contractions are not effective without background excitation, mainly from the myenteric plexus. Motilin drives fasting complexes rather than fed mixing. Sympathetic outflow inhibits contraction. Voluntary sacral outflow controls the external anal sphincter. Colonic stretch signals affect the ileocecal sphincter, not jejunal mixing. Sources: Guyton Ch. 64 (Mixing contractions, segmentation); Costanzo Ch. 8 (segmentation contractions). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in the terminal ileum the maximum segmentation rate is about 8 to 9 per minute (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: segmentation exposes chyme to pancreatic enzymes and the mucosa for absorption (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — enteric nervous system and extrinsic modulation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the migrating motor complex and explain its role in small-intestinal motility during fasting.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man with long-standing type 1 diabetes has early satiety and postprandial vomiting. Gastric emptying scintigraphy shows marked delay. He is started on a low dose of a macrolide antibiotic for its prokinetic effect, and his symptoms improve. Which drug action most likely explains his improvement?","choices":{"A":"Dopamine D2 antagonism","B":"Muscarinic receptor agonism","C":"Serotonin 5-HT3 antagonism","D":"Mu opioid receptor agonism","E":"Motilin receptor agonism"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Erythromycin, a macrolide, acts as a motilin receptor agonist and triggers migrating motor complex activity, promoting gastric and small intestinal emptying. Dopamine D2 antagonism is the metoclopramide mechanism. Muscarinic agonism is bethanechol. 5-HT3 antagonists are antiemetics and slow transit. Mu opioid agonists slow motility. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (motilin initiates interdigestive complexes); Guyton Ch. 63 (motilin); First Aid Gastrointestinal (GI hormones, motilin receptor agonists). Textbook enrichment (Guyton) — detail not in SDL 25 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: metoclopramide is a D2 antagonist prokinetic that can cause tardive dyskinesia (First Aid Gastrointestinal, metoclopramide). COMLEX: motilin is released cyclically during fasting and suppressed by eating (Guyton Ch. 63).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 3 — gastric mixing and propulsion","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Guyton"},{"id":"sdl25_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the migrating motor complex and explain its role in small-intestinal motility during fasting.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with systemic sclerosis has chronic bloating and loose stools. A lactulose breath test shows an early rise in hydrogen. Fasting antroduodenal manometry shows no phase III activity over 6 hours. Serum albumin is 3.6 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.5 g/dL). Loss of which function most likely contributed to her condition?","choices":{"A":"Postprandial mixing of jejunal chyme","B":"Rectal storage of formed feces","C":"Colonic absorption of luminal water","D":"Interdigestive clearance of residue","E":"Gastrocolic reflex after meals"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Absent phase III activity means the migrating motor complex is lost; these fasting contractions recur about every 90 minutes and sweep residual chyme and bacteria from the small intestine, so their loss allows bacterial overgrowth, shown by the early hydrogen rise. Postprandial mixing is segmentation. Rectal storage, colonic water absorption, and the gastrocolic reflex are colonic functions. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (migrating myoelectric complexes clear residual chyme); Guyton Ch. 63 (interdigestive complexes every 90 minutes). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: small intestinal bacterial overgrowth causes bloating, diarrhea, and a positive hydrogen breath test (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption). COMLEX: the interdigestive complex moves through the stomach and small intestine in a fasting person (Guyton Ch. 63).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 4 — pathogenesis of malabsorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the migrating motor complex and explain its role in small-intestinal motility during fasting.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old woman in a fasting motility study has a jejunal recording showing bands of intense contractions that start in the stomach, travel down the small intestine, and recur periodically throughout the fast. At the 6-hour mark she eats breakfast. Blood glucose is 92 mg/dL (reference range: 70–100 mg/dL) before the meal. Which change in her recording is most likely after the meal?","choices":{"A":"Shortening of the complex interval","B":"Spread of complexes to the colon","C":"Interruption of the complex cycle","D":"Increase in phase III force","E":"Doubling of slow wave frequency"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Interdigestive complexes are a fasting pattern; food ingestion inhibits motilin secretion, so the cycle is interrupted and replaced by fed segmentation and peristalsis. Eating does not shorten the interval or strengthen phase III. Complexes do not extend into the colon. Slow wave frequency is intrinsic and does not change with feeding. Sources: Guyton Ch. 63 (motilin secretion inhibited after food; interdigestive complexes); Costanzo Ch. 8 (motilin; small intestinal motility). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: peristalsis rises after a meal through duodenal stretch and the gastroenteric reflex (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: motilin is a candidate hormone secreted from the upper duodenum during fasting (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — hormonal control of gastric and duodenal motility","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain colonic motility, including haustral contractions, mass movements, and the gastrocolic reflex.","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman reports an urge to have a bowel movement within 20 minutes after almost every breakfast. She has no weight loss, blood in the stool, or nocturnal symptoms. Physical examination is normal. Hemoglobin is 13.6 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–16.0 g/dL). Which reflex best explains the timing of her urge?","choices":{"A":"Gastroileal reflex","B":"Rectosphincteric reflex","C":"Enterogastric reflex","D":"Gastrocolic reflex","E":"Peritoneointestinal reflex"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Gastric distention by food increases colonic motility and mass movements through the gastrocolic reflex, which pushes feces into the rectum and produces the urge soon after a meal. The gastroileal reflex empties the ileum into the cecum. The rectosphincteric reflex follows rectal filling rather than a meal. The enterogastric reflex slows gastric emptying. The peritoneointestinal reflex inhibits motility. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Gastrocolic reflex); Guyton Ch. 64 (gastrocolic and duodenocolic reflexes). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: an exaggerated gastrocolic reflex contributes to postprandial urgency in irritable bowel syndrome (First Aid Gastrointestinal, irritable bowel syndrome). COMLEX: the afferent limb of the gastrocolic reflex is parasympathetic (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — enterogastric reflexes and duodenal stimuli","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain colonic motility, including haustral contractions, mass movements, and the gastrocolic reflex.","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old man with ulcerative colitis has 8 to 10 bloody stools a day during a flare. Temperature is 37.9°C and pulse is 102/min. Hemoglobin is 11.2 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL), and C-reactive protein is 48 mg/L (reference range: 0–10 mg/L). Which colonic motility pattern is most likely persistently increased in this patient?","choices":{"A":"Haustral mixing","B":"Mass movements","C":"Migrating complexes","D":"Jejunal segmentation","E":"Reverse peristalsis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Guyton notes that irritation of the colon, as in ulcerative colitis, initiates intense mass movements that persist almost all the time, which drives frequent stools. Haustral mixing is lost during mass movements and in active colitis. Migrating complexes and jejunal segmentation are small intestinal patterns. Retrograde peristalsis belongs to vomiting. Sources: Guyton Ch. 64 (mass movements, ulcerative colitis); Costanzo Ch. 8 (Mass movements). Textbook enrichment (Guyton) — detail not in SDL 25 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ulcerative colitis begins in the rectum and extends proximally in continuous fashion (First Aid Gastrointestinal, inflammatory bowel disease). COMLEX: a series of mass movements lasts 10 to 30 minutes and may recur half a day later (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 5 — lymphatic drainage and ulcerative colitis","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Guyton"},{"id":"sdl25_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain colonic motility, including haustral contractions, mass movements, and the gastrocolic reflex.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman has a barium enema for mild constipation. Images show the cecum and ascending colon divided into regular saclike bulges that shift position over a few minutes, with no mass lesion. Thyroid studies and serum calcium are within normal limits. Which function do these contractions mainly serve?","choices":{"A":"Mixing contents for water absorption","B":"Propelling feces into the rectum","C":"Clearing residue during fasting","D":"Relaxing the internal anal sphincter","E":"Preventing backflow into the ileum"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The saclike bulges are haustra from segmentation contractions of the cecum and proximal colon, which mix contents and expose them to the mucosa for water and electrolyte absorption. Propulsion into the rectum is a mass movement. Fasting clearance is the small intestinal migrating complex. Internal sphincter relaxation is the rectosphincteric reflex. The ileocecal sphincter prevents reflux into the ileum. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (large intestinal segmentation contractions, haustra); Guyton Ch. 64 (haustrations). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: teniae coli are three longitudinal muscle bands that create haustra (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: water absorption in the distal colon makes feces semisolid and harder to move (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 1 — teniae coli and haustra coli","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the defecation reflex and differentiate the neural control and functions of the internal and external anal sphincters.","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-day-old boy has not passed meconium and has abdominal distention and bilious vomiting. He was born at term to a mother with an uncomplicated pregnancy. Digital rectal examination is followed by an explosive release of stool. Serum sodium is 138 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L). Absence of which cells in the distal colon most likely explains his findings?","choices":{"A":"Interstitial cells of Cajal","B":"Striated sphincter muscle fibers","C":"Enterochromaffin cells of the mucosa","D":"Sacral preganglionic parasympathetic neurons","E":"Ganglion cells of the myenteric plexus"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Delayed meconium, distention, and explosive stool after rectal examination indicate Hirschsprung disease, a lack of ganglion cells in the myenteric and submucosal plexuses of the distal colon. Without the intrinsic myenteric defecation reflex, the aganglionic segment stays contracted and the internal sphincter does not relax. Pacemaker cells, striated sphincter fibers, and mucosal endocrine cells are present. Sacral preganglionic neurons lie in the cord, not the colon wall. Sources: Guyton Ch. 64 (intrinsic myenteric defecation reflex); First Aid Gastrointestinal (Hirschsprung disease). Textbook enrichment (Guyton) — detail not in SDL 25 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hirschsprung disease results from failed neural crest migration and is associated with Down syndrome and RET mutations (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Hirschsprung disease). COMLEX: rectal suction biopsy is diagnostic, showing absent ganglion cells (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Hirschsprung disease).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 1 — myenteric and submucosal plexuses","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Guyton"},{"id":"sdl25_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the defecation reflex and differentiate the neural control and functions of the internal and external anal sphincters.","batch":2,"stem":"A 32-year-old woman has fecal urgency and leakage 4 months after a prolonged forceps-assisted vaginal delivery. Anal manometry shows a normal resting pressure but a markedly reduced voluntary squeeze pressure. Neurologic examination of the lower limbs is normal. Urinalysis is unremarkable. Dysfunction of which structure most likely explains her findings?","choices":{"A":"External anal sphincter","B":"Internal anal sphincter","C":"Rectal circular muscle","D":"Sigmoid colon teniae","E":"Ileocecal sphincter"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Voluntary squeeze pressure reflects the striated external anal sphincter, controlled by the somatic pudendal nerve, which can be stretched during difficult delivery. Resting pressure comes mainly from the tonically contracted smooth muscle internal sphincter, which is normal here. Rectal circular muscle and sigmoid teniae propel feces. The ileocecal sphincter is not involved in continence. Sources: Guyton Ch. 64 (Defecation, external sphincter and pudendal nerve); Costanzo Ch. 8 (tonic internal anal sphincter; striated external sphincter). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the pudendal nerve can be blocked at the ischial spine for vaginal delivery (First Aid Reproductive, pudendal nerve). COMLEX: the external sphincter is held contracted subconsciously unless conscious signals inhibit it (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — vagus and pelvic nerve distribution","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the defecation reflex and differentiate the neural control and functions of the internal and external anal sphincters.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man has worsening constipation and weak, ineffective attempts at defecation 6 weeks after a sacral fracture that damaged the S2 to S4 nerve roots. Rectal examination shows a large amount of soft stool in the vault. Serum potassium is 4.3 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). Loss of which pathway most likely explains his weak rectal propulsion?","choices":{"A":"Sympathetic fibers in the hypogastric nerves","B":"Vagal fibers supplying the proximal colon","C":"Intrinsic myenteric fibers in the rectum","D":"Parasympathetic fibers in the pelvic nerves","E":"Somatic fibers to the abdominal wall muscles"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Guyton describes the intrinsic myenteric defecation reflex as weak and dependent on reinforcement by a parasympathetic reflex through sacral cord segments; pelvic nerve fibers intensify peristalsis in the descending colon, sigmoid, and rectum and relax the internal sphincter. Damage to S2 to S4 roots removes that reinforcement. Hypogastric fibers are sympathetic and inhibitory. The vagus supplies proximal gut. The intrinsic plexus is intact. Abdominal wall nerves arise higher. Sources: Guyton Ch. 64 (Defecation reflexes, Fig. 64.6); Guyton Ch. 63 (sacral parasympathetics S2 to S4). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: defecation signals entering the cord also trigger a deep breath, glottic closure, and abdominal muscle contraction (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: sacral parasympathetic fibers via the pelvic nerves supply the distal colon, rectum, and anus (Guyton Ch. 63).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — pelvic nerve parasympathetic distribution","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl25_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the relationship between slow waves and intestinal smooth muscle contraction and the effects of parasympathetic and sympathetic input on intestinal motility.","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old man develops constipation after starting a drug for angina that slows atrioventricular nodal conduction and relaxes vascular smooth muscle. Which current underlying intestinal slow wave depolarization does this drug most directly reduce?","choices":{"A":"Inward sodium current","B":"Inward calcium current","C":"Outward potassium current","D":"Outward chloride current","E":"Inward magnesium current"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Costanzo attributes the depolarizing phase and plateau of the slow wave to cyclic opening of calcium channels with inward Ca2+ current; the drug described, a nondihydropyridine L-type calcium channel blocker such as verapamil, blunts that depolarization and contraction. Outward potassium current repolarizes the slow wave. Sodium, chloride, and magnesium currents are not described as the basis of the slow wave. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Mechanism of slow waves). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: constipation is a common adverse effect of nondihydropyridine calcium channel blockers such as verapamil (First Aid Cardiovascular, calcium channel blockers). COMLEX: even subthreshold slow waves produce weak basal contractions (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 1 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl25_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate segmentation from peristalsis based on their mechanisms and roles in mixing versus propulsion of intestinal contents.","batch":3,"stem":"A 30-year-old healthy volunteer eats a radiolabeled meal for a transit study. About how long does chyme normally take to travel from the pylorus to the ileocecal valve?","choices":{"A":"10 to 20 minutes","B":"30 to 45 minutes","C":"3 to 5 hours","D":"12 to 18 hours","E":"2 to 4 days"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Guyton states that small intestinal peristaltic waves are weak and die out after 3 to 5 cm, so net movement averages about 1 cm/min and passage from pylorus to ileocecal valve takes 3 to 5 hours. Minutes describe a peristaltic rush. Twelve hours to days fit colonic transit. Sources: Guyton Ch. 64 (Peristalsis in the small intestine). Textbook enrichment (Guyton) — detail not in SDL 25 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: peristaltic waves move at 0.5 to 2.0 cm/sec, faster proximally than distally (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: villous contractions driven by the submucosal plexus milk lymph from the central lacteals (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 2 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Guyton"},{"id":"trial_sdl25_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the migrating motor complex and explain its role in small-intestinal motility during fasting.","batch":3,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman has an overnight fasting manometry study. Cyclic bands of strong contractions recur periodically throughout the fast. Over which part of the gut do these bands travel?","choices":{"A":"Stomach through small intestine","B":"Esophagus through duodenum","C":"Cecum through sigmoid colon","D":"Duodenum through rectum","E":"Sigmoid colon through anus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Guyton describes the interdigestive myoelectric complexes as waves that move through the stomach and small intestine every 90 minutes in a fasting person, clearing residual chyme. They do not begin in the esophagus and do not run through the colon or rectum, where haustral contractions and mass movements dominate. Sources: Guyton Ch. 63 (motilin, interdigestive complexes); Costanzo Ch. 8 (migrating myoelectric complexes in stomach and small intestine). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: loss of fasting complexes predisposes to small intestinal bacterial overgrowth (First Aid Gastrointestinal, GI hormones). COMLEX: motilin is secreted by the stomach and upper duodenum (Guyton Ch. 63).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 3 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl25_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate segmentation from peristalsis based on their mechanisms and roles in mixing versus propulsion of intestinal contents.","batch":3,"stem":"A 35-year-old man with an end ileostomy after colectomy notices that his stoma output rises sharply within minutes of each meal. Which reflex best explains this increase?","choices":{"A":"Gastrocolic reflex","B":"Enterogastric reflex","C":"Rectosphincteric reflex","D":"Renointestinal reflex","E":"Gastroileal reflex"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Guyton describes a gastroileal reflex in which eating intensifies ileal peristalsis and forces chyme out of the ileum; with an ileostomy that chyme appears as stoma output. The gastrocolic reflex acts on the colon, which has been removed. The enterogastric reflex slows gastric emptying. The rectosphincteric reflex needs a rectum. The renointestinal reflex inhibits motility. Sources: Guyton Ch. 64 (Peristalsis in the small intestine; ileocecal valve emptying). Textbook enrichment (Guyton) — detail not in SDL 25 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the ileocecal sphincter normally holds chyme in the ileum, prolonging absorption (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: about 1500 to 2000 mL of chyme empties into the cecum each day (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 2 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Guyton"},{"id":"trial_sdl25_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the defecation reflex and differentiate the neural control and functions of the internal and external anal sphincters.","batch":3,"stem":"A 70-year-old man with chronic constipation is taught to aid defecation by bearing down. Which action most directly increases his intraabdominal pressure during this effort?","choices":{"A":"Relaxing the abdominal wall","B":"Contracting the external anal sphincter","C":"Breathing rapidly through the mouth","D":"Expiring against a closed glottis","E":"Inspiring through an open glottis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Costanzo states that the intraabdominal pressure for defecation can be increased by the Valsalva maneuver, expiring against a closed glottis, and Guyton adds deep inspiration and abdominal wall contraction. Relaxing the abdominal wall lowers pressure. Contracting the external sphincter opposes defecation. Rapid breathing and inspiration through an open glottis do not build pressure. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Defecation); Guyton Ch. 64 (Defecation reflexes). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: straining with a Valsalva maneuver raises intraabdominal and intrathoracic pressure and transiently lowers venous return (First Aid Cardiovascular, maneuvers). COMLEX: people who often inhibit natural defecation reflexes become constipated (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 5 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl25_q06","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain colonic motility, including haustral contractions, mass movements, and the gastrocolic reflex.","batch":3,"stem":"A 62-year-old man has marked cecal distention from an obstructing sigmoid tumor. Which reflex response is expected where the small intestine joins the large intestine?","choices":{"A":"Contraction of the ileocecal sphincter","B":"Intensified peristalsis in the ileum","C":"Faster emptying of ileal chyme","D":"Increased jejunal segmentation","E":"Relaxation of the internal sphincter"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Guyton states that cecal distention intensifies contraction of the ileocecal sphincter and inhibits ileal peristalsis, delaying further emptying into the cecum; these reflexes run through the myenteric plexus and extrinsic nerves. Intensified ileal peristalsis and faster emptying are the opposite responses. Jejunal segmentation and internal anal sphincter relaxation are not part of this reflex. Sources: Guyton Ch. 64 (Feedback control of the ileocecal sphincter). Textbook enrichment (Guyton) — detail not in SDL 25 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: large bowel obstruction is most often caused by colorectal cancer in older adults (First Aid Gastrointestinal, intestinal obstruction). COMLEX: an inflamed appendix can cause ileocecal sphincter spasm and partial ileal paralysis (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 4 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Guyton"},{"id":"grid_sdl25_q01","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the defecation reflex and differentiate the neural control and functions of the internal and external anal sphincters.","batch":4,"stem":"A 34-year-old man has a complete spinal cord transection at T6 from a motorcycle crash 1 year ago; spinal shock has resolved. He has episodes of reflex bowel emptying at unpredictable times. Serum sodium is 139 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L). Which of the following sets of anorectal findings is most likely in this patient?","grid":{"headers":["Reflex IAS relaxation","Voluntary EAS squeeze","Rectal fullness sensation"]},"choices":{"A":["↓","↔","↔"],"B":["↔","↓","↔"],"C":["↔","↓","↓"],"D":["↔","↔","↔"],"E":["↔","↔","↓"]},"correct":"C","explanation":"Choice C (↔ reflex IAS relaxation, ↓ voluntary EAS squeeze, ↓ rectal fullness sensation) is correct. The intrinsic myenteric defecation reflex and the sacral parasympathetic reflex through the pelvic nerves lie below the T6 lesion, so rectal distention still relaxes the internal anal sphincter, and Guyton notes that people with transected cords have automatic emptying of the lower bowel. Conscious control of the external anal sphincter through the pudendal nerve needs descending cortical input, and the sensation of rectal fullness needs ascending tracts, so both are lost. Choice A (↓ reflex IAS relaxation, ↔ voluntary EAS squeeze, ↔ rectal fullness sensation) is Hirschsprung disease, in which aganglionic bowel fails to relax the internal sphincter. Choice B (↔ reflex IAS relaxation, ↓ voluntary EAS squeeze, ↔ rectal fullness sensation) is pudendal nerve injury, as after a difficult vaginal delivery. Choice D (↔ reflex IAS relaxation, ↔ voluntary EAS squeeze, ↔ rectal fullness sensation) is normal anorectal function, as in functional constipation. Choice E (↔ reflex IAS relaxation, ↔ voluntary EAS squeeze, ↓ rectal fullness sensation) is a rectal sensory neuropathy, as in long-standing diabetes. Sources: Guyton Ch. 64 (Defecation; Defecation reflexes; transected spinal cords); Costanzo Ch. 8 (Defecation, rectosphincteric reflex); First Aid Gastrointestinal (Hirschsprung disease). The ↔ cells and the diabetic sensory row are inferred because the sources do not describe them as changed. Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the sacral parasympathetic defecation reflex runs from rectal afferents to cord segments S2 to S4 and back through the pelvic nerves (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: spinal cord lesions above the sacral segments leave reflex bowel emptying intact but remove voluntary sphincter control (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — pelvic nerve parasympathetic distribution","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":26,"title":"SDL 26 — Physiology - Digestion & Absorption of the Small Intestine Part 1 (Carbs, Proteins)","questions":[{"id":"sdl26_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe carbohydrate digestion by pancreatic amylase and intestinal brush-border enzymes, and explain the physiologic consequences of lactase deficiency.","batch":1,"stem":"Pancreatic α-amylase digests starch into which products?","choices":{"A":"Glucose, galactose, and free fructose units","B":"Maltose, maltotriose, α-limit dextrins","C":"Sucrose, lactose, and trehalose units","D":"Glycogen, amylopectin, and cellulose","E":"Galactose, maltose, and sucrose units"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pancreatic amylase hydrolyzes interior 1,4-glycosidic bonds in starch and yields three oligosaccharides: α-limit dextrins, maltose, and maltotriose. Brush-border α-dextrinase, maltase, and sucrase then produce glucose. Monosaccharides are the brush-border end products, not amylase products. Sucrose, lactose, and trehalose are dietary disaccharides. Glycogen and amylopectin are substrates, and cellulose is not digested. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of carbohydrates, Fig. 8.27). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: salivary amylase starts starch digestion but is inactivated by low gastric pH (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: human amylase does not hydrolyze the β-1,4 bonds of cellulose (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — brush border of small intestinal villi","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe carbohydrate digestion by pancreatic amylase and intestinal brush-border enzymes, and explain the physiologic consequences of lactase deficiency.","batch":1,"stem":"Brush-border lactase digests lactose into which monosaccharides?","choices":{"A":"Glucose and fructose","B":"Two glucose molecules","C":"Glucose and galactose","D":"Galactose and fructose","E":"Glucose and maltose"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Lactase splits each lactose into one glucose and one galactose. Glucose plus fructose is the sucrase product, and two glucose molecules come from trehalose or maltose. Galactose with fructose is not a dietary disaccharide product, and maltose is a disaccharide. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of carbohydrates). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sucrase splits sucrose into glucose and fructose, and trehalase splits trehalose into two glucose molecules (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: about 80 percent of carbohydrate end product is glucose, with galactose and fructose making up the rest (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — brush border of small intestinal villi","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe carbohydrate digestion by pancreatic amylase and intestinal brush-border enzymes, and explain the physiologic consequences of lactase deficiency.","batch":1,"stem":"Undigested lactose in lactase deficiency causes diarrhea by which mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Active chloride secretion by crypts","B":"Faster colonic motility from gastrin","C":"Mucosal inflammation and blood loss","D":"Bile acid irritation of colonocytes","E":"Osmotic water retention in the lumen"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Nonabsorbable lactose stays in the lumen and holds an equivalent amount of water to keep contents isosmotic, which causes osmotic diarrhea. Crypt chloride secretion drives secretory diarrhea. Gastrin and motility changes are not the mechanism. Inflammation with blood loss suggests colitis. Bile acid diarrhea follows ileal disease. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Disorders of carbohydrate digestion and absorption, Box 8.2). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colonic bacteria ferment undigested lactose and produce gas, causing bloating and flatulence (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: lactose intolerance is managed by avoiding milk products or adding lactase (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — brush border of small intestinal villi","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the intestinal absorption of glucose, galactose, and fructose, including the roles of SGLT1, GLUT5, GLUT2, and the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase.","batch":1,"stem":"Which apical transporter moves glucose and galactose into intestinal epithelial cells?","choices":{"A":"GLUT5","B":"GLUT2","C":"GLUT4","D":"SGLT1","E":"NHE3"},"correct":"D","explanation":"SGLT1, the Na⁺-glucose cotransporter, carries glucose and galactose across the apical membrane against an electrochemical gradient. GLUT5 is the apical fructose transporter. GLUT2 moves monosaccharides across the basolateral membrane. GLUT4 is the insulin-responsive carrier of muscle and fat. NHE3 is an apical Na⁺-H⁺ exchanger. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of carbohydrates, Fig. 8.28). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: SGLT1 in intestine and SGLT2 in the proximal tubule both use the sodium gradient for glucose uptake (First Aid Renal). COMLEX: glucose and galactose absorption resembles transport in the early proximal convoluted tubule (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte apical and basolateral membranes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the intestinal absorption of glucose, galactose, and fructose, including the roles of SGLT1, GLUT5, GLUT2, and the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase.","batch":1,"stem":"What supplies the energy for glucose uptake by SGLT1?","choices":{"A":"Direct ATP hydrolysis by the SGLT1 protein","B":"Sodium gradient set by the Na⁺-K⁺ ATPase","C":"Proton gradient set by apical exchange","D":"Glucose gradient into the enterocyte","E":"Potassium gradient through apical channels"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Costanzo states that the energy for SGLT1 does not come directly from ATP but from the Na⁺ gradient across the apical membrane, which the basolateral Na⁺-K⁺ ATPase creates and maintains. This is secondary active transport. Proton gradients drive peptide uptake. Glucose moves against its gradient on SGLT1. Potassium gradients do not drive it. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of carbohydrates). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secondary active transport couples uphill solute movement to downhill Na⁺ movement (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: Na⁺-glucose cotransport in the small intestine resembles transport in the early proximal tubule (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte apical and basolateral membranes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the intestinal absorption of glucose, galactose, and fructose, including the roles of SGLT1, GLUT5, GLUT2, and the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase.","batch":1,"stem":"How does fructose cross the apical membrane of intestinal epithelial cells?","choices":{"A":"Sodium cotransport via SGLT1","B":"Facilitated diffusion via GLUT4","C":"Proton cotransport via PepT1","D":"Primary active ATP pumping","E":"Facilitated diffusion via GLUT5"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Fructose crosses the apical membrane by facilitated diffusion on the fructose-specific transporter GLUT5 and leaves across the basolateral membrane on GLUT2. No cotransporter or energy-requiring step is involved, so fructose moves down its gradient. SGLT1 carries glucose and galactose. GLUT4 is insulin regulated in muscle and fat. PepT1 carries small peptides. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of carbohydrates). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: fructose absorption by facilitated diffusion does not proceed against an electrochemical gradient (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: glucose and galactose exit the basolateral membrane on GLUT2, as fructose does (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte apical and basolateral membranes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe protein digestion by gastric, pancreatic, and intestinal brush-border proteases.","batch":1,"stem":"Which protease begins protein digestion in the stomach?","choices":{"A":"Trypsin","B":"Elastase","C":"Renin","D":"Pepsin","E":"Thrombin"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Protein digestion begins with pepsin, formed when pepsinogen from chief cells meets low gastric pH. Trypsin and elastase are pancreatic proteases that act in the small intestine. Renin is a renal enzyme, and thrombin is a coagulation protease. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of proteins). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chief cells secrete pepsinogen, and parietal cells secrete H⁺ that activates it (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: pepsin is an endopeptidase that digests collagen in meat (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — gastric HCl secretion by parietal cells","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe protein digestion by gastric, pancreatic, and intestinal brush-border proteases.","batch":1,"stem":"Above what pH is pepsin denatured and inactivated?","choices":{"A":"Above pH 5","B":"Above pH 2","C":"Above pH 3","D":"Above pH 9","E":"Above pH 11"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The three pepsin isozymes have pH optima between 1 and 3, and pepsin is denatured above pH 5, so it stops acting once chyme is neutralized by duodenal bicarbonate. pH 2 and 3 fall in the optimum range. pH 9 and 11 overshoot the textbook threshold. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of proteins). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 26 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic HCO₃⁻ neutralizes duodenal chyme and inactivates pepsin (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: pepsin handles about 10 to 20 percent of total protein digestion (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — gastric HCl secretion by parietal cells","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"},{"id":"sdl26_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe protein digestion by gastric, pancreatic, and intestinal brush-border proteases.","batch":1,"stem":"Which pancreatic proteases are exopeptidases?","choices":{"A":"Trypsin and chymotrypsin","B":"Pepsin and elastase","C":"Carboxypeptidase A and B","D":"Elastase and trypsin","E":"Enterokinase and pepsin"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Exopeptidases hydrolyze one amino acid at a time from the C-terminal end; carboxypeptidases A and B are the pancreatic exopeptidases. Trypsin, chymotrypsin, elastase, and pepsin are endopeptidases that cleave interior bonds. Enterokinase is a brush-border activating enzyme. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of proteins). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: brush-border aminopeptidases and dipeptidases finish oligopeptide digestion to absorbable units (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: elastase digests the elastin fibers that hold meat together (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — pancreatic digestive enzymes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the activation of pancreatic proteases, including enterokinase-mediated activation of trypsin and subsequent activation of other pancreatic zymogens.","batch":1,"stem":"Which enzyme first converts trypsinogen to trypsin?","choices":{"A":"Aminopeptidase","B":"Chymotrypsin","C":"Elastase","D":"Cathepsin B","E":"Enterokinase"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Brush-border enterokinase converts trypsinogen to trypsin; trypsin then activates the remaining zymogens and more trypsinogen. Aminopeptidase is a brush-border exopeptidase that trims oligopeptides. Chymotrypsin and elastase are themselves products of trypsin activation. Cathepsin B is a lysosomal protease. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of proteins, Fig. 8.29). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: enterokinase is also called enteropeptidase (First Aid Gastrointestinal, pancreatic secretions). COMLEX: trypsin activates trypsinogen in an autocatalytic step after the first activation (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — zymogen secretion and enteropeptidase activation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the activation of pancreatic proteases, including enterokinase-mediated activation of trypsin and subsequent activation of other pancreatic zymogens.","batch":1,"stem":"Where is enterokinase located?","choices":{"A":"Pancreatic zymogen granules","B":"Gastric chief cells","C":"Intestinal brush border","D":"Pancreatic ductal cells","E":"Hepatic bile canaliculi"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Enterokinase is a brush-border enzyme of the small intestinal epithelium, so trypsinogen is first activated in the intestinal lumen, not in the pancreas. Zymogen granules hold inactive precursors. Chief cells secrete pepsinogen. Ductal cells secrete HCO₃⁻-rich fluid. Bile canaliculi carry bile. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Exocrine pancreas; Digestion of proteins). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: separating zymogen storage from the activating enzyme protects the pancreas from autodigestion (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: trypsin from the first activation step converts further trypsinogen to trypsin (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — zymogen secretion and enteropeptidase activation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the activation of pancreatic proteases, including enterokinase-mediated activation of trypsin and subsequent activation of other pancreatic zymogens.","batch":1,"stem":"Which enzyme converts chymotrypsinogen, proelastase, and the procarboxypeptidases to active forms?","choices":{"A":"Trypsin","B":"Enterokinase","C":"Pepsin","D":"Lipase","E":"Amylase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Once a small amount of trypsin is made by enterokinase, trypsin catalyzes the conversion of the other inactive precursors to chymotrypsin, elastase, and carboxypeptidases A and B. Enterokinase acts on trypsinogen. Pepsin works in the stomach. Lipase and amylase digest fat and starch and do not activate proteases. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of proteins). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: absence of trypsin alone looks like loss of the pancreatic proteases because trypsin activates each precursor (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: trypsin also activates procolipase and prophospholipase A₂ (First Aid Gastrointestinal, pancreatic secretions).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — zymogen secretion and enteropeptidase activation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of amino acids, dipeptides, and tripeptides, including Na⁺- and H⁺-dependent cotransport.","batch":1,"stem":"Dipeptides and tripeptides cross the apical membrane by which mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Sodium cotransport","B":"Facilitated diffusion","C":"Primary active transport","D":"Proton cotransport","E":"Receptor endocytosis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Separate H⁺-dependent cotransporters carry dipeptides and tripeptides into the cell, using an H⁺ gradient created by the apical Na⁺-H⁺ exchanger. Sodium cotransport carries free L-amino acids. Facilitated diffusion is the basolateral exit route for amino acids. Primary active transport and endocytosis are not the described routes. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of proteins, Fig. 8.31). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the apical peptide carrier is PepT1 (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: peptide carriers allow protein nutrition despite single amino acid transporter defects (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte transport","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of amino acids, dipeptides, and tripeptides, including Na⁺- and H⁺-dependent cotransport.","batch":1,"stem":"In which form is most ingested protein absorbed?","choices":{"A":"Free single amino acids","B":"Dipeptides and tripeptides","C":"Intact dietary protein molecules","D":"Large oligopeptides","E":"Long polypeptide chains"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Costanzo states that most ingested protein is absorbed in the dipeptide and tripeptide forms rather than as free amino acids. Intact proteins and larger oligopeptides are not absorbable and must be broken down by brush-border peptidases. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of proteins). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: proteins are absorbable in larger units than carbohydrates, which must reach monosaccharides (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: oligopeptides are hydrolyzed by brush-border proteases before absorption (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte transport","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of amino acids, dipeptides, and tripeptides, including Na⁺- and H⁺-dependent cotransport.","batch":1,"stem":"What happens to most dipeptides and tripeptides after they enter the epithelial cell?","choices":{"A":"Hydrolysis by cytoplasmic peptidases","B":"Export back out through apical PepT1","C":"Re-esterification in the smooth ER","D":"Packaging into nascent chylomicrons","E":"Conversion to cellular glycogen"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Inside the cell, most dipeptides and tripeptides are hydrolyzed to amino acids by cytoplasmic peptidases, and the amino acids exit across the basolateral membrane by facilitated diffusion; a few peptides are absorbed unchanged. PepT1 imports peptides. Re-esterification and chylomicrons belong to lipid absorption. Glycogen storage is unrelated. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of proteins). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: portal blood receives mostly free amino acids after protein absorption (Guyton Ch. 66). COMLEX: basolateral amino acid exit uses separate carriers for neutral, acidic, basic, and imino amino acids (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte transport","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe carbohydrate digestion by pancreatic amylase and intestinal brush-border enzymes, and explain the physiologic consequences of lactase deficiency.","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old man has bloating, flatulence, and watery diarrhea within 2 hours of drinking milk. Temperature is 36.9°C, pulse is 78/min, and blood pressure is 122/76 mm Hg. A hydrogen breath test is positive after an oral lactose load. Stool is negative for occult blood. Which stool finding is most likely during symptoms?","choices":{"A":"High stool sodium concentration","B":"Fecal leukocytes and blood","C":"Fat globules on Sudan stain","D":"Diarrhea persisting during fasting","E":"Increased stool osmotic gap"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Undigested lactose holds water osmotically in the lumen, so unmeasured osmoles raise the stool osmotic gap, and the diarrhea stops with fasting. High stool sodium with a low gap fits secretory diarrhea. Leukocytes and blood suggest inflammatory diarrhea. Fat on Sudan stain points to fat malabsorption. Persistent diarrhea while fasting suggests secretion. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Box 8.2 lactose intolerance; Disorders of carbohydrate digestion and absorption). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bacterial fermentation of lactose raises breath hydrogen and lowers stool pH (First Aid Gastrointestinal, lactose intolerance). COMLEX: the other brush-border enzymes are preserved in isolated lactase deficiency (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe carbohydrate digestion by pancreatic amylase and intestinal brush-border enzymes, and explain the physiologic consequences of lactase deficiency.","batch":2,"stem":"A 9-month-old girl has had watery diarrhea with each formula feeding for 3 weeks after an episode of rotavirus gastroenteritis. Temperature is 37.0°C, and pulse is 128/min. She is mildly dehydrated. Stool pH is 5.0 (reference range: 7.0–7.5), and stool reducing substances are positive. Which change in her small intestine best explains this course?","choices":{"A":"Low pancreatic amylase output","B":"Loss of brush-border lactase","C":"Defective apical glucose cotransport","D":"Absent enterokinase activity","E":"Deficient bile salt secretion"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Viral injury to villus tip cells reduces brush-border lactase, so formula lactose reaches the colon undigested and is fermented, lowering stool pH with positive reducing substances; the deficit recovers as villi regrow. Low amylase affects starch, not lactose. A glucose cotransport defect would be congenital. Enterokinase loss impairs protein digestion. Bile salt deficiency causes fat malabsorption. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (lactase deficiency, Box 8.2); Guyton Ch. 66 (brush-border disaccharidases). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secondary lactase deficiency follows mucosal injury from gastroenteritis or celiac disease (First Aid Gastrointestinal, lactose intolerance). COMLEX: lactase sits on the villus brush border, where mucosal injury removes it first (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 2 — villus structure and brush border","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe carbohydrate digestion by pancreatic amylase and intestinal brush-border enzymes, and explain the physiologic consequences of lactase deficiency.","batch":2,"stem":"A 57-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes starts acarbose with meals. Two weeks later she reports flatulence and loose stools. Temperature is 36.8°C, and blood pressure is 128/80 mm Hg. Hemoglobin A1c is 7.6% (reference range: 4.0–5.6%). Inhibition of which digestive step most directly explains her symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Brush-border oligosaccharide hydrolysis","B":"Pancreatic triglyceride ester hydrolysis","C":"Basolateral monosaccharide exit","D":"Apical sodium-glucose cotransport","E":"Gastric pepsin protein hydrolysis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Acarbose inhibits brush-border α-glucosidases, so maltose, maltotriose, and α-limit dextrins are not split to glucose and reach the colon, where fermentation causes gas and osmotic stool. Triglyceride hydrolysis is the target of orlistat. Basolateral exit and apical cotransport would act on monosaccharides already formed. Pepsin digests protein. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of carbohydrates); First Aid Endocrine (α-glucosidase inhibitors). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: α-glucosidase inhibitors lower postprandial glucose by delaying carbohydrate digestion (First Aid Endocrine, diabetes drugs). COMLEX: oligosaccharides that escape digestion produce osmotic diarrhea (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 1 — brush-border enzymes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the intestinal absorption of glucose, galactose, and fructose, including the roles of SGLT1, GLUT5, GLUT2, and the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase.","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-week-old boy has severe watery diarrhea with breast milk and with standard formula. Temperature is 36.9°C, pulse is 162/min, and weight is below birth weight. Serum urea nitrogen is 28 mg/dL (reference range: 7–18 mg/dL). Diarrhea stops on a formula whose sole carbohydrate is fructose. Which transporter is most likely defective?","choices":{"A":"Apical fructose-specific uniporter","B":"Basolateral Na⁺-K⁺ ATPase pump","C":"Brush-border lactase hydrolase","D":"Apical sodium-glucose cotransporter","E":"Basolateral monosaccharide uniporter"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Glucose and galactose share the apical SGLT1 carrier, so a defect causes osmotic diarrhea with any glucose or galactose source, while fructose enters through GLUT5 and is tolerated. Loss of the fructose uniporter would spare glucose. A Na⁺-K⁺ ATPase defect would not be selective. Lactase deficiency would tolerate glucose formula. Loss of the basolateral uniporter would block fructose exit too. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of carbohydrates, Fig. 8.28); First Aid Gastrointestinal (malabsorption). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: glucose-galactose malabsorption is an autosomal recessive SGLT1 defect (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: fructose is absorbed independently of sodium (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 1 — solute-coupled water absorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the intestinal absorption of glucose, galactose, and fructose, including the roles of SGLT1, GLUT5, GLUT2, and the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man has profuse watery diarrhea after travel to an area with a cholera outbreak. Temperature is 37.1°C, pulse is 112/min, and blood pressure is 102/64 mm Hg. Serum bicarbonate is 17 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). An oral rehydration solution corrects his volume deficit. Why does this solution work?","choices":{"A":"Glucose blocks crypt chloride secretion","B":"Sodium enters through the apical GLUT5","C":"Glucose stimulates pancreatic fluid outflow","D":"Sodium uptake no longer requires ATP use","E":"Glucose-coupled sodium uptake is preserved"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Cholera toxin drives secretion, but apical SGLT1 still couples glucose entry to sodium entry, and water follows the absorbed solute. Glucose does not block chloride secretion. GLUT5 carries fructose, not sodium. Pancreatic fluid adds to losses. Sodium uptake still requires ATP indirectly, because the gradient depends on the basolateral Na⁺-K⁺ ATPase. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of carbohydrates; oral rehydration in cholera). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: oral rehydration solution uses SGLT1 cotransport of sodium and glucose (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: cholera toxin raises cAMP in crypt cells and opens CFTR chloride channels (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 4 — cholera toxin and secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the intestinal absorption of glucose, galactose, and fructose, including the roles of SGLT1, GLUT5, GLUT2, and the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase.","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old woman training for a marathon tolerates more carbohydrate per hour from a gel that mixes glucose and fructose than from one with glucose alone. Pulse is 58/min, and blood pressure is 112/70 mm Hg. Fasting glucose is 84 mg/dL (reference range: 70–99 mg/dL). Which property of intestinal absorption best explains this?","choices":{"A":"Fructose activates the glucose cotransporter","B":"Fructose uses a separate apical carrier","C":"Glucose stimulates fructose cotransport","D":"Fructose enters against its gradient","E":"Glucose exits through apical GLUT5"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Glucose uptake is limited by apical SGLT1, while fructose enters through a distinct carrier, GLUT5, by facilitated diffusion, so mixing sugars adds absorptive capacity. Fructose does not activate SGLT1, and it is not cotransported. GLUT5 carries fructose down its gradient. Glucose exits across the basolateral membrane on GLUT2. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of carbohydrates). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GLUT2 carries glucose, galactose, and fructose across the basolateral membrane (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: fructose absorption is passive and depends on its concentration gradient (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 2 — monosaccharide transporters","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe protein digestion by gastric, pancreatic, and intestinal brush-border proteases.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man is seen 1 year after total gastrectomy for gastric adenocarcinoma. Pulse is 74/min, and blood pressure is 126/78 mm Hg. Serum albumin is 4.0 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.5 g/dL), and fecal nitrogen is normal. Which statement best describes his protein digestion?","choices":{"A":"Intestinal proteases complete digestion","B":"Protein digestion falls by about half","C":"Free amino acid uptake becomes defective","D":"Trypsinogen activation fails in duodenum","E":"Undigested protein reaches the colon"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Pepsin starts protein digestion, but pancreatic and brush-border proteases can complete it, so protein digestion and absorption stay normal after gastrectomy or without gastric acid. Halved digestion overstates pepsin's role. Amino acid transport and enterokinase activation are intestinal and intact. Normal fecal nitrogen argues against colonic protein loss. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of proteins). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 26 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: after total gastrectomy, vitamin B₁₂ deficiency follows loss of intrinsic factor (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: pepsin accounts for a minor share of protein digestion (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 4 — intrinsic factor and vitamin B12","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"},{"id":"sdl26_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe protein digestion by gastric, pancreatic, and intestinal brush-border proteases.","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old man with chronic pancreatitis from alcohol use has bulky stools and weight loss. Temperature is 36.7°C, and pulse is 88/min. Fecal elastase is 80 µg/g (reference range: >200 µg/g). Fecal nitrogen is increased. Which digestive step is most impaired for dietary protein?","choices":{"A":"Hydrolysis of lactose to monosaccharides","B":"Hydrolysis of proteins to oligopeptides","C":"Activation of pepsinogen in the stomach","D":"Apical uptake of small peptides","E":"Basolateral exit of amino acids"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pancreatic insufficiency removes trypsin, chymotrypsin, elastase, and carboxypeptidases, so protein is not broken down to absorbable units and is lost in stool. Lactose hydrolysis is a brush-border step. Pepsinogen activation depends on gastric acid. Peptide uptake and amino acid exit are epithelial transport steps that remain intact. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Disorders of protein digestion and absorption). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: low fecal elastase is a marker of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency (First Aid Gastrointestinal, chronic pancreatitis). COMLEX: pancreatic enzyme replacement treats both steatorrhea and creatorrhea (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — acute and chronic pancreatitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe protein digestion by gastric, pancreatic, and intestinal brush-border proteases.","batch":2,"stem":"A 39-year-old woman has taken high-dose omeprazole for 2 years for reflux esophagitis. Pulse is 70/min, and blood pressure is 118/74 mm Hg. Intragastric pH during monitoring is 6.0 (reference range: 1.5–3.5). Which change in gastric protein digestion is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Pepsin activity rises in the stomach","B":"Trypsinogen stays inactive in the duodenum","C":"Pepsinogen stays inactive in the stomach","D":"Protein absorption ceases in the jejunum","E":"Chief cells stop secreting pepsinogen"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Pepsinogen is converted to pepsin only at low pH, and pepsin is denatured above pH 5, so gastric proteolysis falls; protein absorption remains normal because pancreatic and brush-border proteases finish the job. Pepsin activity falls, not rises. Trypsinogen activation by enterokinase does not require acid. Chief cells keep secreting. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of proteins). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 26 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: proton pump inhibitors irreversibly block the parietal cell H⁺-K⁺ ATPase (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: pepsin isozymes have optimal activity between pH 1 and 3 (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — regulation and pharmacologic inhibition of gastric acid secretion","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"},{"id":"sdl26_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the activation of pancreatic proteases, including enterokinase-mediated activation of trypsin and subsequent activation of other pancreatic zymogens.","batch":2,"stem":"A 2-month-old boy has failure to thrive, diarrhea, and generalized edema. Temperature is 36.8°C, and pulse is 148/min. Serum albumin is 2.1 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.5 g/dL). Sweat chloride is normal. Duodenal fluid has normal trypsinogen content, and adding a brush-border extract to the sample restores proteolysis. Which finding was most likely in the untreated duodenal fluid?","choices":{"A":"Low pancreatic trypsinogen secretion","B":"Low brush-border lactase activity","C":"Low pancreatic α-amylase activity","D":"Low trypsin and elastase activity","E":"Low gastric pepsin enzyme activity"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Congenital enterokinase deficiency leaves trypsinogen unactivated, and because trypsin activates the other zymogens, chymotrypsin, elastase, and carboxypeptidases stay inactive too. Trypsinogen secretion is normal here. Lactase, amylase, and pepsin do not depend on enterokinase. Normal sweat chloride argues against cystic fibrosis. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of proteins; Disorders of protein digestion and absorption). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypoproteinemic edema reflects low oncotic pressure from protein malabsorption (First Aid Cardiovascular, edema). COMLEX: absence of trypsin mimics loss of the pancreatic proteases (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 3 — pancreatic proteases","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the activation of pancreatic proteases, including enterokinase-mediated activation of trypsin and subsequent activation of other pancreatic zymogens.","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old man has severe epigastric pain radiating to the back after a weekend of heavy drinking. Temperature is 38.0°C, pulse is 116/min, and blood pressure is 104/66 mm Hg. Serum lipase is 1,450 U/L (reference range: 0–160 U/L). Premature activation of which zymogen inside the pancreas most directly starts the autodigestive cascade?","choices":{"A":"Chymotrypsinogen","B":"Proelastase","C":"Trypsinogen","D":"Procarboxypeptidase A","E":"Pepsinogen"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Trypsin activates itself and the other pancreatic zymogens, so intrapancreatic trypsinogen activation triggers the cascade of autodigestion. Chymotrypsinogen, proelastase, and procarboxypeptidase are downstream targets of trypsin. Pepsinogen is gastric. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Exocrine pancreas, autodigestion; Digestion of proteins). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: alcohol and gallstones are the leading causes of acute pancreatitis (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: lipase is more specific than amylase for acute pancreatitis (First Aid Gastrointestinal).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — acute and chronic pancreatitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the activation of pancreatic proteases, including enterokinase-mediated activation of trypsin and subsequent activation of other pancreatic zymogens.","batch":2,"stem":"A 15-year-old girl has had three episodes of acute pancreatitis without gallstones or alcohol use. Temperature is 37.4°C, and pulse is 96/min. Serum triglycerides are 110 mg/dL (reference range: <150 mg/dL). Genetic testing shows loss of function of a protein stored with digestive zymogens in acinar cells. Which protective mechanism is most likely lost?","choices":{"A":"Blocking of early active trypsin","B":"Enterokinase storage in acinar cells","C":"Bicarbonate secretion by duct cells","D":"Secretin release from duodenal cells","E":"Bile reflux into the pancreatic duct"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Inhibitors of pancreatic trypsin are copackaged in secretory granules to block trypsin that forms prematurely, so their loss permits autodigestion. Enterokinase is a brush-border enzyme, not an acinar protein. Duct bicarbonate and secretin regulate fluid output. Bile reflux is a trigger, not a protection. Normal triglycerides exclude a lipid cause. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Exocrine pancreas, why autodigestion does not occur). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 26 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SPINK1 and PRSS1 mutations cause hereditary pancreatitis (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: zymogen granules are impermeable to proteins, keeping enzymes out of the cytoplasm (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — acute and chronic pancreatitis","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"},{"id":"sdl26_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of amino acids, dipeptides, and tripeptides, including Na⁺- and H⁺-dependent cotransport.","batch":2,"stem":"A 12-year-old boy has a photosensitive scaly rash and episodes of ataxia. Pulse is 84/min, and blood pressure is 108/68 mm Hg. Urine shows increased neutral amino acids, and his growth is normal. Serum albumin is 4.3 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.5 g/dL). Which mechanism best explains his normal protein nutrition despite this defect?","choices":{"A":"Absorption on basolateral GLUT2 carriers","B":"Paracellular leak of free amino acids","C":"Pinocytosis of intact dietary protein","D":"Colonic absorption of amino acids","E":"Absorption as dipeptides and tripeptides"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Hartnup disease is a defect in the Na⁺-dependent neutral amino acid transporter, but most protein is absorbed as dipeptides and tripeptides on separate H⁺-dependent carriers, so nutrition is preserved; tryptophan loss causes the niacin-deficiency rash. GLUT2 carries monosaccharides. Paracellular leak and pinocytosis are not significant routes. The colon does not absorb amino acids in useful amounts. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of proteins); First Aid Biochemistry (Hartnup disease). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hartnup disease causes pellagra-like findings from tryptophan loss (First Aid Biochemistry). COMLEX: neutral, acidic, basic, and imino amino acids use separate Na⁺ cotransporters (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 5 — amino acid cotransporters","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of amino acids, dipeptides, and tripeptides, including Na⁺- and H⁺-dependent cotransport.","batch":2,"stem":"In a study of jejunal biopsies from a 41-year-old male volunteer, apical Na⁺-H⁺ exchange is blocked with a selective inhibitor. The bath is kept at 37°C, and the dipeptide glycylglycine is added to the luminal side. Uptake of the dipeptide falls sharply, while brush-border peptidase activity is unchanged. What best explains the fall in uptake?","choices":{"A":"Loss of the luminal sodium gradient","B":"Inhibition of brush-border peptidases","C":"Block of basolateral amino acid exit","D":"Loss of the luminal proton gradient","E":"Reduced pancreatic protease release"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Dipeptide cotransporters use an H⁺ gradient that the apical Na⁺-H⁺ exchanger creates, so blocking the exchanger dissipates the driving force. The sodium gradient drives free amino acid uptake, not peptide uptake. Peptidase activity is unchanged by design. Basolateral exit does not set apical uptake. Biopsies lack pancreatic input. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of proteins). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the apical peptide transporter uses H⁺ cotransport (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: free amino acid uptake mirrors glucose uptake by Na⁺ cotransport (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 2 — apical Na⁺-H⁺ exchange","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of amino acids, dipeptides, and tripeptides, including Na⁺- and H⁺-dependent cotransport.","batch":2,"stem":"A 22-year-old man has recurrent flank pain and passes hexagonal urinary crystals. Temperature is 36.9°C, and blood pressure is 120/78 mm Hg. Serum creatinine is 0.9 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). His defective transporter also fails to absorb cystine, lysine, and arginine in the intestine. Which transport mechanism normally moves these amino acids into enterocytes?","choices":{"A":"Proton-dependent cotransport","B":"Primary active ATP pumping","C":"Sodium-dependent cotransport","D":"Receptor-mediated endocytosis","E":"Paracellular solvent drag"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cystinuria is absence of the Na⁺-amino acid cotransporter for dibasic amino acids in intestine and kidney. Free L-amino acids enter enterocytes by Na⁺-dependent cotransport, like glucose. Proton cotransport carries dipeptides and tripeptides. ATP pumping, endocytosis, and solvent drag are not the described routes. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of proteins; Disorders of protein digestion and absorption). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cystinuria causes hexagonal cystine stones with a positive cyanide-nitroprusside test (First Aid Renal). COMLEX: unabsorbed dibasic amino acids are excreted in feces (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 1 — Na⁺-coupled solute absorption by enterocytes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl26_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe carbohydrate digestion by pancreatic amylase and intestinal brush-border enzymes, and explain the physiologic consequences of lactase deficiency.","batch":3,"stem":"A 6-year-old boy has bloating and watery diarrhea after drinking juice with added cane sugar, but he tolerates milk. Deficiency of which brush-border enzyme is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Lactase","B":"Trehalase","C":"Amylase","D":"Lipase","E":"Sucrase"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Sucrase splits sucrose into glucose and fructose; without it, sucrose stays in the lumen and causes osmotic diarrhea, while lactose is still split by lactase. Lactase deficiency would cause symptoms with milk. Trehalase splits trehalose from mushrooms. Amylase acts on starch, and lipase on fat. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of carbohydrates; Disorders of carbohydrate digestion and absorption). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: congenital sucrase-isomaltase deficiency presents when sucrose enters the diet (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption). COMLEX: sucrase also contributes to digestion of starch oligosaccharides to glucose (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 1 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl26_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the intestinal absorption of glucose, galactose, and fructose, including the roles of SGLT1, GLUT5, GLUT2, and the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase.","batch":3,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman has a normal oral glucose tolerance test. Which transporter carries absorbed glucose out of her enterocytes into the blood?","choices":{"A":"GLUT5","B":"GLUT2","C":"SGLT1","D":"GLUT4","E":"SGLT2"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Glucose and galactose leave the enterocyte across the basolateral membrane by facilitated diffusion on GLUT2, which also carries fructose. GLUT5 is the apical fructose carrier. SGLT1 is the apical sodium-coupled entry step. GLUT4 is insulin regulated in muscle and fat. SGLT2 is a renal proximal tubule carrier. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of carbohydrates, Fig. 8.28). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: SGLT2 inhibitors lower plasma glucose by blocking renal glucose reabsorption (First Aid Endocrine). COMLEX: basolateral exit by facilitated diffusion follows the glucose gradient out of the cell (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 2 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl26_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe protein digestion by gastric, pancreatic, and intestinal brush-border proteases.","batch":3,"stem":"A 19-year-old woman with cystic fibrosis takes pancreatic enzyme capsules with meals. Which protease in the capsules digests the elastin fibers of meat?","choices":{"A":"Elastase","B":"Lipase","C":"Amylase","D":"Pepsin","E":"Colipase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Elastase is a pancreatic endopeptidase that hydrolyzes elastin fibers, and it is activated from proelastase by trypsin. Lipase, amylase, and colipase digest fat and starch or assist lipase. Pepsin is gastric, not a pancreatic product. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of proteins); Guyton Ch. 66 (elastase and meat fibers). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cystic fibrosis causes exocrine pancreatic insufficiency from thick secretions in pancreatic ducts (First Aid Respiratory, cystic fibrosis). COMLEX: endopeptidases cleave interior peptide bonds, unlike exopeptidases (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 3 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl26_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the activation of pancreatic proteases, including enterokinase-mediated activation of trypsin and subsequent activation of other pancreatic zymogens.","batch":3,"stem":"A 53-year-old woman asks why her pancreas does not digest itself. Why are pancreatic proteases secreted as inactive precursors?","choices":{"A":"To speed gastric protein digestion","B":"To allow absorption as intact peptides","C":"To resist bile salt denaturation","D":"To prevent pancreatic autodigestion","E":"To permit storage in pancreatic duct cells"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Pancreatic peptidases are stored in zymogen granules as inactive precursors and are activated in the intestinal lumen, which prevents autodigestion of the pancreas. They act in the duodenum, not the stomach. Zymogen form does not change absorption or bile salt resistance. Duct cells secrete bicarbonate, and zymogens are stored in acinar cells. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Exocrine pancreas, why autodigestion does not occur). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: premature intrapancreatic zymogen activation underlies acute pancreatitis (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: trypsin inhibitors in acinar granules add a second safeguard (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 4 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl26_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of amino acids, dipeptides, and tripeptides, including Na⁺- and H⁺-dependent cotransport.","batch":3,"stem":"A 28-year-old man eats a high-protein meal. By which mechanism do free amino acids leave his enterocytes into the blood?","choices":{"A":"Facilitated diffusion","B":"Proton cotransport","C":"Sodium cotransport","D":"Primary active pumping","E":"Vesicular exocytosis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Free amino acids cross the basolateral membrane into portal blood by facilitated diffusion; the four separate Na⁺-coupled carriers for neutral, acidic, basic, and imino amino acids are the apical entry step. Proton cotransport is the apical route for dipeptides and tripeptides. Sodium cotransport is the apical entry route for free amino acids. Primary active pumping and exocytosis are not described. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of proteins, Fig. 8.31). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: free amino acid entry mirrors glucose entry, with sodium cotransport at the apical membrane (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: absorbed amino acids reach the liver through the portal vein (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 5 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl26_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe carbohydrate digestion by pancreatic amylase and intestinal brush-border enzymes, and explain the physiologic consequences of lactase deficiency.","batch":3,"stem":"A 37-year-old woman chews bread slowly before swallowing. Why does salivary amylase contribute little to her overall starch digestion?","choices":{"A":"Absence of starch substrate","B":"Rapid absorption by the mouth","C":"Inactivation by gastric acid","D":"Inhibition by bile salts","E":"Need for enterokinase activation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Salivary amylase starts starch digestion in the mouth, but it plays little overall role because the low pH of gastric contents inactivates it; pancreatic amylase does most of the work. Bread contains starch. The oral mucosa does not absorb the enzyme. Bile salts and enterokinase do not regulate salivary amylase. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of carbohydrates). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 26 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic amylase secretion is stimulated by CCK and vagal input (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: salivary amylase activity continues inside the food bolus until gastric acid mixes in (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 1 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"},{"id":"grid_sdl26_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe carbohydrate digestion by pancreatic amylase and intestinal brush-border enzymes, and explain the physiologic consequences of lactase deficiency.","batch":4,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman had a total pancreatectomy for an intraductal tumor 6 months ago and has stopped taking her enzyme capsules. She has bulky stools and weight loss. Hemoglobin A1c is 7.9% (reference range: 4.0–5.6%). Which of the following sets of carbohydrate digestion findings is most likely in this patient?","grid":{"headers":["Lactose digestion","Sucrose digestion","Starch digestion"]},"choices":{"A":["↔","↔","↓"],"B":["↓","↔","↔"],"C":["↔","↓","↔"],"D":["↓","↓","↓"],"E":["↔","↔","↔"]},"correct":"A","explanation":"Choice A (↔ lactose digestion, ↔ sucrose digestion, ↓ starch digestion) is correct. Without the pancreas there is no pancreatic α-amylase to split starch into maltose, maltotriose, and α-limit dextrins, so starch digestion falls; salivary amylase is inactivated by gastric acid and gives little help. Lactose and sucrose are already disaccharides and are split by brush-border lactase and sucrase, which are intact. Choice B (↓ lactose digestion, ↔ sucrose digestion, ↔ starch digestion) is lactase deficiency. Choice C (↔ lactose digestion, ↓ sucrose digestion, ↔ starch digestion) is sucrase-isomaltase deficiency, in which sucrose goes undigested while pancreatic amylase and maltase still handle most starch. Choice D (↓ lactose digestion, ↓ sucrose digestion, ↓ starch digestion) is diffuse villous injury, as in celiac disease, which removes brush-border enzymes. Choice E (↔ lactose digestion, ↔ sucrose digestion, ↔ starch digestion) is normal digestion, as with adequate enzyme replacement. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of carbohydrates, Fig. 8.27; Disorders of carbohydrate digestion and absorption); First Aid Gastrointestinal (malabsorption syndromes). The ↔ cells, including starch in sucrase-isomaltase deficiency, are inferred because the sources do not grade them. Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diabetes after total pancreatectomy reflects loss of islet insulin and glucagon (First Aid Endocrine). COMLEX: brush-border α-dextrinase, maltase, and sucrase finish starch digestion to glucose (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — secretion and functions of pancreatic digestive enzymes","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":27,"title":"SDL 27 — Physiology - Digestion & Absorption of the Small Intestine Part 2 (Lipids, Vitamins)","questions":[{"id":"sdl27_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the digestion and emulsification of dietary lipids, including the roles of bile salts, lecithin, pancreatic lipase, colipase, and micelle formation.","batch":1,"stem":"Pancreatic lipase hydrolyzes each triglyceride molecule into which products?","choices":{"A":"One monoglyceride and two fatty acids","B":"Glycerol and three free fatty acids","C":"Two monoglycerides and one fatty acid","D":"Cholesterol and two free fatty acids","E":"Lysolecithin and one free fatty acid"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Pancreatic lipase, secreted in active form, splits a triglyceride into one monoglyceride and two fatty acids. Pancreatic lipase does not release free glycerol, and two monoglycerides would need a second glycerol backbone. Cholesterol ester hydrolase splits cholesterol esters into free cholesterol and fatty acids, and lysolecithin comes from phospholipids. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids; Absorption of lipids). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: lingual and gastric lipases hydrolyze about 10 percent of ingested triglyceride before the chyme reaches the duodenum (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the optimum pH for pancreatic lipase is about 6, so duodenal acid must be neutralized by bicarbonate (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — pancreatic digestive enzymes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the digestion and emulsification of dietary lipids, including the roles of bile salts, lecithin, pancreatic lipase, colipase, and micelle formation.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the main function of colipase in lipid digestion?","choices":{"A":"Emulsifying lipid droplets in the gastric lumen","B":"Activating trypsinogen in the duodenal lumen","C":"Displacing bile salts from lipase","D":"Hydrolyzing cholesterol esters to free cholesterol","E":"Packaging lipids into chylomicrons in enterocytes"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Bile salts displace pancreatic lipase from emulsified droplets. Colipase, activated from procolipase by trypsin, displaces the bile salts and binds lipase so digestion can proceed. Dietary proteins emulsify fat in the stomach, enterokinase activates trypsinogen, cholesterol ester hydrolase acts on cholesterol esters, and enterocytes assemble chylomicrons. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids; Absorption of lipids). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic insufficiency causes malabsorption of fat and fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E, and K (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption syndromes). COMLEX: CCK released by fat in the duodenum slows gastric emptying and gives pancreatic enzymes time to act (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — pancreatic digestive enzymes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the digestion and emulsification of dietary lipids, including the roles of bile salts, lecithin, pancreatic lipase, colipase, and micelle formation.","batch":1,"stem":"Pancreatic phospholipase A2 hydrolyzes dietary phospholipids into which products?","choices":{"A":"Glycerol and fatty acids","B":"Cholesterol and fatty acids","C":"Monoglyceride and fatty acids","D":"Lysolecithin and fatty acids","E":"Bile acids and free glycine"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Phospholipase A2, secreted as a proenzyme and activated by trypsin, converts phospholipids to lysolecithin and fatty acids. Lysolecithin then joins bile salts in emulsifying fat. The other pairs come from triglyceride or cholesterol ester hydrolysis, and bile acids with glycine come from deconjugation. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids; Absorption of lipids). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bile salts are amphipathic, so the hydrophilic side faces water and the hydrophobic side faces the lipid core (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the end products of lipid digestion, other than glycerol, are insoluble in water and must be carried in micelles (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — pancreatic digestive enzymes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the absorption and intracellular processing of lipids, including uptake from micelles, re-esterification, chylomicron formation, and transport through the lymphatic system.","batch":1,"stem":"In which organelle do enterocytes re-esterify absorbed lipid digestion products?","choices":{"A":"Rough endoplasmic reticulum","B":"Golgi secretory apparatus","C":"Apical brush-border membrane","D":"Lysosomal digestive compartment","E":"Smooth endoplasmic reticulum"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Inside the enterocyte, monoglycerides, fatty acids, cholesterol, and lysolecithin are re-esterified on the smooth endoplasmic reticulum to triglycerides, cholesterol esters, and phospholipids. The Golgi packages chylomicrons into secretory vesicles afterward. Lipids cross the brush border by diffusion, and lysosomes digest heme, not lipids. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids; Absorption of lipids). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chylomicrons carry dietary triglyceride and are cleared by lipoprotein lipase in peripheral capillaries (Guyton Ch. 66). COMLEX: lipid crosses the apical membrane by diffusion, so absorption depends on mucosal surface area (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte apical and basolateral membranes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the absorption and intracellular processing of lipids, including uptake from micelles, re-esterification, chylomicron formation, and transport through the lymphatic system.","batch":1,"stem":"Chylomicrons leave the intestinal villus by entering which vessels?","choices":{"A":"Portal venules","B":"Villus blood capillaries","C":"Hepatic sinusoids","D":"Lymphatic lacteals","E":"Mesenteric arterioles"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Chylomicrons are about 1000 Å across, too large to enter vascular capillaries, so they move between endothelial cells into lacteals and reach the blood through the thoracic duct. Portal venules and villus capillaries carry water-soluble nutrients. Hepatic sinusoids and arterioles are not the exit route. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids; Absorption of lipids). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: injury to the thoracic duct leaks chylomicron-rich lymph, giving milky fluid high in triglyceride (Guyton Ch. 66). COMLEX: glycerol is water soluble and does not need micelles for absorption (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte apical and basolateral membranes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the absorption and intracellular processing of lipids, including uptake from micelles, re-esterification, chylomicron formation, and transport through the lymphatic system.","batch":1,"stem":"What happens to the bile salts of a micelle when its lipids are absorbed at the brush border?","choices":{"A":"They enter the enterocyte with the lipids","B":"They are packaged into chylomicrons","C":"They remain in the intestinal lumen","D":"They are re-esterified in the cytosol","E":"They are degraded by brush-border enzymes"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Micelles release lipids at the apical membrane, but the micelles themselves do not enter the cell. The bile salts stay in the lumen and are absorbed downstream in the ileum. They are not part of chylomicrons, not re-esterified, and not degraded at the brush border. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids; Absorption of lipids). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: mixed micelles are disks about 50 Å across with bile salts lining the outside (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: bile salt reuse through the enterohepatic circulation keeps the pool large without heavy hepatic synthesis (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte apical and basolateral membranes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and predict the consequences of terminal ileal disease or resection, including bile salt loss, steatorrhea, and bile acid–induced secretory diarrhea.","batch":1,"stem":"In which intestinal segment are conjugated bile salts actively reabsorbed?","choices":{"A":"Duodenum","B":"Proximal jejunum","C":"Ascending colon","D":"Gastric antrum","E":"Terminal ileum"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Conjugated bile salts are reabsorbed in the ileum and returned to the liver in portal blood, the enterohepatic circulation. About 95 percent of secreted bile acids return this way. The duodenum and jejunum are where bile salts work on lipids. The colon and stomach do not reabsorb them. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Bile; Disorders of lipid digestion and absorption). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: 7α-hydroxylase is the rate-limiting enzyme of bile acid synthesis (First Aid Gastrointestinal, bile). COMLEX: Crohn disease of the terminal ileum can interrupt bile salt reabsorption and cause fat malabsorption (First Aid Gastrointestinal, bile).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and predict the consequences of terminal ileal disease or resection, including bile salt loss, steatorrhea, and bile acid–induced secretory diarrhea.","batch":1,"stem":"Why does surgical removal of the intestinal segment that reabsorbs bile salts shrink the total bile salt pool?","choices":{"A":"Hepatic synthesis stops after resection","B":"Hepatic synthesis lags behind fecal loss","C":"Bile salts are deconjugated in the bile","D":"Gallbladder storage is lost to the colon","E":"Micelles enter the ileal wall intact"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Resecting the reabsorptive segment interrupts the enterohepatic circulation, so bile salts go out in feces. The liver increases synthesis, but synthesis falls short of the fecal loss and the pool shrinks. Synthesis rises rather than stopping. Deconjugation is a bacterial overgrowth mechanism. Gallbladder storage is unaffected, and micelles do not enter cells. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Disorders of lipid digestion and absorption). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ileal bile salt malabsorption from short bowel or Crohn disease can cause fat malabsorption and bile acid diarrhea (First Aid Gastrointestinal, bile). COMLEX: a smaller bile salt pool limits micelle formation and causes steatorrhea (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and predict the consequences of terminal ileal disease or resection, including bile salt loss, steatorrhea, and bile acid–induced secretory diarrhea.","batch":1,"stem":"By which intestinal level are most ingested lipids absorbed?","choices":{"A":"Mid-jejunum","B":"Terminal ileum","C":"Gastric antrum","D":"Proximal colon","E":"Rectosigmoid"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Most dietary lipid is absorbed by the mid-jejunum, so the work of bile salts is done long before they reach the terminal ileum for reabsorption. The ileum recycles bile salts rather than absorbing most fat, and the stomach and colon absorb little lipid. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids; Absorption of lipids). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 27 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: short bowel syndrome follows extensive small bowel resection and causes malabsorption (First Aid Gastrointestinal, short bowel syndrome). COMLEX: bile salts left in the lumen after lipid uptake travel on to the ileum (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"},{"id":"sdl27_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain vitamin B₁₂ absorption, including the roles of intrinsic factor and the terminal ileum, and predict the consequences of impaired absorption in pernicious anemia and terminal ileal disease or resection.","batch":1,"stem":"Which cells secrete intrinsic factor?","choices":{"A":"Gastric chief cells","B":"Pancreatic acinar cells","C":"Gastric parietal cells","D":"Salivary gland cells","E":"Duodenal S cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Intrinsic factor is a glycoprotein secreted by gastric parietal cells, the same cells that secrete H+. Chief cells secrete pepsinogen, acinar cells secrete pancreatic enzymes, salivary glands secrete R proteins, and S cells secrete secretin. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Water-soluble vitamins). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: autoimmune destruction of parietal cells causes chronic gastritis and pernicious anemia (First Aid Gastrointestinal, secretory products). COMLEX: after gastrectomy, oral B12 is not absorbed and B12 must be given by injection (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 2 — parietal cells and intrinsic factor","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain vitamin B₁₂ absorption, including the roles of intrinsic factor and the terminal ileum, and predict the consequences of impaired absorption in pernicious anemia and terminal ileal disease or resection.","batch":1,"stem":"Free vitamin B12 released in the stomach first binds to which carrier?","choices":{"A":"Intrinsic factor","B":"R protein","C":"Transferrin","D":"Apoferritin","E":"Calbindin"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Free B12 binds R proteins from salivary secretions. In the duodenum, pancreatic proteases degrade R proteins and B12 moves to intrinsic factor. Transferrin and apoferritin carry iron, and calbindin binds calcium. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Water-soluble vitamins). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic insufficiency can impair B12 absorption as well as fat absorption (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption syndromes). COMLEX: most water-soluble vitamins use sodium-dependent cotransport, unlike B12 (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 2 — parietal cells and intrinsic factor","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain vitamin B₁₂ absorption, including the roles of intrinsic factor and the terminal ileum, and predict the consequences of impaired absorption in pernicious anemia and terminal ileal disease or resection.","batch":1,"stem":"What event in the duodenum allows vitamin B12 to bind intrinsic factor?","choices":{"A":"Pancreatic proteases","B":"Gastric hydrochloric acid","C":"Conjugated bile salts","D":"Brush-border lactase","E":"Salivary amylase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In the duodenum, pancreatic proteases degrade R proteins, so B12 transfers to intrinsic factor. The B12-intrinsic factor complex resists these proteases and travels to the ileum. Acid and pepsin act earlier in the stomach, and bile salts, lactase, and amylase do not free B12. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Water-soluble vitamins). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: B12 deficiency causes megaloblastic anemia with neurologic findings (Guyton Ch. 72). COMLEX: intrinsic factor is needed for B12 uptake in the terminal ileum (First Aid Gastrointestinal, secretory products).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 2 — parietal cells and intrinsic factor","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium, including the major factors required for their normal absorption.","batch":1,"stem":"Active vitamin D increases intestinal calcium absorption mainly by inducing which protein?","choices":{"A":"Apoferritin storage protein","B":"Transferrin carrier","C":"Pancreatic colipase","D":"Calbindin D-28K","E":"Intrinsic factor"},"correct":"D","explanation":"1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol induces the vitamin D-dependent calcium-binding protein calbindin D-28K in enterocytes, which promotes calcium absorption. Apoferritin and transferrin handle iron, colipase helps lipase, and intrinsic factor binds B12. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Vitamins; Calcium; Iron). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: inadequate calcium absorption causes rickets in children and osteomalacia in adults (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: 25-hydroxycholecalciferol is the main circulating but inactive form of vitamin D (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte transport","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium, including the major factors required for their normal absorption.","batch":1,"stem":"How do absorbed vitamins A, D, E, and K leave the enterocyte?","choices":{"A":"Bound to albumin in portal blood","B":"By sodium cotransport into blood","C":"In micelles across the basal membrane","D":"Bound to transferrin in portal blood","E":"In chylomicrons into lymph"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Fat-soluble vitamins are handled like dietary lipids. They travel in micelles, diffuse into the cell, are incorporated into chylomicrons, and are extruded into lymph. Sodium cotransport handles most water-soluble vitamins, micelles do not enter the cell, and transferrin carries iron. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Vitamins; Calcium; Iron). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: orlistat inhibits gastric and pancreatic lipase and decreases absorption of fat-soluble vitamins (First Aid Gastrointestinal, orlistat). COMLEX: vitamin K deficiency prolongs the prothrombin time (Guyton Ch. 72).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte transport","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium, including the major factors required for their normal absorption.","batch":1,"stem":"How is heme iron converted to free iron inside the enterocyte?","choices":{"A":"Digestion by pancreatic proteases","B":"Digestion by lysosomal enzymes","C":"Hydrolysis by brush-border peptidases","D":"Cleavage by gastric pepsin","E":"Release by bile salt micelles"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Heme iron crosses the apical membrane intact. Inside the enterocyte, lysosomal enzymes digest heme and release free iron, which binds apoferritin and then crosses the basolateral membrane. The other enzymes act in the lumen, and micelles carry lipids. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Vitamins; Calcium; Iron). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hereditary hemochromatosis increases intestinal iron absorption through low hepcidin (First Aid Hematology, hemochromatosis). COMLEX: transferrin carries absorbed iron to the liver and bone marrow (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte transport","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the digestion and emulsification of dietary lipids, including the roles of bile salts, lecithin, pancreatic lipase, colipase, and micelle formation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman starts a weight-loss drug that blocks gastric and pancreatic lipase. One month later she has oily, foul-smelling stools. Temperature is 36.9°C, and blood pressure is 124/78 mm Hg. Stool fat is 14 g/day (reference range: <7 g/day). Which digestive step is most directly impaired?","choices":{"A":"Hydrolysis of dietary triglyceride","B":"Exocytosis of chylomicrons into lymph","C":"Synthesis of apolipoprotein B in enterocytes","D":"Reabsorption of bile salts in the ileum","E":"Activation of procolipase by trypsin enzyme"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Blocking lipase leaves triglycerides unhydrolyzed. Undigested triglycerides are not absorbable and appear in the stool. Chylomicron export and apoprotein synthesis follow digestion. Ileal bile salt reabsorption and procolipase activation are not drug targets here. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Disorders of lipid digestion and absorption); First Aid Gastrointestinal (orlistat). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: orlistat causes steatorrhea and decreased absorption of fat-soluble vitamins (First Aid Gastrointestinal, orlistat). COMLEX: lingual and gastric lipases normally digest only about 10 percent of triglyceride (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — pancreatic digestive enzymes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the digestion and emulsification of dietary lipids, including the roles of bile salts, lecithin, pancreatic lipase, colipase, and micelle formation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man has recurrent duodenal ulcers, diarrhea, and greasy stools. Fasting gastrin is 1200 pg/mL (reference range: <100 pg/mL), and duodenal aspirate pH is 3 (reference range: 6–7). Exocrine enzyme output is normal. Which mechanism best explains his fat malabsorption?","choices":{"A":"Loss of hepatic bile salt secretion","B":"Defective chylomicron assembly in enterocytes","C":"Excess colipase release from acini","D":"Reduced ileal absorptive surface","E":"Acid inactivation of pancreatic lipase"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A gastrin-secreting tumor drives excess H+ secretion that overwhelms pancreatic bicarbonate. Duodenal pH stays far below the lipase optimum of about 6, so pancreatic lipase is inactivated. Bile secretion, chylomicron assembly, and ileal surface are intact, and colipase helps lipase rather than causing malabsorption. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Disorders of lipid digestion and absorption). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 27 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Zollinger-Ellison syndrome is a gastrin-secreting tumor, often of the pancreas or duodenum (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: a proton pump inhibitor reduces the H+ load delivered to the duodenum (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — Zollinger-Ellison syndrome and gastrin","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"},{"id":"sdl27_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the digestion and emulsification of dietary lipids, including the roles of bile salts, lecithin, pancreatic lipase, colipase, and micelle formation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man has jaundice, dark urine, and pale bulky stools. Imaging shows a mass blocking the common bile duct. Total bilirubin is 9.2 mg/dL (reference range: 0.1–1.2 mg/dL). Pancreatic enzyme secretion is normal. Which mechanism best explains his steatorrhea?","choices":{"A":"Loss of pancreatic lipase activity in the duodenal lumen","B":"Failure of trypsin to activate procolipase","C":"Failure of emulsification and micelle formation","D":"Defective apoprotein synthesis in enterocytes","E":"Obstruction of intestinal lymphatic drainage"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Bile salts emulsify fat and form the mixed micelles that carry lipid products to the brush border. When bile fails to reach the duodenum, lipid digestion and micellar transport fail. Pancreatic lipase, colipase activation, apoprotein synthesis, and lymph flow are intact. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids; Absorption of lipids); Costanzo Ch. 8 (Disorders of lipid digestion and absorption). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pale stools and dark urine point to obstructed bile flow with conjugated hyperbilirubinemia (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: bile salts are amphipathic, which lets them line micelles in the aqueous lumen (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — bile formation and micelles","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the absorption and intracellular processing of lipids, including uptake from micelles, re-esterification, chylomicron formation, and transport through the lymphatic system.","batch":2,"stem":"A 7-month-old boy has failure to thrive and pale, foul-smelling stools. Blood smear shows acanthocytes. Fasting triglyceride is 8 mg/dL (reference range: 30–150 mg/dL), and duodenal biopsy shows enterocytes filled with lipid droplets. Which defect best explains his findings?","choices":{"A":"Failure of micelle formation","B":"Inactive pancreatic lipase","C":"Loss of ileal bile salt reabsorption","D":"Failure of chylomicron assembly","E":"Deficient brush-border lactase"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Failure to synthesize Apo B causes abetalipoproteinemia. Chylomicrons do not form or are not exported to lymph, so lipid builds up in enterocytes and plasma lipid stays low. Lipid filling the cells shows that micelle formation, lipase digestion, and uptake into the cell are working. Bile salt uptake and lactase do not explain lipid-filled enterocytes. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids; Absorption of lipids); Costanzo Ch. 8 (Disorders of lipid digestion and absorption). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: abetalipoproteinemia presents with acanthocytes, steatorrhea, and fat-soluble vitamin deficiency (Guyton Ch. 66). COMLEX: apoproteins cover about 20 percent of the chylomicron surface (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 5 — abetalipoproteinemia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the absorption and intracellular processing of lipids, including uptake from micelles, re-esterification, chylomicron formation, and transport through the lymphatic system.","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old man develops a milky right pleural effusion 5 days after esophagectomy. Pleural fluid triglyceride is 420 mg/dL (reference range: <110 mg/dL). He is placed on a diet in which medium-chain triglycerides replace long-chain fats. Why does this diet reduce the leak?","choices":{"A":"Medium-chain fats enter portal blood directly","B":"Medium-chain fats bypass bile salt emulsification","C":"Medium-chain fats increase lacteal reabsorption","D":"Medium-chain fats block chylomicron uptake by tissues","E":"Medium-chain fats seal the thoracic duct wall"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Long-chain fats are re-esterified and packaged in chylomicrons, which travel in lacteals to the thoracic duct. Small amounts of short- and medium-chain fatty acids are more water soluble and diffuse straight into portal blood, so less lipid enters the injured duct. The other options are not mechanisms of this diet. Bypassing bile salt emulsification is not why the leak falls; medium-chain fats diffuse into portal capillaries rather than entering lymph. Medium-chain fats do not increase lacteal reabsorption; they avoid lacteals altogether by diffusing across the basolateral membrane into portal capillaries. Medium-chain fats do not block chylomicron uptake; they simply bypass re-esterification and chylomicron packaging and enter portal blood. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids; Absorption of lipids); Guyton Ch. 66 (Direct absorption of fatty acids into the portal blood). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the thoracic duct carries intestinal lymph and chylomicrons into the left subclavian vein (Guyton Ch. 66). COMLEX: chylomicrons are too large to enter vascular capillaries (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 2 — villus lacteals","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Bypassing bile salt emulsification is not why the leak falls","C":"Medium-chain fats do not increase lacteal reabsorption; they","D":"Medium-chain fats do not block chylomicron uptake; they simp"}},{"id":"sdl27_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the absorption and intracellular processing of lipids, including uptake from micelles, re-esterification, chylomicron formation, and transport through the lymphatic system.","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman has bloating, weight loss, and greasy stools. Tissue transglutaminase IgA is positive, and duodenal biopsy shows villous atrophy. Hemoglobin is 10.4 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–15.5 g/dL). Pancreatic function is normal. Which mechanism best explains her impaired fat absorption?","choices":{"A":"Absent pancreatic lipase secretion into the duodenum","B":"Reduced mucosal surface for lipid diffusion","C":"Deficient apoprotein B synthesis in enterocytes","D":"Loss of bile salt conjugation","E":"Blocked lacteal drainage by tumor"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Lipid products leave micelles and cross the apical membrane by diffusion, which depends on surface area. Villous loss reduces that area and impairs fat absorption. Lipase secretion is normal, apoprotein synthesis is not the lesion, bile salt conjugation is hepatic, and no lymphatic tumor is present. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Disorders of lipid digestion and absorption). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: celiac disease mainly affects the distal duodenum and proximal jejunum and is linked to iron deficiency anemia (First Aid Gastrointestinal, celiac disease). COMLEX: celiac disease gives an abnormal D-xylose test, unlike pancreatic insufficiency (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption syndromes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 2 — villus structure and brush border","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and predict the consequences of terminal ileal disease or resection, including bile salt loss, steatorrhea, and bile acid–induced secretory diarrhea.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman with Crohn disease had 100 cm of terminal ileum resected. She now has steatorrhea and flank pain, and imaging shows a renal stone. Stone analysis shows a calcium stone. Serum calcium is 9.1 mg/dL (reference range: 8.5–10.2 mg/dL). Which mechanism best explains her kidney stone?","choices":{"A":"Increased colonic oxalate absorption","B":"Increased hepatic bile acid synthesis","C":"Decreased renal calcium filtration","D":"Increased intestinal calcium uptake","E":"Decreased colonic bacterial overgrowth"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Lost bile salts shrink the pool, so fat is malabsorbed. Unabsorbed fatty acids bind luminal calcium, leaving oxalate free to be absorbed in the colon and excreted in urine as calcium oxalate stones. Increased hepatic synthesis falls short but does not form stones. Calcium uptake falls rather than rises. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Disorders of lipid digestion and absorption); First Aid Gastrointestinal (bile). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 27 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: fat malabsorption from ileal bile salt loss causes calcium to bind fat, so oxalate is absorbed and forms calcium oxalate stones (First Aid Gastrointestinal, bile). COMLEX: about 95 percent of secreted bile acids normally return to the liver (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"},{"id":"sdl27_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and predict the consequences of terminal ileal disease or resection, including bile salt loss, steatorrhea, and bile acid–induced secretory diarrhea.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man had 30 cm of terminal ileum removed after a penetrating injury. He now has watery diarrhea several times daily without weight loss, and it improves with cholestyramine. Stool fat is 6 g/day (reference range: <7 g/day). Which mechanism best explains his diarrhea?","choices":{"A":"Undigested lactose holding water in the lumen","B":"Gastrin-driven acid overload of the duodenum","C":"Bile acids stimulating colonic secretion","D":"Bacterial deconjugation in the jejunum","E":"Loss of colonic surface for water uptake"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A short ileal resection lets bile acids spill into the colon, where they stimulate secretion and cause watery diarrhea. The liver still makes enough bile acid to keep micelles adequate, so stool fat stays near normal. A binding resin removes the irritant. Lactose, gastrin, deconjugation, and colonic loss are not the mechanism here. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Disorders of lipid digestion and absorption); First Aid Gastrointestinal (bile). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ileal bile salt malabsorption can cause bile acid diarrhea as well as fat malabsorption (First Aid Gastrointestinal, bile). COMLEX: longer ileal resections deplete the pool faster than the liver can replace it, adding steatorrhea (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 4 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and predict the consequences of terminal ileal disease or resection, including bile salt loss, steatorrhea, and bile acid–induced secretory diarrhea.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman with a surgical blind loop of small bowel has bloating, weight loss, and greasy stools. A hydrogen breath test is positive soon after a glucose load. Vitamin B12 is 160 pg/mL (reference range: 200–900 pg/mL). Which mechanism best explains her fat malabsorption?","choices":{"A":"Acid inactivation of pancreatic lipase","B":"Loss of the ileal bile salt carrier","C":"Failure of chylomicron export to lymph","D":"Bacterial deconjugation of bile salts","E":"Autoimmune loss of intrinsic factor"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Bacteria remove glycine and taurine from bile salts, making nonionized bile acids that are absorbed too early, before micelles finish their work. The pool reaching the lumen falls and fat absorption fails. Acid inactivation follows gastrinoma, carrier loss follows ileal disease, chylomicron failure is abetalipoproteinemia, and intrinsic factor loss does not cause steatorrhea. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Disorders of lipid digestion and absorption). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 27 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: bacterial overgrowth can cause B12 deficiency because bacteria consume the vitamin (Guyton Ch. 72). COMLEX: low luminal pH also promotes early bile acid absorption by converting bile acids to the nonionized form (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"},{"id":"sdl27_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain vitamin B₁₂ absorption, including the roles of intrinsic factor and the terminal ileum, and predict the consequences of impaired absorption in pernicious anemia and terminal ileal disease or resection.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman has fatigue and numbness in both feet. Hemoglobin is 9.1 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–15.5 g/dL), and mean corpuscular volume is 112 fL (reference range: 80–100 fL). Antibodies to parietal cells are positive. Which route of vitamin B12 replacement is most reliable for her?","choices":{"A":"Oral tablets taken with food","B":"Oral tablets with antacid","C":"Sublingual tablets at bedtime","D":"Oral liquid with pancreatic enzymes","E":"Intramuscular injection"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Autoimmune loss of parietal cells removes intrinsic factor, so dietary or oral B12 is not absorbed in the ileum. Costanzo states that B12 must be given by injection in this setting. Antacids and pancreatic enzymes do not replace intrinsic factor. Sublingual tablets still deliver B12 by a non-injected route, and without intrinsic factor parenteral intramuscular B12 is required to bypass the intestinal pathway. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Water-soluble vitamins). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pernicious anemia reflects autoimmune loss of intrinsic factor from parietal cells (First Aid Gastrointestinal, secretory products). COMLEX: B12 deficiency also causes neurologic findings, which folate does not correct (Guyton Ch. 72).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — parietal cells and intrinsic factor","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Sublingual tablets still deliver B12 by a non-injected route"}},{"id":"sdl27_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain vitamin B₁₂ absorption, including the roles of intrinsic factor and the terminal ileum, and predict the consequences of impaired absorption in pernicious anemia and terminal ileal disease or resection.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man with Crohn disease had 90 cm of terminal ileum resected 4 years ago. He now has fatigue and paresthesias. Mean corpuscular volume is 114 fL (reference range: 80–100 fL), and serum B12 is 110 pg/mL (reference range: 200–900 pg/mL). Gastric biopsy is normal. Which step of B12 absorption is lost?","choices":{"A":"Release of B12 from food by pepsin","B":"Ileal uptake of the B12-intrinsic factor complex","C":"Binding of free B12 to R proteins","D":"Gastric secretion of intrinsic factor by parietal cells","E":"Protease cleavage of R proteins in the duodenum"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The B12-intrinsic factor complex is absorbed by a specific transport mechanism in the ileum. Removing the terminal ileum removes that site even though the stomach makes intrinsic factor normally. Pepsin release, R protein binding, intrinsic factor secretion, and protease cleavage all occur above the ileum. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Water-soluble vitamins). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: intrinsic factor is needed for B12 uptake in the terminal ileum (First Aid Gastrointestinal, secretory products). COMLEX: body B12 stores last years, so deficiency appears long after resection (Guyton Ch. 72).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — parietal cells and intrinsic factor","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain vitamin B₁₂ absorption, including the roles of intrinsic factor and the terminal ileum, and predict the consequences of impaired absorption in pernicious anemia and terminal ileal disease or resection.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old man with chronic alcoholic pancreatitis has greasy stools and weight loss. Mean corpuscular volume is 106 fL (reference range: 80–100 fL), and serum B12 is 170 pg/mL (reference range: 200–900 pg/mL). Gastric biopsy is normal. Which step of B12 absorption is most likely impaired?","choices":{"A":"Release of B12 from food in the stomach","B":"Secretion of intrinsic factor by parietal cells","C":"Uptake of the complex in the terminal ileum","D":"Degradation of R proteins in the duodenum","E":"Binding of B12 to transferrin in plasma"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Pancreatic proteases degrade R proteins in the duodenum so B12 can transfer to intrinsic factor. Without them, B12 stays on R proteins and is poorly absorbed. Pepsin release and parietal cell function are normal, the ileum is intact, and transferrin carries iron. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Water-soluble vitamins). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic insufficiency causes malabsorption of fat, fat-soluble vitamins, and vitamin B12 (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption syndromes). COMLEX: pancreatic insufficiency gives a normal D-xylose test because the mucosa is intact (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption syndromes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — pancreatic digestive enzymes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium, including the major factors required for their normal absorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man on hemodialysis has bone pain and muscle cramps. Serum calcium is 7.6 mg/dL (reference range: 8.5–10.2 mg/dL), and 25-hydroxyvitamin D is normal. Which mechanism best explains his low intestinal calcium absorption?","choices":{"A":"Deficient hepatic 25-hydroxylation","B":"Deficient renal 1α-hydroxylation","C":"Excess intestinal transferrin synthesis","D":"Loss of intrinsic factor secretion","E":"Impaired chylomicron formation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The proximal tubule converts 25-hydroxycholecalciferol to active 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol through 1α-hydroxylase. In chronic kidney disease this step fails, calbindin D-28K induction falls, and calcium absorption drops. Hepatic 25-hydroxylation is intact because 25-hydroxyvitamin D is normal. The other choices involve iron, B12, and lipid. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Vitamins; Calcium; Iron). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in adults, inadequate calcium absorption causes osteomalacia (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: active vitamin D acts on intestine, kidney, and bone (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 5 — calcium absorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium, including the major factors required for their normal absorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man has fatigue, joint pain, and darkening skin. Transferrin saturation is 78% (reference range: 20–50%), and ferritin is 1900 ng/mL (reference range: 24–336 ng/mL). Genetic testing shows a homozygous HFE mutation. Which change best explains his iron overload?","choices":{"A":"High hepcidin increasing iron absorption","B":"Loss of lysosomal heme digestion","C":"Excess intrinsic factor secretion","D":"Excess vitamin D-induced calbindin","E":"Low hepcidin increasing iron absorption"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Absorbed iron moves from apoferritin across the basolateral membrane and onto transferrin. HFE mutation lowers hepcidin, which normally limits that export, so absorption runs high and iron accumulates. High hepcidin would decrease absorption. Lysosomal heme digestion, intrinsic factor, and calbindin are unrelated. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Vitamins; Calcium; Iron); First Aid Hematology (hemochromatosis). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hereditary hemochromatosis is linked to HFE mutation on chromosome 6 (First Aid Hematology, hemochromatosis). COMLEX: iron enters enterocytes as Fe2+ or as heme iron (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 5 — iron absorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium, including the major factors required for their normal absorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 16-year-old girl with cystic fibrosis has stopped taking her pancreatic enzyme capsules. She now has easy bruising and greasy stools. Prothrombin time is 19 seconds (reference range: 11–13.5 seconds), and platelet count is normal. Deficiency of which vitamin best explains her bleeding?","choices":{"A":"Vitamin C","B":"Vitamin B12","C":"Vitamin K","D":"Vitamin B1","E":"Folic acid"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Without pancreatic lipase, fat is not digested and fat-soluble vitamins, which travel with lipids in micelles and chylomicrons, are malabsorbed. Vitamin K deficiency prolongs the prothrombin time. Vitamin C, B1, and folate are water-soluble vitamins absorbed by sodium-dependent cotransport. B12 deficiency causes megaloblastic anemia. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Vitamins; Calcium; Iron); First Aid Gastrointestinal (malabsorption syndromes). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic insufficiency from cystic fibrosis causes malabsorption of vitamins A, D, E, and K (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption syndromes). COMLEX: vitamin K is needed to make clotting factors II, VII, IX, and X (Guyton Ch. 72).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 4 — malabsorption and pancreatic insufficiency","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl27_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the digestion and emulsification of dietary lipids, including the roles of bile salts, lecithin, pancreatic lipase, colipase, and micelle formation.","batch":3,"stem":"A 2-week-old infant digests some milk fat before it reaches the duodenum. Which enzymes start this digestion?","choices":{"A":"Pancreatic phospholipase A2","B":"Brush-border disaccharidases","C":"Hepatic lipoprotein lipase","D":"Lingual and gastric lipases","E":"Salivary α-amylase"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Lingual and gastric lipases hydrolyze about 10 percent of ingested triglyceride before chyme reaches the small intestine. Phospholipase A2 is pancreatic, disaccharidases act on sugars, lipoprotein lipase clears plasma lipoproteins, and amylase digests starch. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 27 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: in the stomach, dietary proteins keep lipid droplets emulsified because bile acids are absent (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: slow gastric emptying gives pancreatic enzymes time to digest lipid (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"},{"id":"trial_sdl27_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the absorption and intracellular processing of lipids, including uptake from micelles, re-esterification, chylomicron formation, and transport through the lymphatic system.","batch":3,"stem":"A 33-year-old man eats a fatty meal. Which molecules cover most of the surface of the chylomicrons his enterocytes export?","choices":{"A":"Phospholipids","B":"Triglycerides","C":"Cholesterol esters","D":"Bile salts","E":"Free fatty acids"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Phospholipids cover about 80 percent of the chylomicron surface, and apoproteins cover the rest. Triglycerides and cholesterol esters form the core. Bile salts stay in the lumen, and free fatty acids are re-esterified. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of lipids). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 27 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Apo B is required for chylomicron formation (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: chylomicrons reach the blood through the thoracic duct (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 2 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"},{"id":"trial_sdl27_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and predict the consequences of terminal ileal disease or resection, including bile salt loss, steatorrhea, and bile acid–induced secretory diarrhea.","batch":3,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman's liver must replace bile acids lost in her stool. Which enzyme limits the rate of new bile acid synthesis?","choices":{"A":"HMG-CoA reductase","B":"Lipoprotein lipase","C":"Pancreatic colipase","D":"Phospholipase A2","E":"Cholesterol 7α-hydroxylase"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Cholesterol 7α-hydroxylase is the rate-limiting enzyme of bile acid synthesis from cholesterol. HMG-CoA reductase limits cholesterol synthesis, lipoprotein lipase clears plasma lipoproteins, and colipase and phospholipase A2 act in the intestinal lumen. Sources: First Aid Gastrointestinal (bile); Costanzo Ch. 8 (Bile). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bile acid loss in feces increases hepatic bile acid synthesis (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: bile acids are conjugated with glycine or taurine before secretion (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 3 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl27_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain vitamin B₁₂ absorption, including the roles of intrinsic factor and the terminal ileum, and predict the consequences of impaired absorption in pernicious anemia and terminal ileal disease or resection.","batch":3,"stem":"A 72-year-old man eats meat as his main source of vitamin B12. What releases the vitamin from food proteins in his stomach?","choices":{"A":"Intrinsic factor","B":"Lysosomal enzymes","C":"Pepsin digestion","D":"Bile salts","E":"Brush-border peptidases"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Dietary B12 is released from food by pepsin in the stomach, then binds R proteins. Intrinsic factor binds B12 later in the duodenum, lysosomal enzymes act inside cells, bile salts form micelles, and brush-border peptidases act in the small intestine. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Water-soluble vitamins). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: B12 is found in animal-derived foods, so strict vegans risk deficiency (Guyton Ch. 72). COMLEX: R proteins come from salivary secretions (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 4 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl27_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium, including the major factors required for their normal absorption.","batch":3,"stem":"A 27-year-old woman takes oral iron for anemia. Which plasma protein carries the absorbed iron to her liver and marrow?","choices":{"A":"Apoferritin","B":"Transferrin","C":"Albumin","D":"Calbindin","E":"Ceruloplasmin"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Transferrin, a β-globulin, carries iron from the intestine to liver stores and then to bone marrow. Apoferritin binds iron inside enterocytes, albumin is not the iron carrier, calbindin binds calcium, and ceruloplasmin carries copper. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Iron). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: low hepcidin in hemochromatosis increases iron absorption (First Aid Hematology, hemochromatosis). COMLEX: heme iron is absorbed intact and freed inside the enterocyte (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 5 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl27_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the digestion and emulsification of dietary lipids, including the roles of bile salts, lecithin, pancreatic lipase, colipase, and micelle formation.","batch":3,"stem":"A 30-year-old man eats eggs rich in sterol esters. Which pancreatic enzyme frees the sterol for micelles?","choices":{"A":"Cholesterol ester hydrolase","B":"Phospholipase A2","C":"Pancreatic amylase","D":"Carboxypeptidase A","E":"Enteropeptidase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cholesterol ester hydrolase is secreted in active form and splits cholesterol esters into free cholesterol and fatty acids. Phospholipase A2 acts on phospholipids, amylase on starch, carboxypeptidase on peptides, and enteropeptidase on trypsinogen. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: free cholesterol is solubilized in mixed micelles for absorption (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: cholesterol ester hydrolase can also release glycerol from triglycerides (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"grid_sdl27_q01","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and predict the consequences of terminal ileal disease or resection, including bile salt loss, steatorrhea, and bile acid–induced secretory diarrhea.","batch":4,"stem":"A 56-year-old man with high LDL cholesterol starts cholestyramine, which binds bile acids in the intestinal lumen. Months later he has mild bloating. LDL cholesterol is 182 mg/dL (reference range: <130 mg/dL) before treatment. Which of the following sets of findings is most likely after treatment begins?","grid":{"headers":["Fecal bile acids","Hepatic bile acid synthesis","Vitamin K absorption"]},"choices":{"A":["↓","↑","↓"],"B":["↑","↑","↓"],"C":["↑","↓","↓"],"D":["↔","↔","↓"],"E":["↓","↓","↑"]},"correct":"B","explanation":"Choice B (↑ fecal bile acids, ↑ hepatic bile acid synthesis, ↓ vitamin K absorption) is correct. Bound bile acids escape ileal reabsorption and leave in stool, interrupting the enterohepatic circulation. The liver raises synthesis to replace the loss, and a smaller luminal bile acid pool reduces micelle formation, so absorption of fat-soluble vitamin K falls. Choice A (↓ fecal bile acids, ↑ hepatic bile acid synthesis, ↓ vitamin K absorption) has the fecal loss backward; a binder increases, not decreases, stool bile acids. Choice C (↑ fecal bile acids, ↓ hepatic bile acid synthesis, ↓ vitamin K absorption) ignores the hepatic response to bile acid loss, in which reduced FXR feedback derepresses synthesis. Choice D (↔ fecal bile acids, ↔ hepatic bile acid synthesis, ↓ vitamin K absorption) fits a lipase inhibitor such as orlistat rather than a bile acid binder. Choice E (↓ fecal bile acids, ↓ hepatic bile acid synthesis, ↑ vitamin K absorption) reverses every effect, as if bile acids were supplemented. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Bile; Disorders of lipid digestion and absorption; Fat-soluble vitamins). The vitamin cell is inferred from micelle dependence; the sources do not grade it for this drug. Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: after ileal resection, hepatic synthesis rises but does not keep pace with fecal bile salt loss (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: bile acid loss in Crohn disease of the ileum can cause calcium oxalate kidney stones (First Aid Gastrointestinal, bile).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":28,"title":"SDL 28 — Physiology - Intestinal Fluid & Electrolyte Transport","questions":[{"id":"sdl28_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption, including the roles of solute transport, osmosis, and epithelial tight junctions. (Fig. 8.34)","batch":1,"stem":"About how much fluid enters the gastrointestinal lumen each day from diet plus secretions?","choices":{"A":"About 2 L","B":"About 4 L","C":"About 15 L","D":"About 9 L","E":"About 0.5 L"},"correct":"D","explanation":"About 2 L of dietary fluid plus about 7 L of salivary, gastric, pancreatic, biliary, and intestinal secretions gives slightly more than 9 L. Most is absorbed by the small intestine and colon. 2 L is the dietary share alone. About 4 L underestimates the load; ingested plus secreted fluid totals roughly 9 L per day. About 15 L overstates normal daily load; volumes above 10–14 L per day are reached only in cholera-type secretory diarrhea. About 0.5 L is far below the roughly 9 L of ingested plus secreted fluid the intestine handles each day. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal fluid and electrolyte transport; Intestinal absorption, Fig. 8.34). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the 9 L absorbed daily is almost equal to the whole extracellular fluid volume, so diarrhea can rapidly deplete it (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: crypt cells add secreted fluid that villus cells then reabsorb (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 2 — Na⁺-coupled solute absorption and Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"About 4 L underestimates the load; ingested plus secreted fl","C":"About 15 L overstates normal daily load; volumes above 10–14","E":"About 0.5 L is far below the roughly 9 L of ingested plus se"}},{"id":"sdl28_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption, including the roles of solute transport, osmosis, and epithelial tight junctions. (Fig. 8.34)","batch":1,"stem":"What is the osmolarity of the fluid absorbed by the intestine compared with plasma?","choices":{"A":"Hyposmotic to plasma","B":"Isosmotic to plasma","C":"Hyperosmotic to plasma","D":"Variable with meal tonicity","E":"Hyperosmotic in the jejunum"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The absorbate is isosmotic: solute and water are absorbed in proportion, as in the renal proximal tubule. It is not hyposmotic or hyperosmotic, and it does not vary with meal tonicity or segment. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal fluid and electrolyte transport; Intestinal absorption, Fig. 8.34). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: jejunal transport mechanisms resemble those of the early renal proximal tubule (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the small remaining volume of 100–200 mL is lost in feces each day (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 2 — Na⁺-coupled solute absorption and Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption, including the roles of solute transport, osmosis, and epithelial tight junctions. (Fig. 8.34)","batch":1,"stem":"What is the first step in intestinal fluid absorption by villus cells?","choices":{"A":"Absorption of solute","B":"Absorption of water","C":"Secretion of chloride","D":"Secretion of potassium","E":"Secretion of mucus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Villus cells first absorb solute, and water follows osmotically so the absorbate stays isosmotic. Water does not move first. Chloride secretion is a crypt function, potassium secretion occurs in the colon, and mucus does not drive fluid uptake. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal fluid and electrolyte transport; Intestinal absorption, Fig. 8.34). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the cell type determines direction, with villus cells absorbing and crypt cells secreting fluid (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: disease that reduces absorptive surface area, such as small intestinal infection, reduces fluid absorption (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 2 — Na⁺-coupled solute absorption and Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare electrolyte absorption in the jejunum, ileum, and colon, emphasizing Na⁺, Cl⁻, and HCO₃⁻ transport mechanisms. (Figs. 8.35- 8.36)","batch":1,"stem":"The jejunum mainly produces net absorption of which salt?","choices":{"A":"Sodium chloride","B":"Potassium chloride","C":"Sodium bicarbonate","D":"Potassium bicarbonate","E":"Calcium chloride"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In the jejunum, H+ leaves on apical Na+-H+ exchange and HCO3- made by carbonic anhydrase is absorbed into blood, giving net NaHCO3 absorption. The ileum adds Cl--HCO3- exchange and absorbs net NaCl. The colon absorbs Na+ and secretes K+. Potassium chloride is not the jejunal absorbate; the colon handles K⁺, secreting it through apical K⁺ channels. Potassium bicarbonate is not absorbed in the jejunum; H⁺ is exchanged for Na⁺, so the cation absorbed with HCO₃⁻ is sodium. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Jejunum; Ileum; Colon, Figs. 8.35–8.36). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the jejunum is the major site of Na+ absorption in the small intestine (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: jejunal Na+ also enters on Na+-glucose, Na+-galactose, and Na+-amino acid cotransporters (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 2 — SGLT1 Na⁺ cotransport and basolateral Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Potassium chloride is not the jejunal absorbate; the colon h","D":"Potassium bicarbonate is not absorbed in the jejunum; H⁺ is "}},{"id":"sdl28_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare electrolyte absorption in the jejunum, ileum, and colon, emphasizing Na⁺, Cl⁻, and HCO₃⁻ transport mechanisms. (Figs. 8.35- 8.36)","batch":1,"stem":"Which hormone induces synthesis of apical Na⁺ channels in colonic epithelial cells?","choices":{"A":"Antidiuretic hormone","B":"Natriuretic peptide","C":"Cholecystokinin","D":"Aldosterone","E":"Somatostatin"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Like renal principal cells, colonocytes have apical Na+ and K+ channels, and aldosterone induces Na+ channel synthesis. This increases Na+ absorption and, secondarily, K+ secretion. ADH acts on renal water channels, ANP promotes natriuresis, and CCK and secretin regulate pancreatic and biliary secretion. Somatostatin from D cells is inhibitory, suppressing other GI hormones and gastric H+ secretion, and does not induce colonic Na+ channels. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Jejunum; Ileum; Colon, Figs. 8.35–8.36). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colonic cells resemble the principal cells of the late distal tubule and collecting duct (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: increased Na+ entry raises basolateral pumping, which drives K+ into the cell for secretion (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 2 — SGLT1 Na⁺ cotransport and basolateral Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Somatostatin from D cells is inhibitory, suppressing other G"}},{"id":"sdl28_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare electrolyte absorption in the jejunum, ileum, and colon, emphasizing Na⁺, Cl⁻, and HCO₃⁻ transport mechanisms. (Figs. 8.35- 8.36)","batch":1,"stem":"Which apical exchanger is present in the ileum but not the jejunum?","choices":{"A":"Na⁺-H⁺ exchanger","B":"Cl⁻-HCO₃⁻ exchanger","C":"Na⁺-glucose cotransporter","D":"Na⁺-K⁺-2Cl⁻ cotransporter","E":"H⁺-K⁺ ATPase pump"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The ileum has the jejunal mechanisms plus apical Cl--HCO3- exchange and a basolateral Cl- transporter. Combined with Na+-H+ exchange, this gives net NaCl absorption. Na+-H+ exchange and Na+-glucose cotransport are present in both segments. The Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter is basolateral in crypt cells, and H+-K+ ATPase is gastric. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Jejunum; Ileum; Colon, Figs. 8.35–8.36). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in the ileum, HCO3- is secreted into the lumen rather than absorbed into blood (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the ileal basolateral membrane has a Cl- transporter instead of an HCO3- transporter (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 2 — SGLT1 Na⁺ cotransport and basolateral Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of intestinal Cl⁻ and water secretion, including the roles of CFTR channels, cAMP, and VIP. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":1,"stem":"Which intestinal cells secrete chloride and water into the lumen?","choices":{"A":"Villus tip enterocytes","B":"Goblet mucus cells","C":"Paneth defensin cells","D":"Enteroendocrine I cells","E":"Crypt epithelial cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Epithelial cells lining the crypts secrete fluid and electrolytes, while cells lining the villi absorb them. Goblet cells secrete mucus, Paneth cells secrete defensins, and I cells secrete CCK. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal secretion, Fig. 8.37). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: crypts of Lieberkühn contain stem cells that replace enterocytes and goblet cells (First Aid Gastrointestinal, digestive tract histology). COMLEX: normally villus cells reabsorb what crypt cells secrete (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — neurocrines including VIP","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of intestinal Cl⁻ and water secretion, including the roles of CFTR channels, cAMP, and VIP. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":1,"stem":"Which basolateral transporter brings chloride from the blood into chloride-secreting intestinal cells?","choices":{"A":"Na⁺-glucose cotransporter","B":"Cl⁻-HCO₃⁻ exchanger","C":"Apical Na⁺-H⁺ exchanger protein","D":"Na⁺-K⁺-2Cl⁻ cotransporter","E":"Basolateral K⁺ leak channel"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Crypt cells have a basolateral Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter like the one in the thick ascending limb. Cl- enters on it and exits through apical Cl- channels. Na+-glucose cotransport and Na+-H+ exchange are apical absorptive carriers, and Cl--HCO3- exchange is an apical ileal mechanism. The basolateral K⁺ leak channel only recycles K⁺ that NKCC1 brings in; it is not the carrier that loads Cl⁻ into crypt cells. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal secretion, Fig. 8.37). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the same three-ion cotransporter in the thick ascending limb is the target of loop diuretics (First Aid Renal). COMLEX: the Na+-K+ ATPase on the basolateral membrane keeps intracellular Na+ low for the cotransporter (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — neurocrines including VIP","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"The basolateral K⁺ leak channel only recycles K⁺ that NKCC1 "}},{"id":"sdl28_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of intestinal Cl⁻ and water secretion, including the roles of CFTR channels, cAMP, and VIP. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":1,"stem":"How does sodium reach the lumen during intestinal chloride secretion?","choices":{"A":"Passively by the paracellular route","B":"Through apical Na⁺ channels into the lumen","C":"On the basolateral Na⁺-K⁺ ATPase pump","D":"By apical Na⁺-H⁺ exchange","E":"By vesicular transcytosis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cl- is secreted through apical channels, and Na+ follows passively by the paracellular route between cells. Water then follows the NaCl. Apical Na+ channels and Na+-H+ exchange move Na+ into cells, and the Na+-K+ ATPase is basolateral. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal secretion, Fig. 8.37). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: leaky small-intestinal tight junctions permit substantial paracellular movement (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the colon has high-resistance tight junctions that limit paracellular flow (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — neurocrines including VIP","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathophysiology of secretory diarrhea, including cholera toxin–mediated increases in cAMP and subsequent electrolyte and water loss. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":1,"stem":"Which covalent change does cholera toxin make to the αs subunit of Gs?","choices":{"A":"Tyrosine phosphorylation","B":"Glycosylation","C":"Ubiquitination","D":"Methylation","E":"ADP-ribosylation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The A subunit of cholera toxin catalyzes ADP-ribosylation of the αs subunit of the Gs protein. The other modifications are not the toxin's action. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cholera toxin ADP-ribosylates the αs subunit so Gs stays GTP-bound and adenylyl cyclase stays active (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the toxin enters crypt cells across the apical membrane and acts on Gs at the basolateral side (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 2 — sodium-coupled glucose absorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathophysiology of secretory diarrhea, including cholera toxin–mediated increases in cAMP and subsequent electrolyte and water loss. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":1,"stem":"What is the direct effect of cholera toxin on the αs subunit of Gs?","choices":{"A":"Loss of its GTP binding","B":"Faster GDP rebinding","C":"Loss of its GTPase activity","D":"Faster receptor desensitizing","E":"Gain of Gi-type coupling"},"correct":"C","explanation":"ADP-ribosylation inhibits the GTPase activity of αs, so GTP stays bound and the subunit stays active. GTP binding is kept, not lost. Faster GDP rebinding would end the signal, and the toxin does not change receptor desensitization or coupling. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: with GTP permanently bound, cAMP stays high and apical Cl- channels stay open (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the secreted fluid volume can reach 10 to 12 L per day (Guyton Ch. 67).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 2 — sodium-coupled glucose absorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathophysiology of secretory diarrhea, including cholera toxin–mediated increases in cAMP and subsequent electrolyte and water loss. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":1,"stem":"Why does cholera cause massive diarrhea despite intact villus cells?","choices":{"A":"Villus cells switch over to net fluid secretion","B":"Crypt secretion exceeds villus absorption","C":"Colonic water absorption is abolished","D":"Toxin destroys the villus epithelium","E":"Bacteria invade the submucosal layer"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cholera stimulates crypt Cl- and fluid secretion so strongly that it overwhelms the absorptive capacity of villus cells and the colon. Villus cells keep absorbing, which is why glucose-coupled Na+ absorption still works. The toxin does not destroy the epithelium or cause invasion. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal secretion, Fig. 8.37); Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Vibrio cholerae and some Escherichia coli strains cause secretory diarrhea through toxins (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the colon can reabsorb a maximum of about 6 to 8 L per day (Guyton Ch. 67).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 2 — sodium-coupled glucose absorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate osmotic and secretory diarrhea based on their underlying mechanisms, clinical characteristics, and effects on intestinal fluid and electrolyte balance.","batch":1,"stem":"Which laxative class includes lactulose and polyethylene glycol?","choices":{"A":"Osmotic laxatives","B":"Stimulant laxatives","C":"Bulk-forming laxatives","D":"Emollient laxatives","E":"Opioid antidiarrheals"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Osmotic laxatives provide a nonabsorbable osmotic load that draws water into the lumen, the mechanism of osmotic diarrhea. Stimulant laxatives act on enteric nerves, bulk-forming agents are soluble fibers, emollients lower stool surface tension, and opioid agents slow motility. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea); First Aid Gastrointestinal (anticonstipation drugs). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 28 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: lactulose also treats hepatic encephalopathy because gut bacteria acidify the colon and trap NH4+ (First Aid Gastrointestinal, anticonstipation drugs). COMLEX: bacteria degrade some nonabsorbed solutes into more osmotically active particles (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 1 — lactase deficiency","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"},{"id":"sdl28_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate osmotic and secretory diarrhea based on their underlying mechanisms, clinical characteristics, and effects on intestinal fluid and electrolyte balance.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the major cause of secretory diarrhea?","choices":{"A":"Brush-border lactase deficiency","B":"Exocrine pancreatic insufficiency","C":"Nonabsorbable laxative ingestion","D":"Loss of absorptive mucosal area","E":"Enteropathic bacterial overgrowth"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Costanzo names overgrowth of enteropathic bacteria such as Vibrio cholerae or Escherichia coli as the major cause of secretory diarrhea. Lactase deficiency and laxatives cause osmotic diarrhea, pancreatic insufficiency causes malabsorption, and reduced mucosal area is a separate category. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: VIPoma causes watery diarrhea, hypokalemia, and achlorhydria (First Aid Gastrointestinal, VIP). COMLEX: enteritis increases mucosal secretion and motility to flush the infectious agent (Guyton Ch. 67).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 1 — lactase deficiency","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate osmotic and secretory diarrhea based on their underlying mechanisms, clinical characteristics, and effects on intestinal fluid and electrolyte balance.","batch":1,"stem":"Diarrheal fluid is rich in which anion because intestinal secretions contain it?","choices":{"A":"Chloride ion","B":"Phosphate ion","C":"Bicarbonate","D":"Sulfate ion","E":"Lactate ion"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Salivary, pancreatic, and intestinal juices have a high HCO3- content, so diarrheal fluid does too. Loss of HCO3- relative to Cl- causes hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis. Chloride is retained relative to bicarbonate. Phosphate, sulfate, and lactate are not the named anion. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diarrhea causes normal anion gap metabolic acidosis (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: diarrheal fluid is also high in K+ from flow-dependent colonic secretion (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 1 — lactase deficiency","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption, including the roles of solute transport, osmosis, and epithelial tight junctions. (Fig. 8.34)","batch":2,"stem":"A 54-year-old man has a high-output jejunostomy after bowel surgery. He drinks about 2 L per day, yet stoma output is 3.5 L per day. Blood pressure is 102/64 mm Hg, and BUN is 31 mg/dL (reference range: 7–20 mg/dL). Which best explains why output can exceed what he drinks?","choices":{"A":"Upper gut secretions add to dietary fluid","B":"Excess colonic secretion of potassium-rich fluid","C":"Renal water loss into the bowel","D":"Bacterial fermentation of dietary starch","E":"Stoma mucosa secreting mucus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"About 7 L of salivary, gastric, pancreatic, and biliary secretions joins dietary fluid each day. Normally the distal small intestine and colon reabsorb it, so losing them lets secreted fluid pour out. The colon is bypassed, the kidney does not empty into the bowel, and fermentation and mucus are minor. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal fluid and electrolyte transport; Intestinal absorption, Fig. 8.34). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a high-output stoma can cause dehydration and prerenal azotemia because proximal secretions are not reabsorbed (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: diarrheal loss of extracellular-type fluid lowers arterial pressure (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — pancreatic bicarbonate-rich secretion adds to luminal fluid","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption, including the roles of solute transport, osmosis, and epithelial tight junctions. (Fig. 8.34)","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old man receives a hypertonic enteral feed directly into the jejunum and develops cramps and watery output. Temperature is 37.0°C, and serum sodium is 139 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L). Which process best explains the early increase in luminal fluid?","choices":{"A":"Active water pumping by crypt epithelial cells","B":"Closure of jejunal tight junctions","C":"Water entry until contents are isosmotic","D":"Toxin-driven chloride channel opening","E":"Aldosterone-driven colonic secretion"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Water crosses the leaky jejunal epithelium osmotically, so hypertonic contents draw water into the lumen until they approach isosmolarity. Water does not move by active pumping. Jejunal tight junctions are leaky. No toxin is present, and aldosterone acts on colonic absorption. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal fluid and electrolyte transport; Intestinal absorption, Fig. 8.34). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hyperosmotic luminal contents draw water into the lumen until they become isosmotic (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: intestinal absorbate is isosmotic, much like proximal tubular fluid (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — duodenal osmolality and gastric emptying","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption, including the roles of solute transport, osmosis, and epithelial tight junctions. (Fig. 8.34)","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old woman has an end ileostomy after colectomy. Her stoma effluent sodium stays close to her plasma sodium, whereas normal stool sodium is much lower. Blood pressure is 108/68 mm Hg. Which feature of the small intestine best explains why it fails to lower effluent sodium as the colon would?","choices":{"A":"Absent basolateral Na⁺-K⁺ ATPase","B":"Resistance of enterocytes to aldosterone","C":"High colonic potassium secretion","D":"Bile salt-driven crypt secretion","E":"Leaky low-resistance tight junctions"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Small-intestinal tight junctions are low resistance and permit paracellular movement, so Na+ leaks back and a steep gradient against the lumen is hard to hold. The colon has tight high-resistance junctions and aldosterone-induced Na+ channels. The Na+-K+ ATPase is present, and colonic K+ secretion is absent after colectomy. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal fluid and electrolyte transport; Intestinal absorption, Fig. 8.34); Costanzo Ch. 8 (Jejunum; Ileum; Colon, Figs. 8.35–8.36). The effluent-sodium comparison is inferred from tight-junction resistance. Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: aldosterone increases colonic Na+ absorption through apical Na+ channels (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: loss of the colon removes the high-resistance segment that finishes salt and water absorption (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 2 — enterocyte apical and basolateral membranes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare electrolyte absorption in the jejunum, ileum, and colon, emphasizing Na⁺, Cl⁻, and HCO₃⁻ transport mechanisms. (Figs. 8.35- 8.36)","batch":2,"stem":"A 71-year-old woman has 3 days of profuse watery diarrhea from norovirus. She is weak, and blood pressure is 104/66 mm Hg. Serum potassium is 2.9 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). Which mechanism most directly explains her low potassium?","choices":{"A":"Renal K⁺ wasting from loop diuretics","B":"Shift of K⁺ into cells from alkalosis","C":"Reduced dietary potassium intake","D":"Flow-dependent colonic K⁺ secretion","E":"Crypt-cell K⁺ secretion via channels"},"correct":"D","explanation":"As in renal principal cells, colonic K+ secretion depends on flow. High diarrheal flow increases K+ secretion and fecal K+ loss, causing hypokalemia. No diuretic is given, diarrhea causes acidosis rather than alkalosis, intake is not the main factor, and crypt cells secrete Cl-. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Jejunum; Ileum; Colon, Figs. 8.35–8.36); Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 28 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypokalemia can cause muscle weakness and flattened T waves (First Aid Renal). COMLEX: aldosterone also increases colonic K+ secretion through Na+ channel synthesis (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — electrolyte effects of diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"},{"id":"sdl28_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare electrolyte absorption in the jejunum, ileum, and colon, emphasizing Na⁺, Cl⁻, and HCO₃⁻ transport mechanisms. (Figs. 8.35- 8.36)","batch":2,"stem":"A 49-year-old man has hypertension and muscle cramps. Serum potassium is 3.0 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), plasma renin is suppressed, and an adrenal adenoma is found. Which change in his colonic epithelium is expected?","choices":{"A":"Reduced colonic K⁺ secretion","B":"Increased opening of crypt Cl⁻ channels by cAMP","C":"Increased Cl⁻-HCO₃⁻ exchange activity","D":"Decreased basolateral K⁺ uptake","E":"Increased apical Na⁺ channel synthesis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Aldosterone induces synthesis of apical Na+ channels in colonocytes. Na+ absorption rises, and K+ secretion rises secondarily through increased pumping, adding to K+ loss. Decreased channel synthesis is the opposite. Crypt Cl- channels respond to cAMP agonists, and Cl--HCO3- exchange is ileal. Basolateral K+ uptake increases. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Jejunum; Ileum; Colon, Figs. 8.35–8.36). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: primary hyperaldosteronism causes hypertension, hypokalemia, and low renin (First Aid Endocrine). COMLEX: the colonic transport pattern mirrors the late distal tubule and collecting duct (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 2 — colonic electrolyte transport","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare electrolyte absorption in the jejunum, ileum, and colon, emphasizing Na⁺, Cl⁻, and HCO₃⁻ transport mechanisms. (Figs. 8.35- 8.36)","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman takes a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor for glaucoma. Serum bicarbonate is 19 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Which jejunal transport step is most directly reduced?","choices":{"A":"Apical Na⁺-glucose cotransport by SGLT1 carriers","B":"Supply of H⁺ for apical Na⁺-H⁺ exchange","C":"Basolateral Na⁺-K⁺-2Cl⁻ uptake","D":"Apical Na⁺ channel insertion by aldosterone","E":"Paracellular water absorption"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In the jejunum, carbonic anhydrase converts CO2 and H2O to H+ and HCO3-. H+ is secreted on the Na+-H+ exchanger and HCO3- is absorbed. Inhibiting the enzyme limits H+ supply for that exchange and HCO3- absorption. Na+-glucose cotransport is independent, the three-ion cotransporter is in crypt cells, and apical Na+ channels are colonic. Paracellular water absorption follows solute osmotically and does not depend on carbonic anhydrase generating H⁺. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Jejunum; Ileum; Colon, Figs. 8.35–8.36). The drug effect on gut is inferred from the mechanism. Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: carbonic anhydrase inhibitors cause metabolic acidosis by reducing proximal tubular HCO3- reabsorption (First Aid Renal). COMLEX: jejunal electrolyte transport is identical to that of the early proximal tubule (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — carbonic anhydrase and H⁺/HCO₃⁻ generation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Paracellular water absorption follows solute osmotically and"}},{"id":"sdl28_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of intestinal Cl⁻ and water secretion, including the roles of CFTR channels, cAMP, and VIP. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":2,"stem":"A newborn has abdominal distension and fails to pass meconium. Contrast enema shows a microcolon with inspissated meconium in the distal ileum. Sweat chloride later returns 78 mmol/L (reference range: <30 mmol/L). Which defect best explains the thick intestinal contents?","choices":{"A":"Impaired apical Cl⁻ secretion by crypt cells","B":"Excess Na⁺ absorption by colonic channels","C":"Loss of basolateral Na⁺-K⁺-2Cl⁻ uptake","D":"Absent Na⁺-glucose cotransport on villus enterocytes","E":"Overactive apical anion exchange"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Crypt Cl- secretion exits through apical Cl- channels, the CFTR channel. Without it, Cl-, Na+, and water are not secreted, and luminal contents become dehydrated. The basolateral cotransporter is intact, and absorption by villus Na+-glucose cotransport is not the defect. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal secretion, Fig. 8.37). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cystic fibrosis causes exocrine pancreatic insufficiency (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption syndromes). COMLEX: Na+ follows secreted Cl- passively between cells, and water follows NaCl (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — pancreatic bicarbonate secretion and CFTR","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of intestinal Cl⁻ and water secretion, including the roles of CFTR channels, cAMP, and VIP. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman has 3 L per day of watery diarrhea that continues while she is fasting. Serum potassium is 2.6 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), and gastric acid output is low. CT shows a pancreatic tail mass. Which mechanism best explains her diarrhea?","choices":{"A":"Nonabsorbed solute holding water","B":"Bile acid stimulation of colonic epithelium","C":"Peptide-driven crypt-cell cAMP excess","D":"Mucosal invasion by bacteria","E":"Rapid transit from gastrin excess"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A VIP-secreting tumor activates basolateral receptors on crypt cells, raising cAMP and opening apical Cl- channels. Na+ and water follow, causing large-volume secretory diarrhea that continues during fasting. Nonabsorbed solute would stop with fasting, no ileal disease is present, no invasion is suggested, and gastrin is not the hormone. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal secretion, Fig. 8.37); First Aid Gastrointestinal (VIP). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: VIPoma causes watery diarrhea, hypokalemia, and achlorhydria (WDHA syndrome) (First Aid Gastrointestinal, VIP). COMLEX: octreotide is used to treat VIPoma (First Aid Gastrointestinal, octreotide).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 5 — VIPoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of intestinal Cl⁻ and water secretion, including the roles of CFTR channels, cAMP, and VIP. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old farmworker has vomiting, drooling, small pupils, and profuse watery diarrhea after spraying an organophosphate pesticide. Heart rate is 48/min. Plasma cholinesterase is 900 U/L (reference range: 3000–8000 U/L). Which mechanism best explains increased intestinal fluid secretion?","choices":{"A":"Excess VIP release from a pancreatic islet tumor","B":"Excess acetylcholine at crypt cell receptors","C":"Toxin-mediated Gs activation in crypt cells","D":"Nonabsorbed pesticide holding water","E":"Loss of colonic Na⁺ channel synthesis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Costanzo lists acetylcholine among the basolateral agonists that open apical Cl- channels in crypt cells. Cholinesterase inhibition lets acetylcholine build up, so Cl-, Na+, and water secretion increases. VIP excess is a tumor syndrome, ADP-ribosylation is the toxin mechanism, the pesticide is not an osmotic load, and aldosterone controls colonic channels. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal secretion, Fig. 8.37). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: organophosphates irreversibly inhibit acetylcholinesterase and cause a cholinergic toxidrome (First Aid Pharmacology). COMLEX: atropine blocks muscarinic effects and pralidoxime regenerates acetylcholinesterase (First Aid Pharmacology).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — enteric nervous system and autonomic input","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathophysiology of secretory diarrhea, including cholera toxin–mediated increases in cAMP and subsequent electrolyte and water loss. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old aid worker has profuse rice-water stools 2 days after drinking untreated water. Blood pressure is 92/58 mm Hg, and stool culture grows Vibrio cholerae. Which intracellular messenger remains persistently high in his crypt cells?","choices":{"A":"Cyclic AMP","B":"Cyclic GMP","C":"Inositol trisphosphate","D":"Diacylglycerol","E":"Nitric oxide"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Toxin-modified αs keeps adenylyl cyclase active, so cAMP stays high and apical Cl- channels stay open. Cyclic GMP is the messenger of heat-stable E. coli toxin, not cholera. IP3 and diacylglycerol come from Gq signaling, and nitric oxide relaxes smooth muscle. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea); Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal secretion, Fig. 8.37). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cholera toxin causes secretory diarrhea by permanently activating Gs (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: intravenous replacement of fluid and electrolytes is the main basis of therapy in severe cholera (Guyton Ch. 67).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — Gs/cAMP receptor signaling pathways","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathophysiology of secretory diarrhea, including cholera toxin–mediated increases in cAMP and subsequent electrolyte and water loss. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman returns from Mexico with 4 days of watery diarrhea from toxigenic Escherichia coli. Serum sodium is 138 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L), chloride 114 mEq/L (reference range: 98–106 mEq/L), and bicarbonate 15 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Which acid-base disorder is most likely?","choices":{"A":"High anion gap metabolic acidosis","B":"Hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis","C":"Acute respiratory acidosis","D":"Hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis","E":"Acute respiratory alkalosis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Secretions lost in diarrhea are rich in HCO3-, so HCO3- falls relative to Cl-. The anion gap is 138 − (114 + 15) = 9, normal, which fits hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis. A high gap would need unmeasured acids. Alkalosis follows vomiting, and respiratory disorders are not primary. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diarrhea is a classic cause of normal anion gap metabolic acidosis (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: Escherichia coli enterotoxins cause secretory diarrhea like cholera toxin (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — electrolyte effects of diarrhea","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathophysiology of secretory diarrhea, including cholera toxin–mediated increases in cAMP and subsequent electrolyte and water loss. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-year-old boy in a flood-relief camp has had 12 watery stools in a day. He is lethargic with sunken eyes. Heart rate is 168/min, and blood pressure is 70/40 mm Hg. Which consequence of his secretory diarrhea most directly threatens his life?","choices":{"A":"Hyperkalemic cardiac arrhythmia","B":"Loss of extracellular fluid volume","C":"Hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis from vomiting","D":"Bacteremic septic shock","E":"Hypernatremic seizures"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Diarrhea loses extracellular-type fluid, decreasing ECF and intravascular volume and arterial pressure. Baroreceptor and renin-angiotensin-aldosterone responses fail if the loss is too great or too fast, leading to circulatory collapse. K+ is lost rather than gained, the acidosis is not alkalosis, and toxin-mediated diarrhea is not invasive. Hypernatremic seizures do not fit, because diarrheal secretions are isosmotic extracellular-type fluid whose loss shrinks volume rather than raising sodium. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: without fluid replacement, up to 50 percent of patients with cholera die (Guyton Ch. 67). COMLEX: antibiotics can shorten the duration and volume of cholera diarrhea (Guyton Ch. 67).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — electrolyte effects of diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Hypernatremic seizures do not fit, because diarrheal secreti"}},{"id":"sdl28_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate osmotic and secretory diarrhea based on their underlying mechanisms, clinical characteristics, and effects on intestinal fluid and electrolyte balance.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man starts milk of magnesia daily for constipation and develops watery diarrhea. It stops within a day when he is kept nil by mouth for a procedure. Serum magnesium is 2.6 mg/dL (reference range: 1.7–2.2 mg/dL). Which mechanism best explains his diarrhea?","choices":{"A":"Peptide-driven crypt Cl⁻ secretion","B":"Toxin-driven Gs activation in crypt cells","C":"Inflammatory loss of villus absorptive surface","D":"Unabsorbed cations retaining luminal water","E":"Bile acid stimulation of colonocytes"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Poorly absorbed magnesium stays in the lumen and holds water, an osmotic diarrhea that stops when the solute is stopped. Peptide-driven or toxin-driven crypt secretion persists during fasting. Villous injury and bile acid diarrhea do not fit a laxative that stopped. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea); First Aid Gastrointestinal (anticonstipation drugs). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: osmotic laxatives such as magnesium salts may be misused by patients with bulimia (First Aid Gastrointestinal, anticonstipation drugs). COMLEX: secretory diarrhea results from excess crypt-cell secretion rather than inadequate absorption (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 1 — lactase deficiency as osmotic diarrhea","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate osmotic and secretory diarrhea based on their underlying mechanisms, clinical characteristics, and effects on intestinal fluid and electrolyte balance.","batch":2,"stem":"An 18-month-old girl has 4 days of vomiting and watery diarrhea during a rotavirus outbreak. Biopsy in a similar case shows blunted villi. Heart rate is 150/min, and capillary refill is 3 seconds. Which category of diarrhea best describes her primary mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Toxin-driven crypt secretion","B":"Peptide hormone-driven secretion","C":"Nonabsorbable osmotic laxative load","D":"Aldosterone-driven colonic K⁺ loss","E":"Decreased absorptive surface area"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Costanzo classifies diarrhea into decreased absorptive surface area, osmotic, and secretory types. Infection and inflammation of the small intestine reduce the surface that absorbs fluid. Toxin-driven and hormone-driven crypt secretion are secretory types, and laxatives are osmotic. Aldosterone does not cause diarrhea. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 28 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: rotavirus is a leading cause of watery diarrhea in young children and is preventable by vaccine (First Aid Microbiology). COMLEX: viral injury to villus tips can cause secondary lactase deficiency (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption syndromes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 2 — villus structure and brush border","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"},{"id":"sdl28_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate osmotic and secretory diarrhea based on their underlying mechanisms, clinical characteristics, and effects on intestinal fluid and electrolyte balance.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman has had 5 days of profuse watery diarrhea. She feels dizzy on standing. Blood pressure is 96/60 mm Hg, and urine sodium is 8 mEq/L (reference range: 40–220 mEq/L). Which compensatory response is most likely active?","choices":{"A":"Suppressed antidiuretic hormone release","B":"Increased atrial natriuretic peptide","C":"Activated renin-angiotensin-aldosterone","D":"Reduced sympathetic outflow","E":"Suppressed baroreceptor reflex activity"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Loss of extracellular-type fluid lowers arterial pressure. Baroreceptors increase sympathetic outflow, and the renin-angiotensin II-aldosterone system is activated to retain Na+, giving low urine sodium. ADH increases rather than falls, ANP falls with low volume, and sympathetic and baroreceptor responses increase. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 28 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: low urine sodium in hypovolemia reflects avid renal sodium retention (First Aid Renal). COMLEX: compensation fails if diarrheal fluid loss is too large or too rapid (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — electrolyte effects of diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"},{"id":"trial_sdl28_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption, including the roles of solute transport, osmosis, and epithelial tight junctions. (Fig. 8.34)","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with an ileostomy asks which bowel segment normally has the tightest epithelial junctions. Which segment has high-resistance tight junctions?","choices":{"A":"Duodenum","B":"Jejunum","C":"Colon","D":"Ileum","E":"Stomach"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Colonic tight junctions are tight, with high resistance, and do not permit paracellular movement. Small-intestinal tight junctions are leaky and permit substantial paracellular flow. The stomach is not described in this comparison. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal fluid and electrolyte transport). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colonic transport resembles that of renal principal cells (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: tight-junction permeability decides whether fluid moves by cellular or paracellular routes (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 1 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl28_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare electrolyte absorption in the jejunum, ileum, and colon, emphasizing Na⁺, Cl⁻, and HCO₃⁻ transport mechanisms. (Figs. 8.35- 8.36)","batch":3,"stem":"A 39-year-old woman has extensive Crohn disease of the small bowel. Which small-intestinal segment is normally the major site of Na⁺ absorption?","choices":{"A":"Duodenum","B":"Ileum","C":"Cecum","D":"Rectum","E":"Jejunum"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Costanzo names the jejunum as the major site of Na+ absorption in the small intestine, through Na+-coupled cotransporters and Na+-H+ exchange. The ileum adds Cl--HCO3- exchange. The cecum and rectum are colon. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Jejunum). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Crohn disease can involve any part of the GI tract, most often the terminal ileum (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: Na+ entering jejunal cells is pumped out by the basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 2 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl28_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of intestinal Cl⁻ and water secretion, including the roles of CFTR channels, cAMP, and VIP. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":3,"stem":"A 5-year-old boy with cystic fibrosis has recurrent distal intestinal obstruction. Which protein is defective in his intestinal crypt cells?","choices":{"A":"CFTR chloride channel","B":"Na⁺-K⁺-2Cl⁻ cotransporter","C":"Na⁺-H⁺ exchanger","D":"Epithelial Na⁺ channel","E":"H⁺-K⁺ ATPase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"CFTR is the apical Cl- channel through which crypt cells secrete Cl-, with Na+ and water following. The basolateral three-ion cotransporter, Na+-H+ exchange, and epithelial Na+ channels are intact, and H+-K+ ATPase is gastric. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal secretion, Fig. 8.37). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: without crypt Cl- secretion, Na+ and water do not follow into the lumen, so contents become dehydrated (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: cAMP opens apical Cl- channels in crypt cells (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 3 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl28_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathophysiology of secretory diarrhea, including cholera toxin–mediated increases in cAMP and subsequent electrolyte and water loss. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":3,"stem":"A 24-year-old man has severe rice-water diarrhea from Vibrio cholerae. Which enzyme stays persistently active in his crypt cells?","choices":{"A":"Guanylyl cyclase","B":"Phospholipase C","C":"Protein kinase C","D":"Adenylyl cyclase","E":"Carbonic anhydrase"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Cholera toxin ADP-ribosylates αs, so Gs keeps adenylyl cyclase active and cAMP stays high. Guanylyl cyclase is targeted by heat-stable E. coli toxin, phospholipase C and protein kinase C belong to Gq signaling, and carbonic anhydrase supplies H+ and HCO3-. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Secretory diarrhea, Fig. 8.37). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cholera toxin ADP-ribosylates αs, keeping Gs active and cAMP high (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: cholera can secrete 10 to 12 L per day into the intestine (Guyton Ch. 67).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 4 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl28_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate osmotic and secretory diarrhea based on their underlying mechanisms, clinical characteristics, and effects on intestinal fluid and electrolyte balance.","batch":3,"stem":"A 57-year-old woman has a pancreatic neuroendocrine tumor causing large-volume watery diarrhea despite fasting. Which drug reduces the tumor's hormone secretion?","choices":{"A":"Loperamide","B":"Octreotide","C":"Lactulose","D":"Bisacodyl","E":"Psyllium"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Octreotide is a long-acting somatostatin analog used for VIPoma that inhibits secretion of splanchnic vasodilatory hormones. Loperamide slows motility, lactulose is an osmotic laxative, bisacodyl is a stimulant laxative, and psyllium is bulk-forming. Sources: First Aid Gastrointestinal (octreotide); Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal secretion). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 28 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: octreotide is also used for acute variceal bleeds, acromegaly, and carcinoid tumors (First Aid Gastrointestinal, octreotide). COMLEX: octreotide can cause steatorrhea and cholelithiasis (First Aid Gastrointestinal, octreotide).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"},{"id":"trial_sdl28_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption, including the roles of solute transport, osmosis, and epithelial tight junctions. (Fig. 8.34)","batch":3,"stem":"A healthy 22-year-old woman eats a normal diet. About how much water does she lose in feces each day?","choices":{"A":"About 100–200 mL","B":"About 1–2 L","C":"About 2–3 L","D":"About 500–800 mL","E":"About 5–10 mL"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Of slightly more than 9 L that enters the lumen daily, almost all is absorbed by the small intestine and colon, leaving about 100–200 mL in feces. Losses of liters per day mean diarrhea. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal fluid and electrolyte transport, Fig. 8.34). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 28 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: diarrhea can lose 9 L or more of fluid per day (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: about 2 L of daily luminal fluid comes from the diet (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 1 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"},{"id":"grid_sdl28_q01","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathophysiology of secretory diarrhea, including cholera toxin–mediated increases in cAMP and subsequent electrolyte and water loss. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":4,"stem":"A 30-year-old man in a cholera outbreak has had 2 days of profuse rice-water stools. Blood pressure is 88/56 mm Hg, and heart rate is 124/min. Stool culture grows Vibrio cholerae. Which of the following sets of findings is most likely before treatment?","grid":{"headers":["Plasma aldosterone","Serum K⁺","Serum HCO₃⁻"]},"choices":{"A":["↓","↓","↓"],"B":["↑","↑","↓"],"C":["↑","↓","↓"],"D":["↑","↔","↑"],"E":["↓","↑","↑"]},"correct":"C","explanation":"Choice C (↑ plasma aldosterone, ↓ serum K⁺, ↓ serum HCO₃⁻) is correct. Loss of extracellular-type fluid lowers arterial pressure and activates the renin-angiotensin II-aldosterone system. Diarrheal fluid is high in K⁺ from flow-dependent colonic secretion, which aldosterone adds to, so hypokalemia follows. Diarrheal fluid is also rich in HCO₃⁻ from intestinal secretions, causing hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis. Choice A (↓ plasma aldosterone, ↓ serum K⁺, ↓ serum HCO₃⁻) misses the volume response. Choice B (↑ plasma aldosterone, ↑ serum K⁺, ↓ serum HCO₃⁻) ignores fecal K⁺ loss. Choice D (↑ plasma aldosterone, ↔ serum K⁺, ↑ serum HCO₃⁻) fits vomiting more than diarrhea, with loss of gastric H⁺ raising HCO₃⁻. Choice E (↓ plasma aldosterone, ↑ serum K⁺, ↑ serum HCO₃⁻) reverses each change. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Colon; Diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diarrhea causes normal anion gap metabolic acidosis, whereas vomiting causes metabolic alkalosis (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: intravenous fluid and electrolyte replacement is the main basis of therapy in severe cholera (Guyton Ch. 67).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — electrolyte effects of diarrhea","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":29,"title":"SDL 29 — Pathology - Small Intestine & Colon: Obstructions, Ischemia, and Malabsorption","questions":[{"id":"sdl29_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four major causes of intestinal obstruction (hernias, adhesions, volvulus, and intussusception), including their associated demographics, etiologies, and location (sigmoid colon, intussusceptum).","batch":1,"stem":"About what share of mechanical intestinal obstructions are caused by the four major mechanical causes combined?","choices":{"A":"About 10 percent","B":"About 30 percent","C":"About 50 percent","D":"About 95 percent","E":"About 80 percent"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Hernias, adhesions, intussusception, and volvulus together cause about 80% of mechanical obstructions. Tumors, infarction, and strictures such as Crohn disease make up the rest. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Intestinal obstruction, Fig. 17.25). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 29 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: small bowel obstruction is most commonly caused by intraperitoneal adhesions, tumors, and hernias (First Aid Gastrointestinal, small bowel obstruction). COMLEX: upright abdominal x-ray in small bowel obstruction shows air-fluid levels (First Aid Gastrointestinal, small bowel obstruction).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 1 — midgut rotation and malrotation","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins"},{"id":"sdl29_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four major causes of intestinal obstruction (hernias, adhesions, volvulus, and intussusception), including their associated demographics, etiologies, and location (sigmoid colon, intussusceptum).","batch":1,"stem":"What is the most frequent cause of intestinal obstruction worldwide?","choices":{"A":"Adhesions","B":"Volvulus","C":"Tumors","D":"Hernias","E":"Strictures"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Hernias are the most frequent cause of intestinal obstruction worldwide and the third most common in the United States. Inguinal hernias, with narrow orifices and large sacs, are most often involved. Adhesions are the leading cause of obstruction in the United States and developed nations, not worldwide. Volvulus is rare and occurs most often in the sigmoid colon, so it is not the most frequent cause of obstruction. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hernias). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: femoral hernias protrude below the inguinal ligament and carry a greater risk of strangulation than inguinal hernias (First Aid Gastrointestinal, hernias). COMLEX: acquired hernias are found in up to 5% of the population (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 1 — midgut rotation and malrotation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Adhesions are the leading cause of obstruction in the United","B":"Volvulus is rare and occurs most often in the sigmoid colon,"}},{"id":"sdl29_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four major causes of intestinal obstruction (hernias, adhesions, volvulus, and intussusception), including their associated demographics, etiologies, and location (sigmoid colon, intussusceptum).","batch":1,"stem":"What is the segment of bowel that telescopes into the distal bowel in intussusception called?","choices":{"A":"Intussusceptum","B":"Intussuscipiens","C":"Hernia sac","D":"Mesenteric root","E":"Closed loop"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In intussusception, a segment constricted by a peristaltic wave (the intussusceptum) telescopes into the immediately distal segment (the intussuscipiens). The hernia sac is the serosa-lined peritoneal pouch that protrudes through an abdominal wall defect, not a telescoping bowel segment. A closed loop is formed by fibrous adhesions that trap sliding viscera, an internal hernia mechanism rather than telescoping. The mesentery is pulled along behind the telescoping intussusceptum; it is not the segment that telescopes. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Intestinal obstruction, Fig. 17.25). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: intussusception most commonly occurs at the ileocecal junction (First Aid Gastrointestinal, intussusception). COMLEX: ultrasound or CT of intussusception may show a target sign (First Aid Gastrointestinal, intussusception).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 1 — midgut rotation and malrotation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"The hernia sac is the serosa-lined peritoneal pouch that pro","E":"A closed loop is formed by fibrous adhesions that trap slidi","D":"The mesentery is pulled along behind the telescoping intussu"}},{"id":"sdl29_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the cause, morphology, associated demographics, and clinical features of ischemic bowel diseases (acute mesenteric ischemia, colonic ischemia).","batch":1,"stem":"What causes most acute arterial obstruction of the mesenteric circulation?","choices":{"A":"Systemic vasculitis","B":"Mesenteric venous clot","C":"Tumor compression","D":"Thrombosis or embolism","E":"Vasoconstrictive drugs"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In a large majority of cases, acute mesenteric obstruction is caused by thrombosis or embolism. Severe atherosclerosis is the main risk for thrombosis, and emboli come mostly from aortic atheromas or cardiac mural thrombi. Mesenteric venous thrombosis is a venous cause tied to hypercoagulable states, cirrhosis, and neoplasms, not acute arterial obstruction. Vasoconstrictive drugs such as cocaine cause nonocclusive hypoperfusion rather than arterial obstruction. Vasculitides are a less common cause of mesenteric arterial thrombosis than atherosclerotic thrombosis or embolism. Invasive neoplasms are a cause of mesenteric venous thrombosis, whereas acute arterial obstruction is mostly thrombosis or embolism. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Ischemic bowel disease). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: risk factors for acute mesenteric ischemia include atrial fibrillation, peripheral arterial disease, recent MI, and CHF (First Aid Gastrointestinal, acute mesenteric ischemia). COMLEX: mesenteric venous thrombosis is an uncommon cause of ischemic bowel disease (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut tube divisions and their blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Mesenteric venous thrombosis is a venous cause tied to hyper","E":"Vasoconstrictive drugs such as cocaine cause nonocclusive hy","A":"Vasculitides are a less common cause of mesenteric arterial ","C":"Invasive neoplasms are a cause of mesenteric venous thrombos"}},{"id":"sdl29_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the cause, morphology, associated demographics, and clinical features of ischemic bowel diseases (acute mesenteric ischemia, colonic ischemia).","batch":1,"stem":"Which intestinal component is most vulnerable to ischemic injury?","choices":{"A":"Surface epithelium","B":"Crypt stem cells","C":"Basal crypt cells","D":"Muscularis propria","E":"Myenteric neurons"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Capillaries run from crypt to surface before a hairpin turn, so the surface epithelium is most vulnerable while crypt stem cells are protected. Surface atrophy with normal or hyperproliferative crypts is the signature of ischemia. The muscularis propria is injured only in transmural infarction, typically from acute major vascular occlusion. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Ischemic bowel disease). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic mesenteric ischemia causes postprandial epigastric pain, food aversion, and weight loss (First Aid Gastrointestinal, chronic mesenteric ischemia). COMLEX: chronic intestinal ischemia is accompanied by fibrous scarring of the lamina propria (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut tube divisions and their blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"The muscularis propria is injured only in transmural infarct"}},{"id":"sdl29_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the cause, morphology, associated demographics, and clinical features of ischemic bowel diseases (acute mesenteric ischemia, colonic ischemia).","batch":1,"stem":"In which age group is ischemic disease of the colon most common?","choices":{"A":"Younger than 2 years","B":"Between 20 and 30","C":"Older than 70 years","D":"Between 30 and 40","E":"Between 40 and 50"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Colonic ischemia is most common in patients older than 70 years and is slightly more common in females. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Ischemic bowel disease). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colonic ischemia shows a thumbprint sign on imaging from mucosal edema and hemorrhage (First Aid Gastrointestinal, colonic ischemia). COMLEX: mortality in the first 30 days of colonic ischemia is about 10% with appropriate management (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut tube divisions and their blood supply","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the manifestation (tortuous dilation of vessels), most common location (cecum, right-sided colon), associated age, diagnostic testing (angiography), and associated conditions (ESRD, von Willebrand disease, aortic stenosis) of angiodysplasia.","batch":1,"stem":"Where is angiodysplasia most common?","choices":{"A":"Gastric antrum region","B":"Cecum or right colon","C":"Proximal jejunal loops","D":"Sigmoid colon segment","E":"Lower rectum and anus"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Angiodysplasia is most common in the cecum or right colon and usually comes to attention after the sixth decade. The narrower sigmoid colon lacks the cecum's large diameter and high wall tension that drive angiodysplasia. Angiodysplasia arises from distention-related occlusion of submucosal colonic veins and is most common in the cecum or right colon, not the stomach. Angiodysplasia is most common in the cecum or right colon, where diameter and wall tension are greatest, not in the proximal jejunum. Angiodysplasia favors the cecum or right colon, the segment with the largest diameter and greatest wall tension, not the rectum and anus. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Angiodysplasia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: angiodysplasia presents with hematochezia from tortuous dilated vessels (First Aid Gastrointestinal, angiodysplasia). COMLEX: angiodysplasia bleeding ranges from chronic and intermittent to acute and massive (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"The narrower sigmoid colon lacks the cecum's large diameter ","A":"Angiodysplasia arises from distention-related occlusion of s","C":"Angiodysplasia is most common in the cecum or right colon, w","E":"Angiodysplasia favors the cecum or right colon, the segment "}},{"id":"sdl29_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the manifestation (tortuous dilation of vessels), most common location (cecum, right-sided colon), associated age, diagnostic testing (angiography), and associated conditions (ESRD, von Willebrand disease, aortic stenosis) of angiodysplasia.","batch":1,"stem":"Which test confirms angiodysplasia?","choices":{"A":"Barium swallow","B":"Stool culture","C":"Plain radiograph","D":"Angiography","E":"Breath testing"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Visceral or aortic angiography (with colonoscopy) is the diagnostic test for angiodysplasia. The lesion consists of malformed, dilated, thin-walled submucosal and mucosal vessels. Sources: First Aid Gastrointestinal (angiodysplasia); Robbins Ch. 17 (Angiodysplasia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: angiodysplasia is more common in older patients (First Aid Gastrointestinal, angiodysplasia). COMLEX: angiodysplasia has a prevalence of less than 1% in adults (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the manifestation (tortuous dilation of vessels), most common location (cecum, right-sided colon), associated age, diagnostic testing (angiography), and associated conditions (ESRD, von Willebrand disease, aortic stenosis) of angiodysplasia.","batch":1,"stem":"About what share of major lower intestinal bleeding episodes in older people is due to angiodysplasia?","choices":{"A":"About 1 percent","B":"About 5 percent","C":"About 20 percent","D":"About 50 percent","E":"About 80 percent"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Although its prevalence is less than 1% in adults, angiodysplasia causes about 20% of major lower intestinal bleeding episodes in older populations. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Angiodysplasia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: lower GI bleeding causes include diverticulosis, angiodysplasia, and hemorrhoids (First Aid Gastrointestinal, GI bleeding). COMLEX: angiodysplastic channels are separated from the lumen only by the thin vessel wall and attenuated epithelium (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis of general malabsorption, the relationship between cystic fibrosis and pancreatic insufficiency, and the morphology, serum markers, genetics, and potential complications (malignancy) of Celiac disease.","batch":1,"stem":"What share of patients with cystic fibrosis eventually develop exocrine pancreatic insufficiency?","choices":{"A":"Under 1 percent","B":"About 10 percent","C":"About 25 percent","D":"About 50 percent","E":"Over 80 percent"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In cystic fibrosis, intraductal pancreatic concretions cause duct obstruction, chronic pancreatitis, and exocrine pancreatic insufficiency in more than 80% of patients. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Cystic fibrosis). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic insufficiency causes malabsorption of fat and vitamins A, D, E, and K as well as vitamin B12 (First Aid Gastrointestinal, pancreatic insufficiency). COMLEX: CF pancreatic concretions can begin forming in utero (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis of general malabsorption, the relationship between cystic fibrosis and pancreatic insufficiency, and the morphology, serum markers, genetics, and potential complications (malignancy) of Celiac disease.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the most sensitive serologic test for celiac disease?","choices":{"A":"IgG antigliadin antibodies","B":"IgA anti-TG2 antibodies","C":"Anti-parietal cell antibodies","D":"Antimitochondrial antibodies","E":"Perinuclear ANCA antibodies"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The most sensitive noninvasive test for celiac disease is IgA against tissue transglutaminase 2. IgG anti-TG2 can be used in patients with IgA deficiency. IgA antibodies to tissue transglutaminase (TG2) are the most sensitive serologic test for celiac disease, ahead of antigliadin antibodies. Antibodies to parietal cell H+,K+-ATPase and intrinsic factor mark autoimmune atrophic gastritis, not celiac disease. Antimitochondrial antibody against PDC-E2 is the characteristic finding in primary biliary cholangitis, not celiac disease. p-ANCA is found in up to 75% of ulcerative colitis patients and is not a celiac test. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Celiac disease). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: celiac serology also includes anti-endomysial and anti-deamidated gliadin peptide antibodies (First Aid Gastrointestinal, celiac disease). COMLEX: celiac disease shows an abnormal d-xylose test (First Aid Gastrointestinal, celiac disease).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"IgA antibodies to tissue transglutaminase (TG2) are the most","C":"Antibodies to parietal cell H+,K+-ATPase and intrinsic facto","D":"Antimitochondrial antibody against PDC-E2 is the characteris","E":"p-ANCA is found in up to 75% of ulcerative colitis patients "}},{"id":"sdl29_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis of general malabsorption, the relationship between cystic fibrosis and pancreatic insufficiency, and the morphology, serum markers, genetics, and potential complications (malignancy) of Celiac disease.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the most common cancer in people with celiac disease?","choices":{"A":"Gastric diffuse large B-cell lymphoma tumor","B":"Distal esophageal Barrett adenocarcinoma","C":"Enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma","D":"Hepatocellular carcinoma of the liver","E":"Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The most common cancer in celiac disease is enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma, an aggressive lymphoma of intraepithelial T cells. Small intestinal adenocarcinoma is also more frequent. The most common cancer in celiac disease is a T-cell lymphoma of the intestine (EATL), not a gastric B-cell lymphoma. The cancers increased in celiac disease are enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma (most common) and small intestinal adenocarcinoma, not Barrett adenocarcinoma. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Celiac disease). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: celiac disease is associated with dermatitis herpetiformis and decreased bone density (First Aid Gastrointestinal, celiac disease). COMLEX: a gluten-free diet may reduce celiac complications including anemia, female infertility, and osteoporosis (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The most common cancer in celiac disease is a T-cell lymphom","B":"The cancers increased in celiac disease are enteropathy-asso"}},{"id":"sdl29_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the various malabsorption syndromes (EED, IPEX, lactose intolerance, abetalipoproteinemia) by their pathogenesis, associations, morphology, and genetic components.","batch":1,"stem":"Mutation of which gene causes IPEX?","choices":{"A":"CFTR","B":"MYO5B","C":"HLA-DQ2","D":"RET","E":"FOXP3"},"correct":"E","explanation":"IPEX (immune dysregulation, polyendocrinopathy, enteropathy, X-linkage) is caused by germline loss-of-function mutations in the X-linked FOXP3 gene. CFTR mutation causes cystic fibrosis, impairing chloride and bicarbonate transport and leading to pancreatic insufficiency. HLA-DQ2 is the susceptibility allele of celiac disease, present in about 90% of cases. MYO5B mutations cause microvillus inclusion disease, not IPEX. Loss-of-function RET mutations cause Hirschsprung disease by halting neural crest migration, not the Treg failure of IPEX. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Environmental enteric dysfunction; Autoimmune enteropathy; Lactase deficiency; Abetalipoproteinemia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: microvillus inclusion disease is caused by MYO5B mutations and presents with intractable diarrhea in early infancy (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: autoimmune enteropathy is treated with immunosuppressants such as cyclosporine or with stem cell transplantation (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — lipid absorption and chylomicron formation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"CFTR mutation causes cystic fibrosis, impairing chloride and","C":"HLA-DQ2 is the susceptibility allele of celiac disease, pres","B":"MYO5B mutations cause microvillus inclusion disease, not IPE","D":"Loss-of-function RET mutations cause Hirschsprung disease by"}},{"id":"sdl29_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the various malabsorption syndromes (EED, IPEX, lactose intolerance, abetalipoproteinemia) by their pathogenesis, associations, morphology, and genetic components.","batch":1,"stem":"Which defective molecule causes abetalipoproteinemia?","choices":{"A":"Cystic fibrosis transmembrane regulator","B":"Microsomal triglyceride transfer protein","C":"Sodium-glucose linked transporter protein","D":"Low-density lipoprotein receptor protein","E":"Apolipoprotein E lipid carrier protein"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Abetalipoproteinemia is an autosomal recessive defect in microsomal triglyceride transfer protein (MTP), which transfers lipids to nascent apolipoprotein B in the ER. CFTR dysfunction causes cystic fibrosis, whose malabsorption comes from pancreatic insufficiency rather than failed lipoprotein assembly. Abetalipoproteinemia results from loss of microsomal triglyceride transfer protein, which loads lipids onto apoB in the endoplasmic reticulum; the LDL receptor is not the defect. The defect in abetalipoproteinemia is microsomal triglyceride transfer protein, so apoB-containing lipoproteins are absent; apolipoprotein E is not the missing molecule. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Environmental enteric dysfunction; Autoimmune enteropathy; Lactase deficiency; Abetalipoproteinemia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: abetalipoproteinemia causes acanthocytes on the peripheral smear (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: abetalipoproteinemia presents in infancy with failure to thrive, diarrhea, and steatorrhea (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — lipid absorption and chylomicron formation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"CFTR dysfunction causes cystic fibrosis, whose malabsorption","D":"Abetalipoproteinemia results from loss of microsomal triglyc","E":"The defect in abetalipoproteinemia is microsomal triglycerid"}},{"id":"sdl29_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the various malabsorption syndromes (EED, IPEX, lactose intolerance, abetalipoproteinemia) by their pathogenesis, associations, morphology, and genetic components.","batch":1,"stem":"About how many children worldwide are estimated to be affected by the enteropathy linked to poor sanitation and chronic fecal pathogen exposure?","choices":{"A":"Over 150 million","B":"Around 1 million","C":"Around 10 million","D":"Around 25 million","E":"Around 50 million"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Environmental enteric dysfunction is estimated to affect more than 150 million children worldwide and may contribute to many childhood deaths. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Environmental enteric dysfunction; Autoimmune enteropathy; Lactase deficiency; Abetalipoproteinemia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: tropical sprue responds to antibiotics and is associated with folate and later B12 deficiency (First Aid Gastrointestinal, tropical sprue). COMLEX: high oral vaccine failure rates where EED is endemic suggest a mucosal immune defect (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — lipid absorption and chylomicron formation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four major causes of intestinal obstruction (hernias, adhesions, volvulus, and intussusception), including their associated demographics, etiologies, and location (sigmoid colon, intussusceptum).","batch":2,"stem":"A 14-month-old boy has intermittent screaming episodes with his legs drawn up and red currant-jelly stools after a recent viral illness. A sausage-shaped right-sided mass is felt. What most likely served as the leading edge?","choices":{"A":"Intraluminal adenocarcinoma","B":"Inspissated meconium plug","C":"Fibrous adhesion band","D":"Hyperplastic Peyer patches","E":"Incarcerated hernia sac"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Intussusception is the most common cause of obstruction under age 2. Idiopathic cases follow viral infection or rotavirus vaccine, probably because reactive Peyer patch hyperplasia forms the leading edge. An intraluminal tumor is the usual traction point in adults and older children, not in an infant after a viral illness. Inspissated stool acts as the traction point in about 1% of patients with cystic fibrosis, which this child lacks. Fibrous adhesion bands follow prior abdominal surgery or infection and are an adult cause of obstruction. An incarcerated hernia traps bowel in a sac through a wall defect and does not create a telescoping lead point. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Intestinal obstruction, Fig. 17.25); First Aid Gastrointestinal (intussusception). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in idiopathic intussusception of young children, contrast or air enemas are both diagnostic and therapeutic (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: Meckel diverticulum and bowel wall hematoma from IgA vasculitis are pediatric intussusception lead points (First Aid Gastrointestinal, intussusception).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"An intraluminal tumor is the usual traction point in adults ","B":"Inspissated stool acts as the traction point in about 1% of ","C":"Fibrous adhesion bands follow prior abdominal surgery or inf","E":"An incarcerated hernia traps bowel in a sac through a wall d"}},{"id":"sdl29_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four major causes of intestinal obstruction (hernias, adhesions, volvulus, and intussusception), including their associated demographics, etiologies, and location (sigmoid colon, intussusceptum).","batch":2,"stem":"A 54-year-old woman who had a hysterectomy years ago has abdominal pain, distention, and vomiting. She has no external hernia, and x-ray shows air-fluid levels. What is the most likely cause?","choices":{"A":"Twisting of the sigmoid colon","B":"Postoperative fibrous bands","C":"Ileocecal segment telescoping","D":"Inguinal sac herniation of bowel","E":"Gallstone lodged at the valve"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Surgery, infection, or endometriosis can produce fibrous adhesions that create closed loops. Other viscera slide through and become trapped as an internal hernia, with obstruction and strangulation. Sigmoid volvulus twists a redundant sigmoid loop, whereas prior hysterectomy points to adhesions forming closed loops. Ileocecal telescoping in adults usually needs a tumor as traction point, and prior pelvic surgery instead points to adhesions. Inguinal herniation is unlikely with no external hernia; adhesion bands instead cause internal herniation. After surgery, fibrous adhesions can form bridges that create closed loops and internal herniation, which fits obstruction years after a hysterectomy better than a gallstone. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adhesions); First Aid Gastrointestinal (small bowel obstruction). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: small bowel obstruction is managed with GI decompression, volume resuscitation, and bowel rest (First Aid Gastrointestinal, small bowel obstruction). COMLEX: adhesions can follow peritoneal inflammation such as endometriosis (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 2 — physiologic herniation, omphalocele, and gastroschisis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Sigmoid volvulus twists a redundant sigmoid loop, whereas pr","C":"Ileocecal telescoping in adults usually needs a tumor as tra","D":"Inguinal herniation is unlikely with no external hernia; adh","E":"After surgery, fibrous adhesions can form bridges that creat"}},{"id":"sdl29_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four major causes of intestinal obstruction (hernias, adhesions, volvulus, and intussusception), including their associated demographics, etiologies, and location (sigmoid colon, intussusceptum).","batch":2,"stem":"An 82-year-old man in a nursing home with chronic constipation has sudden abdominal distention. Abdominal x-ray shows a coffee-bean sign. What is the diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Midgut volvulus","B":"Cecal carcinoma","C":"Sigmoid volvulus","D":"Colonic ileus","E":"Ischemic colitis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Volvulus most often involves large redundant sigmoid loops, followed by the cecum. Sigmoid volvulus is more common in older adults and shows a coffee-bean sign. Midgut volvulus affects infants with malrotation, not an older adult with chronic constipation. Ischemic colitis presents with cramping left lower quadrant pain and bloody diarrhea, not a twisted loop on x-ray. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Volvulus); First Aid Gastrointestinal (volvulus). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastric volvulus in adults is more common with paraesophageal hernia (First Aid Gastrointestinal, volvulus). COMLEX: because it is rare, volvulus can be overlooked clinically with catastrophic results (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 1 — midgut rotation and malrotation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Midgut volvulus affects infants with malrotation, not an old","E":"Ischemic colitis presents with cramping left lower quadrant "}},{"id":"sdl29_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the cause, morphology, associated demographics, and clinical features of ischemic bowel diseases (acute mesenteric ischemia, colonic ischemia).","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old man with atrial fibrillation has sudden severe abdominal pain out of proportion to a soft abdomen on exam. What is the most likely cause?","choices":{"A":"Embolic occlusion of the SMA","B":"Thrombosis of a mesenteric vein","C":"Twisting of the sigmoid mesentery","D":"Vasculitis of small serosal arteries","E":"Rupture of a cecal vascular ectasia"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Acute mesenteric ischemia is a critical blockage of intestinal flow, often embolic SMA occlusion, with pain out of proportion to exam. Emboli commonly come from cardiac mural thrombi. Mesenteric venous thrombosis arises with hypercoagulable states, cirrhosis, or neoplasms, while atrial fibrillation favors arterial emboli. Sigmoid volvulus is a twisting obstruction of redundant sigmoid loops, not an embolic event linked to atrial fibrillation. Cecal vascular ectasia (angiodysplasia) presents with lower GI bleeding in older adults rather than sudden severe pain. Vasculitis is a less common cause of mesenteric arterial thrombosis, whereas atrial fibrillation supplies emboli, one of the two main causes of acute arterial obstruction. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Ischemic bowel disease); First Aid Gastrointestinal (acute mesenteric ischemia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: acute arterial infarction produces a sharp demarcation between normal and ischemic bowel (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: coagulative necrosis of the muscularis propria develops within 1 to 4 days of infarction (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut tube divisions and their blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Mesenteric venous thrombosis arises with hypercoagulable sta","C":"Sigmoid volvulus is a twisting obstruction of redundant sigm","E":"Cecal vascular ectasia (angiodysplasia) presents with lower ","D":"Vasculitis is a less common cause of mesenteric arterial thr"}},{"id":"sdl29_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the cause, morphology, associated demographics, and clinical features of ischemic bowel diseases (acute mesenteric ischemia, colonic ischemia).","batch":2,"stem":"A 76-year-old woman recovering from septic shock has crampy left lower abdominal pain, then bloody diarrhea. A colonic biopsy is taken. Which finding best supports ischemia?","choices":{"A":"Granulomas scattered in lamina propria","B":"Crypt abscesses in diffuse colitis","C":"Villous blunting with lymphocytes","D":"Upper mucosa lost but crypts spared","E":"Thickened subepithelial collagen"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Ischemic injury progresses from the lumen downward because crypts receive blood first. Atrophy or sloughing of the upper mucosa with normal or hyperproliferative crypts is the morphologic signature. Villous blunting with intraepithelial lymphocytes is the celiac pattern, not the ischemic pattern of surface loss with spared crypts. A thickened subepithelial collagen band (more than 10 µm) defines collagenous colitis, which presents with watery, not bloody, diarrhea. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Ischemic bowel disease). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colonic ischemia commonly occurs at the splenic flexure and rectosigmoid junction (First Aid Gastrointestinal, colonic ischemia). COMLEX: neutrophils are recruited into ischemic mucosa within hours of reperfusion (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — large-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Villous blunting with intraepithelial lymphocytes is the cel","E":"A thickened subepithelial collagen band (more than 10 µm) de"}},{"id":"sdl29_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the cause, morphology, associated demographics, and clinical features of ischemic bowel diseases (acute mesenteric ischemia, colonic ischemia).","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old man has ischemic colitis confined to the right colon. Why does this location carry a worse prognosis?","choices":{"A":"The right colon wall is especially thin","B":"The right colon lacks a submucosal layer","C":"The right colon has no collateral arcades","D":"Bacteria are far denser in the right colon","E":"Its artery also feeds the small intestine"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Mortality is doubled in right-sided colonic ischemia. The right colon is supplied by the superior mesenteric artery, which also supplies much of the small intestine, so right-sided disease may signal wider compromise. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Ischemic bowel disease). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic mesenteric ischemia most commonly involves the SMA (First Aid Gastrointestinal, chronic mesenteric ischemia). COMLEX: COPD and symptoms lasting more than 2 weeks are poor prognostic signs in colonic ischemia (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut tube divisions and their blood supply","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the manifestation (tortuous dilation of vessels), most common location (cecum, right-sided colon), associated age, diagnostic testing (angiography), and associated conditions (ESRD, von Willebrand disease, aortic stenosis) of angiodysplasia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old woman with aortic stenosis has recurrent painless hematochezia and iron deficiency. Colonoscopy finds small flat red mucosal lesions. What will histology most likely show?","choices":{"A":"Glands showing high-grade dysplasia","B":"Granulation tissue in an ulcer bed","C":"Ectatic nests of thin-walled vessels","D":"Mucosal outpouching through the muscle","E":"Noncaseating granulomas in the mucosa"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Angiodysplasia consists of ectatic nests of tortuous veins, venules, and capillaries, separated from the lumen only by a thin wall and attenuated epithelium. Minor injury makes them bleed. Mucosal outpouching through the muscle wall describes a diverticulum, whereas angiodysplasia shows ectatic nests of tortuous veins, venules, and capillaries. Angiodysplasia histology shows ectatic nests of tortuous thin-walled vessels; granulomas are not a feature. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Angiodysplasia); First Aid Gastrointestinal (angiodysplasia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: false diverticula occur where vasa recta penetrate the muscularis externa (First Aid Gastrointestinal, diverticula of the GI tract). COMLEX: angiodysplasia usually comes to clinical attention after the sixth decade (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Mucosal outpouching through the muscle wall describes a dive","E":"Angiodysplasia histology shows ectatic nests of tortuous thi"}},{"id":"sdl29_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the manifestation (tortuous dilation of vessels), most common location (cecum, right-sided colon), associated age, diagnostic testing (angiography), and associated conditions (ESRD, von Willebrand disease, aortic stenosis) of angiodysplasia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 79-year-old man has bleeding from colonic angiodysplasia. Which mechanical factor is thought to explain its typical location?","choices":{"A":"Lowest blood flow at a watershed zone","B":"Thinnest muscularis of any colonic part","C":"Highest bacterial count in the colon lumen","D":"Largest diameter gives most wall tension","E":"Most lymphoid tissue in the large bowel"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Because the cecum has the largest diameter of any colonic segment, it develops the greatest wall tension. This may explain the cecal and right-colon predominance of angiodysplasia. Watershed zones are the splenic flexure and rectosigmoid, which explain ischemic colitis sites rather than cecal angiodysplasia. The cecal location of angiodysplasia is explained by its having the largest diameter and so the greatest wall tension, not by a thinner muscularis. The proposed mechanism for angiodysplasia is mechanical: distention and contraction occlude submucosal veins, worst in the wide cecum. Bacterial load plays no part. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Angiodysplasia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: angiodysplasia in older patients is associated with aortic stenosis (First Aid Gastrointestinal, angiodysplasia). COMLEX: the pathogenesis of angiodysplasia has been attributed to mechanical and congenital factors (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 4 — colonic motility and haustral contractions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Watershed zones are the splenic flexure and rectosigmoid, wh","B":"The cecal location of angiodysplasia is explained by its hav","C":"The proposed mechanism for angiodysplasia is mechanical: dis"}},{"id":"sdl29_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the manifestation (tortuous dilation of vessels), most common location (cecum, right-sided colon), associated age, diagnostic testing (angiography), and associated conditions (ESRD, von Willebrand disease, aortic stenosis) of angiodysplasia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 71-year-old man has recurrent hematochezia from colonic angiodysplasia. Which of his conditions is classically associated with this lesion?","choices":{"A":"Type 2 diabetes mellitus","B":"End-stage renal disease","C":"Primary hypothyroidism","D":"Gouty arthritis history","E":"Chronic obstructive lung"},"correct":"B","explanation":"First Aid associates angiodysplasia with end-stage renal disease, von Willebrand disease, and aortic stenosis. Sources: First Aid Gastrointestinal (angiodysplasia); Robbins Ch. 17 (Angiodysplasia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: angiodysplasia is confirmed by angiography (First Aid Gastrointestinal, angiodysplasia). COMLEX: normal gut distention may intermittently occlude submucosal veins crossing the muscularis propria (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis of general malabsorption, the relationship between cystic fibrosis and pancreatic insufficiency, and the morphology, serum markers, genetics, and potential complications (malignancy) of Celiac disease.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman has bloating, iron deficiency anemia, and an itchy blistering rash on her elbows. A biopsy of the second part of the duodenum is taken. What will it most likely show?","choices":{"A":"Lymphocytosis with villous atrophy","B":"Vacuolated enterocytes filled with lipid","C":"Foamy macrophages packed in the lamina","D":"Transmural granulomas with deep fissuring","E":"Normal villi without inflammatory cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Celiac disease shows increased intraepithelial CD8+ T cells, crypt hyperplasia, and villous atrophy, best seen in the second part of the duodenum or proximal jejunum. Dermatitis herpetiformis occurs in up to 10% of patients. Lipid-vacuolated enterocytes are the abetalipoproteinemia pattern, from triglyceride that MTP defects leave trapped in cells. Normal villi fit lactase deficiency, a purely biochemical defect, not celiac disease with dermatitis herpetiformis. Lamina propria packed with PAS-positive foamy macrophages is the picture of Whipple disease, not celiac disease with dermatitis herpetiformis. Noncaseating granulomas in any layer of a transmurally inflamed bowel wall are a hallmark of Crohn disease, not celiac disease. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Celiac disease); First Aid Gastrointestinal (celiac disease). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: celiac disease is associated with HLA-DQ2 and HLA-DQ8 (First Aid Gastrointestinal, celiac disease). COMLEX: celiac disease in adults most commonly presents between ages 30 and 60 (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Lipid-vacuolated enterocytes are the abetalipoproteinemia pa","E":"Normal villi fit lactase deficiency, a purely biochemical de","C":"Lamina propria packed with PAS-positive foamy macrophages is","D":"Noncaseating granulomas in any layer of a transmurally infla"}},{"id":"sdl29_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis of general malabsorption, the relationship between cystic fibrosis and pancreatic insufficiency, and the morphology, serum markers, genetics, and potential complications (malignancy) of Celiac disease.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old man with chronic diarrhea has suspected gluten-sensitive enteropathy, and HLA-DQ typing is ordered. How is this genetic result most useful?","choices":{"A":"Positive result confirms the diagnosis","B":"Result predicts the lymphoma risk","C":"Result sets the diet duration needed","D":"Result replaces the need for biopsy","E":"Negative result argues against it"},"correct":"E","explanation":"HLA-DQ2 is present in about 90% of patients, with DQ8 in most of the rest. Their absence has negative predictive value, but their presence does not confirm the diagnosis. A positive HLA-DQ2 or DQ8 result does not confirm celiac disease, so it cannot replace serology and biopsy; its value is in a negative result. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Celiac disease). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: celiac concordance in monozygotic twins is about 75% (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: celiac disease is associated with type 1 diabetes, thyroiditis, Sjögren syndrome, and IgA nephropathy (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — small-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"A positive HLA-DQ2 or DQ8 result does not confirm celiac dis"}},{"id":"sdl29_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis of general malabsorption, the relationship between cystic fibrosis and pancreatic insufficiency, and the morphology, serum markers, genetics, and potential complications (malignancy) of Celiac disease.","batch":2,"stem":"A 7-year-old girl with cystic fibrosis has bulky greasy stools and poor weight gain. Which phase of nutrient absorption is primarily impaired?","choices":{"A":"Brush-border digestion","B":"Intraluminal digestion","C":"Transepithelial transport","D":"Lymphatic lipid transport","E":"Mucosal nutrient uptake"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cystic fibrosis causes exocrine pancreatic insufficiency, which impairs the intraluminal phase of absorption. Oral enzyme supplements treat it. Brush-border (terminal) digestion is the phase lost in lactase deficiency, not pancreatic insufficiency. Cystic fibrosis causes pancreatic exocrine insufficiency, which impairs the intraluminal digestion phase, not transepithelial transport. Lymphatic transport is the last absorptive phase, and it is impaired by lymphatic obstruction rather than by the pancreatic insufficiency of cystic fibrosis. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Cystic fibrosis); Robbins Ch. 17 (Malabsorption and diarrhea, Table 17.6). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic insufficiency gives a normal d-xylose test (First Aid Gastrointestinal, pancreatic insufficiency). COMLEX: malabsorption can disturb intraluminal digestion, terminal digestion, transepithelial transport, or lymphatic transport (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 2 — pancreatic bicarbonate secretion and CFTR","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Brush-border (terminal) digestion is the phase lost in lacta","C":"Cystic fibrosis causes pancreatic exocrine insufficiency, wh","D":"Lymphatic transport is the last absorptive phase, and it is "}},{"id":"sdl29_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the various malabsorption syndromes (EED, IPEX, lactose intolerance, abetalipoproteinemia) by their pathogenesis, associations, morphology, and genetic components.","batch":2,"stem":"A 6-month-old girl has steatorrhea and failure to thrive. Her blood smear shows acanthocytes, and plasma lacks apoB-containing lipoproteins. What will a small-bowel biopsy most likely show?","choices":{"A":"Lipid-vacuolated enterocytes","B":"Flattened villi with lymphocytes","C":"Neutrophils within the crypts","D":"PAS-positive foamy macrophages","E":"Absent ganglion cell clusters"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In abetalipoproteinemia, lipids are not assembled into lipoproteins and accumulate in enterocytes. The triglyceride shows as epithelial vacuolization, highlighted by oil red O after a fatty meal. Flattened villi with intraepithelial lymphocytes describe celiac disease, not failed lipoprotein assembly. Neutrophilic mucosal infiltration characterizes IPEX autoimmune enteropathy rather than abetalipoproteinemia. PAS-positive foamy macrophages are seen in Whipple disease, whereas abetalipoproteinemia shows enterocytes vacuolated by lipid. Absent submucosal and myenteric ganglion cells define Hirschsprung disease, which causes functional obstruction rather than lipid-laden enterocytes. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Environmental enteric dysfunction; Autoimmune enteropathy; Lactase deficiency; Abetalipoproteinemia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: failure to absorb essential fatty acids in abetalipoproteinemia causes fat-soluble vitamin deficiencies (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: abetalipoproteinemia is autosomal recessive (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — lipid absorption and chylomicron formation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Flattened villi with intraepithelial lymphocytes describe ce","C":"Neutrophilic mucosal infiltration characterizes IPEX autoimm","D":"PAS-positive foamy macrophages are seen in Whipple disease, ","E":"Absent submucosal and myenteric ganglion cells define Hirsch"}},{"id":"sdl29_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the various malabsorption syndromes (EED, IPEX, lactose intolerance, abetalipoproteinemia) by their pathogenesis, associations, morphology, and genetic components.","batch":2,"stem":"A 2-year-old boy has severe persistent diarrhea, type 1 diabetes, and eczema. His maternal uncle died in infancy with similar findings. Which cell type is defective?","choices":{"A":"Intraepithelial CD8+ T cells","B":"Mature B lymphocytes","C":"CD4+ regulatory T cells","D":"Intestinal goblet cells","E":"Small-bowel enterocytes"},"correct":"C","explanation":"IPEX is an X-linked autoimmune enteropathy in which loss of FOXP3, a transcription factor of CD4+ regulatory T cells, impairs their development and function. Neutrophils often infiltrate the mucosa, unlike celiac disease. Intraepithelial CD8+ T cells are the cytotoxic effectors that destroy enterocytes in celiac disease, not the FOXP3-dependent cells. Goblet cells are targets of the autoantibodies in IPEX, not the cells whose development fails. Enterocytes are attacked by autoantibodies in IPEX but are not the cell type that FOXP3 loss disables. IPEX is caused by mutations in FOXP3, a transcription factor needed by CD4+ regulatory T cells; B cells are not the defective population. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Environmental enteric dysfunction; Autoimmune enteropathy; Lactase deficiency; Abetalipoproteinemia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: autoantibodies to enterocytes and goblet cells are common in autoimmune enteropathy (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: intraepithelial lymphocytes in autoimmune enteropathy increase less than in celiac disease (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Intraepithelial CD8+ T cells are the cytotoxic effectors tha","D":"Goblet cells are targets of the autoantibodies in IPEX, not ","E":"Enterocytes are attacked by autoantibodies in IPEX but are n","B":"IPEX is caused by mutations in FOXP3, a transcription factor"}},{"id":"sdl29_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the various malabsorption syndromes (EED, IPEX, lactose intolerance, abetalipoproteinemia) by their pathogenesis, associations, morphology, and genetic components.","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-year-old girl from a rural community with poor sanitation in Zambia has stunted growth and malabsorption. No single pathogen is found, and antibiotics do not help. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Classic gluten-sensitive enteropathy","B":"Microvillus inclusion disease","C":"Congenital lipoprotein deficiency","D":"Inherited autoimmune enteropathy","E":"Environmental enteric dysfunction"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Environmental enteric dysfunction occurs where sanitation is poor. It likely reflects chronic fecal pathogen exposure and repeated early diarrhea, no single agent is implicated, and antibiotics and supplements do not reverse it. Gluten-sensitive enteropathy is HLA-DQ2/DQ8-linked and shows more advanced villous atrophy than environmental enteric dysfunction. Congenital lipoprotein deficiency (abetalipoproteinemia) is an autosomal recessive MTP defect with acanthocytes, not a sanitation-linked enteropathy. Inherited autoimmune enteropathy (IPEX) is an X-linked FOXP3 defect with polyendocrinopathy, not an environmental disorder. Microvillus inclusion disease is an inherited MYO5B disorder that starts before 3 months of age, unlike a sanitation-linked enteropathy in a 3-year-old. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Environmental enteric dysfunction; Autoimmune enteropathy; Lactase deficiency; Abetalipoproteinemia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: biopsies in environmental enteric dysfunction show more immune cell infiltration and less villous atrophy than advanced celiac disease (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: stunting from environmental enteric dysfunction may be accompanied by uncorrectable cognitive deficits (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 1 — intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Gluten-sensitive enteropathy is HLA-DQ2/DQ8-linked and shows","C":"Congenital lipoprotein deficiency (abetalipoproteinemia) is ","D":"Inherited autoimmune enteropathy (IPEX) is an X-linked FOXP3","B":"Microvillus inclusion disease is an inherited MYO5B disorder"}},{"id":"trial_sdl29_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four major causes of intestinal obstruction (hernias, adhesions, volvulus, and intussusception), including their associated demographics, etiologies, and location (sigmoid colon, intussusceptum).","batch":3,"stem":"A 45-year-old man has a painful, irreducible inguinal bulge. At the neck of the sac, what is compromised first?","choices":{"A":"Arterial inflow","B":"Lymph drainage","C":"Vagal supply","D":"Peritoneal fluid","E":"Venous drainage"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Pressure at the neck of a hernia sac first impairs venous drainage. Stasis and edema then cause incarceration, then arterial and venous compromise (strangulation) and infarction. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hernias, Fig. 17.26). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: inguinal hernias tend to have narrow orifices and large sacs (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: complicated hernias can present with tenderness, erythema, and fever (First Aid Gastrointestinal, hernias).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 2 — physiologic herniation, omphalocele, and gastroschisis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl29_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the cause, morphology, associated demographics, and clinical features of ischemic bowel diseases (acute mesenteric ischemia, colonic ischemia).","batch":3,"stem":"A 63-year-old woman has her SMA blood flow restored after mesenteric thrombosis. When does most bowel damage occur?","choices":{"A":"Before the clot","B":"During reperfusion","C":"At first hypoxia","D":"After the scarring","E":"During fasting"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ischemic injury occurs in two phases: initial hypoxic injury, then reperfusion, when blood supply returns and the greatest damage occurs. Bacterial products, free radicals, and neutrophils contribute. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Ischemic bowel disease). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: emboli causing mesenteric occlusion most often arise from aortic atheromas or cardiac mural thrombi (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: necrotizing enterocolitis can cause pneumatosis intestinalis and portal venous gas (First Aid Gastrointestinal, necrotizing enterocolitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut tube divisions and their blood supply","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl29_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the manifestation (tortuous dilation of vessels), most common location (cecum, right-sided colon), associated age, diagnostic testing (angiography), and associated conditions (ESRD, von Willebrand disease, aortic stenosis) of angiodysplasia.","batch":3,"stem":"A 77-year-old man has bleeding from colonic vascular ectasias. What process likely produced them?","choices":{"A":"Repeated vein occlusion","B":"Portal hypertension","C":"Inherited telangiectasia","D":"Radiation damage","E":"Embolic infarction"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Normal distention and contraction may intermittently occlude submucosal veins crossing the muscularis propria. This leads to focal dilation and tortuosity of overlying vessels. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Angiodysplasia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: angiodysplasia is associated with von Willebrand disease (First Aid Gastrointestinal, angiodysplasia). COMLEX: angiodysplastic vessels are vulnerable to disruption by even minor injury (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl29_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis of general malabsorption, the relationship between cystic fibrosis and pancreatic insufficiency, and the morphology, serum markers, genetics, and potential complications (malignancy) of Celiac disease.","batch":3,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman with celiac disease has new pain and weight loss despite a strict gluten-free diet. What should be suspected?","choices":{"A":"Lactose overload","B":"Dietary relapse","C":"Bile salt excess","D":"Refractory sprue","E":"Viral enteritis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Pain, diarrhea, and weight loss despite a strict gluten-free diet raise concern for refractory sprue, a possible precursor of T-cell lymphoma, or for cancer. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Celiac disease). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a gluten-free diet usually restores near-normal histology within 6 to 24 months (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: celiac disease primarily affects the distal duodenum and proximal jejunum (First Aid Gastrointestinal, celiac disease).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl29_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the various malabsorption syndromes (EED, IPEX, lactose intolerance, abetalipoproteinemia) by their pathogenesis, associations, morphology, and genetic components.","batch":3,"stem":"A 52-year-old Chinese man has bloating and flatulence after milk. What will a duodenal biopsy most likely show?","choices":{"A":"Flattened villi","B":"Lipid vacuoles","C":"Unremarkable mucosa","D":"Crypt abscesses","E":"Foamy macrophages"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Acquired lactose intolerance comes from age-related downregulation of gene expression. Because the defect is biochemical, histology is generally unremarkable. Flattened villi indicate the villous atrophy of celiac disease. Lipid vacuoles in enterocytes are the triglyceride accumulation of abetalipoproteinemia. Neutrophilic crypt abscesses are a feature of ulcerative colitis. Distended foamy macrophages in the lamina propria indicate Whipple disease. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Lactase deficiency). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the lactose hydrogen breath test is positive if breath hydrogen rises more than 20 ppm over baseline (First Aid Gastrointestinal, lactose intolerance). COMLEX: congenital lactose intolerance is a rare autosomal recessive disorder (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 1 — carbohydrate digestion by amylase and brush-border enzymes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Flattened villi indicate the villous atrophy of celiac disea","B":"Lipid vacuoles in enterocytes are the triglyceride accumulat","D":"Neutrophilic crypt abscesses are a feature of ulcerative col","E":"Distended foamy macrophages in the lamina propria indicate W"}},{"id":"trial_sdl29_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four major causes of intestinal obstruction (hernias, adhesions, volvulus, and intussusception), including their associated demographics, etiologies, and location (sigmoid colon, intussusceptum).","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman has intermittent obstruction from ileal intussusception. What most likely started it?","choices":{"A":"A mass lead point","B":"Bowel wall edema","C":"Recent rotavirus","D":"Midgut rotation","E":"Thin stool water"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In older children and adults, intussusception is usually caused by an intraluminal mass or tumor acting as the traction point, and surgery is needed. Lymphoid hyperplasia from viral infection or rotavirus vaccination underlies intussusception in young children, whereas adult cases arise from a mass lead point. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Intussusception). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: intussusception causes intermittent pain, vomiting, and bloody currant-jelly stools (First Aid Gastrointestinal, intussusception). COMLEX: up to 1% of patients with cystic fibrosis develop intussusception (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 2 — segmentation versus peristalsis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Lymphoid hyperplasia from viral infection or rotavirus vacci"}},{"id":"grid_sdl29_q01","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the various malabsorption syndromes (EED, IPEX, lactose intolerance, abetalipoproteinemia) by their pathogenesis, associations, morphology, and genetic components.","batch":4,"stem":"A 4-year-old boy who lives in a rural village with poor sanitation has chronic diarrhea, bulky greasy stools, and stunted growth, and he failed to respond to two doses of an oral vaccine. He has no family history of gastrointestinal disease and eats a mixed diet that includes wheat. Which of the following sets of findings is most likely in this patient?","grid":{"headers":["Fecal fat","Villus height","Anti-TG2 IgA","Serum apoB"]},"choices":{"A":["↑","↓","↑","↔"],"B":["↑","↔","↔","↔"],"C":["↑","↔","↔","↓"],"D":["↑","↓","↔","↔"],"E":["↔","↔","↔","↔"]},"correct":"D","explanation":"Choice D (↑ fecal fat, ↓ villus height, ↔ anti-TG2 IgA, ↔ serum apoB) is correct. Environmental enteric dysfunction (tropical sprue) follows chronic exposure to fecal pathogens where sanitation is poor; the notes list stunting and high oral vaccine failure rates, and describe pronounced lamina propria immune cell infiltration with villous atrophy that is less advanced than in celiac disease. Loss of absorptive surface causes malabsorption with steatorrhea, and the disorder is non-genetic and not driven by gluten, so celiac serology is negative. Choice A (↑ fecal fat, ↓ villus height, ↑ anti-TG2 IgA, ↔ serum apoB) is celiac disease, in which IgA anti-tissue transglutaminase is the most sensitive serum marker. Choice B (↑ fecal fat, ↔ villus height, ↔ anti-TG2 IgA, ↔ serum apoB) is exocrine pancreatic insufficiency, as in cystic fibrosis, where intraluminal digestion fails but the mucosa is intact. Choice C (↑ fecal fat, ↔ villus height, ↔ anti-TG2 IgA, ↓ serum apoB) is abetalipoproteinemia, in which absent apoB-containing lipoproteins leave enterocytes unable to export chylomicrons, causing steatorrhea in infancy with acanthocytes on the smear. Choice E (↔ fecal fat, ↔ villus height, ↔ anti-TG2 IgA, ↔ serum apoB) is lactase deficiency, an osmotic diarrhea from unabsorbed lactose without fat malabsorption. Sources: Exam 2 lecture notes (SDL 29, sections 4 and 5: general malabsorption, cystic fibrosis, celiac disease, EED, lactose intolerance, abetalipoproteinemia); Robbins Ch. 17 (malabsorption and diarrhea). The ↑ fecal fat for environmental enteric dysfunction is inferred from loss of absorptive surface, and intact villi in the pancreatic, abetalipoproteinemia, and lactase disorders are inferred because the sources describe no villous injury in them.","boardPrep":"Step 1: villous atrophy with negative celiac serology in a child from an area of poor sanitation points to environmental enteric dysfunction (Exam 2 notes, SDL 29). COMLEX: steatorrhea with normal villi suggests a luminal (pancreatic) or export (abetalipoproteinemia) defect rather than mucosal loss.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 2 — micelle formation and chylomicron export","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":30,"title":"SDL 30 — Review","questions":[]}]},{"examNumber":3,"sdls":[{"sdlNumber":31,"title":"SDL 31 — Pathology - Small Intestine & Colon: Infectious Enterocolitis","questions":[{"id":"sdl31_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing bacteria by their symptoms (bloody vs water diarrhea), reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, shape, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":1,"stem":"Which bacterium is the most common bacterial enteric pathogen in high-income countries?","choices":{"A":"Vibrio cholerae","B":"Salmonella typhi","C":"Yersinia enterocolitica","D":"Campylobacter jejuni","E":"Shigella dysenteriae"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Campylobacter jejuni is the most common bacterial enteric pathogen in high-resource countries and an important cause of traveler's diarrhea and food poisoning, most often from improperly cooked chicken. Vibrio cholerae is endemic in India and Africa, not the leading bacterial enteric pathogen in high-income countries. Salmonella typhi is endemic in countries such as India, Mexico, and the Philippines and mainly affects travelers, not high-income populations at large. Yersinia enterocolitica causes clustered winter cases in northern and central Europe but is far less common than Campylobacter. Shigella is highly endemic in under-resourced countries, whereas Campylobacter is the most common bacterial enteric pathogen in high-income countries. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Infectious enterocolitis, Table 17.7). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Campylobacter are comma-shaped, flagellated, gram-negative organisms diagnosed mainly by stool culture (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: Campylobacter infection can be followed by reactive arthritis, mainly in HLA-B27–positive patients (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Vibrio cholerae is endemic in India and Africa, not the lead","B":"Salmonella typhi is endemic in countries such as India, Mexi","C":"Yersinia enterocolitica causes clustered winter cases in nor","E":"Shigella is highly endemic in under-resourced countries, whe"}},{"id":"sdl31_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing bacteria by their symptoms (bloody vs water diarrhea), reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, shape, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":1,"stem":"What is the known reservoir of Shigella?","choices":{"A":"Poultry","B":"Humans","C":"Shellfish","D":"Cattle","E":"Swine"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Humans are the known reservoir of Shigella. Up to 10^9 organisms per gram of stool and an infective dose of several hundred organisms make fecal-oral spread by water and food very efficient. Poultry is a reservoir for Campylobacter and nontyphoid Salmonella, whereas humans are the only known Shigella reservoir. Shellfish and plankton are the reservoir of Vibrio cholerae, not Shigella. Cattle are the reservoir of EHEC O157:H7, whereas Shigella has no animal reservoir. Pigs are a reservoir for Yersinia and Campylobacter, but Shigella infects only humans. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Infectious enterocolitis, Table 17.7). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Shigella are gram-negative, unencapsulated, nonmotile facultative anaerobes closely related to enteroinvasive E. coli (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: in the United States, children in daycare, migrant workers, travelers, and nursing home residents are most affected (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Poultry is a reservoir for Campylobacter and nontyphoid Salm","C":"Shellfish and plankton are the reservoir of Vibrio cholerae,","D":"Cattle are the reservoir of EHEC O157:H7, whereas Shigella h","E":"Pigs are a reservoir for Yersinia and Campylobacter, but Shi"}},{"id":"sdl31_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing bacteria by their symptoms (bloody vs water diarrhea), reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, shape, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":1,"stem":"Which intestinal sites does Yersinia infection preferentially involve?","choices":{"A":"Rectum, sigmoid, and left colon","B":"Stomach, duodenum, and jejunum","C":"Ileum, appendix, and right colon","D":"Esophagus, stomach, and duodenum","E":"Jejunum, transverse colon, and rectum"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Yersinia infections preferentially involve the ileum, appendix, and right colon. Organisms proliferate in lymphoid tissue, causing Peyer patch and regional node hyperplasia. The rectum, sigmoid, and left colon are the favored sites of Shigella, not Yersinia, which targets the ileum, appendix, and right colon. Yersinia spares the stomach and proximal small bowel, localizing instead to the ileum, appendix, and right colon. The esophagus and stomach are not sites of Yersinia, which proliferates in lymphoid tissue of the ileum, appendix, and right colon. Yersinia does not favor the jejunum, transverse colon, or rectum; it localizes to the ileum, appendix, and right colon. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Infectious enterocolitis, Table 17.7). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Yersinia infections are linked to pork, raw milk, and contaminated water and tend to cluster in winter (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: Peyer patch and lymphatic involvement can mimic acute appendicitis in teenagers and young adults (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The rectum, sigmoid, and left colon are the favored sites of","B":"Yersinia spares the stomach and proximal small bowel, locali","D":"The esophagus and stomach are not sites of Yersinia, which p","E":"Yersinia does not favor the jejunum, transverse colon, or re"}},{"id":"sdl31_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing E. coli by their symptoms, reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":1,"stem":"Which E. coli group is the principal cause of traveler's diarrhea?","choices":{"A":"Enterohemorrhagic E. coli","B":"Enterotoxigenic E. coli","C":"Enteroinvasive E. coli","D":"Enteropathogenic E. coli","E":"Enteroaggregative E. coli"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Enterotoxigenic E. coli (ETEC) are the principal cause of traveler's diarrhea and spread via contaminated food or water. In under-resourced countries, children younger than 2 years are particularly susceptible. Enterohemorrhagic E. coli causes sporadic and outbreak bloody diarrhea from undercooked beef and HUS, not traveler's diarrhea. Enteroinvasive E. coli causes Shigella-like dysentery in young children in under-resourced countries, not traveler's diarrhea. Enteropathogenic E. coli causes endemic infant diarrhea and nursery outbreaks in children under 2, not traveler's diarrhea. Enteroaggregative E. coli causes prolonged nonbloody diarrhea via biofilm, but ETEC remains the principal cause of traveler's diarrhea. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Escherichia coli, Table 17.7). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ETEC are noninvasive and produce heat-labile and heat-stable toxins (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: like cholera, ETEC infection produces mild histologic changes (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — secretory diarrhea and cAMP-mediated electrolyte loss","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Enterohemorrhagic E. coli causes sporadic and outbreak blood","C":"Enteroinvasive E. coli causes Shigella-like dysentery in you","D":"Enteropathogenic E. coli causes endemic infant diarrhea and ","E":"Enteroaggregative E. coli causes prolonged nonbloody diarrhe"}},{"id":"sdl31_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing E. coli by their symptoms, reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":1,"stem":"Which E. coli group is bacteriologically similar to Shigella and invades epithelial cells without producing toxins?","choices":{"A":"Enteroinvasive E. coli","B":"Enterohemorrhagic E. coli","C":"Enterotoxigenic E. coli","D":"Enteropathogenic E. coli","E":"Enteroaggregative E. coli"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Enteroinvasive E. coli (EIEC) resemble Shigella bacteriologically. They do not produce toxins but invade epithelial cells and cause a nonspecific acute self-limited colitis. Enterohemorrhagic E. coli produces Shiga-like toxin and is not the toxin-free, Shigella-like invader. Enterotoxigenic E. coli is noninvasive and acts through heat-labile and heat-stable toxins. Enteropathogenic E. coli attaches and effaces microvilli without invading epithelial cells. Enteroaggregative E. coli forms biofilms on the mucosa rather than invading epithelial cells. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Escherichia coli, Table 17.7). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 31 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: EIEC infections are most common among young children in under-resourced countries (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: EIEC are transmitted via food, water, or person-to-person contact (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — secretory diarrhea and cAMP-mediated electrolyte loss","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"B":"Enterohemorrhagic E. coli produces Shiga-like toxin and is n","C":"Enterotoxigenic E. coli is noninvasive and acts through heat","D":"Enteropathogenic E. coli attaches and effaces microvilli wit","E":"Enteroaggregative E. coli forms biofilms on the mucosa rathe"}},{"id":"sdl31_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing E. coli by their symptoms, reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":1,"stem":"What does the heat-stable toxin of ETEC bind on enterocytes?","choices":{"A":"Adenylate cyclase","B":"GM1 ganglioside receptor","C":"Rho family GTPases","D":"Guanylate cyclase","E":"Beta-1 integrin receptors"},"correct":"D","explanation":"ETEC heat-stable toxin (ST) binds guanylate cyclase and raises intracellular cGMP; its effects on epithelial ion transport resemble those of the heat-labile toxin. Adenylate cyclase activation and raised cAMP is the action of the heat-labile toxin, not the heat-stable toxin. GM1 ganglioside is the binding receptor for cholera-like heat-labile toxin, whereas heat-stable toxin acts on guanylate cyclase. Rho GTPases are targets of C. difficile toxins, not ETEC heat-stable toxin, which raises cGMP via guanylate cyclase. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Escherichia coli, Table 17.7). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 31 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the ETEC heat-labile toxin resembles cholera toxin and activates adenylate cyclase, raising cAMP and chloride secretion (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: ETEC are noninvasive, so mucosal biopsies show mild change (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — secretory diarrhea and cAMP-mediated electrolyte loss","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"Adenylate cyclase activation and raised cAMP is the action o","B":"GM1 ganglioside is the binding receptor for cholera-like hea","C":"Rho GTPases are targets of C. difficile toxins, not ETEC hea"}},{"id":"sdl31_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the symptoms (CAN - cardiac, arthralgias, neurologic), PAS staining pattern (positive), demographic, and morphology (foamy macrophages) of Whipple disease.","batch":1,"stem":"What type of organism is Tropheryma whipplei?","choices":{"A":"Gram-negative curved spirochete","B":"Gram-positive actinomycete","C":"Acid-fast mycobacterial rod","D":"Flagellated intestinal protozoan","E":"Gram-negative comma-shaped rod"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Whipple's original postmortem found foamy macrophages filled with gram-positive microbes; the organism was identified as an actinomycete in 1992 and named Tropheryma whipplei. T. whipplei is not a spirochete; it is a gram-positive actinomycete. T. whipplei is a bacterium (actinomycete), not a flagellated protozoan like Giardia. A gram-negative comma-shaped rod describes Vibrio or Campylobacter, not the gram-positive actinomycete T. whipplei. The acid-fast stain separates the two: mycobacteria stain positively, while T. whipplei does not. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Whipple disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 31 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tropheryma whipplei is an intracellular gram-positive organism (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Whipple disease). COMLEX: intact rod-shaped bacilli can be seen within macrophages by electron microscopy (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — pathogenesis of general malabsorption","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"T. whipplei is not a spirochete; it is a gram-positive actin","D":"T. whipplei is a bacterium (actinomycete), not a flagellated","E":"A gram-negative comma-shaped rod describes Vibrio or Campylo","C":"The acid-fast stain separates the two: mycobacteria stain po"}},{"id":"sdl31_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the symptoms (CAN - cardiac, arthralgias, neurologic), PAS staining pattern (positive), demographic, and morphology (foamy macrophages) of Whipple disease.","batch":1,"stem":"Which stain result best separates Whipple disease from intestinal mycobacterial infection?","choices":{"A":"Positive mucicarmine stain","B":"Positive Gram stain result","C":"Negative acid-fast stain","D":"Positive Congo red stain","E":"Negative Giemsa stain result"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Both Whipple disease and intestinal mycobacterial infection show foamy macrophages with PAS-positive organisms. Mycobacteria are acid-fast positive, while T. whipplei is not. Mucicarmine highlights capsules such as Cryptococcus and does not separate Whipple disease from mycobacterial infection. Congo red stains amyloid, not intracellular bacteria, so it cannot distinguish Whipple disease from mycobacteria. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Whipple disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Whipple disease shows PAS-positive foamy macrophages in the intestinal lamina propria (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Whipple disease). COMLEX: mycobacterial infection can mimic Whipple disease, particularly in an immunocompromised host (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — pathogenesis of general malabsorption","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Mucicarmine highlights capsules such as Cryptococcus and doe","D":"Congo red stains amyloid, not intracellular bacteria, so it "}},{"id":"sdl31_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the symptoms (CAN - cardiac, arthralgias, neurologic), PAS staining pattern (positive), demographic, and morphology (foamy macrophages) of Whipple disease.","batch":1,"stem":"Which demographic group is Whipple disease most common in?","choices":{"A":"East Asian women","B":"West African children","C":"Native American teens","D":"South Asian older women","E":"Northern European men"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Whipple disease is most common in males of Northern European descent, particularly farmers and others with occupational exposure to soil or animals. Whipple disease is rare outside men of Northern European descent with soil or animal exposure, not East Asian women. Whipple disease affects mainly Northern European men, typically farmers, not West African children. Whipple disease is a disease of Northern European men with soil or animal exposure, not Native American teenagers. Whipple disease predominates in Northern European males, not South Asian older women. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Whipple disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Whipple disease is most common in older males (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Whipple disease). COMLEX: extraintestinal symptoms may precede malabsorption by months or years (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — pathogenesis of general malabsorption","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Whipple disease is rare outside men of Northern European des","B":"Whipple disease affects mainly Northern European men, typica","C":"Whipple disease is a disease of Northern European men with s","D":"Whipple disease predominates in Northern European males, not"}},{"id":"sdl31_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the symptoms, reservoir, transmission routes, and epidemiology of common viral causes of gastroenteritis (norovirus, rotavirus, adenovirus).","batch":1,"stem":"Which virus has become the most common cause of diarrhea worldwide as rotavirus vaccine use has grown?","choices":{"A":"Norovirus","B":"Rotavirus","C":"Adenovirus","D":"Astrovirus","E":"Sapovirus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"With increasing rotavirus vaccine use, norovirus has become the most common cause of diarrhea worldwide, responsible for about 20% of acute gastroenteritis. Rotavirus incidence has fallen with vaccination, which is exactly why norovirus now leads as the cause of diarrhea worldwide. Adenovirus causes endemic pediatric diarrhea and opportunistic infection but is not the leading cause worldwide. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Viral gastroenteritis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: norovirus is an icosahedral virus with a single-stranded RNA genome (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: infectious particles can be shed before symptoms and for up to 8 weeks afterward in healthy individuals (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Rotavirus incidence has fallen with vaccination, which is ex","C":"Adenovirus causes endemic pediatric diarrhea and opportunist"}},{"id":"sdl31_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the symptoms, reservoir, transmission routes, and epidemiology of common viral causes of gastroenteritis (norovirus, rotavirus, adenovirus).","batch":1,"stem":"Which age range is most vulnerable to rotavirus?","choices":{"A":"Birth to 6 months","B":"2 to 5 years","C":"5 to 12 years","D":"Over 65 years","E":"6 to 24 months"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Children between 6 and 24 months are most vulnerable to rotavirus, probably because breast-milk antibodies protect during the first 6 months of life. Infants under 6 months are relatively protected by breast-milk antibodies, so peak vulnerability starts at 6 months. By 2 to 5 years children are past the peak window of 6 to 24 months. School-age children are well beyond the 6 to 24 month peak of rotavirus vulnerability. Older adults are not the main rotavirus risk group, which is children aged 6 to 24 months. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Viral gastroenteritis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: rotavirus is an nonenveloped virus with a segmented, double-stranded RNA genome (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: the WHO recommends the first rotavirus vaccine dose at 6 weeks of age (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Infants under 6 months are relatively protected by breast-mi","B":"By 2 to 5 years children are past the peak window of 6 to 24","C":"School-age children are well beyond the 6 to 24 month peak o","D":"Older adults are not the main rotavirus risk group, which is"}},{"id":"sdl31_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the symptoms, reservoir, transmission routes, and epidemiology of common viral causes of gastroenteritis (norovirus, rotavirus, adenovirus).","batch":1,"stem":"What is the typical incubation period of adenovirus gastroenteritis?","choices":{"A":"About 12 hours","B":"About 2 days","C":"About 3 weeks","D":"About 1 week","E":"About 2 months"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Adenovirus disease typically presents after an incubation period of about 1 week, with diarrhea, vomiting, and abdominal pain; symptoms generally resolve within 10 days. About 12 hours is far shorter than adenovirus incubation, which is about 1 week. About 2 days is closer to norovirus timing; adenovirus incubates for about 1 week. Three weeks is too long; adenovirus gastroenteritis appears after about 1 week and resolves within 10 days. Two months is far too long; adenovirus incubates for about 1 week and symptoms resolve within 10 days. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Viral gastroenteritis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 31 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: adenovirus is a common cause of pediatric diarrhea that also affects older immunocompromised patients (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: fever and weight loss may accompany adenovirus gastroenteritis (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"About 12 hours is far shorter than adenovirus incubation, wh","B":"About 2 days is closer to norovirus timing; adenovirus incub","C":"Three weeks is too long; adenovirus gastroenteritis appears ","E":"Two months is far too long; adenovirus incubates for about 1"}},{"id":"sdl31_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated disease, transmission route, diagnostic method, and treatment of enterocolitis causing parasites (Giardia lamblia, Entamoeba histolytica, Cryptosporidium).","batch":1,"stem":"How is giardiasis usually documented?","choices":{"A":"Modified acid-fast stool stain","B":"Colonic biopsy for trophozoites","C":"Stool cyst immunofluorescence","D":"Serum antiparasite IgG titers","E":"Stool culture on selective agar"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Giardia infection is usually documented by immunofluorescent detection of cysts in stool samples. Oral antimicrobial therapy is effective, but recurrence is common. A modified acid-fast stain detects Cryptosporidium oocysts, not Giardia. Giardia trophozoites attach to the duodenal brush border, so colonic biopsy is not how giardiasis is documented. Giardia is a protozoan diagnosed by stool cyst immunofluorescence, not by bacterial culture on selective agar. Giardiasis is usually documented by immunofluorescent detection of cysts in stool samples, not by serology. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Parasitic enterocolitis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Giardia trophozoites in duodenal biopsies are pear-shaped with two equally sized nuclei (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: infants, travelers, and immunocompromised patients have increased risk of severe giardiasis (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A modified acid-fast stain detects Cryptosporidium oocysts, ","B":"Giardia trophozoites attach to the duodenal brush border, so","E":"Giardia is a protozoan diagnosed by stool cyst immunofluores","D":"Giardiasis is usually documented by immunofluorescent detect"}},{"id":"sdl31_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated disease, transmission route, diagnostic method, and treatment of enterocolitis causing parasites (Giardia lamblia, Entamoeba histolytica, Cryptosporidium).","batch":1,"stem":"Which drug is the most effective treatment for systemic amebiasis?","choices":{"A":"Oral vancomycin","B":"Albendazole","C":"Praziquantel","D":"Nitazoxanide","E":"Metronidazole"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Entamoeba histolytica lacks mitochondria and Krebs cycle enzymes and depends on fermentation. Metronidazole inhibits pyruvate oxidoreductase, an enzyme required for fermentation, and is the most effective treatment for systemic disease. Oral vancomycin treats C. difficile colitis, not amebiasis. Albendazole is an antihelminthic for worms, not a treatment for the protozoan Entamoeba. Praziquantel treats flukes and tapeworms, not the protozoan Entamoeba histolytica. Nitazoxanide is used for Cryptosporidium and Giardia, whereas metronidazole is the drug of choice for systemic amebiasis. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Parasitic enterocolitis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: E. histolytica cysts have a chitin wall that resists gastric acid (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: amebiasis most often affects the cecum and ascending colon (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Oral vancomycin treats C. difficile colitis, not amebiasis.","B":"Albendazole is an antihelminthic for worms, not a treatment ","C":"Praziquantel treats flukes and tapeworms, not the protozoan ","D":"Nitazoxanide is used for Cryptosporidium and Giardia, wherea"}},{"id":"sdl31_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated disease, transmission route, diagnostic method, and treatment of enterocolitis causing parasites (Giardia lamblia, Entamoeba histolytica, Cryptosporidium).","batch":1,"stem":"What property lets Cryptosporidium oocysts persist in treated but unfiltered water?","choices":{"A":"Chlorine resistance","B":"Heat resistance","C":"Chitin acid shield","D":"Surface antigen variation","E":"Bile acid tolerance"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cryptosporidium oocysts are resistant to chlorine and persist in treated but unfiltered water; contaminated drinking water is the most common means of transmission. A chitin wall gives Entamoeba cysts acid resistance, but Cryptosporidium persists in water because its oocysts resist chlorine. Surface antigen variation is how Giardia evades immunity, not how Cryptosporidium survives water treatment. Cryptosporidium oocysts persist in treated, unfiltered water because they resist chlorine; heat resistance does not explain this. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Parasitic enterocolitis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diagnosis of cryptosporidiosis is based on finding oocysts in the stool (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: the 1993 Milwaukee outbreak from inadequate water purification affected more than 400,000 people (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"A chitin wall gives Entamoeba cysts acid resistance, but Cry","D":"Surface antigen variation is how Giardia evades immunity, no","B":"Cryptosporidium oocysts persist in treated, unfiltered water"}},{"id":"sdl31_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing bacteria by their symptoms (bloody vs water diarrhea), reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, shape, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old man develops ascending flaccid leg weakness and absent reflexes 2 weeks after diarrhea from undercooked chicken. What process most likely links the two illnesses?","choices":{"A":"Molecular mimicry of gangliosides","B":"Direct invasion of nerve roots","C":"Toxin block of acetylcholine release","D":"Immune complexes in muscle fibers","E":"HLA-B27 linked joint inflammation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Guillain-Barré syndrome after Campylobacter infection reflects molecular mimicry: antibodies to C. jejuni lipopolysaccharide cross-react with peripheral and central nervous system gangliosides. It is not HLA-linked. Campylobacter does not invade nerve roots; Guillain-Barré follows antibody cross-reaction with gangliosides. Toxin block of acetylcholine release is botulism, which causes descending paralysis, not post-Campylobacter ascending weakness. Immune complexes in muscle do not explain areflexic ascending weakness, which is a ganglioside-mimicry neuropathy. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Infectious enterocolitis, Table 17.7). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: up to 40% of Guillain-Barré cases have documented Campylobacter infection in the prior 2 weeks (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: Campylobacter infection is associated with postinfectious irritable bowel syndrome (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Campylobacter does not invade nerve roots; Guillain-Barré fo","C":"Toxin block of acetylcholine release is botulism, which caus","D":"Immune complexes in muscle do not explain areflexic ascendin"}},{"id":"sdl31_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing bacteria by their symptoms (bloody vs water diarrhea), reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, shape, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman returns from the Philippines with fever, abdominal pain, and small salmon-colored macules on her chest. Blood cultures grow gram-negative rods. What will her terminal ileum most likely show?","choices":{"A":"Flask-shaped ulcers with a narrow neck","B":"Gray pseudomembranes with volcano lesions","C":"Transmural granulomas with fissures","D":"Raised Peyer patches with oval ulcers","E":"Flattened villi with lymphocytosis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Typhoid fever (S. typhi) invades small intestinal M cells. Peyer patches in the terminal ileum enlarge into plateau-like elevations, and mucosal damage creates oval ulcers oriented along the ileal axis that may perforate. Flask-shaped ulcers with a narrow neck are typical of Entamoeba histolytica in the colon, not typhoid. Gray pseudomembranes with volcano lesions are C. difficile colitis, not typhoid ileitis. Transmural granulomas with fissures suggest Crohn disease, not acute typhoid fever. Flattened villi with intraepithelial lymphocytosis is celiac disease, not Peyer patch hyperplasia from typhoid. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Infectious enterocolitis, Table 17.7) (Typhoid fever). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 31 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: rose spots on the chest and abdomen are a feature of typhoid fever (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: gallbladder colonization with S. typhi may be associated with gallstones and a chronic carrier state (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"Flask-shaped ulcers with a narrow neck are typical of Entamo","B":"Gray pseudomembranes with volcano lesions are C. difficile c","C":"Transmural granulomas with fissures suggest Crohn disease, n","E":"Flattened villi with intraepithelial lymphocytosis is celiac"}},{"id":"sdl31_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing bacteria by their symptoms (bloody vs water diarrhea), reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, shape, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":2,"stem":"A 5-year-old boy in daycare has fever, cramps, and bloody diarrhea, and stool culture grows Shigella sonnei. Which treatment should be avoided?","choices":{"A":"Oral rehydration","B":"Culture-guided antibiotics","C":"Antimotility agents","D":"Acetaminophen","E":"Intravenous saline"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Antidiarrheal medications can prolong symptoms and delay Shigella clearance, so they are contraindicated. Antibiotic treatment shortens both the clinical course and the duration of bacterial shedding. Oral rehydration is safe, standard supportive care for dysentery and should not be withheld. Culture-guided antibiotics shorten the course and shedding of Shigella, so they are appropriate. Intravenous saline is appropriate rehydration for a dehydrated child and is not contraindicated. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Infectious enterocolitis, Table 17.7) (Shigellosis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Shigella infections are most prominent in the left colon, and M-cell tropism can produce aphthous ulcers resembling Crohn disease (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: Shigella dysenteriae serotype 1 releases Shiga toxin, which inhibits protein synthesis (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 2 — antidiarrheal drug classes and their adverse effects","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Oral rehydration is safe, standard supportive care for dysen","B":"Culture-guided antibiotics shorten the course and shedding o","E":"Intravenous saline is appropriate rehydration for a dehydrat"}},{"id":"sdl31_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing E. coli by their symptoms, reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":2,"stem":"A 4-year-old girl has bloody diarrhea after a cookout with undercooked hamburgers. A week later she is pale, platelets are 40,000/µL (reference range 150,000–450,000/µL), and creatinine is rising. Which bacterial product most directly causes these complications?","choices":{"A":"Heat-labile toxin","B":"Heat-stable toxin","C":"Dispersin protein","D":"Shiga-like toxin","E":"Lipopolysaccharide"},"correct":"D","explanation":"EHEC O157:H7 from undercooked ground beef produces Shiga-like toxins and is more likely than non-O157 serotypes to cause bloody diarrhea, hemolytic uremic syndrome, and ischemic colitis. Heat-labile toxin is an ETEC product that raises cAMP and causes watery diarrhea, not HUS. Heat-stable toxin is an ETEC product that raises cGMP and causes watery diarrhea, not HUS. Dispersin is an enteroaggregative E. coli surface protein that aids adherence, whereas Shiga-like toxins of EHEC cause hemolytic uremic syndrome. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Escherichia coli, Table 17.7). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: EHEC lesions show an ischemia-like morphology with surface atrophy and erosion (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: antibiotic treatment can release Shiga-like toxins and raise hemolytic uremic syndrome risk, especially in children (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 2 — ischemic bowel disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Heat-labile toxin is an ETEC product that raises cAMP and ca","B":"Heat-stable toxin is an ETEC product that raises cGMP and ca","C":"Dispersin is an enteroaggregative E. coli surface protein th"}},{"id":"sdl31_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing E. coli by their symptoms, reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":2,"stem":"A 10-month-old boy in a crowded urban daycare has 5 days of watery diarrhea. Research biopsy shows bacteria tightly attached to enterocyte apical membranes with local effacement of microvilli. Which group is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Enteropathogenic E. coli","B":"Enterotoxigenic E. coli","C":"Enterohemorrhagic E. coli","D":"Enteroinvasive E. coli","E":"Enteroaggregative E. coli"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Enteropathogenic E. coli (EPEC) produce attaching and effacing lesions: bacteria attach tightly to small intestinal enterocyte apical membranes and cause local effacement of microvilli. EPEC are a major cause of diarrhea in children under 2 years. Enterotoxigenic E. coli acts through toxins without attaching and effacing microvilli. Enterohemorrhagic E. coli can form A/E lesions but affects the colon with bloody diarrhea, not small-intestinal watery diarrhea in a nursery. Enteroinvasive E. coli invades colonic epithelium and causes dysentery rather than attaching and effacing. Enteroaggregative E. coli forms biofilm in the colon rather than effacing small-intestinal microvilli. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Escherichia coli, Table 17.7). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: assays for the bacterial proteins that insert into enterocyte membranes allow molecular diagnosis of EPEC (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: EPEC are prevalent worldwide and cause endemic diarrhea and outbreaks (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — brush border of small intestinal villi","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Enterotoxigenic E. coli acts through toxins without attachin","C":"Enterohemorrhagic E. coli can form A/E lesions but affects t","D":"Enteroinvasive E. coli invades colonic epithelium and causes","E":"Enteroaggregative E. coli forms biofilm in the colon rather "}},{"id":"sdl31_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing E. coli by their symptoms, reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old man develops watery diarrhea 3 days after arriving in Mexico. Stool testing identifies an E. coli strain producing heat-labile and heat-stable toxins. What would a small-bowel biopsy most likely show?","choices":{"A":"Neutrophilic crypt abscesses","B":"Ischemic surface erosion","C":"Flask-shaped ulcers","D":"Foamy lamina macrophages","E":"Minimal mucosal changes"},"correct":"E","explanation":"ETEC are noninvasive and act through toxins that raise cAMP and cGMP. Like cholera, ETEC infection results in mild histologic changes. Neutrophilic crypt abscesses imply invasive colitis, but ETEC is noninvasive and leaves little histologic change. Ischemic surface erosion fits EHEC Shiga-like toxin injury, not toxin-mediated ETEC secretory diarrhea. Flask-shaped ulcers are Entamoeba histolytica in the colon, not ETEC. Foamy lamina propria macrophages are Whipple disease, not ETEC. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Escherichia coli, Table 17.7). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the ETEC heat-labile toxin activates adenylate cyclase, while the heat-stable toxin raises cGMP (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: ETEC spread through contaminated food or water (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — secretory diarrhea and cAMP-mediated electrolyte loss","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Neutrophilic crypt abscesses imply invasive colitis, but ETE","B":"Ischemic surface erosion fits EHEC Shiga-like toxin injury, ","C":"Flask-shaped ulcers are Entamoeba histolytica in the colon, ","D":"Foamy lamina propria macrophages are Whipple disease, not ET"}},{"id":"sdl31_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the symptoms (CAN - cardiac, arthralgias, neurologic), PAS staining pattern (positive), demographic, and morphology (foamy macrophages) of Whipple disease.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man who farms in rural Wisconsin has diarrhea, weight loss, joint pain, and memory problems. Duodenal biopsy shows lamina propria packed with distended foamy macrophages. What will special staining most likely show?","choices":{"A":"Acid-fast bacilli in macrophages","B":"PAS-positive lysosomal granules","C":"Congo red apple-green deposits","D":"Prussian blue iron granules","E":"Mucicarmine-staining capsules"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The macrophages of Whipple disease contain PAS-positive, diastase-resistant granules representing partially digested bacteria within lysosomes. T. whipplei is acid-fast negative, unlike mycobacteria. Congo red apple-green birefringence identifies amyloid, not the bacteria-laden macrophages of Whipple disease. Prussian blue stains iron, such as in hemochromatosis, not T. whipplei within macrophages. Mucicarmine stains capsules such as Cryptococcus, whereas Whipple macrophages are PAS-positive. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Whipple disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cardiac symptoms, arthralgias, and neurologic symptoms are common in Whipple disease (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Whipple disease). COMLEX: the usual triad is diarrhea, weight loss, and arthralgia (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — pathogenesis of general malabsorption","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Congo red apple-green birefringence identifies amyloid, not ","D":"Prussian blue stains iron, such as in hemochromatosis, not T","E":"Mucicarmine stains capsules such as Cryptococcus, whereas Wh"}},{"id":"sdl31_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the symptoms (CAN - cardiac, arthralgias, neurologic), PAS staining pattern (positive), demographic, and morphology (foamy macrophages) of Whipple disease.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man working on a dairy farm has steatorrhea, mesenteric lymphadenopathy, and arthralgias. Duodenal biopsy shows lamina propria filled with foamy macrophages, and tissue PCR detects Tropheryma whipplei. What best explains his malabsorption?","choices":{"A":"Impaired lymph drainage","B":"Brush-border enzyme loss","C":"Pancreatic enzyme deficiency","D":"Bile salt deconjugation","E":"Toxin-driven chloride secretion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In Whipple disease, bacteria-laden macrophages accumulate in the small intestinal lamina propria and mesenteric lymph nodes and obstruct lymphatics. The malabsorptive diarrhea results from impaired lymph drainage. Brush-border enzyme loss fits celiac disease or viral villous damage, but Whipple malabsorption comes from lymphatic obstruction. Pancreatic enzyme deficiency causes maldigestion in pancreatic disease, not in Whipple disease. Bile salt deconjugation results from bacterial overgrowth, not from macrophage-mediated lymphatic obstruction. Toxin-driven chloride secretion is the mechanism of cholera or ETEC, not Whipple disease. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Whipple disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diarrhea and steatorrhea occur later in the course of Whipple disease (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Whipple disease). COMLEX: the rarity of infection despite many healthy carriers suggests genetic risk factors (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — lipid absorption and chylomicron formation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Brush-border enzyme loss fits celiac disease or viral villou","C":"Pancreatic enzyme deficiency causes maldigestion in pancreat","D":"Bile salt deconjugation results from bacterial overgrowth, n","E":"Toxin-driven chloride secretion is the mechanism of cholera "}},{"id":"sdl31_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the symptoms (CAN - cardiac, arthralgias, neurologic), PAS staining pattern (positive), demographic, and morphology (foamy macrophages) of Whipple disease.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old man has had migratory joint pain for 2 years and now has diarrhea, weight loss, and a new heart murmur. Biopsy shows foamy macrophages expanding duodenal villi. What will endoscopy most likely show?","choices":{"A":"Gray adherent pseudomembranes","B":"Cobblestoning with skip lesions","C":"Yellow-white mucosal plaques","D":"Flask-shaped undermined ulcers","E":"Scalloped duodenal folds"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Dense macrophage infiltrates expand villi and give a shaggy surface. Lymphatic dilation and mucosal lipid deposition produce endoscopically visible white to yellow plaques. Macrophages can also accumulate in cardiac valves, synovium, and brain. Gray adherent pseudomembranes are C. difficile colitis, not Whipple disease. Cobblestoning with skip lesions is Crohn disease, not Whipple disease. Flask-shaped undermined ulcers are Entamoeba histolytica, not Whipple disease. Scalloped duodenal folds suggest celiac disease, whereas Whipple disease shows yellow-white plaques. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Whipple disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the shaggy mucosal surface reflects villous expansion by dense macrophage infiltrates (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: extraintestinal symptoms such as arthritis may precede malabsorption by months or years (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Gray adherent pseudomembranes are C. difficile colitis, not ","B":"Cobblestoning with skip lesions is Crohn disease, not Whippl","D":"Flask-shaped undermined ulcers are Entamoeba histolytica, no","E":"Scalloped duodenal folds suggest celiac disease, whereas Whi"}},{"id":"sdl31_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the symptoms, reservoir, transmission routes, and epidemiology of common viral causes of gastroenteritis (norovirus, rotavirus, adenovirus).","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old woman on a cruise ship develops vomiting and watery diarrhea during a shipwide outbreak and recovers in 2 days. Her 70-year-old husband, equally exposed, never becomes ill, and his saliva lacks blood group antigens. Which trait most likely protects him?","choices":{"A":"HLA-B27 positive genotype","B":"Heterozygous CFTR mutation","C":"Sickle hemoglobin trait carrier","D":"HLA-DQ2 haplotype expression","E":"Homozygous FUT2 inactivation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Mutations inactivating FUT2 prevent fucosylation of blood group antigens that serve as norovirus binding sites. These homozygous nonsecretors are naturally resistant; about 20% of people of European and African descent carry this genotype. HLA-B27 predisposes to reactive arthritis after Shigella and other enteric infections, not norovirus resistance. Sickle cell trait is linked to Salmonella osteomyelitis risk, not norovirus resistance. HLA-DQ2 is the celiac disease susceptibility haplotype, not a norovirus resistance factor. Natural resistance to norovirus comes from homozygous FUT2 inactivation (nonsecretor status), which removes the blood group antigens the virus binds; CFTR heterozygosity does not do this. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Viral gastroenteritis) (Norovirus). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: norovirus outbreaks spread in schools, hospitals, nursing homes, and cruise ships through droplets, surfaces, and fomites (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: an infective dose of fewer than 20 viral particles makes prevention difficult (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 3 — small-intestine epithelial cell types","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"HLA-B27 predisposes to reactive arthritis after Shigella and","C":"Sickle cell trait is linked to Salmonella osteomyelitis risk","D":"HLA-DQ2 is the celiac disease susceptibility haplotype, not ","B":"Natural resistance to norovirus comes from homozygous FUT2 i"}},{"id":"sdl31_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the symptoms, reservoir, transmission routes, and epidemiology of common viral causes of gastroenteritis (norovirus, rotavirus, adenovirus).","batch":2,"stem":"A 14-month-old unvaccinated boy has vomiting and watery diarrhea during a daycare outbreak. The virus has a segmented, double-stranded RNA genome. Which viral factor may mediate the enterocyte damage?","choices":{"A":"Hemagglutinin-neuraminidase","B":"Nonstructural protein 4","C":"Reverse transcriptase","D":"Spike glycoprotein","E":"Envelope fusion protein"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Rotavirus selectively infects and destroys mature small intestinal enterocytes; damage may be mediated by nonstructural protein 4 (NSP4), which can induce apoptosis. Immature cells then repopulate the villus surface. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Viral gastroenteritis) (Rotavirus). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 31 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the minimal infective inoculum of rotavirus is about 10 viral particles (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: rotavirus vaccines are incompletely effective in under-resourced regions, possibly due to malnutrition (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — small-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins"},{"id":"sdl31_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the symptoms, reservoir, transmission routes, and epidemiology of common viral causes of gastroenteritis (norovirus, rotavirus, adenovirus).","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man on immunosuppression after a heart transplant has 6 days of diarrhea, vomiting, and fever. Stool PCR detects adenovirus. What would a small intestinal biopsy most likely show?","choices":{"A":"Prominent intranuclear viral inclusions","B":"Foamy macrophages in lamina propria","C":"Villous atrophy with crypt hyperplasia","D":"Flask-shaped ulcers with trophozoites","E":"Transmural granulomas with fissures"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Adenovirus biopsies can show epithelial degeneration but more often show nonspecific villous atrophy and compensatory crypt hyperplasia. Viral nuclear inclusions are uncommon. Foamy lamina propria macrophages are Whipple disease, not adenovirus enteritis. Flask-shaped ulcers with trophozoites are amebiasis, not adenovirus. Transmural granulomas with fissures are Crohn disease, not adenovirus. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Viral gastroenteritis) (Adenovirus). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: adenovirus is a common cause of pediatric diarrhea (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: adenovirus symptoms generally resolve within 10 days (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — villi and microvilli as absorptive surface area","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Foamy lamina propria macrophages are Whipple disease, not ad","D":"Flask-shaped ulcers with trophozoites are amebiasis, not ade","E":"Transmural granulomas with fissures are Crohn disease, not a"}},{"id":"sdl31_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated disease, transmission route, diagnostic method, and treatment of enterocolitis causing parasites (Giardia lamblia, Entamoeba histolytica, Cryptosporidium).","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man from Mexico had bloody diarrhea a month ago and now has fever and right upper quadrant pain. CT shows a 9-cm liver abscess, and colon biopsy showed organisms ingesting red cells. How did the parasite most likely reach the liver?","choices":{"A":"Ascent through bile ducts","B":"Lymphatic spread to liver","C":"Direct transmural extension","D":"Portal venous embolization","E":"Hepatic artery seeding"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Entamoeba histolytica invades crypts and burrows into the lamina propria, forming flask-shaped ulcers. Parasites penetrate splanchnic vessels and embolize to the liver, producing abscesses in about 40% of patients with amebic dysentery. Amebae do not ascend the bile ducts; they invade colonic splanchnic vessels and embolize through the portal vein. Entamoeba reaches the liver through the portal venous system, not lymphatics. Direct transmural extension from the colon does not explain a liver abscess, which arises from portal embolization. Colonic venous blood drains to the liver through the portal vein, not the hepatic artery. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Parasitic enterocolitis) (Entamoeba histolytica). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: amebic liver abscesses can exceed 10 cm and have a shaggy fibrinous lining with scant marginal inflammation (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: metronidazole is the most effective treatment for systemic amebiasis (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 3 — ascending cholangitis, liver abscess, and sepsis in bacterial liver infections","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Amebae do not ascend the bile ducts; they invade colonic spl","B":"Entamoeba reaches the liver through the portal venous system","C":"Direct transmural extension from the colon does not explain ","E":"Colonic venous blood drains to the liver through the portal "}},{"id":"sdl31_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated disease, transmission route, diagnostic method, and treatment of enterocolitis causing parasites (Giardia lamblia, Entamoeba histolytica, Cryptosporidium).","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman who drank stream water while camping has had intermittent bloating and greasy diarrhea for 4 months. Duodenal biopsy shows pear-shaped organisms with two nuclei on the brush border. Which parasite feature best explains her months of symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Invasion into the lamina propria","B":"Chitin wall resisting acid","C":"Intracellular vacuole residence","D":"Red cell ingestion by trophozoites","E":"Variant surface protein switching"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Giardia evade immune clearance through continuous modification of their major surface antigen, variant surface protein, and can persist for months or years with intermittent symptoms. They do not invade. Giardia does not invade the lamina propria; it stays attached to the brush border. A chitin acid-resistant wall describes Entamoeba cysts and does not explain prolonged Giardia infection. Intracellular vacuole residence describes Cryptosporidium, not Giardia. Red cell ingestion by trophozoites is diagnostic of Entamoeba histolytica, not Giardia. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Parasitic enterocolitis) (Giardia lamblia). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 31 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Giardia decrease brush-border enzyme expression and cause microvillous damage and epithelial apoptosis (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: Giardia cysts resist chlorine, so infection is endemic in unfiltered public water supplies (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 1 — carbohydrate digestion by amylase and brush-border enzymes","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"Giardia does not invade the lamina propria; it stays attache","B":"A chitin acid-resistant wall describes Entamoeba cysts and d","C":"Intracellular vacuole residence describes Cryptosporidium, n","D":"Red cell ingestion by trophozoites is diagnostic of Entamoeb"}},{"id":"sdl31_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated disease, transmission route, diagnostic method, and treatment of enterocolitis causing parasites (Giardia lamblia, Entamoeba histolytica, Cryptosporidium).","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old man with advanced HIV has weeks of profuse watery diarrhea. Ileal biopsy shows small blue spheres that appear to sit on the brush border, and stool shows oocysts. Where does the organism actually reside?","choices":{"A":"Free in lumen over villi","B":"Vacuole within enterocytes","C":"Inside lamina propria macrophages","D":"Within submucosal lymphatics","E":"Inside flask-shaped ulcers"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cryptosporidium sporozoites attach to the brush border and induce enterocytes to engulf them into an endocytic vacuole, so the intracellular parasite appears to sit on the apical membrane. Sodium malabsorption, chloride secretion, and tight junction leak cause watery diarrhea. Cryptosporidium is not free in the lumen; it lies within an endocytic vacuole of the enterocyte. Organisms inside lamina propria macrophages describe Whipple disease or MAC, not Cryptosporidium. Flask-shaped ulcers belong to Entamoeba histolytica, not Cryptosporidium. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Parasitic enterocolitis) (Cryptosporidium). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 31 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cryptosporidium causes sodium malabsorption, chloride secretion, and increased tight junction permeability, producing watery diarrhea (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: in immunodeficient hosts, organisms can involve the biliary and respiratory tracts (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 1 — intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"Cryptosporidium is not free in the lumen; it lies within an ","C":"Organisms inside lamina propria macrophages describe Whipple","E":"Flask-shaped ulcers belong to Entamoeba histolytica, not Cry"}},{"id":"trial_sdl31_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing bacteria by their symptoms (bloody vs water diarrhea), reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, shape, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":3,"stem":"An 18-year-old man has fever and right lower quadrant pain after eating undercooked pork in Germany. At surgery the appendix is mildly inflamed and mesenteric nodes are enlarged. Which organism is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Yersinia enterocolitica","B":"Campylobacter jejuni","C":"Shigella sonnei","D":"Vibrio cholerae","E":"Salmonella enteritidis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Yersinia infections are linked to pork, raw milk, and water, are more common in Europe, and involve the ileum, appendix, and right colon. Peyer patch and lymphatic involvement can mimic acute appendicitis in teenagers and young adults. Campylobacter is classically acquired from poultry and causes colitis, not pork-associated pseudoappendicitis. Shigella has a human reservoir and mainly involves the left colon. Vibrio cholerae is linked to shellfish and endemic regions such as India and Africa. Salmonella enteritidis is classically acquired from poultry and eggs. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Yersinia, Table 17.7). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: iron enhances Yersinia virulence, so patients with hemochromatosis are at greater risk of sepsis (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: postinfectious Yersinia complications include reactive arthritis and erythema nodosum (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Campylobacter is classically acquired from poultry and cause","C":"Shigella has a human reservoir and mainly involves the left ","D":"Vibrio cholerae is linked to shellfish and endemic regions s","E":"Salmonella enteritidis is classically acquired from poultry "}},{"id":"trial_sdl31_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing E. coli by their symptoms, reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":3,"stem":"A 5-year-old girl has bloody diarrhea 3 days after a visit to a dairy farm, and stool grows E. coli O157:H7. Why should antibiotics generally be withheld?","choices":{"A":"Prolongs the asymptomatic carrier state","B":"Raises hemolytic uremic syndrome risk","C":"Selects for pseudomembranous colitis","D":"Masks a developing appendicitis","E":"Triggers a reactive arthritis flare"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Antibiotic treatment of EHEC can lead to release of Shiga-like toxins and increased risk of hemolytic uremic syndrome, especially in children. Pseudomembranous colitis is caused by C. difficile after broad-spectrum antibiotics, not the reason to withhold antibiotics in EHEC. Reactive arthritis is a classic sequela of Shigella infection. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Escherichia coli). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: E. coli O157:H7 outbreaks often come from inadequately cooked ground beef, reflecting a reservoir in cows (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: contaminated milk and vegetables are also vehicles for EHEC (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 2 — ischemic bowel disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Pseudomembranous colitis is caused by C. difficile after bro","E":"Reactive arthritis is a classic sequela of Shigella infectio"}},{"id":"trial_sdl31_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the symptoms (CAN - cardiac, arthralgias, neurologic), PAS staining pattern (positive), demographic, and morphology (foamy macrophages) of Whipple disease.","batch":3,"stem":"A 52-year-old man has diarrhea, weight loss, and arthralgias, and duodenal biopsy shows lamina propria packed with distended foamy macrophages. Besides the gut and lymph nodes, where else can these macrophages accumulate?","choices":{"A":"Skeletal muscle","B":"Thyroid gland","C":"Cardiac valves","D":"Renal glomeruli","E":"Adrenal cortex"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In Whipple disease, bacteria-laden macrophages can accumulate within mesenteric lymph nodes, synovial membranes, cardiac valves, brain, and other sites. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Whipple disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the original Whipple case described malabsorption, lymphadenopathy, and arthritis of undefined origin (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: white to yellow endoscopic plaques reflect lymphatic dilation and mucosal lipid deposition (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl31_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the symptoms, reservoir, transmission routes, and epidemiology of common viral causes of gastroenteritis (norovirus, rotavirus, adenovirus).","batch":3,"stem":"A 16-month-old girl develops vomiting and watery diarrhea from rotavirus, but her exclusively breastfed 3-month-old brother stays well. What most likely protects the infant?","choices":{"A":"Immature villus receptors","B":"Higher gastric acidity","C":"Lower infective inoculum","D":"Faster enterocyte turnover","E":"Antibodies in breast milk"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Children between 6 and 24 months are most vulnerable to rotavirus, probably because antibodies in breast milk confer protection during the first 6 months of life. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Rotavirus). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 31 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: rotavirus outbreaks in hospitals and daycare centers are common (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: rotavirus has a short incubation period followed by several days of vomiting and watery diarrhea (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins"},{"id":"trial_sdl31_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated disease, transmission route, diagnostic method, and treatment of enterocolitis causing parasites (Giardia lamblia, Entamoeba histolytica, Cryptosporidium).","batch":3,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman from Colombia has bloody diarrhea, and colon biopsy shows amoebas that resemble macrophages. How does the first-line drug for systemic disease act on this parasite?","choices":{"A":"Inhibits bacterial cell wall synthesis","B":"Binds 50S ribosomal subunits","C":"Blocks DNA gyrase activity","D":"Inhibits pyruvate oxidoreductase","E":"Disrupts ergosterol synthesis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Entamoeba histolytica lacks mitochondria and Krebs cycle enzymes and depends on glucose fermentation. Metronidazole inhibits pyruvate oxidoreductase, an enzyme required for fermentation. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Entamoeba histolytica). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: amebic dysentery forms flask-shaped ulcers with a narrow neck and broad base (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: liver abscesses develop in about 40% of patients with amebic dysentery (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 3 — ascending cholangitis, liver abscess, and sepsis in bacterial liver infections","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl31_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing bacteria by their symptoms (bloody vs water diarrhea), reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, shape, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":3,"stem":"A 33-year-old woman has fever and diarrhea after a potluck egg salad, and stool culture grows nontyphoidal Salmonella. She is otherwise healthy. Why are antibiotics generally not recommended?","choices":{"A":"They can prolong the carrier state","B":"They suppress mucosal IgA secretion","C":"They trigger Guillain-Barré syndrome","D":"They cause a rebound toxin release","E":"They worsen secretory chloride loss"},"correct":"A","explanation":"For nontyphoidal Salmonella, antibiotic therapy is not generally recommended because it can prolong the carrier state or cause relapse and does not shorten diarrhea. Guillain-Barré syndrome follows Campylobacter infection rather than antibiotic treatment of Salmonella. Antibiotic-triggered release of Shiga-like toxin is the concern in EHEC infection, not nontyphoidal Salmonella. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Salmonella). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 31 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: absent gastric acid, as in atrophic gastritis or acid suppression, lowers the Salmonella inoculum needed (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: Salmonella infection is associated with postinfectious irritable bowel syndrome (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"C":"Guillain-Barré syndrome follows Campylobacter infection rath","D":"Antibiotic-triggered release of Shiga-like toxin is the conc"}},{"id":"grid_sdl31_q01","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing E. coli by their symptoms, reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":4,"stem":"A 6-year-old girl has 3 days of crampy abdominal pain and frequent stools that began 2 days after she ate a hamburger at a county fair. Temperature is 37.2°C (99.0°F). Five days later she is pale and has petechiae and decreased urine output; a peripheral smear shows schistocytes, and the platelet count is 42,000/mm³ (reference range: 150,000–400,000/mm³). Which of the following sets of features best characterizes the organism causing this patient's illness?","grid":{"headers":["Bloody diarrhea","Epithelial invasion","Main site"]},"choices":{"A":["Absent","Present","Small bowel"],"B":["Present","Absent","Colon"],"C":["Absent","Absent","Small bowel"],"D":["Present","Present","Small bowel"],"E":["Absent","Absent","Colon"]},"correct":"B","explanation":"Choice B (bloody diarrhea present, epithelial invasion absent, colon) is correct. Enterohemorrhagic E. coli O157:H7 comes from cattle and is spread by undercooked ground beef; the notes list severe bloody diarrhea, localization to the colon, and hemolytic uremic syndrome as its key complication. It injures the colon through Shiga-like toxin rather than by invading the epithelium. Choice A (bloody diarrhea absent, epithelial invasion present, small bowel) is Yersinia enterocolitica, which invades the ileum and its lymphoid tissue and causes fever, pain, and diarrhea with mesenteric adenitis that can mimic appendicitis. Choice C (bloody diarrhea absent, epithelial invasion absent, small bowel) is Vibrio cholerae, which stays in the small-intestinal lumen and causes rice-water stool. Choice D (bloody diarrhea present, epithelial invasion present, small bowel) is Salmonella typhi, which invades Peyer patches in the terminal ileum and causes enteric fever with rose spots. Choice E (bloody diarrhea absent, epithelial invasion absent, colon) is Clostridioides difficile, which causes toxin-mediated watery diarrhea after antibiotics. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 31, sections 1 and 2: gastroenteritis-causing bacteria and E. coli subgroups); Robbins Ch. 17 (Table 17.7, infectious enterocolitis). The absent invasion for EHEC and C. difficile and the invasive, usually nonbloody ileal course of Yersinia are from Robbins, since the notes do not grade them.","boardPrep":"Step 1: EHEC O157:H7 releases Shiga-like toxin, does not ferment sorbitol, and causes HUS; antibiotics can increase toxin release (Exam 3 notes, SDL 31). COMLEX: bloody diarrhea after undercooked beef followed by anemia, thrombocytopenia, and renal failure is HUS.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 31 Obj 1 — bloody versus watery bacterial diarrhea","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":32,"title":"SDL 32 — Pathology - Small Intestine & Colon: IBS, IBD, Diverticular Disease, and Polyps","questions":[{"id":"sdl32_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the diagnostic criteria, associations (fibromyalgia, mood disorders), and first line treatments (lifestyle/diet modifications) for irritable bowel syndrome.","batch":1,"stem":"What do endoscopic and microscopic evaluations show in irritable bowel syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Normal mucosal findings","B":"Patchy crypt abscesses","C":"Thickened collagen band","D":"Diffuse villous blunting","E":"Scattered aphthous ulcers"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Despite real symptoms, endoscopic and microscopic evaluations are normal in IBS, so the diagnosis rests on clinical symptoms and functional testing. Patchy crypt abscesses indicate inflammatory bowel disease, which must be excluded because IBS biopsies are normal. A thickened subepithelial collagen band is collagenous colitis, not IBS, whose mucosa is normal. Diffuse villous blunting suggests celiac disease, an organic cause excluded before diagnosing IBS. Scattered aphthous ulcers point to Crohn disease, whereas IBS shows no mucosal abnormality. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Irritable bowel syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: IBS has no structural abnormalities and is most common in middle-aged females (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Irritable bowel syndrome). COMLEX: IBS is divided into diarrhea-predominant, constipation-predominant, and mixed subtypes (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 2 — pharmacologic therapy of IBS by symptom pattern","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Patchy crypt abscesses indicate inflammatory bowel disease, ","C":"A thickened subepithelial collagen band is collagenous colit","D":"Diffuse villous blunting suggests celiac disease, an organic","E":"Scattered aphthous ulcers point to Crohn disease, whereas IB"}},{"id":"sdl32_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the diagnostic criteria, associations (fibromyalgia, mood disorders), and first line treatments (lifestyle/diet modifications) for irritable bowel syndrome.","batch":1,"stem":"Which pair of conditions is associated with irritable bowel syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Sacroiliitis and uveitis","B":"Gallstones and kidney stones","C":"Erythema nodosum and arthritis","D":"Fibromyalgia and depression","E":"Pyoderma and episcleritis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The Elentra objective lists fibromyalgia and mood disorders as IBS associations, and First Aid notes IBS may be associated with fibromyalgia and mood disorders such as anxiety and depression. The distractors are extraintestinal or metabolic complications of inflammatory bowel disease. Sources: SDL 32 Elentra Objective 1; First Aid Gastrointestinal (Irritable bowel syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (FA) — detail not in SDL 32 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IBS may be associated with fibromyalgia and with anxiety or depression (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Irritable bowel syndrome). COMLEX: candidate IBS genes include serotonin reuptake transporters and cannabinoid receptors (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 2 — pharmacologic therapy of IBS by symptom pattern","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"FA"},{"id":"sdl32_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the diagnostic criteria, associations (fibromyalgia, mood disorders), and first line treatments (lifestyle/diet modifications) for irritable bowel syndrome.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the first-line treatment for irritable bowel syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Oral glucocorticoid course","B":"Lifestyle and diet changes","C":"Long-term opioid therapy","D":"Empiric antibiotic course","E":"Elective subtotal colectomy"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The Elentra objective and First Aid both give lifestyle modification and dietary changes as first-line IBS treatment. Glucocorticoids target inflammatory bowel disease, and colectomy is not indicated for a disorder without structural disease. Long-term opioids are not IBS therapy; first-line care is lifestyle and diet change, with symptom-directed drugs such as loperamide only for IBS-D. Empiric antibiotics are not first-line for IBS, which starts with diet, fiber, exercise, and stress reduction. Sources: SDL 32 Elentra Objective 1; First Aid Gastrointestinal (Irritable bowel syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: first-line IBS treatment is lifestyle modification and dietary change (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Irritable bowel syndrome). COMLEX: 5-HT3 receptor antagonists are effective in many cases of diarrhea-predominant IBS (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 2 — pharmacologic therapy of IBS by symptom pattern","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Long-term opioids are not IBS therapy; first-line care is li","D":"Empiric antibiotics are not first-line for IBS, which starts"}},{"id":"sdl32_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the various inflammatory bowel diseases (Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis, microscopic colitis) by their location, epidemiology, pathogenesis, morphology, complications, symptoms, and treatment.","batch":1,"stem":"Which gene encoding an intracellular sensor of bacterial muramyl dipeptide is most strongly associated with Crohn disease?","choices":{"A":"APC","B":"CFTR","C":"NOD2","D":"HFE","E":"KRAS"},"correct":"C","explanation":"NOD2 encodes an intracellular sensor of muramyl dipeptide and is the gene most strongly associated with Crohn disease in European populations, though risk alleles occur in about 30% of patients. NOD2 variants are protective in ulcerative colitis. APC mutations drive familial adenomatous polyposis and sporadic colon cancer, not Crohn disease. CFTR mutations cause cystic fibrosis, not Crohn disease, which is linked to the bacterial sensor NOD2. HFE mutations cause hereditary hemochromatosis, not Crohn disease. KRAS is an oncogene in colorectal adenoma progression, not a sensor of muramyl dipeptide. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Inflammatory bowel disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ATG16L1 and IRGM, which act in autophagosome formation, are additional Crohn disease risk genes (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: very early onset IBD can be monogenic, most often from IL10 or IL-10 receptor mutations (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 3 — drug therapy of ulcerative colitis and Crohn disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"APC mutations drive familial adenomatous polyposis and spora","B":"CFTR mutations cause cystic fibrosis, not Crohn disease, whi","D":"HFE mutations cause hereditary hemochromatosis, not Crohn di","E":"KRAS is an oncogene in colorectal adenoma progression, not a"}},{"id":"sdl32_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the various inflammatory bowel diseases (Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis, microscopic colitis) by their location, epidemiology, pathogenesis, morphology, complications, symptoms, and treatment.","batch":1,"stem":"Which form of microscopic colitis shows a strong association with celiac disease?","choices":{"A":"Collagenous colitis","B":"Diversion colitis","C":"Ischemic colitis","D":"Ulcerative proctitis","E":"Lymphocytic colitis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Lymphocytic colitis is strongly associated with celiac disease and autoimmune diseases including Graves disease, rheumatoid arthritis, and autoimmune gastritis. Its collagen layer is of normal thickness, and intraepithelial lymphocytes often exceed 20 per 100 colonocytes. Collagenous colitis is defined by a thick subepithelial collagen band, whereas the celiac-associated form is lymphocytic colitis with normal collagen. Ischemic colitis is a vascular injury with abnormal endoscopy, not a form of microscopic colitis. Ulcerative proctitis is a limited form of ulcerative colitis with visible mucosal disease, not microscopic colitis. Diversion colitis arises in a surgically defunctioned colon segment, shows prominent mucosal lymphoid follicles, and resolves when fecal flow is restored. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Microscopic colitis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 32 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: microscopic colitis is most common in older females and has a normal endoscopic appearance (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Microscopic colitis). COMLEX: both forms of microscopic colitis cause chronic, nonbloody, watery diarrhea without weight loss (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 3 — drug therapy of ulcerative colitis and Crohn disease","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"Collagenous colitis is defined by a thick subepithelial coll","C":"Ischemic colitis is a vascular injury with abnormal endoscop","D":"Ulcerative proctitis is a limited form of ulcerative colitis","B":"Diversion colitis arises in a surgically defunctioned colon "}},{"id":"sdl32_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the various inflammatory bowel diseases (Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis, microscopic colitis) by their location, epidemiology, pathogenesis, morphology, complications, symptoms, and treatment.","batch":1,"stem":"Which antibodies are often present in Crohn disease but typically absent in ulcerative colitis?","choices":{"A":"Anti-Saccharomyces antibodies","B":"Antimitochondrial antibodies","C":"Anti-smooth muscle antibodies","D":"Anti-tissue transglutaminase IgA","E":"Anti-parietal cell antibodies"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Crohn disease patients often have anti-Saccharomyces cerevisiae antibodies, which are typically absent in ulcerative colitis. In contrast, up to 75% of ulcerative colitis patients have p-ANCA. Antimitochondrial antibodies mark primary biliary cholangitis, not Crohn disease. Anti-smooth muscle antibodies mark autoimmune hepatitis, not Crohn disease. Anti-tissue transglutaminase IgA is the serologic test for celiac disease, not Crohn disease. Anti-parietal cell antibodies mark autoimmune gastritis, not Crohn disease. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Inflammatory bowel disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 32 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Crohn disease may be positive for anti-Saccharomyces cerevisiae antibodies (ASCA) (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Inflammatory bowel diseases). COMLEX: fecal calprotectin helps distinguish IBD activity from noninflammatory disease such as IBS (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Inflammatory bowel diseases).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 3 — drug therapy of ulcerative colitis and Crohn disease","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"B":"Antimitochondrial antibodies mark primary biliary cholangiti","C":"Anti-smooth muscle antibodies mark autoimmune hepatitis, not","D":"Anti-tissue transglutaminase IgA is the serologic test for c","E":"Anti-parietal cell antibodies mark autoimmune gastritis, not"}},{"id":"sdl32_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathogenesis, symptoms, complications (diverticulitis, abscess, fistula, obstruction, perforation) and demographics of Meckel diverticulum and sigmoid diverticular disease.","batch":1,"stem":"Where in the colonic wall do sigmoid diverticula form?","choices":{"A":"Bases of colonic crypts","B":"Lymphoid aggregate sites","C":"Vasa recta entry points","D":"Mesenteric fat attachments","E":"Haustral fold crests"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Focal gaps in the inner circular muscle occur where nerves and arterial vasa recta penetrate it. In the colon these gaps are unreinforced, so raised intraluminal pressure herniates mucosa through them. Diverticula are not at crypt bases; they herniate through muscle gaps where vasa recta penetrate. Lymphoid aggregates are not the weak points; diverticula form at vasa recta entry gaps in the circular muscle. Haustral fold crests are not the weak points; diverticula herniate where vasa recta penetrate the inner circular muscle. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Sigmoid diverticular disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: false diverticula occur especially where vasa recta penetrate the muscularis externa (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Diverticula of the GI tract). COMLEX: colonic diverticula are 0.5 to 1 cm flask-like outpouchings that are uniformly spaced (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 3 — vitelline duct remnants and Meckel diverticulum","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Diverticula are not at crypt bases; they herniate through mu","B":"Lymphoid aggregates are not the weak points; diverticula for","E":"Haustral fold crests are not the weak points; diverticula he"}},{"id":"sdl32_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathogenesis, symptoms, complications (diverticulitis, abscess, fistula, obstruction, perforation) and demographics of Meckel diverticulum and sigmoid diverticular disease.","batch":1,"stem":"About what share of Western adults older than 60 years have colonic diverticular disease?","choices":{"A":"About 5 percent","B":"About 15 percent","C":"About 25 percent","D":"About 90 percent","E":"About 50 percent"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Colonic diverticula are rare before age 30, but prevalence approaches 50% in Western adults older than 60 years. They are less common in Japan and under-resourced countries, probably from dietary differences. About 5 percent is far too low; prevalence approaches 50% in Western adults over 60. About 15 percent underestimates diverticular disease, which approaches 50% in Western adults over 60. About 25 percent underestimates diverticular disease, which approaches 50% in Western adults over 60. About 90 percent overestimates diverticular disease, which approaches 50% in Western adults over 60. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Sigmoid diverticular disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diverticulosis is associated with obesity and diets low in fiber and high in total fat or red meat (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Diverticulosis). COMLEX: diverticula tend to occur in the right colon in Asian and African populations (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 3 — vitelline duct remnants and Meckel diverticulum","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"About 5 percent is far too low; prevalence approaches 50% in","B":"About 15 percent underestimates diverticular disease, which ","C":"About 25 percent underestimates diverticular disease, which ","D":"About 90 percent overestimates diverticular disease, which a"}},{"id":"sdl32_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathogenesis, symptoms, complications (diverticulitis, abscess, fistula, obstruction, perforation) and demographics of Meckel diverticulum and sigmoid diverticular disease.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the sex ratio of Meckel diverticula?","choices":{"A":"Twice as common in males","B":"Twice as common in females","C":"Equal in males and females","D":"Four times more in females","E":"Ten times more in males"},"correct":"A","explanation":"By the rule of 2s, Meckel diverticula have a male-to-female ratio of 2:1, occur in about 2% of people, lie within 2 feet of the ileocecal valve, are about 2 inches long, and are most often symptomatic by age 2. Meckel diverticula are twice as common in males, not females, per the rule of 2s. Meckel diverticula are not equal by sex; the rule of 2s gives a 2:1 male predominance. Meckel diverticula favor males 2:1, so a fourfold female excess is wrong. A tenfold male excess overstates the 2:1 male-to-female ratio of Meckel diverticula. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Meckel diverticulum). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a Meckel diverticulum extends from the antimesenteric side of the ileum and may contain ectopic pancreatic tissue (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: about 4% of Meckel diverticula ever become symptomatic (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 3 — vitelline duct remnants and Meckel diverticulum","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Meckel diverticula are twice as common in males, not females","C":"Meckel diverticula are not equal by sex; the rule of 2s give","D":"Meckel diverticula favor males 2:1, so a fourfold female exc","E":"A tenfold male excess overstates the 2:1 male-to-female rati"}},{"id":"sdl32_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize hyperplastic and inflammatory intestinal polyps by their symptoms, anatomical location, malignant potential, morphology, and causes.","batch":1,"stem":"Where in the colon are hyperplastic polyps most commonly found?","choices":{"A":"Right colon","B":"Transverse colon","C":"Cecum and appendix","D":"Left colon","E":"Terminal ileum"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Hyperplastic polyps are most common in the left colon, are typically less than 5 mm, and are frequently multiple in the sigmoid colon and rectum. The right colon is the usual site of sessile serrated polyps, whereas hyperplastic polyps favor the left colon. The transverse colon is not the typical site; hyperplastic polyps cluster in the sigmoid colon and rectum. Hyperplastic polyps favor the left colon, sigmoid, and rectum rather than the cecum and appendix. The terminal ileum is small bowel, while hyperplastic polyps are colonic lesions mostly of the left colon. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hyperplastic and inflammatory polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hyperplastic polyps are the most common colonic polyps and are predominantly located in the rectosigmoid region (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colonic polyps). COMLEX: colonic hyperplastic polyps are typically discovered in the sixth and seventh decades (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 1 — colorectal adenomas and malignant potential","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The right colon is the usual site of sessile serrated polyps","B":"The transverse colon is not the typical site; hyperplastic p","C":"Hyperplastic polyps favor the left colon, sigmoid, and rectu","E":"The terminal ileum is small bowel, while hyperplastic polyps"}},{"id":"sdl32_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize hyperplastic and inflammatory intestinal polyps by their symptoms, anatomical location, malignant potential, morphology, and causes.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the proposed pathogenesis of colonic hyperplastic polyps?","choices":{"A":"Biallelic APC gene inactivation","B":"Germline STK11 gene mutation","C":"Repeated mucosal prolapse injury","D":"Mismatch repair gene silencing","E":"Delayed epithelial shedding"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Hyperplastic polyps are thought to result from decreased epithelial turnover and delayed shedding of surface epithelial cells, leading to a piling up of goblet and absorptive cells. Mucosal prolapse drives solitary rectal ulcer polyps instead. Biallelic APC inactivation starts adenomas, but hyperplastic polyps are nonneoplastic and come from delayed epithelial shedding. Germline STK11 mutation causes Peutz-Jeghers hamartomas, not hyperplastic polyps. Mismatch repair silencing drives microsatellite-unstable neoplasia, not the nonneoplastic hyperplastic polyp. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hyperplastic and inflammatory polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hyperplastic polyps are composed of mature goblet and absorptive cells with a serrated surface architecture (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: epithelial hyperplasia can occur as a nonspecific reaction overlying any mass and may signal an adjacent lesion (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 1 — colorectal adenomas and malignant potential","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Biallelic APC inactivation starts adenomas, but hyperplastic","B":"Germline STK11 mutation causes Peutz-Jeghers hamartomas, not","D":"Mismatch repair silencing drives microsatellite-unstable neo"}},{"id":"sdl32_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize hyperplastic and inflammatory intestinal polyps by their symptoms, anatomical location, malignant potential, morphology, and causes.","batch":1,"stem":"What underlying abnormality causes solitary rectal ulcer syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Prior pelvic radiation for carcinoma","B":"Poor anorectal sphincter relaxation","C":"Hypoperfusion of the rectal watershed","D":"Recurrent enteric bacterial infection","E":"Ectopic gastric mucosa in the rectum"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Impaired relaxation of the anorectal sphincter creates a sharp angle at the anterior rectal shelf, causing recurrent abrasion and ulceration of the overlying mucosa. Entrapment of the resulting polyp in the fecal stream leads to mucosal prolapse. Hypoperfusion of a watershed causes ischemic colitis, not solitary rectal ulcer syndrome, which comes from poor sphincter relaxation. Recurrent infection is not the cause; solitary rectal ulcer results from impaired anorectal sphincter relaxation and mucosal prolapse. Ectopic gastric mucosa is the ulcer-causing feature of Meckel diverticulum, not of solitary rectal ulcer. Solitary rectal ulcer syndrome comes from impaired anorectal sphincter relaxation and recurrent abrasion of the anterior rectal shelf, not from radiation. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hyperplastic and inflammatory polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: solitary rectal ulcer syndrome presents with rectal bleeding, mucus discharge, and an inflammatory lesion of the anterior rectal wall (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: inflammatory polyps form through repeated cycles of injury and healing (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 1 — colorectal adenomas and malignant potential","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Hypoperfusion of a watershed causes ischemic colitis, not so","D":"Recurrent infection is not the cause; solitary rectal ulcer ","E":"Ectopic gastric mucosa is the ulcer-causing feature of Mecke","A":"Solitary rectal ulcer syndrome comes from impaired anorectal"}},{"id":"sdl32_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand hamartomatous polyps based on their morphology, mutated genes and pathways involved, and their associated syndromes (juvenile polyposis and Peutz-Jeghers syndrome).","batch":1,"stem":"Which gene is the most common mutation identified in autosomal dominant juvenile polyposis?","choices":{"A":"STK11","B":"KRAS","C":"MUTYH","D":"SMAD4","E":"PTEN"},"correct":"D","explanation":"SMAD4, which encodes a signaling intermediate in the TGF-beta pathway, is the most common mutation in autosomal dominant juvenile polyposis. BMPR1A mutations account for other cases, and together they explain fewer than half of patients. STK11 mutations cause Peutz-Jeghers syndrome, not juvenile polyposis. KRAS is an oncogene in sporadic adenomas, not the germline cause of juvenile polyposis. MUTYH mutations cause an autosomal recessive adenomatous polyposis, not autosomal dominant juvenile polyposis. PTEN mutations cause Cowden syndrome hamartomas, whereas juvenile polyposis most often involves SMAD4. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hamartomatous polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 32 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: juvenile polyposis is an autosomal dominant syndrome of numerous hamartomatous polyps in the colon, stomach, and small bowel (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: patients with SMAD4 mutations often have both juvenile polyposis and hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 2 — familial adenomatous polyposis","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"STK11 mutations cause Peutz-Jeghers syndrome, not juvenile p","B":"KRAS is an oncogene in sporadic adenomas, not the germline c","C":"MUTYH mutations cause an autosomal recessive adenomatous pol","E":"PTEN mutations cause Cowden syndrome hamartomas, whereas juv"}},{"id":"sdl32_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand hamartomatous polyps based on their morphology, mutated genes and pathways involved, and their associated syndromes (juvenile polyposis and Peutz-Jeghers syndrome).","batch":1,"stem":"Where are the polyps of Peutz-Jeghers syndrome most common?","choices":{"A":"Gastric antrum","B":"Small intestine","C":"Rectosigmoid colon","D":"Distal esophagus","E":"Ascending colon"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Peutz-Jeghers polyps are most common in the small intestine, although they may occur in the stomach and colon and rarely in the bladder and lungs. Grossly they are large, pedunculated, and lobulated. Peutz-Jeghers polyps can occur in the stomach but are most common in the small intestine. Peutz-Jeghers polyps are less common in the colon than in the small intestine. The distal esophagus is not a typical site of Peutz-Jeghers polyps, which favor the small intestine. The ascending colon is a less common site; Peutz-Jeghers polyps favor the small intestine over the colon. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hamartomatous polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Peutz-Jeghers syndrome features numerous hamartomatous polyps throughout the GI tract with hyperpigmented macules (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: Peutz-Jeghers syndrome presents at a median age of 11 years (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 2 — familial adenomatous polyposis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Peutz-Jeghers polyps can occur in the stomach but are most c","C":"Peutz-Jeghers polyps are less common in the colon than in th","D":"The distal esophagus is not a typical site of Peutz-Jeghers ","E":"The ascending colon is a less common site; Peutz-Jeghers pol"}},{"id":"sdl32_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand hamartomatous polyps based on their morphology, mutated genes and pathways involved, and their associated syndromes (juvenile polyposis and Peutz-Jeghers syndrome).","batch":1,"stem":"Which pathway does the Peutz-Jeghers tumor suppressor STK11 regulate?","choices":{"A":"BMP receptor signaling","B":"Wnt signaling","C":"AMPK signaling","D":"DNA mismatch repair","E":"Hedgehog signaling"},"correct":"C","explanation":"STK11 is a tumor suppressor that regulates AMP-activated protein kinase (AMPK) pathways, which control cell polarization and brake growth and anabolic metabolism. Germline heterozygous loss-of-function STK11 mutations occur in about half of familial cases. BMP receptor signaling is the TGF-beta pathway mutated in juvenile polyposis (BMPR1A), not the STK11 pathway. Wnt signaling is disrupted by APC loss in adenomas, not by STK11 in Peutz-Jeghers. DNA mismatch repair defects cause Lynch syndrome, not Peutz-Jeghers, whose STK11 regulates AMPK. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hamartomatous polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Peutz-Jeghers syndrome is autosomal dominant (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: the second normal STK11 copy is often lost through somatic mutation in Peutz-Jeghers-associated cancers (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 2 — familial adenomatous polyposis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"BMP receptor signaling is the TGF-beta pathway mutated in ju","B":"Wnt signaling is disrupted by APC loss in adenomas, not by S","D":"DNA mismatch repair defects cause Lynch syndrome, not Peutz-"}},{"id":"sdl32_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the diagnostic criteria, associations (fibromyalgia, mood disorders), and first line treatments (lifestyle/diet modifications) for irritable bowel syndrome.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman has crampy lower abdominal pain on about 5 days each month for 6 months, with bloating and alternating loose and hard stools. Colonoscopy and biopsies are normal. Which additional feature best supports the diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Nocturnal bloody stools","B":"Weight loss over 10%","C":"Elevated stool calprotectin","D":"Pain eased by defecation","E":"Symptom onset after age 60"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Robbins lists IBS criteria as abdominal pain or discomfort at least 3 days per month over 3 months with improvement after defecation and a change in stool frequency or form, after other causes are excluded. An elevated fecal calprotectin points toward organic disease. Nocturnal bloody stools are an alarm feature of organic disease such as IBD, not support for IBS. Weight loss over 10% suggests organic disease that must be excluded, not IBS. Onset after age 60 raises concern for organic disease, whereas IBS typically begins in younger adults. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Irritable bowel syndrome); First Aid Gastrointestinal (Irritable bowel syndrome, Inflammatory bowel diseases). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: IBS is recurrent abdominal pain with at least 2 of: related to defecation, change in stool frequency, change in stool form, so under FA no single feature is mandatory (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Irritable bowel syndrome). COMLEX: IBS prevalence in high-income countries is about 5% to 10%, peaking between 20 and 40 years (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 4 — colonic motility, haustral contractions, and mass movements","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Nocturnal bloody stools are an alarm feature of organic dise","B":"Weight loss over 10% suggests organic disease that must be e","E":"Onset after age 60 raises concern for organic disease, where"}},{"id":"sdl32_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the diagnostic criteria, associations (fibromyalgia, mood disorders), and first line treatments (lifestyle/diet modifications) for irritable bowel syndrome.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man has 2 years of urgent loose stools and intermittent abdominal cramping. Colonoscopy is normal, and he does not have celiac disease. A test shows excess fecal bile acids. Which mechanism best explains this subtype of his disorder?","choices":{"A":"Gluten-induced villous injury","B":"Subepithelial collagen band","C":"Bile acid malabsorption","D":"Bacterial invasion of crypts","E":"Granulomatous ileal disease"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Excess bile acid synthesis or bile acid malabsorption is one identified cause of diarrhea-predominant IBS, likely through effects of bile acids on intestinal motility and epithelial ion transport. Gluten-induced villous injury is celiac disease, which has already been excluded. A subepithelial collagen band is collagenous colitis, which a normal colonoscopy with biopsy would reveal, not IBS. Bacterial invasion of crypts would cause inflammatory colitis with abnormal biopsies, not IBS. Granulomatous ileal disease is Crohn disease, which would be visible on colonoscopy, unlike IBS. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Irritable bowel syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 32 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: diarrhea-predominant IBS shows increased colonic contractions and transit rates (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: opioids and anticholinergic psychoactive drugs are also used for diarrhea-predominant IBS (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — enterohepatic circulation and terminal ileal bile salt loss","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"Gluten-induced villous injury is celiac disease, which has a","B":"A subepithelial collagen band is collagenous colitis, which ","D":"Bacterial invasion of crypts would cause inflammatory coliti","E":"Granulomatous ileal disease is Crohn disease, which would be"}},{"id":"sdl32_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the diagnostic criteria, associations (fibromyalgia, mood disorders), and first line treatments (lifestyle/diet modifications) for irritable bowel syndrome.","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman returns from Mexico after a week of fever and watery diarrhea. Six months later she has recurring cramps, bloating, and variable stool form with normal colonoscopy and biopsies. Which mechanism is proposed for this onset pattern?","choices":{"A":"Shift in the gut microbiome","B":"Antibodies to enteric neurons","C":"Persistent mucosal granulomas","D":"Chronic bacterial tissue invasion","E":"Subepithelial collagen deposits"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Some IBS patients relate onset to a bout of infectious gastroenteritis, suggesting that immune activation or a shift in the gut microbiome triggers disease. Granulomas, invasion, and collagen deposition would produce abnormal biopsies. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Irritable bowel syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: IBS is chronic and may be diarrhea-predominant, constipation-predominant, or mixed (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Irritable bowel syndrome). COMLEX: defective brain-gut axis signaling is proposed in IBS (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the various inflammatory bowel diseases (Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis, microscopic colitis) by their location, epidemiology, pathogenesis, morphology, complications, symptoms, and treatment.","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old man has 8 months of right lower quadrant pain, nonbloody diarrhea, and a draining perianal tract. Resection of a strictured terminal ileum shows a thick rubbery wall with deep fissures. Which additional gross finding is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Continuous disease of mucosa","B":"Thin wall with smooth serosa","C":"Superficial broad ulcers","D":"Flat atrophic mucosal surface","E":"Creeping fat over the serosa"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In transmural Crohn disease, mesenteric adipose tissue frequently extends over the serosal surface as creeping fat. Wall thickening from edema, fibrosis, and muscular hypertrophy drives strictures. Continuous mucosal disease with a normal serosa fits ulcerative colitis. A thin wall with smooth serosa contradicts Crohn disease, whose transmural inflammation thickens the wall and draws creeping fat. Superficial broad-based ulcers are typical of ulcerative colitis, whereas Crohn disease has deep linear fissuring ulcers. A flat atrophic surface does not fit Crohn disease, which shows cobblestoning from deep ulcers between spared mucosa. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Inflammatory bowel disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Crohn disease shows cobblestone mucosa, creeping fat, and a string sign on small bowel follow-through (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Inflammatory bowel diseases). COMLEX: disease often recurs at the anastomosis, and up to 40% need more resection within 10 years (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 3 — drug therapy of ulcerative colitis and Crohn disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"A thin wall with smooth serosa contradicts Crohn disease, wh","C":"Superficial broad-based ulcers are typical of ulcerative col","D":"A flat atrophic surface does not fit Crohn disease, which sh"}},{"id":"sdl32_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the various inflammatory bowel diseases (Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis, microscopic colitis) by their location, epidemiology, pathogenesis, morphology, complications, symptoms, and treatment.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old man with newly diagnosed ulcerative colitis has pruritus and an alkaline phosphatase of 420 U/L (reference range 44–147 U/L). MRCP shows beaded intrahepatic and extrahepatic bile ducts. When should colonoscopic dysplasia surveillance begin?","choices":{"A":"About 8 years after diagnosis","B":"At the time of diagnosis","C":"Beginning at 50 years of age","D":"After the first bloody relapse","E":"Upon confirmation of pancolitis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Patients with IBD typically enter surveillance about 8 years after diagnosis. The major exception is IBD with primary sclerosing cholangitis, which carries greater cancer risk, so surveillance starts at diagnosis. Age 50 is the average-risk screening start, but PSC-associated IBD requires surveillance from diagnosis. Surveillance does not wait for a relapse; PSC with IBD warrants surveillance from diagnosis. Surveillance in PSC-associated colitis begins at diagnosis regardless of disease extent. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Inflammatory bowel disease) (Colitis-associated neoplasia). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 32 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ulcerative colitis is associated with primary sclerosing cholangitis and MPO-ANCA/p-ANCA (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Inflammatory bowel diseases). COMLEX: colitis-associated cancer risk rises sharply 8 to 10 years after onset (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 5 — primary sclerosing cholangitis and cholestatic disease","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"C":"Age 50 is the average-risk screening start, but PSC-associat","D":"Surveillance does not wait for a relapse; PSC with IBD warra","E":"Surveillance in PSC-associated colitis begins at diagnosis r"}},{"id":"sdl32_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the various inflammatory bowel diseases (Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis, microscopic colitis) by their location, epidemiology, pathogenesis, morphology, complications, symptoms, and treatment.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old woman has 4 months of watery, nonbloody diarrhea without weight loss. Colonoscopy is normal, but biopsies show more than 20 intraepithelial lymphocytes per 100 colonocytes with a collagen layer of normal thickness. Her medications are listed. Which is most likely contributing?","choices":{"A":"Metformin","B":"Lisinopril","C":"Levothyroxine","D":"Naproxen","E":"Amlodipine"},"correct":"D","explanation":"These biopsies show lymphocytic microscopic colitis. Similar histologic changes can be induced by NSAIDs and possibly proton pump inhibitors. Microscopic colitis changes like these can be induced by NSAIDs and possibly proton pump inhibitors; metformin is not among the drugs implicated. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Microscopic colitis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: microscopic colitis histology shows lamina propria lymphocytes with intraepithelial lymphocytosis or a thickened subepithelial collagen band (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Microscopic colitis). COMLEX: collagenous colitis has a subepithelial collagen layer thicker than 10 µm (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 4 — large-intestine lamina propria and the collagen layer","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Microscopic colitis changes like these can be induced by NSA"}},{"id":"sdl32_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathogenesis, symptoms, complications (diverticulitis, abscess, fistula, obstruction, perforation) and demographics of Meckel diverticulum and sigmoid diverticular disease.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man had left lower quadrant pain and fever with a WBC of 16,000/µL (reference range 4,500–11,000/µL) 2 months ago. He now has recurrent urinary tract infections and air in his urine. Which complication best explains his current findings?","choices":{"A":"Diverticular bleeding","B":"Sigmoid volvulus","C":"Ischemic stricture","D":"Free perforation","E":"Colovesical fistula"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Inflammation in sigmoid diverticula can extend through the thin wall to cause serositis, abscesses, sinus tracts, and perforation. A tract into the bladder produces pneumaturia and recurrent infection. Diverticular bleeding causes hematochezia, not pneumaturia and recurrent urinary infections. Sigmoid volvulus causes obstruction and distention, not air in the urine. An ischemic stricture causes obstruction, not a tract into the bladder producing pneumaturia. Free perforation causes acute peritonitis, not the chronic pneumaturia of a colovesical fistula. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Sigmoid diverticular disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diverticulitis complications include abscess, colovesical fistula with pneumaturia, obstruction, and perforation (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Diverticulitis). COMLEX: surgery is generally reserved for severe or recurrent diverticulitis (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 34 → Obj 5 — high-fat, low-fiber diets in nutritional disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Diverticular bleeding causes hematochezia, not pneumaturia a","B":"Sigmoid volvulus causes obstruction and distention, not air ","C":"An ischemic stricture causes obstruction, not a tract into t","D":"Free perforation causes acute peritonitis, not the chronic p"}},{"id":"sdl32_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathogenesis, symptoms, complications (diverticulitis, abscess, fistula, obstruction, perforation) and demographics of Meckel diverticulum and sigmoid diverticular disease.","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old woman with a lifelong low-fiber diet has dozens of sigmoid outpouchings on colonoscopy, but small bowel imaging shows no outpouchings. Which structural difference best explains the sparing of her small bowel?","choices":{"A":"Lower intraluminal small bowel pressure","B":"Absence of penetrating vasa recta","C":"Denser submucosal lymphoid tissue","D":"Continuous longitudinal muscle coat","E":"Thicker muscularis mucosae layer"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Both bowels have gaps in the inner circular muscle where nerves and vasa recta penetrate. In the small intestine these gaps are reinforced by a continuous outer longitudinal layer, whereas in the colon that layer is gathered into taeniae coli. Small bowel pressure is not the key difference; the small bowel's continuous longitudinal coat reinforces the muscle gaps. Vasa recta penetrate both small bowel and colon, so their absence does not explain small bowel sparing. The muscularis mucosae is not what reinforces the gaps; the continuous outer longitudinal layer does. The small bowel is spared because a continuous outer longitudinal muscle layer reinforces the gaps where vasa recta penetrate; lymphoid density is not the reason. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Sigmoid diverticular disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diverticulosis is caused by intraluminal pressure and focal weakness in the colonic wall (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Diverticulosis). COMLEX: hypertrophy of the inner circular muscle is commonly associated with diverticulosis (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Small bowel pressure is not the key difference; the small bo","B":"Vasa recta penetrate both small bowel and colon, so their ab","E":"The muscularis mucosae is not what reinforces the gaps; the ","C":"The small bowel is spared because a continuous outer longitu"}},{"id":"sdl32_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathogenesis, symptoms, complications (diverticulitis, abscess, fistula, obstruction, perforation) and demographics of Meckel diverticulum and sigmoid diverticular disease.","batch":2,"stem":"An 18-month-old boy has episodes of screaming with legs drawn up, vomiting, and a stool of blood and mucus. A sausage-shaped right abdominal mass is palpated. At surgery, a congenital ileal pouch lined by gastric mucosa is found at the leading point. Which complication of this pouch explains his presentation?","choices":{"A":"Diverticulitis","B":"Intussusception","C":"Small bowel volvulus","D":"Peptic perforation","E":"Adhesive obstruction"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A Meckel diverticulum can act as a lead point for intussusception and can also cause hematochezia, volvulus, or obstruction near the terminal ileum. Meckel diverticulitis mimics appendicitis but does not produce a sausage-shaped mass with currant-jelly stool. Volvulus causes twisting with obstruction but not a sausage-shaped mass with blood and mucus at a lead point. Peptic perforation from ectopic gastric mucosa causes peritonitis, not the telescoping mass of intussusception. Adhesive obstruction follows surgery and does not produce a sausage-shaped mass with currant-jelly stool. Sources: First Aid Gastrointestinal (Meckel diverticulum); Robbins Ch. 17 (Meckel diverticulum). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Meckel diverticulum can cause painless hematochezia, RLQ pain, intussusception, volvulus, or obstruction near the terminal ileum (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Meckel diverticulum). COMLEX: symptomatic Meckel cases can mimic acute appendicitis or obstruction (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 3 — vitelline duct remnants and Meckel diverticulum","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Meckel diverticulitis mimics appendicitis but does not produ","C":"Volvulus causes twisting with obstruction but not a sausage-","D":"Peptic perforation from ectopic gastric mucosa causes perito","E":"Adhesive obstruction follows surgery and does not produce a "}},{"id":"sdl32_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize hyperplastic and inflammatory intestinal polyps by their symptoms, anatomical location, malignant potential, morphology, and causes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old man has several small smooth colonic polyps. Histology shows mature goblet and absorptive cells with a saw-tooth outline limited to the upper third of the crypts. Which premalignant lesion must be distinguished from these?","choices":{"A":"Tubular adenoma","B":"Juvenile hamartoma","C":"Sessile serrated polyp","D":"Inflammatory pseudopolyp","E":"Lymphoid aggregate polyp"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hyperplastic polyps have no malignant potential, but they must be distinguished from sessile serrated polyps, which are histologically similar and premalignant. A tubular adenoma is dysplastic from the start and does not mimic the saw-tooth pattern of a hyperplastic polyp. A juvenile hamartoma has cystic mucin-filled glands, not serrated crypt tops, and is not the key mimic. An inflammatory pseudopolyp is nonneoplastic regenerating mucosa, not a premalignant serrated mimic. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hyperplastic and inflammatory polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: serrated polyps show a saw-tooth crypt pattern, BRAF mutations, and CpG island methylation (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colonic polyps). COMLEX: hyperplastic polyps may occur singly but are frequently multiple (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — colonic epithelial renewal and stem cells","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A tubular adenoma is dysplastic from the start and does not ","B":"A juvenile hamartoma has cystic mucin-filled glands, not ser","D":"An inflammatory pseudopolyp is nonneoplastic regenerating mu"}},{"id":"sdl32_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize hyperplastic and inflammatory intestinal polyps by their symptoms, anatomical location, malignant potential, morphology, and causes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman with 15 years of ulcerative pancolitis has colonoscopy showing many mucosal projections, some joined at their tips into bridges. Biopsies show no dysplasia. How do these projections form?","choices":{"A":"Hamartomatous stromal overgrowth","B":"Clonal adenomatous epithelial growth","C":"Submucosal lymphoid hyperplasia","D":"Mucin retention in cystic glands","E":"Regrowth of mucosa between ulcers"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In ulcerative colitis, isolated islands of regenerating mucosa bulge into the lumen to create pseudopolyps, and their tips may fuse into mucosal bridges. Hamartomatous stromal overgrowth describes Peutz-Jeghers or juvenile polyps, not UC pseudopolyps. Clonal adenomatous growth would be dysplastic, but these biopsies show none; pseudopolyps are regenerating mucosa. Mucin retention in cystic glands describes juvenile retention polyps, not UC pseudopolyps. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Inflammatory bowel disease); Robbins Ch. 17 (Hyperplastic and inflammatory polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: inflammatory pseudopolyps are due to mucosal erosion in inflammatory bowel disease (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colonic polyps). COMLEX: pseudopolyps are marked in ulcerative colitis and moderate in Crohn disease (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 3 — drug therapy of ulcerative colitis and Crohn disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Hamartomatous stromal overgrowth describes Peutz-Jeghers or ","B":"Clonal adenomatous growth would be dysplastic, but these bio","D":"Mucin retention in cystic glands describes juvenile retentio"}},{"id":"sdl32_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize hyperplastic and inflammatory intestinal polyps by their symptoms, anatomical location, malignant potential, morphology, and causes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman has rectal bleeding, mucus discharge, and chronic straining. A polypoid lesion is on the anterior rectal wall. Biopsy shows erosion and epithelial hyperplasia. Which additional histologic feature indicates mucosal prolapse?","choices":{"A":"Lamina propria smooth muscle","B":"Noncaseating submucosal granulomas","C":"Thickened subepithelial collagen","D":"Apoptotic bodies in crypt bases","E":"Foamy macrophages in lamina"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Solitary rectal ulcer syndrome polyps show mixed inflammation, erosion, and epithelial hyperplasia with prolapse-induced fibromuscular hyperplasia of the lamina propria. Noncaseating granulomas suggest Crohn disease, not mucosal prolapse. Thickened subepithelial collagen is collagenous colitis, not prolapse. Foamy lamina propria macrophages are Whipple disease, not mucosal prolapse. Prolapse in solitary rectal ulcer syndrome is marked by fibromuscular hyperplasia (smooth muscle) in the lamina propria, not by apoptotic crypt bodies. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hyperplastic and inflammatory polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: solitary rectal ulcer polyps are an example of an inflammatory lesion (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: granulation tissue-like capillary proliferation in the lamina propria reflects repeated erosion (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 5 — defecation reflex and anal sphincter control","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Noncaseating granulomas suggest Crohn disease, not mucosal p","C":"Thickened subepithelial collagen is collagenous colitis, not","E":"Foamy lamina propria macrophages are Whipple disease, not mu","D":"Prolapse in solitary rectal ulcer syndrome is marked by fibr"}},{"id":"sdl32_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand hamartomatous polyps based on their morphology, mutated genes and pathways involved, and their associated syndromes (juvenile polyposis and Peutz-Jeghers syndrome).","batch":2,"stem":"A 4-year-old boy has painless rectal bleeding, and a red mass prolapses through the anus. The resected pedunculated polyp is smooth, with cystic glands filled with mucin and inflammatory debris in an expanded lamina propria. He has no family history of polyps. What is the diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Inflammatory pseudopolyp","B":"Juvenile retention polyp","C":"Tubulovillous adenoma","D":"Sessile hyperplastic polyp","E":"Submucosal leiomyoma"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Sporadic juvenile polyps, also called retention polyps, are usually solitary, often rectal, and occur mostly before age 5. They show cystic mucin-filled glands in inflamed lamina propria and can prolapse through the anal sphincter. An inflammatory pseudopolyp arises in chronic IBD, which this child lacks. A tubulovillous adenoma is a dysplastic lesion of adults, not a cystic mucin-filled polyp in a 4-year-old. A hyperplastic polyp is small with serrated crypt tops, not cystic mucin-filled glands. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hamartomatous polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: solitary hamartomatous polyps do not have significant risk of transformation (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colonic polyps). COMLEX: dysplasia is rare in sporadic juvenile polyps (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 3 — gastric polyps and precursor epithelial lesions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"An inflammatory pseudopolyp arises in chronic IBD, which thi","C":"A tubulovillous adenoma is a dysplastic lesion of adults, no","D":"A hyperplastic polyp is small with serrated crypt tops, not "}},{"id":"sdl32_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand hamartomatous polyps based on their morphology, mutated genes and pathways involved, and their associated syndromes (juvenile polyposis and Peutz-Jeghers syndrome).","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old woman has dozens of hamartomatous polyps in the colon, recurrent nosebleeds, lip telangiectasias, and a pulmonary arteriovenous malformation. Her father had similar polyps. Which malignancy is she at greatest risk of developing by her mid-40s?","choices":{"A":"Medulloblastoma","B":"Papillary thyroid carcinoma","C":"Colonic adenocarcinoma","D":"Hepatocellular carcinoma","E":"Pancreatic islet tumor"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Juvenile polyposis syndrome is associated with dysplasia in polyps and separate adenomas, and 30% to 50% of patients develop colonic adenocarcinoma by age 45. Pulmonary arteriovenous malformations are recognized extraintestinal features. Hepatocellular carcinoma is not a juvenile polyposis risk; the major risk is colonic adenocarcinoma by age 45. Pancreatic islet tumors are not juvenile polyposis features; colonic adenocarcinoma is the main risk. Juvenile polyposis with hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia (SMAD4) carries a 30-50% risk of colonic adenocarcinoma by age 45, not of medulloblastoma. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hamartomatous polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: juvenile polyposis syndrome is associated with increased risk of colorectal cancer (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: colectomy may be needed to limit chronic hemorrhage from ulcerated juvenile polyps (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Hepatocellular carcinoma is not a juvenile polyposis risk; t","E":"Pancreatic islet tumors are not juvenile polyposis features;","A":"Juvenile polyposis with hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasi"}},{"id":"sdl32_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand hamartomatous polyps based on their morphology, mutated genes and pathways involved, and their associated syndromes (juvenile polyposis and Peutz-Jeghers syndrome).","batch":2,"stem":"An 11-year-old boy has dark blue-brown macules on his lips, nostrils, and palms, and imaging shows multiple GI polyps. A resected polyp has an arborizing network of smooth muscle and normal epithelium. Which neoplasm warrants surveillance starting at birth?","choices":{"A":"Sex cord stromal tumor","B":"Gastric adenocarcinoma","C":"Small intestinal carcinoma","D":"Pancreatic carcinoma","E":"Lung adenocarcinoma"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Peutz-Jeghers syndrome carries a lifetime cancer risk near 40%. Surveillance begins at birth for testicular sex cord tumors, in late childhood for gastric and small intestinal cancers, and in the second and third decades for colon, pancreatic, breast, lung, ovarian, and uterine cancers. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hamartomatous polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 32 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Peutz-Jeghers syndrome carries increased risk of breast and GI cancers, including colorectal, gastric, small bowel, and pancreatic (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: smooth muscle mixed with lamina propria distinguishes Peutz-Jeghers polyps from juvenile polyps (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 3 — hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer and extracolonic cancers","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins"},{"id":"trial_sdl32_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the diagnostic criteria, associations (fibromyalgia, mood disorders), and first line treatments (lifestyle/diet modifications) for irritable bowel syndrome.","batch":3,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman has 1 year of bloating, hard infrequent stools, and abdominal pain. Colonoscopy and biopsies are normal. Which motility change is typical of her subtype?","choices":{"A":"Accelerated colonic transit","B":"Mucosal crypt destruction","C":"Slowed colonic transit","D":"Absent rectal ganglion cells","E":"Bile acid overproduction"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Patients with constipation-predominant IBS tend to have decreased colonic contractions and transit rates, while diarrhea-predominant patients show increased rates. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Irritable bowel syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: IBS pathophysiology is multifaceted, and chronic symptoms may be diarrhea-predominant, constipation-predominant, or mixed (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Irritable bowel syndrome). COMLEX: psychologic stressors, diet, and increased enteric sensory responses contribute to IBS (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 2 — pharmacologic therapy of IBS by symptom pattern","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl32_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the various inflammatory bowel diseases (Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis, microscopic colitis) by their location, epidemiology, pathogenesis, morphology, complications, symptoms, and treatment.","batch":3,"stem":"A 27-year-old man develops bloody diarrhea with stringy mucus. Colonoscopy shows continuous mucosal inflammation from the distal colon upward. Which recent habit change most likely preceded his onset?","choices":{"A":"Quitting cigarette smoking","B":"Starting oral contraceptives","C":"Starting daily NSAID use","D":"Adopting a high fiber diet","E":"Beginning heavy alcohol use"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Unlike Crohn disease, onset of ulcerative colitis can be associated with smoking cessation, and smoking may give partial symptomatic relief. Smoking is a strong risk factor for Crohn disease. NSAIDs are a trigger for microscopic colitis, which does not produce continuous grossly visible inflammation. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Ulcerative colitis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ulcerative colitis is treated with 5-aminosalicylic acid preparations, 6-mercaptopurine, infliximab, or colectomy (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Inflammatory bowel diseases). COMLEX: most ulcerative colitis patients have at least one relapse within 10 years (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 3 — drug therapy of ulcerative colitis and Crohn disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"NSAIDs are a trigger for microscopic colitis, which does not"}},{"id":"trial_sdl32_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathogenesis, symptoms, complications (diverticulitis, abscess, fistula, obstruction, perforation) and demographics of Meckel diverticulum and sigmoid diverticular disease.","batch":3,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman learns at screening colonoscopy that she has many sigmoid outpouchings. She has no abdominal symptoms and asks how often people with this finding develop symptoms. What is the best estimate?","choices":{"A":"About 2 percent","B":"About 20 percent","C":"About 50 percent","D":"About 75 percent","E":"About 95 percent"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Most individuals with diverticular disease remain asymptomatic, but about 20% develop intermittent cramping, lower abdominal discomfort, constipation, distention, or tenesmus. Even diverticulitis most often resolves spontaneously. About 50% is the prevalence of sigmoid diverticula in Western adults over 60, not the fraction who develop symptoms. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Sigmoid diverticular disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diverticulitis classically causes LLQ pain, fever, and leukocytosis (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Diverticulitis). COMLEX: diverticular disease can cause alternating constipation and diarrhea that mimics IBS (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 34 → Obj 5 — high-fat, low-fiber diets in nutritional disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"About 50% is the prevalence of sigmoid diverticula in Wester"}},{"id":"trial_sdl32_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize hyperplastic and inflammatory intestinal polyps by their symptoms, anatomical location, malignant potential, morphology, and causes.","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old man has several small smooth colonic polyps on screening colonoscopy, and biopsies show mature goblet and absorptive cells. What size are these lesions typically?","choices":{"A":"About 1 to 2 cm","B":"About 2 to 3 cm","C":"About 3 to 5 cm","D":"More than 5 cm","E":"Under 5 mm"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Hyperplastic polyps are typically less than 5 mm in diameter and appear as smooth, nodular protrusions, often on the crests of mucosal folds. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hyperplastic polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hyperplastic polyps are the most common colonic polyps and are generally small (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colonic polyps). COMLEX: crypt base epithelium of hyperplastic polyps may have a regenerative appearance (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 1 — colorectal adenomas and malignant potential","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl32_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand hamartomatous polyps based on their morphology, mutated genes and pathways involved, and their associated syndromes (juvenile polyposis and Peutz-Jeghers syndrome).","batch":3,"stem":"A 14-year-old girl has colicky abdominal pain from multiple hamartomatous GI polyps and dark brown macules on her lips and fingers. Her mother says the spots look like freckles. Which feature distinguishes these macules from freckles?","choices":{"A":"Involvement of the oral mucosa","B":"Darkening with sun exposure","C":"Raised verrucous surface","D":"Blanching with pressure","E":"Symmetric facial distribution"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Peutz-Jeghers macules are dark blue to brown and appear on the lips, nostrils, buccal mucosa, palms, genitalia, and perianal region. They resemble freckles but are distinguished by buccal mucosal involvement. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Peutz-Jeghers syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Peutz-Jeghers hyperpigmented macules involve the mouth, lips, hands, and genitalia (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: STK11 testing helps diagnose patients with polyps who lack mucocutaneous pigmentation (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 2 — familial adenomatous polyposis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl32_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the various inflammatory bowel diseases (Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis, microscopic colitis) by their location, epidemiology, pathogenesis, morphology, complications, symptoms, and treatment.","batch":3,"stem":"A 22-year-old man with Crohn disease has resection of a fibrotic terminal ileal stricture. The surgeon plans to remove as little bowel as possible. Which outcome makes this bowel preservation important?","choices":{"A":"Postoperative toxic megacolon","B":"Loss of colonic water absorption","C":"Adhesive small bowel obstruction","D":"Recurrence at the anastomosis","E":"Postsurgical bile reflux gastritis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Crohn disease often recurs at the site of anastomosis, and up to 40% of patients need additional resections within 10 years, so bowel preservation is an important part of surgical planning. Toxic megacolon is a complication of ulcerative colitis and is unrelated to how much ileum is preserved. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Crohn disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 32 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Crohn disease is treated with glucocorticoids, azathioprine, antibiotics, and biologics such as infliximab and adalimumab (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Inflammatory bowel diseases). COMLEX: extensive small bowel Crohn disease can cause hypoalbuminemia and malabsorption of vitamin B12 and bile salts (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — enterohepatic circulation and terminal ileal bile salt loss","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"Toxic megacolon is a complication of ulcerative colitis and "}},{"id":"grid_sdl32_q01","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the various inflammatory bowel diseases (Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis, microscopic colitis) by their location, epidemiology, pathogenesis, morphology, complications, symptoms, and treatment.","batch":4,"stem":"A 24-year-old woman who has never smoked has had 3 months of recurrent bloody diarrhea with stringy mucus, tenesmus, and lower abdominal cramps that ease after defecation. Stool studies for enteric pathogens are negative. Colonoscopy is performed, and biopsies of the involved segment are examined. Which of the following sets of findings is most likely in this patient?","grid":{"headers":["Gross lesions","Trans­mural","Collagen band","Many IELs"]},"choices":{"A":["+","+","−","−"],"B":["−","−","+","+"],"C":["+","−","−","−"],"D":["−","−","−","+"],"E":["−","−","−","−"]},"correct":"C","explanation":"Choice C (+ gross lesions, − transmural, − collagen band, − many IELs) is correct. Ulcerative colitis begins in the rectum and extends proximally without skip lesions; nonsmokers are at higher risk, and attacks of bloody diarrhea with stringy mucus, tenesmus, and cramps relieved by defecation are typical. The mucosa is diffusely granular and friable with broad-based ulcers and pseudopolyps, but inflammation is limited to the mucosa and superficial submucosa, with neutrophilic crypt abscesses and crypt distortion. Choice A (+ gross lesions, + transmural, − collagen band, − many IELs) is Crohn disease, with transmural inflammation, skip lesions, cobblestoning, strictures, and fistulae, usually in the terminal ileum and favored by smoking. Choice B (− gross lesions, − transmural, + collagen band, + many IELs) is collagenous colitis, a microscopic colitis of older women with a normal-looking colon and a dense subepithelial collagen band >10 µm. Choice D (− gross lesions, − transmural, − collagen band, + many IELs) is lymphocytic colitis, with normal collagen thickness but >20 intraepithelial T lymphocytes per 100 colonocytes; both microscopic colitides cause nonbloody watery diarrhea. Choice E (all −) is irritable bowel syndrome, in which endoscopy and biopsies are characteristically normal. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 32, sections 1–2: IBS, Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis, microscopic colitis); Robbins Ch. 17 (inflammatory bowel disease) [Robbins]. The absence of an intraepithelial lymphocytosis and collagen band in Crohn disease and ulcerative colitis is inferred, as the notes list these features only for microscopic colitis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: continuous rectal-up mucosal inflammation in a nonsmoker with bloody diarrhea is ulcerative colitis; transmural disease with skip lesions is Crohn (Exam 3 notes, SDL 32). COMLEX: chronic watery diarrhea in an older woman with a normal colonoscopy calls for biopsy to find microscopic colitis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 — IBD drugs (5-ASA, budesonide, anti-TNF)","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":33,"title":"SDL 33 — Pathology - Small Intestine & Colon: Malignant Lesions, Anal Canal, and Appendix","questions":[{"id":"sdl33_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recognize the morphology, risk factors, and complications of colorectal adenomas and understand the relationship between size and malignancy.","batch":1,"stem":"Which histologic hallmark defines colorectal adenomas?","choices":{"A":"Crypt base serration","B":"Mucosal prolapse fibrosis","C":"Epithelial dysplasia","D":"Granulomatous inflammation","E":"Hamartomatous smooth muscle"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The hallmark of colorectal adenomas is epithelial dysplasia, seen as nuclear hyperchromasia, elongation, and stratification, often with prominent nucleoli and fewer goblet cells. Serration is the architecture of hyperplastic and sessile serrated polyps, whereas adenomas are defined by epithelial dysplasia. Prolapse-induced fibromuscular change marks solitary rectal ulcer polyps, not adenomas. Granulomatous inflammation suggests Crohn disease, not an adenoma. Arborizing smooth muscle defines Peutz-Jeghers hamartomas, not adenomas. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenomas). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: adenomatous polyps are neoplastic and arise via the chromosomal instability pathway with APC and KRAS mutations (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colonic polyps). COMLEX: colorectal adenomas range from 0.3 to 10 cm and have a velvet or raspberry-like surface (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 5 — large-intestine lymphatic drainage and adenoma spread","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Serration is the architecture of hyperplastic and sessile se","B":"Prolapse-induced fibromuscular change marks solitary rectal ","D":"Granulomatous inflammation suggests Crohn disease, not an ad","E":"Arborizing smooth muscle defines Peutz-Jeghers hamartomas, n"}},{"id":"sdl33_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recognize the morphology, risk factors, and complications of colorectal adenomas and understand the relationship between size and malignancy.","batch":1,"stem":"About what share of colorectal adenomas larger than 4 cm contain foci of invasive cancer?","choices":{"A":"About 5 percent","B":"About 40 percent","C":"About 15 percent","D":"About 75 percent","E":"About 95 percent"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Size is the most important characteristic correlating with malignancy risk in an adenoma. Cancer is rare in adenomas under 1 cm, while nearly 40% of lesions larger than 4 cm contain invasive cancer. About 5 percent underestimates the risk; nearly 40% of adenomas larger than 4 cm contain invasive cancer. About 15 percent underestimates the risk; nearly 40% of adenomas larger than 4 cm harbor invasive cancer. About 75 percent overstates the risk; nearly 40% of adenomas larger than 4 cm contain invasive cancer. About 95 percent overstates the risk; nearly 40% of adenomas larger than 4 cm contain invasive cancer. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenomas). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: villous histology carries more malignant potential than tubular histology, and tubulovillous is intermediate (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colonic polyps). COMLEX: villous adenomas harbor cancer more often than tubular adenomas, partly because they tend to be larger (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 5 — large-intestine lymphatic drainage and adenoma spread","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"About 5 percent underestimates the risk; nearly 40% of adeno","C":"About 15 percent underestimates the risk; nearly 40% of aden","D":"About 75 percent overstates the risk; nearly 40% of adenomas","E":"About 95 percent overstates the risk; nearly 40% of adenomas"}},{"id":"sdl33_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recognize the morphology, risk factors, and complications of colorectal adenomas and understand the relationship between size and malignancy.","batch":1,"stem":"Why does intramucosal carcinoma within an adenoma carry little metastatic potential?","choices":{"A":"Absent mucosal lymphatics","B":"Intact muscularis propria","C":"Low tumor mitotic activity","D":"Peritumoral lymphocyte barrier","E":"Rapid surface cell shedding"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Functional lymphatic channels are absent in the colonic mucosa, so intramucosal carcinoma has little or no metastatic potential and complete polypectomy is generally curative. Invasion beyond the muscularis mucosae carries a risk of spread. The muscularis propria is not the barrier; lack of mucosal lymphatics limits spread, and invasion past the muscularis mucosae already carries risk. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenomas). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 33 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: adenomas are usually asymptomatic but may present with occult bleeding (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colonic polyps). COMLEX: invasion into the submucosal stalk of a pedunculated polyp constitutes invasive adenocarcinoma (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 5 — large-intestine lymphatic drainage and adenoma spread","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"B":"The muscularis propria is not the barrier; lack of mucosal l"}},{"id":"sdl33_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the genetics, diagnostic criteria, polyp location (pancolonic), timing (teenager), morphology, and complications of familial adenomatous polyposis.","batch":1,"stem":"How many adenomas are needed for a diagnosis of classic familial adenomatous polyposis?","choices":{"A":"At least 10","B":"At least 25","C":"At least 100","D":"At least 50","E":"At least 1000"},"correct":"C","explanation":"At least 100 polyps are necessary for a diagnosis of classic FAP, though thousands may be present. Apart from their number, FAP polyps look like sporadic adenomas. Ten adenomas is far below the FAP threshold of at least 100. Twenty-five adenomas is below the classic FAP threshold of at least 100. Fifty adenomas falls short of the at-least-100 threshold for classic FAP. Thousands may be present, but the diagnostic threshold is at least 100, not 1000. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Familial adenomatous polyposis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: FAP polyps arise after puberty, are pancolonic, and involve the rectum (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: about 75% of FAP cases are inherited and the remainder arise from de novo mutations (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 3 — gastric polyps and precursor epithelial lesions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Ten adenomas is far below the FAP threshold of at least 100.","B":"Twenty-five adenomas is below the classic FAP threshold of a","D":"Fifty adenomas falls short of the at-least-100 threshold for","E":"Thousands may be present, but the diagnostic threshold is at"}},{"id":"sdl33_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the genetics, diagnostic criteria, polyp location (pancolonic), timing (teenager), morphology, and complications of familial adenomatous polyposis.","batch":1,"stem":"What does the protein product of the gene mutated in familial adenomatous polyposis normally do?","choices":{"A":"Repairs base mismatches","B":"Activates RAS signaling","C":"Inhibits mTOR signaling","D":"Blocks TGF beta signals","E":"Degrades beta catenin"},"correct":"E","explanation":"APC is a key negative regulator of Wnt signaling. It binds and promotes degradation of beta catenin, so its loss lets beta catenin enter the nucleus and drive MYC and cyclin D1 transcription. Repairing base mismatches is the job of mismatch repair genes such as MSH2 and MLH1, not APC. APC does not activate RAS; it degrades beta-catenin, while KRAS activation is a separate later step in the adenoma-carcinoma sequence. Inhibiting mTOR via AMPK is the role of STK11 in Peutz-Jeghers, not APC. APC does not block TGF-beta; TGF-beta pathway genes such as SMAD4 are lost later in the sequence or in juvenile polyposis. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Familial adenomatous polyposis); Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenocarcinoma). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: FAP is an autosomal dominant mutation of the APC tumor suppressor gene on chromosome 5q21-q22 (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: colon cancers without APC mutations often carry beta catenin mutations that resist APC-dependent degradation (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 3 — gastric polyps and precursor epithelial lesions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Repairing base mismatches is the job of mismatch repair gene","B":"APC does not activate RAS; it degrades beta-catenin, while K","C":"Inhibiting mTOR via AMPK is the role of STK11 in Peutz-Jeghe","D":"APC does not block TGF-beta; TGF-beta pathway genes such as "}},{"id":"sdl33_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the genetics, diagnostic criteria, polyp location (pancolonic), timing (teenager), morphology, and complications of familial adenomatous polyposis.","batch":1,"stem":"Which extraintestinal manifestation of familial adenomatous polyposis can be detected at birth?","choices":{"A":"Multiple mandibular osteomas","B":"Abdominal wall desmoid tumors","C":"Multiple epidermoid skin cysts","D":"Retinal pigment hypertrophy","E":"Impacted supernumerary teeth"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Congenital hypertrophy of the retinal pigment epithelium can be detected at birth and may be an adjunct to early FAP screening. Osteomas, desmoids, skin cysts, and dental anomalies appear later in variants such as Gardner syndrome. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Familial adenomatous polyposis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Gardner syndrome adds osseous and soft tissue tumors and impacted or supernumerary teeth to FAP (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: specific APC mutations partly explain FAP variants (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 3 — gastric polyps and precursor epithelial lesions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the associated mutation, complication, associated conditions (endometrial, ovarian, and skin cancers), and location (proximal colon) of hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer (aka Lynch syndrome).","batch":1,"stem":"Which genes are mutated in most patients with Lynch syndrome?","choices":{"A":"APC or CTNNB1","B":"BRCA1 or BRCA2","C":"SMAD4 or BMPR1A","D":"KRAS or BRAF","E":"MSH2 or MLH1"},"correct":"E","explanation":"There are at least five mismatch repair genes, but most HNPCC patients have mutations in MSH2 or MLH1. Patients inherit one mutant and one normal allele. APC mutations cause FAP, not Lynch syndrome, which involves mismatch repair genes. SMAD4 or BMPR1A mutations cause juvenile polyposis, not Lynch syndrome. KRAS and BRAF are somatic oncogene mutations in sporadic tumors, not the germline defect in Lynch syndrome. Most patients with Lynch syndrome carry mutations in the mismatch repair genes MSH2 or MLH1; BRCA1 and BRCA2 are not the genes involved. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lynch syndrome is an autosomal dominant mismatch repair defect with about 80% progression to colorectal cancer (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Lynch syndrome). COMLEX: the human genome has about 50,000 to 100,000 microsatellites (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 4 — gastric adenocarcinoma risk factors and precursors","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"APC mutations cause FAP, not Lynch syndrome, which involves ","C":"SMAD4 or BMPR1A mutations cause juvenile polyposis, not Lync","D":"KRAS and BRAF are somatic oncogene mutations in sporadic tum","B":"Most patients with Lynch syndrome carry mutations in the mis"}},{"id":"sdl33_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the associated mutation, complication, associated conditions (endometrial, ovarian, and skin cancers), and location (proximal colon) of hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer (aka Lynch syndrome).","batch":1,"stem":"About what share of all colorectal cancers are attributed to hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer?","choices":{"A":"About 10 to 15 percent","B":"About 2 to 4 percent","C":"About 20 to 25 percent","D":"About 40 to 50 percent","E":"About 70 to 80 percent"},"correct":"B","explanation":"HNPCC accounts for about 2% to 4% of colorectal cancers, making it the most common syndromic form of colon cancer. Its cancers occur at younger ages than sporadic colon cancers. Ten to 15 percent overstates HNPCC, which accounts for about 2% to 4% of colorectal cancers. Twenty to 25 percent overstates HNPCC, which accounts for about 2% to 4% of colorectal cancers. Forty to 50 percent grossly overstates HNPCC, which accounts for about 2% to 4% of colorectal cancers. Seventy to 80 percent is roughly the share of sporadic APC-pathway cancers, not HNPCC. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 33 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lynch syndrome is associated with endometrial, ovarian, and skin cancers (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Lynch syndrome). COMLEX: HNPCC patients have fewer polyps and develop cancer later than FAP patients (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 4 — gastric adenocarcinoma risk factors and precursors","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"Ten to 15 percent overstates HNPCC, which accounts for about","C":"Twenty to 25 percent overstates HNPCC, which accounts for ab","D":"Forty to 50 percent grossly overstates HNPCC, which accounts","E":"Seventy to 80 percent is roughly the share of sporadic APC-p"}},{"id":"sdl33_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the associated mutation, complication, associated conditions (endometrial, ovarian, and skin cancers), and location (proximal colon) of hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer (aka Lynch syndrome).","batch":1,"stem":"Which colonic region is characteristically involved in Lynch syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Sigmoid colon","B":"Rectal ampulla","C":"Proximal colon","D":"Splenic flexure","E":"Descending colon"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The objective and First Aid place Lynch syndrome in the proximal colon, and Robbins notes HNPCC colon cancers are often in the right colon. The sigmoid colon is a left-sided site, whereas Lynch cancers favor the proximal colon. The rectum is distal, whereas Lynch syndrome cancers favor the cecum and ascending colon. The splenic flexure is not characteristic; Lynch syndrome involves the proximal colon. The descending colon is left-sided, whereas Lynch syndrome favors the right colon. Sources: SDL 33 Elentra Objective 3; Robbins Ch. 17 (Hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the proximal colon is involved in Lynch syndrome (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Lynch syndrome). COMLEX: microsatellite unstable and CIMP tumors are frequently located in the right colon (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 4 — gastric adenocarcinoma risk factors and precursors","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The sigmoid colon is a left-sided site, whereas Lynch cancer","B":"The rectum is distal, whereas Lynch syndrome cancers favor t","D":"The splenic flexure is not characteristic; Lynch syndrome in","E":"The descending colon is left-sided, whereas Lynch syndrome f"}},{"id":"sdl33_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the morphology, pathogenesis, epidemiology, risk factors, symptoms, and diagnosis (iron deficiency anemia), of adenocarcinoma of the colon (colorectal cancer).","batch":1,"stem":"At what age does colorectal cancer incidence peak?","choices":{"A":"20 to 30 years","B":"30 to 40 years","C":"40 to 50 years","D":"60 to 70 years","E":"80 to 90 years"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Colorectal cancer incidence peaks at 60 to 70 years of age. Fewer than 20% of cases occur before age 50, though incidence before age 40 is rising. Colorectal cancer is uncommon at 20 to 30 years; incidence peaks at 60 to 70. Thirty to 40 years is too young; incidence peaks at 60 to 70 years. Forty to 50 years is too early, as fewer than 20% of cases occur before age 50. Eighty to 90 years is past the peak, which is 60 to 70 years. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenocarcinoma). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: most colorectal cancer patients are older than 50 years, and about 25% have a family history (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colorectal cancer). COMLEX: colorectal adenocarcinoma causes nearly 15% of US cancer deaths, second to lung cancer (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 1 — chemotherapy classes for colorectal cancer","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Colorectal cancer is uncommon at 20 to 30 years; incidence p","B":"Thirty to 40 years is too young; incidence peaks at 60 to 70","C":"Forty to 50 years is too early, as fewer than 20% of cases o","E":"Eighty to 90 years is past the peak, which is 60 to 70 years"}},{"id":"sdl33_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the morphology, pathogenesis, epidemiology, risk factors, symptoms, and diagnosis (iron deficiency anemia), of adenocarcinoma of the colon (colorectal cancer).","batch":1,"stem":"Which dietary pattern is most closely associated with increased colorectal cancer risk?","choices":{"A":"Low vegetable fiber diet","B":"High citrus fruit intake","C":"Low red meat consumption","D":"High fish oil consumption","E":"Low refined sugar intake"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Low intake of unabsorbable vegetable fiber with high intake of refined carbohydrates and fat is most closely tied to colorectal cancer. Reduced stool bulk may prolong mucosal contact with bacterial oxidative byproducts. Low red meat intake is not a risk; high animal fat intake raises colorectal cancer risk. Low refined sugar intake is not a risk; high refined carbohydrate intake is. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenocarcinoma). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colorectal cancer risk factors include adenomatous and serrated polyps, IBD, tobacco use, and processed meat with low fiber (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colorectal cancer). COMLEX: high fat intake raises hepatic bile acid synthesis, and bacteria can convert bile acids into carcinogens (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 1 — chemotherapy classes for colorectal cancer","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Low red meat intake is not a risk; high animal fat intake ra","E":"Low refined sugar intake is not a risk; high refined carbohy"}},{"id":"sdl33_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the morphology, pathogenesis, epidemiology, risk factors, symptoms, and diagnosis (iron deficiency anemia), of adenocarcinoma of the colon (colorectal cancer).","batch":1,"stem":"Which organ is the most common site of distant metastasis from colon cancer?","choices":{"A":"Lung","B":"Liver","C":"Bone","D":"Brain","E":"Ovary"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Because the colon drains through the portal circulation, the liver is the most common site of distant metastases. Regional nodes, lungs, and bones can also be involved. Lungs can be involved, but portal drainage makes the liver the most common distant site. Bone can be involved, but portal drainage makes the liver the most common distant site. Colon cancer can spread to lymph nodes, lungs, and bone, but portal drainage makes the liver the most common distant site, ahead of the brain. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenocarcinoma). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 33 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: rectosigmoid is the most common colorectal cancer site, followed by ascending and descending colon (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colorectal cancer). COMLEX: the rectum does not drain via the portal circulation, so metastatic anal carcinomas often circumvent the portal route (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 1 — chemotherapy classes for colorectal cancer","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"Lungs can be involved, but portal drainage makes the liver t","C":"Bone can be involved, but portal drainage makes the liver th","D":"Colon cancer can spread to lymph nodes, lungs, and bone, but"}},{"id":"sdl33_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the symptoms, risk factors, and diagnostic criteria associated with appendicitis (McBurney sign) as well as symptoms and risk factors of hemorrhoids and tumors of the anal canal and appendix.","batch":1,"stem":"Deep tenderness two-thirds of the way from the umbilicus to the right anterior superior iliac spine is known by what name?","choices":{"A":"Rovsing sign","B":"Psoas sign","C":"Murphy sign","D":"McBurney sign","E":"Obturator sign"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The McBurney sign is deep tenderness two-thirds of the distance from the umbilicus to the right anterior superior iliac spine, a classic finding in acute appendicitis. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Appendix). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: acute appendicitis may also elicit psoas, obturator, and Rovsing signs (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Appendicitis). COMLEX: acute appendicitis has a lifetime risk of 7% and affects males slightly more often (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 4 — portal hypertension and portosystemic varices","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the symptoms, risk factors, and diagnostic criteria associated with appendicitis (McBurney sign) as well as symptoms and risk factors of hemorrhoids and tumors of the anal canal and appendix.","batch":1,"stem":"Which is the most common tumor of the appendix?","choices":{"A":"Mucinous cystadenoma","B":"Signet ring carcinoma","C":"Burkitt lymphoma","D":"Gastrointestinal stromal tumor","E":"Neuroendocrine tumor"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The well-differentiated neuroendocrine (carcinoid) tumor is the most common appendiceal tumor. It is usually incidental, forms a bulbous swelling at the distal tip, and rarely spreads. Mucinous neoplasms occur in the appendix but are less common than the well-differentiated neuroendocrine tumor. The most common appendiceal tumor is the well-differentiated neuroendocrine (carcinoid) tumor, not a GIST. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Appendix). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: somatostatin analogs such as octreotide are used to treat carcinoid tumors (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Octreotide). COMLEX: conventional adenomas and adenocarcinomas of the appendix can cause obstruction that mimics acute appendicitis (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 4 — portal hypertension and portosystemic varices","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Mucinous neoplasms occur in the appendix but are less common","D":"The most common appendiceal tumor is the well-differentiated"}},{"id":"sdl33_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the symptoms, risk factors, and diagnostic criteria associated with appendicitis (McBurney sign) as well as symptoms and risk factors of hemorrhoids and tumors of the anal canal and appendix.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the major risk factor for HPV-associated squamous cell carcinoma of the anal canal?","choices":{"A":"Receptive anal intercourse","B":"Chronic laxative overuse","C":"Portal venous hypertension","D":"Low dietary fiber intake","E":"Untreated ulcerative colitis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Anal squamous cell carcinoma is frequently associated with high-risk HPV strains, and the major risk factor for these HPV-associated lesions is anal sex. Low-risk HPV strains cause condyloma acuminatum. Portal hypertension predisposes to hemorrhoids, not HPV-related anal squamous carcinoma. Low fiber intake is a colorectal adenocarcinoma risk, not a risk for HPV-driven anal squamous carcinoma. Ulcerative colitis raises colonic adenocarcinoma risk, not HPV-associated anal squamous carcinoma. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Anal canal). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: squamous cell carcinoma arises below the pectinate line, while adenocarcinoma arises above it (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Pectinate line). COMLEX: basaloid anal squamous cell carcinoma has been called cloacogenic carcinoma (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 4 — portal hypertension and portosystemic varices","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Portal hypertension predisposes to hemorrhoids, not HPV-rela","D":"Low fiber intake is a colorectal adenocarcinoma risk, not a ","E":"Ulcerative colitis raises colonic adenocarcinoma risk, not H"}},{"id":"sdl33_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recognize the morphology, risk factors, and complications of colorectal adenomas and understand the relationship between size and malignancy.","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman has a 3 cm sessile colonic polyp with long, slender surface projections on biopsy. Her daughter asks what best predicts whether such a growth harbors cancer. Which feature is most important?","choices":{"A":"Goblet cell number","B":"Degree of inflammation","C":"Lesion diameter","D":"Tubular gland pattern","E":"Patient sex and age"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Size is the most important characteristic correlating with malignancy in an adenoma. Villous architecture correlates with cancer partly because villous adenomas are larger, and architectural category has little significance in isolation. Architectural pattern has little significance alone; size is the main predictor of cancer in an adenoma. Villous adenomas harbor cancer more often than tubular ones, but this tracks with their larger size; inflammation is not a predictor. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenomas). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: tubular adenomas have less malignant potential than villous adenomas (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colonic polyps). COMLEX: tubular adenomas tend to be small pedunculated polyps of rounded glands (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 3 — gastric polyps and precursor epithelial lesions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Architectural pattern has little significance alone; size is","B":"Villous adenomas harbor cancer more often than tubular ones,"}},{"id":"sdl33_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recognize the morphology, risk factors, and complications of colorectal adenomas and understand the relationship between size and malignancy.","batch":2,"stem":"A 59-year-old man has three colonic adenomas removed. One shows high-grade dysplasia and the others show low-grade dysplasia. How does the high-grade lesion affect cancer risk in his remaining polyps?","choices":{"A":"Higher risk in other polyps","B":"Unchanged risk in other polyps","C":"Field cancerization of colon","D":"Germline repair defect implied","E":"Doubled risk in other polyps"},"correct":"B","explanation":"High-grade dysplasia is a risk factor for cancer in that individual polyp but does not increase cancer risk in other polyps within that patient. High-grade dysplasia raises risk only in that polyp, not in his other polyps. High-grade dysplasia does not double risk in other polyps; it affects only the polyp containing it. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenomas). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 33 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: adenomatous polyps progress through an adenoma-carcinoma sequence with APC loss followed by KRAS mutation (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colorectal cancer). COMLEX: high-grade dysplasia raises cancer risk within the individual polyp (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 3 — chronic reflux injury and Barrett dysplasia","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"High-grade dysplasia raises risk only in that polyp, not in ","E":"High-grade dysplasia does not double risk in other polyps; i"}},{"id":"sdl33_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recognize the morphology, risk factors, and complications of colorectal adenomas and understand the relationship between size and malignancy.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old woman has small adenomas removed at each of several surveillance endoscopies. She asks why repeated procedures lower her colon cancer risk. Which explanation is best?","choices":{"A":"Ablation of mucosal lymphatics","B":"Reversal of APC mutations","C":"Induction of mucosal immunity","D":"Removal of precursor polyps","E":"Shrinkage of hyperplastic polyps"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Adenomas are precursors of colorectal adenocarcinoma, and removed polyps do not progress to adenocarcinoma, so repeated surveillance is both diagnostic and therapeutic. Most adenomas do not progress, and no tool identifies those that will. Polypectomy does not ablate lymphatics; it lowers risk by removing adenomas before they progress. Endoscopy cannot reverse APC mutations; it removes precursor polyps. Hyperplastic polyps have no malignant potential, so shrinking them would not lower cancer risk. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenomas). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: average-risk screening begins at age 45 (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colorectal cancer). COMLEX: adenomas develop in about 30% of Western adults by age 60 years (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — colonic epithelial renewal and stem cells","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Polypectomy does not ablate lymphatics; it lowers risk by re","B":"Endoscopy cannot reverse APC mutations; it removes precursor","E":"Hyperplastic polyps have no malignant potential, so shrinkin"}},{"id":"sdl33_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the genetics, diagnostic criteria, polyp location (pancolonic), timing (teenager), morphology, and complications of familial adenomatous polyposis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old woman with familial adenomatous polyposis had a prophylactic colectomy at age 20. Which site most needs continued endoscopic surveillance for adenomas?","choices":{"A":"Ampulla of Vater","B":"Distal esophagus","C":"Gallbladder fundus","D":"Pancreatic tail","E":"Hepatic hilum"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Colectomy prevents colorectal cancer in FAP, but patients remain at risk elsewhere. The ampulla of Vater and the stomach are common extracolonic sites of adenomas and, if untreated, cancer. The distal esophagus is not an FAP adenoma site; extracolonic adenomas favor the stomach, duodenum, and ampulla. The gallbladder is not a recognized FAP adenoma site; surveillance targets the stomach, duodenum, and ampulla. The pancreatic tail is not an FAP adenoma site; the ampulla of Vater is. The hepatic hilum is not an FAP adenoma site; the ampulla of Vater is. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Familial adenomatous polyposis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: without prophylactic colectomy, 100% of FAP patients progress to colorectal cancer (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: untreated FAP leads to colorectal adenocarcinoma often before age 30 and in nearly all untreated patients by age 50 (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 4 — location-dependent presentation of pancreatic head and periampullary tumors","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The distal esophagus is not an FAP adenoma site; extracoloni","C":"The gallbladder is not a recognized FAP adenoma site; survei","D":"The pancreatic tail is not an FAP adenoma site; the ampulla ","E":"The hepatic hilum is not an FAP adenoma site; the ampulla of"}},{"id":"sdl33_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the genetics, diagnostic criteria, polyp location (pancolonic), timing (teenager), morphology, and complications of familial adenomatous polyposis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 42-year-old man has about 30 colonic adenomas plus several serrated and hyperplastic polyps. His parents have normal screening exams, but his brother has a similar polyp burden. APC testing is negative. Which gene is most likely mutated?","choices":{"A":"STK11","B":"SMAD4","C":"PTEN","D":"MSH6","E":"MUTYH"},"correct":"E","explanation":"MUTYH-associated polyposis is an autosomal recessive base-excision repair disorder in patients without APC loss. It has fewer than 100 polyps that appear later, with frequent sessile serrated and hyperplastic polyps. STK11 mutations cause Peutz-Jeghers hamartomas, not adenomas with serrated polyps. SMAD4 mutations cause autosomal dominant juvenile polyposis, not recessive adenomatous polyposis. PTEN mutations cause Cowden hamartomas, not adenomas with recessive inheritance. MSH6 is a Lynch syndrome gene, which causes few polyps with dominant inheritance, not about 30 adenomas in siblings. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Familial adenomatous polyposis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 33 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MUTYH-associated polyposis is also associated with duodenal adenomas and ovarian and bladder cancers (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: colon cancer development in MUTYH-associated polyposis is delayed relative to FAP (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 4 — hyperplastic and inflammatory intestinal polyps","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"STK11 mutations cause Peutz-Jeghers hamartomas, not adenomas","B":"SMAD4 mutations cause autosomal dominant juvenile polyposis,","C":"PTEN mutations cause Cowden hamartomas, not adenomas with re","D":"MSH6 is a Lynch syndrome gene, which causes few polyps with "}},{"id":"sdl33_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the genetics, diagnostic criteria, polyp location (pancolonic), timing (teenager), morphology, and complications of familial adenomatous polyposis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old woman with numerous colonic adenomas also has firm bony masses of the skull and a fibrous mass in the abdominal wall. Which variant best describes her disease?","choices":{"A":"Turcot syndrome","B":"Cowden syndrome","C":"Juvenile polyposis","D":"Gardner syndrome","E":"Li Fraumeni syndrome"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Gardner syndrome is an APC-related FAP variant with multiple adenomas plus osteomas, thyroid and desmoid tumors, and skin cysts. Turcot syndrome adds CNS tumors such as medulloblastoma. Cowden syndrome is a PTEN hamartoma syndrome, not an APC variant with osteomas and desmoids. Juvenile polyposis features hamartomatous polyps, not adenomas with osteomas and desmoids. Li-Fraumeni syndrome is an inherited TP53 mutation with a broad tumor spectrum led by sarcomas, breast cancers, leukemias, and brain tumors, not adenomas with osteomas. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Familial adenomatous polyposis) (Table 17.10). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Turcot syndrome is FAP or Lynch syndrome plus a malignant CNS tumor such as medulloblastoma or glioma (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: attenuated FAP is also caused by APC mutation (Robbins Ch. 17, Table 17.10).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 5 — hamartomatous polyposis syndromes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Cowden syndrome is a PTEN hamartoma syndrome, not an APC var","C":"Juvenile polyposis features hamartomatous polyps, not adenom","E":"Li-Fraumeni syndrome is an inherited TP53 mutation with a br"}},{"id":"sdl33_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the associated mutation, complication, associated conditions (endometrial, ovarian, and skin cancers), and location (proximal colon) of hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer (aka Lynch syndrome).","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman has endometrial carcinoma. Her mother had colon cancer at 45, and tumor staining shows loss of a mismatch repair protein. Which mutational mechanism most likely drives her tumors?","choices":{"A":"Chromosomal instability","B":"Telomere attrition crisis","C":"Oncogene amplification","D":"Whole chromosome aneuploidy","E":"Microsatellite instability"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In HNPCC, loss of the second mismatch repair allele raises mutation rates up to 1000-fold, mostly in short repeats called microsatellites. Chromosomal instability is the hallmark of the APC/beta catenin pathway. Whole-chromosome aneuploidy reflects chromosomal instability of the APC pathway, not mismatch repair loss. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer); Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenocarcinoma). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lynch syndrome mismatch repair defects cause subsequent microsatellite instability (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Lynch syndrome). COMLEX: MSI can target TGFBR2 and BAX, promoting growth and survival of abnormal clones (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 1 — chemotherapy classes for colorectal cancer","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Whole-chromosome aneuploidy reflects chromosomal instability"}},{"id":"sdl33_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the associated mutation, complication, associated conditions (endometrial, ovarian, and skin cancers), and location (proximal colon) of hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer (aka Lynch syndrome).","batch":2,"stem":"A 78-year-old woman with no family history of cancer has a mucinous large bowel adenocarcinoma with peritumoral lymphocytes and mismatch repair deficiency. Which finding best supports a sporadic origin rather than a hereditary syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Mucinous differentiation","B":"Advanced patient age","C":"Peritumoral lymphocytes","D":"BRAF activating mutation","E":"Repair protein loss"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Sporadic mismatch repair–deficient cancers often arise through the CpG island hypermethylation pathway, which silences MLH1. The combination of MSI, BRAF mutation, and methylation is the signature of this pathway. Mucinous change, lymphocytes, and right-sided location occur in both. Repair protein loss occurs in both Lynch and sporadic MSI tumors, so it cannot distinguish them. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenocarcinoma). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: serrated polyps follow the CIMP pathway, which may silence MLH1 and lead to BRAF mutation (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colonic polyps). COMLEX: KRAS and TP53 are typically intact in CIMP cancers (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 4 — hyperplastic and inflammatory intestinal polyps","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Repair protein loss occurs in both Lynch and sporadic MSI tu"}},{"id":"sdl33_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the associated mutation, complication, associated conditions (endometrial, ovarian, and skin cancers), and location (proximal colon) of hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer (aka Lynch syndrome).","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman's brother had colon cancer at 38 and her mother had endometrial cancer at 50. Genetic testing confirms a germline mismatch repair mutation. Which other cancer is she at increased risk for?","choices":{"A":"Ovarian carcinoma","B":"Breast carcinoma","C":"Thyroid carcinoma","D":"Lung carcinoma","E":"Cervical carcinoma"},"correct":"A","explanation":"HNPCC clusters cancers of the colorectum, endometrium, stomach, ovary, ureters, brain, small bowel, hepatobiliary tract, pancreas, and skin. The objective names endometrial, ovarian, and skin cancers. Thyroid cancer is linked to Gardner syndrome, not Lynch syndrome. The cancer sites of Lynch syndrome include the endometrium, ovary, stomach, and urinary tract; breast is not among them. The cancers that cluster in Lynch syndrome include the colorectum, endometrium, stomach, ovary, ureters, brain, small bowel, hepatobiliary tract, pancreas, and skin; lung is not on the list. The cancer sites of Lynch syndrome include the endometrium and ovary, but not the cervix. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer); SDL 33 Elentra Objective 3. Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the Lynch syndrome mnemonic CEOS stands for colon, endometrial, ovarian, and skin cancers (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Lynch syndrome). COMLEX: identifying germline mismatch repair mutations matters for genetic counseling and second malignancy risk (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 4 — gastric adenocarcinoma risk factors and precursors","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Thyroid cancer is linked to Gardner syndrome, not Lynch synd","B":"The cancer sites of Lynch syndrome include the endometrium, ","D":"The cancers that cluster in Lynch syndrome include the color","E":"The cancer sites of Lynch syndrome include the endometrium a"}},{"id":"sdl33_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the morphology, pathogenesis, epidemiology, risk factors, symptoms, and diagnosis (iron deficiency anemia), of adenocarcinoma of the colon (colorectal cancer).","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman has fatigue. Hemoglobin is 9.8 g/dL (reference 12 to 16), MCV is 72 fL (reference 80 to 100), and ferritin is 8 ng/mL (reference 15 to 150). She denies visible bleeding. Which next step is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Bone marrow biopsy","B":"Colonoscopy referral","C":"Hemoglobin electrophoresis","D":"Empiric oral iron","E":"Serum B12 level"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Right-sided colon cancers often present with fatigue and weakness from iron deficiency anemia. Iron deficiency anemia in an older male or postmenopausal female is GI cancer until proven otherwise. Bone marrow biopsy is unnecessary when low ferritin already confirms iron deficiency, whose source must be sought by colonoscopy. Hemoglobin electrophoresis evaluates thalassemia, but a low ferritin confirms iron deficiency instead. Empiric oral iron without colonoscopy risks missing GI cancer, which iron deficiency in an older woman implies until proven otherwise. B12 deficiency causes macrocytic anemia, whereas her microcytic, low-ferritin anemia points to GI blood loss. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenocarcinoma). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: iron deficiency anemia in males older than 50 and postmenopausal females raises suspicion for colorectal cancer (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colorectal cancer). COMLEX: proximal colon tumors grow as polypoid masses that rarely obstruct (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — intestinal absorption of iron, calcium, and fat-soluble vitamins","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Bone marrow biopsy is unnecessary when low ferritin already ","C":"Hemoglobin electrophoresis evaluates thalassemia, but a low ","D":"Empiric oral iron without colonoscopy risks missing GI cance","E":"B12 deficiency causes macrocytic anemia, whereas her microcy"}},{"id":"sdl33_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the morphology, pathogenesis, epidemiology, risk factors, symptoms, and diagnosis (iron deficiency anemia), of adenocarcinoma of the colon (colorectal cancer).","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old man has months of narrowing stool caliber, crampy left lower quadrant pain, and bloating. Imaging shows a short constricting sigmoid mass. Which gross appearance is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Sessile serrated plaque","B":"Large pedunculated villous polyp","C":"Diffuse linitis plastica","D":"Flat atrophic mucosa","E":"Annular napkin-ring lesion"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Distal colon carcinomas tend to be annular napkin-ring constrictions that narrow the lumen, sometimes to obstruction. Left-sided tumors produce visible bleeding, bowel habit change, and left lower quadrant cramping. A sessile serrated plaque is a right-sided precursor lesion, not a constricting sigmoid mass. Large polypoid masses are typical of the right colon, whereas left-sided cancers are annular and constricting. Flat atrophic mucosa does not produce a constricting mass or narrowed stools. Linitis plastica is the rigid leather bottle wall of diffuse infiltrative gastric cancer, not a short constricting sigmoid mass. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenocarcinoma). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: left-sided colorectal cancer is associated with hematochezia and obstruction (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colorectal cancer). COMLEX: invasive colon cancer elicits a desmoplastic response responsible for its firm consistency (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A sessile serrated plaque is a right-sided precursor lesion,","B":"Large polypoid masses are typical of the right colon, wherea","D":"Flat atrophic mucosa does not produce a constricting mass or","C":"Linitis plastica is the rigid leather bottle wall of diffuse"}},{"id":"sdl33_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the morphology, pathogenesis, epidemiology, risk factors, symptoms, and diagnosis (iron deficiency anemia), of adenocarcinoma of the colon (colorectal cancer).","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man takes daily low-dose aspirin after a myocardial infarction and asks whether it affects his colon cancer risk. Which mechanism most likely explains a protective effect?","choices":{"A":"Lipoxygenase inhibition","B":"Thromboxane receptor blockade","C":"Cyclooxygenase 2 inhibition","D":"Platelet P2Y12 blockade","E":"Phospholipase A2 inhibition"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Aspirin and other NSAIDs are protective, likely through inhibition of COX-2, which is highly expressed in 90% of colorectal carcinomas and 40% to 90% of adenomas. COX-2 drives prostaglandin E2, which promotes epithelial proliferation. Aspirin and NSAIDs are thought to protect against colon cancer by inhibiting COX-2, which is overexpressed in most colorectal carcinomas and drives PGE2-mediated proliferation; lipoxygenase is not the target. Aspirin lowers thromboxane A2 synthesis by irreversibly acetylating cyclooxygenase rather than blocking the thromboxane receptor. Irreversible P2Y12 ADP receptor blockade is the mechanism of clopidogrel, prasugrel, and ticlopidine, not aspirin. Phospholipase A2 is inhibited by corticosteroids through annexins (lipocortins), not by aspirin. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenocarcinoma). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 33 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: overexpression of COX-2 has been linked to colorectal cancer, and NSAIDs may be chemopreventive (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colorectal cancer). COMLEX: some NSAIDs cause polyp regression in FAP patients whose rectum was left in place (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 2 — NSAIDs and H. pylori in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"Aspirin and NSAIDs are thought to protect against colon canc","B":"Aspirin lowers thromboxane A2 synthesis by irreversibly acet","D":"Irreversible P2Y12 ADP receptor blockade is the mechanism of","E":"Phospholipase A2 is inhibited by corticosteroids through ann"}},{"id":"sdl33_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the symptoms, risk factors, and diagnostic criteria associated with appendicitis (McBurney sign) as well as symptoms and risk factors of hemorrhoids and tumors of the anal canal and appendix.","batch":2,"stem":"A 17-year-old boy has periumbilical pain that moved to the right lower quadrant, then nausea and low-grade fever. His resected appendix is examined. Which histologic finding is required for the diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Muscularis propria neutrophils","B":"Mucosal neutrophils with ulcers","C":"Lymphoid follicle hyperplasia","D":"Submucosal eosinophil clusters","E":"Granulomas in lamina propria"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Mucosal neutrophils and focal superficial ulceration are not specific. Diagnosis of acute appendicitis requires neutrophilic infiltration of the muscularis propria. Lymphoid hyperplasia can obstruct the appendix in young patients, but it is a cause rather than the diagnostic finding. Granulomas in the lamina propria suggest Crohn disease or infection, not acute appendicitis. The diagnosis of acute appendicitis requires neutrophils in the muscularis propria; submucosal eosinophils are not the criterion. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Appendix). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: appendicitis can be due to fecalith obstruction in adults or lymphoid hyperplasia in children (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Appendicitis). COMLEX: complications of appendicitis include pylephlebitis, portal venous thrombosis, abscess, and bacteremia (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 → Obj 1 — bacterial enterocolitis, including Yersinia mimicking appendicitis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Lymphoid hyperplasia can obstruct the appendix in young pati","E":"Granulomas in the lamina propria suggest Crohn disease or in","D":"The diagnosis of acute appendicitis requires neutrophils in "}},{"id":"sdl33_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the symptoms, risk factors, and diagnostic criteria associated with appendicitis (McBurney sign) as well as symptoms and risk factors of hemorrhoids and tumors of the anal canal and appendix.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with chronic constipation and straining notices bright red blood on toilet tissue. Anoscopy shows dilated anal venous cushions. Which condition must still be excluded?","choices":{"A":"Anal fistula","B":"Pilonidal cyst","C":"Colon carcinoma","D":"Perianal abscess","E":"Rectal prolapse"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hemorrhoids present with pain and bright red blood on toilet tissue, but colon cancer can coexist and must be excluded. Straining at defecation and venous stasis of pregnancy are the main predisposing influences. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hemorrhoids). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: internal hemorrhoids receive visceral innervation and are not painful (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Pectinate line). COMLEX: hemorrhoidal bleeding can be treated by sclerotherapy, rubber band ligation, or infrared coagulation (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 5 — defecation reflex and anal sphincter control","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the symptoms, risk factors, and diagnostic criteria associated with appendicitis (McBurney sign) as well as symptoms and risk factors of hemorrhoids and tumors of the anal canal and appendix.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman has progressive abdominal distention. Surgery finds tenacious, semisolid mucin filling the peritoneal cavity and a ruptured, mucin-distended appendix with pushing tumor margins. Which primary lesion is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Appendiceal carcinoid tumor","B":"Appendiceal mucinous neoplasm","C":"Ovarian serous carcinoma","D":"Peritoneal mesothelioma","E":"Gastric signet ring cell carcinoma"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Appendiceal mucinous neoplasms occur in adults, most often in the sixth decade, with pushing margins and extracellular mucin that can rupture the appendix. Peritoneal spread causes pseudomyxoma peritonei. An appendiceal carcinoid is a small solid tip mass that rarely spreads, not a mucin-distended ruptured appendix. Pseudomyxoma peritonei with a ruptured, mucin-distended appendix and pushing margins points to an appendiceal mucinous neoplasm, not gastric signet ring carcinoma. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Appendix). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: peritoneal spread of appendiceal mucinous neoplasms can produce pseudomyxoma peritonei (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: GNAS, encoding Gαs, is frequently mutated in appendiceal mucinous neoplasms and is thought to drive excess mucin production (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 3 — mucinous pancreatic cystic neoplasms","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"An appendiceal carcinoid is a small solid tip mass that rare","E":"Pseudomyxoma peritonei with a ruptured, mucin-distended appe"}},{"id":"trial_sdl33_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recognize the morphology, risk factors, and complications of colorectal adenomas and understand the relationship between size and malignancy.","batch":3,"stem":"A 60-year-old man has a 6 mm pedunculated polyp on screening endoscopy that proves to be a tubular adenoma. Which gross texture is typical of this lesion?","choices":{"A":"Velvety raspberry-like surface","B":"Smooth glistening dome","C":"Umbilicated central pit","D":"Firm rubbery nodule","E":"Translucent cystic bleb"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Colorectal adenomas can be pedunculated or sessile, and both have a surface texture resembling velvet or a raspberry. Pedunculated adenomas have slender fibromuscular stalks with submucosal vessels. A small, smooth surface is typical of hyperplastic polyps rather than adenomas. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenomas). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colonic polyps may be flat, sessile, or pedunculated (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colonic polyps). COMLEX: the stalk of a pedunculated adenoma is usually covered by nonneoplastic epithelium (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 5 — large-intestine lymphatic drainage and adenoma spread","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"A small, smooth surface is typical of hyperplastic polyps ra"}},{"id":"trial_sdl33_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the genetics, diagnostic criteria, polyp location (pancolonic), timing (teenager), morphology, and complications of familial adenomatous polyposis.","batch":3,"stem":"A 15-year-old girl with a known germline APC mutation has random biopsies of normal-appearing colonic mucosa. They show isolated foci of one or two dysplastic crypts. What are these foci?","choices":{"A":"Hyperplastic polyps","B":"Inflammatory polyps","C":"Hamartomatous polyps","D":"Microscopic adenomas","E":"Lymphoid aggregates"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In FAP, flat or depressed adenomas are prevalent, and microscopic adenomas of one or two dysplastic crypts occur in otherwise normal-appearing mucosa. Hyperplastic polyps are composed of mature goblet and absorptive cells and are not dysplastic. Inflammatory polyps arise from cycles of injury and healing and are nonneoplastic. Hamartomatous polyps are disorganized, nonneoplastic masses of mature cells rather than dysplastic crypts. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Familial adenomatous polyposis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: FAP follows the two-hit hypothesis for the APC tumor suppressor gene (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: FAP patients develop numerous colorectal adenomas as teenagers (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — colonic epithelial renewal and stem cells","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Hyperplastic polyps are composed of mature goblet and absorp","B":"Inflammatory polyps arise from cycles of injury and healing ","C":"Hamartomatous polyps are disorganized, nonneoplastic masses "}},{"id":"trial_sdl33_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the associated mutation, complication, associated conditions (endometrial, ovarian, and skin cancers), and location (proximal colon) of hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer (aka Lynch syndrome).","batch":3,"stem":"A 31-year-old man carries a germline mismatch repair gene mutation, and his colon looks unremarkable. Which event in a single crypt cell is needed before mismatch repair fails?","choices":{"A":"Gain of oncogene copies","B":"Paternal gene imprinting","C":"Mitochondrial DNA depletion","D":"Telomerase gene silencing","E":"Loss of the normal allele"},"correct":"E","explanation":"HNPCC patients inherit one mutant gene and one normal allele. When the second copy is lost through mutation or epigenetic silencing, mismatch repair fails and mutations accumulate. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lynch syndrome is inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Lynch syndrome). COMLEX: HNPCC patients develop cancer at younger ages than sporadic colon cancer patients (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 4 — gastric adenocarcinoma risk factors and precursors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl33_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the morphology, pathogenesis, epidemiology, risk factors, symptoms, and diagnosis (iron deficiency anemia), of adenocarcinoma of the colon (colorectal cancer).","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman has a cecal adenocarcinoma found during evaluation for fatigue. How does a tumor at this site typically grow?","choices":{"A":"Linitis-like infiltration","B":"Diffuse wall thickening","C":"Polypoid exophytic mass","D":"Flat ulcerated plaque","E":"Submucosal cystic mass"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Proximal colon tumors often grow as polypoid, exophytic masses along one wall of the large-caliber cecum and ascending colon, and they rarely cause obstruction. Annular, circumferential napkin-ring growth that obstructs is typical of distal (left) colon tumors. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenocarcinoma). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ascending colon cancer presents as an exophytic mass with weight loss (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colorectal cancer). COMLEX: some poorly differentiated colorectal carcinomas form few glands (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Annular, circumferential napkin-ring growth that obstructs i"}},{"id":"trial_sdl33_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the symptoms, risk factors, and diagnostic criteria associated with appendicitis (McBurney sign) as well as symptoms and risk factors of hemorrhoids and tumors of the anal canal and appendix.","batch":3,"stem":"A 20-year-old man has a perforated appendix removed. A week later he has spiking fevers, right upper quadrant pain, and septic thrombosis of the portal vein. Which complication has occurred?","choices":{"A":"Ascending cholangitis","B":"Hepatic sarcoidosis","C":"Viral hepatitis","D":"Pylephlebitis","E":"Subphrenic hematoma"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Complications of appendicitis include pylephlebitis, portal venous thrombosis, liver abscess, and bacteremia. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Appendix). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: appendicitis can perforate and cause peritonitis (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Appendicitis). COMLEX: surgeons accept removing some normal appendices rather than delaying resection of a diseased one (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 1 — circulatory disorders of the liver","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl33_q06","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the morphology, pathogenesis, epidemiology, risk factors, symptoms, and diagnosis (iron deficiency anemia), of adenocarcinoma of the colon (colorectal cancer).","batch":3,"stem":"A 70-year-old man has a resected sigmoid adenocarcinoma. He asks which tumor features best predict his prognosis. Which is one of the two most important?","choices":{"A":"Histologic tumor grade","B":"Depth of invasion","C":"Age at diagnosis","D":"Tumor location","E":"Mucin production"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The two most important prognostic factors are depth of invasion and lymph node metastases. Poorly differentiated and mucinous histology are associated with worse prognosis but matter less. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenocarcinoma). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colorectal cancer screening alternatives include FIT, FIT-fecal DNA, and CT colonography (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colorectal cancer). COMLEX: the overall 5-year survival for colorectal cancer in the United States is 65% (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 5 — large-intestine lymphatic drainage and adenoma spread","isHighYield":true},{"id":"grid_sdl33_q01","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the associated mutation, complication, associated conditions (endometrial, ovarian, and skin cancers), and location (proximal colon) of hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer (aka Lynch syndrome).","batch":4,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman has 2 months of fatigue. Hemoglobin is 9.8 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–16.0 g/dL) with microcytic indices. Colonoscopy shows a mass, and biopsy shows a mucinous adenocarcinoma with signet-ring cells and dense intraepithelial lymphocytes. Her mother had endometrial cancer at age 46, and a maternal uncle had colon cancer at age 44. She has no history of inflammatory bowel disease. Which of the following sets of features best characterizes the disorder underlying this patient's cancer?","grid":{"headers":["Inheritance","Microsatellite status","Usual location"]},"choices":{"A":["Autosomal dominant","MSI-high","Proximal colon"],"B":["Autosomal dominant","Stable","Pancolonic"],"C":["Sporadic","MSI-high","Proximal colon"],"D":["Sporadic","Stable","Distal colon"],"E":["Acquired (colitis)","Stable","Pancolonic"]},"correct":"A","explanation":"Choice A (autosomal dominant, MSI-high, proximal colon) is correct. Hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer (Lynch syndrome) is autosomal dominant and caused by germline loss-of-function mutations in DNA mismatch repair genes, most often MSH2 or MLH1; loss of the second allele causes microsatellite instability. The notes place these cancers predominantly in the proximal (right) colon at young ages, describe mucinous and signet-ring differentiation with intraepithelial lymphocytes, and list endometrial cancer as the most common extraintestinal malignancy in women. Choice B (autosomal dominant, stable, pancolonic) is familial adenomatous polyposis, an APC germline disorder with at least 100 adenomas throughout the colon beginning in adolescence and progressing through the chromosomally unstable APC/beta-catenin pathway. Choice C (sporadic, MSI-high, proximal colon) is the sporadic CIMP (serrated) pathway, in which CpG island hypermethylation silences MLH1 and BRAF is activated, arising from sessile serrated lesions without a family pattern. Choice D (sporadic, stable, distal colon) is sporadic adenocarcinoma through the classic APC, KRAS, SMAD4, TP53 sequence with chromosomal instability, which accounts for about 80% of sporadic cases. Choice E (acquired from colitis, stable, pancolonic) is colitis-associated cancer arising in long-standing ulcerative pancolitis, an acquired risk factor. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 33, sections 2–4: FAP, Lynch syndrome, colorectal cancer pathways and risk factors); Robbins Ch. 17 (colonic neoplasia). The proximal location of CIMP tumors, the distal predominance of CIN-pathway sporadic cancers, and the microsatellite-stable, pancolonic profile of colitis-associated cancer are from Robbins and are not stated in the notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lynch syndrome = germline mismatch repair defect (MLH1, MSH2) → MSI-high, right-sided colon cancer before 50, plus endometrial and ovarian cancer (Exam 3 notes, SDL 33). COMLEX: sporadic MSI-high cancer from MLH1 promoter hypermethylation with BRAF mutation is the main look-alike.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 2 — FAP genetics and pancolonic polyposis","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":34,"title":"SDL 34 — Nutritional Diseases","questions":[{"id":"sdl34_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate marasmus and kwashiorkor based on their underlying nutritional deficiencies, affected protein compartments, clinical manifestations, and associated laboratory findings.","batch":1,"stem":"Which protein compartment is depleted most severely in kwashiorkor?","choices":{"A":"Somatic protein compartment","B":"Bone mineral compartment","C":"Plasma lipid compartment","D":"Intracellular water compartment","E":"Visceral protein compartment"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Kwashiorkor results when protein deprivation is relatively greater than the reduction in total calories, and it causes marked loss of the visceral protein compartment, chiefly the liver, with resulting hypoalbuminemia. Marasmus predominantly depletes the somatic compartment of skeletal muscle. Bone mineral is not the compartment targeted in kwashiorkor, which depletes visceral protein, chiefly the liver. Plasma lipid is not a protein compartment; kwashiorkor depletes visceral protein stores. Intracellular water is not the depleted compartment; kwashiorkor instead causes edema from visceral protein loss. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Protein Energy Malnutrition). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the kwashiorkor mnemonic MEALS lists malnutrition, edema, anemia, liver malfunction, and skin lesions (First Aid Biochemistry, Malnutrition). COMLEX: serum albumin and transferrin reflect the visceral protein compartment (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — chylomicron formation and lipoprotein transport","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Bone mineral is not the compartment targeted in kwashiorkor,","C":"Plasma lipid is not a protein compartment; kwashiorkor deple","D":"Intracellular water is not the depleted compartment; kwashio"}},{"id":"sdl34_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate marasmus and kwashiorkor based on their underlying nutritional deficiencies, affected protein compartments, clinical manifestations, and associated laboratory findings.","batch":1,"stem":"Marasmus is defined when a child's weight falls to about what percentage of normal for sex, height, and age?","choices":{"A":"About 30 percent","B":"About 60 percent","C":"About 45 percent","D":"About 75 percent","E":"About 90 percent"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Robbins defines marasmus as weight falling to about 60% of normal for sex, height, and age, with growth retardation and loss of muscle and subcutaneous fat. In kwashiorkor, weight is typically 60% to 80% of normal, and the true loss is masked by edema. About 30 percent is far below the threshold; marasmus is defined at about 60% of normal weight. About 45 percent is below the defining threshold of about 60% of normal weight. About 75 percent falls within the kwashiorkor range of 60% to 80%, not the marasmus threshold. About 90 percent is near normal weight and does not define marasmus. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Marasmus). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 34 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: absence of edema distinguishes marasmus from kwashiorkor (First Aid Biochemistry, Malnutrition). COMLEX: in marasmus the head appears large relative to the emaciated body (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — chylomicron formation and lipoprotein transport","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"About 30 percent is far below the threshold; marasmus is def","C":"About 45 percent is below the defining threshold of about 60","D":"About 75 percent falls within the kwashiorkor range of 60% t","E":"About 90 percent is near normal weight and does not define m"}},{"id":"sdl34_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate marasmus and kwashiorkor based on their underlying nutritional deficiencies, affected protein compartments, clinical manifestations, and associated laboratory findings.","batch":1,"stem":"The enlarged fatty liver in kwashiorkor results mainly from which process?","choices":{"A":"Increased delivery of dietary fat","B":"Hepatic venous congestion","C":"Hepatocyte iron accumulation","D":"Reduced apolipoprotein synthesis","E":"Excess hepatic glycogen storage"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In kwashiorkor the liver is enlarged and fatty because synthesis of the carrier protein components of lipoproteins is reduced, so triglyceride is not exported efficiently. The diet is typically carbohydrate based rather than fat rich. Iron accumulation is not the cause; the fatty liver comes from failure to make lipoprotein carrier proteins. Excess glycogen is not the problem; triglyceride accumulates because apolipoprotein synthesis falls. Hepatic venous congestion produces centrilobular hemorrhagic necrosis, the mottled nutmeg liver, not a diffusely fatty liver. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Kwashiorkor). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 34 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid attributes the fatty change in kwashiorkor to decreased apolipoprotein synthesis (First Aid Biochemistry, Malnutrition). COMLEX: superimposed fatty change can occur in marasmus as well, but it is characteristic of kwashiorkor (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — chylomicron formation and lipoprotein transport","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"C":"Iron accumulation is not the cause; the fatty liver comes fr","E":"Excess glycogen is not the problem; triglyceride accumulates","B":"Hepatic venous congestion produces centrilobular hemorrhagic"}},{"id":"sdl34_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the biochemical functions, metabolism, and clinical manifestations of deficiencies in fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K), particularly in the context of gastrointestinal malabsorption.","batch":1,"stem":"Acute vitamin A toxicity produces headache, vomiting, and blurred vision that can mimic which condition?","choices":{"A":"Bacterial meningitis","B":"Pseudotumor cerebri","C":"Migraine with aura","D":"Subarachnoid hemorrhage","E":"Hypertensive encephalopathy"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Acute toxicity from a large dose of vitamin A causes headache, dizziness, vomiting, stupor, and blurred vision, symptoms that may be confused with a brain tumor, known as pseudotumor cerebri. Chronic toxicity causes weight loss, anorexia, nausea, and bone and joint pain. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Vitamin A, Toxicity). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 34 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: vitamin A excess causes idiopathic intracranial hypertension, and isotretinoin is teratogenic (First Aid Biochemistry, Vitamin A). COMLEX: retinoic acid stimulates osteoclast production and activity, so chronic excess raises fracture risk (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins"},{"id":"sdl34_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the biochemical functions, metabolism, and clinical manifestations of deficiencies in fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K), particularly in the context of gastrointestinal malabsorption.","batch":1,"stem":"Vitamin K is required for hepatic carboxylation of which group of clotting factors?","choices":{"A":"Factors I, V, VIII, and XIII","B":"Factors VIII, IX, XI, and XII","C":"Factors II, VII, IX, and X","D":"Factors II, V, VIII, and X","E":"Factors III, VII, XI, and XIII"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Vitamin K is a cofactor for hepatic carboxylation of procoagulant factors II, VII, IX, and X and of proteins C and S. Deficiency produces a bleeding diathesis, especially in fat malabsorption and after broad antibiotic courses. Factors I, V, VIII, and XIII are not vitamin K dependent; the dependent factors are II, VII, IX, and X. Factors VIII, XI, and XII are not vitamin K dependent; only IX of this set is. Factors V and VIII are not vitamin K dependent, so this set is wrong despite including II and X. Factors III, XI, and XIII are not vitamin K dependent; only VII of this set is. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Vitamin K). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: warfarin blocks vitamin K epoxide reductase and lowers functional factor levels (First Aid Hematology, Warfarin). COMLEX: vitamin K is fat soluble, so biliary and pancreatic disease reduce its absorption (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Factors I, V, VIII, and XIII are not vitamin K dependent; th","B":"Factors VIII, XI, and XII are not vitamin K dependent; only ","D":"Factors V and VIII are not vitamin K dependent, so this set ","E":"Factors III, XI, and XIII are not vitamin K dependent; only "}},{"id":"sdl34_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the biochemical functions, metabolism, and clinical manifestations of deficiencies in fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K), particularly in the context of gastrointestinal malabsorption.","batch":1,"stem":"Which factor stimulates renal 1-alpha hydroxylase to increase active vitamin D production?","choices":{"A":"Parathyroid hormone","B":"Fibroblast growth factor 23","C":"Serum hypercalcemia","D":"Serum hyperphosphatemia","E":"Calcitriol excess"},"correct":"A","explanation":"After hepatic 25-hydroxylation, the kidney converts 25-hydroxyvitamin D to the active 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D. Renal 1-alpha hydroxylase is stimulated by PTH and hypophosphatemia and inhibited by 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D and FGF23. Hypocalcemia acts indirectly by raising PTH. Hypercalcemia suppresses PTH and therefore 1-alpha hydroxylase; it is hypocalcemia that raises PTH. Hyperphosphatemia does not stimulate the enzyme; hypophosphatemia does. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Vitamin D, Metabolism). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 34 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic kidney disease reduces renal activation of vitamin D (First Aid Biochemistry, Vitamin D). COMLEX: 25-hydroxyvitamin D is the major circulating form and the usual marker of vitamin D status (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"C":"Hypercalcemia suppresses PTH and therefore 1-alpha hydroxyla","D":"Hyperphosphatemia does not stimulate the enzyme; hypophospha"}},{"id":"sdl34_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Correlate deficiencies of water-soluble vitamins (B-complex and C) and essential trace minerals with their biochemical functions, characteristic clinical manifestations, and associated gastrointestinal disorders.","batch":1,"stem":"Riboflavin functions in the body after conversion to which coenzyme?","choices":{"A":"Nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide","B":"Thiamine pyrophosphate","C":"Pyridoxal phosphate","D":"Flavin adenine dinucleotide","E":"Tetrahydrofolic acid"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Riboflavin is converted to flavin mononucleotide and flavin adenine dinucleotide, which serve as cofactors for many enzymes in intermediary metabolism. Deficiency causes cheilosis, stomatitis, glossitis, dermatitis, and corneal vascularization. NAD is the coenzyme derived from niacin, not riboflavin. Thiamine pyrophosphate is the active form of vitamin B1, not riboflavin. Pyridoxal phosphate is the active form of vitamin B6, not riboflavin. Tetrahydrofolic acid is derived from folate, not riboflavin. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Table 9.9, Vitamins). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: riboflavin deficiency causes cheilosis and corneal vascularization, the two Cs of B2 (First Aid Biochemistry, Vitamin B2). COMLEX: niacin forms the nicotinamide coenzymes, while thiamine forms the pyrophosphate (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 4 — vitamin B12 absorption and terminal ileal disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"NAD is the coenzyme derived from niacin, not riboflavin.","B":"Thiamine pyrophosphate is the active form of vitamin B1, not","C":"Pyridoxal phosphate is the active form of vitamin B6, not ri","E":"Tetrahydrofolic acid is derived from folate, not riboflavin."}},{"id":"sdl34_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Correlate deficiencies of water-soluble vitamins (B-complex and C) and essential trace minerals with their biochemical functions, characteristic clinical manifestations, and associated gastrointestinal disorders.","batch":1,"stem":"Copper is an essential component of which enzyme needed for collagen cross-linking?","choices":{"A":"Lysyl oxidase","B":"Glutathione peroxidase","C":"Carbonic anhydrase","D":"Xanthine oxidase","E":"Alcohol dehydrogenase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Copper is a component of cytochrome c oxidase, dopamine beta hydroxylase, tyrosinase, lysyl oxidase, and other enzymes. Deficiency causes muscle weakness, neurologic defects, and abnormal collagen cross-linking, and it may follow excess zinc intake. Glutathione peroxidase is a selenium-containing antioxidant enzyme, not the copper enzyme of collagen cross-linking. Carbonic anhydrase is a zinc enzyme, not copper-dependent lysyl oxidase. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Table 9.10, Trace Elements). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Menkes disease impairs copper transport and lysyl oxidase activity (First Aid Biochemistry, Menkes disease). COMLEX: glutathione peroxidase depends on selenium, not copper (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 4 — vitamin B12 absorption and terminal ileal disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Glutathione peroxidase is a selenium-containing antioxidant ","C":"Carbonic anhydrase is a zinc enzyme, not copper-dependent ly"}},{"id":"sdl34_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Correlate deficiencies of water-soluble vitamins (B-complex and C) and essential trace minerals with their biochemical functions, characteristic clinical manifestations, and associated gastrointestinal disorders.","batch":1,"stem":"Selenium deficiency is the cause of which endemic cardiomyopathy?","choices":{"A":"Wet beriberi","B":"Chagas disease","C":"Keshan disease","D":"Kawasaki disease","E":"Loeffler endocarditis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Selenium is a component of glutathione peroxidase, an antioxidant enzyme. Deficiency causes myopathy and congestive cardiomyopathy known as Keshan disease, described in regions with selenium-poor soil. Wet beriberi is high-output heart failure from thiamine deficiency, not selenium deficiency. Chagas disease is a myocarditis caused by Trypanosoma cruzi parasitizing myofibers, not a selenium deficiency. Kawasaki disease is an arteritis of young children that damages coronary arteries, not a nutritional cardiomyopathy. Loeffler endomyocarditis is endomyocardial fibrosis driven by eosinophil products in hypereosinophilia, not selenium deficiency. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Table 9.10, Trace Elements). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: glutathione peroxidase is a free radical scavenging enzyme (First Aid Pathology, Free radical injury). COMLEX: the antioxidant role of selenium overlaps functionally with vitamin E (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 4 — vitamin B12 absorption and terminal ileal disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Wet beriberi is high-output heart failure from thiamine defi","B":"Chagas disease is a myocarditis caused by Trypanosoma cruzi ","D":"Kawasaki disease is an arteritis of young children that dama","E":"Loeffler endomyocarditis is endomyocardial fibrosis driven b"}},{"id":"sdl34_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the hormonal and hypothalamic mechanisms regulating appetite, satiety, and energy homeostasis, including the roles of leptin, ghrelin, GLP-1, and hypothalamic neuropeptides.","batch":1,"stem":"The central processing system that integrates peripheral signals of energy balance lies mainly in which hypothalamic nucleus?","choices":{"A":"Supraoptic nucleus","B":"Suprachiasmatic nucleus","C":"Preoptic nucleus","D":"Arcuate nucleus","E":"Mammillary nucleus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Robbins describes energy balance as an afferent system, a central processing system in the hypothalamus, and an efferent system. Within the arcuate nucleus, POMC and CART neurons promote catabolism and NPY and AgRP neurons promote anabolism. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Obesity, Regulation of Energy Balance). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a ventromedial hypothalamic lesion causes hyperphagia and obesity (First Aid Neurology, Hypothalamus). COMLEX: arcuate NPY and AgRP neurons also inhibit neighboring POMC neurons (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 6 — orexigenic and anorexigenic pathways","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the hormonal and hypothalamic mechanisms regulating appetite, satiety, and energy homeostasis, including the roles of leptin, ghrelin, GLP-1, and hypothalamic neuropeptides.","batch":1,"stem":"Peptide YY and glucagon-like peptide 1 are secreted by which intestinal cell type?","choices":{"A":"K cells","B":"I cells","C":"S cells","D":"G cells","E":"L cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Peptide YY and GLP-1 are secreted by L cells in the distal ileum and colon. GIP comes from K cells in the duodenum and jejunum. These gut signals act on the hypothalamus and pancreas after a meal. I cells of the duodenum and jejunum secrete cholecystokinin, not peptide YY or GLP-1. S cells of the duodenum secrete secretin, not peptide YY or GLP-1. G cells of the stomach secrete gastrin, not peptide YY or GLP-1. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Gut Hormones). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GIP and GLP-1 are the incretin hormones of the small intestine (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Gastrointestinal regulatory substances). COMLEX: incretins act on pancreatic beta cells after enteral glucose (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 6 — orexigenic and anorexigenic pathways","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"I cells of the duodenum and jejunum secrete cholecystokinin,","C":"S cells of the duodenum secrete secretin, not peptide YY or ","D":"G cells of the stomach secrete gastrin, not peptide YY or GL"}},{"id":"sdl34_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the hormonal and hypothalamic mechanisms regulating appetite, satiety, and energy homeostasis, including the roles of leptin, ghrelin, GLP-1, and hypothalamic neuropeptides.","batch":1,"stem":"Binding of GLP-1 to its receptor on pancreatic beta cells increases which second messenger to promote insulin release?","choices":{"A":"Cyclic AMP","B":"Cyclic GMP","C":"Inositol trisphosphate","D":"Diacylglycerol","E":"Nitric oxide"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Incretin receptors on beta cells are G protein coupled receptors that activate adenylate cyclase. The resulting rise in cyclic AMP enhances glucose-stimulated insulin secretion. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Gut Hormones). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 34 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the incretin effect explains why oral glucose releases more insulin than IV glucose (First Aid Endocrine, Insulin). COMLEX: GIP and GLP-1 are the two principal incretins (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 4 — oral glucose, incretins, and insulin release","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins"},{"id":"sdl34_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Analyze the pathophysiologic consequences of obesity and dietary factors, including insulin resistance, metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease, cholesterol gallstones, and gastrointestinal malignancies.","batch":1,"stem":"Gallstones are about how many times more common in obese individuals than in lean individuals?","choices":{"A":"About two times","B":"About six times","C":"About three times","D":"About ten times","E":"About twenty times"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Gallstones are six times more common in obese than in lean individuals. Obesity increases total body cholesterol turnover and biliary cholesterol excretion, which promotes cholesterol stone formation. Two times underestimates the risk; gallstones are about six times more common in obese individuals. Three times underestimates the risk; gallstones are about six times more common in obesity. Ten times overstates the risk; gallstones are about six times more common in obesity. Twenty times overstates the risk; gallstones are about six times more common in obesity. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Obesity, Gallbladder Disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cholesterol stones are linked to obesity, female sex, and estrogen (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Cholelithiasis). COMLEX: biliary cholesterol supersaturation is the key event in cholesterol stones (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 2 — biochemical pathogenesis of cholesterol gallstones","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Two times underestimates the risk; gallstones are about six ","C":"Three times underestimates the risk; gallstones are about si","D":"Ten times overstates the risk; gallstones are about six time","E":"Twenty times overstates the risk; gallstones are about six t"}},{"id":"sdl34_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Analyze the pathophysiologic consequences of obesity and dietary factors, including insulin resistance, metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease, cholesterol gallstones, and gastrointestinal malignancies.","batch":1,"stem":"Obesity is associated with about what share of all cancers diagnosed in the United States?","choices":{"A":"About 5 percent","B":"About 15 percent","C":"About 40 percent","D":"About 70 percent","E":"About 90 percent"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Robbins states that about 40% of all cancers diagnosed in the United States are associated with being overweight or obese. Gastrointestinal sites include the esophagus, upper stomach, colorectum, gallbladder, liver, and pancreas. About 5 percent understates obesity's role, which accounts for about 40% of US cancers. About 15 percent understates obesity's role, which accounts for about 40% of US cancers. About 70 percent overstates obesity's role, which accounts for about 40% of US cancers. About 90 percent overstates obesity's role, which accounts for about 40% of US cancers. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Obesity and Cancer). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: obesity raises esophageal adenocarcinoma risk through reflux and Barrett esophagus (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Esophageal cancer). COMLEX: hyperinsulinemia and adipokine changes link obesity to cancer (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — MASLD versus alcohol-associated liver disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"About 5 percent understates obesity's role, which accounts f","B":"About 15 percent understates obesity's role, which accounts ","D":"About 70 percent overstates obesity's role, which accounts f","E":"About 90 percent overstates obesity's role, which accounts f"}},{"id":"sdl34_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Analyze the pathophysiologic consequences of obesity and dietary factors, including insulin resistance, metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease, cholesterol gallstones, and gastrointestinal malignancies.","batch":1,"stem":"Obesity raises free estrogen in part by reducing hepatic synthesis of which protein?","choices":{"A":"Thyroxine binding globulin","B":"Corticosteroid binding globulin","C":"Vitamin D binding protein","D":"Retinol binding protein","E":"Sex hormone binding globulin"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Obesity increases estrogen synthesis from androgens in adipose tissue and reduces hepatic synthesis of sex hormone binding globulin, raising free estrogen. This mechanism helps explain the higher risk of endometrial and breast cancer. Retinol binding protein carries vitamin A from the liver and does not regulate free estrogen. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Obesity and Cancer). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 34 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: aromatase in adipose tissue converts androgens to estrogen (First Aid Reproductive, Estrogen). COMLEX: hyperinsulinemia also suppresses sex hormone binding globulin (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — MASLD versus alcohol-associated liver disease","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"D":"Retinol binding protein carries vitamin A from the liver and"}},{"id":"sdl34_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate marasmus and kwashiorkor based on their underlying nutritional deficiencies, affected protein compartments, clinical manifestations, and associated laboratory findings.","batch":2,"stem":"A 2-year-old boy in a refugee camp was weaned after his sister was born and now eats mainly maize gruel. He is apathetic and has generalized pitting edema, peeling hyperpigmented skin patches, pale banded hair, and hepatomegaly. Serum albumin is 1.8 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.5 g/dL). Which change most directly produces his edema?","choices":{"A":"Increased capillary hydrostatic pressure","B":"Increased endothelial permeability","C":"Obstructed lymphatic drainage","D":"Decreased plasma oncotic pressure","E":"Increased interstitial sodium retention"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This child has kwashiorkor. Loss of visceral protein lowers serum albumin, which reduces plasma oncotic pressure and allows fluid to leave capillaries, producing generalized edema. The flaky paint dermatosis and banded hair are characteristic accompanying findings. Obstructed lymphatics cause lymphedema, whereas kwashiorkor edema results from hypoalbuminemia. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Kwashiorkor). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a small child with a swollen belly and peeling skin is the classic kwashiorkor picture (First Aid Biochemistry, Malnutrition). COMLEX: kwashiorkor is also seen with protein-losing enteropathy, nephrotic syndrome, and burns (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — chylomicron formation and lipoprotein transport","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Obstructed lymphatics cause lymphedema, whereas kwashiorkor "}},{"id":"sdl34_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate marasmus and kwashiorkor based on their underlying nutritional deficiencies, affected protein compartments, clinical manifestations, and associated laboratory findings.","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-year-old girl in a drought region has had little food of any kind for months. She is emaciated with wasted limbs, no subcutaneous fat, and a head that looks large for her body. There is no edema. Her weight is 55% of expected for age. Which hormonal change most likely contributes to the loss of her fat stores?","choices":{"A":"Increased cortisol secretion","B":"Increased leptin secretion","C":"Increased insulin secretion","D":"Decreased glucagon secretion","E":"Decreased epinephrine secretion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This child has marasmus. Low leptin levels stimulate the hypothalamic pituitary adrenal axis, and the resulting high cortisol contributes to lipolysis. Fat and muscle are mobilized as energy sources. Leptin falls, not rises, in marasmus as fat stores shrink, and low leptin drives the cortisol rise. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Marasmus). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 34 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: marasmus results from overall calorie deficiency rather than isolated protein lack (First Aid Biochemistry, Malnutrition). COMLEX: marasmus also causes immune deficiency, especially of T cell mediated immunity (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — chylomicron formation and lipoprotein transport","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"B":"Leptin falls, not rises, in marasmus as fat stores shrink, a"}},{"id":"sdl34_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate marasmus and kwashiorkor based on their underlying nutritional deficiencies, affected protein compartments, clinical manifestations, and associated laboratory findings.","batch":2,"stem":"An 18-month-old boy is treated for protein malnutrition with generalized edema, skin desquamation, and a fatty liver. After starting full-strength milk-based feeds he develops bloating and watery diarrhea. Stool is acidic and positive for reducing substances. Which mechanism most likely explains his diarrhea?","choices":{"A":"Pancreatic lipase deficiency","B":"Mucosal disaccharidase loss","C":"Cow milk protein allergy","D":"Ileal bile acid malabsorption","E":"Bacterial enterotoxin secretion"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In kwashiorkor, small bowel crypt mitoses decrease, causing mucosal atrophy, loss of villi and microvilli, and secondary disaccharidase deficiency. Infants may therefore respond poorly to full-strength milk diets until the mucosa recovers. Acidic stool with reducing substances points to undigested sugar. Ileal bile acid malabsorption would not produce acidic stool with reducing substances, which marks undigested sugar. Bacterial enterotoxin secretion causes secretory diarrhea, not acidic stool with reducing substances after milk feeds. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Kwashiorkor). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 34 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: lactase deficiency gives osmotic diarrhea with low stool pH (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Lactose intolerance). COMLEX: the mucosal changes of kwashiorkor reverse with treatment (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — chylomicron formation and lipoprotein transport","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"D":"Ileal bile acid malabsorption would not produce acidic stool","E":"Bacterial enterotoxin secretion causes secretory diarrhea, n"}},{"id":"sdl34_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the biochemical functions, metabolism, and clinical manifestations of deficiencies in fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K), particularly in the context of gastrointestinal malabsorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman with Crohn disease had 120 cm of distal ileum resected 3 years ago. She has greasy stools and now has trouble seeing when driving at dusk. Examination shows dry conjunctivae with small gray foamy plaques. The deficient vitamin normally serves which function?","choices":{"A":"Cofactor for gamma carboxylation","B":"Membrane lipid antioxidant","C":"Regulator of calcium absorption","D":"Component of visual pigment","E":"Cofactor for collagen hydroxylation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Vitamin A deficiency develops in fat malabsorption such as Crohn disease because absorption requires bile and lipid digestion. Retinal combines with opsin to form rhodopsin, so night blindness appears first, followed by xerophthalmia and Bitot spots. Gamma carboxylation is the role of vitamin K, whose deficiency causes bleeding, not night blindness. Membrane antioxidant protection is vitamin E, whose deficiency causes ataxia and hemolysis, not xerophthalmia. Regulating calcium absorption is vitamin D, whose deficiency causes osteomalacia, not night blindness. Collagen hydroxylation needs vitamin C, whose deficiency causes scurvy, not Bitot spots. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Vitamin A, Deficiency). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bitot spots and night blindness indicate vitamin A deficiency (First Aid Biochemistry, Vitamin A). COMLEX: vitamin A deficiency also causes squamous metaplasia of airway and urinary epithelium (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — terminal ileal disease and bile salt loss","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Gamma carboxylation is the role of vitamin K, whose deficien","B":"Membrane antioxidant protection is vitamin E, whose deficien","C":"Regulating calcium absorption is vitamin D, whose deficiency","E":"Collagen hydroxylation needs vitamin C, whose deficiency cau"}},{"id":"sdl34_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the biochemical functions, metabolism, and clinical manifestations of deficiencies in fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K), particularly in the context of gastrointestinal malabsorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 17-year-old girl with cystic fibrosis rarely takes her pancreatic enzymes. She has a wide-based gait, absent ankle reflexes, and loss of vibration and position sense. Hemoglobin is 10.1 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–16.0 g/dL) with MCV 88 fL (reference range: 80–100 fL). Serum methylmalonic acid is normal. Which process most likely underlies her findings?","choices":{"A":"Myelin methylation failure","B":"Clotting factor undercarboxylation","C":"Membrane lipid peroxidation","D":"Collagen hydroxylation failure","E":"Rhodopsin regeneration failure"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Vitamin E is a fat-soluble antioxidant that protects membrane lipids from free radical peroxidation. Deficiency, seen with fat malabsorption such as pancreatic insufficiency, causes spinocerebellar degeneration and hemolytic anemia. A normal methylmalonic acid argues against B12 deficiency. Undercarboxylated clotting factors cause bleeding from vitamin K deficiency, not spinocerebellar degeneration. Failed collagen hydroxylation is scurvy, which causes bleeding gums and petechiae, not ataxia. Failed rhodopsin regeneration is vitamin A deficiency, which causes night blindness, not ataxia. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Vitamin E). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: vitamin E deficiency mimics B12 neuropathy without megaloblastosis or raised methylmalonic acid (First Aid Biochemistry, Vitamin E). COMLEX: vitamin E prevents oxidation of unsaturated fats in cell membranes (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Undercarboxylated clotting factors cause bleeding from vitam","D":"Failed collagen hydroxylation is scurvy, which causes bleedi","E":"Failed rhodopsin regeneration is vitamin A deficiency, which"}},{"id":"sdl34_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the biochemical functions, metabolism, and clinical manifestations of deficiencies in fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K), particularly in the context of gastrointestinal malabsorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman with poorly controlled celiac disease has diffuse bone pain and proximal muscle weakness. Serum calcium is 8.6 mg/dL (reference range: 8.4–10.2 mg/dL), phosphorus 2.1 mg/dL (reference range: 3.0–4.5 mg/dL), alkaline phosphatase 210 U/L (reference range: 25–100 U/L), and PTH 98 pg/mL (reference range: 10–65 pg/mL). A bone biopsy would most likely show which finding?","choices":{"A":"Thin trabeculae with normal mineralization","B":"Mosaic pattern of lamellar bone","C":"Marrow fibrosis with brown tumors","D":"Necrotic trabeculae with empty lacunae","E":"Thickened seams of unmineralized osteoid"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Vitamin D malabsorption leads to secondary hyperparathyroidism, which restores calcium toward normal but leaves phosphate low. Impaired mineralization in the adult produces osteomalacia, in which excess unmineralized osteoid accumulates on bone surfaces. Thin trabeculae with normal mineralization describe osteoporosis, not vitamin D-deficient osteomalacia. A mosaic pattern of lamellar bone is the hallmark of Paget disease, not vitamin D-deficient osteomalacia. Necrotic trabeculae with empty lacunae mark osteonecrosis, an infarction of bone, not defective mineralization. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Vitamin D, Deficiency States). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: osteomalacia shows low calcium or phosphate with high PTH and alkaline phosphatase (First Aid Musculoskeletal, Osteomalacia). COMLEX: the same defect in children causes rickets with growth plate deformities (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Thin trabeculae with normal mineralization describe osteopor","B":"A mosaic pattern of lamellar bone is the hallmark of Paget d","D":"Necrotic trabeculae with empty lacunae mark osteonecrosis, a"}},{"id":"sdl34_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Correlate deficiencies of water-soluble vitamins (B-complex and C) and essential trace minerals with their biochemical functions, characteristic clinical manifestations, and associated gastrointestinal disorders.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with alcohol use disorder is admitted with confusion and receives an IV dextrose infusion. Hours later he has nystagmus, bilateral lateral gaze palsy, and a wide-based gait. Serum lactate is 42 mg/dL (reference range: 4.5–19.8 mg/dL). Reduced activity of which enzyme most directly explains his lactate rise?","choices":{"A":"Lactate dehydrogenase","B":"Pyruvate carboxylase","C":"Pyruvate dehydrogenase","D":"Fructose bisphosphatase","E":"Phosphofructokinase"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Thiamine pyrophosphate is the coenzyme for oxidative decarboxylation reactions, including pyruvate dehydrogenase. Without it, pyruvate is shunted to lactate, and a glucose load worsens the deficit. Ophthalmoplegia, ataxia, and confusion indicate Wernicke encephalopathy. Lactate dehydrogenase is not thiamine-dependent and actually forms the excess lactate. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Table 9.9, Vitamins). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Guyton Ch. 72 (Thiamine).","boardPrep":"Step 1: give thiamine before dextrose in alcohol use disorder to avoid precipitating Wernicke encephalopathy (First Aid Biochemistry, Vitamin B1). COMLEX: thiamine deficiency also causes dry and wet beriberi (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 4 — vitamin B12 absorption and terminal ileal disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Lactate dehydrogenase is not thiamine-dependent and actually"}},{"id":"sdl34_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Correlate deficiencies of water-soluble vitamins (B-complex and C) and essential trace minerals with their biochemical functions, characteristic clinical manifestations, and associated gastrointestinal disorders.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman has a metastatic midgut neuroendocrine tumor with episodic flushing and diarrhea. Over months she develops a rough dermatitis on sun-exposed skin of her neck and forearms and new forgetfulness. Diversion of which amino acid most likely explains her new findings?","choices":{"A":"Tyrosine","B":"Histidine","C":"Methionine","D":"Phenylalanine","E":"Tryptophan"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Niacin can be synthesized from tryptophan. Carcinoid tumors divert tryptophan toward serotonin, reducing niacin synthesis and producing pellagra with dermatitis, diarrhea, and dementia. Sources: First Aid Biochemistry (Vitamin B3: niacin from tryptophan; carcinoid syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pellagra occurs with carcinoid syndrome, Hartnup disease, and isoniazid use (First Aid Biochemistry, Vitamin B3). COMLEX: niacin forms NAD and NADP for redox reactions (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 4 — vitamin B12 absorption and terminal ileal disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Correlate deficiencies of water-soluble vitamins (B-complex and C) and essential trace minerals with their biochemical functions, characteristic clinical manifestations, and associated gastrointestinal disorders.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man has received home parenteral nutrition for 6 months using a trace element mix that omitted zinc, with other trace elements supplied after extensive small bowel resection. He now has a red scaly rash around his mouth, nose, and anus, worsening diarrhea, poor wound healing, and loss of taste. Which additional finding is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Impaired night vision","B":"Perifollicular hemorrhages","C":"Ring sideroblastic anemia","D":"Goiter with hypothyroidism","E":"Dilated cardiomyopathy"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Zinc deficiency from zinc-free parenteral nutrition causes a periorificial rash like acrodermatitis enteropathica, anorexia, diarrhea, poor wound healing, and impaired night vision. Zinc is a component of many enzymes, principally oxidases. Perifollicular hemorrhages indicate vitamin C deficiency, not zinc deficiency. Ring sideroblastic anemia indicates copper deficiency, not zinc deficiency. Goiter with hypothyroidism indicates iodine deficiency, not zinc deficiency. Dilated cardiomyopathy indicates selenium deficiency (Keshan disease), not zinc deficiency. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Table 9.10, Trace Elements). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: zinc deficiency causes delayed wound healing, dysgeusia, and hypogonadism (First Aid Biochemistry, Zinc). COMLEX: acrodermatitis enteropathica is the inherited form of zinc malabsorption (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 4 — vitamin B12 absorption and terminal ileal disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Perifollicular hemorrhages indicate vitamin C deficiency, no","C":"Ring sideroblastic anemia indicates copper deficiency, not z","D":"Goiter with hypothyroidism indicates iodine deficiency, not ","E":"Dilated cardiomyopathy indicates selenium deficiency (Keshan"}},{"id":"sdl34_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the hormonal and hypothalamic mechanisms regulating appetite, satiety, and energy homeostasis, including the roles of leptin, ghrelin, GLP-1, and hypothalamic neuropeptides.","batch":2,"stem":"A 4-year-old girl has had insatiable hunger and severe obesity since infancy. Serum leptin is undetectable, and sequencing shows a homozygous mutation in the leptin gene. She begins daily recombinant leptin injections. The expected decrease in her food intake is mediated mainly by which hypothalamic effect?","choices":{"A":"Activation of NPY and AgRP neurons","B":"Activation of lateral orexin neurons","C":"Activation of lateral MCH neurons","D":"Inhibition of paraventricular TRH neurons","E":"Activation of POMC and CART neurons"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Leptin acts in the arcuate nucleus to stimulate POMC and CART neurons, which release alpha MSH to act on melanocortin receptors and decrease food intake. It also inhibits NPY and AgRP neurons that drive feeding. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Leptin). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: leptin is secreted by adipose tissue and decreases appetite (First Aid Endocrine, Appetite regulation). COMLEX: mutations of leptin or its receptor cause massive obesity (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 6 — orexigenic and anorexigenic pathways","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the hormonal and hypothalamic mechanisms regulating appetite, satiety, and energy homeostasis, including the roles of leptin, ghrelin, GLP-1, and hypothalamic neuropeptides.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman with a BMI of 44 undergoes Roux-en-Y gastric bypass. Six months later she reports much less hunger before meals than she had with prior diets. A blunted rise of which hormone most likely contributes?","choices":{"A":"Leptin","B":"Peptide YY","C":"Ghrelin","D":"Insulin","E":"Cholecystokinin"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Ghrelin is an orexigenic gut hormone that increases food intake. It is produced mainly in the stomach, rises before meals, and acts on NPY and AgRP neurons. Its preprandial rise is blunted after Roux-en-Y gastric bypass, which may contribute to weight loss. Leptin is a long-term fat-mass signal that suppresses appetite, not the premeal hunger hormone blunted by bypass. Peptide YY rises after meals and reduces appetite, so a blunted rise would not lessen hunger. Insulin suppresses appetite, so a blunted insulin rise would not explain reduced hunger. Cholecystokinin is released after food ingestion and suppresses further feeding, so it does not drive pre-meal hunger as fasting ghrelin does. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Gut Hormones). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ghrelin is increased in Prader-Willi syndrome and with sleep deprivation (First Aid Endocrine, Appetite regulation). COMLEX: ghrelin levels are typically lower in obese individuals (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 6 — orexigenic and anorexigenic pathways","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Leptin is a long-term fat-mass signal that suppresses appeti","B":"Peptide YY rises after meals and reduces appetite, so a blun","D":"Insulin suppresses appetite, so a blunted insulin rise would","E":"Cholecystokinin is released after food ingestion and suppres"}},{"id":"sdl34_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the hormonal and hypothalamic mechanisms regulating appetite, satiety, and energy homeostasis, including the roles of leptin, ghrelin, GLP-1, and hypothalamic neuropeptides.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with type 2 diabetes and a BMI of 34 starts a weekly injected drug that mimics a hormone of ileal and colonic endocrine cells. Over 6 months his hemoglobin A1c improves and he loses 9 kg. Which action of the drug most likely accounts for his weight loss?","choices":{"A":"Inhibition of intestinal fat absorption","B":"Central suppression of food intake","C":"Increased brown fat thermogenesis","D":"Increased renal glucose excretion","E":"Decreased hepatic glycogen storage"},"correct":"B","explanation":"GLP-1 from intestinal L cells is an incretin that augments insulin release, and it also acts on the hypothalamus to decrease food intake and slows gastric emptying. GLP-1 receptor agonists produce weight loss mainly through reduced appetite. Inhibition of intestinal fat absorption is the action of the GI lipase inhibitor orlistat, not of a GLP-1 mimetic. Increased renal glucose excretion is the action of SGLT2 inhibitors, not of a GLP-1 receptor agonist. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Gut Hormones). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Costanzo Ch. 8 (Hunger and Satiety).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GLP-1 agonists decrease appetite and slow gastric emptying (First Aid Endocrine, Diabetes mellitus therapy). COMLEX: lipase inhibitors reduce fat absorption, a different weight loss mechanism (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 4 — oral glucose, incretins, and insulin release","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Inhibition of intestinal fat absorption is the action of the","D":"Increased renal glucose excretion is the action of SGLT2 inh"}},{"id":"sdl34_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Analyze the pathophysiologic consequences of obesity and dietary factors, including insulin resistance, metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease, cholesterol gallstones, and gastrointestinal malignancies.","batch":2,"stem":"A 49-year-old man with a BMI of 37 has acanthosis nigricans and a waist circumference of 118 cm. Fasting glucose is 118 mg/dL (reference range: 70–100 mg/dL), triglycerides 260 mg/dL (reference range: <150 mg/dL), and HDL 32 mg/dL (reference range: 40–60 mg/dL). Which mediator, released after inflammasome activation by excess free fatty acids, most likely drives his insulin resistance?","choices":{"A":"Interleukin 10","B":"Interleukin 4","C":"Transforming growth factor beta","D":"Interleukin 1","E":"Interferon alpha"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In obesity, excess free fatty acids and lipid within cells activate the inflammasome, which releases interleukin 1 and creates a systemic proinflammatory state. This inflammation promotes insulin resistance, a core feature of the metabolic syndrome. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Obesity, Metabolic Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 34 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: metabolic syndrome combines central obesity, hypertension, hyperglycemia, and dyslipidemia (First Aid Endocrine, Metabolic syndrome). COMLEX: obesity is associated with high triglycerides and low HDL (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — MASLD versus alcohol-associated liver disease","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins"},{"id":"sdl34_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Analyze the pathophysiologic consequences of obesity and dietary factors, including insulin resistance, metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease, cholesterol gallstones, and gastrointestinal malignancies.","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old woman with a BMI of 38 and type 2 diabetes drinks alcohol rarely. ALT is 78 U/L (reference range: 10–40 U/L) and AST is 52 U/L (reference range: 12–38 U/L). Ultrasound shows a diffusely echogenic liver, and viral and autoimmune studies are negative. A reduced serum level of which adipose hormone most likely contributes to her liver disease?","choices":{"A":"Adiponectin","B":"Leptin","C":"Resistin","D":"Angiotensinogen","E":"Testosterone"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This is metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease. Adiponectin, which is reduced in obesity, increases fatty acid oxidation and insulin sensitivity in liver and muscle. Its decline contributes to insulin resistance, type 2 diabetes, and hepatic steatosis. Leptin is elevated, not reduced, in obesity because of central leptin resistance. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Adiponectin; Obesity, Nonalcoholic Fatty Liver Disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 34 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MASLD is the hepatic manifestation of metabolic syndrome, with ALT usually above AST (First Aid Gastrointestinal, MASLD). COMLEX: obesity related steatosis may progress to steatohepatitis and fibrosis (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — MASLD versus alcohol-associated liver disease","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"B":"Leptin is elevated, not reduced, in obesity because of centr"}},{"id":"sdl34_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Analyze the pathophysiologic consequences of obesity and dietary factors, including insulin resistance, metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease, cholesterol gallstones, and gastrointestinal malignancies.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man with a BMI of 39 and fasting hyperinsulinemia is diagnosed with sigmoid adenocarcinoma. His physician explains that high insulin raises free levels of a growth factor that stimulates tumor cell proliferation. Through which signaling route does this growth factor mainly act?","choices":{"A":"Wnt and catenin pathways","B":"RAS and PI3K pathways","C":"Notch and Hedgehog pathways","D":"TGF beta and SMAD pathways","E":"JAK and STAT pathways"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hyperinsulinemia raises free insulin-like growth factor 1, a mitogen whose receptor activates the RAS and PI3K AKT pathways and promotes growth of tumor cells. This is one proposed link between obesity and colorectal and other cancers. Wnt and beta-catenin signaling is the APC pathway, not the target of insulin-like growth factor 1. TGF-beta and SMAD signaling is a growth-inhibitory pathway lost in colon cancer, not the IGF-1 mitogenic route. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Obesity and Cancer). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colorectal cancer risk factors include obesity, red meat, and a low fiber diet (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colorectal cancer). COMLEX: excess adipose estrogen production is another obesity cancer link (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma risk factors","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Wnt and beta-catenin signaling is the APC pathway, not the t","D":"TGF-beta and SMAD signaling is a growth-inhibitory pathway l"}},{"id":"trial_sdl34_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate marasmus and kwashiorkor based on their underlying nutritional deficiencies, affected protein compartments, clinical manifestations, and associated laboratory findings.","batch":3,"stem":"A 14-month-old girl in the United States has been fed only a rice beverage since weaning. She has generalized edema, flaky skin, and a fatty liver. Which tissue is relatively spared?","choices":{"A":"Subcutaneous fat stores","B":"Hepatic protein stores","C":"Plasma albumin pool","D":"Thymic lymphoid tissue","E":"Intestinal villous mucosa"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In kwashiorkor, the visceral compartment is markedly depleted while loss of subcutaneous fat and muscle mass is relatively mild and masked by edema. Thymic and lymphoid atrophy and small bowel villous loss are prominent. Robbins notes cases in developed countries from milk replacement with rice-based beverages. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Kwashiorkor). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: kwashiorkor follows a diet deficient in protein despite adequate calories (First Aid Biochemistry, Malnutrition). COMLEX: marasmus depletes subcutaneous fat and muscle more severely (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — chylomicron formation and lipoprotein transport","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl34_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the biochemical functions, metabolism, and clinical manifestations of deficiencies in fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K), particularly in the context of gastrointestinal malabsorption.","batch":3,"stem":"A 68-year-old man has taken no food by mouth for 2 weeks while receiving broad-spectrum antibiotics. He now oozes from IV sites. PT is 24 s (reference range: 11–15 s). He takes no anticoagulants. What is the main cause?","choices":{"A":"Increased hepatic vitamin breakdown","B":"Increased renal vitamin loss","C":"Blocked epoxide reductase activity","D":"Loss of gut bacterial synthesis","E":"Impaired platelet granule release"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Vitamin K comes from the diet and from intestinal bacteria. Without oral intake and with gut flora suppressed by antibiotics, vitamin K dependent factors fall and the PT rises. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Vitamin K). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Guyton Ch. 72 (Vitamin K).","boardPrep":"Step 1: vitamin K deficiency prolongs PT and aPTT with a normal bleeding time (First Aid Biochemistry, Vitamin K). COMLEX: vitamin K is a cofactor for hepatic carboxylation of clotting factors (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl34_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Correlate deficiencies of water-soluble vitamins (B-complex and C) and essential trace minerals with their biochemical functions, characteristic clinical manifestations, and associated gastrointestinal disorders.","batch":3,"stem":"A 72-year-old widower eats mostly tea and toast. He has bleeding gums, corkscrew hairs, and perifollicular hemorrhages. The deficient vitamin is a cofactor for which enzyme?","choices":{"A":"Gamma glutamyl carboxylase","B":"Prolyl hydroxylase","C":"Pyruvate carboxylase","D":"Glutathione peroxidase","E":"Transketolase"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Vitamin C activates prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases needed for collagen hydroxylation. Deficiency, seen in older adults with poor diets, causes scurvy with gum bleeding and perifollicular hemorrhages. Gamma-glutamyl carboxylase in the liver uses vitamin K as its cofactor. Glutathione peroxidase depends on selenium, not vitamin C. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Vitamin C). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: scurvy causes swollen gums, bruising, and corkscrew hair (First Aid Biochemistry, Vitamin C). COMLEX: vitamin C excess can cause oxalate stones and iron overload (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 4 — vitamin B12 absorption and terminal ileal disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Gamma-glutamyl carboxylase in the liver uses vitamin K as it","D":"Glutathione peroxidase depends on selenium, not vitamin C."}},{"id":"trial_sdl34_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the hormonal and hypothalamic mechanisms regulating appetite, satiety, and energy homeostasis, including the roles of leptin, ghrelin, GLP-1, and hypothalamic neuropeptides.","batch":3,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman with a BMI of 41 has a high serum leptin level. She remains hungry despite large fat stores. What best explains this?","choices":{"A":"Hypothalamic leptin resistance","B":"Rapid renal leptin clearance","C":"Circulating antileptin antibodies","D":"Reduced adipocyte leptin release","E":"Excess hepatic leptin uptake"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Leptin is secreted in proportion to fat stores, so obese people usually have high leptin. Most are resistant to its central effects, so the satiety signal fails despite abundant hormone. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Leptin). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: leptin acts on the ventromedial hypothalamus to promote satiety (First Aid Neurology, Hypothalamus). COMLEX: leptin gene mutations are a rare cause of massive obesity (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 6 — orexigenic and anorexigenic pathways","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl34_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Analyze the pathophysiologic consequences of obesity and dietary factors, including insulin resistance, metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease, cholesterol gallstones, and gastrointestinal malignancies.","batch":3,"stem":"A 50-year-old man with obesity and type 2 diabetes has steatotic liver disease on biopsy without fibrosis. He drinks no alcohol. Without weight loss, which complication is he at risk for?","choices":{"A":"Budd-Chiari syndrome","B":"Hepatic angiosarcoma","C":"Hepatic cirrhosis","D":"Biliary cholangitis","E":"Autoimmune hepatitis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Steatotic liver disease linked to obesity and metabolic syndrome can progress to steatohepatitis, fibrosis, and cirrhosis. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Obesity, Nonalcoholic Fatty Liver Disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: MASH can progress to cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma (First Aid Gastrointestinal, MASLD). COMLEX: obesity is a major cause of steatotic liver disease (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — MASLD versus alcohol-associated liver disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl34_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the biochemical functions, metabolism, and clinical manifestations of deficiencies in fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K), particularly in the context of gastrointestinal malabsorption.","batch":3,"stem":"A 10-month-old breastfed infant living in a northern city has soft skull bones and bowed legs. Which chest finding is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Sternal pectus excavatum","B":"Costochondral beading","C":"Clavicular callus formation","D":"Inferior rib notching","E":"Scapular winging"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Vitamin D deficiency in children causes rickets. Overgrowth of cartilage at costochondral junctions produces the rachitic rosary, along with craniotabes and leg bowing. Breastfed infants with little sun exposure are at risk. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Vitamin D, Rickets). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 34 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: rickets shows rachitic rosary, bowed legs, and craniotabes (First Aid Biochemistry, Vitamin D). COMLEX: the chest may also show a pigeon breast deformity (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins"},{"id":"grid_sdl34_q01","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Correlate deficiencies of water-soluble vitamins (B-complex and C) and essential trace minerals with their biochemical functions, characteristic clinical manifestations, and associated gastrointestinal disorders.","batch":4,"stem":"A 52-year-old man who drinks 10 beers daily and eats little besides crackers has fatigue and a sore, smooth red tongue. Neurologic examination, including vibration and position sense, is normal. Hemoglobin is 9.6 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL), and a peripheral smear shows hypersegmented neutrophils. Which of the following sets of laboratory findings is most likely in this patient?","grid":{"headers":["MCV","Serum methylmalonic acid","Serum homocysteine"]},"choices":{"A":["↑","↑","↑"],"B":["↓","↔","↔"],"C":["↓","↔","↑"],"D":["↔","↑","↑"],"E":["↑","↔","↑"]},"correct":"E","explanation":"Choice E (↑ MCV, ↔ serum methylmalonic acid, ↑ serum homocysteine) is correct. Poor intake with heavy alcohol use causes folate deficiency; the notes describe folate as essential for one-carbon transfers in purine and thymidylate synthesis, so deficiency causes a megaloblastic anemia with hypersegmented neutrophils but no neurologic deficits. Without methyl-tetrahydrofolate, homocysteine is not remethylated and rises, while methylmalonyl-CoA mutase, which needs B12 but not folate, keeps working. Choice A (↑ MCV, ↑ serum methylmalonic acid, ↑ serum homocysteine) is vitamin B12 deficiency, as after ileal resection or in atrophic gastritis, which also causes subacute combined degeneration with loss of vibration and position sense. Choice B (↓ MCV, ↔ serum methylmalonic acid, ↔ serum homocysteine) is iron deficiency, a microcytic anemia. Choice C (↓ MCV, ↔ serum methylmalonic acid, ↑ serum homocysteine) is vitamin B6 deficiency, which causes a microcytic sideroblastic anemia and impairs the B6-dependent conversion of homocysteine to cystathionine. Choice D (↔ MCV, ↑ serum methylmalonic acid, ↑ serum homocysteine) is the anemia of chronic kidney disease, a normocytic anemia from low erythropoietin in which reduced renal clearance raises both metabolites. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 34, section on water-soluble vitamins: B12, folate); First Aid 2025 Biochemistry (folate, B12, and B6 deficiency; methylmalonic acid and homocysteine) and Hematology (microcytic and macrocytic anemias). The laboratory arrows are from First Aid because the notes do not list them; the raised metabolites in chronic kidney disease are from general clinical chemistry and are inferred.","boardPrep":"Step 1: folate and B12 deficiency both raise homocysteine, but only B12 deficiency raises methylmalonic acid and causes neurologic deficits (First Aid 2025 Biochemistry). COMLEX: replacing folate in B12 deficiency can correct the anemia while the neurologic damage progresses.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — intrinsic factor and B12 absorption","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":35,"title":"SDL 35 — Intestinal Helminths","questions":[{"id":"sdl35_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the modes of transmission and life cycle entry points of major intestinal helminths, including ingestion of eggs or encysted larvae, and skin penetration by larvae.","batch":1,"stem":"How do Strongyloides stercoralis larvae enter the human host?","choices":{"A":"Penetration of intact skin","B":"Ingestion of eggs in soil","C":"Bite of a sandfly vector","D":"Inhalation of soil dust","E":"Ingestion of raw fish meat"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Strongyloides worms live in soil, and humans are infected when larvae penetrate the skin. The larvae travel in the circulation to the lungs, move up the trachea, and are swallowed. Ingesting eggs from soil is how Ascaris, Trichuris, and pinworm are acquired, not Strongyloides, whose larvae penetrate skin. Raw fish transmits the tapeworm Diphyllobothrium latum, not Strongyloides. Sandfly bites transmit Leishmania, not Strongyloides, whose larvae penetrate skin from soil. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Strongyloidiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Strongyloides, Ancylostoma, and Necator enter through the skin, while Enterobius, Ascaris, Toxocara, Trichinella, and Trichuris are ingested (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: Strongyloides occurs sporadically in the United States, including in Appalachia (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Ingesting eggs from soil is how Ascaris, Trichuris, and pinw","E":"Raw fish transmits the tapeworm Diphyllobothrium latum, not ","C":"Sandfly bites transmit Leishmania, not Strongyloides, whose "}},{"id":"sdl35_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the modes of transmission and life cycle entry points of major intestinal helminths, including ingestion of eggs or encysted larvae, and skin penetration by larvae.","batch":1,"stem":"How do humans acquire Ascaris lumbricoides?","choices":{"A":"Ingestion of metacercariae on plants","B":"Ingestion of eggs passed in stool","C":"Ingestion of cysts in pond water","D":"Bites of infected deer flies","E":"Inhalation of larvae in soil dust"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ascaris is acquired by ingesting eggs from soil contaminated with human stool (A. lumbricoides) or pig stool (A. suum). Metacercariae on water plants transmit liver flukes such as Fasciola, not Ascaris. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Ascariasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ascaris is spread fecal-orally, and its knobby-coated oval eggs are seen in feces (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: about a billion people are estimated to be infected with Ascaris (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Metacercariae on water plants transmit liver flukes such as "}},{"id":"sdl35_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the modes of transmission and life cycle entry points of major intestinal helminths, including ingestion of eggs or encysted larvae, and skin penetration by larvae.","batch":1,"stem":"Humans acquire the adult intestinal form of Taenia solium by ingesting what?","choices":{"A":"Eggs in fecally soiled water","B":"Metacercariae on water plants","C":"Cysticerci in undercooked pork","D":"Cercariae in fresh lake water","E":"Eggs in undercooked raw beef meat"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Ingested cysticerci in undercooked pork attach to the intestinal wall and develop into adult tapeworms several meters long. This route completes the life cycle without causing disseminated cysticercosis. Ingesting T. solium eggs produces tissue cysticercosis, not the adult intestinal tapeworm. Metacercariae on water plants transmit flukes, not Taenia solium. Cercariae in fresh water penetrate skin to cause schistosomiasis, not taeniasis. Raw beef transmits Taenia saginata via cysticerci, not T. solium, and eggs are not the infective form in meat. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Cysticercosis and tapeworm infections). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Taenia solium intestinal tapeworm comes from encysted larvae in pork, and cysticercosis comes from eggs in food contaminated with human feces (First Aid Microbiology (Cestodes)). COMLEX: tapeworm proglottids contain both male and female reproductive organs (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Ingesting T. solium eggs produces tissue cysticercosis, not ","B":"Metacercariae on water plants transmit flukes, not Taenia so","D":"Cercariae in fresh water penetrate skin to cause schistosomi","E":"Raw beef transmits Taenia saginata via cysticerci, not T. so"}},{"id":"sdl35_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how the worm burden in helminth infections is determined and differentiate species capable of autoinfection from those that are not.","batch":1,"stem":"For most intestinal helminths, which factor determines the adult worm burden?","choices":{"A":"Number of infective exposures","B":"Rate of adult worm replication","C":"Host gastric acid output","D":"Duration of fecal egg shedding","E":"Peak blood eosinophil count"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Most intestinal helminths do not multiply to new adults inside the human host, so each adult worm reflects a separately acquired infective egg or larva. Strongyloides is the key exception. Adult worms do not replicate in the human host, so burden depends on the number of exposures. Egg shedding reflects existing burden but does not determine it, since eggs must mature outside the host. Eosinophil count reflects tissue invasion, not the number of adult worms. Sources: Elentra objective framing (Robbins does not state this directly); Robbins Ch. 8 (Strongyloidiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Strongyloides hyperinfection reflects accelerated autoinfection in the immunocompromised (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: Ascaris symptoms vary with the burden of worms, and light infections are often asymptomatic (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Adult worms do not replicate in the human host, so burden de","D":"Egg shedding reflects existing burden but does not determine","E":"Eosinophil count reflects tissue invasion, not the number of"}},{"id":"sdl35_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how the worm burden in helminth infections is determined and differentiate species capable of autoinfection from those that are not.","batch":1,"stem":"In strongyloidiasis, what allows the worm burden to rise without new exposure from soil?","choices":{"A":"Adult worms dividing by fission","B":"Eggs hatching in moist soil","C":"Larvae maturing within the host","D":"Cysts forming in skeletal muscle","E":"Proglottids budding off the scolex"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Most Strongyloides larvae pass in stool, but some develop into infective forms within the host, leading to autoinfection. Females in the small intestinal mucosa produce eggs by parthenogenesis. Helminth adults do not divide by fission; Strongyloides burden rises through autoinfection by larvae maturing in the host. Eggs hatching in soil require new exposure, which is exactly what autoinfection bypasses. Cysts in skeletal muscle describe Trichinella, not Strongyloides autoinfection. Proglottids budding from a scolex describe tapeworms, not Strongyloides. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Strongyloidiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: rhabditiform Strongyloides larvae are seen in feces (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: immunocompetent hosts with Strongyloides are often asymptomatic but may have diarrhea, bloating, or malabsorption (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Helminth adults do not divide by fission; Strongyloides burd","B":"Eggs hatching in soil require new exposure, which is exactly","D":"Cysts in skeletal muscle describe Trichinella, not Strongylo","E":"Proglottids budding from a scolex describe tapeworms, not St"}},{"id":"sdl35_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how the worm burden in helminth infections is determined and differentiate species capable of autoinfection from those that are not.","batch":1,"stem":"Which drug class both suppresses eosinophil function and stimulates female Strongyloides to produce more infective larvae?","choices":{"A":"Beta blockers","B":"Antihistamines","C":"Statin agents","D":"Loop diuretic agents","E":"Corticosteroids"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Corticosteroids inhibit eosinophils and other immune cells that accumulate in response to infection, and they stimulate female worms to increase larva production. Prolonged therapy can lead to fatal hyperinfection. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Strongyloidiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ivermectin or bendazoles treat Strongyloides (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: organ transplantation and lymphoma also increase the risk of hyperinfection (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify clinical manifestations associated with specific intestinal helminths and describe mechanisms of disease including nutrient competition, mechanical obstruction, and mucosal damage.","batch":1,"stem":"How do hookworms cause anemia?","choices":{"A":"Hemolysis of circulating red cells","B":"Blood loss from the intestinal wall","C":"Marrow invasion by migrating larvae","D":"Competition for dietary cobalamin","E":"Malabsorption of dietary folate"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ancylostoma and Necator attach to the intestinal wall and suck blood, causing chronic blood loss and microcytic anemia. Hookworms do not hemolyze red cells; they suck blood from the intestinal wall, causing iron deficiency. Competition for cobalamin is the mechanism of Diphyllobothrium latum, not hookworm. Sources: First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes) (Robbins Ch. 8 does not cover hookworm; Elentra objective framing). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: iron deficiency anemia is the classic parasite hint for Ancylostoma and Necator (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: hookworm larvae penetrate skin after walking barefoot on contaminated soil (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — intestinal absorption of iron, calcium, and fat-soluble vitamins","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Hookworms do not hemolyze red cells; they suck blood from th","D":"Competition for cobalamin is the mechanism of Diphyllobothri"}},{"id":"sdl35_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify clinical manifestations associated with specific intestinal helminths and describe mechanisms of disease including nutrient competition, mechanical obstruction, and mucosal damage.","batch":1,"stem":"Which nutrient deficiency results from Diphyllobothrium latum infection?","choices":{"A":"Vitamin B6","B":"Folic acid","C":"Thiamine","D":"Vitamin B12","E":"Ascorbic acid"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Diphyllobothrium latum competes with the host for vitamin B12 in the intestine, causing megaloblastic anemia. Robbins notes that in humans it lives only in the gut and does not form cysticerci. Folate is not consumed by Diphyllobothrium; it competes for vitamin B12. Diphyllobothrium latum competes with the host for dietary vitamin B12 and can cause megaloblastic anemia; vitamin B6 is not affected. Thiamine deficiency is typical of alcohol use disorder, whereas the fish tapeworm competes for vitamin B12. Sources: First Aid Microbiology (Cestodes); Robbins Ch. 8 (Cysticercosis and tapeworm infections). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Diphyllobothrium latum is acquired by ingesting larvae in raw freshwater fish (First Aid Microbiology (Cestodes)). COMLEX: the beef tapeworm and fish tapeworm are acquired by eating undercooked meat or fish (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — intestinal absorption of iron, calcium, and fat-soluble vitamins","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Folate is not consumed by Diphyllobothrium; it competes for ","A":"Diphyllobothrium latum competes with the host for dietary vi","C":"Thiamine deficiency is typical of alcohol use disorder, wher"}},{"id":"sdl35_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify clinical manifestations associated with specific intestinal helminths and describe mechanisms of disease including nutrient competition, mechanical obstruction, and mucosal damage.","batch":1,"stem":"Heavy Ascaris burdens can mechanically obstruct which structure?","choices":{"A":"Ileocecal valve","B":"Pyloric sphincter","C":"Lower esophageal sphincter","D":"Internal anal sphincter","E":"Duodenojejunal flexure"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Heavy Ascaris burdens can cause abdominal obstruction requiring surgery, including obstruction of the ileocecal valve and the appendix. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Ascariasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 35 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ascaris may also cause biliary obstruction and intestinal perforation (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: adult Ascaris worms are 15 to 35 cm long and live mainly in the jejunum (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — intestinal absorption of iron, calcium, and fat-soluble vitamins","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins"},{"id":"sdl35_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the role of eosinophilia in helminth infections and describe when eosinophilia is expected based on tissue invasion.","batch":1,"stem":"Which Th2 cytokine stimulates eosinophils in helminth infections?","choices":{"A":"Interleukin 2","B":"Interleukin 12","C":"Interleukin 17","D":"Interferon gamma","E":"Interleukin 5"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Invasive helminths stimulate a Th2 response. IL-5 stimulates eosinophils, and IL-4 drives IgE production. IL-12 drives Th1 responses, not the Th2 eosinophil response to helminths. Interferon gamma is a Th1 cytokine, whereas helminths elicit Th2 responses with IL-5. IL-2 is the T-cell growth factor that drives T-cell proliferation, not the cytokine that stimulates eosinophils. IL-17 is the Th17 cytokine that recruits neutrophils and monocytes, not the Th2 eosinophil stimulator. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Trichinellosis); Robbins Ch. 8 (Filariasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 35 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: eosinophils produce major basic protein and respond to IL-5 (First Aid Hematology (Eosinophils)). COMLEX: Th2 responses increase intestinal contractility, which expels adult Trichinella worms in animal models (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"B":"IL-12 drives Th1 responses, not the Th2 eosinophil response ","D":"Interferon gamma is a Th1 cytokine, whereas helminths elicit","A":"IL-2 is the T-cell growth factor that drives T-cell prolifer","C":"IL-17 is the Th17 cytokine that recruits neutrophils and mon"}},{"id":"sdl35_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the role of eosinophilia in helminth infections and describe when eosinophilia is expected based on tissue invasion.","batch":1,"stem":"When is peripheral eosinophilia most expected during a helminth infection?","choices":{"A":"When adults reach peak fecundity","B":"When eggs pass in the stool","C":"When worms attach by a scolex","D":"When larvae invade host tissues","E":"When adults reside in the cecum"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Eosinophilia is driven by Th2 responses to parasites that migrate through or encyst in tissue. Trichinella larvae invading muscle cause marked eosinophilia, and Ascaris larvae in the lungs cause eosinophilic pneumonitis. Peak fecundity of luminal adults does not drive eosinophilia, which requires tissue invasion. Eggs in stool reflect luminal adults, which do not cause systemic eosinophilia. Scolex attachment is luminal tapeworm behavior, and luminal tapeworms do not cause eosinophilia. Adults in the cecum, like pinworms, stay luminal and do not cause eosinophilia. Sources: Elentra objective framing (Robbins does not state this directly); Robbins Ch. 8 (Trichinellosis); Robbins Ch. 8 (Ascariasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: parasites lead the PACMAN mnemonic for causes of eosinophilia (First Aid Hematology (Eosinophils)). COMLEX: granulomas around schistosome eggs contain eosinophils, which are distinctive for helminth infections (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Peak fecundity of luminal adults does not drive eosinophilia","B":"Eggs in stool reflect luminal adults, which do not cause sys","C":"Scolex attachment is luminal tapeworm behavior, and luminal ","E":"Adults in the cecum, like pinworms, stay luminal and do not "}},{"id":"sdl35_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the role of eosinophilia in helminth infections and describe when eosinophilia is expected based on tissue invasion.","batch":1,"stem":"Which eosinophil granule product is toxic to helminths?","choices":{"A":"Cathelicidin peptide","B":"Major basic protein","C":"Serum amyloid A","D":"Lysozyme enzyme","E":"Lactoferrin protein"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Eosinophils defend against helminths and release major basic protein, a helminthotoxin, along with eosinophil cationic protein and eosinophil-derived neurotoxin. Cathelicidins are antimicrobial proteins of neutrophils, whereas major basic protein is the eosinophil product toxic to helminths. Serum amyloid A is a hepatic acute-phase protein induced by IL-1 and TNF, not an eosinophil granule product. Lysozyme is a neutrophil granule enzyme, not the eosinophil helminthotoxin major basic protein. Lactoferrin is found in neutrophil specific granules, not eosinophil granules. Sources: First Aid Hematology (Eosinophils); Elentra objective framing (Robbins does not state this directly). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 35 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: eosinophils have bilobate nuclei and large uniform eosinophilic granules (First Aid Hematology (Eosinophils)). COMLEX: corticosteroids inhibit eosinophils that accumulate in tissues in response to Strongyloides (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"Cathelicidins are antimicrobial proteins of neutrophils, whe","C":"Serum amyloid A is a hepatic acute-phase protein induced by ","D":"Lysozyme is a neutrophil granule enzyme, not the eosinophil ","E":"Lactoferrin is found in neutrophil specific granules, not eo"}},{"id":"sdl35_q25","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the diagnostic findings and appropriate pharmacologic treatments of major intestinal helminth infections, including identification of eggs, larvae, or adult worms and the mechanisms of action of commonly used anthelmintic agents.","batch":1,"stem":"How does pyrantel pamoate eliminate susceptible intestinal nematodes?","choices":{"A":"Inhibition of tubulin polymerization","B":"Increased tegument calcium permeability","C":"Neuromuscular blockade of worms","D":"Inhibition of parasite folate synthesis","E":"Uncoupling of oxidative phosphorylation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Pyrantel is a poorly absorbed neuromuscular blocking agent that releases acetylcholine and inhibits cholinesterase, paralyzing luminal worms, which are then expelled. Inhibiting tubulin polymerization is the action of benzimidazoles such as albendazole, not pyrantel. Increasing tegument calcium permeability is the action of praziquantel, not pyrantel. Uncoupling of oxidative phosphorylation is the presumed action of niclosamide against tapeworms, whereas pyrantel is a neuromuscular blocking agent. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Pyrantel Pamoate). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pyrantel pamoate is listed with ivermectin, mebendazole, and diethylcarbamazine as antihelminthic therapy (First Aid Microbiology (Antihelminthic therapy)). COMLEX: pyrantel is ineffective against Trichuris and Strongyloides infections (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Inhibiting tubulin polymerization is the action of benzimida","B":"Increasing tegument calcium permeability is the action of pr","E":"Uncoupling of oxidative phosphorylation is the presumed acti"}},{"id":"sdl35_q26","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the diagnostic findings and appropriate pharmacologic treatments of major intestinal helminth infections, including identification of eggs, larvae, or adult worms and the mechanisms of action of commonly used anthelmintic agents.","batch":1,"stem":"What shape do Enterobius vermicularis eggs have on microscopic examination?","choices":{"A":"Barrel shape with polar plugs","B":"Oval with one flattened side","C":"Round with a thick knobby shell","D":"Oval with a large lateral spine","E":"Operculated with a terminal knob"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pinworm eggs have a characteristic elongated oval shape with one of the long sides of the oval flattened. Barrel-shaped eggs with polar plugs are Trichuris, not pinworm. Round eggs with a thick knobby (mammillated) shell are Ascaris, not pinworm. Oval eggs with a large lateral spine are Schistosoma mansoni, not pinworm. Operculated eggs with a terminal knob are Diphyllobothrium, not pinworm. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Enterobiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pinworm causes anal pruritus worse at night and is most common in children aged 5 to 10 (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: adult pinworms live in the cecum and appendix, and females migrate out of the anus at night (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Barrel-shaped eggs with polar plugs are Trichuris, not pinwo","C":"Round eggs with a thick knobby (mammillated) shell are Ascar","D":"Oval eggs with a large lateral spine are Schistosoma mansoni","E":"Operculated eggs with a terminal knob are Diphyllobothrium, "}},{"id":"sdl35_q27","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the diagnostic findings and appropriate pharmacologic treatments of major intestinal helminth infections, including identification of eggs, larvae, or adult worms and the mechanisms of action of commonly used anthelmintic agents.","batch":1,"stem":"Which drug of choice treats intestinal infection with the fish tapeworm Diphyllobothrium latum?","choices":{"A":"Praziquantel","B":"Ivermectin","C":"Diethylcarbamazine","D":"Pyrantel pamoate","E":"Mebendazole"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Katzung lists praziquantel or niclosamide as the drugs of choice for Diphyllobothrium latum and Taenia saginata, which live only in the gut lumen. Ivermectin is the drug of choice for Strongyloides and Onchocerca, not tapeworms. Diethylcarbamazine treats filarial infections, not intestinal tapeworms. Pyrantel pamoate treats luminal nematodes such as pinworm, Ascaris, and hookworm, not tapeworms. Mebendazole is first-line for intestinal roundworms, whereas praziquantel is the drug of choice for tapeworms. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Table 53-1); Robbins Ch. 8 (Cysticercosis and tapeworm infections). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: tapeworms such as Diphyllobothrium latum are covered with the cestodes (First Aid Microbiology (Cestodes)). COMLEX: a single 2-g dose of niclosamide cures more than 85% of Diphyllobothrium latum infections (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Ivermectin is the drug of choice for Strongyloides and Oncho","C":"Diethylcarbamazine treats filarial infections, not intestina","D":"Pyrantel pamoate treats luminal nematodes such as pinworm, A","E":"Mebendazole is first-line for intestinal roundworms, whereas"}},{"id":"sdl35_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the modes of transmission and life cycle entry points of major intestinal helminths, including ingestion of eggs or encysted larvae, and skin penetration by larvae.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old pig farmer passes a 25-cm worm in his stool. He recalls a dry cough with fever several weeks earlier. After the eggs were ingested, which route did the larvae take before maturing in his intestine?","choices":{"A":"Portal flow to the hepatic lobules","B":"Lymphatic flow to the dermis","C":"Direct growth in the jejunal lumen","D":"Lung passage then reswallowing","E":"Bile flow back into the duodenum"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Ingested Ascaris eggs release larvae in the small intestine. The larvae penetrate the cecal mucosa, travel to the lungs in blood or lymph, mature for 10 to 14 days, and are coughed up and swallowed. Ascaris suum is acquired from pig stool. Ascaris larvae do not mature directly in the lumen; they migrate to the lungs and are reswallowed, unlike Trichuris. Bile flow back into the duodenum is not part of the Ascaris life cycle; larvae migrate through the lungs. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Ascariasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: migration of Ascaris larvae to alveoli causes Löffler syndrome with pulmonary eosinophilia (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: each female Ascaris releases about 200,000 eggs per day (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Ascaris larvae do not mature directly in the lumen; they mig","E":"Bile flow back into the duodenum is not part of the Ascaris "}},{"id":"sdl35_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the modes of transmission and life cycle entry points of major intestinal helminths, including ingestion of eggs or encysted larvae, and skin penetration by larvae.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old vegetarian woman who emigrated from rural Guatemala has new seizures. MRI shows multiple small cystic brain lesions. How did she most likely acquire the infection?","choices":{"A":"Larvae in undercooked pork","B":"Metacercariae on watercress","C":"Eggs from dog feces","D":"Cercariae in lake water","E":"Eggs from human feces"},"correct":"E","explanation":"When humans ingest Taenia solium eggs in food or water contaminated with human feces, oncospheres penetrate the gut wall and spread hematogenously to form cysticerci, including in the brain. Adult tapeworms do not form by this route. Larvae in undercooked pork produce the adult intestinal tapeworm, not brain cysticerci, and she is vegetarian. Metacercariae on watercress transmit Fasciola, not cysticercosis. Eggs from dog feces cause echinococcal hydatid cysts, whereas T. solium eggs come from human feces. Cercariae in lake water cause schistosomiasis, not neurocysticercosis. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Cysticercosis and tapeworm infections). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: brain cysts with seizures is the parasite hint for neurocysticercosis (First Aid Microbiology (Cestodes)). COMLEX: viable cysticerci evade immunity with taeniaestatin and paramyosin (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Larvae in undercooked pork produce the adult intestinal tape","B":"Metacercariae on watercress transmit Fasciola, not cysticerc","C":"Eggs from dog feces cause echinococcal hydatid cysts, wherea","D":"Cercariae in lake water cause schistosomiasis, not neurocyst"}},{"id":"sdl35_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the modes of transmission and life cycle entry points of major intestinal helminths, including ingestion of eggs or encysted larvae, and skin penetration by larvae.","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old aid worker waded in a freshwater lake in East Africa. Months later she has hepatomegaly and eggs in her stool. Which mechanism allowed the parasite to enter her body?","choices":{"A":"Larval proteolytic enzymes","B":"Ingestion of snail tissue","C":"Bite of an infected black fly","D":"Inhalation of aerosolized eggs","E":"Ingestion of contaminated watercress"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Schistosome cercariae swim in fresh water and penetrate human skin with proteolytic enzymes that degrade the keratinized layer, causing minimal skin reaction. S. mansoni affects mainly the liver and gut. Contaminated watercress transmits Fasciola metacercariae, whereas schistosome cercariae penetrate skin. Black flies transmit Onchocerca volvulus near fast-moving rivers, not schistosomes, whose cercariae penetrate skin in fresh water. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Schistosomiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 35 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: portal hypertension is the parasite hint for Schistosoma mansoni and S. japonicum (First Aid Microbiology (Trematodes); Parasite hints). COMLEX: females produce hundreds to thousands of eggs per day (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 4 — portal hypertension and portosystemic varices","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"E":"Contaminated watercress transmits Fasciola metacercariae, wh","C":"Black flies transmit Onchocerca volvulus near fast-moving ri"}},{"id":"sdl35_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how the worm burden in helminth infections is determined and differentiate species capable of autoinfection from those that are not.","batch":2,"stem":"A 7-year-old boy has nocturnal perianal itching that returns weeks after one course of treatment. He often sucks his thumb, and no one else at home is affected. What best explains his reinfection?","choices":{"A":"Contaminated drinking water","B":"Hand-to-mouth egg transfer","C":"Adult worm division in cecum","D":"Insect vector bite","E":"Eating undercooked meat"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pinworm females migrate out of the anus at night to deposit eggs on perianal skin. The eggs become infectious within about 6 hours and spread from the hands or bed linens, allowing self-reinfection. Adult worms do not divide; pinworm reinfection comes from re-ingesting eggs. Undercooked meat transmits tapeworms and Trichinella, not pinworm. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Enterobiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Enterobius is spread fecal-orally and diagnosed with the tape test (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: pinworm is the most common helminthic infection in the United States (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 5 — defecation reflex and the anal sphincters","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Adult worms do not divide; pinworm reinfection comes from re","E":"Undercooked meat transmits tapeworms and Trichinella, not pi"}},{"id":"sdl35_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how the worm burden in helminth infections is determined and differentiate species capable of autoinfection from those that are not.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man from rural Kentucky takes high-dose prednisone after a lung transplant. He develops diffuse pulmonary infiltrates and Escherichia coli bacteremia, and larvae are found in his sputum. What is the source of the bacteremia?","choices":{"A":"Contaminated central venous line","B":"Aspiration of gastric contents","C":"Larval damage to the bowel wall","D":"Infected urinary catheter","E":"Obstructed common bile duct"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In Strongyloides hyperinfection, larvae invade the intestinal wall, and intestinal bacteria enter the blood through the damaged wall, causing sepsis. Worms of all stages may be found in the lungs and sputum. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Strongyloidiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 35 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Strongyloides causes pulmonary symptoms such as dry cough and hemoptysis (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: hyperinfection larvae invade the colonic submucosa, lymphatics, and blood vessels (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — small-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins"},{"id":"sdl35_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how the worm burden in helminth infections is determined and differentiate species capable of autoinfection from those that are not.","batch":2,"stem":"A 51-year-old man from Jamaica with no history of immunosuppressive drug use develops overwhelming strongyloidiasis with larvae throughout his body. Which underlying infection most likely predisposed him?","choices":{"A":"Chronic hepatitis C virus","B":"Latent Epstein-Barr virus","C":"High-risk papillomavirus","D":"Reactivated human cytomegalovirus","E":"Human T-lymphotropic virus 1"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Human T-lymphotropic virus type I infection predisposes to very high Strongyloides worm burdens through uncontrolled autoinfection, leading to fatal disease. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Strongyloidiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 35 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hyperinfection syndrome results from accelerated autoinfection in the immunocompromised (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: Strongyloides is endemic in tropical South America, sub-Saharan Africa, and Southeast Asia (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 2 — choosing among antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins"},{"id":"sdl35_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify clinical manifestations associated with specific intestinal helminths and describe mechanisms of disease including nutrient competition, mechanical obstruction, and mucosal damage.","batch":2,"stem":"A 9-year-old girl in rural Colombia passed a long roundworm last month. She now has severe epigastric pain radiating to the back, and imaging shows a worm in the common bile duct. Which complication has developed?","choices":{"A":"Acute pancreatitis","B":"Perforated duodenal ulcer","C":"Acute mesenteric ischemia","D":"Splenic infarction","E":"Ureteral colic"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Ascaris worms that enter the biliary tree can cause acute pancreatitis and cholangitis. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Ascariasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ascaris may cause biliary obstruction and can emerge from the nose or mouth (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: Ascaris infections are diagnosed by eggs in the stool or passed adult worms (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 2 — acute and chronic pancreatitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify clinical manifestations associated with specific intestinal helminths and describe mechanisms of disease including nutrient competition, mechanical obstruction, and mucosal damage.","batch":2,"stem":"A 10-year-old boy in rural Southeast Asia who walks barefoot has fatigue and pallor. Hemoglobin is 8.2 g/dL (reference 11.5–15.5 g/dL) and MCV is 68 fL (reference 77–95 fL). Stool shows nematode eggs. Which additional laboratory finding is expected?","choices":{"A":"Low serum folate","B":"Low serum ferritin","C":"High indirect bilirubin","D":"High methylmalonic acid","E":"Positive Coombs test"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hookworms suck blood from the intestinal wall, and chronic blood loss causes iron deficiency with microcytic anemia and depleted iron stores. High indirect bilirubin implies hemolysis, but hookworm anemia comes from chronic blood loss. High methylmalonic acid indicates B12 deficiency, as with fish tapeworm, not the microcytic anemia of hookworm. A positive Coombs test indicates immune hemolysis, not hookworm blood loss. Sources: First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes) (Robbins Ch. 8 does not cover hookworm; Elentra objective framing). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hookworm can also cause cutaneous larva migrans, a pruritic serpiginous rash (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: hookworm larvae enter through skin after walking barefoot on contaminated beach or soil (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — intestinal absorption of iron, calcium, and fat-soluble vitamins","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"High indirect bilirubin implies hemolysis, but hookworm anem","D":"High methylmalonic acid indicates B12 deficiency, as with fi","E":"A positive Coombs test indicates immune hemolysis, not hookw"}},{"id":"sdl35_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify clinical manifestations associated with specific intestinal helminths and describe mechanisms of disease including nutrient competition, mechanical obstruction, and mucosal damage.","batch":2,"stem":"A 7-year-old girl with nightly perianal itching develops vulvar itching and discharge. A tape test is positive. What mechanism explains her new symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Eggs carried in the bloodstream","B":"Larvae entering through the skin","C":"Bacteria spreading from the bladder","D":"Worms moving into the vagina","E":"Cysts forming in the vulvar tissue"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Enterobius adults live in the cecum and appendix, and females migrate out of the anus at night. Worms moving into the vagina can cause vulvovaginitis. Pinworm is acquired by ingesting eggs, not by larvae entering through skin. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Enterobiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Enterobius is most common in children aged 5 to 10 years (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: very high pinworm burdens rarely cause rectal prolapse (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 5 — appendicitis and anal canal lesions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Pinworm is acquired by ingesting eggs, not by larvae enterin"}},{"id":"sdl35_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the role of eosinophilia in helminth infections and describe when eosinophilia is expected based on tissue invasion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old hunter ate undercooked bear meat 3 weeks ago. He has fever, myalgias, and periorbital edema, and the eosinophil fraction is 32% (reference range: 1–4%). Where are the parasites now located?","choices":{"A":"Skeletal muscle cells","B":"Hepatic sinusoids","C":"Lumen of the cecum","D":"Intrahepatic bile ducts","E":"Renal tubular cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Trichinella larvae disseminate hematogenously and penetrate striated muscle cells, causing fever, myalgias, marked eosinophilia, and periorbital edema. The infected muscle cell gains a collagenous capsule and new blood vessels. Trichinella adults live briefly in the intestine, but by 3 weeks the larvae have encysted in skeletal muscle. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Trichinellosis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 35 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Trichinella larvae encyst in striated muscle after undercooked pork ingestion (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: humans are dead-end hosts for Trichinella (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"C":"Trichinella adults live briefly in the intestine, but by 3 w"}},{"id":"sdl35_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the role of eosinophilia in helminth infections and describe when eosinophilia is expected based on tissue invasion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 5-year-old boy has dry cough, wheezing, fever, and transient pulmonary infiltrates with eosinophilia. Two stool examinations show no eggs, but 2 months later he passes a large roundworm. Why were the early stool tests negative?","choices":{"A":"Eggs were destroyed by acid","B":"Larvae had not yet matured","C":"Worms were in the bile duct","D":"Infection was in the skin","E":"Eggs were shed in urine"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The initial pulmonary stage of ascariasis causes Loeffler syndrome, a self-limited eosinophilic pneumonitis. Stool examination may not reveal the parasites if they have not yet matured in the intestine. Ascaris is acquired by ingesting eggs, so it has no skin phase. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Ascariasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Löffler syndrome is pulmonary eosinophilia from Ascaris larvae in the alveoli (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: heavy Ascaris burdens can cause malnutrition (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 2 — choosing among antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Ascaris is acquired by ingesting eggs, so it has no skin pha"}},{"id":"sdl35_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the role of eosinophilia in helminth infections and describe when eosinophilia is expected based on tissue invasion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old Ethiopian man who eats raw beef passes motile flat segments in his stool. The eosinophil fraction is 3% (reference range: 1–4%). What best explains the normal eosinophil count?","choices":{"A":"Steroid suppression of eosinophils","B":"Concurrent bacterial sepsis","C":"Adult worm confined to gut lumen","D":"Eosinophils trapped in spleen","E":"Low parasite egg output"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In humans, Taenia saginata lives only in the gut lumen and does not form tissue cysticerci, so it lacks the tissue phase that drives strong Th2 eosinophilic responses. He has no steroid exposure; his normal count reflects a luminal adult worm without tissue invasion. Egg output does not drive eosinophilia; tissue invasion does. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Cysticercosis and tapeworm infections); Elentra objective framing (Robbins does not state this directly). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: parasites are a classic cause of eosinophilia in the PACMAN mnemonic (First Aid Hematology (Eosinophils)). COMLEX: tapeworm mature distal proglottids contain many eggs and detach into the feces (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"He has no steroid exposure; his normal count reflects a lumi","E":"Egg output does not drive eosinophilia; tissue invasion does"}},{"id":"sdl35_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the diagnostic findings and appropriate pharmacologic treatments of major intestinal helminth infections, including identification of eggs, larvae, or adult worms and the mechanisms of action of commonly used anthelmintic agents.","batch":2,"stem":"A 7-year-old girl has 10 days of perianal itching that wakes her at night. A morning tape test is positive for pinworm eggs. Her CBC is normal, with hemoglobin 12.6 g/dL (reference range: 11.5–15.5 g/dL). Which treatment plan is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Mebendazole now and at 2 weeks","B":"Niclosamide as one single dose","C":"Oral ivermectin daily for 2 days","D":"Oral metronidazole for 7 days","E":"Oral vancomycin given for 10 days"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Tape test eggs flattened on one side confirm Enterobius. Katzung gives mebendazole 100 mg once for pinworm, repeated at 2 weeks, because reinfection from newly hatched worms is common. Niclosamide treats tapeworms, not pinworm. Ivermectin is the drug of choice for Strongyloides, not pinworm, which is treated with mebendazole or albendazole. Metronidazole treats protozoa such as Giardia and Entamoeba, not pinworm. Oral vancomycin treats C. difficile colitis, not helminths. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Enterobiasis); Katzung Ch. 53 (Mebendazole). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pinworm eggs are visualized via the tape test and treated with bendazoles or pyrantel pamoate (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: pinworm eggs mature rapidly and become infectious in about 6 hours (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Niclosamide treats tapeworms, not pinworm.","C":"Ivermectin is the drug of choice for Strongyloides, not pinw","D":"Metronidazole treats protozoa such as Giardia and Entamoeba,","E":"Oral vancomycin treats C. difficile colitis, not helminths."}},{"id":"sdl35_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the diagnostic findings and appropriate pharmacologic treatments of major intestinal helminth infections, including identification of eggs, larvae, or adult worms and the mechanisms of action of commonly used anthelmintic agents.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man from rural Kentucky has 3 months of intermittent diarrhea and bloating. His eosinophils are 14% (reference range: 1–3%). Stool microscopy shows motile larvae; no ova are seen. Which mechanism explains the action of the first-line drug for this infection?","choices":{"A":"Blocked tubulin polymerization in cells","B":"Intensified GABA signaling in nerves","C":"Increased calcium influx in tegument","D":"Blocked folate synthesis in cytoplasm","E":"Inhibited cell wall synthesis in worms"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Larvae rather than ova in stool indicate Strongyloides stercoralis, whose larvae are mostly passed in the stool. Its drug of choice, ivermectin, paralyzes nematodes by intensifying GABA-mediated transmission in peripheral nerves. Blocking tubulin polymerization is the benzimidazole mechanism, not that of ivermectin, the Strongyloides drug of choice. Calcium influx in the tegument is praziquantel's action against flukes and tapeworms. Blocking folate synthesis is the sulfonamide action on bacterial dihydropteroate synthase, not the action of an antihelminthic. Cell wall synthesis inhibition targets bacterial peptidoglycan, a polymer unique to bacteria and absent from worms. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Strongyloidiasis); Katzung Ch. 53 (Ivermectin; Table 53-1). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: rhabditiform larvae of Strongyloides are seen in feces under the microscope (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: Strongyloides is endemic in tropical regions and occurs sporadically in Appalachia (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Blocking tubulin polymerization is the benzimidazole mechani","C":"Calcium influx in the tegument is praziquantel's action agai","D":"Blocking folate synthesis is the sulfonamide action on bacte","E":"Cell wall synthesis inhibition targets bacterial peptidoglyc"}},{"id":"sdl35_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the diagnostic findings and appropriate pharmacologic treatments of major intestinal helminth infections, including identification of eggs, larvae, or adult worms and the mechanisms of action of commonly used anthelmintic agents.","batch":2,"stem":"An 8-year-old boy who recently moved from rural Bangladesh passes a 20-cm pink worm from his rectum. Stool examination shows numerous eggs. His hemoglobin is 11.9 g/dL (reference range: 11.5–15.5 g/dL). Which drug is first-line for this infection?","choices":{"A":"Niclosamide","B":"Diethylcarbamazine","C":"Albendazole","D":"Doxycycline","E":"Metronidazole"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Adult worms 15 to 35 cm long passed from the rectum, with eggs in stool, diagnose Ascaris lumbricoides. Katzung lists albendazole, pyrantel pamoate, or mebendazole as the drugs of choice. Niclosamide treats tapeworms, not the roundworm Ascaris. Diethylcarbamazine treats filarial infections, not Ascaris. Metronidazole treats protozoa, not intestinal roundworms. Doxycycline acts indirectly by killing Wolbachia, a symbiont of filarial parasites, so it is not a treatment for an intestinal roundworm. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Ascariasis); Katzung Ch. 53 (Table 53-1). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ascaris has knobby-coated oval eggs in feces and is treated with bendazoles (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: each adult female Ascaris releases about 200,000 eggs per day into the stool (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Niclosamide treats tapeworms, not the roundworm Ascaris.","B":"Diethylcarbamazine treats filarial infections, not Ascaris.","E":"Metronidazole treats protozoa, not intestinal roundworms.","D":"Doxycycline acts indirectly by killing Wolbachia, a symbiont"}},{"id":"trial_sdl35_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the modes of transmission and life cycle entry points of major intestinal helminths, including ingestion of eggs or encysted larvae, and skin penetration by larvae.","batch":3,"stem":"A 45-year-old man in northern Minnesota has megaloblastic anemia, and his stool shows broad tapeworm segments. He eats home-cured raw lake fish. How did he acquire the parasite?","choices":{"A":"Metacercariae on water plants","B":"Larvae in raw fish","C":"Bite of an infected sandfly","D":"Eggs shed by household dogs","E":"Cercariae in fresh lake water"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Diphyllobothrium latum, the fish tapeworm, is acquired by eating undercooked or raw fish and lives only in the gut. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Cysticercosis and tapeworm infections). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the fish tapeworm competes for vitamin B12 in the intestine, causing megaloblastic anemia (First Aid Microbiology (Cestodes)). COMLEX: the fish tapeworm does not form cysticerci in humans (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 4 — vitamin B12 absorption and the terminal ileum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl35_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how the worm burden in helminth infections is determined and differentiate species capable of autoinfection from those that are not.","batch":3,"stem":"A 6-year-old girl with pinworm infection scratches her perianal skin at night. About how soon do freshly laid eggs become infectious?","choices":{"A":"About 6 hours","B":"About 6 days","C":"About 2 weeks","D":"About 6 weeks","E":"About 6 months"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Enterobius eggs deposited on perianal skin mature rapidly and are infectious in approximately 6 hours, so scratching and hand-to-mouth spread allow quick reinfection. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Enterobiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 35 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Enterobius causes anal pruritus that is worse at night (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: the eggs spread from hands or bed linens (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 5 — defecation reflex and the anal sphincters","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins"},{"id":"trial_sdl35_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify clinical manifestations associated with specific intestinal helminths and describe mechanisms of disease including nutrient competition, mechanical obstruction, and mucosal damage.","batch":3,"stem":"A 5-year-old boy in rural Southeast Asia has a heavy whipworm (Trichuris trichiura) burden with loose stools and anemia. Which complication is classically described in children?","choices":{"A":"Biliary obstruction","B":"Hepatic cysts","C":"Rectal prolapse","D":"Seizure disorder","E":"Myocarditis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Trichuris trichiura is often asymptomatic but can cause loose stools, anemia, and rectal prolapse in children. Biliary obstruction is a complication of Ascaris, which can form worm balls and migrate into the biliary tree. Sources: First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes) (Robbins Ch. 8 does not cover Trichuris; Elentra objective framing). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Trichuris is spread fecal-orally and treated with bendazoles (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: Trichuris infection is often asymptomatic, so symptoms track heavy worm burden (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — gross and mucosal features of the large intestine","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Biliary obstruction is a complication of Ascaris, which can "}},{"id":"trial_sdl35_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the role of eosinophilia in helminth infections and describe when eosinophilia is expected based on tissue invasion.","batch":3,"stem":"A duodenal biopsy from a 38-year-old woman with mild strongyloidiasis shows larvae in the crypts without tissue invasion. Which inflammatory pattern is expected in the lamina propria?","choices":{"A":"Eosinophil-rich infiltrate","B":"Neutrophilic crypt abscesses","C":"Noncaseating granulomas","D":"Dense plasma cell sheets","E":"Transmural lymphoid nodules"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In mild strongyloidiasis, larvae are in the duodenal crypts but not the underlying tissue, with an eosinophil-rich lamina propria infiltrate and mucosal edema. Neutrophilic crypt abscesses are characteristic of ulcerative colitis. Noncaseating granulomas are characteristic of Crohn disease. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Strongyloidiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 35 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: eosinophils defend against helminths and release major basic protein (First Aid Hematology (Eosinophils)). COMLEX: in hyperinfection, many adult worms, larvae, and eggs fill the duodenal and ileal crypts with a mononuclear infiltrate (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — small-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"B":"Neutrophilic crypt abscesses are characteristic of ulcerativ","C":"Noncaseating granulomas are characteristic of Crohn disease."}},{"id":"trial_sdl35_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the diagnostic findings and appropriate pharmacologic treatments of major intestinal helminth infections, including identification of eggs, larvae, or adult worms and the mechanisms of action of commonly used anthelmintic agents.","batch":3,"stem":"A 61-year-old woman from eastern Tennessee has 4 months of intermittent loose stools and bloating. Her eosinophils are 11% (reference range: 1–3%). On a stool ova and parasite examination, which parasite form is most typical of the likely nematode?","choices":{"A":"Plugged barrel eggs","B":"Rhabditoid larvae","C":"Operculated eggs","D":"Mammillated eggs","E":"Gravid proglottids"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Strongyloides stercoralis produces eggs in the intestinal mucosa, but most larvae hatch and are passed in the stool, so stool examination shows rhabditoid larvae. Mammillated eggs are the stool finding in Ascaris infection. Gravid proglottids in stool indicate a tapeworm, not a nematode. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Strongyloidiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Strongyloides causes duodenitis, dry cough, and pruritus, and hyperinfection occurs with immunosuppression (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: Strongyloides females reproduce by parthenogenesis in the small-intestinal mucosa (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Mammillated eggs are the stool finding in Ascaris infection.","E":"Gravid proglottids in stool indicate a tapeworm, not a nemat"}},{"id":"trial_sdl35_q06","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify clinical manifestations associated with specific intestinal helminths and describe mechanisms of disease including nutrient competition, mechanical obstruction, and mucosal damage.","batch":3,"stem":"A 29-year-old man has had cystic brain lesions without symptoms for years. He now has new seizures, and imaging shows edema surrounding one lesion. What best explains the change?","choices":{"A":"Adult worm growth in the brain","B":"Cyst rupture into the bloodstream","C":"New egg release within the cyst","D":"Inflammation around a dying cyst","E":"Bacterial abscess within the cyst"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Viable Taenia solium cysts often do not cause symptoms and evade immunity, but an inflammatory response develops when cysticerci die and degenerate. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Cysticercosis and tapeworm infections). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: neurocysticercosis is treated with albendazole (First Aid Microbiology (Cestodes)). COMLEX: larval cysts in tissues outside the intestine do not develop into mature worms (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 2 — choosing among antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true},{"id":"grid_sdl35_q01","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how the worm burden in helminth infections is determined and differentiate species capable of autoinfection from those that are not.","batch":4,"stem":"A 58-year-old man who emigrated from rural Southeast Asia 30 years ago is treated with high-dose prednisone for a flare of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease. Two weeks later he has abdominal pain, diarrhea, cough, and wheezing, followed by fever and hypotension; blood cultures grow Escherichia coli. Microscopy of stool and sputum shows motile larvae. Which of the following sets of features best characterizes the organism causing this patient's illness?","grid":{"headers":["Entry route","Autoinfection","Eosinophilia"]},"choices":{"A":["Egg ingestion","No","Present"],"B":["Skin penetration","No","Present"],"C":["Skin penetration","Yes","Present"],"D":["Egg ingestion","Yes","Absent"],"E":["Larvae in fish","No","Absent"]},"correct":"C","explanation":"Choice C (skin penetration, autoinfection yes, eosinophilia present) is correct. Strongyloides stercoralis filariform larvae in soil penetrate intact skin and migrate through the lungs to the gut. The notes describe rhabditiform larvae that molt into filariform larvae inside the host and re-penetrate the bowel wall or perianal skin, so infection can persist for decades and become hyperinfection with gram-negative sepsis after corticosteroids; tissue migration causes eosinophilia, though steroids can blunt it. Choice A (egg ingestion, no autoinfection, eosinophilia present) is Ascaris lumbricoides, whose eggs hatch in the small intestine and whose larvae migrate through the lungs, causing transient eosinophilic pneumonitis, but which needs soil maturation and cannot reinfect the host. Choice B (skin penetration, no autoinfection, eosinophilia present) is hookworm (Necator, Ancylostoma), which enters through skin and migrates through the lungs but requires external development. Choice D (egg ingestion, autoinfection yes, eosinophilia absent) is Enterobius vermicularis, which reinfects by hand-to-mouth transfer of perianal eggs but stays in the lumen. Choice E (larvae in fish, no autoinfection, eosinophilia absent) is Diphyllobothrium latum, acquired from undercooked freshwater fish and confined to the lumen, where it competes for vitamin B12. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 35, sections 1, 2, and 4: transmission, autoinfection, and eosinophilia); Robbins Ch. 8 (helminths). Suppression of eosinophilia by corticosteroids is from general clinical knowledge and is not in the notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Strongyloides is the intestinal nematode that autoinfects, persists for decades, and causes hyperinfection with gram-negative sepsis on corticosteroids; stool shows larvae, not eggs, and ivermectin is the drug of choice (Exam 3 notes, SDL 35). COMLEX: screen and treat Strongyloides before steroids in patients from endemic areas.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 2 — choosing ivermectin versus albendazole versus praziquantel","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":36,"title":"SDL 36 — Pharmacology of Antihelminths","questions":[{"id":"sdl36_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, clinical uses and adverse reactions for the following medications used in the treatment of helminthic infections: albendazole, bithionol, diethylcarbamazine, doxycycline, ivermectin, mebendazole, moxidectin, praziquantal and thiabendazole.","batch":1,"stem":"Benzimidazoles such as mebendazole are thought to kill nematodes by inhibiting what?","choices":{"A":"Ergosterol synthesis","B":"Folate synthesis","C":"Peptidoglycan synthesis","D":"Chitin synthesis","E":"Microtubule synthesis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Benzimidazoles, including mebendazole and albendazole, are thought to act against nematodes by inhibiting microtubule synthesis. Ergosterol is the fungal membrane sterol targeted by antifungals such as amphotericin B, not by benzimidazoles. Folate synthesis via dihydropteroate synthase is the target of sulfonamides, not of benzimidazoles. Peptidoglycan is the bacterial cell wall polymer whose synthesis beta-lactam antibiotics block; it is not a target of antihelminthics. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Albendazole; Mebendazole). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: mebendazole binds tubulin and is listed with the microtubule-acting drugs (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: less than 10% of an oral mebendazole dose is absorbed (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Ergosterol is the fungal membrane sterol targeted by antifun","B":"Folate synthesis via dihydropteroate synthase is the target ","C":"Peptidoglycan is the bacterial cell wall polymer whose synth"}},{"id":"sdl36_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, clinical uses and adverse reactions for the following medications used in the treatment of helminthic infections: albendazole, bithionol, diethylcarbamazine, doxycycline, ivermectin, mebendazole, moxidectin, praziquantal and thiabendazole.","batch":1,"stem":"Which antibiotic kills adult filarial worms indirectly by eliminating their Wolbachia bacterial symbionts?","choices":{"A":"Doxycycline","B":"Azithromycin","C":"Ciprofloxacin","D":"Vancomycin","E":"Clindamycin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Doxycycline has macrofilaricidal activity against Wuchereria bancrofti and activity against onchocerciasis because it kills Wolbachia, an intracellular bacterial symbiont of filarial parasites. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Doxycycline). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: tetracyclines cause tooth discoloration and are generally avoided in pregnancy and in children, except doxycycline (First Aid Antimicrobials — Tetracyclines). COMLEX: Katzung reports better activity against adult W bancrofti than any other available drug (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, clinical uses and adverse reactions for the following medications used in the treatment of helminthic infections: albendazole, bithionol, diethylcarbamazine, doxycycline, ivermectin, mebendazole, moxidectin, praziquantal and thiabendazole.","batch":1,"stem":"Praziquantel paralyzes trematodes and cestodes by increasing their membrane permeability to which ion?","choices":{"A":"Chloride","B":"Sodium","C":"Calcium","D":"Potassium","E":"Magnesium"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Praziquantel increases the permeability of trematode and cestode cell membranes to calcium, causing paralysis, dislodgement, and death of the worm. Increased chloride signaling via GABA or glutamate-gated channels is ivermectin's mechanism, not praziquantel's. Praziquantel increases the permeability of trematode and cestode cell membranes to calcium, not sodium, causing paralysis and dislodgement. Praziquantel increases trematode and cestode membrane permeability to calcium, not potassium. Praziquantel increases trematode and cestode membrane permeability to calcium, not magnesium. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Praziquantel). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: praziquantel treats Schistosoma, Clonorchis, and Taenia infections (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: praziquantel tablets are swallowed without chewing because the bitter taste can induce retching (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Increased chloride signaling via GABA or glutamate-gated cha","B":"Praziquantel increases the permeability of trematode and cestode cell membranes to calcium, n","D":"Praziquantel increases trematode and cestode membrane permea","E":"Praziquantel increases trematode and cestode membrane permeability to calcium, not m"}},{"id":"sdl36_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Appreciate when one antihelminthic medication may be chosen over another in a clinical scenario.","batch":1,"stem":"Which drug of choice is used for lymphatic filariasis from Wuchereria bancrofti and for loiasis?","choices":{"A":"Diethylcarbamazine","B":"Triclabendazole","C":"Pyrantel pamoate","D":"Piperazine citrate","E":"Niclosamide tablets"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Diethylcarbamazine is a drug of choice for W bancrofti, Brugia, and Loa loa because of its efficacy and lack of serious toxicity; it immobilizes microfilariae and exposes them to host defenses. Triclabendazole is the drug of choice for Fasciola hepatica, not filariasis. Pyrantel pamoate treats luminal nematodes such as Ascaris and pinworm, not filariae. Niclosamide is a second-line drug for tapeworm infections and has no role in filariasis or loiasis. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Diethylcarbamazine citrate). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Loa loa (deer fly) and Wuchereria bancrofti (mosquito) are both treated with diethylcarbamazine (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: the plasma half-life of diethylcarbamazine is longer in alkaline urine (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 3 — clinical manifestations of intestinal helminths","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Triclabendazole is the drug of choice for Fasciola hepatica,","C":"Pyrantel pamoate treats luminal nematodes such as Ascaris an","E":"Niclosamide is a second-line drug for tapeworm infections an"}},{"id":"sdl36_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Appreciate when one antihelminthic medication may be chosen over another in a clinical scenario.","batch":1,"stem":"For neurocysticercosis, why is albendazole now generally preferred over praziquantel?","choices":{"A":"Avoids need for corticosteroids","B":"Single oral dose regimen","C":"No liver toxicity with long use","D":"Better subarachnoid penetration","E":"Proven safety in early pregnancy"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Albendazole is preferred for neurocysticercosis because of a shorter course, lower cost, improved penetration into the subarachnoid space, and higher drug levels with corticosteroids. Albendazole does not avoid corticosteroids; its levels actually rise when steroids are co-administered. Albendazole is preferred for a shorter course, not a single-dose regimen. Long-term albendazole can cause hepatotoxicity and requires liver monitoring. Albendazole is contraindicated in pregnancy. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Albendazole). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: neurocysticercosis is treated with albendazole, while intestinal taeniasis is treated with praziquantel (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: corticosteroids are usually given with the antihelminthic to limit inflammation from dying organisms (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 3 — clinical manifestations of intestinal helminths","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Albendazole does not avoid corticosteroids; its levels actua","B":"Albendazole is preferred for a shorter course, not a single-","C":"Long-term albendazole can cause hepatotoxicity and requires ","E":"Albendazole is contraindicated in pregnancy."}},{"id":"sdl36_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Appreciate when one antihelminthic medication may be chosen over another in a clinical scenario.","batch":1,"stem":"For uncomplicated strongyloidiasis, which drug is given as 200 mcg/kg once daily for 2 days?","choices":{"A":"Praziquantel","B":"Ivermectin","C":"Niclosamide","D":"Oxamniquine","E":"Pyrantel pamoate"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ivermectin is the drug of choice for strongyloidiasis, given as 200 mcg/kg once daily for 2 days; thiabendazole is an alternative but is more toxic. Praziquantel treats flukes and tapeworms, not Strongyloides. Pyrantel pamoate is for Ascaris and pinworm, whereas ivermectin is the drug of choice for strongyloidiasis. Niclosamide is a second-line drug for tapeworms, whereas ivermectin is the drug of choice for strongyloidiasis. Oxamniquine is an alternative to praziquantel for Schistosoma mansoni, not a treatment for strongyloidiasis. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Ivermectin; Thiabendazole). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Strongyloides is treated with ivermectin or bendazoles (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: in disseminated disease in immunosuppressed patients, monthly suppressive therapy may help (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 3 — clinical manifestations of intestinal helminths","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Praziquantel treats flukes and tapeworms, not Strongyloides.","E":"Pyrantel pamoate is for Ascaris and pinworm, whereas ivermec","C":"Niclosamide is a second-line drug for tapeworms, whereas ive","D":"Oxamniquine is an alternative to praziquantel for Schistosom"}},{"id":"sdl36_q07","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, clinical uses and adverse reactions for the following medications used in the treatment of helminthic infections: albendazole, bithionol, diethylcarbamazine, doxycycline, ivermectin, mebendazole, moxidectin, praziquantal and thiabendazole.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old sheep farmer with a large hepatic hydatid cyst starts several months of albendazole before surgery. Which monitoring is advised during this long course?","choices":{"A":"Blood counts and liver tests","B":"Serum creatinine and urinalysis","C":"Serial ECG for QT changes","D":"Audiometry and visual fields","E":"Serum magnesium and phosphate"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Long-term albendazole for hydatid disease is usually well tolerated but can cause raised liver enzymes and pancytopenia, so blood counts and liver function are monitored. Long-term albendazole threatens the liver and marrow, not the kidneys, so creatinine and urinalysis are not the advised monitoring. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Albendazole). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Echinococcus hydatid cysts are treated with albendazole, with surgery for some cysts (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: albendazole should be avoided in patients with cirrhosis (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Long-term albendazole threatens the liver and marrow, not th"}},{"id":"sdl36_q08","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, clinical uses and adverse reactions for the following medications used in the treatment of helminthic infections: albendazole, bithionol, diethylcarbamazine, doxycycline, ivermectin, mebendazole, moxidectin, praziquantal and thiabendazole.","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman at 8 weeks' gestation has perianal itching, and a tape test shows pinworm eggs. Her physician defers mebendazole. What property of mebendazole explains this?","choices":{"A":"Causes neonatal gray syndrome","B":"Inhibits fetal folate uptake","C":"Causes fetal tooth staining","D":"Teratogenic in animal studies","E":"Causes fetal renal tubule damage"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Mebendazole is teratogenic in animals and is therefore contraindicated in pregnancy. Fetal tooth staining is a doxycycline (tetracycline) concern, not a mebendazole one. Neonatal gray syndrome results from chloramphenicol accumulation in infants, not from mebendazole. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Mebendazole). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 36 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Enterobius is treated with bendazoles or pyrantel (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: for pinworm, mebendazole 100 mg is given once and repeated at 2 weeks (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 1 — transmission and entry routes of intestinal helminths","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung","whyNot":{"C":"Fetal tooth staining is a doxycycline (tetracycline) concern","A":"Neonatal gray syndrome results from chloramphenicol accumula"}},{"id":"sdl36_q09","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, clinical uses and adverse reactions for the following medications used in the treatment of helminthic infections: albendazole, bithionol, diethylcarbamazine, doxycycline, ivermectin, mebendazole, moxidectin, praziquantal and thiabendazole.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man from Ghana with onchocerciasis receives a single oral dose of the drug of choice, a macrocyclic lactone. Concurrent use of which drug class should be avoided?","choices":{"A":"Proton pump inhibitors","B":"Thiazide diuretics","C":"Beta-blockers","D":"ACE inhibitors","E":"Benzodiazepines"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Ivermectin paralyzes nematodes by intensifying GABA-mediated transmission, so it is best not combined with other GABA-enhancing drugs such as barbiturates, benzodiazepines, or valproic acid. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Ivermectin). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Onchocerca volvulus causes black skin nodules and river blindness (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: treatment reactions in onchocerciasis come mainly from killing microfilariae and peak on day 2 (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 4 — eosinophilia and tissue invasion in helminth infections","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q10","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Appreciate when one antihelminthic medication may be chosen over another in a clinical scenario.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man from Cameroon has a worm crossing his conjunctiva and a very high Loa loa microfilarial load, well above the 2500/mL high-risk level. Which initial approach is safest?","choices":{"A":"Immediate full-dose diethylcarbamazine","B":"Single-dose praziquantel","C":"Slower-acting albendazole first","D":"Topical thiabendazole cream","E":"Weekly oral niclosamide"},"correct":"C","explanation":"For heavy Loa loa burdens (more than 2500 parasites/mL), Katzung lists apheresis or therapy with albendazole, which is slower acting and better tolerated, before definitive filaricidal treatment. Full-dose DEC in heavy Loa loa loads risks encephalopathy and retinal hemorrhage, so burden is lowered first. Praziquantel treats flukes and tapeworms and has no role in loiasis. Thiabendazole is an alternative for strongyloidiasis and larva migrans, not loiasis. Niclosamide is a second-line tapeworm drug, minimally absorbed from the gut, with no activity against Loa loa microfilariae. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Diethylcarbamazine citrate; Ivermectin). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Loa loa causes skin swelling and a worm in the conjunctiva (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: reactions to dying microfilariae are occasionally severe in L loa infections (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 4 — eosinophilia and tissue invasion in helminth infections","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Full-dose DEC in heavy Loa loa loads risks encephalopathy an","B":"Praziquantel treats flukes and tapeworms and has no role in ","D":"Thiabendazole is an alternative for strongyloidiasis and lar","E":"Niclosamide is a second-line tapeworm drug, minimally absorb"}},{"id":"sdl36_q11","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Appreciate when one antihelminthic medication may be chosen over another in a clinical scenario.","batch":2,"stem":"A 22-year-old woman with symptomatic parenchymal neurocysticercosis is taking dexamethasone for cerebral edema. What effect does dexamethasone have on praziquantel?","choices":{"A":"Reduced plasma drug levels","B":"Increased plasma drug levels","C":"Prolonged elimination half-life","D":"Greater CSF drug penetration","E":"Faster cyst calcification"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Corticosteroids can decrease plasma praziquantel levels by up to 50%, whereas albendazole levels increase, one reason albendazole is preferred when steroids are needed. Dexamethasone lowers praziquantel levels by up to 50%; it is albendazole levels that rise. Superior CSF penetration is an albendazole advantage, not an effect of dexamethasone on praziquantel. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Praziquantel; Albendazole). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Taenia solium cysticercosis follows ingestion of eggs in fecally contaminated food (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: some workers caution against praziquantel in spinal neurocysticercosis (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 3 — clinical manifestations of intestinal helminths","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Dexamethasone lowers praziquantel levels by up to 50%; it is","D":"Superior CSF penetration is an albendazole advantage, not an"}},{"id":"sdl36_q12","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Appreciate when one antihelminthic medication may be chosen over another in a clinical scenario.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man from Peru has right upper quadrant pain, eosinophilia, and Fasciola hepatica eggs after eating watercress. Why is praziquantel a poor choice for him?","choices":{"A":"Excessive toxicity in hepatic disease","B":"Lack of activity in this infection","C":"Poor oral bioavailability in adults","D":"Need for prolonged IV dosing","E":"High rates of liver fluke relapse"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Praziquantel treats most trematodes but was not effective for fascioliasis even at high doses; triclabendazole is the drug of choice, with bithionol as an alternative. Praziquantel has about 80% oral bioavailability; its problem in fascioliasis is Fasciola resistance. Praziquantel is given orally; it fails in fascioliasis because Fasciola is resistant. Praziquantel is generally well tolerated even in the hepatosplenic stage of advanced schistosomiasis; its problem in fascioliasis is lack of efficacy. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Praziquantel; Triclabendazole; Bithionol). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: praziquantel treats trematodes such as Schistosoma and Clonorchis (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: triclabendazole may prolong the QT interval (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 3 — hydatid cysts and other parasitic liver infections","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Praziquantel has about 80% oral bioavailability; its problem","D":"Praziquantel is given orally; it fails in fascioliasis becau","A":"Praziquantel is generally well tolerated even in the hepatos"}},{"id":"trial_sdl36_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, clinical uses and adverse reactions for the following medications used in the treatment of helminthic infections: albendazole, bithionol, diethylcarbamazine, doxycycline, ivermectin, mebendazole, moxidectin, praziquantal and thiabendazole.","batch":3,"stem":"A 60-year-old man with hydatid liver disease starts albendazole. How should he take it for this tissue infection?","choices":{"A":"With a fatty meal","B":"On an empty stomach","C":"With an antacid dose","D":"With grapefruit juice","E":"At bedtime with milk"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Albendazole is taken on an empty stomach for intraluminal parasites but with a fatty meal for tissue parasites, which increases its erratic absorption. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Albendazole). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: albendazole is used for Echinococcus and neurocysticercosis (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: albendazole is converted on first pass to the active metabolite albendazole sulfoxide (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 3 — hydatid cysts and other parasitic liver infections","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl36_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, clinical uses and adverse reactions for the following medications used in the treatment of helminthic infections: albendazole, bithionol, diethylcarbamazine, doxycycline, ivermectin, mebendazole, moxidectin, praziquantal and thiabendazole.","batch":3,"stem":"A 33-year-old woman has strongyloidiasis. Why has thiabendazole been largely replaced by other agents?","choices":{"A":"Lower cure rates than other agents","B":"Greater toxicity than other agents","C":"Need for prolonged IV infusions","D":"Widespread parasite drug resistance","E":"Poor absorption from the gut"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Thiabendazole is much more toxic than other benzimidazoles and ivermectin; irreversible liver failure and fatal Stevens-Johnson syndrome have been reported. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Thiabendazole). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bendazoles treat most intestinal nematodes (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: thiabendazole cream can be applied topically for cutaneous larva migrans (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 2 — worm burden and autoinfection","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl36_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, clinical uses and adverse reactions for the following medications used in the treatment of helminthic infections: albendazole, bithionol, diethylcarbamazine, doxycycline, ivermectin, mebendazole, moxidectin, praziquantal and thiabendazole.","batch":3,"stem":"A 30-year-old man with river blindness receives a single 8-mg oral dose of a drug approved in 2018 with a half-life of 3–4 weeks. Which drug is it?","choices":{"A":"Triclabendazole","B":"Oxamniquine","C":"Moxidectin","D":"Niclosamide","E":"Metrifonate"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Moxidectin, a macrocyclic lactone approved in 2018 for onchocerciasis in persons 12 years or older, is given once orally and has a half-life of 3–4 weeks. Triclabendazole is the drug of choice for fascioliasis, not onchocerciasis. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Moxidectin). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Onchocerca volvulus is spread by the female black fly (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: moxidectin lowered skin microfilarial loads more than the older standard drug in a recent trial (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 4 — eosinophilia and tissue invasion in helminth infections","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Triclabendazole is the drug of choice for fascioliasis, not "}},{"id":"trial_sdl36_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Appreciate when one antihelminthic medication may be chosen over another in a clinical scenario.","batch":3,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman with fascioliasis has a long QT interval, so the drug of choice is withheld. Which alternative is listed for this infection?","choices":{"A":"Bithionol","B":"Praziquantel","C":"Pyrantel pamoate","D":"Mebendazole","E":"Oxamniquine"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Bithionol is an alternative to triclabendazole for fascioliasis and to praziquantel for paragonimiasis. Fasciola is resistant to praziquantel, so it is not an alternative for fascioliasis. Pyrantel pamoate is used for pinworm, Ascaris, and hookworm, not liver flukes. Mebendazole is used for intestinal roundworms, not fascioliasis. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Bithionol; Triclabendazole). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 36 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: liver flukes such as Clonorchis are acquired from undercooked fish (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: skin rashes after a week of bithionol suggest a reaction to antigens from dying worms (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 3 — hydatid cysts and other parasitic liver infections","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung","whyNot":{"B":"Fasciola is resistant to praziquantel, so it is not an alter","C":"Pyrantel pamoate is used for pinworm, Ascaris, and hookworm,","D":"Mebendazole is used for intestinal roundworms, not fasciolia"}},{"id":"trial_sdl36_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Appreciate when one antihelminthic medication may be chosen over another in a clinical scenario.","batch":3,"stem":"An 8-year-old boy with hookworm anemia is given albendazole rather than mebendazole. What is the main reason?","choices":{"A":"Lower cost per complete course of care","B":"Safety in the first trimester","C":"Once-weekly dosing schedule","D":"Better efficacy in this infection","E":"Topical form for skin lesions"},"correct":"D","explanation":"For hookworm infection and trichuriasis, albendazole 400 mg daily for 3 days is recommended, with improved efficacy over mebendazole for hookworm. Albendazole is contraindicated in pregnancy, so first-trimester safety is not an advantage. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Albendazole). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 36 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ancylostoma and Necator cause microcytic anemia and are treated with bendazoles or pyrantel (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: mebendazole cure rates for hookworm are low, though worm burden falls sharply (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 3 — clinical manifestations of intestinal helminths","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung","whyNot":{"B":"Albendazole is contraindicated in pregnancy, so first-trimes"}},{"id":"trial_sdl36_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Appreciate when one antihelminthic medication may be chosen over another in a clinical scenario.","batch":3,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman has many viable parenchymal brain cysts from Taenia solium. Which regimen improves the parasiticidal effect?","choices":{"A":"Niclosamide plus mebendazole","B":"Pyrantel plus piperazine","C":"Thiabendazole plus pyrantel","D":"Metrifonate plus oxamniquine","E":"Albendazole plus praziquantel"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Combining albendazole with praziquantel improves efficacy in patients with multiple brain cysts. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Albendazole; Praziquantel). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 36 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: cysticercosis is treated with albendazole, plus praziquantel in some cases (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: praziquantel is contraindicated in ocular cysticercosis (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 1 — transmission and entry routes of intestinal helminths","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung"},{"id":"grid_sdl36_q01","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Appreciate when one antihelminthic medication may be chosen over another in a clinical scenario.","batch":4,"stem":"A 27-year-old man who emigrated from a rural area of Central America has a first generalized seizure. Brain CT shows multiple small cystic lesions, several containing an eccentric scolex. An ophthalmologic examination shows no ocular cysts. Dexamethasone is started, and an antiparasitic drug is chosen that gives a shorter course, reaches the subarachnoid space well, and is not weakened by the corticosteroid. Which of the following sets of features best characterizes the antiparasitic drug chosen?","grid":{"headers":["Molecular action","Level on steroids","Site of action"]},"choices":{"A":["↑ Ca²⁺ permeability","↓","Tissue and lumen"],"B":["Binds β-tubulin","↑","Tissue and lumen"],"C":["Binds β-tubulin","↔","Gut lumen"],"D":["Opens GABA/glutamate Cl⁻","↔","Tissue and lumen"],"E":["Binds β-tubulin","↑","Gut lumen"]},"correct":"B","explanation":"Choice B (binds β-tubulin, level rises on steroids, tissue and lumen) is correct. Albendazole is preferred over praziquantel for neurocysticercosis. The notes describe a benzimidazole that binds parasite β-tubulin to block microtubule synthesis and is converted to albendazole sulfoxide, which distributes widely into tissues, CSF, bile, and hydatid cysts. The course is shorter and cheaper, CSF penetration is better, and levels of the active metabolite rise when corticosteroids are added. Choice A (raises Ca²⁺ permeability, level falls on steroids, tissue and lumen) is praziquantel, which causes Ca²⁺ influx and paralysis of cestodes and trematodes, but dexamethasone lowers its plasma level by up to 50%. Choice C (binds β-tubulin, no steroid effect, gut lumen) is mebendazole, a benzimidazole with less than 10% systemic bioavailability that acts in the gut lumen against intestinal roundworms. Choice D (opens GABA/glutamate Cl⁻ channels, no steroid effect, tissue and lumen) is ivermectin, which paralyzes nematodes and arthropods and is the drug of choice for strongyloidiasis and onchocerciasis, not cysticercosis. Choice E (binds β-tubulin, level rises on steroids, gut lumen) matches no agent in the notes; it pairs the corticosteroid interaction of albendazole with the luminal action of mebendazole, but a luminal drug could not treat brain cysts. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 36, sections 1 and 2: albendazole, mebendazole, ivermectin, praziquantel, and drug choice in neurocysticercosis); Katzung Ch. 53 (antihelminthic drugs). The unchanged corticosteroid effect on mebendazole and ivermectin levels is inferred because the notes describe no such interaction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: neurocysticercosis → albendazole (β-tubulin) plus corticosteroids; steroids raise albendazole sulfoxide but lower praziquantel levels by up to 50% (Exam 3 notes, SDL 36). COMLEX: exclude ocular cysticercosis before therapy, because dying cysts in the eye cause irreversible damage.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 2 — Taenia solium cysticercosis from egg ingestion","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":37,"title":"SDL 37 — Pharmacology of GI motility, IBS, and short-bowel syndrome","questions":[{"id":"sdl37_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of action, clinical uses, and important adverse effects of medications (prokinetic and cholinomimetic agents) used to stimulate gastrointestinal motility.","batch":1,"stem":"Metoclopramide speeds gastric emptying and raises lower esophageal sphincter pressure mainly by what action?","choices":{"A":"Muscarinic M3 receptor agonism","B":"Dopamine D2 receptor blockade","C":"Serotonin 5-HT3 receptor blockade","D":"Histamine H2 receptor blockade","E":"Mu opioid receptor agonism"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Metoclopramide is a D2 antagonist; blocking inhibitory dopamine receptors in the gut raises LES pressure, enhances esophageal peristalsis and speeds gastric emptying, with little effect on small bowel or colon. Direct muscarinic agonism is bethanechol's mechanism, not metoclopramide's. 5-HT3 blockade is alosetron's mechanism for IBS-D, not metoclopramide's prokinetic effect. Mu opioid agonism, as with loperamide, slows transit rather than speeding gastric emptying. H2 receptor blockade competitively inhibits parietal cell acid secretion and does not speed gastric emptying. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Dopamine Receptor Antagonists). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: metoclopramide is a D2 antagonist used for diabetic gastroparesis and persistent GERD (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: metoclopramide also blocks D2 receptors in the area postrema chemoreceptor trigger zone (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — CCK and GIP slow gastric emptying","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Direct muscarinic agonism is bethanechol's mechanism, not me","C":"5-HT3 blockade is alosetron's mechanism for IBS-D, not metoc","E":"Mu opioid agonism, as with loperamide, slows transit rather ","D":"H2 receptor blockade competitively inhibits parietal cell ac"}},{"id":"sdl37_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of action, clinical uses, and important adverse effects of medications (prokinetic and cholinomimetic agents) used to stimulate gastrointestinal motility.","batch":1,"stem":"Which agent given intravenously is used to treat acute colonic pseudo-obstruction (Ogilvie syndrome)?","choices":{"A":"Atropine","B":"Octreotide","C":"Ondansetron","D":"Neostigmine","E":"Dicyclomine"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Neostigmine, an acetylcholinesterase inhibitor, raises acetylcholine at enteric synapses; a 2 mg IV dose rapidly evacuates colonic gas and stool in acute pseudo-obstruction. Dicyclomine is an antispasmodic for IBS cramping and would worsen colonic pseudo-obstruction. The anticholinergic properties of atropine add to its antidiarrheal effect, the opposite of what colonic pseudo-obstruction needs; the acetylcholinesterase inhibitor neostigmine is used instead. Ondansetron is a 5-HT3 antagonist antiemetic that has no effect on gastric motility and may slow colonic transit, so it would not relieve pseudo-obstruction. Octreotide inhibits intestinal secretion and at higher doses inhibits motility, so it does not decompress a dilated colon. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Cholinomimetic Agents). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: neostigmine is a quaternary (charged) AChE inhibitor that does not enter the CNS and treats postoperative ileus and urinary retention (First Aid Autonomic Drugs (cholinomimetic agents)). COMLEX: cholinergic effects of the IV dose include salivation, vomiting and bradycardia (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — CCK and GIP slow gastric emptying","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Dicyclomine is an antispasmodic for IBS cramping and would w","A":"The anticholinergic properties of atropine add to its antidi","C":"Ondansetron is a 5-HT3 antagonist antiemetic that has no eff","B":"Octreotide inhibits intestinal secretion and at higher doses"}},{"id":"sdl37_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of action, clinical uses, and important adverse effects of medications (prokinetic and cholinomimetic agents) used to stimulate gastrointestinal motility.","batch":1,"stem":"Why does domperidone cause extrapyramidal effects far less often than metoclopramide?","choices":{"A":"Poor blood-brain barrier entry","B":"Faster renal clearance of the drug","C":"Weaker binding at gut D2 receptors","D":"Added serotonin 5-HT4 agonism","E":"Greater pituitary prolactin release"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Domperidone shares the D2-blocking mechanism but crosses the blood-brain barrier poorly, so central extrapyramidal effects are rare. Domperidone has the same peripheral D2-blocking prokinetic mechanism as metoclopramide, so weaker gut binding is not the reason. Domperidone is a D2 antagonist without 5-HT4 agonism; its CNS sparing reflects poor blood-brain barrier entry. Domperidone still causes hyperprolactinemia, which does not explain fewer extrapyramidal effects. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Dopamine Receptor Antagonists). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 37 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: centrally acting D2 blockers cause parkinsonism and acute dystonia as drug-induced movement disorders (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: domperidone still acts at the area postrema, which lies outside the barrier (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — CCK and GIP slow gastric emptying","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung","whyNot":{"C":"Domperidone has the same peripheral D2-blocking prokinetic m","D":"Domperidone is a D2 antagonist without 5-HT4 agonism; its CN","E":"Domperidone still causes hyperprolactinemia, which does not "}},{"id":"sdl37_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare and contrast the pharmacologic therapies for irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) based on the predominant symptom pattern, including IBS with diarrhea, IBS with constipation, and mixed-type IBS.","batch":1,"stem":"Alosetron use for diarrhea-predominant IBS is restricted mainly because of what serious adverse effect?","choices":{"A":"Torsades de pointes","B":"Ischemic colitis","C":"Agranulocytosis","D":"QT shortening","E":"Hepatic necrosis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Alosetron, a 5-HT3 antagonist, causes constipation in up to 30% and ischemic colitis in up to 3 per 1000 patients, so it is restricted to women with severe IBS with diarrhea. Torsades and cardiac toxicity were the problem with older 5-HT4 agonists like cisapride, not alosetron. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Drugs Used in the Treatment of Irritable Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: 5-HT3 antagonists such as ondansetron are antiemetics whose adverse effects include headache and constipation (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: alosetron blocks 5-HT3 receptors on extrinsic afferents that carry visceral pain signals (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 1 — irritable bowel syndrome diagnosis and first-line treatment","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Torsades and cardiac toxicity were the problem with older 5-"}},{"id":"sdl37_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare and contrast the pharmacologic therapies for irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) based on the predominant symptom pattern, including IBS with diarrhea, IBS with constipation, and mixed-type IBS.","batch":1,"stem":"Lubiprostone relieves constipation-predominant IBS by which mechanism?","choices":{"A":"ClC-2 chloride channel activation","B":"Mu opioid receptor activation in gut","C":"Na+/K+ ATPase pump inhibition","D":"Bulk retention of stool water","E":"5-HT3 receptor inhibition on afferents"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Lubiprostone, a prostanoic acid derivative, activates type 2 chloride channels (ClC-2) on the apical enterocyte membrane, increasing chloride-rich fluid secretion into the lumen. Mu opioid activation, as with loperamide, slows transit and treats diarrhea, not constipation. Bulk retention of stool water describes fiber such as psyllium, not lubiprostone. 5-HT3 inhibition is alosetron's mechanism for IBS-D and causes constipation. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Chloride Channel Activator). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: lubiprostone appears in the anticonstipation table with diarrhea and nausea as adverse effects (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: lubiprostone is approved for women with IBS-C and avoided in women who may become pregnant (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 1 — irritable bowel syndrome diagnosis and first-line treatment","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Mu opioid activation, as with loperamide, slows transit and ","D":"Bulk retention of stool water describes fiber such as psylli","E":"5-HT3 inhibition is alosetron's mechanism for IBS-D and caus"}},{"id":"sdl37_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare and contrast the pharmacologic therapies for irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) based on the predominant symptom pattern, including IBS with diarrhea, IBS with constipation, and mixed-type IBS.","batch":1,"stem":"Eluxadoline for diarrhea-predominant IBS should be avoided in a patient with which history?","choices":{"A":"Prior appendectomy","B":"Hypothyroidism","C":"Prior pancreatitis","D":"Recurrent gout attacks","E":"Migraine headaches"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Eluxadoline, a mu-opioid agonist, can cause sphincter of Oddi spasm with pancreatitis, so it is avoided with prior pancreatitis, alcoholism or sphincter of Oddi disease. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Antidiarrheal Agents). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: peripherally acting mu agonists slow gut motility with low addiction potential (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: a lower 75 mg dose is used after cholecystectomy because sphincter of Oddi complications are more common (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 1 — irritable bowel syndrome diagnosis and first-line treatment","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanism of action and clinical use of teduglutide in the management of short-bowel syndrome","batch":1,"stem":"Teduglutide is an analog of which gut hormone?","choices":{"A":"Glucagon-like peptide 1","B":"Glucose insulinotropic peptide","C":"Cholecystokinin","D":"Glucagon-like peptide 2","E":"Pancreatic polypeptide"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Teduglutide is a GLP-2 analog used for short-bowel syndrome; GLP-2 is an intestinotrophic peptide released from L cells. GLP-1 receptor agonists such as exenatide are glucose-lowering drugs for diabetes, whereas teduglutide is a GLP-2 analog. Cholecystokinin is the hormone that stimulates gallbladder emptying, not the peptide mimicked by teduglutide. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Glucagon-Like Peptide 2 Analog for Short-Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GLP-1 analogs such as exenatide are incretin drugs, unlike the intestinotrophic analog used for short gut (First Aid Endocrine Pharmacology). COMLEX: teduglutide binds receptors on enteric neurons and endocrine cells (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — enterohepatic circulation and terminal ileal bile salt loss","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"GLP-1 receptor agonists such as exenatide are glucose-loweri","C":"Cholecystokinin is the hormone that stimulates gallbladder e"}},{"id":"sdl37_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanism of action and clinical use of teduglutide in the management of short-bowel syndrome","batch":1,"stem":"By which route is teduglutide given for short-bowel syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Weekly intravenous infusion pump","B":"Twice-daily oral tablet","C":"Monthly intramuscular depot","D":"Daily rectal suppository","E":"Daily subcutaneous injection"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Teduglutide is given at 0.05 mg/kg once daily by subcutaneous injection. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Glucagon-Like Peptide 2 Analog for Short-Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: teduglutide is a GLP-2 analog given by subcutaneous injection (Katzung Ch. 62). COMLEX: teduglutide dosing is weight based at 0.05 mg/kg per day (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — enterohepatic circulation and terminal ileal bile salt loss","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanism of action and clinical use of teduglutide in the management of short-bowel syndrome","batch":1,"stem":"Teduglutide helps patients with short-bowel syndrome mainly by promoting what?","choices":{"A":"Faster emptying of the stomach","B":"Slower colonic transit","C":"Mucosal epithelial growth","D":"Pancreatic enzyme output","E":"Hepatic bile acid output"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Through trophic hormones, teduglutide stimulates mucosal epithelial growth of the remaining bowel and enhances fluid absorption. Teduglutide actually delays gastric emptying rather than speeding it. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Glucagon-Like Peptide 2 Analog for Short-Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: short-bowel syndrome causes malabsorption of nutrients and fluid after extensive resection (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: teduglutide acts on enteric neurons and endocrine cells to adapt the residual gut (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — enterohepatic circulation and terminal ileal bile salt loss","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Teduglutide actually delays gastric emptying rather than spe"}},{"id":"sdl37_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of action, clinical uses, and important adverse effects of medications (prokinetic and cholinomimetic agents) used to stimulate gastrointestinal motility.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman with diabetic gastroparesis has taken metoclopramide for 2 years. She now has repetitive lip smacking and tongue thrusting that persist after the drug is stopped. Which adverse effect is this?","choices":{"A":"Acute dystonic reaction","B":"Akathisia restlessness","C":"Serotonin syndrome","D":"Tardive dyskinesia","E":"Essential tremor"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Long-term metoclopramide can cause tardive dyskinesia that may be irreversible, so prolonged use is avoided, especially in older adults. Acute dystonia occurs early in therapy, whereas persistent movements after long-term use indicate tardive dyskinesia. Akathisia is subjective restlessness, not the persistent orofacial movements of tardive dyskinesia. Serotonin syndrome is an acute hyperthermic syndrome from serotonergic drugs such as SSRIs, not persistent orofacial movements after long-term D2 blockade. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Dopamine Receptor Antagonists). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: delayed orofacial choreiform movements follow long-term D2 blockade by antipsychotics or prokinetics (First Aid Pharmacology (drug adverse effects)). COMLEX: tardive movements are more likely with long-term use in older patients (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — CCK and GIP slow gastric emptying","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Acute dystonia occurs early in therapy, whereas persistent m","B":"Akathisia is subjective restlessness, not the persistent oro","C":"Serotonin syndrome is an acute hyperthermic syndrome from se"}},{"id":"sdl37_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of action, clinical uses, and important adverse effects of medications (prokinetic and cholinomimetic agents) used to stimulate gastrointestinal motility.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman takes metoclopramide for postsurgical gastroparesis. Months later she has milky nipple discharge and missed periods. Which laboratory finding is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Low serum prolactin","B":"High serum cortisol","C":"High serum prolactin","D":"Low free thyroxine level","E":"High serum calcium"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Dopamine tonically inhibits prolactin release; D2 blockade raises prolactin and can cause galactorrhea, amenorrhea and gynecomastia. Metoclopramide's D2 blockade raises prolactin, causing galactorrhea, not lowering it. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Dopamine Receptor Antagonists). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: dopamine antagonists are a drug cause of hyperprolactinemia with galactorrhea (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: domperidone is used off-label for postpartum lactation through the same pituitary effect (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system control of motility","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Metoclopramide's D2 blockade raises prolactin, causing galac"}},{"id":"sdl37_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of action, clinical uses, and important adverse effects of medications (prokinetic and cholinomimetic agents) used to stimulate gastrointestinal motility.","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man with diabetic gastroparesis improves on oral erythromycin, which induces fasting migrating motor complexes. After several weeks the benefit fades despite the same dose. What best explains this?","choices":{"A":"Gastric acid breakdown of the drug","B":"Rapid development of tolerance","C":"Induced CYP3A4 metabolism","D":"Selection of resistant flora","E":"Worsening autonomic damage"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Erythromycin stimulates motilin receptors and initiates the migrating motor complex, which normally recurs about every 90 minutes during fasting; tolerance develops rapidly, limiting long-term use. Erythromycin inhibits rather than induces CYP3A, forming a metabolite complex that inactivates the enzyme. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Macrolides). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 37 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: macrolides inhibit CYP3A4 and cause QT prolongation as notable interactions (First Aid Antimicrobials (macrolides)). COMLEX: a single IV macrolide dose of 3 mg/kg can clear the stomach of blood before upper endoscopy (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 3 — migrating motor complex during fasting","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung","whyNot":{"C":"Erythromycin inhibits rather than induces CYP3A, forming a m"}},{"id":"sdl37_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare and contrast the pharmacologic therapies for irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) based on the predominant symptom pattern, including IBS with diarrhea, IBS with constipation, and mixed-type IBS.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman has IBS with diarrhea: frequent loose stools and urgency but mild pain. She wants an inexpensive over-the-counter medicine. Which is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Loperamide","B":"Lubiprostone","C":"Psyllium","D":"Bisacodyl","E":"Neomycin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Loperamide, a peripherally acting mu-opioid agonist, reduces stool frequency and urgency in IBS with diarrhea; it does not relieve abdominal pain. Lubiprostone is a prescription chloride channel activator for IBS-C and would worsen diarrhea. Bisacodyl is a stimulant laxative for constipation and would worsen diarrhea. Psyllium is a bulk-forming fiber, and colonic bacterial digestion of fiber can increase bloating and flatus. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Drugs Used in the Treatment of Irritable Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: antidiarrheal mu agonists have poor CNS penetration and cause constipation and nausea (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: diphenoxylate is combined with atropine to discourage overdosage (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 1 — irritable bowel syndrome diagnosis and first-line treatment","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Lubiprostone is a prescription chloride channel activator fo","D":"Bisacodyl is a stimulant laxative for constipation and would","C":"Psyllium is a bulk-forming fiber, and colonic bacterial dige"}},{"id":"sdl37_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare and contrast the pharmacologic therapies for irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) based on the predominant symptom pattern, including IBS with diarrhea, IBS with constipation, and mixed-type IBS.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man has IBS with constipation and bloating. Fiber and over-the-counter laxatives have not helped. He wants a capsule taken each morning that raises enterocyte cGMP. Which drug fits?","choices":{"A":"Lubiprostone","B":"Linaclotide","C":"Alosetron","D":"Eluxadoline","E":"Hyoscyamine"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Linaclotide (290 mcg daily) activates guanylyl cyclase C, raising cGMP, which opens CFTR and increases chloride and bicarbonate secretion; lubiprostone is approved in women. Alosetron is a 5-HT3 antagonist for IBS-D in women and would worsen constipation. Eluxadoline treats IBS-D and is constipating, so it suits diarrhea, not constipation. Hyoscyamine is an antispasmodic for acute cramping and does not raise cGMP. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Guanylate Cyclase C Agonists). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GC-C agonists such as plecanatide cause diarrhea, bloating and flatulence (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: GC-C agonists are contraindicated in children because of dehydration risk (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 3 — CFTR and intestinal chloride secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Alosetron is a 5-HT3 antagonist for IBS-D in women and would","D":"Eluxadoline treats IBS-D and is constipating, so it suits di","E":"Hyoscyamine is an antispasmodic for acute cramping and does "}},{"id":"sdl37_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare and contrast the pharmacologic therapies for irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) based on the predominant symptom pattern, including IBS with diarrhea, IBS with constipation, and mixed-type IBS.","batch":2,"stem":"A 36-year-old woman has mixed-type IBS, alternating stool form with chronic abdominal pain as her main complaint. Which nightly drug best targets the pain?","choices":{"A":"High-dose bismuth salts","B":"Daily lactulose syrup by mouth","C":"Oral metoclopramide","D":"Low-dose amitriptyline","E":"Weekly erythromycin"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Low-dose tricyclic antidepressants alter central processing of visceral afferent input and help chronic IBS pain; their anticholinergic effect also slows stools. Katzung does not separate mixed-type therapy, so this item is objectives-first. Lactulose is a nonabsorbable sugar laxative that colonic bacteria ferment into flatus and cramps, so it does not target IBS pain. Metoclopramide is a prokinetic and antiemetic, not a treatment for chronic visceral pain. Bismuth subsalicylate is used short term for dyspepsia and acute or traveler's diarrhea, not as chronic pain therapy for IBS. Erythromycin stimulates motilin receptors to treat gastroparesis, and tolerance develops rapidly; it does not relieve IBS pain. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Drugs Used in the Treatment of Irritable Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: tricyclic antidepressants cause anticholinergic, sedative and cardiac conduction effects (First Aid Psychiatry Pharmacology). COMLEX: desipramine is dosed at 10 to 50 mg per day for chronic IBS pain (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 1 — irritable bowel syndrome diagnosis and first-line treatment","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Lactulose is a nonabsorbable sugar laxative that colonic bac","C":"Metoclopramide is a prokinetic and antiemetic, not a treatme","A":"Bismuth subsalicylate is used short term for dyspepsia and a","E":"Erythromycin stimulates motilin receptors to treat gastropar"}},{"id":"sdl37_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanism of action and clinical use of teduglutide in the management of short-bowel syndrome","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man had massive small-bowel resection after a volvulus and needs home intravenous nutrition 6 nights a week. Teduglutide is started. Which outcome best shows that it is working?","choices":{"A":"Higher fasting glucose","B":"Faster gastric emptying","C":"Lower serum prolactin level","D":"Rise in serum bilirubin","E":"Reduced infusion frequency"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In trials, 54% of patients given teduglutide cut parenteral support by at least 1 day per week, versus 23% given placebo. Teduglutide delays gastric emptying, and reduced parenteral support is the measure of benefit. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Glucagon-Like Peptide 2 Analog for Short-Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: extensive resection removes absorptive surface and causes malabsorption of nutrients and fluids (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: patients with under 200 cm of small intestine usually need partial or complete parenteral support (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — pathogenesis of general malabsorption","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Teduglutide delays gastric emptying, and reduced parenteral "}},{"id":"sdl37_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanism of action and clinical use of teduglutide in the management of short-bowel syndrome","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with short-bowel syndrome is being considered for teduglutide. Which screening is most important because of a known drug risk?","choices":{"A":"Echocardiography for valves","B":"Bone density scanning","C":"Colonoscopy for neoplasia","D":"Audiometry for hearing","E":"Slit-lamp eye examination"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Teduglutide is trophic to the mucosa and may raise the risk of colorectal and small-bowel neoplasia, so colonoscopy is done before and during therapy. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Glucagon-Like Peptide 2 Analog for Short-Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: trophic stimulation of intestinal epithelium raises concern for adenomatous growth (Katzung Ch. 62). COMLEX: GLP-2 analog therapy carries a neoplasia warning for colon and small bowel (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 1 — colorectal adenomas and malignant potential","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanism of action and clinical use of teduglutide in the management of short-bowel syndrome","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with short-bowel syndrome after Crohn resections starts teduglutide. Its effect on the remaining mucosa is mediated downstream by release of which hormone?","choices":{"A":"Insulin-like growth factor","B":"Histamine from mast cells","C":"Somatostatin from D cells","D":"Substance P from neurons","E":"Gastrin from antral G cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Teduglutide binds enteric neurons and endocrine cells and stimulates release of trophic hormones, including insulin-like growth factor, that drive epithelial growth and fluid absorption. Gastrin from antral G cells stimulates parietal cell acid secretion, not the trophic response of small-bowel mucosa to teduglutide. Somatostatin, mimicked by octreotide, inhibits intestinal secretion and hormone release rather than driving mucosal growth. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Glucagon-Like Peptide 2 Analog for Short-Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: VIP from enteric neurons increases intestinal water and electrolyte secretion (First Aid Gastrointestinal Physiology (GI regulatory substances)). COMLEX: octreotide may be used for high-output diarrhea in short-bowel syndrome (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system control of motility","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Gastrin from antral G cells stimulates parietal cell acid se","C":"Somatostatin, mimicked by octreotide, inhibits intestinal se"}},{"id":"trial_sdl37_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of action, clinical uses, and important adverse effects of medications (prokinetic and cholinomimetic agents) used to stimulate gastrointestinal motility.","batch":3,"stem":"A 72-year-old man has urinary retention and slow gut transit. Why is bethanechol seldom used for GI motility today?","choices":{"A":"Rapid loss of the drug's effect","B":"Frequent tardive movement disorders","C":"Widespread cholinergic actions","D":"Serious hepatic toxicity","E":"Marked drop in white blood cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Bethanechol stimulates M3 receptors on muscle and myenteric neurons but is seldom used for GI motility because of its broad cholinergic effects and newer alternatives. Tardive dyskinesia is the black box warning for metoclopramide, a dopamine receptor antagonist. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Cholinomimetic Agents). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the -chol suffix marks a direct muscarinic agonist resistant to AChE that treats urinary retention (First Aid Autonomic Drugs (cholinomimetic agents)). COMLEX: bethanechol still sees occasional use for postoperative or neurogenic bladder retention (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system control of motility","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Tardive dyskinesia is the black box warning for metocloprami"}},{"id":"trial_sdl37_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of action, clinical uses, and important adverse effects of medications (prokinetic and cholinomimetic agents) used to stimulate gastrointestinal motility.","batch":3,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman with chronic constipation and long QT starts prucalopride. Why is it safer than older 5-HT4 agonists?","choices":{"A":"Little CYP3A4 hepatic metabolism","B":"Little hERG channel affinity","C":"Strong D2 receptor blockade","D":"Shorter plasma half-life","E":"Partial muscarinic blockade"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cisapride and tegaserod were withdrawn for cardiovascular events; prucalopride is a high-affinity 5-HT4 agonist without significant hERG K+ channel or 5-HT1B affinity. Dopamine receptor blockade is the mechanism of metoclopramide and domperidone, not of 5-HT4 agonists. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Serotonin 5-HT4-Receptor Agonists). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: 5-HT4 agonism stimulates enteric nerves to increase peristalsis and secretion (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: prucalopride dosing falls to 1 mg daily when creatinine clearance is below 30 mL/min (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system control of motility","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Dopamine receptor blockade is the mechanism of metoclopramid"}},{"id":"trial_sdl37_q03","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare and contrast the pharmacologic therapies for irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) based on the predominant symptom pattern, including IBS with diarrhea, IBS with constipation, and mixed-type IBS.","batch":3,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman with IBS with constipation bloats badly on psyllium. Which inexpensive agent is a sensible next step?","choices":{"A":"Diphenoxylate tablets","B":"Alosetron tablets","C":"Cholestyramine","D":"Polyethylene glycol","E":"Bismuth subsalicylate"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Fiber can worsen bloating in IBS with constipation; osmotic laxatives such as polyethylene glycol or milk of magnesia soften stools and are often tried next. Diphenoxylate is a mu-opioid agonist antidiarrheal that slows colonic transit and would worsen constipation. Alosetron inhibits colonic motility and extends transit, so it suits diarrhea-predominant rather than constipation-predominant IBS. Cholestyramine treats bile acid diarrhea and commonly causes constipation and bloating. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Drugs Used in the Treatment of Irritable Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: osmotic laxatives draw water into the lumen and can cause diarrhea and dehydration (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: bulk-forming fiber can increase gas and distension in IBS (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 1 — irritable bowel syndrome diagnosis and first-line treatment","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Diphenoxylate is a mu-opioid agonist antidiarrheal that slow","B":"Alosetron inhibits colonic motility and extends transit, so ","C":"Cholestyramine treats bile acid diarrhea and commonly causes"}},{"id":"trial_sdl37_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare and contrast the pharmacologic therapies for irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) based on the predominant symptom pattern, including IBS with diarrhea, IBS with constipation, and mixed-type IBS.","batch":3,"stem":"A 70-year-old man with IBS cramping takes dicyclomine. Which adverse effect is most likely at higher doses?","choices":{"A":"Slow heart rate","B":"Excess salivation","C":"Blurred near vision","D":"Loose watery stools","E":"Pinpoint pupils"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Dicyclomine and hyoscyamine are antimuscarinic antispasmodics that give short-term cramp relief; higher doses cause dry mouth, blurred near vision and constipation. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Drugs Used in the Treatment of Irritable Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: muscarinic antagonists cause dry mouth, mydriasis and cycloplegia (First Aid Autonomic Drugs (cholinomimetic agents)). COMLEX: antispasmodic benefit in IBS is short term and modest (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system control of motility","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl37_q05","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanism of action and clinical use of teduglutide in the management of short-bowel syndrome","batch":3,"stem":"A 47-year-old woman had extensive small-bowel resection. Below what residual length do such patients usually need IV nutrition?","choices":{"A":"Less than 20 cm","B":"Less than 600 cm","C":"Less than 400 cm","D":"Less than 200 cm","E":"Less than 50 cm"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Patients with less than 200 cm of small intestine, with or without colon resection, usually depend on partial or complete parenteral support. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Glucagon-Like Peptide 2 Analog for Short-Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: loss of absorptive length impairs absorption of fluids and nutrients (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: the adult small intestine is several meters long, so short-bowel syndrome reflects major loss (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — pathogenesis of general malabsorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl37_q06","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanism of action and clinical use of teduglutide in the management of short-bowel syndrome","batch":3,"stem":"A 39-year-old man with short-bowel syndrome starts teduglutide. Which additional effect of the drug reduces his stool output?","choices":{"A":"Enhanced fluid absorption","B":"Faster colonic transit","C":"Reduced hepatic bile flow","D":"Increased mucus output","E":"Greater gut colonization"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Teduglutide stimulates trophic hormone release that promotes epithelial growth and enhances intestinal fluid absorption. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Glucagon-Like Peptide 2 Analog for Short-Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: volume of fluid lost after resection reflects reduced absorptive surface (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: GLP-2 receptors on enteric neurons and endocrine cells mediate the trophic response (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — enterohepatic circulation and terminal ileal bile salt loss","isHighYield":true},{"id":"grid_sdl37_q01","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare and contrast the pharmacologic therapies for irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) based on the predominant symptom pattern, including IBS with diarrhea, IBS with constipation, and mixed-type IBS.","batch":4,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman has had 3 years of urgent loose stools with crampy lower abdominal pain relieved by defecation. Colonoscopy and celiac serologies are normal. Loperamide and dicyclomine did not control her symptoms. She is enrolled in a restricted prescribing program and begins a new drug. Six weeks later she has sudden left-sided abdominal pain and bloody diarrhea, and colonoscopy shows segmental mucosal edema and ulceration consistent with ischemic colitis. Which of the following sets of effects best characterizes the drug she was given?","grid":{"headers":["Colonic transit","Intestinal fluid secretion","Visceral pain"]},"choices":{"A":["↑","↑","↓"],"B":["↑","↑","↔"],"C":["↓","↔","↔"],"D":["↑","↔","↔"],"E":["↓","↔","↓"]},"correct":"E","explanation":"Choice E (↓ colonic transit, ↔ intestinal fluid secretion, ↓ visceral pain) is correct. Alosetron is a selective 5-HT3 receptor antagonist approved only for women with severe diarrhea-predominant IBS refractory to conventional therapy. The notes describe blockade of 5-HT3 receptors on enteric cholinergic neurons, which slows colonic transit, and on visceral sensory afferents, which reduces pain, cramps, and urgency; severe constipation and ischemic colitis carry a black box warning and require a REMS program. Choice A (↑ colonic transit, ↑ fluid secretion, ↓ visceral pain) is linaclotide, a guanylyl cyclase-C agonist that raises cGMP and activates CFTR, accelerates colonic transit, and blunts visceral nociception in IBS-C. Choice B (↑ colonic transit, ↑ fluid secretion, ↔ visceral pain) is lubiprostone, which activates ClC-2 chloride channels to drive fluid secretion in IBS-C. Choice C (↓ colonic transit, ↔ fluid secretion, ↔ visceral pain) is loperamide, a mu-opioid agonist that slows transit and increases fluid reabsorption but does not relieve visceral pain. Choice D (↑ colonic transit, ↔ fluid secretion, ↔ visceral pain) is prucalopride, a 5-HT4 agonist that triggers high-amplitude propagating contractions in chronic constipation. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 37, sections 1 and 2: prokinetic agents and IBS therapy by subtype); Katzung Ch. 62 (drugs used in IBS). The unchanged secretion for alosetron, loperamide, and prucalopride and the unchanged pain for lubiprostone and prucalopride are inferred because the notes describe no such effect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: alosetron (5-HT3 antagonist) for severe IBS-D in women; black box warning for ischemic colitis and severe constipation (Exam 3 notes, SDL 37). COMLEX: IBS-C secretagogues (linaclotide, lubiprostone) cause diarrhea; IBS-D agents (alosetron, eluxadoline, loperamide) cause constipation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 3 — CFTR-mediated Cl⁻ and water secretion","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":38,"title":"SDL 38 — Pharmacology of laxatives, antidiarrheals, and IBD","questions":[{"id":"sdl38_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the mechanisms of action of medications used to treat constipation, including stool softeners, osmotic laxatives, and stimulant laxatives, and explain their clinical applications.","batch":1,"stem":"Docusate softens stool mainly by which mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Lowering surface tension","B":"Drawing water in by osmosis","C":"Stimulating enteric neurons","D":"Forming a bulky fiber gel","E":"Opening apical ion channels"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Docusate is a stool surfactant that lets water and lipids penetrate the stool; it is often given to hospitalized patients to minimize straining. Drawing water in by osmosis is the action of osmotic laxatives such as PEG and lactulose, not docusate. Stimulating enteric neurons is the action of stimulant laxatives such as senna and bisacodyl, not docusate. Forming a bulky fiber gel describes bulk laxatives such as psyllium, not the surfactant docusate. Opening apical ion channels describes secretagogues such as lubiprostone and linaclotide, not docusate. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Stool Surfactant Agents). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: emollient laxatives are surfactants that promote water entry into stool (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: glycerin suppositories are another stool surfactant option given rectally (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Drawing water in by osmosis is the action of osmotic laxativ","C":"Stimulating enteric neurons is the action of stimulant laxat","D":"Forming a bulky fiber gel describes bulk laxatives such as p","E":"Opening apical ion channels describes secretagogues such as "}},{"id":"sdl38_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the mechanisms of action of medications used to treat constipation, including stool softeners, osmotic laxatives, and stimulant laxatives, and explain their clinical applications.","batch":1,"stem":"A woman has used an anthraquinone laxative daily for years. Which colonoscopy finding is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Pseudomembranes","B":"Cobblestone mucosa","C":"Melanosis coli","D":"Crypt abscesses","E":"Angiodysplasia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Aloe, senna and cascara are hydrolyzed in the colon; chronic use causes brown colonic pigmentation called melanosis coli, without a proven link to colorectal cancer. Pseudomembranes indicate C. difficile colitis, not chronic anthraquinone use. Cobblestone mucosa is a feature of Crohn disease, not laxative use. Crypt abscesses indicate active colitis such as ulcerative colitis, not anthraquinone pigmentation. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Anthraquinone Derivatives). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 38 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: stimulant laxatives act by enteric nerve stimulation and colonic contraction, with diarrhea as an adverse effect (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: oral anthraquinones act in 6 to 12 hours and rectal doses within 2 hours (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung","whyNot":{"A":"Pseudomembranes indicate C. difficile colitis, not chronic a","B":"Cobblestone mucosa is a feature of Crohn disease, not laxati","D":"Crypt abscesses indicate active colitis such as ulcerative c"}},{"id":"sdl38_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the mechanisms of action of medications used to treat constipation, including stool softeners, osmotic laxatives, and stimulant laxatives, and explain their clinical applications.","batch":1,"stem":"Long-term use of mineral oil as a lubricant laxative can cause which nutritional problem?","choices":{"A":"Copper deficiency anemia","B":"Iron overload in the liver","C":"Zinc deficiency rash","D":"Fat-soluble vitamin loss","E":"Excess vitamin C levels"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Mineral oil coats stool and retards water absorption; long-term use impairs absorption of vitamins A, D, E and K, and aspiration can cause lipid pneumonitis. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Stool Surfactant Agents). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: absorption of fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E and K depends on bile emulsification, and mineral oil intake can cause their deficiency (First Aid Biochemistry, fat-soluble vitamins). COMLEX: mineral oil is used for fecal impaction in young children and debilitated adults (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — intestinal absorption of fat-soluble vitamins and calcium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and adverse effects of major classes of antidiarrheal medications, including opioid agonists, bile acid–binding resins, and octreotide, and identify clinical scenarios in which each is indicated.","batch":1,"stem":"Why do diphenoxylate tablets contain a small amount of atropine?","choices":{"A":"Enhancing pain relief","B":"Preventing nausea","C":"Discouraging abuse","D":"Delaying tolerance","E":"Speeding absorption"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Diphenoxylate is a prescription opioid agonist with CNS effects and dependence at higher doses; 0.025 mg atropine is added to discourage overdosage and may add to the antidiarrheal action. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Opioid Agonists). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diphenoxylate is a mu agonist that slows gut motility with constipation and nausea as adverse effects (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: opioids slow colonic transit by inhibiting presynaptic cholinergic nerves in the enteric plexuses (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — secretory diarrhea and cAMP-mediated electrolyte loss","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and adverse effects of major classes of antidiarrheal medications, including opioid agonists, bile acid–binding resins, and octreotide, and identify clinical scenarios in which each is indicated.","batch":1,"stem":"Long-term somatostatin-analog therapy for acromegaly most commonly causes which problem?","choices":{"A":"Kidney calcium oxalate stones","B":"Severe symptomatic hypercalcemia","C":"Sustained sinus tachycardia","D":"Hyperthyroid storm crisis","E":"Gallbladder sludge or stones"},"correct":"E","explanation":"By inhibiting gallbladder contractility and altering fat absorption, long-term octreotide causes sludge or gallstones in over 50% of patients; it can also cause steatorrhea, hyperglycemia, hypothyroidism and bradycardia. Octreotide causes bradycardia, not tachycardia. Octreotide is associated with hypothyroidism, not thyroid storm. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Octreotide). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 38 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: somatostatin analogs raise cholelithiasis risk through CCK inhibition (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: a monthly intramuscular depot form of the somatostatin analog is available (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — secretory diarrhea and cAMP-mediated electrolyte loss","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung","whyNot":{"C":"Octreotide causes bradycardia, not tachycardia.","D":"Octreotide is associated with hypothyroidism, not thyroid st"}},{"id":"sdl38_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and adverse effects of major classes of antidiarrheal medications, including opioid agonists, bile acid–binding resins, and octreotide, and identify clinical scenarios in which each is indicated.","batch":1,"stem":"Colestipol for chronic diarrhea should be taken at least 2 hours apart from other medicines. Why?","choices":{"A":"It induces hepatic CYP3A4","B":"It binds many oral drugs","C":"It raises gastric pH","D":"It blocks P-glycoprotein","E":"It speeds gastric emptying"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cholestyramine and colestipol bind many drugs and reduce their absorption, so they are separated from other drugs by 2 hours; colesevelam has little effect on drug absorption. Colestipol is a resin that is not absorbed, so it does not induce hepatic CYP3A4. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Bile Salt-Binding Resins). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bile acid resins cause GI upset and decreased absorption of other drugs (First Aid Cardiovascular Pharmacology, lipid-lowering agents). COMLEX: resin adverse effects include bloating, flatulence, constipation and fecal impaction (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — secretory diarrhea and cAMP-mediated electrolyte loss","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Colestipol is a resin that is not absorbed, so it does not i"}},{"id":"sdl38_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the major pharmacologic therapies used to treat ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease, including aminosalicylates, glucocorticoids, purine analogs, and anti-tumor necrosis factor therapy with emphasis on their mechanisms of action, therapeutic roles, and key adverse effects.","batch":1,"stem":"Sulfasalazine releases active 5-aminosalicylic acid in the colon through which process?","choices":{"A":"Bacterial azoreductase cleavage","B":"Hepatic CYP450 oxidation of it","C":"Gastric acid hydrolysis of the bond","D":"Pancreatic esterase cleavage","E":"pH-dependent resin release"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Sulfasalazine links 5-ASA to sulfapyridine by an azo bond; colonic bacteria cleave it with azoreductase, releasing 5-ASA in the terminal ileum and colon. pH-dependent release describes delayed-release mesalamine, whereas sulfasalazine is an azo compound cleaved by colonic bacteria. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Aminosalicylates). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 38 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: sulfasalazine combines antibacterial sulfapyridine with anti-inflammatory 5-ASA and is activated by colonic bacteria (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: mesalamine products instead use timed-release granules or pH-sensitive coatings (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung","whyNot":{"E":"pH-dependent release describes delayed-release mesalamine, w"}},{"id":"sdl38_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the major pharmacologic therapies used to treat ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease, including aminosalicylates, glucocorticoids, purine analogs, and anti-tumor necrosis factor therapy with emphasis on their mechanisms of action, therapeutic roles, and key adverse effects.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient starting sulfasalazine for ulcerative colitis should take which daily supplement?","choices":{"A":"Vitamin B6","B":"Vitamin D3","C":"Iron sulfate","D":"Folic acid","E":"Zinc salts"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Sulfasalazine impairs folate absorption and processing, so 1 mg/d folic acid is recommended. Pyridoxine (vitamin B6) is given with isoniazid to prevent its neuropathy, not with sulfasalazine. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Aminosalicylates). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 38 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: sulfasalazine adverse effects include malaise, nausea, sulfonamide toxicity and reversible oligospermia (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: slow acetylators of sulfapyridine have more frequent and severe adverse effects (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung","whyNot":{"A":"Pyridoxine (vitamin B6) is given with isoniazid to prevent i"}},{"id":"sdl38_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the major pharmacologic therapies used to treat ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease, including aminosalicylates, glucocorticoids, purine analogs, and anti-tumor necrosis factor therapy with emphasis on their mechanisms of action, therapeutic roles, and key adverse effects.","batch":1,"stem":"Before starting azathioprine for Crohn disease, which test helps predict severe marrow toxicity?","choices":{"A":"G6PD enzyme activity","B":"TPMT enzyme activity","C":"HLA-B*5701 typing","D":"Serum ferritin level","E":"CYP2C19 genotype"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Thiopurine methyltransferase catabolizes 6-MP; activity is low in 11% and absent in 0.3% of people, raising active thioguanine nucleotides and marrow suppression risk. G6PD activity predicts oxidant hemolysis of red cells, not thiopurine marrow toxicity. HLA-B*5701 screening is done before abacavir to prevent hypersensitivity, not before azathioprine. CYP2C19 genotype predicts poor activation of clopidogrel, not thiopurine toxicity. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Purine Analogs). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 38 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: azathioprine is an antimetabolite precursor of 6-mercaptopurine that causes pancytopenia (First Aid Immunology (immunosuppressants and therapeutic antibodies)). COMLEX: complete blood counts and liver tests are monitored in every patient on purine analogs (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung","whyNot":{"A":"G6PD activity predicts oxidant hemolysis of red cells, not t","C":"HLA-B*5701 screening is done before abacavir to prevent hype","E":"CYP2C19 genotype predicts poor activation of clopidogrel, no"}},{"id":"sdl38_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the mechanisms of action of medications used to treat constipation, including stool softeners, osmotic laxatives, and stimulant laxatives, and explain their clinical applications.","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old man with stage 4 chronic kidney disease has taken milk of magnesia nightly for months. He now has weakness, absent reflexes and bradycardia. Which disturbance is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Hypercalcemia","B":"Hypokalemia","C":"Hypermagnesemia","D":"Hyperphosphatemia","E":"Hypophosphatemia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Magnesium hydroxide should not be used for long periods in renal insufficiency because of the risk of hypermagnesemia. Hypokalemia is listed among the electrolyte risks of sodium phosphate preparations, whereas magnesium hydroxide retained in renal insufficiency causes hypermagnesemia. Hyperphosphatemia is a risk of sodium phosphate purgatives, not of magnesium hydroxide. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Osmotic Laxatives). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 38 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: magnesium-containing laxatives provide an osmotic load and can cause diarrhea and dehydration (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: the colon neither concentrates nor dilutes fecal fluid, so nonabsorbed solute obligates stool water (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung","whyNot":{"B":"Hypokalemia is listed among the electrolyte risks of sodium ","D":"Hyperphosphatemia is a risk of sodium phosphate purgatives, "}},{"id":"sdl38_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the mechanisms of action of medications used to treat constipation, including stool softeners, osmotic laxatives, and stimulant laxatives, and explain their clinical applications.","batch":2,"stem":"A frail 79-year-old woman with mild renal insufficiency takes sodium phosphate tablets for colonoscopy preparation. A week later her creatinine has doubled. Biopsy would most likely show which change?","choices":{"A":"Urate nephropathy","B":"Nephrocalcinosis","C":"Oxalate nephropathy","D":"Myoglobin casts","E":"Light-chain cast nephropathy"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Sodium phosphate can cause hyperphosphatemia and hypocalcemia, and acute renal failure from tubular calcium phosphate deposition (nephrocalcinosis); it is avoided in frail, elderly or renal patients. Urate nephropathy is uric acid crystal obstruction of collecting ducts, typical of treated leukemia or lymphoma, not phosphate preparations. Oxalate crystals in the tubules are typical of ethylene glycol poisoning, not sodium phosphate exposure. Light-chain cast nephropathy results from Bence-Jones proteins in multiple myeloma, not bowel preparation. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Osmotic Laxatives). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 38 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: osmotic agents draw water into the lumen and can be misused by patients with bulimia (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: balanced isotonic lavage solutions avoid intravascular fluid and electrolyte shifts during preparation (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung","whyNot":{"A":"Urate nephropathy is uric acid crystal obstruction of collec","C":"Oxalate crystals in the tubules are typical of ethylene glyc","E":"Light-chain cast nephropathy results from Bence-Jones protei"}},{"id":"sdl38_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the mechanisms of action of medications used to treat constipation, including stool softeners, osmotic laxatives, and stimulant laxatives, and explain their clinical applications.","batch":2,"stem":"An 83-year-old bed-bound nursing home resident has had no bowel movement for 4 days. Staff want a stimulant laxative that works within an hour. Which is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Oral senna tablets","B":"Oral psyllium powder","C":"Oral docusate capsule","D":"Bisacodyl suppository","E":"Oral lactulose syrup"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Bisacodyl, a diphenylmethane stimulant, works in 30 to 60 minutes rectally versus 6 to 10 hours orally, with minimal systemic absorption. Oral senna is a stimulant but acts over hours, not within an hour like rectal bisacodyl. Psyllium is a bulk-forming fiber, not a stimulant, and acts slowly. Docusate is a stool softener used to prevent straining, not a fast-acting stimulant. Lactulose is an osmotic laxative, not a stimulant, and does not act within an hour. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Diphenylmethane Derivatives). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: stimulant laxatives cause colonic contraction through enteric nerve stimulation (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: long-term stimulant use is probably safe and may be needed in neurologically impaired patients (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 5 — defecation reflex and anal sphincter control","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Oral senna is a stimulant but acts over hours, not within an","B":"Psyllium is a bulk-forming fiber, not a stimulant, and acts ","C":"Docusate is a stool softener used to prevent straining, not ","E":"Lactulose is an osmotic laxative, not a stimulant, and does "}},{"id":"sdl38_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and adverse effects of major classes of antidiarrheal medications, including opioid agonists, bile acid–binding resins, and octreotide, and identify clinical scenarios in which each is indicated.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old man had 90 cm of terminal ileum resected for Crohn disease. He now has watery postprandial diarrhea without steatorrhea. Which drug best treats it?","choices":{"A":"Cholestyramine","B":"Pancrelipase","C":"Ondansetron","D":"Diphenoxylate","E":"Metronidazole tablets"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Ileal disease or resection causes malabsorbed bile salts that trigger colonic secretory diarrhea; bile salt-binding resins reduce it, though they can worsen fat malabsorption if the bile pool is depleted. Diphenoxylate slows transit nonspecifically but does not bind the malabsorbed bile acids driving this secretory diarrhea. Pancrelipase replaces enzymes for exocrine pancreatic insufficiency with steatorrhea, not bile acid diarrhea after ileal resection. Ondansetron is a 5-HT3 antagonist antiemetic for chemotherapy and postoperative emesis and does not bind bile acids. Metronidazole targets anaerobic bacteria and sensitive protozoa, which do not cause bile acid diarrhea after ileal resection. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Bile Salt-Binding Resins). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 38 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: bile acid resins prevent intestinal reabsorption of bile acids, prompting the liver to use cholesterol to make more (First Aid Cardiovascular Pharmacology, lipid-lowering agents). COMLEX: resin powders are taken one to three times daily before meals (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — enterohepatic circulation and terminal ileal bile salt loss","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung","whyNot":{"D":"Diphenoxylate slows transit nonspecifically but does not bin","B":"Pancrelipase replaces enzymes for exocrine pancreatic insuff","C":"Ondansetron is a 5-HT3 antagonist antiemetic for chemotherap","E":"Metronidazole targets anaerobic bacteria and sensitive proto"}},{"id":"sdl38_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and adverse effects of major classes of antidiarrheal medications, including opioid agonists, bile acid–binding resins, and octreotide, and identify clinical scenarios in which each is indicated.","batch":2,"stem":"A 57-year-old woman with metastatic carcinoid tumor has flushing, wheezing and voluminous watery diarrhea. Her liver lesions cannot be removed surgically. Which drug best controls her symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Diphenoxylate","B":"Octreotide","C":"Bismuth","D":"Lubiprostone","E":"Prednisone"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Octreotide inhibits hormone secretion by carcinoid and VIPoma tumors, reducing secretory diarrhea and flushing, and may slow tumor progression. Diphenoxylate slows transit but does not inhibit tumor hormone secretion or control carcinoid flushing. Lubiprostone stimulates intestinal chloride secretion and would worsen diarrhea. Bismuth subsalicylate reduces stool frequency in acute infectious diarrhea and does not inhibit carcinoid hormone secretion. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Octreotide). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: carcinoid syndrome is diagnosed by urinary 5-HIAA and treated with a somatostatin analog (First Aid Endocrine, carcinoid syndrome). COMLEX: higher subcutaneous doses of 100 to 250 mcg inhibit motility in dumping or vagotomy diarrhea (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — secretory diarrhea and cAMP-mediated electrolyte loss","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Diphenoxylate slows transit but does not inhibit tumor hormo","D":"Lubiprostone stimulates intestinal chloride secretion and wo","C":"Bismuth subsalicylate reduces stool frequency in acute infec"}},{"id":"sdl38_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and adverse effects of major classes of antidiarrheal medications, including opioid agonists, bile acid–binding resins, and octreotide, and identify clinical scenarios in which each is indicated.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old man has bloody diarrhea, a temperature of 39.4°C and abdominal tenderness after eating undercooked chicken. Why should loperamide be avoided?","choices":{"A":"Risk of central opioid dependence","B":"Risk of respiratory depression in adults","C":"Risk of severe serotonin syndrome","D":"Risk of worsened underlying illness","E":"Risk of tardive movement disorder"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Antidiarrheals are safe for mild to moderate acute diarrhea but should not be used with bloody diarrhea, high fever or systemic toxicity because they may worsen the underlying condition. Loperamide does not cross the blood-brain barrier, so central dependence is not the concern. Loperamide does not cross the blood-brain barrier, so respiratory depression is not the concern. Serotonin syndrome arises from serotonergic interactions such as SSRIs with MAOIs, not from loperamide. Tardive dyskinesia follows prolonged central dopamine blockade by drugs such as metoclopramide, not opioid antidiarrheals like loperamide. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Antidiarrheal Agents). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: peripheral mu agonists have poor CNS penetration and low addictive potential (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: antidiarrheals are stopped when diarrhea worsens despite therapy (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 → Obj 1 — bacterial enterocolitis, including Yersinia mimicking appendicitis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Loperamide does not cross the blood-brain barrier, so centra","B":"Loperamide does not cross the blood-brain barrier, so respir","C":"Serotonin syndrome arises from serotonergic interactions suc","E":"Tardive dyskinesia follows prolonged central dopamine blocka"}},{"id":"sdl38_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the major pharmacologic therapies used to treat ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease, including aminosalicylates, glucocorticoids, purine analogs, and anti-tumor necrosis factor therapy with emphasis on their mechanisms of action, therapeutic roles, and key adverse effects.","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman with Crohn disease is maintained on azathioprine. She is started on allopurinol for gout and 3 weeks later has a white cell count of 1,100/µL (reference range: 4,500–11,000/µL). What explains this?","choices":{"A":"Reduced renal drug excretion","B":"Increased drug absorption","C":"Reduced 6-MP breakdown","D":"Allopurinol marrow allergy","E":"Folate antagonism"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Allopurinol blocks xanthine oxidase catabolism of the purine analogs, increasing active 6-thioguanine nucleotides and causing severe leukopenia. Folate antagonism is a sulfasalazine concern, whereas allopurinol blocks xanthine oxidase breakdown of azathioprine. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Purine Analogs). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: 6-MP is degraded by xanthine oxidase, so its toxicity rises with allopurinol (First Aid Immunology (immunosuppressants and therapeutic antibodies)). COMLEX: febuxostat raises toxicity the same way, and purine-analog benefit in IBD has a median delay of 17 weeks because thioguanine nucleotides accumulate slowly (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Folate antagonism is a sulfasalazine concern, whereas allopu"}},{"id":"sdl38_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the major pharmacologic therapies used to treat ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease, including aminosalicylates, glucocorticoids, purine analogs, and anti-tumor necrosis factor therapy with emphasis on their mechanisms of action, therapeutic roles, and key adverse effects.","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old man with fistulizing Crohn disease who emigrated from the Philippines will begin infliximab. Which test must be done first?","choices":{"A":"Latent tuberculosis test","B":"Serum ceruloplasmin level","C":"Hepatitis A antibody titer","D":"Stool culture panel","E":"Repeat colonic biopsy"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Anti-TNF therapy suppresses the TH1 response; all patients need a tuberculin skin test or interferon gamma release assay first, and positive patients get prophylaxis before therapy. Serum ceruloplasmin is low in Wilson disease from ATP7B mutation and is not a screen required before anti-TNF therapy. Anti-TNF therapy risks reactivation of hepatitis B, not hepatitis A, alongside the mandatory tuberculosis screen. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Anti-Tumor Necrosis Factor Therapy). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: TNF is important in granuloma formation and stabilization, and anti-TNF drugs can cause drug-induced lupus (First Aid Immunology (immunosuppressants and therapeutic antibodies)). COMLEX: serious infection risk rises markedly with concurrent corticosteroids (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Serum ceruloplasmin is low in Wilson disease from ATP7B muta","C":"Anti-TNF therapy risks reactivation of hepatitis B, not hepa"}},{"id":"sdl38_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the major pharmacologic therapies used to treat ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease, including aminosalicylates, glucocorticoids, purine analogs, and anti-tumor necrosis factor therapy with emphasis on their mechanisms of action, therapeutic roles, and key adverse effects.","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old woman has mild to moderate Crohn disease of the ileum and ascending colon. She wants a glucocorticoid with fewer systemic adverse effects. Which is best?","choices":{"A":"Oral prednisone at high dose","B":"Hydrocortisone retention enema","C":"IV methylprednisolone","D":"Oral dexamethasone daily","E":"Controlled-release budesonide"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Budesonide has high glucocorticoid receptor affinity but extensive CYP3A4 first-pass metabolism; the distal ileum and colon release form treats mild to moderate ileocolonic Crohn disease with less systemic toxicity. High-dose oral prednisone is effective but causes the systemic adverse effects she wants to avoid. Hydrocortisone enemas treat distal colonic flares and cannot reach the ileum and ascending colon. IV methylprednisolone is systemic therapy for severe disease, not a low-systemic option for mild disease. Dexamethasone is a potent systemic glucocorticoid with about 30 times the anti-inflammatory potency of hydrocortisone, so it does not limit systemic effects. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Glucocorticoids). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: glucocorticoids inactivate NF-kB and inhibit synthesis of almost all cytokines (First Aid Immunology (immunosuppressants and therapeutic antibodies)). COMLEX: potent CYP3A4 inhibitors raise plasma levels of the controlled-release steroid severalfold (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"High-dose oral prednisone is effective but causes the system","B":"Hydrocortisone enemas treat distal colonic flares and cannot","C":"IV methylprednisolone is systemic therapy for severe disease","D":"Dexamethasone is a potent systemic glucocorticoid with about"}},{"id":"trial_sdl38_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the mechanisms of action of medications used to treat constipation, including stool softeners, osmotic laxatives, and stimulant laxatives, and explain their clinical applications.","batch":3,"stem":"A 62-year-old woman on lactulose for constipation has cramps and flatus. What causes these effects?","choices":{"A":"Colonic bacterial fermentation","B":"Small-bowel enzyme deficiency","C":"Direct irritation of the mucosa","D":"Enteric nerve stimulation","E":"Gastric acid hypersecretion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Sorbitol and lactulose are nonabsorbable sugars metabolized by colonic bacteria, producing flatus and cramps; PEG does not. Direct enteric nervous system stimulation is the mechanism of stimulant laxatives such as senna and bisacodyl, not lactulose. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Osmotic Laxatives). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 38 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: lactulose also treats hepatic encephalopathy by trapping NH4+ in the colon (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: high-dose osmotic purgatives evacuate the bowel within 1 to 3 hours (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung","whyNot":{"D":"Direct enteric nervous system stimulation is the mechanism o"}},{"id":"trial_sdl38_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the mechanisms of action of medications used to treat constipation, including stool softeners, osmotic laxatives, and stimulant laxatives, and explain their clinical applications.","batch":3,"stem":"A 45-year-old man starts psyllium for mild constipation. How does it act?","choices":{"A":"Surfactant softening of stool","B":"Direct enteric nerve activation","C":"Mu opioid receptor blockade","D":"Water-absorbing bulky gel","E":"Apical chloride channel opening"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Bulk-forming laxatives are indigestible hydrophilic colloids that absorb water to form a bulky gel, distending the colon and promoting peristalsis. Surfactant softening is the action of docusate and glycerin, not bulk-forming fiber. Direct enteric nerve stimulation is how stimulant laxatives such as senna and bisacodyl work. Mu-opioid receptor blockade in the gut is the mechanism of peripheral opioid antagonists such as methylnaltrexone and naloxegol. Opening of the type 2 chloride channel (ClC-2) is the mechanism of lubiprostone. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Bulk-Forming Laxatives). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 38 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: soluble fibers draw water into the lumen and form a viscous liquid, with bloating as an adverse effect (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: polycarbophil is a synthetic bulk-forming fiber alongside psyllium and methylcellulose (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 5 — defecation reflex and anal sphincter control","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung","whyNot":{"A":"Surfactant softening is the action of docusate and glycerin,","B":"Direct enteric nerve stimulation is how stimulant laxatives ","C":"Mu-opioid receptor blockade in the gut is the mechanism of p","E":"Opening of the type 2 chloride channel (ClC-2) is the mechan"}},{"id":"trial_sdl38_q03","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and adverse effects of major classes of antidiarrheal medications, including opioid agonists, bile acid–binding resins, and octreotide, and identify clinical scenarios in which each is indicated.","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman on several heart medicines needs a bile acid resin for diarrhea. Which choice has the fewest drug-binding interactions?","choices":{"A":"Colestipol","B":"Sucralfate","C":"Kaolin-pectin","D":"Activated charcoal","E":"Colesevelam"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Colesevelam does not appear to affect absorption of other drugs significantly, unlike older resins that must be separated from other medicines. Colestipol, like cholestyramine, binds coadministered oral medications, which must be given 2 hours apart. Sucralfate is a sucrose–aluminum hydroxide salt that forms a protective paste over ulcers and is not a bile acid resin. Kaolin-pectin is an OTC adsorbent antidiarrheal rather than a bile acid–binding resin. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Bile Salt-Binding Resins). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bile acid resins lower LDL by interrupting enterohepatic bile acid recycling (First Aid Cardiovascular Pharmacology, lipid-lowering agents). COMLEX: removing more bile acids from a small pool can worsen fat malabsorption (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — enterohepatic circulation and terminal ileal bile salt loss","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Colestipol, like cholestyramine, binds coadministered oral m","B":"Sucralfate is a sucrose–aluminum hydroxide salt that forms a","C":"Kaolin-pectin is an OTC adsorbent antidiarrheal rather than "}},{"id":"trial_sdl38_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and adverse effects of major classes of antidiarrheal medications, including opioid agonists, bile acid–binding resins, and octreotide, and identify clinical scenarios in which each is indicated.","batch":3,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman has scleroderma with intestinal pseudo-obstruction. A somatostatin analog is given at 50 mcg SC. What is its intended effect?","choices":{"A":"Decreased gastric acid output","B":"Increased pancreatic secretion","C":"Increased small-bowel motility","D":"Decreased portal venous flow","E":"Increased hepatic bile flow"},"correct":"C","explanation":"At low doses (50 mcg SC) octreotide stimulates small-bowel motility, used in bacterial overgrowth or scleroderma pseudo-obstruction; higher doses inhibit motility. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Octreotide). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 38 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: somatostatin analogs are used for acute variceal bleeds and VIPoma (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: subcutaneous dosing gives a 6 to 12 hour duration of action (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — secretory diarrhea and cAMP-mediated electrolyte loss","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung"},{"id":"trial_sdl38_q05","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the major pharmacologic therapies used to treat ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease, including aminosalicylates, glucocorticoids, purine analogs, and anti-tumor necrosis factor therapy with emphasis on their mechanisms of action, therapeutic roles, and key adverse effects.","batch":3,"stem":"A 38-year-old man's ulcerative colitis flare resolves on prednisone. Why should it not be continued long term?","choices":{"A":"Ineffective for active colitis","B":"Ineffective for maintenance","C":"Rapid loss of oral absorption","D":"Frequent drug-induced lupus","E":"Blocks folate absorption"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Corticosteroids are not useful for maintaining remission; aminosalicylates or immunosuppressants are used instead, and steroids are tapered after a response. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Glucocorticoids). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: glucocorticoids inhibit NF-kB and cause Cushing syndrome and osteoporosis with long-term use (First Aid Immunology (immunosuppressants and therapeutic antibodies)). COMLEX: prednisone induces remission in moderate to severe IBD; oral prednisone is started at 40 to 60 mg/d, and higher doses add toxicity (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl38_q06","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the major pharmacologic therapies used to treat ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease, including aminosalicylates, glucocorticoids, purine analogs, and anti-tumor necrosis factor therapy with emphasis on their mechanisms of action, therapeutic roles, and key adverse effects.","batch":3,"stem":"A 27-year-old woman given episodic infliximab for ulcerative colitis loses response and has infusion reactions. What is the likely cause?","choices":{"A":"Rapid hepatic drug clearance","B":"TNF receptor gene mutation","C":"Proteolysis in the gut lumen","D":"Antibodies against the drug","E":"Tolerance at receptor level"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Antibodies to the antibody can blunt response and raise infusion reaction risk; they are more likely with episodic than scheduled dosing and less likely with concurrent immunomodulators. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Anti-Tumor Necrosis Factor Therapy). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: infliximab is a chimeric anti-TNF-alpha monoclonal antibody (First Aid Immunology (immunosuppressants and therapeutic antibodies)). COMLEX: infusion reactions are more common with second or later infusions (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true},{"id":"grid_sdl38_q01","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the major pharmacologic therapies used to treat ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease, including aminosalicylates, glucocorticoids, purine analogs, and anti-tumor necrosis factor therapy with emphasis on their mechanisms of action, therapeutic roles, and key adverse effects.","batch":4,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman has moderate-to-severe ileocolonic Crohn disease. She has needed 3 courses of prednisone in the past year and has a flare each time the dose is tapered. Her gastroenterologist starts an oral steroid-sparing drug and explains that full benefit may take several months, and that an injectable biologic might later be combined with it partly to prevent antibodies against the biologic. Which of the following sets of features best characterizes the oral drug started?","grid":{"headers":["Disease activity treated","Maintains remission","Marrow suppression"]},"choices":{"A":["Mild–moderate","Yes","Yes"],"B":["Moderate–severe","No","No"],"C":["Moderate–severe","Yes","No"],"D":["Moderate–severe","Yes","Yes"],"E":["Mild–moderate","No","No"]},"correct":"D","explanation":"Choice D (moderate–severe disease, maintains remission, marrow suppression yes) is correct. Azathioprine and 6-mercaptopurine are prodrugs converted to 6-thioguanine nucleotides that inhibit purine synthesis and promote T-cell apoptosis. The notes describe their use to induce and maintain remission in moderate-to-severe UC and Crohn disease, allowing steroids to be reduced, with onset delayed by up to 17 weeks and frequent combination with anti-TNF biologics to suppress anti-drug antibodies. Bone marrow suppression (leukopenia) is the key toxicity and becomes life-threatening with allopurinol. Choice A (mild–moderate disease, maintains remission, myelosuppression yes) is sulfasalazine, first-line for inducing and maintaining remission in mild-to-moderate UC, whose sulfonamide component causes myelosuppression. Choice B (moderate–severe disease, no maintenance, myelosuppression no) is systemic prednisone, which induces rapid remission but does not maintain it and causes adrenal suppression, osteoporosis, and hyperglycemia. Choice C (moderate–severe disease, maintains remission, myelosuppression no) is an anti-TNF antibody such as infliximab, given by infusion or injection for refractory disease; its risks are infusion reactions and latent tuberculosis reactivation. Choice E (mild–moderate disease, no maintenance, myelosuppression no) is controlled-release oral budesonide, used for mild-to-moderate ileal and ascending colon Crohn disease with fewer systemic effects, but it is a glucocorticoid and does not maintain remission. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 38, section 3: aminosalicylates, glucocorticoids, purine analogs, anti-TNF therapy); Katzung Ch. 62 (drugs used in IBD). The absence of myelosuppression for glucocorticoids and anti-TNF agents is inferred because the notes do not list it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: azathioprine/6-MP → 6-thioguanine nucleotides; steroid-sparing maintenance in IBD; leukopenia, hepatotoxicity, pancreatitis; severe leukopenia with allopurinol (xanthine oxidase) (Exam 3 notes, SDL 38). COMLEX: glucocorticoids induce but never maintain IBD remission.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 32 Obj 3 — Crohn disease versus ulcerative colitis","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":39,"title":"SDL 39 — Pharmacology - chemo for colorectal cancer (CRC)","questions":[{"id":"sdl39_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, mechanisms of action, metabolism, clinical applications and adverse effects for each of the following classes of chemotherapy: fluoropyrimidines, platinum analogs, epidophyllotoxins and growth factor receptor inhibitors.","batch":1,"stem":"The 5-FU metabolite FdUMP exerts its main cytotoxic effect by binding which enzyme?","choices":{"A":"Dihydrofolate reductase","B":"Ribonucleotide reductase","C":"DNA polymerase alpha","D":"Thymidylate synthase","E":"Thymidine kinase enzyme"},"correct":"D","explanation":"FdUMP forms a covalent ternary complex with thymidylate synthase and 5,10-methylenetetrahydrofolate, blocking de novo thymidylate synthesis (\"thymineless death\"); FUTP and FdUTP are also incorporated into RNA and DNA, and the drug acts in S phase. Dihydrofolate reductase is inhibited by methotrexate; FdUMP binds thymidylate synthase. Ribonucleotide reductase is the target of hydroxyurea, not FdUMP. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Fluoropyrimidines). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: 5-FU is a pyrimidine analog bioactivated to 5-FdUMP, lowering dTMP and DNA synthesis, and its effects are enhanced by adding leucovorin (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: 5-FU is given IV because it is broken down rapidly in the GI tract, and its plasma half-life is about 10 to 15 minutes (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Dihydrofolate reductase is inhibited by methotrexate; FdUMP ","B":"Ribonucleotide reductase is the target of hydroxyurea, not F"}},{"id":"sdl39_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, mechanisms of action, metabolism, clinical applications and adverse effects for each of the following classes of chemotherapy: fluoropyrimidines, platinum analogs, epidophyllotoxins and growth factor receptor inhibitors.","batch":1,"stem":"A 58-year-old man receiving FOLFOX for stage III colon cancer notices tingling in his fingers and throat when he holds a chilled drink during the infusion. Which toxicity is this?","choices":{"A":"Acute sensory neurotoxicity","B":"Cumulative motor axonopathy","C":"Cryoglobulin vasculitis flare","D":"Hypocalcemic tetany episode","E":"Cerebellar ataxia toxicity"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The platinum agent in FOLFOX causes dose-limiting peripheral sensory neuropathy in two forms: an acute form triggered and worsened by cold, and a chronic cumulative dose-dependent form that is more readily reversible than cisplatin neuropathy. The chronic oxaliplatin neuropathy is cumulative and sensory, whereas cold-triggered symptoms during infusion are the acute form. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Platinum Analogs). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: platinum compounds cross-link DNA, are cell cycle nonspecific, and cause peripheral neuropathy, nephrotoxicity, and ototoxicity (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: FOLFOX is the most widely used first-line regimen for metastatic colorectal cancer (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The chronic oxaliplatin neuropathy is cumulative and sensory"}},{"id":"sdl39_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, mechanisms of action, metabolism, clinical applications and adverse effects for each of the following classes of chemotherapy: fluoropyrimidines, platinum analogs, epidophyllotoxins and growth factor receptor inhibitors.","batch":1,"stem":"Before cetuximab is added for metastatic colorectal cancer, the tumor must show which molecular feature?","choices":{"A":"HER2 gene amplification","B":"Wild-type KRAS and NRAS","C":"High EGFR protein expression","D":"BRAF V600E mutation","E":"Microsatellite instability"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cetuximab is a chimeric IgG1 antibody against the extracellular domain of EGFR; its efficacy is restricted to tumors with wild-type RAS (KRAS and NRAS), because a mutant RAS signals downstream of the blocked receptor. HER2 amplification selects patients for trastuzumab, not cetuximab. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Epidermal Growth Factor Receptor Inhibitors). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: anti-EGFR antibodies cause rash, elevated LFTs, and diarrhea (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: because cetuximab is IgG1, part of its antitumor effect may be immune mediated (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"HER2 amplification selects patients for trastuzumab, not cet"}},{"id":"sdl39_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the various medication names within each class and appreciate the important differences with each medication within these classes.","batch":1,"stem":"Which orally available prodrug is converted to 5-FU by thymidine phosphorylase within tumor tissue?","choices":{"A":"Gemcitabine","B":"Cytarabine","C":"Methotrexate","D":"Hydroxyurea","E":"Capecitabine"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Capecitabine has 70% to 80% oral bioavailability; liver carboxylesterase and cytidine deaminase form 5'-deoxy-5-fluorouridine, which thymidine phosphorylase (high in colorectal tumors) converts to 5-FU. It is paired with oxaliplatin as CAPOX. Methotrexate is an antifolate that inhibits dihydrofolate reductase and is not converted to 5-FU. Hydroxyurea inhibits ribonucleotide reductase and is not a fluoropyrimidine prodrug. Gemcitabine is a deoxycytidine analog activated by deoxycytidine kinase, not a prodrug converted to 5-FU. Cytarabine is a cytidine analog converted to ara-CTP that inhibits DNA polymerase, not a 5-FU prodrug. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Capecitabine). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: capecitabine is the prodrug of 5-FU listed with the pyrimidine antimetabolites (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: CAPOX is used first line in metastatic disease and as adjuvant therapy for stage III colon cancer (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Methotrexate is an antifolate that inhibits dihydrofolate re","D":"Hydroxyurea inhibits ribonucleotide reductase and is not a f","A":"Gemcitabine is a deoxycytidine analog activated by deoxycyti","B":"Cytarabine is a cytidine analog converted to ara-CTP that in"}},{"id":"sdl39_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the various medication names within each class and appreciate the important differences with each medication within these classes.","batch":1,"stem":"Which DNA cross-linking agent has myelosuppression, with much less renal and GI toxicity than cisplatin, as its main dose-limiting effect?","choices":{"A":"Cisplatin","B":"Ifosfamide","C":"Carboplatin","D":"Cyclophosphamide","E":"Busulfan"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Carboplatin shares the mechanism, resistance, and spectrum of cisplatin, but its dose-limiting toxicity is myelosuppression with significantly less renal and GI toxicity, so it is easier to give and has replaced cisplatin in many regimens. Cisplatin's dose-limiting toxicities are nephrotoxicity and emesis, with minimal myelosuppression. Cyclophosphamide is an alkylating agent whose distinctive toxicities are alopecia and hemorrhagic cystitis, preventable with hydration. Busulfan, used for CML, is associated with skin pigmentation, pulmonary fibrosis and adrenal insufficiency. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Platinum Analogs). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: platinum compounds cross-link DNA, and cisplatin nephrotoxicity can be prevented with amifostine (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: platinum agents bind mainly the N7 position of guanine and work in all cell cycle phases (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Cisplatin's dose-limiting toxicities are nephrotoxicity and ","D":"Cyclophosphamide is an alkylating agent whose distinctive to","E":"Busulfan, used for CML, is associated with skin pigmentation"}},{"id":"sdl39_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the various medication names within each class and appreciate the important differences with each medication within these classes.","batch":1,"stem":"Which anti-EGFR antibody is a fully human IgG2 with fewer infusion reactions?","choices":{"A":"Panitumumab","B":"Cetuximab","C":"Necitumumab","D":"Trastuzumab","E":"Pertuzumab"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Panitumumab is a fully human IgG2 anti-EGFR antibody, so it is not expected to have immune-mediated effects, and infusion reactions are much less common than with chimeric cetuximab; acneiform rash is a main adverse effect. Cetuximab is a chimeric IgG1 with a higher risk of severe infusion reactions. Necitumumab is a fully human IgG1 for squamous NSCLC, not an IgG2. Trastuzumab is a humanized IgG1 against HER2, not EGFR. Pertuzumab is a humanized IgG1 against HER2 subdomain II, not EGFR. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Epidermal Growth Factor Receptor Inhibitors). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the -umab ending marks a human monoclonal antibody and -ximab a chimeric human-mouse one (First Aid Pharmacology, biologic agents). COMLEX: necitumumab is an IgG1 anti-EGFR antibody used for squamous NSCLC and carries thromboembolic risk (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Cetuximab is a chimeric IgG1 with a higher risk of severe in","C":"Necitumumab is a fully human IgG1 for squamous NSCLC, not an","D":"Trastuzumab is a humanized IgG1 against HER2, not EGFR.","E":"Pertuzumab is a humanized IgG1 against HER2 subdomain II, no"}},{"id":"sdl39_q07","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, mechanisms of action, metabolism, clinical applications and adverse effects for each of the following classes of chemotherapy: fluoropyrimidines, platinum analogs, epidophyllotoxins and growth factor receptor inhibitors.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old woman with stage III colon cancer gets her first cycle of an IV fluoropyrimidine with leucovorin and a platinum agent. Within a week she has severe mouth ulcers, profuse diarrhea, and confusion; absolute neutrophil count is 300/µL (reference 1,500–8,000/µL). Deficiency of which enzyme best explains this?","choices":{"A":"Thiopurine methyltransferase","B":"Dihydropyrimidine dehydrogenase","C":"Uridine glucuronosyltransferase","D":"Hypoxanthine phosphoribosyl transferase","E":"Cytidine deaminase enzyme"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Most of an administered 5-FU dose is catabolized by DPD. Partial or complete DPD deficiency, an autosomal recessive trait seen in up to 5% of cancer patients (Katzung Ch. 54 elsewhere cites up to 10% of the North American population), causes severe toxicity with the triad of myelosuppression, GI toxicity (diarrhea, mucositis), and neurotoxicity. TPMT deficiency raises thiopurine toxicity, not 5-FU toxicity. UGT1A1 deficiency increases irinotecan toxicity, not that of the fluoropyrimidine with oxaliplatin. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Fluoropyrimidines). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: 5-FU causes myelosuppression and is used for colon and pancreatic cancer (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: not every DPD-deficient patient presents with this classic triad (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"TPMT deficiency raises thiopurine toxicity, not 5-FU toxicit","C":"UGT1A1 deficiency increases irinotecan toxicity, not that of"}},{"id":"sdl39_q08","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, mechanisms of action, metabolism, clinical applications and adverse effects for each of the following classes of chemotherapy: fluoropyrimidines, platinum analogs, epidophyllotoxins and growth factor receptor inhibitors.","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man receives FOLFIRI for metastatic colon cancer. Two hours into the infusion he has abdominal cramps, watery stools, sweating, and salivation. Which drug best treats this early reaction?","choices":{"A":"Loperamide","B":"Octreotide","C":"Atropine","D":"Ondansetron","E":"Metoclopramide"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Irinotecan causes two forms of diarrhea: an early form within 24 hours that is a cholinergic event effectively treated with atropine, and a late form 2 to 10 days later that may cause severe electrolyte imbalance and dehydration. Loperamide treats the late irinotecan diarrhea, whereas this early cholinergic reaction responds to atropine. Ondansetron is a 5-HT3 antagonist whose action is restricted to vagally mediated and chemotherapy emesis; it does not block muscarinic receptors. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Camptothecins). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 39 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: irinotecan and topotecan block topoisomerase I and cause severe myelosuppression and diarrhea (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: irinotecan and its metabolite are cleared mainly in bile and feces, so liver dysfunction requires a dose reduction (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — secretory diarrhea and cAMP-mediated electrolyte loss","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung","whyNot":{"A":"Loperamide treats the late irinotecan diarrhea, whereas this","D":"Ondansetron is a 5-HT3 antagonist whose action is restricted"}},{"id":"sdl39_q09","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, mechanisms of action, metabolism, clinical applications and adverse effects for each of the following classes of chemotherapy: fluoropyrimidines, platinum analogs, epidophyllotoxins and growth factor receptor inhibitors.","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old man with metastatic nonseminomatous testicular cancer receives cisplatin, bleomycin, and a semisynthetic mayapple-root derivative. The derivative acts mainly by which mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Topoisomerase II blockade","B":"Topoisomerase I blockade","C":"Microtubule stabilization","D":"Guanine N7 cross-linking","E":"DNA polymerase blockade"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Etoposide, a semisynthetic podophyllotoxin from the mayapple root, complexes with topoisomerase II and DNA, preventing religation of double-strand breaks and impairing DNA synthesis. It is active in germ cell and lung cancers; renal dysfunction needs dose reduction. Topoisomerase I blockade is the mechanism of irinotecan and topotecan, not etoposide. Microtubule stabilization is the action of taxanes, not the mayapple-derived etoposide. Guanine N7 cross-linking is the mechanism of cisplatin, already in the regimen, not etoposide. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Epipodophyllotoxins). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: topoisomerase II inhibitors cause myelosuppression and alopecia and treat testicular and small cell lung cancer (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: topoisomerase inhibitors arrest cells in the S and G2 phases (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Topoisomerase I blockade is the mechanism of irinotecan and ","C":"Microtubule stabilization is the action of taxanes, not the ","D":"Guanine N7 cross-linking is the mechanism of cisplatin, alre"}},{"id":"sdl39_q10","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the various medication names within each class and appreciate the important differences with each medication within these classes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 59-year-old woman with metastatic colorectal cancer is starting second-line therapy. Her oncologist adds to FOLFIRI a humanized antibody that binds circulating VEGF-A. Which drug was added?","choices":{"A":"Cetuximab","B":"Necitumumab","C":"Trastuzumab","D":"Bevacizumab","E":"Rituximab"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Bevacizumab binds all forms of VEGF-A and keeps it from reaching VEGF receptors; it combines with 5-FU-, irinotecan-, and oxaliplatin-based therapy in metastatic colorectal cancer; unlike the anti-EGFR antibodies, it targets the ligand rather than EGFR. Hypertension, arterial thromboembolism, GI perforation, and proteinuria are key risks. Cetuximab is a chimeric antibody against EGFR, not a humanized anti-VEGF-A antibody. Necitumumab targets EGFR and is approved for squamous NSCLC, not VEGF-A. Trastuzumab targets HER2, not circulating VEGF-A. Rituximab is an anti-CD20 antibody used with CHOP for lymphoma, not an anti-VEGF-A antibody. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Vascular Endothelial Growth Factor). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the anti-VEGF antibody also treats renal cell carcinoma, NSCLC, and angioproliferative retinopathy, with hemorrhage and blood clots as adverse effects (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: it does not appear to worsen the usual toxicities of cytotoxic chemotherapy (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 1 — colorectal adenomas and malignant potential","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Cetuximab is a chimeric antibody against EGFR, not a humaniz","B":"Necitumumab targets EGFR and is approved for squamous NSCLC,","C":"Trastuzumab targets HER2, not circulating VEGF-A.","E":"Rituximab is an anti-CD20 antibody used with CHOP for lympho"}},{"id":"sdl39_q11","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the various medication names within each class and appreciate the important differences with each medication within these classes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man has metastatic colon cancer whose cells lack mismatch repair function and resist the older platinum drugs in culture. Which DNA cross-linking agent is least affected by this resistance?","choices":{"A":"Cisplatin","B":"Ifosfamide","C":"Busulfan","D":"Cyclophosphamide","E":"Oxaliplatin"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Tumors resistant to cisplatin or carboplatin through mismatch repair defects are not cross-resistant to oxaliplatin, which is used with 5-FU and leucovorin (FOLFOX) in metastatic and adjuvant colorectal cancer. Cisplatin is one of the older platinum drugs that mismatch repair-deficient tumors resist. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Platinum Analogs). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: platinum compounds treat GI, testicular, bladder, ovarian, and lung tumors (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: oxaliplatin regimens are also active in pancreatic, gastroesophageal, and hepatocellular cancer (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 3 — hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer and extracolonic cancers","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Cisplatin is one of the older platinum drugs that mismatch r"}},{"id":"sdl39_q12","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the various medication names within each class and appreciate the important differences with each medication within these classes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 57-year-old man on an anti-EGFR antibody plus FOLFOX for metastatic colorectal cancer develops an acneiform facial rash, muscle cramps, and tremor. Which lab abnormality is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Low serum magnesium","B":"High serum potassium","C":"Low serum sodium","D":"High serum calcium","E":"Low serum phosphate"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Hypomagnesemia and acneiform skin rash are the two main adverse effects shared by cetuximab and panitumumab; neuromuscular irritability such as cramps and tremor fits low magnesium. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Epidermal Growth Factor Receptor Inhibitors). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: anti-EGFR antibodies treat metastatic colorectal cancer and head and neck cancer (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: cetuximab given with radiation for head and neck cancer carries a small (1%) sudden-death risk and a black-box warning (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl39_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, mechanisms of action, metabolism, clinical applications and adverse effects for each of the following classes of chemotherapy: fluoropyrimidines, platinum analogs, epidophyllotoxins and growth factor receptor inhibitors.","batch":3,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman on an oral fluoropyrimidine for colon cancer has red, painful, peeling palms and soles. What is this reaction?","choices":{"A":"Acneiform drug eruption","B":"Fixed drug eruption","C":"Contact dermatitis","D":"Hand-foot syndrome","E":"Erythema multiforme"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Diarrhea and hand-foot syndrome are the main toxicities of capecitabine; the 5-FU it forms causes the same palmar-plantar reaction. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Capecitabine). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: 5-FU adverse effects include myelosuppression and palmar-plantar erythrodysesthesia (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: myelosuppression, mucositis, and alopecia are less common with the oral prodrug than with IV 5-FU (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl39_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, mechanisms of action, metabolism, clinical applications and adverse effects for each of the following classes of chemotherapy: fluoropyrimidines, platinum analogs, epidophyllotoxins and growth factor receptor inhibitors.","batch":3,"stem":"A 64-year-old man with metastatic colorectal cancer receiving an anti-VEGF-A antibody needs elective hernia repair. Why is surgery delayed after stopping the drug?","choices":{"A":"Prolonged neuromuscular block","B":"Impaired wound healing","C":"Raised anesthetic drug levels","D":"Adrenal insufficiency risk","E":"Reduced platelet production"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The main safety concerns with anti-VEGF-A therapy include hypertension, arterial thromboembolic events, wound healing complications, GI perforation, and proteinuria. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Vascular Endothelial Growth Factor). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: anti-VEGF antibody therapy causes hemorrhage and blood clots (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: postoperative chemotherapy usually begins 4 to 6 weeks after surgery to let the incision heal (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl39_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, mechanisms of action, metabolism, clinical applications and adverse effects for each of the following classes of chemotherapy: fluoropyrimidines, platinum analogs, epidophyllotoxins and growth factor receptor inhibitors.","batch":3,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman starts FOLFIRI. Which enzyme converts irinotecan to its 1000-fold more potent active metabolite?","choices":{"A":"Carboxylesterase","B":"Cytidine deaminase","C":"Thymidine phosphorylase","D":"Xanthine oxidase","E":"Monoamine oxidase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Irinotecan is a prodrug converted mainly in the liver by carboxylesterase to SN-38, a topoisomerase I poison about 1000-fold more potent than the parent drug. Cytidine deaminase converts a capecitabine intermediate to 5′-deoxy-5-fluorouridine, mainly in the liver. Thymidine phosphorylase performs the final intratumoral step converting capecitabine to 5-FU. Xanthine oxidase inactivates 6-mercaptopurine to 6-thiouric acid. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Camptothecins). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 39 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the -tecan ending marks a topoisomerase I inhibitor (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: myelosuppression and diarrhea are the two most common adverse events of FOLFIRI's camptothecin (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung","whyNot":{"B":"Cytidine deaminase converts a capecitabine intermediate to 5","C":"Thymidine phosphorylase performs the final intratumoral step","D":"Xanthine oxidase inactivates 6-mercaptopurine to 6-thiouric "}},{"id":"trial_sdl39_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the various medication names within each class and appreciate the important differences with each medication within these classes.","batch":3,"stem":"A 31-year-old man with small cell lung cancer is prescribed an epipodophyllotoxin whose oral dose is about twice its IV dose. Which drug is this?","choices":{"A":"Irinotecan","B":"Topotecan","C":"Etoposide","D":"Vincristine","E":"Paclitaxel"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Etoposide has IV and oral forms; oral bioavailability is about 50%, so oral dosing is twice IV dosing, and 30% to 50% is excreted in urine. Irinotecan is a camptothecin topoisomerase I inhibitor prodrug, not an epipodophyllotoxin. Topotecan is a camptothecin topoisomerase I inhibitor, not an epipodophyllotoxin. Vincristine is an M phase–specific vinca alkaloid, not an epipodophyllotoxin. Paclitaxel is an M phase–specific taxane, not an epipodophyllotoxin. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Epipodophyllotoxins). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the epipodophyllotoxins treat leukemia and lymphoma as well as testicular and small cell lung cancer (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: teniposide is the related epipodophyllotoxin listed with it (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Irinotecan is a camptothecin topoisomerase I inhibitor prodr","B":"Topotecan is a camptothecin topoisomerase I inhibitor, not a","D":"Vincristine is an M phase–specific vinca alkaloid, not an ep","E":"Paclitaxel is an M phase–specific taxane, not an epipodophyl"}},{"id":"trial_sdl39_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the various medication names within each class and appreciate the important differences with each medication within these classes.","batch":3,"stem":"A 60-year-old woman whose metastatic colorectal cancer progressed on first-line therapy starts an IgG1 antibody that targets VEGFR-2 itself rather than a VEGF ligand. Which drug is this?","choices":{"A":"Ramucirumab","B":"Ziv-aflibercept","C":"Cetuximab","D":"Regorafenib","E":"Pembrolizumab"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Ramucirumab is an IgG1 antibody against VEGFR-2, the main receptor mediating tumor angiogenesis; ziv-aflibercept is a decoy fusion protein that traps VEGF-A, VEGF-B, and PlGF. Cetuximab is a chimeric IgG1 antibody against the EGFR extracellular domain, not VEGFR-2. Regorafenib is a small-molecule tyrosine kinase inhibitor, not an antibody. Pembrolizumab is an immune checkpoint inhibitor that targets the PD-1 receptor on T cells. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Vascular Endothelial Growth Factor). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: monoclonal antibodies (-mab) target overexpressed cell surface receptors, while -ib small molecules act inside cells (First Aid Pharmacology, drug names). COMLEX: anti-VEGF agents raise blood pressure and the risk of GI perforation (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Cetuximab is a chimeric IgG1 antibody against the EGFR extra","D":"Regorafenib is a small-molecule tyrosine kinase inhibitor, n","E":"Pembrolizumab is an immune checkpoint inhibitor that targets"}},{"id":"trial_sdl39_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the various medication names within each class and appreciate the important differences with each medication within these classes.","batch":3,"stem":"A 68-year-old man whose metastatic colorectal cancer progressed on 5-FU starts an oral combination in which one component blocks breakdown of the other. Which combination is this?","choices":{"A":"Leucovorin-fluorouracil","B":"Irinotecan-cetuximab","C":"Gemcitabine-cisplatin","D":"Cytarabine-daunorubicin","E":"Trifluridine-tipiracil"},"correct":"E","explanation":"TAS-102 combines trifluridine, a weak TS inhibitor incorporated into DNA, with tipiracil, which inhibits thymidine phosphorylase so trifluridine levels stay higher; it works in 5-FU-resistant and RAS-mutant tumors, with neutropenia as the main dose-limiting toxicity. Leucovorin is a reduced folate that stabilizes the FdUMP–thymidylate synthase complex rather than blocking 5-FU breakdown. Cetuximab is a monoclonal antibody against EGFR, so the irinotecan-cetuximab pair is not an oral combination. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Fluoropyrimidines). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pyrimidine analogs such as 5-FU and cytarabine interfere with DNA synthesis (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: TAS-102 also causes diarrhea, nausea, fatigue, and anorexia (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Leucovorin is a reduced folate that stabilizes the FdUMP–thy","B":"Cetuximab is a monoclonal antibody against EGFR, so the irin"}},{"id":"grid_sdl39_q01","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the various medication names within each class and appreciate the important differences with each medication within these classes.","batch":4,"stem":"A 63-year-old man with resected stage III colon cancer begins adjuvant chemotherapy with intravenous oxaliplatin every 3 weeks plus a tablet he takes twice daily. Six weeks later the skin of his palms and soles is red, painful, and peeling. He has no mouth ulcers, and his neutrophil count is within the reference range. Which of the following sets of features best characterizes the oral drug most likely responsible?","grid":{"headers":["Primary action","Route","Key toxicity"]},"choices":{"A":["TS inhibition","Oral","Hand-foot syndrome"],"B":["TS inhibition","IV","Mucositis, cytopenias"],"C":["Incorporation into DNA","Oral","Neutropenia"],"D":["HER2 kinase inhibition","Oral","Hand-foot syndrome"],"E":["DNA cross-linking","IV","Sensory neuropathy"]},"correct":"A","explanation":"Choice A (TS inhibition, oral, hand-foot syndrome) is correct. Capecitabine is an oral fluoropyrimidine carbamate prodrug converted to 5-FU in the tumor; the last step uses thymidine phosphorylase, which is overexpressed in solid tumors. Its metabolite FdUMP forms a ternary complex with thymidylate synthase and methylenetetrahydrofolate, blocking thymidylate synthesis. The notes say it causes less myelosuppression and mucositis than intravenous 5-FU but a markedly higher incidence of hand-foot syndrome, and it pairs with oxaliplatin in CAPOX. Choice B (TS inhibition, IV, mucositis and cytopenias) is 5-fluorouracil, which must be given intravenously because DPD degrades it rapidly and which causes high rates of mucositis, diarrhea, and myelosuppression. Choice C (incorporation into DNA, oral, neutropenia) is TAS-102: trifluridine is incorporated into DNA, tipiracil blocks its breakdown, and neutropenia rather than hand-foot syndrome is dose-limiting. Choice D (HER2 kinase inhibition, oral, hand-foot syndrome) is an oral HER2 tyrosine kinase inhibitor such as lapatinib or tucatinib, which causes severe diarrhea and hand-foot syndrome but is not a CRC adjuvant fluoropyrimidine. Choice E (DNA cross-linking, IV, sensory neuropathy) is oxaliplatin, the intravenous partner drug, whose dose-limiting toxicity is peripheral sensory neuropathy with cold-induced dysesthesia, not palmar-plantar erythrodysesthesia. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 39, sections 1 and 2: fluoropyrimidines, platinum analogs, growth factor receptor inhibitors, and intra-class distinctions); Katzung Ch. 54 (cancer chemotherapy).","boardPrep":"Step 1: capecitabine = oral 5-FU prodrug activated by thymidine phosphorylase in tumor; more hand-foot syndrome, less myelosuppression and mucositis than IV 5-FU; DPD deficiency causes severe toxicity with either drug (Exam 3 notes, SDL 39). COMLEX: oxaliplatin → cold-induced neuropathy; TAS-102 → neutropenia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 3 — colorectal cancer pathways and staging context","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":40,"title":"SDL 40 — Review","questions":[]}]}]};